                                                      <p>If a bid is inadequate it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is sweetened it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is unsolicited it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is unwelcome it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p> </p>        <h3><b>7       Giants and midgets. </b></h3>        <p>Complete this extract using the expressions listed.</p>      <p>(<b>a</b> occurs four times, <b>c</b> three times and the others once each.)</p>      <p> </p>      <p><b>a</b> junk bonds    <b>c</b> corporate      <b>e</b> takeovers </p>      <p><b>b</b> junk bond     <b>d</b> equity            <b>f</b> leverage</p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=611 valign=top>                     <h1>ACQUISITION VIA JUNK</h1>                        <p><b> </b></p>                        <p>During the 1980s, new issues of [$1,text 12] multiplied   tenfold as more  and more companies issued large quantities of low-grade debt   to finance [$1,text 12]  or defend themselves against being taken over. The   development of this market for low-grade [$1,text 12] bonds was largely the   brainchild of the investment banking firm Drexel Burnham Lambert.</p>                        <p> </p>                        <p>The result was that for the first time [$1,text 12]   midgets were able to take control of [$1,text 12] giants, and they could finance   this activity by issues of debt. However, issuers of [$1,text 12] often had   debt ratios of 90 or 95 per cent.</p>                        <p> </p>                        <p>Many worried that these high levels of [$1,text 12]   resulted in undue risk and pressed for legislation to ban [$1,text 12]. Between   1986 and 1988 Campeau  Corporation amassed a huge retailing empire by   acquiring major department  store chains such as Federated Department Stores   and Allied Stores.</p>                        <p> </p>                        <p>Unfortunately, it also amassed $10.9 billion in   debt, which was  supported by just $l billion of [$1,text 12]. So when in   September 1989 Campeau  announced that it was having difficulties meeting the   interest payments  on the debt, the [$1,text 12] market took a nose-dive and   worries about the riskiness  of [$1,text 12] intensified.</p>                        <p> </p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>      <p> </p>      <p> </p>      <h3>8       Company relationships. </h3>      <p><b> </b></p>      <p>The following excerpt, from the <i>Financial Times, </i>looks at the whole  area of strategic alliances. It also uses the language of personal relationships.  Match the expressions to their definitions and then use the expressions to  complete the article, rearranging the paragraphs into a logical order. </p>      <p>(One of the expressions is used twice.)</p>      <p> </p>   <p><b>1</b> alliances - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>   <p> </p>            <p><b>2</b> lovers - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>            <p><b>3</b> liaisons- [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>            <p><b>4</b> one-night stands - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships formed between countries or   organizations/insect  trap known for being very difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships  lasting only a short time]            </p>            <p><b>5</b> spider\'s web - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>        <p> </p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=611 valign=top>                     <p align=center><b>DANGEROUS [$1,text 20]</b></p>                        <p align=center><b> </b></p>                        <p>[$1, select:/1/2/3]<br>  Equally, does IBM really understand where it   figures in Toshiba\'s strategy  of [$1,text 20] with all and sundry? The same   applies within the US itself, to IBM\'s place in Apple\'s growing [$1,text 20] of   relationships -  ­and vice versa.</p>                        <p><b> </b></p>                        <p>[$1, select:/1/2/3]<br>   AMD,   in its trumpeting of this week\'s agreement with Fujitsu, seemed to take   little account of the Japanese company\'s extensive network of relationships    with western companies, including ICL, Siemens and Amdahl. Does it understand    how it features, and could be manipulated, in Fujitsu\'s global battle against    IBM?</p>                        <p> </p>                        <p>[$1, select:/1/2/3]<br>   [$1,text 20] may give companies unprecedented flexibility. But, by the same token,  they render them unstable.   To revert to analogies from personal life, it  is far easier to control one\'s   destiny if one has just a couple of mutually  compliant [$1,text 20] than if one tries   to run a series of ever-changing [$1,text 20]</p>                        <p> </p>                        <p> </p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>           <br> ', 1, NULL),
(1984, 1, 'Dobrý den', '', 49, '', '     <h1> Dobrý den </h1>      <p> <br>    </p>      <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-obr1.gif alt= width=607 height=264>     <br>    <br>      <p> </p>      <p> </p>      <table width=99% border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=2>    <tbody>    <tr>    <td align=center valign=middle width=18% height=25>                     <p><img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-obr2.gif alt= width=15 height=11>  8:30</p>    </td>    <td align=center valign=middle width=38% height=25>                     <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-obr3.gif alt= width=38 height=12>     <br> </td>    <td align=center valign=middle width=22% height=25>                     <p>19:00 <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-obr4.gif alt= width=16 height=11> </p>    </td>    <td align=center valign=middle width=22% height=25>                     <p>22:00 <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-obr4.gif alt= width=16 height=11>  </p>    </td>    </tr>    <tr>    <td align=center valign=top width=18% height=100>                     <p>ráno</p>                        <p> <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-kohout.jpg border=0 width=97 height=74 alt=>    </p>    </td>    <td align=center valign=top width=38% height=100>                     <p>den</p>                        <p> <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-slunicko.jpg border=0 width=120 height=77 alt=>    </p>    </td>    <td align=center valign=top width=22% height=100>                     <p>večer</p>                        <p> <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-zapad.jpg border=0 width=135 height=47 alt=>    </p>    </td>    <td align=center valign=top width=22% height=100>                     <p>noc</p>                        <p> <img src=/tuit/img/textbook/aa-spanek.jpg border=0 width=107 height=80 alt=>    </p>    </td>    </tr>    <tr>    <td align=center valign=middle width=18% height=25>                     <p>dobré ráno</p>    </td>    <td align=center valign=middle width=38% height=25>                     <p>dobrý den</p>    </td>    <td colspan=2