(910, 13, 'Miss Prompan Nam', 'r_lim@hotmail.com', 'Re: I want person to traslate Czech languge to be English', 'Hello \r\n\r\n	I want person to translate Czech language to be Thai language or Czech language to be English language for an article quickly . \r\n	Part-time job  and  Allowance high . From computer company in Thailand .\r\n\r\n	If whoever want part-time job this , please to be in contact with r_lim@hotmail.com  quickly\r\n\r\n		\r\n				Thank you very much \r\n\r\n				Miss  Prompan Nam \r\n		        	           ( Secretary Manager )\r\n\r\n', '2003-04-02 17:03:13', 828, 0),
(911, 17, 'Nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: 17 pádů českého jazyka', 'Jejda, uz teď je mi líto vech studentů četiny - hlavně těch, kteří jsou teprve na začátku a vezmou těch 17 pádů váně :)))', '2003-04-03 18:56:01', 902, 0),
(912, 13, 'Carol', 'cdamioli@praguepost.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'You say you had two months to learn the language  did you succeed? If so, how, exactly? And what is the advantage of listening to Czech-language radio stations over the Internet, as opposed to just turning on the radio?', '2003-04-03 23:05:47', 908, 1),
(913, 14, 'Ke Cleveland', 'tsukisan84@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Legend', 'Man, that horse is so scary. I went to the movies there once, and I was like, Oh my God! But now I think it\'s cool.', '2003-04-04 00:49:50', 869, 0),
(914, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'Some people dont live in Czech and have to go online to listen to Czech radio.\r\n:)\r\n\r\n', '2003-04-04 02:45:54', 912, 0),
(915, 13, 'Matt Blewett', 'matthew.blewett@tiscali.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'See Scott\'s comment.  I can try sending you some mp3\'s which may be helpful, if you\'re interested give me your email address.', '2003-04-04 06:04:40', 912, 1),
(916, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'v centru?', 'I learned yesterday that when saying something like \" Oběd v centru je asi 150 Kc nebo 250 Kc\", that the \'v\' is not pronounced with a v sound but rather with a slight \'s\' sound.  My question is this: is it used like this in formal Czech as well or only when using every day speach?  Also, when there is another word that starts with a \'c\' following a \'v\' is it also pronouced the same way using the \'s\' sound?\r\nThanks!\r\nScott', '2003-04-05 02:43:15', 0, 0),
(917, 15, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'Hi Scott,\r\n\r\nIf \"v\" before \"c\" is pronounced similarly to \"s\", that\'s news to me! My experience - and textbook doctrine - has been that v changes to f before, among other things, c, but I\'m open to learn new things.\r\n\r\nFor the rules governing the changing of voiced consonants to unvoiced, see http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/reference/pronunciation/contrasts.htm. \r\n\r\nThis page covers the issue perfectly except for ignoring the fact that some times, the second consontant does not govern the first in Bohemia, but still sometimes does in Moravia. Not sure what sets these siutations apart, I mean, I\'m not sure if there\'s any logic in it. The most famous example is \"na shledanou\", which is pronounced as \"na schledanou\" in Bohemia, but sometimes? often? pronounced \"na zhledanou\" in Moravia. Theoretically it should be standard Moravian pronunciation, but I have trouble hearing it. Maybe I just have a tin ear.\r\n\r\nBy the way, if you have any Czech friends with whom you speak frequently, you can often hear the voiced/unvoiced conversions in practice in their English. For example, if you meanly force them to say \"I put my hat on my head\", it may come out as \"I put my het on my het.\" In other words, besides changing short a -- which is nonexistent in Czech -- to short e, they might unconsciously apply the voiced-at-end-of-sentence-is-unvoiced rule, changing the \"d\" to a \"t\". This also happens a lot with the \"z\" in the pronunciation of English plurals: \"I bought some bunss\" instead of \"I bought some buns.\" What\'s interesting is that, as far as I can tell, there is the occasional voiced/unvoiced conversion, or at least pair, in English: \"She owns catss.\" vs. \"She owns catz and (or: catzand) dogs.\"\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-04-07 16:18:38', 916, 1),
(918, 13, 'Panagiotis Asilanis', 'asilan@LAR.FORTHNET.GR', 'Translation', 'I know a girl who is from Prague. Please help me how can I say to her :\r\n1) I like you very much \r\n2) Would you like to go out with me ?\r\n\r\nSend me the answers and also the way they are pronunciated. I thank you in advance Panagiotis ', '2003-04-08 01:32:32', 0, 0),
(919, 15, 'Jakub', '', 'Re: v centru?', 'It must be definitely a missspell/misshear. There is no reason to pronounce \"s\" in this position.', '2003-04-08 10:50:20', 916, 0),
(920, 13, 'Jakub', '', 'Re: Translation', '1) Mám tě moc rád. [ma:m tje mots ra:t]\r\n2) la bys se mnou ven? [la bys se mnou ven] (question - raising intonation)\r\nor la by ses se mnou projít? [la by ses se mnou proji:t] (question - raising intonation)\r\n\r\n[] = like \"sh\" in english', '2003-04-08 10:58:59', 918, 0),
(921, 15, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'V odstavci \"Voiced vs. unvoiced\" (.../contrasts.htm) chybí jetě jedna dvojice:\r\n\r\n    afrikáty   c - dz', '2003-04-08 14:09:31', 917, 0),
(922, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'I agree Erik.  It was a surprise to me as well.  My fiance corrected me with this when I was asking her a question.  She said to pronounce the v as in \"v centru\" as an \'s\' or more of a \'vs\' sound.  One factor may be that she is from Morava.  Vsetin to be exact, where many people speak with Moravian accents :).  That is one factor that affects my Czech haha!  She doesn\'t pronounce the voiced/unvoiced sounds at the end of English words as much now that she has lived in London for 2 years, but when I first met here she did quite a bit.  However, I have friends in Czech who do.  They all laugh at my funny American accent when I speak Czech, because of the fact that I do the same thing.  \r\n\r\nThat none of you have heard of this rule before, maybe it is just Moravian slang.  What do you think?  I was under the impression that when you say this that you would make an \"f\" sound as you have all stated, but she confides that it is easier and more normal to make a \'vs\', or perhaps the way she made it sound it was \'fs\', sound before beginning the next word when it starts with a \'c\'.  Is it common when one is not paying attention to anunciation to make a sound like this when saying \'v centru\'?  Any comments or suggestions are greatly appreciated.  I would rather speak proper than with a Moravian accent :).\r\nThanks.\r\n\r\nScott  ', '2003-04-10 00:36:08', 917, 0),
(923, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Wow Erik!!!  Thank for the advice about not relating any one word in Czech to another in English.  That is so right that I always ahve to think outside the box.  I didn\'t expect such a vivid explanation to that simple question.  You are awesome! \r\n\r\nThanks again,\r\nScott.', '2003-04-10 01:03:55', 893, 0),
(924, 15, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'Hello Scott,\r\n\r\nActually, the \"Moravian\" accent is kind of like the \"US Southern\" accent - if you live there, you find it\'s actually a collection of accents. (I personally live in Brno. Admittedly, it\'s not a typical Moravian dialect region - it\'s kind a mix of standard spoken Czech and Brno idiosyncrasies. They\'re mostly in vocabulary, but in a thick Brno \"hantec\" accent there are pronunciation quirks, like ó for ou, as well.)\r\n\r\nI have heard a lot of curious pronunciations, but have yet to have heard \"vsentru\" or \"fsentru\". I would expect \"ftsentru\" just about anywhere. Keep in mind that the \"ts\" is kind of on the border with being alien to English - of course we have t, and we have s, but the transition between them in the Czech \"c\" is so fast that it kind of feels different than \"just\" ts. It helps to think of it one sound, even though it \"is\" two.\r\n\r\nAlso keep in mind that joining of one-letter prepositions and the words following them is standard Czech pronunciation (on all language levels), and it might be what your gf was pointing to. \"fcentru,\" \"fpraze,\" \"vbrně,\" \"zhořčicí,\" \"spepřem,\" \"skama se?\" (\"odkud jsi?\" - prob. Moravia-specific - z kam-a se), \"zbrna,\" etc. etc.\r\n\r\nBy the way, there\'s nothing wrong with speaking with a regional accent. It impresses some people when your acquaintance with the language is visibly tied to some real language-region, and not an imaginary \"national accent.\" At least in the first years after the Revolution, this was all the more true because textbooks taught spisovný Czech and acted like you could work with it in real life. When I started forcing people to speak with me to get my learning past the textbooks, I felt like I was learning the language all over again. NO j\'s at the beginning of to-be, HARDLY ANY é endings (replaced by ý), COUNTLESS weird yet ubiquitous endings like the dual-number -ama on tacked onto just about anything a speaker feels like (s kámoama), CONSTANT \"zeivotnění neivotného\" as I call it even with totally \"unjustifiable\" situations - \"má s sebou mobila?\" - let alone ones with some excuse - \"kup mi ledňáka [chocolate-covered cream pop]!\", \"dám si panáka [a shotglass or, here, a shot],\" \"nael jsem kozáka [a mushroom type],\" etc. etc. \r\n\r\nOne more example that deserves its own sentence: WEIRD-ASS **androgynous** say-what-the-foreigner-isn\'t words, like hadr/hadra [rag]. (Me: má hadru? Czech: ne, hadr nemám. Me, one month later: má hadr? Czech: ne, hadru nemám.) Fortunately, the only examples I know are hadr and řádek/radka. Well, I do encounter one other pair, prostor/prostora, but apparently it has some logic to it and it isn\'t all that common anyway.\r\n\r\nSorry for the long aside. In short - don\'t fret about speaking perfectly, don\'t worry about picking up local quirks, and fight the good fight against the lazy urge of both you and your Czech friends to speak English, because even today, you\'ll probably be missing out on a lot until you move past textbooks.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-04-10 16:43:20', 922, 1),
(925, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi Panagiotis,\r\nInteresting point, my perspective is slightly different from Jakubs. It\'s probably just me, but if you\'re trying to ask her out, I assume you\'re not (yet) on very intimate terms, so I personally would be a bit careful with expressing \'I like you\' in Czech (\'mit rad\' feels to me quite strong, I might run if I was told by someone I didn\'t know well... but again, that\'s me, she might like it, depends). \r\nA clever evasion here might be either a comment on something (a feature) you like about her or just saying that you like being/doing xy with her, which could then lead nicely to the invitation (again, I personaly would feel more comfortable to accept if you invited me \'somewhere\' not just vaguely \'out\').\r\nWhat do you think? Have you already asked her or would you like some additinal translation (how are you actually going to communicate on the date?)\r\nAnyway, fingers crossed,\r\nJana', '2003-04-10 21:17:42', 918, 0),
(926, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'Hello Erik,\r\n\r\nThank you so much for your help.  I cant tell you how much your advice has opened my eyes to some things.  I guess you could say that I am not looking inside of the box anymore :).  \r\n\r\nI do understand what you mean about the Southern Accent, as I live in Texas and have been here since I was 5.  I was born in England, and had a suffolk accent then when I came here I lost that and gained a Texan accent.  After much work, my father broke me of that and now I guess I have a slight way of pronouncing some words like a Texan, but for the most part I sound like a midwestern speaker.  So, being under so many accent changes I have analized our language and find it intriguing.  Especially Texan slang as it is a mixture of British, Southern, and Mexican accents.  People here say things like \"Ya heard \'bout that game t\'morra. -Yup.- Ya \'anta go? -Dunno- Ya\'an to? -Aight.\" It sounds funny, but when you disect these words, they are a mix of different variations that have been adapted over the past few centuries.  I enjoy figuring out how a certain dialect and words in that dialect came to be.  \r\n\r\nI said all of that to say that you are right and since I am more than likely going to have a Moravian accent, I should appreciate its distinctness.  It is hard however to do much talking with a native at all right now as I am still here.  However, in August I will be moving to Norwich where I will have more opportunities to speak Czech.  A few years from now I plan to move to Czech (more than likely Moravia),  upon where I plan to be an English teacher.  \r\n\r\nI am looking forward to moving past the textbooks and actually having conversations.  Thanks for all the help and encouragement.  Oh, yes I do the \"ts\" sound in \'c\' down.  My pronunciations are pretty good, despite the occassional studdering around the \'ř\' in some words.  It is not hard to pronouce the sound itself, but it is hard when it is in certain letter orders to do so, as my mouth just doesn\'t seem to form into some positions :).  I thought it was funny how one computer store salesman in Zlín told me that my \'ř\' sounded the same way his 6 year old son pronounced it. \r\n\r\nSorry for my large aside, but maybe that helps you to understand my situation.\r\nHave a great day!\r\nScott', '2003-04-11 01:56:52', 924, 0),
(929, 13, 'J', '', 'Ad %', '10% = desetiprocentní (bez mezery)\r\n10 % = deset procent (s mezerou)\r\n\r\nPodobně:\r\n50m = padesátimetrový\r\n50 m = padesát metrů\r\n\r\nKdy píu mezeru ve slovním vyjádření, píe se také ve značce.\r\n\r\nVýjimkami jsou fyzikální jednotky teploty:\r\n50 °C = padesát stupňů Celsia\r\n\r\na úhlové jednotky\r\n10° = deset stupňů i desetistupňový', '2003-04-16 02:11:28', 8, 0),
(930, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: Does Hyrman Mean Anything?', 'Yes, Heřman is derived from German Hermann.\r\nAnd heřmánek is derived from Heřman...', '2003-04-16 02:51:16', 569, 0),
(931, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: transgressive', 'You compared well: berouc = bioršc = taking and minuvi = minšwszy = having passed. The only difference is that in Polish the transgressives are commonly used in everyday speach, while in Czech they are considered realy archaic.\r\n\r\nI\'m not sure whether in Polish they change suffixes in masc./fem./neu. and sg./pl. In Czech they do.', '2003-04-16 03:08:40', 504, 0),
(932, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: stěna vs. zeď', 'Technicaly \"stěna\" means every vertical planar(2D) construction. \"Zeď\" is stěna that is build of small elements like stones or bricks.', '2003-04-16 03:42:07', 97, 0),
(933, 13, 'Jerry Horna', 'jerry.horna@usa.net', 'Greeting Cards in the Czech Language', 'Where can someone in the USA purchase greeting cards printed in the Czech language?', '2003-04-16 22:48:41', 0, 0),
(934, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Greeting Cards in the Czech Language', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nI went to google.com and typed in \"Czech greeting cards\" and received tons of feedback.  I think the easiest way to find what you are looking for is to do the same.  I honestly think one of the only ways you will find them in America is on the internet. \r\n\r\nScott', '2003-04-17 00:43:33', 933, 0),
(935, 15, 'Shannon', 'odinhuxley@hotmail.com', 'Seeking Czech Teacher in Austin, Texas', 'I am looking for a Czech tutor in Austin, Texas. If you are one or know of one, please email me at: odinhuxley@hotmail.com', '2003-04-18 07:09:03', 0, 1),
(936, 16, 'Shannon Elliott', 'odinhuxley@hotmail.com', 'Seeking Czech Tutor in Austin, Texas', 'If you are a Czech tutor in Austin or know of one, email me at odinhuxley@hotmail.com.', '2003-04-18 07:10:14', 0, 1),
(937, 17, 'J', '', 'Velikonoce - The Easter', 'Téma ke konverzaci mezi studenty (případně i učiteli):\r\n\r\nJak jste strávili Velikonoce. Co se Vám nejvíce líbilo? Co Vás nejvíce zaujalo na českých Velikonocích? Jak slavíte Velikonoce ve své zemi?', '2003-04-21 22:13:01', 0, 0),
(938, 17, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Velikonoce - The Easter', 'Letos u kamaráda - Holanďana, který ije na moravské venkově, v Sebranicích. li jsme s manelkou a dcerou na procházky, mezi procházkami jsem se díval na vagra, jak hraje Lord of the Rings na Playstationu. Vstal jsem o Velikonocích pozdě, protoe jsme se dívali na film Lord of the Rings dlouho do noci (vlastně do velikonočního rána).\r\n\r\nSamozřejmě jsem se povinně musel uchopit mrskačky, ale stejně jsem dostal od manelky vynadáno za to, e to dělám moc lehce. \"Natěstí\" kolovaly po vesnici i mistní chlapi, kteří mrskali tak, jak se prý má. Jetě k tomu pouívali větvičky nějakého jehličnatého stromu - jahlovice??? nevím - aby to bylo típlavějí.\r\n\r\nNic \"velikonočního\" jsme neuvařili - najedli jsme se v motorestu - ale dcerka namalovala vajíčka a manelka pekla sladký chleba.\r\n\r\n\"Co Vás nejvíce zaujalo na českých Velikonocích?\" - asi uhadnete.\r\n\r\n\r\nJak slavíte Velikonoce ve své zemi? - Nemám země své.\r\nErik', '2003-04-23 16:16:11', 937, 1),
(939, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Práci?', 'Promiňte muj Český protoe to je patný, ale mám otázku.  Jak se řekne Anglický práci? e.g. \"Kdy konči zítra v práci?\" nebo \"Začináte dneska v práci v 7 ráno?\"\r\n\r\n\r\nCan you please correct any of my mistakes both with grammatics and sentence structure.\r\nTaké, jak se řekne Český \"can you please correct my mistakes\".  To je matoucí mě.\r\n\r\nDekuji,\r\nScott', '2003-04-24 01:02:58', 0, 0),
(940, 13, 'Penny', '', 'Need help translating', 'Need help translating some church records.  Cannot include the correct diacritics because of my american keyboard. \r\n Die 14 Nonem Cris(?) 1899 Sep elivi Marti nuni Rezac aesoctis 67 annoruni.\r\n\r\n Die 22 Decem Cris(?) 1899 Sysrlivi (or Syselivi) Annsini Rezac aetatis 58 annorum.\r\n \r\n Die 21 mensis Junii(?) A.D. 1908 Baptizavi Jaroslaum Aron Karpisek nat um die 17 mensis Junii(?) A.D. 1907.  ex Fransico Karpisek et Rosa Kavan. Patrini Fuerunt Antonius-Francisca Kriz.  Adnotationes: cioroliter juncti.\r\n\r\n Die 21 mensis Junii(?)A.D. 1908 baptizavi Albinum-Wenceslaum Kriz nat um die 1ma (ma is raised up) mensis Martii A.D. 1908 ex Antonio Kriz et Francisca n. Karpisek.  Patrini Fuerunt Wenceslaus et Agnes Kavan .  Thank you for your help.', '2003-04-24 03:26:54', 0, 1),
(941, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Need help translating', 'Hello Penny,\r\n\r\nThese records are in Latin, not Czech. \r\n\r\n- I think \"Nonem Cris\" might actually be \"Novembris\", and thus November. Same with \"Decem Cris\". \r\n- \"anno...\" refers to years.\r\n- Marti nuni Rezac will probably by Martin Řezáč (or whatever - see old discussion), with a declension on the end - but I don\'t speak Latin, so I can\'t say which one. Likewise \"Ann\".\r\n- \"Baptizavi\" - something about baptism.\r\n- Patrini - most likely godparents.\r\n- ex... - most likely parents.\r\n- 1ma - 1st. 1ma mensis Martii - perhaps first week of March, although mensis sounds more like month.\r\n\r\nSorry I can\'t help more.\r\n', '2003-04-24 15:08:11', 940, 0),
(942, 15, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Práci?', 'I\'ll take a crack at it, though my Czech will certainly also contain mistakes.\r\n\r\nNejdřív bych se chtěl omluvit za svou patnou četinu. Neotravil bych s ní, ale mám otázku. Jak se anglicky řekne \"práce\"? Např. \"Kdy končí zítra v práci\"? nebo \"Dneska začínáte v práci v 7 hodin ráno\"?\r\n\r\n- Punctuation always follows quote marks in Czech\r\n- I\'m not bold enough to use the Czech for \"Excuse me... but...\" - it strongly feels like an <i>anglicismus</i> to me. Keep in mind that the opposition in \"but\" is very abstract in \"Excuse me... but...\" (it really means \"I regret that I am bothering you... but\"). I checked Google for instances of \"ale mám otázku\" and couldn\'t find an equivalent of the English idiom, so I chickened out and chose the \"polopatické\" [1] solution above.\r\n   - overall - develop an ear for what\'s an idiom in English. If you think something is an idiom, say it in a roundabout way and keep your ears open for how the person speaking with you expresses the same notion.\r\n- Jak se (lang-adv) řekne, not Jak se řekne (lang-adj)\r\n- language adjectives are not capitalized\r\n- not sure why, but \"kdy zítra končí...\" sounds more natural to me than \"kdy končí zítra...\"\r\n- If I\'m any indication, then years of torture await you as you learn to hear, remember, and systematically think about long and short vowels, even though you\'re doing extremely well.\r\n\r\n\"can you please correct my mistakes\" - \r\n\"Mohl bych (Tě/Vás) poprosit, abys(te) mi opravil(a) chyby (v tomto dokumentu)?\"\r\n\r\nTo mě vdycky mate. \r\n(The structure \"That\'s xxxx-ing for me\" is pretty rare in Czech:\r\nThat\'s confusing for me - see above\r\nThat\'s frustrating for me - to je frustrující\r\nThat\'s depressing for me - to mě deptá\r\nThat was pretty time-consuming for me - To mi trvalo dost dlouho.\r\n\r\n[1] polopatický - po + lopatě + ický - shovel-by-shovel + adj. - made painfully explicit and step-by-step, usually for reasons of dumbing-down (here more for reasons of my own dumbness). Often appears as an adverb (polopaticky).\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nErik', '2003-04-24 15:37:21', 939, 1),
(943, 15, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Práci?', 'Just a couple more notes - \r\n- watch out for cases where you need to use \"sv-\" and not a regular pronominal adjective (m-, va-, tv-, etc.). The key question here is: does it refer to the subject of the given clause? If so, use the reflexive \"sv-\".\r\n\r\nDál mi moje hodinky - \"He gave me my watch.\" \r\n   - Here, the subject of the given clause - the sentence\'s main clause, in other words, the sentence\'s subject - is \"he\", the adjective is \"my\": no problem\r\n\r\nMám s sebou hodinky svoje - \"I have <i>my</i> watch with me, not somebody else\'s.\"\r\n   - Here, the subject of the given clause is \"I\", the adjective is \"my\"; it thus refers back to the subject, and thus the reflexive \"svoje\" is needed.\r\n\r\nPromíňte mi moji patnou četinu. Omlouvám se za svoji patnou četinu. Omluvte, prosím, moji patnou četinu.\r\n\r\n- I just realized that the advice \"Jak se (lang-adv) řekne\" is a bit too strict - \"Jak se řekne (lang-adv)\" is OK too. In fact, the latter has more hits on Google (although many are caused by a joke existing on multiple joke pages).', '2003-04-24 15:55:11', 942, 0),
(944, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Práci?', 'Thanks for all the help!  I just have two questions.  When saying \"Omlouvám\" or \"Omluvte\" what is the significance of the \'O\'.  I understand the place that it is taking, but when I look the word up I cannot find any root of this, nor have I anywhere else I have looked.  I would like to have a complete understanding of it.  Also, I really am curious about the question I asked.  What does \"Práci\" mean?  Does it mean work?  The way it is used in many sentences leads me to think yes, but I would like confirmation.  \r\n\r\nThe hardest part I am having with speaking or writing Czech is thinking how to structure the sentences.  It is so foreign to my thinking of English or Spanish.  It has some similarities to Spanish sentences, but is still much different.  I guess that is one of the hard points about any language.  Like you said, much practice and a lot of suffering will be the only solution.\r\n\r\nThanks Erik,\r\nScott', '2003-04-25 01:49:04', 943, 0),
(945, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Need help translating', 'Die 14 Nevembris 1899 sepelivi Martinum Řezáč aetatis 67 annorum.\r\nDie 22 Decembris 1899 sepelivi Annam Řezáč aetatis 58 annorum.\r\n\r\nDne 14. listopadu 1899 pohřbil jsem Martina Řezáče ve věku 67 let.\r\nDne 22. prosince 1899 pohřbil jsem Annu Řezáčovou ve věku 58 let.', '2003-04-25 13:29:07', 940, 0),
(946, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Need help translating', '21. dne měsíce června L.P. 1908 pokřtil jsem Jaroslava Árona Karpíka nar. dne 17. června 1907 z Frantika Karpíka a Růeny Kavanové. Kmotry byli Antonín a Frantika Kříovi. Poznámka: ? spojeni.\r\n\r\nDne 21. června 1908 pokřtil jsem Albína Václava Kříe nar. 1. března 1908 z Antonína Kříe a Frantiky roz. Karpíkové. Kmotry byli Václav a Aneka Kavanovi.', '2003-04-25 13:47:33', 940, 0),
(947, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Need help translating', 'On November 14th, 1899, I laid to rest Martin Řezáč at the age of 67 years.\r\nOn December 22nd, 1899, I laid to rest Anna Řezáčová at the age of 58 years.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-04-25 16:58:48', 945, 0),
(948, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Need help translating', 'One the 21nd day of the month of June in AD 1908, I baptised Jaroslav Áron Karpiek, born on June 17th, 1907 to Frantiek Karpíek and Růena Kavanová (?!?!!!). His godparents were Antonín and Frantiek Kří.\r\n\r\nOn the day of June 21st, 1908, I baptised Albín Václav Kří, born on March 1st, 1908, to Antonín Kří and Frantika Kříová, born Karpíková. His godparents were Václav and Aneka Kavan.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-04-25 17:04:36', 946, 0),
(949, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Need help translating', '... His godparents were Antonín Kří and Frantika (lat. Francisca) Kříová. ...', '2003-04-25 17:28:14', 948, 0),
(950, 15, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Práci?', 'All of us probably have a lot of grief awaiting us for <a href=\"http://www.internet-tips.net/Email/SPAM_spiders.htm\">posting our real email addresses here</a>, but that\'s another issue...\r\n\r\nThe \"o\" in omluvit and omlouvat is kind of hard to decipher. Generally speaking, \"o\" is easy to decipher - it works like its own shape - it\'s for motions or actions that are \"round\", \"around\", or, more abstractly, \"whole\":\r\n\r\notelefonovat lidi - to call around (\"to call-around people\")\r\nobíhat - to run around\r\novládnout - to take control over (e.g. \"ovládnout svět, muahahahahaaa\")\r\nochránit - pf. of chránit - to successfully (\"wholly\") defend\r\nosuit - pf. of suit - to successfully (\"wholly\") dry sth. out\r\notáčet se - to turn around\r\noidit někoho - pf. of idit - to successfully (\"wholly\") cheat sb.\r\nokouřit - pf. - VULG.!! - what Monica did\r\nomývat se - animals - to lick itself clean\r\noznámit - to announce - \"to spread known-ness (the root \"znám\") around and around\"\r\nochutnat - pf. of chutnat - to taste (the whole thing, I suppose)\r\n\r\nOmlouvat - as well as a number of other verbs - doesn\'t fit into this scheme very well. I suppose you could associate it and a number of these mystery verbs (ocitnout se, opovrhovat čím) with the preposition \"o\", \"about\".\r\n\r\nWatch out, there is also the harder to explain, though basically similar, root ob-.\r\n\r\nPRÁCE\r\n\r\nPráce is indeed work, and the less dignified \"job\" and the more dignified \"labo(u)r\" (as evidenced by \"Héraklovy práce\" (Hercules\' Labors) and the Communist greeting \"Čest práci\'\", roughly \"May labor be honored!\" or \"Honor unto labor!\"). As in English, it also has meanings like [pronoun]\'s-workplace and [pronoun]\'s-work.\r\n\r\nSENTENCE STRUCTURE:\r\nNot much I can say besides the advice in that other post, except:\r\n- sentence structure is quirky in every language - try finding a logic in which English adverbs follow their verbs and which ones precede their verbs, for example. <i>Čest praxi, soudruhu</i>...\r\n- there\'s also the \"helper verbs and direct-object/indirect-object pronouns pile up in the \'second position in the clause,\' whatever that is, except when they don\'t\" rule:\r\nDal-jsem mu facku. (Past-tense-\'give\' past-tense-1st-p-sing-helper to-him a-slap -- I slapped him.)\r\nPřili mu ho dát (Past-tense-\'come\'-incl.-3rd-p-plural him-dative it-accusative to-give -- They came to give it to him.)\r\nNicméně mě to mate (Nevertheless me-acc. it (\"that\")-nom. confuses -- Nevertheless, it\'s confusing for me.)\r\nPodle naeho názoru mu ho dali před týdnem. (According-to our-gen. opinion-gen. him-dat. it-acc. give-past-3rd-p.-pl -- In our opinion, they gave it to him last week.)\r\n\r\nI can\'t think of except-when-they-don\'t examples right now, but I swear they\'re out there. Note the last example, where the \"second position\" doesn\'t start until the whole introductory phrase, \"in our opinion\", is over.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-04-25 18:17:40', 944, 0),
(951, 13, 'dangerdave', 'david_sypniak@email.cz', 'polish point of view', 'well.... it all here seemed to be so cool. i finnish my high school 2day and i \r\nreally dunno what to do so i thought about learning czech language.\r\ngood idea i think, now, 2know some extra unusuall language like....czech. french and english is not enough. or.... i\'ll work on my spelling in english.\r\nbig wet kiss from southern poland.\r\na jak nie po anglicku nie umiecie to tu macie sampla moich mozliwosci po polsku\r\n. jak mozecie cos o tym jezyku ciekawego przekazac albo\r\nznacie jakies linki to piszcie na tamten czeski adres\r\nmejlmi bejbi:)', '2003-04-25 18:54:27', 0, 0),
(952, 13, 'Emil', 'zukini1@yahoo.com', 'help with small Translation (Czech to English) Thanks', 'Jak na to:\r\nPripajet tenky dratek na test-point. Pripojit dratek na 3.5V (5Vmax!!) a pripojit telefon k emidroid nebo roemmi boxu. Zapnout telefon a po konektu telefonu s boxem rychle dratek od napeti odpojit.\r\n', '2003-04-27 20:13:37', 0, 1),
(953, 13, 'Emil', 'zukini1@yahoo.com', 'Re: help with small Translation (Czech to English) Thanks', 'Pouziti:\r\nPro pod-flahovani verse CB-13-xx, pro unlock, reflash, nebo opravu founu. Po podflashovani se na displeji objevi \"phone failed see suplier\"). K oprave pouzijte flex.\r\nK oprave zmrsenyho founu budete potrebovat full flash a eeprom', '2003-04-27 20:18:39', 952, 0),
(954, 13, 'anais', 'anais7814@hotmail.com', 'aide', 'y\'a t-il quelqu\'un qui parle français et qui pourrait m\'aider pour apprendre &#224; parler Tch&#232;que?? ( bah, oui, ce site est cool mais je comprend pas grand chose &#224; l\'anglais). Merci d\'avance.', '2003-04-27 21:51:20', 0, 0),
(955, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Need help translating', 'oops, typo. Thanks for the fix.', '2003-04-28 16:21:29', 949, 0),
(956, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: help with small Translation (Czech to English) Thanks', 'Instructions:\r\nConnect a thin wire to the test point. Connect the wire to 3.5V (5V max.!) and connect the telepohone to ??? (not a Czech word) or the ??? (not a Czech word) box. Turn on the telephone, and after it is connected to the box, quickly remove the wire from the voltage supply.', '2003-04-28 16:25:12', 952, 0),
(957, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: help with small Translation (Czech to English) Thanks', 'Actually, considering that this is a page about cell phone hacking from a site dedicated to cell phone hacking with a forum populated by cell phone hackers, I guess I should have even answered the first one.', '2003-04-28 16:35:21', 953, 0),
(958, 13, 'Emil', 'zukini1@yahoo.com', 'Thanks  Erik', 'Usage:\r\n\r\nIn order to under-flash version CB-13-xx, for unlock, reflash or phone \r\nrepair. After underflash, \"phone failed see supplier\" message appears \r\non display. Use flex to repair.\r\n\r\nYou will need full flash and eeprom to repair damaged phone.\r\n\r\nHow to do it:\r\nsolder thin wire to test-point. connect the wire on 3.5 V (max!!!) and \r\nconnect phone to emidroid or roemmi box. switch on the phone and once \r\nthe phone connects to the box, disconnect the wire quickly from the \r\nsource.\r\n\r\n', '2003-04-28 18:20:12', 956, 0),
(959, 13, 'Krassimir', 'krkd@mail.com', 'Czech Text-to-Speach engine', 'Do You know what is TTS engine? You TYPE a word, the SB of your PC\r\n SPEAKS it. English TTS are freely downloadable from Microsoft,\r\nthey \"drive\" the so called \"MS Agent\" - little buddy in the\r\ncorner of your screen. Exists something like this for the\r\nCzech language? Where could I download it? Please help me, if you\r\nknow anything about! PROSIM !!!\r\n\r\n\r\n              Kr.K.D. Sofia, Bulgaria ', '2003-04-29 09:18:32', 0, 0),
(960, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Czech Text-to-Speach engine', 'Try some of these links I found on google.com:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.pvoice.org/msagent.html\r\nhttp://www.aldostools.com/msagent.html\r\nhttp://avargas.tripod.com/msagent.html\r\n\r\nIf these do not give you what you are looking for, try going to google.com and typing in various things. e.g. TTS in Czech, Czech TTS.\r\n\r\nScott', '2003-05-01 10:51:29', 959, 0),
(961, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: Czech Text-to-Speach engine', 'http://www.pvoice.org/msagent.html.en', '2003-05-01 19:28:08', 959, 0),
(962, 13, 'Mira', 'mirathespy@hotmail.com', 'Where I put myself', 'So I have the text for this Czech song and I understand almost all of it except for one line: \"Kde jsem se tu vlastne vzal\". It definitely doesn\'t mean \"where I actually got married\". I assume \"vzit se\" means not just get married but also put yourself, as in \"put yourself into somebody else\'s shoes\", but I don\'t know how this fits into the sentence. Does it mean something like \"how I actually got here\" or \"where I actually came from\"? I know that\'s kind of a stretch...And this sort of sentence is the kind that nearly gives me aneurysms on a regular basis, with the important parts on the ends and all the little particles in the middle. I have twice as many problems with this sort of thing as I do with \"dostat\" and the imperfect and consonant clusters and adjective endings.\r\n\r\nTwo other things: what does \"kudy dal\" mean? This is from the same song (in fact, they use \"dal\" to rhyme with \"vzal\"...the rest of the song I have figured out). And where is the preposition \"u\" used, aside from the names of pubs?\r\n\r\nThank you ;)', '2003-05-05 10:15:47', 0, 0),
(963, 13, 'Krassimir', '', 'Re: Czech Text-to-Speach engine', 'Thank You very much, will try this links!\r\n                           KrKD, BG', '2003-05-07 13:14:42', 960, 0),
(964, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Where I was myself here he takes', '<b><i>So I have the text for this Czech song and I understand almost all of it except for one line: \"Kde jsem se tu vlastne vzal\".</b></i>\r\n\r\nIt is indeed a weird phrase. In short, in natural English: a cross between \"How the heck did I get here?\" and \"from where the heck did I come from/have I come?\"\r\n\r\nBelow are three more examples from the SYNEK extract from the Czech National Corpus. There were found using the following query:\r\n([] [word=\"kde\"] [] [word=\"se\"] [] [lemma=\"vzít\"])\r\nI was too lazy to learn how to look up the sources automatically (I\'m just learning to use it).\r\n\r\nA najednou vám... od jisté rychlosti... začne brizance děsně stoupat. Roste... kvadraticky. Já koukám jako blázen. Odkud se to bere? <b>Kde kde kde se najednou vzala</b> ta energie?\" naléhal Prokop zimničně. \"Tak řekněte.\" \"Nu, třeba v atomu,\" mínil Plinius.\r\n(obviously taken from Čapek\'s <i>Krakatit</i>)\r\n\r\n\"Dostaneme kníky a budeme číst. Sníme něco, vypijeme skleničku vína a - nehýbejte se! Já vás neukousnu.\" \"Co mně je?\" ptal se Prokop nesměle. Doktor se vztyčil. \"No, nic u. Poslechněte, <b>kde jste se tady vzal</b>?\" \"Kde tady?\" \"Tady, v Týnici. Sebrali jsme vás na podlaze a... Odkud jste, člověče, přiel?\" \"Já nevím. Z Prahy, ne?\" vzpomínal Prokop.\r\n(obviously also taken from Čapek\'s <i>Krakatit</i>)\r\n\r\n6:11 - Ve stanici zastavuje vlak z Berouna, jene bez Marky... Dáme jí akademickou čtvrthodinku. Blbost, <b> kde by se tu vzala</b>, jetě spí, jdeme na to...\r\n(unknown source)\r\n\r\nAs for \"vzít se\" in general, here are other ways it is used in the first fifty (NOT top 50!) instances I found in SYNEK (of which some were thrown out due to my clumsy query):\r\n\"kdy se to vezme (...)\" - when you take X (description of Y way, e.g. \"z hlediska úplného konce\")\r\n\r\n	kdy se to vezme kolem a kolem - when all is said and done\r\n\r\n\"kdy se to vezme do [e.g.] ruky\" - just the typical Czech reflexive equivalent of the English passive construction or \"you\"/\"one\" construction (when it\'s taken into someone\'s hand/when one takes it into its hand/when you take it into your hand)\r\n\r\n\"vzít se\" to marry, when speaking of both partners, as you mentioned.\r\n\r\n<b><i>I assume \"vzit se\" means not just get married but also put yourself, as in \"put yourself into somebody else\'s shoes\"</b></i>\r\n\r\nNope. Just an idiom (a weird one), like, for that matter, \"to end up\" in English.\r\n\r\n<b><i>And this sort of sentence is the kind that nearly gives me aneurysms on a regular basis, with the important parts on the ends and all the little particles in the middle.</b></i>\r\n\r\nIf you search the forum archives for the last two months or so, you\'ll find some posts I wrote for Scott touching briefly on Czech sentence structure - in short though, your complaint, intentionally or not, is a good succint, if simplistic, description of how Czech sentence structure works. I wouldn\'t worry about the simplistic part - you <i>should</i> start simple, and then refine or you\'ll just get frustrated, IMO.\r\n\r\n<b><i>I have twice as many problems with this sort of thing as I do with \"dostat\" and the imperfect and consonant clusters and adjective endings.</b></i>\r\n\r\nThat\'s understandable, I think, since it seems to be undertaught in Czech courses (it\'s so subtle and abstract on first glance that maybe Czechs don\'t even want to <b>try</b> to explain it - it\'s even worse than trying to explain English article English if you speak English IMO). The things you mention here are much more concrete.\r\n\r\n<b><i>Two other things: what does \"kudy dal\" mean?</b></i>\r\n\"which way to go on\", \"which way to go from here\", \"where to go from here\", \"where X should go on/from here\". Literally \"which-way onwards\".\r\n\r\n<b><i>And where is the preposition \"u\" used, aside from the names of pubs?</b></i>\r\nHere are the ways it is used in the first 50 hits in SYNEK: (adj./adv. in means adjective or adverb - either single-word, or adj/adv phrase.)\r\n\r\nat (e.g. u benzinové pumpy, u pultu, u luky, u dveří)\r\n\r\ncountless idioms dominated by other words, e.g.:\r\n\r\n	s pěnou u pusy - foaming at the mouth (presumably, not 100% sure)\r\n\r\n	co musím udělat je odkrtané u kadé poloky - every item of what I must do is checked off\r\n	\r\n	eny pěkně u plotny - th\'wimmn barefoot\'n\'pregnant like dey should (plotna means a burner on a stove)\r\n	\r\n	directly-or-indirectly-emotional-verb + u + srdce - chovat u srdce, hřeje vás u srdce\r\n\r\nu X - at X\'s place\r\n\r\n[verb] u [organization] - with/for (e.g. pracovat u velké společnosti, also: má místo u velké firmy - he\'s got a job with a big company)\r\n\r\nby (e.g. u řeky, u jezera, u pláe, u luky (!), u nohou, u dveří (!)) - as you can see, u is used for both at and by, but IME it\'s not a problem to determine which is meant from the context.\r\n\r\nwith (e.g. zůstal u něj)\r\n\r\nX je adjectoverbed u [group] - X is adjectoverbed among [group of people/animals] (e.g. pampeliky jsou oblibené u včel - dandelions are much favored among bees, more naturally with this particular adjective - dandelions are one of bees\' favorite flowers)\r\n\r\nto/at (návtěva u koho)\r\n\r\nX [<--some issue] skončí u Y - X ends up on Y\'s \"plate\"\r\n\r\nX-which-can-be-a-part-of-Y u Y - a Y X (kapsička u bundy - a jacket pocket, zip u kalhotek - a pants zipper, prsty u nohou - lower-limbs fingers - that is, yer toes!)\r\n\r\nX [usually a person] to má u Y [adj.] - X is adj. with Y (e.g. Jestli umí hrát, má to u mě dobrý - If you know how to play, you\'re alright with me)\r\n\r\nX je u person-Y [adj.] - X is [adv. or adv.-phrase] in Y\'s case/life/situation; X is adj. for Y (e.g. rozpory mezi ivotem a vírou jsou u ní zanedbatelné - in her case, the differences between life and faith are negligible; bylo to u něho vzácné - it was rare for him; Výraz jeho tváře jsem často vídával u hráčů pokru - he wore an expression I\'d often seen in poker players).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAs you can see, in the world of Czech (and BTW not only Czech) prepositions, saying a certain Czech preposition means a certain English one will only take you so far. It\'s helpful for a start, but then you have to throw it away and say... \"u\" means \"u\"!\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-07 17:59:28', 962, 0),
(965, 13, 'Charlotte', 'charloshka@yahoo.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'This is a great list. Do you have a corresponding list of these words in English? \r\nThank you,\r\nCharlotte', '2003-05-08 08:22:11', 906, 0),
(966, 13, 'Charlotte', 'charloshka@yahoo.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'This is a great list. Do you have a corresponding list of these words in English? \r\nThank you,\r\nCharlotte', '2003-05-08 08:22:38', 906, 0),
(967, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Woe is me', 'I really find the sentence structure infuriating, most likely because I\'m still trapped in my little English-speaking mind, and since English is a language whose sentence structure is made out of reinforced steel, as opposed to Czech whose sentences seem to be made of whatever material they make those bridges that sway back and forth. It also doesn\'t help that the prepositions can mean about eighty million different things. \r\n\r\nThank you, thank you, thank you, for your reply. It helped a whole lot.\r\n\r\nAlso: how do you say \"that makes me\" or \"you make me\" as in, \"You make me mad!\" or \"That made me happy!\"', '2003-05-08 10:59:08', 964, 0),
(968, 15, 'David Lester', 'lstr3@cs.com', 'what to take on trip', 'Hello,\r\nI am going to Czech in July.  I would like to take items that are difficult to obtain in the Czech Republic as gifts to the people I am living with.  Can you recommend things to bring?\r\nThank you,\r\nDave Lester', '2003-05-08 21:15:51', 0, 1),
(969, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Re: what to take on trip', 'Hi Dave,\r\n\r\nI know when I went to see my future mother-in-law, I took some things like chocalate chips, peanut butter, and various cooking things that are not common there.  I also brought some things for other people like Texas belt buckles(since I live in Texas), American Coin collection items (e.g. Birth Sets if you know the years of their birth), Shirts with things like American Flags on them(Anything with something American on it most children love or if you are in Britain, anything British would do the same), hats, and so on.  If you can talk to the people you are going to live with, I would ask them what their interests are and try to get a good image of them, then I would buy gifts based on what they are like.  I think a lot of it depends on the person.  I think that if you see something you think is special that it would be great.  I am not sure where you are from, but I know from experience that anything special I brought from America was greatly appreciated.  I hope this helps.\r\nMaybe someone else could give some more input.\r\n\r\nCheers!\r\nScott', '2003-05-09 00:43:30', 968, 0),
(970, 13, 'Penny', 'nickel@gwtc.net', 'Re: Need help translating', 'thank you again for your help with my translations.  Am doing family history and it is hard at times when there is a language barrier.', '2003-05-09 03:21:33', 941, 0);
\r\n\r\nJakub', '2003-12-30 05:30:11', 1248, 0),
(1250, 15, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Online Czech Morphological Analyzer', 'For online demo Czech M.A. see\r\n\r\nhttp://www.xrce.xerox.com/competencies/content-analysis/demos/czech\r\n\r\nLooks fine:)', '2003-12-30 05:36:19', 0, 0),
(1251, 13, 'Cyndi', 'cdwyer@lps.org', 'Re: phrase', 'Kevin,\r\n\r\nThanks for the information.  Could you please post it with the haceks so I can have it properly inscribed.  I will just give it as a late present.  Thanks so much.\r\n', '2003-12-30 09:18:16', 1246, 0),
(1252, 13, 'don', 'donek@xs4all.nl', 'Re: help - translation', 'Thanks, Jakub, I rather like the second version and will use it.\r\nIf I can help anybody with English or Dutch, don\'t hesitate to mail.\r\nHappy New Year!\r\nDon', '2003-12-30 15:34:06', 1249, 1),
(1253, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Levou botu', 'hi mark!\r\ni think it\'s got to do with something called \"dokonavost\", i can\'t think of a direct parallel in english but it\'s got to do with distinguishing wether or not the action carried by the verb has been finished or not (possibly, it can also refer to one-time vs. repetitive action, to be sure pls look up the precise definition, i\'d hate to confuse you). \r\n\r\nhere the meaning of the sentence is +/- \"he\'s put his shoe on his left foot and now he\'s putting on the right one\" but in more word-for-word translation the left shoe has been already put on and now the right is being put on.\r\n\r\nwhat exactly do you mean by \"verbs like...\"? i\'d say \"obouvat\" is used quite frequenly (well, as frequently as one needs to refer to the action). if you meant the \"obout\" vs \"obouvat\" difference, i\'d say some verbs can be modified this way to mark the timing of the action like (\"obleknout x oblekat\" - put on/wear about clothing, \"obout x obouvat\" is only for shoes) but some other verbs would sound funny/oldfashioned.\r\n\r\ni hope this helps a bit (this is quite advanced stuff, congrats and fingers crossed). happy new year,\r\njana', '2003-12-30 15:34:15', 1247, 0),
(1254, 13, 'mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'Re: Levou botu', 'thanks for the reply, Jana.\r\nPfff, Czech is really difficult. What I meant with \"verbs like..\": In Dutch we have an equivalent for the verb obleknout-oblekat (zich aankleden)which is frequently used. We also have an equivalent for the verb obout - obouvat (zich schoeien = which is only used for shoes)but in Dutch \"zich schoeien\" sounds oldfashioned. These little differences between languages make the study often hard. \r\nHappy Newyear\r\n', '2003-12-30 16:48:43', 1253, 1),
(1255, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: phrase', 'Cyndi, with czech keyboard now working, máme rádi nae vnočata hopefully is correct along with your name', '2003-12-31 00:02:16', 1251, 0),
(1256, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: phrase', 'A little correction:\r\n\r\nMáme rádi nae vnoučata!\r\n\r\n(vnuk, vnučka, vnouče)', '2003-12-31 13:16:41', 1255, 0),
(1257, 13, 'Tarla', 'nuriel@poczta.fm', 'is there any good soul who could teach me some czech?', 'or at least show me any good czech-teaching site? I would join the mailing list, but I have no proper access to the internet when I\'m in the city where I study, and therefore can\'t let my mailbox get clogged.. \r\nI don\'t know any czech, I\'m afraid.. my native lg is polish, and I speak a little bit of spanish and french,too.. should I mention english?;)\r\nI\'d be very grateful if anyone could help me..\r\n', '2003-12-31 23:12:30', 0, 1),
(1258, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: help - translation', 'I\'m glad to help you, Don.\r\n\r\nIf I can help you with Czech, also don\'t hesitate to e-mail.\r\n\r\nPF 2004\r\nJakub', '2004-01-02 22:23:26', 1252, 0),
