(1275, 20, 'Mary Tanghe', 'mtanghe@mn.rr.com', 'mail', 'Now that the PCV\'s are at their posts does anyome have any suggeations for mailing magazines and books to the volunteers? \r\nThanks... Mary Tanghe', '2004-01-30 09:34:04', 0, 0),
(1276, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: anglicismy v cestine..', 'Kdyz by nikdo neprisel s \"trefou do cerneho\", asi bych doporucil navstevu web stranek z oboru a trochu primeho vyzkumu. Budete muset sama sestavit nejake kriteria, podle kterych odlisete \"vicemene univerzalne\" anglicismy od \"zargonu jen nekterych\". Asi bude nejjednodussi pouzivat jako zdroj jen stranky o webu psane pro laiky.\r\n\r\nSefredaktorkou casopisu Computer byla a mozna jeste je byvala prekladatelka, nebo presneji receno zargonem lokalizatorka, software a i moji byvalou kolegyni (i kdyz jsme se temer neznali). Dokonce i psavala kdysi (a mozna jeste pise) o jazyce a pocitacich do Computru. Mozna Vam pomuze.', '2004-01-30 18:42:37', 1264, 0),
(1277, 13, 'Markos', 'jrs@bigvalley.net', 'Czech for exchange program...', 'Hey everyone! \r\nI hope you don\'t mind me bugging you.\r\nI am a high school student in California and have sparked an interest in spending a year abroad with the AFS (an American foreign exchange program). My attention was drawn to the Czech Republic because I thought it would be neat to see where some of my family came from. However, when I did some research on the language, I became a bit discouraged because the grammar appears to be very difficult! I speak German, but I didn\'t think that it would help because it isn\'t a Slavic language like Czech. I\'ve have always heard that immersing yourself in a culture will drastically improve learning a language, but I was wondering if any of you might be able to give some input. During my stay there, would it take months before I would be able to communicate effectively? Is Czech a language that doens\'t take too long to \"catch on\" to? Any advice would be much appreciated...thank you!', '2004-01-31 04:44:53', 0, 0),
(1278, 13, 'Jakube', '', 'Valentines Day!', 'Can somebody please translate \"Happy Valentines day, my crazy little gypsy !\" into Czech for me ??\r\n\r\nThanks very much,\r\n\r\nJakube', '2004-02-02 01:07:08', 0, 1),
(1279, 13, 'mynamemilan@seznam.cz', '', 'Re: Czech for exchange program...', 'If I were in your place,I would come to the Czech republic without any knowledge of the language.If you speak German than you must know DIE ÚBUNG MACHT DEN MEISTER.And practising here is the best thing you can do.If you have any questions,send me it,I\'ll be helpful.Cheers.', '2004-02-03 04:08:28', 1277, 0),
(1280, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: conjugations -- la plej utila pagxo', 'V časování českých sloves jsou chyby. Jedna je systematická: u vech sloves chybí 3. os. sg. ind.', '2004-02-03 20:43:08', 1242, 0),
(1281, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech for exchange program...', 'hi markos,\r\n\r\nyou\'re right, it takes time to cope with a new langugage. it\'s hard to say how much time it could take you (took me ages and still does) but your german would probably help quite a bit (czech has gramatical gender concept as german and 7 cases on the top of it. czech morpology is quite complex but spelling is much more phonetic than in english. also, it\'s relatively flexible in wordorder). \r\n\r\nthe good news is that many czechs speak english or german (i\'d say most young people english and some of the older german) and there\'s a large english speaking community in prague (why don\'t you have a look on the expats pages?).\r\n\r\nthe best advice i can think of is to consider what exactly you expect from your stay and how comfortable you feel about coping with language (and culture) barrier.\r\n\r\ngood luck,\r\njana', '2004-02-04 15:46:00', 1277, 0),
(1282, 17, 'Marco Bucchianeri', 'bucchia85@inwind.it', 'Pomoc! Help!', 'Hi! I\'m an italian guy, and I\'m interested in learning Czech language, because on April I\'ll go to Prague in a school trip and I want to know the local speech, and I want to speak it as best as I can.\r\n\r\nTo the creator of this site: I find your grammar very completed and I\'m learning all about nouns, adjectives, verbs, pronouns, and now I\'m ready to compose easy sentences and I think I\'m almost ready to compose the first complicated sentences. The only problem is that I can do this only by writing. The pronunciation is my problem. I think I learnt how to pronounce all consonants, the \"ř\" also, but I could not understand in any way how to pronounce the vowels, because my computer can\'t read the files you put as example for vowels.\r\nSo, I would ask you if you could have these files in a different form, as .mp3 or .wma or .wav or if you could send me a page, where you could explain me this pronounce by writing, by making comparison with the english ones.\r\nI will be eternally grateful with you. In advance, thank you very much.\r\n\r\nTo the readers of this forum: If you also can help me, I\'ve written my e-mail adress. I will be eternally grateful with you also!\r\n\r\nThank you very much! Na shledanou!', '2004-02-07 04:16:29', 0, 0),
(1283, 14, 'David', 'd.carr@baesystems.com', 'Re: Pivo in Praha 6', 'Yes - good bar.  I\'d been trying to remember its name. Any idea? Good food, too.  I had the fried goose liver.  But isn\'t it actually on Evropska?  \r\n\r\nI visited it last year with a couple of simpatico Italians.  We discussed attitudes to nudity in different cultures.  To the British, the attitude to the pictures would normally be \"nudge nudge, wink wink\".  To the Italians (and presumably the Czechs), the attitude is \"What beautiful pictures\"  (And Americans?...well!  at least they weren\'t wearing silver nipple shields!)\r\n\r\nI asked a waitress, and most of the pictures are by Tono Stano and Frantisek Drtkol (the R is the one with the hat which nobody can pronounce).  The National Gallery has lots of their stuff, and the shop sells an excellent book of Czech nudes over the last 100 years or so.', '2004-02-09 19:38:47', 1272, 0),
(1284, 14, 'David', 'd.carr@baesystems.com', 'Re: Slang, idioms, and other such fun things', '\"zakecat se\" definitely applies to me!  Here\'s something:  English has lots of words and idioms to do with ships.  I\'ll bet there is no easy Czech word for \"the horizontal mast that sticks out at the front of a sailing ship\".  In English - \"bowsprit\"  This must make the novels of Patrick O\'Brian pretty much untranslatable.\r\n\r\nI heard on the radio the other day that there is a Northern Indian language that has words for \"to express anger with a sideward glance\", \"to pretend to love someone\" and \"the noise made by mud and water when you stick your hand into a hole to catch a crab\".', '2004-02-09 21:00:18', 308, 0),
(1285, 13, 'Marco Bucchianeri', 'bukkia85@libero.it', 'Pomoc! Help!', 'Hi! I\'m an italian guy, and I\'m interested in learning Czech language, because on April I\'ll go to Prague in a school trip and I want to know the local speech, and I want to speak it as best as I can. \r\n\r\nTo the creator of this site: I find your grammar very completed and I\'m learning all about nouns, adjectives, verbs, pronouns, and now I\'m ready to compose easy sentences and I think I\'m almost ready to compose the first complicated sentences. The only problem is that I can do this only by writing. The pronunciation is my problem. I think I learnt how to pronounce all consonants, the \"ř\" also, but I could not understand in any way how to pronounce the vowels, because my computer can\'t read the files you put as example for vowels. \r\nSo, I would ask you if you could have these files in a different form, as .mp3 or .wma or .wav or if you could send me a page, where you could explain me this pronounce by writing, by making comparison with the english ones. \r\nI will be eternally grateful with you. In advance, thank you very much. \r\n\r\nTo the readers of this forum: If you also can help me, I\'ve written my e-mail adress. I will be eternally grateful with you also! \r\n\r\nThank you very much! Na shledanou!', '2004-02-10 02:38:06', 0, 0),
(1286, 13, 'Marco Bucchianeri', 'bukkia85@libero.it', 'Pomoc! Help!', 'Hi! I\'m an italian guy, and I\'m interested in learning Czech language, because on April I\'ll go to Prague in a school trip and I want to know the local speech, and I want to speak it as best as I can. \r\n\r\nTo the creator of this site: I find your grammar very completed and I\'m learning all about nouns, adjectives, verbs, pronouns, and now I\'m ready to compose easy sentences and I think I\'m almost ready to compose the first complicated sentences. The only problem is that I can do this only by writing. The pronunciation is my problem. I think I learnt how to pronounce all consonants, the \"ř\" also, but I could not understand in any way how to pronounce the vowels, because my computer can\'t read the files you put as example for vowels. \r\nSo, I would ask you if you could have these files in a different form, as .mp3 or .wma or .wav or if you could send me a page, where you could explain me this pronounce by writing, by making comparison with the english ones. \r\nI will be eternally grateful with you. In advance, thank you very much. \r\n\r\nTo the readers of this forum: If you also can help me, I\'ve written my e-mail adress. I will be eternally grateful with you also! \r\n\r\nThank you very much! Na shledanou!', '2004-02-10 03:27:52', 0, 0),
(1287, 14, 'Kiley', 'kmcermak@earthlink.net', 'Gypsies', 'Are Bohemians ancestorially gypsies?', '2004-02-10 22:29:57', 0, 1),
(1288, 13, 'Hanka', '', 'Re: Valentines Day!', 'Rekla bych, ze neco jako: Stastneho Valentynka, ma blazniva cikanecko!\r\nHanka', '2004-02-11 23:23:09', 1278, 0),
(1289, 14, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Gypsies', 'Kiley, There quite a few romanies, or gypsies in Czech but I doubt many Czechs would appreciate a gypsy ancestry. They are a slavic people with I guess bits and pieces of Vikings, Germans and probably a few others. However in my experience there is a significant prejudice against minorities such as the gypsy. Without it getting to heavy it might be interesting what others think on this', '2004-02-12 03:39:45', 1287, 0),
(1290, 13, 'Chris', 'idriveatruck@earthlink.net', 'Bride and Groom', 'My aunt is getting married and her fiance has a strong Czech background. My sister wants to get a personalized wedding gift saying bride and groom in Czech. Can someone please tell me what bride and groom are in Czech? I\'m pretty sure bride is nevesta, but I don\'t know groom. Any help is greatly appreciated. Thanks.', '2004-02-13 02:47:19', 0, 1),
(1291, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Bride and Groom', 'bride: nevěsta (neve^sta)\r\ngroom: enich (z^enich)\r\n\"To the bride and groom\": \"Nevěstě a enichovi\" (Neve^ste^ a z^enichovi)\r\n(encoding windows-1250)\r\n', '2004-02-14 04:48:53', 1290, 0),
(1292, 13, 'Pavel', 'patt@email.cz', 'Re: Bride and Groom', 'Bride is \"nevesta\" and groom is \"zenich\". If written correctly in Czech, \"nevesta\" has a little hook above the second E and \"zenich\" has the hook above Z. :-).\r\n', '2004-02-16 02:49:40', 1290, 0),
(1293, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: conjugations', 'Verbs are conjugated only in present in Czech. Otherwise, there is only one past tense which is composed (to be + past participle).', '2004-02-16 02:59:16', 1239, 0),
(1294, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'Are all people from the Czech Republic, or from Bohemia (the CR\'s western part) of the Romany (gypsy) ethnic group? Certainly not. Gypsies are a minority  -- maybe 2%, probably less -- of the people in the Czech Republic as a whole.\r\n\r\nAre all people who are ethnically Czech ethnically Romany? No, they are two vastly different ethnic groups, of which the most striking difference is much darker skin among Romanies. Their heritage leads back to India; Czechs\' leads back to the Russian steppes or something (and includes many more centuries spent in Bohemia).\r\n\r\nViewing even a single sentence of racist trash regarding Romany makes me dang well sick. I hope that none enters this discussion.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-02-16 13:43:30', 1287, 0),
(1295, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'vchod - east/exit', 'Is there an interesting reason the words in czech for \"east\" and \"exit\" are the same?  And are the other compass directions (jih, zapad, sever) much the same in other slavic languages or are these words unique to czech?\r\n\r\n', '2004-02-17 03:43:58', 0, 0),
(1296, 14, 'kiley', 'kmcermak@earthlink.net', 'Re: Gypsies', 'It seems that more Bohemians would be gypsies because the word Bohemian means Gypsy', '2004-02-17 07:37:11', 1294, 1),
(1297, 14, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'Bohemian does mean Gypsy? Maybe in your language.\r\nIn Latin, Bohemia (or Boiohaemia) means \"country of the Boii\".\r\nBTW, Gypsy means (ethymologically) Egyptian = another mistake of yours!\r\n\r\nBoii: a Celtic tribe, one section of which settled in Cisalpine Gaul around Bononia (Bologna, Italy) and another in what was later Bohemia, to which it gave its name.\r\n\r\nBohemia: (Czech Czechy, German Böhmen) historic country of central Europe that was a kingdom in the Holy Roman Empire and subsequently a province in the Habsburgs\' Austrian Empire. Bohemia was bounded on the south by Austria, on the west by Bavaria, on the north by Saxony and Lusatia, on the northeast by Silesia, and on the east by Moravia. ...  (Encyclopaedia www.Britannica.com)\r\n', '2004-02-17 17:39:18', 1296, 0),
(1298, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: vchod - east/exit', 'Yes, \"východ\" means both East and exit, because East is the direction where Sun exits up (literally \"vychází\") from the under-horizon.\r\nAnd \"západ\" is the direction where Sun falls down (\"zapadá\").\r\n\r\nIn Slovak it is the same, including \"sever\" - North and \"juh\" - South (\"jih\" in Czech).\r\n\r\nIn Polish \"wzchod\" and \"zachod\" are  similar to Czech. But North is \"polnoc\" - midnight and South is \"poludznie\" - midday or noon.\r\n\r\nI don\'t speak Russian or any other Slavic language, so I cannot give you examples in another languages.\r\n', '2004-02-17 22:10:30', 1295, 0),
(1299, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'czechonline@bohemica.com', 'Re: vchod - east/exit', 'Actually, English uses a similar word for \'slunce vychází\', i.e \'the sun is coming out\' which is more or less a literal equivalent.', '2004-02-17 22:18:01', 1298, 0),
(1300, 13, 'Jeff Grant', 'ardra@paradise.net.nz', 'Czech Palindromes', 'Greetings from New Zealand!I have made a lifetime study of palindromes in various languages.apalindrome is a word that reads the same backwards as forwards,such as \"madam\" in English and \"muzum\",dative of \"muzi\"(man)in Czech.\r\nI have seen the following Czech palindromes on an Internet list(accents omitted)and am hoping someone can tell me what the words mean.Thank you.\r\nCITIC,JETEJ,JEZEJ,JECEJ,METEM,MAVAM,MIJIM,MYM,NEPOTOPEN,NEZASAZEN,NEZARAZEN,\r\nSLILS,VYMYV,VEDEV\r\nI have also noticed the following words used on Czech sites on the Internet.Are they proper Czech words? If so,I would like to know what they mean too.Thanks.Is it possible some may be Slovak/ian not Czech?\r\nUOBOU,VOJOV,VYKYV,VYZYZ\r\nI am currently compiling a dictionary of palindromes,and it would be nice to have some more Czech examples.\r\nAny help you can give me would be greatly appreciated.Thank you.\r\nOur daughter had nearly a year in Sevetin as an exchange student in 1994,but her language skills aren\'t quite good enough for these words!  ', '2004-02-18 12:35:17', 0, 0),
(1301, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'navrhnout', 'Ahoj, all!\r\n\r\nI am still struggling with slovesy.\r\n\r\nThe latest is the sloveso \'navrhnout\' which my slovnik says\r\nmeans to suggest, draft, propose, etc.\r\n\r\nIn the various cases that would be what?\r\n\r\nI am a bit dense about the forms for this one.\r\n\r\nDekuju,\r\n\r\nGlo', '2004-02-19 05:21:37', 0, 0),
(1302, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'navrhnout', 'Ahoj, all!\r\n\r\nI am still struggling with slovesy.\r\n\r\nThe latest is the sloveso \'navrhnout\' which my slovnik says\r\nmeans to suggest, draft, propose, etc.\r\n\r\nIn the various cases that would be what?\r\n\r\nI am a bit dense about the forms for this one.\r\n\r\nDekuju,\r\n\r\nGlo', '2004-02-19 05:21:40', 0, 1),
(1303, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: východ - east/exit, vchod means entry, entrance.', 'In Russian:\r\n\r\nvýchod  -  vostok (east, orient),  but voschod solnca (= sunrise, sunup)\r\nzápad   -  zapad (west, occident), but zachod solnca (= sunset, sundown)\r\nsever   -  sever\r\njih     -  jug\r\n', '2004-02-19 13:46:19', 1298, 0),
(1304, 14, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'It must be really interesting school you are studying...............:-))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))', '2004-02-20 00:36:03', 1296, 0),
(1305, 13, 'David', '', 'Cheating Boyfriends and Girlfriends!!!!!!!!!!!!!!', 'This is a bit unusual to ask I know but can somebody translate the following into czech language for me : \r\n\r\n\"You don\'t know me, but I know you ! I think you should know that your boyfriend Pavel is having sex with my girlfriend.\"', '2004-02-21 22:20:28', 0, 0),
(1306, 13, 'pm', '@', 'Re: Cheating Boyfriends and Girlfriends!!!!!!!!!!!!!!', 'Ty mě nezná, ale já tebe ano! Myslím, e by jsi měla vědět, e tvůj kluk Pavel spí s mou holkou.', '2004-02-22 23:18:33', 1305, 0),
(1307, 13, 'vp', '', 'Re: Cheating Boyfriends and Girlfriends!!!!!!!!!!!!!!', 'Myslím, e bys měla vědět, e se píe BYS.', '2004-02-23 13:20:02', 1306, 0),
(1308, 13, 'Jan Vaněk jr.', 'jan . vanek . jr @ seznam . cz', 'Re: Czech Palindromes', 'I\'m replying with diacritics as they are very important.\r\ncítíc = feeling or smelling (feminine gender of participle to be used in constructions like Feeling unwell, she didn\'t go out)\r\njetej - that would be colloquial masculine form of, literally, ridden with possible meanings of aged and damaged by use, or also drunk\r\njeej - colloquial for they bristle\r\nječej - coll. they yell\r\nmetem - coll. we sweep (there\'s also medem, instrumental case of honey)\r\nmávám - I wave\r\nmíjím - I pass by\r\nmým - a form of possessive pronoun my/mine\r\nnepotopen - unsunk\r\nnezasazen - unplanted\r\nnezařazen - unclassed (all three masculine)\r\nslils - you poured together/off\r\nvymyv - having washed out (participle like above, masc.)\r\nvěděv would be having known (masc.), but I\'m not sure if it is formed properly.\r\n\r\nAs for the others, \"u obou\" (two words) is \"at both\" and výkyv (asymmetric accent, BTW the same would hold for muům) a swing aside; vojov is Slovak plural genitive case of, um, archaic word for column of soldiers.\r\n\r\nJust to make sure you have them, I\'ll also add a few palindromic sentences: The best-known is Kobyla má malý bok (Mare has a small flank), and than there\'s Jelenovi pivo nelej (Don\'t pour beer to a deer), followed by Adéla mu rum ale dá (Adela, though, gives it rum).', '2004-02-25 20:25:34', 1300, 0),
(1309, 14, 'Naďka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Slang, idioms, and other such fun things', '\r\nBowsprit is čelní stěeň (or čeleň) in Czech.', '2004-02-27 19:39:53', 1284, 0),
(1310, 13, 'Kylie', '', 'AFS exchange?', 'I am going to be an exchange student to europe, but I can\'t decide between going to france or the czech republic. I speak some french and a tiny bit of german and i would love to be fluent in french, but i think this is a great oppurtunity to learn a language i would never learn otherwise. Also, france does not seem as exciting! However my dad has scared me about going to the czech republic, he says the air is very polluted and it is still a dangerous country. How true is this? What does the average town look like? Is French a common language to study in school? Thanks anyone who can help!', '2004-03-06 03:29:00', 0, 0),
(1311, 13, 'Milly', 'elvinchick@hotmail.com', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'If I had the chance, I\'d go Czech. French is a more popular language and you can take classes just about anywhere in the US on it. Czech is a different story, and I wouldn\'t miss out on the oppurtunity if it were me. Best of Luck to you! \r\nMilly M', '2004-03-07 02:04:43', 1310, 0),
(1312, 13, 'PhDr. Marie Hádková, Ph.D.', 'maruskah@ffnw.upol.cz', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'You can study in Olomouc - what about some Czech for foreigners + politics, culture etc. \r\nLet me know - if you are interested in university course of Czech for foreigners.\r\nM. Hádková', '2004-03-08 21:01:52', 1310, 0),
(1313, 20, 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', '', 'Re: Rock Climbing in Albania?', 'Sorry for the belated response, Sarah. There is no official Peace Corps policy at the moment on rock climbing but as in all activities, Volunteers are urged to practice their best judgement and not put themselves in unnecessary danger. You should check with the Country Director and/or Medical Officer before you embark on anything that might possibly put you in unnecessary risk of injury. In short, you will have to wait till you get to Albania to get more details. \r\n\r\nDominik', '2004-03-10 19:56:00', 1270, 0),
(1314, 20, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: mail', 'Each Volunteer should have a mailing address at their site which you can use (it is up to them to share it). There is no unified address for reaching all volunteers.  \r\n\r\nDominik Lukes, PST Director', '2004-03-10 20:00:20', 1275, 0),
(1315, 13, 'Milly', 'elvinchick@hotmail.com', 'Where can I learn Czech online?', 'I want to learn Czech, but my only resource is the internet. Where is the best place to learn Czech online?\r\nMILLY', '2004-03-12 09:48:10', 0, 1),
(1316, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'kylie,\r\ni\'ve heard going to the states is dangerous, everybody owns a gun and i\'ll be robbed by a drug addict in daylight. i don\'t really think such stories are based on facts, bad things can happen everywhere, just have a look on the crime statistics. i\'m sorry to hear that your dad has such low opinion about my country and surpriced how persistant the cold war propaganda can be. the damages will probably take long, long time to repair. \r\nthe pollution situation is improving, although there is still space for improvements. policies and regulations got much tougher in the past years but as with everythig, it\'s matter of funding.\r\nin my eyes, towns look everywhere more or less the same - some houses, streets and service buildings put to one place. if you\'re thinking of university level, you\'ll need a city and my recommendation would be prague. the english speaking community is quite large there, so you\'d have the option if you need to cushion the culture shock a little.\r\ni wouldn\'t say french is very common language in the czech republic, most young people take english and some of the older generation speak german. i\'m pretty sure you could study in english there but you\'d need to check this (as it might depend on what exactly you want to study).\r\ngood luck with your studies, with your enthusiasm i\'m sure you\'ll have lots of fun and learn much. \r\nfingers crossed,\r\njana', '2004-03-12 15:19:21', 1310, 0),
(1317, 13, 'John', '', '', 'How do you write \"Happy Birthday\" in Czech', '2004-03-12 22:31:24', 0, 0),
(1318, 14, 'Amnon', 'Wahle@yotvata.org.il', 'burning the wittch-april 30', 'Dear readers.    Does anybody know anything about an old tradition of \"burning the witch\", celebrated annually on the 30th of April ? (Sorry, I forgot the Czech name of this tradition or feast). Thank you for any information !', '2004-03-19 14:50:39', 0, 1),
(1319, 14, 'Amnon', 'Wahle@yotvata.org.il', 'burning the witch-april 30', 'Dear readers.    Does anybody know anything about an old tradition of \"burning the witch\", celebrated annually on the 30th of April ? (Sorry, I forgot the Czech name of this tradition or feast). Thank you for any information !', '2004-03-19 14:51:29', 0, 1),
(1320, 14, 'Amnon', 'Wahle@yotvata.org.il', 'Re: Welcome to Czech Culture Forum', 'Dear Lukes. Thank you for your fast reply (or was it an automatic one ?!)\r\nHopefully anyone will read my question and answer quickly too (I understand that not many people take active part in the \'Forum\'...)\r\nBy-the-way: One of the questions I saw in the \'Forum\' refered to Holesovice quarter in Praha. My grandfather was born there, lived there as a child and teenager and left in 1936. In 1992 he and both myself and my sister visited the area, renewing \"on site\" some of his memories. Is there more information about Holesovice you can direct me to ?  Thank you for your reply, and ofcourse - for the so interesting for me and for my father website you have generated !!      Amnon.   ', '2004-03-19 15:30:51', 217, 1),
(1321, 13, 'Lindsey', 'gabe41@msn.com', 'The Czech Language', 'I\'m a college student and I\'m trying to put together a Czech language presentation. I need to figure out what, if any, language challenges a native Czech speaker would encounter while learning Englsh. Also, any social challenges that they might encounter. If anyone has any input, I greaty appreciate it! Thankyou! Dekuju! \r\n', '2004-03-24 04:10:47', 0, 1),
(1322, 20, 'Klejdi', 'clay@yahoo.com', 'Re: Tennis anyone?', 'Well why dont you guys try at that new place,hmm the one on the bottom of Dajti Mountain.I cant remember the name.', '2004-03-25 07:43:22', 1122, 0),
(1323, 13, 'Aliga', 'uruelia@yahoo.com.ar', 'Translation', '\r\nPlease, can somebody tell me how to say \'Be quiet!\' in Czech (the way you\'ll say that to a friend or a little boy)?\r\n\r\nDěkuji!!!', '2004-03-26 21:50:30', 0, 0),
(1324, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Translation', 'I think it must be \"bud\' ticha\" as I hear that constantly aimed by the wife at our daughter. If it helps in English the pronunciation would be more like butch tickar.', '2004-03-28 05:13:20', 1323, 0),
(1325, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'An first obvious challenge is the use of the and a.', '2004-03-28 05:16:03', 1321, 0),
(1326, 13, 'kevin', '', 'Re:', 't\'astny narozeniny, probably spelt wrong', '2004-03-28 05:19:39', 1317, 0),
(1327, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'You\'re not really expecting an unbiased answer from a site of czechophiles and czechs. Theres a lot to be said for France its a wonderful and beautiful country. The language is far more likely to be of future use to you than czech in isolation. Having said that it will be a far uniquer thing to speak czech. It will also open the possibility of studying other slavic languages which overall then might be more use than French. The CR is changing rapidly and as each year passes there is less of the original character and more tourism, so it would be good to go before its just another part of greater europe. Lastly your Dad is talking rubbish. Go to CR but make some visits to France.', '2004-03-28 05:32:38', 1310, 0),
(1328, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: navrhnout', 'Blimey Gloria I amazed none of the clever dicks on this site have answered you. I assume you mean the conjugation, being pretty dense myself I hope I\'m right. Navrhnu, ne, ne, neme, nete, nou hopefully. Also slovesy should be slovesa, that I\'m a bit more certain of', '2004-03-28 05:52:24', 1302, 0),
(1329, 13, 'Sue', 'smkil01@aol.com', 'translation', 'Can someone translate for me?  I am trying to book a hotel room via e-mail - I think this response means there\'s not a room available, but wanted to be sure.\r\n\r\nJe mi velice lito, ale s timto hotelem nespolupracujeme.  \r\n\r\nS pozdravem.', '2004-03-29 00:55:09', 0, 0),
(1330, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re:', 'If I were in your place,I wouldn\'t use this phrase tastny narozeniny.The Czechs usually say Ve nejlepí k narozeninám,in informal way just Vechno nejlepí.Of course,you can use another phrase,but as i said I wouldn\'t use the phrase Stastny narozeniny.Cheers Milan', '2004-03-29 02:19:44', 1326, 0),
(1331, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Translation', 'About the hotel:We don\'t collaborate with the hotel.Sorry.\r\nAbout BE QUIET.Either you can say Bud\' zticha or BudĄticho.Cheers', '2004-03-29 02:22:55', 1324, 0),
(1332, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'articles, phrasal verbs, intonation, tags, word order\r\npoliteness concepts and markers, indirect signals\r\npls let me know if you need more specific info.\r\nj.', '2004-03-29 15:08:21', 1321, 0),
(1333, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: translation', 'The text means:\r\n\r\nWe are very sorry, but we do not work together with this hotel.\r\n\r\nBest regards.', '2004-03-29 20:52:25', 1329, 0),
(1334, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'Not much to add, but:\r\n\r\npronunciation -- sounds that don\'t exist in Czech (hard and soft th, \"uh,\" etc.); carryover of Czech pronunciation rules, most notably Czech\'s g->k, d->t, z->s etc. conversion before certain consonants and at ends of words\r\n\r\nverb tenses, most notably the description vs. event distinction (he was coming vs. he came), which has no precise parallel in Czech (but unfortunately, does have the confusing imprecise parallel of dokonavy vs. nedokonavy)\r\n\r\ncarryover of Czech prepositions (a problem between any two languages), e.g. \"Welcome in Prague\"\r\n\r\nfalse friends (false cognates), e.g. \"actual news\" for up-to-date (aktuální) news\r\n\r\nhorrible fake British accents ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-03-29 21:05:53', 1332, 0),
(1335, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'oh yeap, prepositions! the trully are awful! i\'d say they probably are the most difficult one together with collocations. and one more i forgot: spelling!\r\nj.\r\n', '2004-03-30 15:16:59', 1334, 0),
(1336, 13, 'Heather Daniel', 'heatherdaniel10@msn.com', 'Help needed', 'How would you say in Czech - \'Can I buy you a drink?\'\r\n\r\nAny help would be greatly appreciated. \r\n\r\nThanks, Heather', '2004-03-30 22:56:33', 0, 1),
(1337, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Help needed', 'You can say Mohu ti koupit pití?,Mohu ti koupit drink?Or you can say among friends Můu tě pozvat na panáka?Or just Zvu tě na panáka.Or just Můu tě pozvat?', '2004-03-31 00:12:48', 1336, 0),
(1338, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Help needed', 'If you aren¨t ´being on first names,you have to say Mohu VÁM koupit drink(pití) or Mohu VÁS pozvat?', '2004-03-31 00:14:35', 1336, 0),
(1339, 13, 'PP', '', 'Translation', 'Can anyone teach me how to say \'I love you\' in Czech? I know it is \'mijuli te\' but I forgot the pronounciation. and also, what is \' I am exited to come in June.\'? Big Thanks! PP', '2004-04-01 02:17:28', 0, 0),
(1340, 14, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: burning the wittch-april 30', 'Looking this up on the web its the same as some German one word multi syllabled event. Didn\'t get much more info but I spoke to the wife and she said till the end of the communist times it was kept but called mírové ohně, rather than čarodějnice, to make it more acceptable alongside of May day. Is this a Czech tradition or is something imposed by the Austrians or whoever.', '2004-04-02 03:17:05', 1318, 0),
(1341, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nit\'s \"miluji te^\" (i.e. the e has a hacek), and is pronounced:\r\nMIH-loo-jih tyeh\r\n\r\n\"I am excited to come in June\" shouldn\'t be translated literally, as it would sound a bit odd word-for-word. I\'ll leave the translation to the actual Czechs, as I\'m afraid I would come up with something unnatural-sounding.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-02 20:03:15', 1339, 0),
(1342, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'I think the biggest problem with prepositions between any two languages is they\'re the first area where learners of their first foreign language realize (or fail to realize) the startling notion that \"vechno můe úplně, ale úplně jinak\", that words don\'t \"mean\" one or two things in another language, but just map to one or more words -- and with prepositions, it\'s really depressing, because e.g. \"everyone knows you go TO someone on a visit,\" whereas \"kady ví, e se chodí na návtěvu ZA někým\" -- I mean, it\'s something that feels really fundamental and set in stone, and all of a sudden you\'re using \"NA\" to visit someone in English, or \"BEHIND\" to visit someone in Czech, and it\'s... frustrating.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-02 20:09:09', 1335, 0),
(1343, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'There has been a tendency to name Gypsies, if you will, by the country they passed through, or were thought to have passed through, on the way to the country speaking a given language.\r\n\r\nGypsies - \"they must be from Egypt\"\r\nRomanichels (FR, almost certainly misspelled), Romany, Romové - although this is probably based on their word for \"man\" (Rom), often people think on this basis that they come from Romania\r\nBohemes, perhaps? French? In any case, just based on the mistaken belief that when they said \"we got here from Bohemia\", that they must be Bohemians.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-02 20:18:47', 1296, 0),
(1344, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: burning the wittch-april 30', 'It\'s also called Pálení čarodějnic. \r\n\r\nhttp://www.ceske-tradice.cz/Carodejnice/Index.htm describes it in detail, but unfortunately, the webmaster decided it would be a good idea to include special code on the page to block any kind of copying from the page. It\'s thus kind of hard to do a (partial) translation.\r\n\r\n- German Walpurgisnacht, CZ Valpurina noc (not that I\'ve ever heard that in practice)\r\n- night from April 30th to May 1st\r\n- a night when evil forces allegedly have greater power than otherwise\r\n- to protect oneself from evil on that night, one traditionally was to keep at hand a fern leaf, holy chalk (?!?), a communion wafer, and other special objects.\r\n- people believed that the sky was full of witches that night, flying to meet up for a black sabbath, which included various strange aspects as bountiful food, mouthwatering to look out, but without flavor or nutrition.\r\n- various local and culture-wide practices related to protecting oneself from this night existed, no point translating...\r\n- ...but above all, as protection against witches, it become customary to burn a fire in an elevated place. This practice is extremely old, and apparently did begin in Germany, though it would not seem that they were not \"imposed,\" but rather that the practice spread naturally.\r\n\r\n...more later, posting from work.', '2004-04-02 20:39:05', 1340, 0),
(1345, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation', 'hm, it\'s probably a matter of personal choice, but i think i\'d rather say \"mam te rad(a)\", then \"miluji te\" - it sounds to me a bit, i don\'t know, theatral (well, i\'ve never used it).\r\n\r\nyou\'re right, the direct translation would be likely to give the wrong impression, \"vzruseni\" relates more to describing physical (i.e. sexual) states. i\'d say the translation closest to the intended meaning would be something like \"tesim se, az prijedu v cervnu\" (+/- i\'m looking forward to...). the register is neutral, it can be used it in any context (at least, i can\'t think of an inappropriate one). spontaneously, i\'d probably say \"tesim se na tebe\" (+/- i\'m looking forward to seeing you but stronger, conveys this kind of desire to be together with the other person).\r\nj.', '2004-04-05 19:14:59', 1341, 0),
(1346, 13, 'Dave Windsor', 'dave.windsor@mildmay.org', '\"Let\'s do ...\"', 'Would someone help me with a general translation for phrases beginning with, for example, \"Let\'s do..\" or \"Let\'s go....\".  But given my, albeit limited, knowledge of the Czech language, I fear that the answer may not be so possible and that there may not be a general \'one size fits all\' translation.\r\n\r\nDave.', '2004-04-07 15:44:33', 0, 1),
(1347, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re:', 'I don\'t know if it is enough for you but I think the general phrase is:PojdĄme\r\nExamples:LET\'S have a drink--POJDĄME SE napít.\r\nLET\'S have a chat-POJDĄME SI povídat.\r\nLET\'S have a smoke-POJDĄME SI zakouřit.(unfortunately for you,it is not the only possibility,but I do believe it will help you too.Cheers.Milan)', '2004-04-07 22:25:20', 1346, 0),
(1348, 13, 'Erik', '', 'Re: Translation', 'miluji Tě -- I mean, not that I love you, Jana, I\'m just talking about the phrase -- yeah, you\'re right. It\'s the most subtle false friends that are the hardest to get rid of. I\'ll probably STILL go on using \"miluji Tě\" even after this n-th reminder that I shouldn\'t, just because it feels so weird to me to say (what feels to me like) \"I like you\" to mean \"I love you\" that I can only do it when I\'m conciously thinking about what I\'m saying, which is rarely. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-14 17:54:51', 1345, 0),
(1349, 13, 'J', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Let\'s do', 'Milan, and many other native speakers, including TV and other media, have a bad habit using exhortative phrases beginning with \"Pojďme\". This sounds rather colloquial to me (and not only to me), as it is used only a few years, but it\'s O.K. to use it at everyday speach now.\r\n\r\nBut in written or formal language you should not use first person plural \r\nimperative on \"jít\" and then use infinitive verb. You should change the \"main\" verb directly.\r\n\r\nThe Milan\'s examples then change to\r\nLet\'s have a drink. -- Napijme se. or Popijme.\r\nLet\'s have a chat. -- Popovídejme si.\r\nLet\'s have a smoke. -- Zakuřme si.\r\n\r\nMilan, would you translate \"Let\'s go.\" as \"Poj\'dme jít.\" nebo \"Pojďme.\"?', '2004-04-18 02:28:45', 1347, 0);
