(1701, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: One phrase:', 'I don\'t know about modern British English, but modern American English knows no such phrase as \"no one smaller than x\". And perhaps not even outdated American English.\r\n\r\nAnother useful English equivalent would be \'Well Charles of course!\'\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-06-29 19:10:14', 1697, 0),
(1702, 13, 'kristin', 'kdave_91@hotmail.com', 'czech word for fun, party', 'Hi - I want to do some artwork incorporating a czech word. We bought a 100+ year old house in traverse city, michigan (USA) that was originally built by people of czech origin. I am also a 1/4 czech (last name chuhak) on my father\'s side. \r\n\r\nSo my question: What are some good words to describe \"fun, party, having a good time, celebrate\"?\r\n\r\nI looked up some words on an english-czech dictionary such as: legrace, zabava, bavit. I like zabava the best. But how do you say it? \r\n\r\nAny other ideas for \"fun\" words in czech? \r\n\r\nThanks for your help,\r\nKristin', '2005-06-30 00:12:58', 0, 1),
(1703, 13, 'Simona', 'simona.kohlickova@centrum.cz', 'Re: translation please?', 'Hi, this is Simona living in Czech and I\'ve got a contact on this websides just today. If you dont have anybody for translation of the letter from your Czech friend still I will glad to help you! Bye for now', '2005-06-30 21:12:55', 1696, 0),
(1704, 13, 'Thomas', 'tomasbb2001@eresmas.com', '', '', '2005-07-06 20:33:08', 0, 0),
(1705, 13, 'thomas', 'tomasbb2001@yahoo.com', 'czech dictionary', 'I am used to learn by means of monolingual dictionaries, it is so a powerful technique... You read a definition in simple words and examples right in the language. Trouble is, in less studied languages like Czech I suppose there is no dictionaries intended for foreigners, so what I do in this cases is resort to children dictionaries. Ideally it should have definition, examples, grammatical and stylistical information, synonims and the like, and illustrations. I know is a dream, but can anyone tell me such a thing exist in Czech?  Much grateful.\r\nTomas, Spain ', '2005-07-06 20:42:24', 0, 0),
(1706, 13, 'Dave Windsor', 'windsok47@hotmai.co.uk', 'Prefix \'pidi-\'', 'Hi.  Recently, I keep coming across the prefix, \'pidi\', as in \'pidihrátky\', \'pidibába\',\'pidinitka\', \'pidihrádku\'.  I\'m assuming that it means small or diminutive, but I can\'t find anything in my Czech-English dictionaries.  \r\n\r\nThanks, \r\n\r\nDave.', '2005-07-07 13:04:12', 0, 1),
(1707, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Prefix \'pidi-\'', 'pidi- derived from píď = span (an old unit of length, 9 inches?)\r\n\r\nprimarily pidimuík = dwarf, manikin, gnome\r\n\r\nhence: prefix pidi- = mini-\r\n', '2005-07-07 21:01:19', 1706, 0),
(1708, 13, 'Dave Windsor', 'windsok47@hotmai.co.uk', 'Re: Prefix \'pidi-\'', 'Vladimír,\r\n\r\nNice one!  Thanks -- your explanation helps a lot.  Dave.', '2005-07-08 16:48:30', 1707, 0),
(1709, 13, 'Sarah', 'jadelimeade@yahoo.co.uk', 'Translating a sentence into Czech! Help please!', 'Ok, I\'ve got a friend who is Czech and I want to impress her by sending a text in Czech, but I don\'t speak a word of it (well, dobre den and prosim, but that\'s about it).\r\n\r\nHas anyone got any idea how to say in Czech:\r\n\r\n\"Hi, hope you have fun tonight. I look forward to seeing you on Sunday at Bovinton market.\"\r\n\r\nPlease help!', '2005-07-08 21:01:57', 0, 0),
(1710, 13, 'Sarah', 'jadelimeade@yahoo.co.uk', 'Translating a sentence into Czech! Help please!', 'Ok, I\'ve got a friend who is Czech and I want to impress her by sending a text in Czech, but I don\'t speak a word of it (well, dobre den and prosim, but that\'s about it).\r\n\r\nHas anyone got any idea how to say in Czech:\r\n\r\n\"Hi, hope you have fun tonight. I look forward to seeing you on Sunday at Bovinton market.\"\r\n\r\nPlease help!', '2005-07-08 21:02:41', 0, 0),
(1711, 15, 'Inn26', 'inn26_katia@yahoo.com', 'Advertising Sales Agents - Careers in Inn26.com', 'Inn26.com - World Accommodation Guide is looking for advertising agents for Prague. International employment. Online application. Payment is based upon performance. High starting commissions. For more information, please visit the link:http://www.inn26.biz ', '2005-07-11 14:29:37', 0, 0),
(1712, 13, 'George', 'happy2woof@hotmail.com', 'Re: translation please?', 'Hi Simona,\r\n> \r\n> Thank you very much for your help! I was thinking that no one \r\n> would be able to help me. Here is the text of the message. I \r\n> am sorry if the message is too personal but I do not know \r\n> what Petra will say!\r\n\r\nPlease send me your email address so I can send you Petra\'s message to me.\r\n\r\nThanks!!!!!', '2005-07-12 06:05:43', 1703, 0),
(1713, 13, 'Thomas', 'tomasbb@eresmas.com', 'Re to myself', 'Sorry, I rush into the forum I hadn\'t read \"Bookstore\" in this site. So seems that Filipec\'s is what\'s available. But I still think has gotta be some school dictionary for children usable by foreigners.', '2005-07-19 18:49:15', 1704, 0),
(1714, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'August is from Roman Emperor Augustine. July is from Julius Caeser. English, Italian and even Russian are very similar. December is similar in Russian language. But why Czech language is so different? Tell me the reason/origine of names of month in Czech language.\r\n(Cech beginner from Japan.)\r\n', '2005-07-22 13:15:47', 0, 0),
(1715, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Whatever I say, Vladimír will say it better, so this is just a holdover until he arrives. :-)\r\n\r\nThere was a period, during the era of Hapsburg domination, where Czech was nearly \"rescued from the dead,\" as it was in danger of going extinct -- Czechs were required to speak German. In the 18th century, there began a reawakening of the Czech national identity, and part of that effort was the revitalization of the Czech language. Since this was an artificial process -- specific decisions on the new face of the revitalized Czech language rather than the way a language naturally develops -- there are many words that can be traced back to specific \"reawakeners\". One of these is a man named Josef Jungmann. (It\'s ironic that a reawakener of the Czech language and identity has such a German name.) And it is often said that \"Jungmann devised the names of the Czech months.\" Whether or not this is actually true or just a myth, I don\'t know, though. My intuition is that this is incorrect -- that he picked up or at least worked with existing folk month names.\r\n\r\nI\'d also like to add my intuition that other languages had their own month names and lost them for Latin-based names for reasons of convenience, whereas having *Czech* names for the months was a matter of national pride for the reawakeners. Again, this is only my intuition. \r\n\r\nEven though this is one of the most asked questions about Czech, I know of no reliable online source of information on the etymology of months, let alone in English. However, I did find just now a few spots of reasonable veracity. Most useful was this page:\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.libochovice.cz/ln/art_pub_detail.asp?art_ID=589\">http://www.libochovice.cz/ln/art_pub_detail.asp?art_ID=589</a>\r\n\r\nThe alternative theory for březen comes from this page and exact copies of it:\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://voda.webz.cz/ces.html\">http://voda.webz.cz/ces.html</a>\r\n\r\nSo for what it\'s worth...\r\n\r\nLeden --> The month of led (ice)\r\nÚnor --> The vnoření (sinking) of ice during the February thaw\r\nBřezen --> One explanation is the budding of the bříza (birch) and the start of the time when it releases sap, formerly used as an elixir; the other explanation is that important kinds of livestock are březí (pregnant) at this time.\r\nDuben --> The budding of the dub (oak).\r\nKvěten --> The whole of nature květe (is in bloom).\r\nČerven --> The time for gathering the nachtový červec (some sort of bug, whose name recalls the word červ which, today at least, means \"worm\"; you can see the entry from the archaic Ottův slovník naučný <a href=http://kaktusy.box.sk/vyuzitie/kosenila.php>here</a>) used for červený (red) dye.\r\nČervenec --> See červen. \r\nSrpen --> The month of the srp (sickle), the harvest month.\r\nZáří --> Hmm, they say here that it\'s Za (during) the ří[je] (having sex) of game animals. I knew that about říjen, but září? Plus březen! THOSE ANIMAL-SEX-CRAZED MEDIEVAL CZECHS!!\r\nŘíjen --> Game animals having sex.\r\nListopad --> The pad (falling) of listy (leaves).\r\nProsinec --> The Libochovice source says \"prosiněti\" (until 50 years ago or so, it was standard for the infinitives of Czech verbs to be written with an utterly superfluous -i at the end until someone realized that was stupid), meaning prosvítati, meaning prosvítat, meaning \"to shine through,\" meaning heck if I know what they were thinking of. A <a href=\"http://www.muzeum-teplice.cz/historie/em0404.htm\">less reliable-seeming source</a> (one that says Jungmann invented the Czech month names) links it to \"prasinec\" (pig roast, something quite common in old-time Czech decembers). That explanation sounds to me to be a little bit too appealing to be true.\r\n\r\nJust FYI: Note that -en is a dead suffix; it is not used for word formation in modern words.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-07-22 16:17:40', 1714, 0),
(1716, 13, 'Milan Pokorny', 'czechlanguage@hotmail.co.uk', 'To Erik Piper', 'Dear Erik\r\nI have been watching your answers here to some people and it amazes me how much you know about my mother tongue.Therefore I would like to ask you.Are you English or any other national? Why are you interested in Czech ? I am sorry if I am too curious so if you prefer to send me a mail I will be happy to read it.If you do not want to reply then that is fine too .Thank you Milan', '2005-07-22 20:35:11', 0, 1),
(1717, 13, 'tom', 'ymmot24@comcast.net', 'Re: hospoda, hospodarnost, Hospodin', 'My last name is \"Hospod\" and from what I know it is of Old Church Slavonic origin and it means \"Lord\". i think that is the farthest back the meaning of the word goes.', '2005-07-26 09:52:00', 1618, 0),
(1718, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'I *live* here. :-) In the Czech Republic, I mean. Been here since fall of 93. So I *have* to be interested in Czech. I like helping other people and relaxing from my work at the same time, so I check this board and try to help if I can, whenever I can remember to do so.\r\n\r\nI\'m American.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-07-26 14:16:13', 1716, 0),
(1719, 13, 'Lizzie', 'slizz_@hotmail.com', 'names', 'My grandmother recently passed away and at her funeral I found out that her family (who came over from czechoslovakia) called her cachi (I spelled it phonetically, I don\'t know how it was actually spelled).  Her name was Caroline and so I was wondering if this is a common nickname for Caroline or if it means something else?\r\nthanks so much', '2005-07-30 06:12:38', 0, 1),
(1720, 14, 'Kateřina', '', 'Re: Is Czech food good?', 'Good^^', '2005-07-31 20:13:21', 219, 0),
(1721, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Thanks Erik,\r\nIt was a very kind information. I appreciate it very much. As a matter of fact, I have been to Praha on July 28-30 and just returned to Japan. I bought some books on Czech languages. The reason I started to learn Czech was the suprise of the similarity of Czech and Russian languages, the latter I learned when I was in collage 30 years ago.\r\nI am preparing a Homepage of Introductory Czech langauage for people who knows Russian language.\r\nIf you happened to find any interesting topics from Czech/Russian similarity/disimailarity, put it on the Board. \r\nThank you very much. \r\nnomonomo', '2005-08-01 06:33:41', 1715, 0),
(1722, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: names', 'There is a name Karolina in Czech, but it would be far more typical to use the nickname Kája (kah - yah) for that name. The nickname Káča, in the sixth \"case\" -- form of the word -- which is used for addessing people, is informally Káči (kah - chi), and thus probably what they were saying. This nickname, however, is associated with the name Kateřina (Catherine); why they were using it for a Caroline is a mystery to me.\r\n\r\nErik ', '2005-08-01 13:33:56', 1719, 0),
(1723, 15, 'inn26', 'inn26_katia@yahoo.com', 'Additional incomes for students...', 'Additional incomes for students, frequent travellers and dynamic individuals with flexible approach to their job-related duties. Inn26.com World Accommodation Guide is looking for Advertising Sales Agents worldwide - aged over 18, with excellent interpersonal and presentation sklills, positive attitude and great work ethic. \r\nPayment is based upon performance. High starting commissions. More information on http://www.inn26.biz and http://www.inn26.com. ', '2005-08-01 20:24:33', 0, 0),
(1724, 13, 'Kevin', 'Handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: names', 'One of the most endearing things in czech is the total flexibility when it comes to nicknames and/or terms of endearment. If you can shorten or lengthen the name with just a small connection you\'ve got it. To my wife and daughter I can be even with my totally unczech name, Kevinka, Kevinek, Kevko, Dadko, Dadisek and more. The freedom of expression in this is a wonderful thing and although it probably is not usual to call Caroline \"Kachi\" in Czech there seem to be no absolute rules, so if it suits use it (and long may it be so). I have noticed the Slovaks often use i where the Czechs use o my wife Stana (Stanislava) is addressed in Czech as Stano but by Slovak friends as Stani. So maybe there is a Slovak connection!? \r\nSome of these pronunciations will not be as we expect in english, and have no haceks etc., but hopefully this will be enough for you to get the gist', '2005-08-04 03:10:27', 1719, 0),
(1725, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'So what about you Milan why are you nosing about on a site for Czech yearner/learners, surely you should be looking at Albionca.cz if it existed', '2005-08-04 03:17:12', 1716, 0),
(1726, 13, 'Kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'Erik how come if you are so good at languages you can\'t spell Eric', '2005-08-04 03:25:37', 1718, 0),
(1727, 14, 'Kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Is Czech food good?', 'I know what you mean, but at least theres usually a lot of it', '2005-08-04 03:33:06', 1720, 0),
(1728, 13, 'Milan Pokorny', '', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'I am here because I want to learn something new about my mother tongue since I am a Czech teacher in Dorset UK and I really appreciate this web pages.Don\'t you want to know about English anything new as well?This pages are really brilliant.......Thanks for that', '2005-08-04 21:16:02', 1725, 1),
(1729, 13, 'milan pokorny', 'cazechlanguage@hotmail.co.uk', 'Re: names', 'Eric you confuse me because I do not know about you but I was always taught at school that addressing people is the fifth case. It is not really important but I just wanted to mention it.Milan', '2005-08-04 21:19:07', 1722, 0),
(1730, 13, 'Perica Baća (Baa)', 'bacciap4@kulawireless.net', '***Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Also Croatian, Ukrainian, Byelorussian and Polish language has theirown names of months which are similar with Czech.', '2005-08-05 01:53:01', 1715, 1),
(1731, 13, 'Perica Baća (Baa)', 'bacciap4@kulawireless.net', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'Director of Slavic department of Faculty of Filology Belegrade\r\n\r\n&#1059;&#1087;&#1088;&#1072;&#1074;&#1085;&#1080;&#1082;:\r\n\r\n&#1087;&#1088;&#1086;&#1092;. &#1076;&#1088; &#1055;&#1088;&#1077;&#1076;&#1088;&#1072;&#1075; &#1055;&#1080;&#1087;&#1077;&#1088; (Predrag Piper)\r\nhttp://www.fil.bg.ac.yu/katedre/slavistika/index.htm\r\nIs he your relative? Do you speak other language (serbo-croat)? I speak!\r\n\r\n\"Piper\" is name of one Slavic tribe from Montenegro :)\r\nI suppose next year \"Independent\" state!\r\n\r\nBye!', '2005-08-05 02:09:37', 1718, 1),
(1732, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: names', 'You are correct and I was in error. The worst part is, I know this; I just didn\'t check back over my post.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:04:53', 1729, 0),
(1733, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: names', '-i endings for fifth-case endings are quite common where I live (Brno) -- but I think in general, the Moravian dialect group is somewhat more \"Slovakish\" than the Bohemian one -- it only makes sense due to us being nearer to the Slovaks geographically than the Bohemians are.\r\n\r\nI\'m surprised to see the -ko endings above, since these are connected with feminine nouns and with masculine nouns ending in -a, but not with the -k diminutive endings. Are you sure they\'re not -ku?\r\n\r\nRegarding Karolina getting the colloquial root for Kateřina -- it could just be someone (including her herself) wishing she were named Kateřina and acting to fit. :-) Or perhaps a middle name... these aren\'t as big a deal in Czech as in (American?) English, but they still have their place.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:13:01', 1724, 0),
(1734, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'Actually, I\'ve always wondered why other people can\'t spell Erik. :-)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:15:40', 1726, 0),
(1735, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'I get no farther in Croatian than Dobrodoli a Dobar dan. :-) And I have no idea if those are the same throughout Serbo-Croat, especially since everyone started beating it into two languages out of spite (as far as I can tell). My blood is mostly from the Isles and from Germany, plus an eighth from Poland (but that has no connection to my being here in CZ).\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:18:58', 1731, 0),
(1736, 13, 'Perica Baća (Baa)', 'bacciap4@kulawireless.net', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'It\'s interesting for me that your surname is \"Piper\" and director of Slavic department of Filological Faculty Prerag Piper. Surname \"Piper\" is rare in the World as mine Baa (Baća) - shortly means Batiná (Baa as Tomá Baa from Zlín).\r\n\r\nSerbian and Croatian languages now are very similar. Croatian is 99%understandable in Serbia and Serbian is 99% understandable in Croatia! Standard Serbian and Croatian derived from neighbour similar dialects: Dialect of Dubrovnik (Croatian) Dialect of East Herzegovina (Serbian). That was the problem! Distance between these two (or one) dialects are about 20km. Croatia has a large number of dialects. But a large Serbs settled all ower Croatia during Ottoman period to defence zone \"Krajina\". These Serbs speak this dialect of Dubrovnik-East Herzegovina. So Croatian Reformer Ljudevit Gaj stopped one literar language (kajkavski - Zagreb region) and suggest to be used that dialect. The reason of that is creation of united Croatian language. This dialect was well known between Croatians. One time the Serbian and Croatian was united. But 10 years before war 1991 Serbian and Croatian was divided in two languages. Now there are Bosnian, with turkish and arabic words and the newest Montenegin, the language of Montenegro. But it\'s one language. Croatian has very large number of words with Czech origin. Montenegrin has a lot of words that are similar with Czech(npr. těpan - ćepan, ul - ul(ite)...).\r\n\r\nI used ex-Serbo-Croat term becouse I would like to comunicate with all people from ex-Yugoslavia. I suppose that tolerant peoples understand me.\r\n\r\nEric, I appologise if I cause embarassment, to force you to count blood cells.\r\nThanks for answer!\r\nBye!', '2005-08-05 17:39:36', 1735, 1),
(1737, 14, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'Czech painter Staffa or Stauffa from 1890s', 'Dear Forum,  This is a real stumper.  Can anyone tell me about a Czech\r\npainter named Staffa or Stauffa?   I have two little paintings by this painter\r\nof his daughters dressed in folk costumes.  Staffa or Stauffa lived in Prague\r\nandf these paintings were from about the 1890s.\r\n\r\nI was wondering is these have other than sentimental value or where they might\r\nbe appraised in addition.\r\n\r\nSince we are going to Praha next summer, I need to finance part of this trip.\r\nWhile I like these paintings by Staffa, I could part with them if they\r\nhelped with that.\r\n\r\nThanks for any tips,\r\nGloria McMillan', '2005-08-09 02:51:05', 0, 1),
(1738, 14, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'Re: Czech painter Staffa or Stauffa from 1890s', 'BTW, there is a hacek over the initial \"S\" in Staffa...', '2005-08-09 02:53:00', 1737, 0),
(1739, 13, 'Brian', 'bjlanc@yahoo.com', 'confused!', 'I have been attempting to learn the czech language and it hasn\'t been as easy as I thought it would be.  I bought a book to teach czech and I also got a czech english dictionary and all too often, there are a lot of different czech words for one word, for example,\"bring\".  I ask my mom and she only knows of one of the czech words for an english word.  She didn\'t know what the other czech words were.  My Grandpa is the same way.  Please help me to know why so many czech words for one enlish word.  THANK YOU!!', '2005-08-10 02:47:09', 0, 0),
(1740, 13, 'Brian', 'bjlanc@yahoo.com', 'confused!', 'I have been attempting to learn the czech language and it hasn\'t been as easy as I thought it would be.  I bought a book to teach czech and I also got a czech english dictionary and all too often, there are a lot of different czech words for one word, for example,\"bring\".  I ask my mom and she only knows of one of the czech words for an english word.  She didn\'t know what the other czech words were.  My Grandpa is the same way.  Please help me to know why so many czech words for one enlish word.  THANK YOU!!', '2005-08-10 02:47:15', 0, 0),
