(1744, 13, 'paola', 'paola.pajno@tesoro.it', '', 'please, how can I say in Czech \"I\'ll have some chances/hopes then!\"? ', '2005-08-10 14:35:50', 0, 0),
(1745, 13, 'Ralph', 'ralbion@hotmail.com', 'Obalis', 'Hello everybody,\r\n\r\nI come from Switzerland and my sister wants to call her daughter Obalis. Nice name, but I noticed that it means something in Czech...\r\n\r\nSo, what does Obalis means in Czech ?\r\n\r\nI would greatly appreciate your quick reply. Greetings from Switzelrand.\r\n\r\nRalph', '2005-08-10 16:33:33', 0, 0),
(1746, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re:', '\"Pak budu mít nějakou naději\", I think.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-10 20:07:17', 1744, 0),
(1747, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Obalis', '\"Obalis\" means nothing.\r\n\r\n\"Obalí\" means \"you [tu/du not vous/Sie] will wrap\".\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-10 20:09:33', 1745, 0),
(1748, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: confused!', 'There are a lot of words in *any* language for many of the words in *any* language. Language is a map of meaning; it\'s not meaning itself. Think of a political map of the USA and a geographic map: what is just \"Ohio\" in one will contain parts and wholes of a variety of geographic features in the other. What is just \"the Appalachians\" will contain parts of several states in the other. \r\n\r\nSometimes what one language likes to divide into several shades with different words gets lumped into one word in another language. More often it\'s even more complicated than that, and each language is using various shades, but they cover different things.\r\n\r\nSo for example:\r\n\r\ncarry - nést, přinést (carry to), odnést (carry from), etc., nosit, přináet, etc. ...\r\nbring - přinést, přináet ...\r\n\r\nand much much more...\r\n\r\nGood dictionaries will give at least a synonym for each word on the \"other side\", but since only the Czechs really care enough to make good Czech/English dictionaries, this will usuallly mean a Czech synonym. If you take a look at a good French/English dictionary in a bookstore, you\'ll see what I mean.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-10 20:19:48', 1743, 0),
(1749, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: ***Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Good information. They should have a same root. Somebody, tell me the origine, please.', '2005-08-11 10:16:17', 1730, 0),
(1750, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Names of floors of a building', 'The first floor is actually upstairs in England. It differs from ground floor. So is in many european languages including Russian. But in US and Japan first floor is equal to the ground floor. Someone told me Czech is the same as in US and Japan. Is it true? And why? Somebody tell me the reason.', '2005-08-12 11:22:16', 0, 0),
(1751, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Difference between venkov and vesnice', 'What is the difference betrween venkov and vesnice? In Russian dictionary both are dipicted as de\'revnya( &#1076;&#1077;&#1088;&#1077;&#1074;&#1085;&#1103; ). The russian is not so strict - this may be an answer.', '2005-08-12 11:28:06', 0, 0),
(1752, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Names of floors of a building', 'It\'s false. In Czech the \"první patro\", also called the \"první poschodí\", is the one above the ground floor, which is called the \"přízemí\" (\"při\" is \"by\" and \"zemi\" is \"the ground\").\r\n\r\nI had trouble with it too, despite previous experience with French. :-)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-12 15:46:25', 1750, 0),
(1753, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Difference between venkov and vesnice', 'In Czech the difference is quite simple and fundamental - \"venkov\" is \"rural areas\" (but it\'s a collective noun in Czech), while \"vesnice\" is \"village\".\r\n\r\nVesnice is an ordinary part of day to day speech, while venkov is used in some specific contexts...\r\n\r\n- the naming of administrative districts, e.g. Brno-venkov (Brno being my \"home\" city) means \"Brno\'s administrative district for the rural areas that are within its competency.\" (Actually, looking at the Web pages containing \"venkov,\" it seems that maybe no other district is named \"venkov\". Maybe my vision is distorted by my location.)\r\n- the rural areas of (generally) the Czech Republic, as a whole, e.g. \"Centrum obnovy venkovy\"... although phrases like \"český venkov\" and \"ná venkov\" are also used. (However, \"ná venkov\" -- which I saw with no prior direct indication   that \"our\" means \"Czech\" -- is a nice example of the tendency to use \"our\" to mean \"Czech\" in Czech.)\r\n\r\nOkay, enough, enough.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-12 15:56:37', 1751, 0),
(1754, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech painter Staffa or Stauffa from 1890s', 'Sadly, Google is of no help -- no painter taffa\'s are coming up.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-08-16 18:31:55', 1737, 0),
(1755, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Names of floors of a building', 'Thanks, Erik. I will look into this matter further.', '2005-08-22 13:38:10', 1752, 0),
(1756, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Difference between venkov and vesnice', 'Thaks a lot, Erik. It is very kind and informative. Actually, I fell in love with Czech!', '2005-08-22 13:42:00', 1753, 0),
(1757, 16, 'HELPER1', 'ALCOR69_69@YAHOO.COM', 'I found the best phone card ever to CZECH REPUBLIC', '\r\nI have been using www.callingforcheap.com for a couple months now. \r\nI call home to my country for 3 cents a minute, no connection fees/maintenance fees and nearest minute rounding! \r\n\r\nI LOVE the online account management, SPEED DIALING \r\nand NO PINS EVER!\r\n\r\nI used to go to the local convenience store to buy prepaids, but all the extra fees drove almost drove me crazy.. a 2 hour card was really worth an hourwhat a scam.  \r\nI love www.callingforcheap.com card. I also got 368 minutes free on sign up! I think you can get up to 450 or so depending on the plan. The call quality has been great too. I suggest everyone check them out..\r\nThis is the best international calling card ever! \r\nTo bad it took me almost 2 years here in the US to find it.\r\n', '2005-08-23 04:06:34', 0, 0),
(1758, 14, 'Czechoslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU)', 'SVU1@comcast.net', 'New book about Czechs and Slovaks in America', 'New Book about Czechs and Slovaks in America \r\n\r\nOn the occasion of his 75th birthday, the Czechosloslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU) has just published a volume of selected writings of Miloslav Rechcigl, Jr., who is the Society\'s current President. It comprises general surveys, essays, reflections and personal insights of the author relating to the history and the contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America and their descendants. \r\n\r\nThe material covered is based on Rechcigl\'s extensive research, which he began soon after his arrival in the US in 1950. It encompasses a wealth of information about the Czech and Slovak immigrants in America, from the time of the first known Bohemian who put his foot on American soil toward the end of 16th century, some thirty-five years before the arrival of the Pilgrims at Plymouth, MA in 1620, to date. Dr. Rechcigl has always been very intrigued by the life and accomplishments of Augustine Herman, the first historically known Bohemian to permanently settle in America a - the eminent Maryland and Virginia pioneer map maker, the Bohemian Jesuit missionary work in Latin America in the late 17th century, the Moravian Brethren?s Diaspora and their cultural contributions to America, and the immigration and the achievements of the Jewish pioneer settlers from the territory of the former Czechoslovakia, which is reflected in the monograph. Covered are also numerous contributions of Czech and Slovaks to the growth and development oi the United States, including science scholarship. \r\n\r\nApart from American ethnic history, Rechcigl has become an authority on the Czech American bibliography and genealogy which is the reason why the editors have also included his writings in this area. It complements the rest of the material remarkably well and enriches its contents and the utility of the volume as a documentary reference.The writings are based on the heretofore unpublished author\'s manuscripts, as well as on his selected periodical articles and book chapters that have been, for the most part, brought up to date. The volume is supplemented with Rechcigl\'s biographical sketch and his fascinating and provocative self-assessment ?What makes him tick? from his yet unpublished Memoirs, and the listing of his writings relating to the subject of this monograph, as well as articles about him \r\n\r\nThe monograph is a tribute to Mila Rechcigl for all his work of almost fifty years which he has devoted to building SVU, an international society aimed at the advancement of the Czech and Slovak studies worldwide and the fostering of intellectual collaboration with the Czech and Slovak Republics, which extends to almost fifty fears of his professional life and to his extraordinary scholarship relating to the history and contributions of Czechs and Slovaks abroad.We highly recommend this timely and unique publication to anyone who is interested in the history and contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America. The book is an important source of information much of which has not been generally known. \r\n\r\nThe book which normally sells for $40, can be purchased by SVU members for $20 plus $5 for postage and handling. Please send your orders with your check to SVU Treasurer Frank Mucha: 36 West 88th St., New York, NY 10024. \r\n\r\n\r\n                                                                                 SVU Press Release \r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n', '2005-08-23 18:14:16', 0, 1),
(1759, 14, 'Czechoslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU)', 'SVU1@comcast.net', 'Re: Czech immigration to Americas?', 'New Book about Czechs and Slovaks in America \r\n\r\nOn the occasion of his 75th birthday, the Czechosloslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU) has just published a volume of selected writings of Miloslav Rechcigl, Jr., who is the Society\'s current President. It comprises general surveys, essays, reflections and personal insights of the author relating to the history and the contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America and their descendants. \r\n\r\nThe material covered is based on Rechcigl\'s extensive research, which he began soon after his arrival in the US in 1950. It encompasses a wealth of information about the Czech and Slovak immigrants in America, from the time of the first known Bohemian who put his foot on American soil toward the end of 16th century, some thirty-five years before the arrival of the Pilgrims at Plymouth, MA in 1620, to date. Dr. Rechcigl has always been very intrigued by the life and accomplishments of Augustine Herman, the first historically known Bohemian to permanently settle in America a - the eminent Maryland and Virginia pioneer map maker, the Bohemian Jesuit missionary work in Latin America in the late 17th century, the Moravian Brethren?s Diaspora and their cultural contributions to America, and the immigration and the achievements of the Jewish pioneer settlers from the territory of the former Czechoslovakia, which is reflected in the monograph. Covered are also numerous contributions of Czech and Slovaks to the growth and development oi the United States, including science scholarship. \r\n\r\nApart from American ethnic history, Rechcigl has become an authority on the Czech American bibliography and genealogy which is the reason why the editors have also included his writings in this area. It complements the rest of the material remarkably well and enriches its contents and the utility of the volume as a documentary reference.The writings are based on the heretofore unpublished author\'s manuscripts, as well as on his selected periodical articles and book chapters that have been, for the most part, brought up to date. The volume is supplemented with Rechcigl\'s biographical sketch and his fascinating and provocative self-assessment ?What makes him tick? from his yet unpublished Memoirs, and the listing of his writings relating to the subject of this monograph, as well as articles about him \r\n\r\nThe monograph is a tribute to Mila Rechcigl for all his work of almost fifty years which he has devoted to building SVU, an international society aimed at the advancement of the Czech and Slovak studies worldwide and the fostering of intellectual collaboration with the Czech and Slovak Republics, which extends to almost fifty fears of his professional life and to his extraordinary scholarship relating to the history and contributions of Czechs and Slovaks abroad.We highly recommend this timely and unique publication to anyone who is interested in the history and contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America. The book is an important source of information much of which has not been generally known. \r\n\r\nThe book which normally sells for $40, can be purchased by SVU members for $20 plus $5 for postage and handling. Please send your orders with your check to SVU Treasurer Frank Mucha: 36 West 88th St., New York, NY 10024. \r\n\r\n\r\n                                                                                 SVU Press Release \r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n', '2005-08-23 18:15:45', 1608, 1);

