.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.slovnik.cz\">http://www.slovnik.cz</a></b><br>A decent online dictionary which does not use diacritics.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.locallingo.com\">http://www.locallingo.com</a></b><br>Local Lingo provides much useful information about the Czech culture and habits.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.wordbook.cz\">http://www.wordbook.cz</a></b><br> A decent English/Czech - Czech/English dictionary. Contains an ingenious system for typing in letters with accents for those without a Czech keyboard.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.translator.cz\">http://www.translator.cz<br> </a></b> Multilingual Dictionary and Translator also available as a commercial application. It translates between Czech and six other languages: English, German, French, Spanish, Italian and Russian. Note: To reverse the direction of translation click on button Otoč.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://users.ox.ac.uk/~tayl0010\">http://users.ox.ac.uk/<br>/~tayl0010</a></b><br> James Naughtons page offers a learners dictionary to download and use offline. Also many classical Czech texts with annotations and translations.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.mtranslations.cz\">http://www.mtranslations.cz</a></b><br> Moravia Translations provide a decent dictionary. It tends to show less frequent equivalents first, however. Also contains a description of the Czech alphabet. The company specializes in software localization.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.czechprimer.org\">http://www.czechprimer.org</a></b><br> A cute picture dictionary offering the most basic vocabulary with hand-drawn illustrations.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.travlang.com\">http://www.travlang.com</a></b> <br> Basic Czech phrases with sound plus an online dictionary using a peculiar transcription system. (Also available for many other languages.)'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://mujweb.cz/www/grammarcz\">http://mujweb.cz/www/<br>/grammarcz</a></b><br> Good overview of Czech declension and conjugation and mentions of other parts of speech. Provided by authors of WinCzEdu CD-ROM at <b><a href=\"http://mujweb.cz/www/winczeduc\">http://mujweb.cz/www/<br>/winczeduc</a></b>. Also contains Czech history at <b><a href=\"http://mujweb.cz/www/historycz\">http://mujweb.cz/www/<br>/historycz</a></b>.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline\">http://www.bohemica.com/<br>/czechonline</a></b><br> Complete detailed grammar of Czech (still under development).'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://cnk.ff.cuni.cz\">http://ucnk.ff.cuni.cz</a></b> <br> Czech National Corpus with a limited search tool available to the public.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.slavic.ohio-state.edu/people/holdeman/czech\">http://www.slavic.ohio-state.edu/<br>/people/holdeman/czech</a></b><br> A list of resources for teachers and learners of Czech as a foreign language. Particularly useful to US residents. Also contains database of places where Czech can be learned around the world.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.czech-language.cz\">http://www.czech-<br>language.cz</a></b><br>Excellent site describing Czech from a linguistic perspective. Contains many useful links.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.ujc.cas.cz\">http://www.ujc.cas.cz<br> </a></b>Ústav pro jazyk český Akademie věd (Czech Language Institute at the Academy of Sciences) is all in Czech but contains an online FAQ about the language from the point of view of native speakers. You can also ask your own questions in Czech.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/Athens/Forum/7953/Intro.html\">http://www.geocities.com/Athens/<br>/Forum/7953/Intro.html</a></b> <br> A site dealing with Czech-English translation problems.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.ceskaliteratura.cz\">http://www.ceskaliteratura.cz</a></b><br> Probably the most complete Czech-based resource for Czech literature. Contains many texts and excerpts and links to others.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.brown.edu/Departments/CLS/Czech/index.html\">http://www.brown.edu/<br>/Departments/CLS/Czech<br> </a></b><i>Brown University Online Czech Anthology</i> is an excellent starting place for anyone wanting to read Czech literature in the original. It contains excerpts from Čapek and Haek with voice recordings and vocabulary links. There are also several exercises.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.lf1.cuni.cz/~dcasl/litera3.html\">http://www.lf1.cuni.cz/<br>/~dcasl/litera3.html</a></b><br> Czech literature page with many excerpts and further links (all in Czech).'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.noveknihy.cz\">http://www.noveknihy.cz</a></b><br> Online version of weekly New Books magazine which containing a searchable database of nearly all books published in Czech since roughly 1990. Over 30,000 titles (including translations from other languages) with brief bibliographical information and a synopsis are listed by the site.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://maj.kgb.cz\">http://maj.kgb.cz</a></b><br> Complete text and translation of a famous romantic poem by K. H. Mácha <i>Máj</i>.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.czech.cz\">http://www.czech.cz</a></b><br> Information resource about Czech history, culture and tourism in English, provided by the Czech Ministry of foreign Affairs.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.klic.cz/remesla\">http://www.klic.cz/<br>/remesla</a></b><br> An excellent site in Czech describing traditional Czech Arts and Crafts and folklore. Also contains a season by season description of Czech customs and superstitions.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.recepty.cz\">http://www.recepty.cz</a></b><br> Traditional as well as international recipes in Czech. Great resource for language learning with much appeal to the fifth sense.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.ceskenoviny.cz\">http://www.ceskenoviny.cz</a></b><br> Daily online paper published by CTK (Czech Press Agency).'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.idnes.cz\">http://www.idnes.cz<br> </a></b><b><a href=\"http://www.mfdnes.cz\">http://www.mfdnes.cz</a></b><br> Online edition of the best selling Czech daily Mladá Fronta Dnes, offering up-to-date news and many online only features such as Inet interviews with celebrities and politicians. (Second link goes directly to electronic version of the paper edition).'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.lidovky.cz\">http://www.lidovky.cz</a></b><br> Online edition of one of the most respectable Czech dailies.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://pes.eunet.cz\">http://pes.eunet.cz</a></b> <br> Neviditelný Pes (Invisible Dog) is the first Czech online paper edited by a sci-fi writer Ondřej Neff. Its commentary is often used by mainstream printed media, such as <b><a href=\"http://www.lidovky.cz\">Lidové Noviny</a></b>.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://blisty.internet.cz\">http://blisty.internet.cz</a></b><br> News and commentary by a Czech journalist, Jan Čulík, living in Glasgow. Summarizes daily news and comments on Czech realities from the UK perspective.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.radia.cz\">http://www.radia.cz<br> </a></b>Radia (Radios) lists all Czech radios both online and terrestrial. It contains links to many other interesting sites.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.radio.cz\">http://www.radio.cz</a></b> <br> Czech Radio broadcasts aimed abroad in several languages including Czech. It also offers daily email with a news summary in several languages.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.bbc.co.uk/czech\">http://www.bbc.co.uk/<br>/czech</a></b><br> Broadcasts of the World Service in Czech. Also offers written news in Czech and English lessons for Czechs. Has some instructions in English.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.voa.gov\">http://www.voa.gov/<br>/czech</a></b><br> Voice of America broadcasts in Czech. No special services available.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.tv-nova.cz\">http://www.tv-nova.cz<br> </a></b>The largest commercial Czech TV station broadcasts selected programs online.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.ct1.cz\">http://www.ct1.cz</a></b><br> Czech public TV station broadcasts the news and other selected programs online. The site also contains an archive of news broadcasts.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.seznam.cz\">http://www.seznam.cz</a></b><br> \"Czech Yahoo\" this is the most successful Czech web site with over 50000 hits a day (increasing constantly). Offers both full text search and search by category.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.atlas.cz\">http://www.atlas.cz</a></b><br> The second most important portal. Offers all that Seznam does and adds further services. Its services include: free web hosting, web mail, online radio, map search. In cooperation with MSN.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.zlatestranky.cz\">http://www.zlatestranky.cz</a></b><br> Czech Yellow Pages online.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.centraleurope.com\">http://www.centraleurope.com</a></b><br> An English portal and news source for things Central European with an extensive Czech section.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.muselik.com\">http://www.muselik.com</a></b><br> Provides extensive information about the Czech Republic in English.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.czechland.org\">http://www.czechland.org</a></b><br> <i>A Czech Culture-Heritage Web Site</i> from Nebraska contains many useful links to expatriate sites around the world.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.mapy.cz\">http://www.mapy.cz</a></b><br> Free maps of Prague and most of Czech Republic.'),
(2, '<b><a href=\"http://www.czech-books.com\">http://www.czech-books.com</a></b><br> Distributor of Czech books, mostly in the original, in the US.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0844238554/bohemicacom\">Teach Yourself Czech<br> </b></font></a> <font color=\"#800000\">by David Short (1993)</font>  - A professional-looking book. It is very well organized. Even more advanced learners may profit from the dialogues which often contain less common phrases.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0415161363/bohemicacom\">Colloquial Czech</a>&nbsp;</b></font><br> <font color=\"#800000\">by James Naughton (1999) </font> New much improved version of an older textbook. Contains detailed grammar information.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0893570982/bohemicacom\">Contemporary Czech<br> </a></b></font><font color=\"#800000\">by Michael Heim<span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;\"> (</span>1982)</font><font color=\"#808000\"> </font>- A more advanced book presupposing some knowledge of a related Slavic language (Russian, Polish), certainly not very good for communicative study but could be useful for reference.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/078944433X/bohemicacom\">Hugo: Czech In Three Months (with Cassette)</b></font></a><br> <font color=\"#800000\"> by Elisabeth Billington. Audio Cassette (1999)&nbsp;</font> Not a bad book despite its greatly misleading title. No language can be learned in three months no matter what. But this book provides good info.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Communicative Czech (Elementary and Intermediate)<br> </b></font><font color=\"#800000\">by Ivana Bednářová and Magda Pintarová, (1996)</font> Although far from perfect, these are probably the best books in print. They cover a wide range of grammar and communicative topics. A vocabulary list and a collection of grammar charts are useful supplements. Probably the best choice for a course book on the market.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Do You Want To Speak Czech</b></font><br> <font color=\"#800000\">by &#268;echová M., H. Trabelsiová, H. Putz, (1993) </font>Do You Want To Speak Czech is not very suitable as a course book. The language is too hard and sometimes unnatural. Very often grammar introduced in a lesson is not sufficiently practiced in the subsequent exercises. More advanced students, however, will find this book very useful. It will help them to see what they have learned in a different light.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Czech For Foreigners&nbsp;</b></font><br> <font color=\"#800000\">by Parolková and Nováková, (1993) </font>Contains a plenty of pictures and flashcards. It also has some very interesting exercises. It is far from comprehensive and generally disliked by students using it regularly. It may be a good springboard to better things.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/8085927489/bohemicacom0d\">English-Czech/Czech-English Dictionary</a></b></font><br> <font color=\"#800000\">by J. Fronek (1999) </font>Without argument the very best dictionary for learners of Czech on the market. Provides useful grammatical information, context and clear layout. A must have for a serious learner.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>&#268;etina pro cizince</b></font><font color=\"#800000\"><br> by Hronová and Turzíková , (1989)</font> A revised edition of a classic. Very comprehensive but also very traditional. Includes many dialogues, pictures, charts, and a lot of useful phrases. In the back, it has a small dictionary with equivalents in 3 languages.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Czech For You</b></font><br> <font color=\"#800000\">by Milena Kelly&nbsp; (1993)</font> Contains many drills and countless flashcards. Otherwise it is very superficial and not well outlined. The dialogues are sometimes inappropriate to the context, and not very well structured. Its main virtue is the 8 tapes, all recorded by professional speakers, giving you enough time to repeat everything several times. The book contains several mistakes.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0781806534/bohemicacom\">Anglicko-&#268;eský / &#268;esko-Anglický Dictionary</b></font></a> <br> <font color=\"#800000\">by Ivan Poldauf, Jan Caha, Alena Kepecká, Ji&#345;í Krámský(1998) </font><br> Good general dictionary which does not provide as much information for learners as <a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/8085927489/bohemicacom0d\">Fronek</a>.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0870529811/bohemicacom\">Czech-English/English-Czech Concise Dictionary</a><br> </b></font><font color=\"#800000\">by Nina Trnka </font>The only dictionary published in the US, designed specifically for non-native speakers. It is the only dictionary to give phonetic transcriptions of Czech words. Its main limitation is the vocabulary range. While potentially useful for complete beginners, it will not be enough for serious learners.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0415047552/bohemicacom\">The Slavonic Languages (Routledge Language Family Descriptions)</a><br> </b></font><font color=\"#800000\">by Bernard Comrie, Greville G. Corbett (Editor). Hardcover (1993) </font>Pricey at $160.00 but it contains the only respectable English language description of Czech grammar written by David Short.&nbsp; You get other Slavonic languages as well in the same book.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0140182748/bohemicacom\">The Good Soldier vejk and His Fortunes in the World War</a><br> </b></font><font color=\"#990033\">by Jaroslav Haek, Josef Lada (Illustrator), Cecil Parrott (Translator)&nbsp;</font> <br>Most popular humorous novel from the First World War considered by many to be a reflection of the Czech attitude to authority and Czech national character in general.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0192810103/bohemicacom\">R. U. R. and the Insect Play</b></font></a><font color=\"#000099\"><b>&nbsp;<br> </b></font><font color=\"#990033\"> by Karel &#268;apek (1961) (Insect Play co-written with brother Josef &#268;apek)</font> RUR is a science-fiction play from the 1930s which introduced the word <i>robot</i> into worlds languages.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0060932139/bohemicacom\">The Unbearable Lightness of Being (Perennial Classics)</a></b></font><font face=\"verdana,arial,helvetica\" size=\"-1\"><br> </font><font color=\"#990033\"> by Milan Kundera, Michael Henry Heim (Translator) </font>Popular novel by the internationally best-known Czech novelist currently living in France.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0679727868/bohemicacom\">I Served the King of England<br> </a></b></font><font color=\"#990033\"> by Bohumil Hrabal</font><br>A short novel by a popular recently deceased Czech novelist.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/091294675X/bohemicacom\">The Cowards</a>&nbsp;<br> </b></font><font color=\"#990033\">by Josef kvorecký</font><font color=\"#000099\"><b> </b></font>Novel describing situation in post-war Czechoslovakia by a popular Czech novelist currently living in Canada.'),
(3, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0864426240/bohemicacom\"><font face=\"verdana,arial,helvetica\">Lonely Planet Prague (3rd Ed)<br> </font></b></font></a><font color=\"#990033\">by John King, Richard Nebesky (1999)&nbsp;<br> </font><span lang=\"EN-US\">A very accurate guide for short stays in Prague. Part of well known series.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"http://www.czech-books.com\">http://www.czech-books.com</a></b><br> Distributor of Czech books, mostly in the original, in the US.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/redirect-home/bohemicacom\">http://www.amazon.com</a></b><br>Carries many of the most important books for Czech learners.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/redirect-home?tag=bohemicacom0d&site=amazon\">http://www.amazon.co.uk</a></b><br>UK branch of the American etailer. Carries some books not found in America.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"http://www.bibliofind.com\">http://www.bibliofind.com</a></b><br>Best resource for used-books. Lists many hard-to-find gems in all languages.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"www.baylanguagebooks.co.uk\">www.baylanguagebooks.co.uk</a></b><br>Online shopfront of a major distributor of language textbooks with an extensive selection of Czech language materials hard to get elswhere.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"http://www.leda.cz\">http://www.leda.cz</a></b><br>Major Czech publisher of dictionaries and multimedia.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"http://www.kanzelsberger.cz\">http://www.kanzelsberger.cz</a></b><br>Major Czech bookseller department store chain. They sell many Czech textbooks and offer online ordering.'),
(4, '<b><a href=\"http://www.vltava.cz\">http://www.vltava.cz</a></b><br>Online book and music store. Sells Czech music on CD and as MP3s.'),
(5, 'The area of the Czech Republic is 78,864 sq km (30,450 sq miles)'),
(5, 'The Czech Republic shares a border with Austria, Germany, Poland, Slovakia.'),
(5, 'The capital of the Czech Republic is Praha (Prague) with 1,214,000 inhabitants.'),
(5, 'The largest cities in the Czech Republic are Praha (Prague) - 1,214,000, Brno ű 388,000, Ostrava ű 327,000, Plze (Pilsen) ű 173,000.'),
(5, 'Czech Republic became an independent state in 1993 after the split up of Czechoslovakia.'),
(5, 'The Prime Minister of the Czech Republic is Milo Zeman of the Socialist Democrats.'),
(5, 'The President of the Czech Republic is Václav Havel a former playwright and dissident.'),
(5, 'The total population of the Czech Republic is about 10 million people. The population shows slight decline.'),
(5, 'Most Czechs are not religious. They are atheist (40%), Roman Catholic (40%), Protestant (5%) and other (16%).'),
(5, 'Major Czech industries are fuels, ferrous metallurgy, machinery and equipment, motor vehicles, glass, textile, chemicals, armaments.'),
(5, 'Between 1948 and 1989 Czechoslovakia was under a pro-Soviet communist regime.'),
(5, 'Communist rule in Czechoslovakia was toppled following a student demonstration on November 17 1989.'),
(5, 'The Czech Republic consists of two provinces Bohemia (west) and Moravia (east). The northernmost part of Moravia is called Silesia.'),
(5, 'Independent Czechoslovakia was established on October 28 1918 with T.G. Masaryk as the first president.'),
(5, 'Between 1938 and 1945 Czech Republic was a protectorate of Germany. In the meantime Slovakia was an independent state.'),
(5, 'From the 16th century until 1918 Czech lands were part of the Habsburg empire.'),
(5, 'Charles University (founded in 1348) was the first university in Central Europe.'),
(5, 'The Hussite uprising in the early 1400s following the burning at the stake of John Hus in 1415 was the first reformation movement in Europe.'),
(5, 'The word <i>robot</i> was created by the Czech author Karel Čapek in his 1930s play R.U.R.'),
(6, 'Get a basket or a cart in all self-service stores.'),
(6, 'Learn military time since all train and bus schedules use it.'),
(6, 'In buses or trains do not put your feet on the seat.'),
(6, 'It is normal (but not compulsory) to leave a few 10-haller-coins as a tip at stores to round off the sum you pay (never add anything to a round sum).'),
(6, 'In small shops, it is customary to greet the shopkeeper and everybody in the shop Dobrý den.'),
(6, 'Tipping is usual in restaurants, bars, snack shops, cafes, at hairdressers, taxi drivers etc.'),
(6, 'Tips are not calculated by percentage but rounded up to the nearest round figure. The tip increases as the total amount increases.'),
(6, 'Do not press the tip into the waiters hand - tell the waiter the total sum you want to pay including the tip, and he will give you change accordingly. Leaving the tip on the table is not common but possible. Leaving no tip is not common but possible.'),
(6, 'When entering a bar, man goes in first. Otherwise the man lets the woman to be the first to walk through the door.'),
(6, 'Seat yourself in a restaurant. Some tourist oriented or foreign restaurants may require you to wait to be shown to a seat.'),
(6, 'At restaurant expect to sit with other people if there is space - others will sit with you.'),
(6, 'People go to theaters and concerts formally dressed (men wear suits, women wear dresses, skirts).'),
(6, 'You buy tickets for a certain row and seat in a movie theater.'),
(6, 'When moving into your row in a theater, walk sideways facing people already sitting there.'),
(6, 'When visiting a Czech home take your shoes off at the door, put on slippers.'),
(6, 'Important!!! Give only odd numbers of flowers. Even numbers are given at funerals.'),
(6, 'It is not usual for Czechs to give written thanks for hospitality.'),
(6, 'Czech week starts with Monday. Date order is date/month/year.'),
(6, 'It is not common for strangers to smile when they pass each other in the street.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/9027215146/bohemicacom0d\">Some Basic and Less Known Aspects of the Prague Linguistic School<br> </a></b>Josef Vachek (Editor), Philip Luelsdorff (Introduction) (1983) Collection of Czech linguistic writings compiled by one of the  younger members of the Prague Linguistic Circle.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0686226097/bohemicacom\">Selected Writings in English and General Linguistics </a></b> <br>by Josef Vachek (1976) A collection of writings of one of the most important members of the Prague linguistic circle.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/9027930775/bohemicacom\">A Functional Analysis of Present Day English on a General Linguistic Basis</a></b><br> by Vilém Mathesius (1975) An important book by the founder of the Prague linguistic school.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/9027254419/bohemicacom0d\">Prague Linguistic Circle Papers Vol 1: Travaux Du Cercle Linguistique De Prague Nouvelle Serie <br> </a></b>Eva Hajičová, Miroslav &#268;ervenka, Oldřich Leka, Petr Sgall, Joseph Vachek (Editors) (1995) Renewed series building on pre-war Travaux and other publications from the 1960s. Contains articles by younger Czech linguists.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/9027215480/bohemicacom0d\">Variation in Language</a></b><br> Petr Sgall, Jiri Hronek, Alexandr Stich, Jan Horecký (1992) An essential book both for understanding the dual (diglossic) nature of Czech (i.e. coexistence of literary and common Czech) as well as diglossia in general.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/9027220646/bohemicacom0d\">Written Language Revisited<br> </a></b>Josef Vachek, Philip A. Luelsdorff (Editor) (1989) Collection of papers from a conference on written language.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/9027718385/bohemicacom0d\">The Meaning of the Sentence in Its Semantic and Pragmatic Aspects</a></b><br> Petr Sgall, Eva Hajičová, Jarmila Panevová, Jjacob L. Mey (1986) An essential book outlining the work of the new school of Prague linguistics, namely the functional sentence perspective.'),
(7, '<b><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/8085927489/bohemicacom0d\">English-Czech/Czech-English Dictionary</a></b></b><br> by J. Fronek (1999) Without argument the very best dictionary for learners of Czech on the market. Provides useful grammatical information, context and clear layout. A must have for a serious learner.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/9027221464/bohemicacom0d\">Discourse and Meaning, Papers in Honor of Eva Hajičová</a></b><br> Barbara Partee, Petr Sgall (Editors) (1995)'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/0792352890/bohemicacom0d\">Topic-focus Articulation, Tripartite Structures, and Semantic Content</a></b><br> Eva Hajičová, Barbara H. Partee (1998)'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/3110126729/bohemicacom\">Geography of Case Semantics</a></b><br> Laura A. Janda (1993) Using the dative in Czech and instrumental in Russian as case studies for an exploration in cognitive grammar.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/3110151545/bohemicacom0d\">Contact Linguistics Vol 2</a></b> <br> Hans Goebl, Peter H. Nelde, Zdeněk Starý, Wolfgang Wolck (Editors) (1997) An important collection of papers on different aspects of the linguistic situation in Central and Eastern Europe. Co-edited by a Czech linguist Zdeněk Starý.'),
(7, '<b><b><a href=\"www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0415047552/bohemicacom\">The Slavonic Languages (Routledge Language Family Descriptions)</a></b><br> </b>by Bernard Comrie, Greville G. Corbett (Editor). Hardcover (1993) Pricey at $160.00 but it contains the only respectable English language description of Czech grammar written by David Short.&nbsp; You get other Slavonic languages as well in the same book.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/exec/obidos/ASIN/0903425432/bohemicacom0d\">Essays in Czech and Slovak Language and Literature, SSEES Occasional Papers</a></b><br> by David Short (1996)  Contains articles on a variety of interesting subjects from grammar to literature. Important reading for Slavists.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/089357211X/bohemicacom\">Description of Spoken Prague Czech</a></b><b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/089357211X/bohemicacom\"><br> </a></b> by Charles E. Townsend (1990) The name says it all. Important to Slavists.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0262200961/bohemicacom\">The Magic of a Common Language : Jakobson, Mathesius, Trubetzkoy, and the Prague Linguistic Circle (Current Studies in Linguistics, No 26) </a></b><br> by Jindřich Toman (1995) A new look at the Prague Linguistic Circle by a Czech linguist living in the US. Good to read in conjunction with some of the books by Josef Vachek or a Czech book by Zdeněk Starý.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0674635361/bohemicacom\">On Language</a></b><br> by Roman Jakobson, Linda R. Waugh (Editor), Monique Monville-Burston (Editor) (1995)  Collection of linguistic writings by arguably the greatest linguist of all time. Although Jakobson was Russian by origin he seriously started his career in Prague in the late 1920s and is often associated with the Prague school although he moved to the US after WWII and successfully continued his career there.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/902793178X/bohemicacom\">Selected Writings</a></b><br> by Roman Jakobson (1982)  A more complete collection of Jakobsons extensive writings.'),
(7, '<b><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1556192673/bohemicacom\">The Syntax of Sentence and Text : A Festschrift for Frantisek Dane (Linguistic and Literary Studies in Eastern Europe, Vol 42)</a></b><br> by Svetla &#268;mejrková, Frantisek tícha (Editor) (1994) Another collection of articles from Czech linguists. This one reflects another strand of current Czech linguistic thought which builds on the original Prague Linguistic Circle.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Svatý Václav (St. Wenceslas  900s)</b></font> Patron of the Czech lands. Peace-loving king opposing war with the Germans, killed by his brother Boleslav. According to legend he and his soldiers lie under the mountain Blaník and will rise to help the Czech nation in the gravest hour of need.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Karel IV. (Charles IV.) (1316  1378)</b></font> Holy Roman emperor (135578) and founder of Charles University (1348). Under his rule, Czech lands were the political and cultural center of Europe.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Jan Hus (1372 - 1415)</b></font> Reform preacher and linguist, author of modern Czech spelling. Burned at stake for heresy. Inspired the Hussite movement.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Jan ika (d. 1424)</b></font> Successful military leader of the Hussite forces. Under him, Czech rebels defeated crusaders sent by the Roman emperor. Famous for directing battles even being blind.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Jan Ámos Komenský (Comenius) (15921670)</b></font> Famous educator and bishop of the Czech Brethren, sometimes nicknamed the Teacher of the nations. Wrote the first modern compendium of pedagogy. Was exiled during 30-Year War and worked on the educational systems of Sweden and Holland.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Antonín Dvořák (1841-1904)</b></font> World-famous composer. Spent several years in the United States, where he wrote his most famous symphony From the New World.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>T. G. Masaryk (1850-1937)</b></font> Politician and philosopher. First president (1918-35) of the pre-war Czechoslovakia, founded at the end of World War I after the collapse of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Václav Havel (b. 1936)</b></font> First president of Czechoslovakia after the fall of communism. Human rights fighter and playwright.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Jára Cimrman (1865  1898  1915  1927)</b></font> A fictious figure from the turn of the century, attributed with most famous inventions. Cimrman was created in the late 60s by a group of Czech actors who produce plays in his name. Gained enormous popularity on the scale of Monty Python in Great Britain.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Bedřich Smetana (1828  1884)</b></font> Popular composer of last century. Like Beethoven composed some of his best works while completely deaf. His works include: Bartered Bride, Dalibor, The Kiss, My Country.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Jaroslav Haek (1883-1923)</b></font> Writer and journalist. Wrote satirical short stories. During WWI took part in the Russian Revolution. Creator of Good Soldier vejk a popular satire from WWI translated into many over 20 languages including English.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Leo Janáček (1854  1928)</b></font> Most famous Czech composer of this century. His operas include: Káea Kabanová, Věc Makropulos, Lika Bystrouka.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Karel Čapek (1890-1938)</b></font> Writer, journalist and playwright. His 1928 sci-fi drama R.U.R. first used the word robot. His other works include War of the Newts, White Illness, The Macropulos Thing, Krakatit.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Bohumil Hrabal (1914-1997)</b></font> Major works: I Served the King of England, Larks on a String, Closely Watched Trains, Too Loud a Solitude.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Josef kvorecký (*1924)</b></font> Emigrated after 1968. Currently lives in Canada. Started and runs publishing firm 68Publishers. Major works: The Cowards, Sins for Father Knox, Swell Season, Tank Platoon.'),
(8, '<font color=\"#000099\"><b>Milan Kundera (*1929)</b></font> Emigrated after 1968. Currently lives in France. Wrote his last novel in French. Major works: The Joke,  Unbearable Lightness of Being, Laughable Loves, Immortality.'),
