(23, 'Addressing People', '', 'Modified from <i>Yellow\r\nPages of Czech Language by Dominik Luke\r\nand Jitka Kauerová</i>', '<h3>How to address people</h3>\r\n<p>You must change the form of a persons name when you are speaking directly to them, i.e. addressing them. If you learn no other grammar for Czech, learn this. It feels very different for a Czech to be addressed properly, even if youre using English for other communication.</p>\r\n<h4><a name=\"_Toc441309569\">Men</a></h4>\r\n<table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"170\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Ending</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\">=></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>Address</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"322\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>Examples</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"170\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"left\"><b>-b,\r\n      -d, -f, -l, -n, -p, -r, -t, -v</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>+</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-e</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"322\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Micha<b>l </b>=>\r\n      Michal<u>e</u>, Marti<b>n </b>=>\r\n      Martin<u>e</u></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"170\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"left\">-<b>C<i>e</i>C\r\n      </b>(only\r\n      the above)</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>=></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\">-CC<u>e</u></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"322\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Ka<b>r<i>e</i>l</b>=>\r\n      Karl<u>e</u>, Pa<b>v<i>e</i>l</b>=>\r\n      Pavl<u>e</u></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"170\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"left\"><b>-Cr</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>=></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-Cř</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"322\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"left\">Pe<b>tr</b>=>\r\n      Pet<b>ř<u>e</u></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"170\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"left\"><b>-č,\r\n      -, -ř, -, -j, -c, -s</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>+</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-i</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"322\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Tomá<b></b>=>\r\n      Tomá<u>i</u>, Luká<b> </b>=>\r\n      Luká<u>i</u>, </p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"170\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"left\"><b>-k,\r\n      -h, -ch, -g</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>+</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-u</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"322\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Domini<b>k</b>=>\r\n      Dominik<u>u</u>, Jindři<b>ch</b>=>\r\n      Jindřich<u>u</u></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"170\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"left\"><b>-a</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>=></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-o</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"322\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Jirk<u>a</u>=>\r\n      Jirk<u>o</u>, Honz<u>a</u>=>\r\n      Honz<u>o</u>, Frant<u>a</u>=>\r\n      Frant<u>o</u></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<h4><a name=\"_Toc441309570\">Women</a></h4>\r\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"112\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Ending</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>=></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"86\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>Address</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"368\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Examples</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"112\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-a</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>=></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"86\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-o</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"368\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Jan<b>a</b>=>\r\n      Jan<u>o</u>, Katk<b>a</b>=>\r\n      Katk<u>o</u>, Veronik<b>a</b>=>\r\n      Veronik<u>o</u></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"112\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-e</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\">=</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"86\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b>-e</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"368\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Lucie =>\r\n      Lucie, Marie =>\r\n      Marie</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"112\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b><i>any\r\n      nocsonant</i></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"38\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\">=</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"86\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><b><i>no\r\n      change</i></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"368\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Karin\r\n      =>\r\n      Karin, Dagmar =>\r\n      Dagmar</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<h3>How to address people with academic titles</h3>\r\n<p>Czechs are proud\r\nof their academic achievements and do their utmost to advertise them. For that\r\npurpose, Czech uses a variety of acronyms that can precede or be appended to a\r\npersons name. These are the most common titles ranked more or less by\r\nimportance.</p>\r\n<table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"61\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Mgr.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>magistr  MA</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"57\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>RNDr.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"274\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>doktor  sciences specialization</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"61\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Ing.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>inenýr  MSc</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"57\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>CSc.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"274\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>kandidát věd after name,\r\n      not awarded now</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"61\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>PhDr.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>doktor  PhD, general</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"57\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>DrSc.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"274\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>doktor věd after name, not\r\n      awarded now</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"61\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>JUDr.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>doktor  legal specialization</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"57\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Doc.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"274\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>docent  associate professor\r\n      independent of a school</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"61\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>MUDr.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>doktor  medical doctor</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"57\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Prof.</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"274\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>profesor - professor independent\r\n      of a school</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<p>When addressing a person with an\r\nacademic title use the polite Mr. or Mrs. with the title without the name.</p>\r\n<table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"149\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Men:</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p> </p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b>Women:</b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"142\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p> </p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"149\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>Mgr.\r\n      Kovanda</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>pane\r\n      magistře</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>Mgr.\r\n      Reslová</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"142\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>paní\r\n      magistro</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"149\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>dr.\r\n      Novák</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>pane\r\n      doktore</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>PhDr.\r\n      Nováková</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"142\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>paní\r\n      doktorko</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"149\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>ing.\r\n      Pinc</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>pane\r\n      inenýre</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>ing.\r\n      Pincová</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"142\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>paní\r\n      inenýrko</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"149\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>doc.\r\n      ebák</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>pane\r\n      docente</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>doc.\r\n      ebáková</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"142\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>paní\r\n      docentko</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"149\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>prof.\r\n      Machovec</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>pane\r\n      profesore</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>prof.\r\n      Machovec</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"142\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><i>paní\r\n      profesorko</i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<p><b>Note: </b>When youre speaking with a person in English, you\r\ndont have to use their title but it is not inappropriate to do so.\r\n</p>', '2002-09-23 01:30:56', 4931, 32, 100),
(24, 'Private and Public Space', '', 'How the Czech concept of space influences values such as friendship.', '<h3>Observations by Foreigners</h3>\r\n<p>These observations have been made by\r\nforeigners living in the Czech Republic.</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>Czechs are self-contained when in public.</li>\r\n  <li>Public displays of affections are common.</li>\r\n  <li>\r\nPublic events on the streets - no personal\r\nspace, people will crowd in around you.</li>\r\n  <li>Have to be assertive in train or bus lines,\r\npeople will push by you if you let them.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Some Facts</h3>\r\n<p>It is polite and expected to greet\r\npeople you dont know when you walk into a small store, restaurant or waiting\r\nroom, but not to engage in conversation with them. For example, if you walk into\r\na small store, you say <i>Dobr</i><i>ý\r\nden</i> to everybody in general but do not engage in\r\nconversation.</p>\r\n<p>It is normal to sit with strangers at\r\none table in a restaurant for hours and not exchange a word but it would be\r\nimpolite not to say <i>Nashledanou </i>(Good\r\nbye) when you leave. Also if you want to sit down at a table with strangers, you\r\nhave to ask for permission saying <i>Je tu\r\nvolno?</i></p>\r\n<h3>The Czech Space</h3>\r\n<p>Czech private space is smaller but its\r\nboundary is firmer. This is especially obvious when compared with Americans who\r\nrequire larger personal space but are much more willing to let people get into\r\nit.</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\"><img src=\"images/czechculture/czechvalues/concept_of_space.gif\" v:shapes=\"_x0000_i1025\" width=\"755\" height=\"333\"></p>\r\n<p>This\r\ndifference influences much that is typical of Czech culture. For example, a\r\nperson has fewer friends who are closer. Also, people generally do not smile or\r\neven initiate contact with strangers. Public displays of affection are common,\r\nhowever, because they are happening within that close personal space with much\r\nstronger boundaries. Making noises, or anything that disturbs other people is\r\nfrowned upon.</p>\r\nIt may also explain the sometimes sharp contrast between dirty and shabby public\r\nspaces and almost religiously clean homes, or other private spaces.\r\n', '2002-09-23 01:38:56', 12670, 32, 3);
\r\nand can generally be used in times of doubt.</p>\r\n<p>2. <b><i>D&#283;kuju</i></b>(dyeh-kooyoo)</p>\r\n<p>Thank you, is a universally useful phrase.</p>\r\n<p>3. <b><i>Ano\r\n</i></b>(ah-noh)</p>\r\n<p>Yes. <i>Ano</i> is often\r\nshortened to <i>no</i>, sometimes resulting\r\nin cross-language confusion.</p>\r\n<p>4. <b><i>Ne</i></b>(neh)</p>\r\n<p>No. Fairly straightforward and direct.</p>\r\n<p>5. <b><i>Dobrý\r\nden </i></b>(dob-ree den)</p>\r\n<p>Hello, Good afternoon. Use with people you dont know or\r\nwho are superior to you.</p>\r\n<p>6. <b><i>Nashledanou </i></b>(nus-hle-dah-no)\r\nGood bye. Use with people you dont know or who are superior to you.</p>\r\n<p>7. <b><i>Ahoj\r\n</i></b>(ah-hoy)</p>\r\n<p>Hi. or Bye. Much like Aloha this word can be used both when\r\nmeeting and leaving. You will often hear Czechs saying hi while waving you\r\ngoodbye. <i>&#268;au </i>is another informal equivalent.</p>\r\n<p>8. <b><i>Kde\r\nje toaleta? </i></b>(kdeh yeh toh-ah-le-ta) Where is the bathroom? Obviously\r\nuseful but getting you into the realm of phrases where you need to understand\r\nthe answer.</p>\r\n<p>9. <b><i>Kolik\r\nto stojí?</i></b> (koh-leek toh stoh-yee)</p>\r\n<p>How much is it? Ditto.</p>\r\n<p>10. <b><i>Co\r\nje to?</i> </b>(tso yeh toh)</p>\r\n<p>What is it? This phrase is especially useful to expand your\r\nvocabulary.</p>\r\n<h3>How to Say Czech in Czech</h3>\r\n<p>Where there is one word in English\r\nthere are five in Czech.</p>\r\n<table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"253\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b><i>&#269;etina\r\n      </i></b>[chesh-tyi-nah]</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"351\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Czech language</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"253\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b><i>&#268;eka\r\n      </i></b>[chesh-kah]</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"351\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Czech woman</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"253\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b><i>&#268;ech\r\n      </i></b>[chekh]</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"351\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Czech man</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"253\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b><i>&#269;eský,\r\n      &#269;eská, &#269;eské </i></b>[chess-keeee]</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"351\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>adjective as in <i>&#268;eská republika </i> Czech Republic</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"253\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p><b><i>&#269;esky\r\n      </i></b>[chess-kee]</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"351\" valign=\"top\">\r\n      <p>Czech as in <i>I speak Czech </i>- an adverb</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<b>Note:\r\n</b>It\r\nis a common error in English to say <i>Czech </i>instead\r\nof <i>Czech Republic</i>. However, <i>Czech\r\n</i>in English can only be used as an adjective or the name of the language.\r\n', '2002-09-23 02:13:04', 9902, 32, 7),
(31, 'The Long and the Short of It', 'Erik Piper', 'The very first essay. Long and short vowels are the scourge of the student. Here\'s what Erik thinks about it.', '<h1><b><span style=\"font-size:18.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;\r\nmso-ansi-language:EN-US\">The Long and Short of It\r\n\r\n</span></b></h1>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"> </span><b><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Introduction\r\n\r\n</span></b></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">First the basics:\r\nlong vowels are pronounced longer, short vowels are pronounced more shortly.\r\nNeither type affects stress (how loudly vowels are pronounced). Except for long\r\nu, spelled </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">ů</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\"> when its not in a words first syllable, long vowels have an acute\r\n( </span><b><u><span lang=\"CS\">´</span></u><span lang=\"CS\"> </span></b><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">)\r\naccent mark on top.\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Long and short vowels\r\nin Czech are tricky for English speakers inasmuch as we are not used to\r\nlistening for themwe do not have a natural ear for them.<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\"> \r\n</span>While many long vowels are predictable (e.g. adjective endings), since\r\ntheyre a matter of grammar, others are only a tendency. Even some\r\ngrammatical long vowels are tricky due to exceptions to grammar rules.\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">In other words, three\r\nthings are important for getting a hang of <i>writing </i>long and short vowels:\r\nlearning grammar rules (and exceptions), learning some rules of thumb, and a lot\r\nof rote learning. \r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">To <i>pronounce </i>long\r\nand short vowels properly, you should above all avoid making the mistake I made,\r\nand start practicing early, since bad habits are hard to break. Personally, I\r\nfind myself stressing long vowels by mistake. I know in my head its wrong,\r\nbut theres no time to think when youre talking. Get the right habit under\r\nyour skin, early.\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Remembering Where\r\nThey Are\r\n\r\n</span></b></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Most vowels are short,\r\nso its easiest to remember where the long ones are. Again, the three aids are\r\n<i>grammar</i>, <i>rules of thumb</i>, and <i>rote learning </i>(OK, thats\r\nactually no help at all). Ill cover the first two belowfor the third, just\r\nremember to listen and read carefully and actively, and even take notes on what\r\nyou hear and see when time permits.\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Grammatical\r\nlong/short vowels</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">The classic here is\r\nadjectives: the basic forms of nearly all adjectives end in a long vowel: <i>rychlý\r\nip</i>, <i>rychlá volba</i>, <i>rychlí lidé</i>, <i>rychlé auto</i>, <i>inteligentní\r\nkluk/holka/lidé/řeení</i>. Even in the various cases, they start with long\r\nvowels (e.g. <i></i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">ých, ím, éeho</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">),\r\nwith only one exception: </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">-á</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">\r\nturns to -<i>ou</i></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"> in 4<sup>th</sup>\r\n(accusative) case.\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\">Adjectives based on passive verbs, like <i>asten</i>,\r\nnever have long vowels. These are in any case </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">rare and a bit bookish. Possessive adjectives like </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">babiččin\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">(grandmas) and <i>bratrův</i> (the brothers)\r\nhave an unusual and complex pattern of endings, but are rarer than in English.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\">Verbal nouns, that is, to oversimplify\r\nmatters, verbs made into </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"CS\">-ing</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\nnouns, always end in the vowel </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">í</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">, although other than that their endings vary greatly. (The most\r\nconfusing pair here is <i>-</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">aní/-ání</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">.\r\nNote that <i>ání</i> is more common.)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\">Past-tense verb endings (that is, </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"CS\">-?-l</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"CS\">)\r\nnever contain long vowels, except for a few one-syllable <i>-át</i> </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">verbs:</span> <i><span lang=\"CS\">hrál</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">, <i>přál</i>,\r\netc.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><i><span lang=\"CS\">Rules of thumb and tips\r\n\r\n</span></i></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\">-- Most, though not all, present-tense\r\nverbs end in long vowels, so if you have to guess blindly, try a long vowel (esp.\r\n<i>í</i></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">). Of course, you\r\nshouldnt be guessing blindly anyway :-). \r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">-- Most word\r\nfamilies have the same vowel length in their root: <i>lekat, lekav</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">ý,\r\nleknutí </span></i><span lang=\"CS\">(frighten, easily-frightened, fright)</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">;<i>\r\n</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">lék, lékárník, lékárna </span></i><span lang=\"CS\">(a\r\nmedicine, pharmacy, pharmacist)</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">.\r\nWatch out, though, for black sheep like </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">lékář</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">.\r\n(Even such exceptions can usually be explained, but at first its simpler just\r\nto learn them rote.)\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\">-- If you dont know a </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"CS\">grammatical</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\nreason to use a long vowel, and you must guess, a short vowel is the safest bet.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\">-- Long </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">e, o and u are especially uncommon (</span><span lang=\"CS\">judging\r\nfrom p. 42 of Jelínek and Styblík</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">s</span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\">Čtení o českém jazyce</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">).</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\">-- Learn standard prefixes and suffixes!\r\nThis is useful anyway, and most (if they contain vowels) always have vowels of\r\nthe same length: <i>roz-, ne-, mezi-, pře-</i>, <i>ek, </i>-<i>ík, ák, -ost, </i>etc.\r\nSome, however, come in pairs and thus </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">arent helpful here: <i>v</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">y/vý, při/pří</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Curiosities:\r\n\r\n</span></b></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">-- Speakers from\r\nSilesia (northern Moravian region; historical capital is Ostrava) tend to\r\nshorten vowels: e.g. <i>dobry stryček </i>instead of <i>dobr</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">ý\r\nstrýček</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">. </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">Whence the saying: </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">Slezáci mají krátké zobáky</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n(Silesians have short beaks).\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">-- In colloquial (mainly\r\nspoken) Czech, -</span><i><span lang=\"CS\">ý</span></i><span lang=\"CS\"> tends to\r\nbecome -<i>ej</i>.</span></p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 19:31:19', 1864, 83, 11);
\r\nof, as in <i>ni</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">zozemské vlády</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">,\r\nbut </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">2<sup>nd</sup> casebelongingis\r\nthe default for expressing possession in Czech, just as possessivesowningare\r\nthe default for English. Incidentally, this is why Czechs without close contact\r\nwith English often do say things like the wages of the staff of the company\r\n(or even wages of staff of company)it just seems natural.\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Brother-in-law\r\nBought Me a Beer\r\n\r\n</span></b></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Since English word\r\nendings tell so relatively little about words relationships and grammatical\r\nroles, we need many other tools to express these. <i>Determiners</i> are one\r\nsuch tool: they determine nouns in terms of whose they are (possessives),\r\nspecifying them in relation to what weve mentioned before or where were\r\npointing (demonstratives, like that), whether or not they are\r\nknown or assumable subjects of discussion (articles, like the),\r\nor numerical aspects (any, one, several). \r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">&nbsp;\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">English grammar\r\nstronly emphasises determination, even to the point of ridiculous\r\nredundancy, e.g. my brother-in-law is my wifes brother or sisters\r\nhusband. I assume this obsession is fueled by the lack of other tools for\r\nexpressing relationships, and/or it was something that those who shaped English\r\n(i.e. early English-speakers) found important. However, redundancy in languages\r\nis nothing unusual; for example, most European languages express plurality at\r\nleast twice, in subjects and verbs: <b>We</b> have arrived / </span><b><span lang=\"CS\">Nous</span></b><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n<b>sommes</b> arrivé<b>s </b></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">/ </span><span lang=\"CS\">Do<b>li\r\njsme</b>. Again, I assume grammatical redundancy expresses the notion that some\r\nfact (gender, number, antecedents, etc.) is important enough to deserve </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">protection\r\nfrom being overheard in spoken communication.<i>\r\n\r\n</i></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">&nbsp;\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">I Combed Hair for\r\nMyself\r\n\r\n</span></b></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Here again, Czech and\r\nEnglish seek functionality and efficiency within their own rules. I combed my\r\nhair is more efficient than I combed hair for myself. It also gives\r\nhair a determiner, something English tends to desire. Meanwhile, </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">česal\r\njsem si vlasy</span></i><span lang=\"CS\"> is quite efficient in Czech (even more\r\nthan <i>česal jsem </i></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\"><i>své</i> <i>vlasy</i>). Moreover, this approach opens up <i>své</i>\r\nfor use in <i>emphasizing</i> ownership: <i>To je neuvěřitelný! Kadeřnice\r\ntam stála před zákazníkem and česala <b>své </b>vlasy. </i>(Unbelievable!\r\nThe hairdresser was standing there in front of a customer and combing her own\r\nhair.). In fact, any use of possessives in cases where Czech provides other\r\nmeans <i>emphasises</i> possession, so </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">česat své\r\nvlasy</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">, if allowed to mean </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">to\r\ncomb ones hair</span><span lang=\"CS\">, would be distracting and confusing.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">* * *\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Curiosities and\r\nSide Notes:\r\n\r\n</span></b></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:36.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo2;\r\ntab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Wingdings;\r\nmso-ansi-language:EN-US\">n<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">In colloquial Czech, <i>od</i>\r\ncan also be used for relationships between persons, where we would use a\r\npossessive: </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">Brácha od Jany</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n(Jana</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">s brother), </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">Mára\r\nod Oliny </span></i><span lang=\"CS\">(Olga</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">s\r\nboyfriend, Marek). This colloquial form can also connect persons to things: <i>Včera\r\nzas příel ten </i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">od novin</span></i><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n(That guy from the newspaper came again yesterday).</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:36.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo2;\r\ntab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Wingdings;\r\nmso-ansi-language:EN-US\">n<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Czechs do not always use\r\nreflexive pronouns where we would:\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:72.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo2;\r\ntab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;\r\nmso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">o<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">I myself dont like it. (</span><i><span lang=\"CS\">Ale\r\njá to nemám rád, Já osobně </span></i><span lang=\"CS\">[personally]<i> to\r\nnemám rád</i></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">). Here,\r\nmyself is not really used reflexively in English, but rather for emphasis.\r\nCzech takes advantage here of the fact that personal pronouns arent normally\r\nused.\r\n\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:72.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo2;\r\ntab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;\r\nmso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">o<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Phrases like He asked\r\nJohn and myself and Mr. Jones and myself are undecided are frowned on\r\nby grammaticians, according to the American Heritage Dictionary; they should\r\ncontain <i>me </i>and <i>I </i>respectively. These mistakes do not exist in\r\nCzech, and thus: <i>Prosil mne a Johna </i>[or <i>s Johnem</i>]; <i>Pan Jones a\r\nj</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\">á se nedokáeme rozhodnout.</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n', '2002-10-12 19:32:59', 1695, 83, 12);
?</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n(How do I get to?) or even </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">Kde je </span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">?</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n(Where is ?) cannot elicit anything but an incomprehensible (to anybody whos\r\nlikely to have read this far) stream of sounds. If youre lost, hold up a map\r\nto somebody and ask </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">Kde jsem</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">?</span></i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n(Where am I?) and youll be fine. You may not be fluent but that doesnt\r\nmean you have to be stupid. There are dozens of phrases that you may only need\r\nonce in your Czech speaking career. Trying to memorize dozens of words on the\r\noff-chance that you might need them is a waste of time. Learn the most important\r\nones and get a phrasebook for the rest and point your way through communication.\r\nIf youre serious about learning Czech, take a course! </span><i><span lang=\"CS\">Prosím</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">!\r\n\r\n</span></i></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><i><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Dominik Luke</span><span lang=\"CS\">,\r\n</span><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">author runs an online Czech course\r\non http://www.bohemica.com</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n\r\n</span></i></p>\r\n<h2><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Most useful phrases\r\n\r\n</span></h2>\r\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;\r\n border:none;mso-border-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-padding-alt:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt\">\r\n  <tr style=\"height:18.6pt\">\r\n    <td width=\"96\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:71.9pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;height:18.6pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"NormalFirst\"><i><span lang=\"CS\">prosím \r\n      \r\n      </span></i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"146\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:109.5pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-left:none;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:18.6pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"NormalFirst\"><span lang=\"CS\">PROH-seeem</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"364\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:272.85pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-left:none;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:18.6pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"NormalFirst\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">please,\r\n      youre welcome, here you are, what would you like, repeat please\r\n      \r\n      </span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr style=\"height:18.65pt\">\r\n    <td width=\"96\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:71.9pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:18.65pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:0cm\"><i><span lang=\"CS\">pivo\r\n      \r\n      </span></i></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"146\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:109.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;height:18.65pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:0cm\"><span lang=\"CS\">PE-voh</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"364\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:272.85pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;height:18.65pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:0cm\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\n  EN-US\">most likely better than what you call beer\r\n      \r\n      </span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr style=\"height:18.65pt\">\r\