(101, 'Zpěněný pohár', 'Frantiek Gellner (1914)', '', '<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Já drím pohár ve své dlani.<?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\" /><o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Je zpěněný a přetéká.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Já drím pohár ve své dlani,<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">jen čeká na rty člověka. <o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\"><o:p> </o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Jen čeká, zdali víno jeho<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">se do brázd vyschlých rozleje,<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">na snivých květech v jiných světech<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">zda zavěsí své krůpěje. <o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\"><o:p> </o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Jen čeká, zda se sehnou květy<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">pod onou tíí ku zemi.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Jen čeká, zdali jiné světy<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">rozzáří svými vůněmi. <o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\"><o:p> </o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Já drím pohár ve své dlani,<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">jen čeká na rty člověka.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Já drím pohár ve své dlani: <o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-ansi-language: CS; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-language: AR-SA\">své srdce, které přetéká.</SPAN></P>', '2002-11-25 13:16:27', 1332, 204, 20);
</P>\r\n<P align=justify>&nbsp;</P></FONT><B><FONT size=4>\r\n<P align=justify>Vocabulary</P></B></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>učebna = </FONT><FONT size=3>lecture hall</P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>informační tabule = </P></FONT><B><FONT face=Arial size=5>\r\n<P align=justify>Scénka D</P></B></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Co musí studenti napsat na formulář:</P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P align=justify>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P align=justify>&nbsp;</P></FONT><B><FONT size=4>\r\n<P align=justify>Vocabulary</P></B></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>studijní obor = </P>\r\n<P align=justify>datum a místo narození = </P></FONT>', '2002-11-26 17:14:34', 346, 209, 110);
, identifies three species of mendacity, each worse than the one before - lies, damned lies, and statistics.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-prop-change: \'Dominik Luke\' 20021203T1913\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">The larger issue that creates a common distrust or contempt for statistics is more troubling. Many people make an unfortunate and invalid separation between heart and mind, or feeling and intellect. In some contemporary traditions, abetted by attitudes stereotypically centered on </SPAN><?xml:namespace prefix = st1 ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags\" /><st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Southern California</SPAN></st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">, feelings are exalted as more real and the only proper basis for action - if it feels good, do it - while intellect gets short shrift as a hang-up of outmoded elitism. Statistics, in this absurd dichotomy, often become the symbol of the enemy. As Hilaire Belloc wrote, Statistics are the triumph of the quantitative method, and the quantitative method is the victory of sterility and death.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">This is a personal story of statistics, properly interpreted, as profoundly nurturant and life-giving. It declares holy war on the downgrading of intellect by telling a small story about the utility of dry, academic knowledge about science. Heart and head are focal points of one body, one personality.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">In 1982, I learned that I was suffering from abdominal mesothelioma, a rare and serious cancer usually associated with exposure to asbestos. When I revived after surgery, I asked my first question of my doctor and chemotherapist: What is the best technical literature about mesothelioma? She replied, with a touch of diplomacy , that the medical literature contained nothing really worth reading.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">If a little learning could ever be a dangerous thing, I had encountered a classic example. Attitude clearly matters in fighting cancer. We don\'t know why (from my old-style materialistic perspective, I suspect that mental states feed back upon the immune system). But match people with the same cancer for age, class, health, socioeconomic status, and, in general, those with positive attitudes, with a strong will and purpose for living, with commitment to struggle, with an active response to aiding their own treatment and not just a passive acceptance of anything doctors say, tend to live longer. A few months later I asked Sir Peter Medawar, my personal scientific guru and a Nobelist in immunology, what the best prescription for success against cancer might be. A sanguine personality, he replied. <o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Hence the dilemma for humane doctors: since attitude matters so critically, should such a sombre conclusion be advertised, especially since few people have sufficient understanding of statistics to evaluate what the statements really mean?  I have developed this technical knowledge - and I am convinced that it played a major role in saving my life. Knowledge is indeed power.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">It has become, in my view, a bit too trendy to regard the acceptance of death as something tantamount to intrinsic dignity. Of course I agree with the preacher of Ecclesiastes that there is a time to love and a time to die - and when my skein runs out I hope to face the end calmly and in my own way. For most situations, however, I prefer the more martial view that death is the ultimate enemy - and I find nothing reproachable in those who rage mightily against the dying of the light.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">The swords of battle are numerous, and none more effective than humor. My death was announced at a meeting of my colleagues in </SPAN><st1:country-region><st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Scotland</SPAN></st1:place></st1:country-region><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">, and I almost experienced the delicious pleasure of reading my obituary penned by one of my best friends (the so-and-so got suspicious and checked; he too is a statistician). Still, the incident provided my first good laugh after the diagnosis. Just think, I almost got to repeat Mark Twain\'s most famous line of all: the reports of my death are greatly exaggerated. </SPAN></P>', '2002-12-18 13:21:00', 297, 227, 100),
(118, 'Top 10 Lists', '', 'Tři sváteční seznamy humorných věcí.', '<B><FONT face=Arial,helvetica size=5>\r\n<P align=justify>Ten signs your sick of the holidays</P>\r\n<UL></B></FONT><FONT size=2>\r\n<P align=justify></P></FONT>\r\n<LI>You\'ve got red and green bags under your eyes </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>You\'re serving reindeer pot pie </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>When you hear, \"Sleigh bells ring, are you listenin\'?,\" you scream, \"No! I\'m not listening!\" </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>You climb on your roof and start shooting carolers with your air gun </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>You think you hear your Christmas tree taunting you. </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Instead of spending time with family, you\'re watching some guy make photo copies </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>You\'re busted for running through town wearing nothing but mistletoe. </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>You\'ve got eggnog coming out of your ears </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Your standard response, \"And happy holidays to you too, you bastard\" </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Two words: tinsel rash </LI>\r\n<P></P></UL><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>http://www.christmasjokes.co.uk/humour/10signssickholiday.html</P>\r\n<P align=justify>&nbsp;</P></FONT><B><FONT face=Arial,helvetica size=5>\r\n<P align=justify>Top Ten Signs Santa Hates You</P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>10.</B> Your stocking is ticking</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>9.</B> Every kid gets a candy cane, you get a ball of rusty barbed wire</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>8.</B> He brings you a new car -- right through the living room wall</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>7.</B> His expression doesn\'t seem to be \"jolly\" so much as \"seething and vengeful\"</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>6.</B> FBI bursts into your house saying, \"We got a tip from Santa Claus you\'re hiding Mullah Omar\"</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>5.</B> You\'re being stalked by an elf hitman</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>4.</B> You spend ten minutes telling him what you want -- he says, \"Oh, I\'m sorry, were you talking to me?\"</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>3.</B> Only item he leaves: a note reading \"Your wife was great\"</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>2.</B> \"Gift\" he just gave you -- 2 weeks on a Disney cruise with Trent Lott</P><B>\r\n<P align=justify>1.</B> His distinctive laugh: \"Ho, ho, go screw yourself\"</P>\r\n<P align=justify>http://www.cbs.com/latenight/lateshow/top_ten/archive/ls_topten_archive2002/ls_topten_archive_20021212.shtml</P></FONT><B><FONT face=Arial,helvetica size=5>\r\n<P align=justify>Top Ten Signs You Are Becoming Blindly Patriotic 09/28/2002</P></B></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>10. You recently found that you can fit 37,052 American Flags on your AMC Pacer. /Mr. Mister </P>\r\n<P align=justify>9. You don\'t think there\'s anything \"funny\" about this list. /eric... </P>\r\n<P align=justify>8. Fighting preemptive war with no allies in a world that already hates you seems like a sure-fire bet. /the machine formats your imput </P>\r\n<P align=justify>7. Your screen name goes from HotStudInOklahoma to UncleSamRoxs. /Hephestos </P>\r\n<P align=justify>6. You play the national anthem even before you use the toilet. /derrick </P>\r\n<P align=justify>5. Instead of buying a smaller more economical car, you accidentally buy a monstrous SUV with an American flag sunshield. /DrummaBen </P>\r\n<P align=justify>4. You lose interest on everything that\'s even loosely foreign, including french fries. /Maciel </P>\r\n<P align=justify>3. You refuse to let your children see \"Aladdin,\" since it\'s obviously a propaganda movie. /eric... </P>\r\n<P align=justify>2. \"I think I like paying my taxes now.\" /the1cooljay </P>\r\n<P align=justify>derrick thinks he better sell a few American flags... </P>\r\n<P align=justify>1. George Bush starts to make sense.</P>\r\n<P align=justify>http://interactivespace.com/topten/previous/index.php?id=587</P></FONT>', '2002-12-18 17:09:47', 315, 228, 100),
(119, 'Slyet vlastní hlas', 'Iva Nachtmannová', 'Článek o postavení en v současné české společnosti.', '<H5 style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><I><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-ansi-language: CS\">RESPEKT c. 51 - 13. -- 19. 12. 1999</SPAN></I><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-ansi-language: CS\"><?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\" /><o:p></o:p></SPAN></H5>\r\n<H2 style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-ansi-language: CS\">Slyet vlastní hlas<o:p></o:p></SPAN></H2>\r\n<H4 style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><I><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-ansi-language: CS\">České eny pomalu ztrácejí iluze o své o rovnoprávnosti</SPAN></I><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-ansi-language: CS\"><o:p></o:p></SPAN></H4>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS>\"Jsem samostatná a sebevědomá ena. Mám perspektivní práci, která mě baví a motivuje k dalímu sebevzdělávání. Vydělávám stejně jako můj kolega s podobnou kvalifikací. S manelem se dělíme o domácí práce a oba se snaíme věnovat dětem co nejvíce volného času. Manel nechce, aby kvůli jeho kariéře měly děti pocit, e tatínek je tu jen k vydělávání peněz.\" Pokud se v některém státě na světě můe větina en s tímto prohláením ztotonit, jde o zemi, která dosáhla rovnoprávnosti mezi mui a enami. Je na čase, tvrdí české feministky, poloit takovou otázku i u nás. </SPAN></P>\r\n<P><B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial\">Nechtějí si to přiznat <o:p></o:p></SPAN></B></P>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS>Václav Bělohradský se nedávno podivil tomu, jak Čeky zpočátku protestovaly proti jeho tvrzení, e jsou diskriminovány, ačkoli z pohledu emigranta, který se vrátil z Itálie bylo jasné, e zde eny vykonávaly větinou jen práce, které se podle muské optiky pro mue nehodí (např. sekretářky, učitelky základních kol, sociální pracovnice), a nebyly téměř v ádné vedoucí funkci. </SPAN></P>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS>Feminismem se přitom logicky necítí ohroeny jen české eny, ale podle Magdaleny Frouzové, studující mimo jiné i postavení en v asijských zemích, také mnozí \"mui typu DDD\": \"Tato zkratka vyjadřuje tři základní věci, které takoví mui od en ádají, a na jejich vykonávání by se podle nich měly eny omezit: Domácnost, Děti, Dobrý vzhled\", vysvětluje Frouzová. </SPAN></P>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS>Studiem vzorců a vitých stereotypů v chování en i muů a proměn jejich sociálních rolí se zabývá obor gender studies neboli studia rodu (původní význam gender je mluvnický rod). Jejich centrum a knihovnu u nás zaloila socioloka Jiřina iklová. Zásluhou lidí, kteří se do aktivit centra zapojili, se o gender tématech začalo mluvit  vznikají sociologické práce na téma role en i muů, pořádají se semináře a přednáky nejen na praských vysokých kolách, diskutuje se i na internetu (např. www.feminismus.cz). Studie nového oboru potvrzují, e eny u nás nemají rovnocennou startovní pozici -- socioloka Hana Havelková uvádí, e parita zastoupení en mezi studenty na vysokých kolách se před dvěma lety zastavila na 44 procentech a dále neroste, eny také převaují mezi lidmi s pouze základním vzděláním. Dosavadní výzkumy ukazují, e eny jsou odměňovány v průměru o třetinu méně ne mui ve stejné pracovní pozici. Podle Hany Havelkové vak stále jetě chybí výzkumy, které by poukazovaly na existenci na první pohled neviditelných mechanismů odsunování en na vedlejí kolej, odrazování od určitých činností apod. \"Jsem přesvědčena, e tyto výzkumy by ukázaly řadu zcela přímých diskriminací -- mám například zprávy, e některé střední i vysoké koly zcela běně uplatňují znevýhodňující kritéria pro dívky nebo limity pro počet jejich přijetí\", tvrdí Hana Havelková. </SPAN></P>\r\n<P><B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Arial\">Politik v sukni <o:p></o:p></SPAN></B></P>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS>České eny se proti tradičnímu, muskému pohledu na svět u nás za deset let nedokázaly prosadit. Myslí si to i známá filmová dokumentaristka Olga Sommerová, a dokazuje to na příkladu z politiky: \"Současné české političky přebírají muské vzorce chování i mylení. Místo kalhot obléknou sukni, jiný rozdíl neexistuje. Skutečně ensky myslící bytosti se v naí politice takřka nevyskytují.\" eny v politice se podle ní málo věnují kolství, zdravotnictví či ekologii, nepřináejí ani jiný pohled na ostatní obory. Větina českých političek se také dosud distancuje od čehokoli, co by jen vzdáleně souvisí s feminismem či sociálními otázkami. Politika ale podle feministek na celém světě nabízí rychlou monost, jak prosadit \"enský pohled\" na svět. Nedaří-li se jim prorazit muskou konkurenci jako u nás (viz sloení vlády), mohou pomoci enské kvóty na kandidátních listinách politických stran. </SPAN></P>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS>Iva Nachtmannová </SPAN></P>\r\n<P><SPAN lang=CS><A href=\"http://www.respekt.cz\">http://www.respekt.cz</A></SPAN></P>', '2003-01-09 02:49:13', 701, 226, 100),
(127, 'Druhá část', '', '<B><FONT face=Arial size=5>\r\n<P align=justify>Úkol</P></B></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Připravte si prezentaci na téma: <I>Co dělám pro ekologický ivot a co bych mohl(a) dělat víc</I>, připravte otázky a seznam slovíček pro své kolegy. Pouijte slovíčka a fráze z&nbsp;článků na </FONT><A href=\"http://www.quido.cz/ekologie\"><FONT size=3>http://www.quido.cz/ekologie</FONT></A><FONT size=3>.</P></FONT>', '<B><FONT face=Arial size=5>\r\n<P align=center>Desatero domácí ekologie</P></FONT><FONT face=Arial size=5>\r\n<P align=justify>3. Spotřební zboí</P></B></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Pamatujme, e kvalitnějí zboí nám větinou poslouí mnohem déle a uetří se materiál i energie. Kdosi řekl, e není tak bohatý, aby si mohl kupovat levné věci. Mnozí se přesvědčili o tom, jak mohou levné věci přijít draho. Zvame proto pečlivě, ne něco koupíme. \"Bezejmenné\" (\"noname\") výrobky raději zavrhněme hned: jaká asi je kvalita výrobku, na který se firma stydí podepsat? Podezřelé jsou také levné značkové výrobky, zejména textil. Po prvním vyprání či vyč</FONT><FONT size=3>i</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>tění obvykle zjistíme, e místo \"značkového\" výrobce by nám lépe poslouil tuzemský. Oči mějme otevřené i při různých výprodejních akcích, nebo důvody k výprodeji mohou být různé: ty skutečné obvykle zjistíme a doma, kdy výrobek chceme pouít. Ale ani</FONT><FONT size=3> </FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>se značkovými výrobky to není jednoduché. ádný výrobce např. elektrické ruční vrtačky nikam nenapíe \"nae vrtačka má ozubená kola z umělé hmoty a proto má malou ivotnost\" ale označí ji jako \"HOBBY\". </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Při nákupu se snaíme dát přednost přírodním nebo přírodě blízkým materiálům (bavlna, vlna, len, sklo, dřevo, kůe, keramika atd.). </P>\r\n<P align=justify>U nápojů a ostatních tekutin bychom sice měli dávat přednost těm, které jsou prodávány ve vratných lahvích, ale po pravdě řečeno, v takovém případě bychom asi museli být alkoholiky, protoe dnes se kromě tvrdého alkoholu, vína a piva téměř nic v klasických lahvích neprodává. Dokonce u i tradiční minerálky jsou degradovány plastikovými lahvemi. Pravda, některé nápoje jsou prodávány ve vratných lahvích a větina ostatních je ozn</FONT><FONT size=3>a</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>čena jako \"recyklovatelný obal\". Ale tímto označením celá \"recyklace\" končí. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Baterie do elektrospotřebičů podle moností nahrazujeme síovým napáječem nebo alespoň akumulátory, které vydrí několik let. Výhodné jsou solární spotřebiče (kalkulačky). Pokud jsme nuceni pouívat baterie, volíme takové, které neobsahují rtu (mercury) a kadmium (cadmium). </P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify></FONT><A href=\"http://www.quido.cz/ekologie\"><FONT size=3>http://www.quido.cz/ekologie</FONT></A></P><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>1. voda, 2. energie, </FONT><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>3. spotřební zboí</B></FONT><FONT size=3>, 4. ovzduí, 5. odpadky a druhotné surovin</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>y, 6. doprava a pohyb, 7. zahrádka, 8. strava, 9. zdraví, 10. úcta k&nbsp;ivotu</P></FONT><B><FONT face=Arial size=5>\r\n<P align=justify>Slovíčka a fráze</P></B></FONT>\r\n<TABLE cellSpacing=1 cellPadding=9 width=773 border=1>\r\n<TBODY>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>dát přednost</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>prefer</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>vratná láhev</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>returnable bottle</I></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>ekologický</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>environmentally friendly</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>výrobce</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>manufacturer</I></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>nahradit</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>replace</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>výrobek</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>product</I></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>napáječ</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>power source</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>značkový</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>brand name</I></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>obal</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>packaging</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>zváit</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>weigh</I></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>obsahovat</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>contain</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>ivotnost</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>product life</I></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>pečlivě</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>carefully, meticulously</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>pouitelnost</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>usability</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>přírodní</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>natural</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>spotřebič</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>appliance</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>tuzemský</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>local (this countrys)</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>umělý</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\"><I><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>artificial</I></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"25%\">&nbsp;</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify></P></FONT>', '2003-02-17 17:36:03', 361, 244, 20);
\"<?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\" /><o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">Sentences like this, with a mysterious, seemingly useless dative in the middle, are extremely common in spoken Czech, but you\'re unlikely to find them in books on </SPAN><?xml:namespace prefix = st1 ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags\" /><st1:country-region><st1:place><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">Czech.</SPAN></st1:place></st1:country-region><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"> It\'s a shame. The ethical dative, sayeth Webster\'s 1913 dictionary, is \" a use of the dative of a pronoun to signify that the person or thing spoken of is regarded with interest by some one.\" seeLRC\'s \"Case Book\" (get it?) for Czech has a bit of a nicer definition: \" the speaker is using the dative case in order to capture the hearers attention, or to express aggression or solidarity. \"<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>Let\'s take a look at what this means in practice.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">First, a refresher on the dative case. It\'s also spoken of as the third case or, when discussing with Czechs who don\'t remember formal names for their language\'s cases, as \"komu/</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">čemu</SPAN><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">\", i.e. \"to whom/to what.\"<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">Hodil psovi handgranát </SPAN></B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"> He threw a dog a handgrenade (from <A href=\"http://www.novinky.cz/Index/Boulevard/12310.html\">http://www.novinky.cz/Index/Boulevard/12310.html</A>)  used as a indirect object answering the question \"to whom\". (Well actually, this would best be translated as \"Man throws dog handgrenade \"  but th</SPAN><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">at\'s a subject for a different Long and Short of It.)<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">Jaké bydlení připravit psovi na zimu</SPAN></B><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">  What living conditions you should give a dog over the winter. (Literally: What type of living to prepare for a dog for the winter; from <A href=\"http://pes.eunet.cz/zviretnik/clanky/6570_8_6_0.html\">http://pes.eunet.cz/zviretnik/clanky/6570_8_6_0.html</A>) - used as an indirect object answering the question \"for whom?\"<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">The dative is also required, a bit idiomatically, by certain prepositions, most notably \"k\" (simple enough for English speakers  \"k\" usually means \"to\") and \"o\" (which means \"about\" well, live with it).<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">The ethical dative is on a different plane from this and most other Czech datives. Here are some examples in practice:<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">On ti dal Honzovi spousty prachů, ale teďka prý nemá na rozdávání </SPAN></B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"> <B>Hey listen </B>[Ti], he gave Honza lots of dough, but now  <I>he</I> says </SPAN><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">[</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">prý</SPAN><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">] </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"> he doesn\'t have any [omitted in the CZ] to spread around [na rozdávání]. <I>Here, a dative is being used to request the listener\'s attention, even though the verb\'s action does not directly affect the listener, at least in terms that are expressed by proper grammar but indeed, the speaker wants the listener to feel she <B>is</B> affected in the larger context, and thus should listen.</I><o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoBodyText style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><I><SPAN lang=CS><FONT size=2>Note how, strictly grammatically speaking, there are two datives, \"ti\" and \"Honzovi\" for the same verb in this sentence. I\'m sure this drives proponents of proper grammar mad.<o:p></o:p></FONT></SPAN></I></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<H2 style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><FONT size=2><SPAN lang=CS>On nám začal chodit za kolou.</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal\">  He started playing hooky </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>on us</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal\">. <I>In this case, this \"improper\" dative is being used to say \"and this </I></SPAN><I><SPAN lang=CS>is </SPAN></I><I><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal\">our business/ is having a negative effect on us.\" Note the words \"on us\" at the end of the last sentence  it</SPAN></I><I><SPAN style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">\'s no wonder that the English equivalent of this usage is \"on us.\"<o:p></o:p></SPAN></I></FONT></H2>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">On nám včera bolel prstíček, viď?</SPAN></B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">  Your widdle finger hurt yesterday, <B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\">huh</B>? / (Or: <B>Our </B>widdle finger hurt yesterday, <B>huh</B>?) <I>Here, the ethical dative is saying: your problem is <B>our </B>problem  I sympathize. Of course, spoken<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>ironically, it could also be sarcastic.</I></SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"> </SPAN><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">The <I>on</I> is often used with ethical dative even if the subject is expressed for additional emphasis.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">Just as an ethical dative can exist alongside other types of datives, a sentence can in extreme cases even contain multiple ethical datives, each of a different type:<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">Moje dcera ti n</SPAN></B><B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">ám </SPAN></B><B><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">zlomila Honzovi nohu!  You know what? </SPAN></B><SPAN style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">That daughter of mine went and broke Honza\'s leg <B>on us</B>! <I>Honzovi is a possessive dative giving us a grand total of three datives all related, strictly speaking, to the same verb.<o:p></o:p></I></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoBodyText2 style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p><EM><FONT size=2>&nbsp;</FONT></EM></o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoBodyText2 style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-STYLE: normal\"><FONT size=2>From the above you can see that the ethical dative is restricted to first- and second-person pronouns (\"I,\" \"we,\" and \"you\"), and is used for insisting that these actors  you, me, and us  are involved in a sentence, in a way that can\'t be implied using standard grammar. So the next time you hear a Czech say something that seems to have a few more dative pronouns than you expected, listen up  it may be exactly what she\'s looking for.<o:p></o:p></FONT></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoBodyText2 style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"FONT-STYLE: normal\"><o:p><FONT size=2>&nbsp;</FONT></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoBodyText2 style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><FONT size=2><B><SPAN style=\"FONT-STYLE: normal\">Já ti doufám, e mi z toho </SPAN></B><B><SPAN lang=CS style=\"FONT-STYLE: normal; mso-ansi-language: CS\">mýho </SPAN></B><B><SPAN style=\"FONT-STYLE: normal\">výkladu něco bude mít!<o:p></o:p></SPAN></B></FONT></P>', '2003-03-01 20:56:24', 1619, 83, 30),
(130, 'Karolína Světlá', '', 'Článek ze serveru <a href=http://www.zivotopisy.cz>http://www.zivotopisy.cz</a>', '<FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Karolína Světlá se narodila </FONT><FONT size=3><STRONG>24. 2. 1830</FONT></STRONG><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3> jako Johana Rottová. Její tatínek, Eustach Rott, přiel do Prahy z Českého Brodu. Johanka chodila do německé koly, kde se odehrály i její první autorské pokusy v němčině (jinak psát neuměla). Tyto texty jako závadné posílal učitel jejímu otci a ten ji pak za ně plísnil. Nicméně ve svých pokusech pokračovala dál. Tajně doma tvořila do zvlátního seitu. Kdy ho omylem vzala do koly, dostal se opět do rukou učitele. Ten objevil v Johance velký literární talent, co se tehdy bohuel povaovalo za hanbu a netěstí. Celou záleitost oznámil jejím rodičům. Ti ji ze koly vzali a učila se pak jen doma francouztině a hře na klavír. Zbytek času musela věnovat ručním pr</FONT><FONT size=3>a</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>cím. Zhruba kolem dvanácti let si začala vést deník v četině, aby se v ní lépe pocvičila. Nicméně deník nael otec. Musela mu odpřisáhnout, e u nikdy nevezme pero do ruky. Ale Johanka chtěla alespoň číst, kdy jí psát zakázali. Osud ji vyslechl v podobě osoby policejního komisaře Josefa Kreuzmanna, který jí umonil pravidelnou četbu německé literatury. Zde mne napadá paralela s Boenou Němcovou, je se zhruba ve stejném věku seznamovala s literaturou na zámku ve Chvalkovicích. Němcová ale vyrůstala na venkově v souznění s přírodou, ponechána sama sobě. Kdeto výchova Světlé byla měansky konvenční. Johanka se svou citlivou a hloubavou povahou v ovzduí nesmyslných konvencí trpěla. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>S příchodem rodinného učitele hudby Petra Muáka nastalo v rodině Rottových volnějí české smýlení. Muák se stýkal s tehdejími</FONT><FONT size=3> vlastenci</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3> a mnoho jich přivedl do Rottovy rodiny. Muák tak přivedl v roce 1849 Boenu Němcovou, která u několik let patřila mezi literáty. Johanka se s Němcovou přátelila do roku 1853. Němcová, silná osobnost, měla na Johanku velký vliv. Neuznávala konvence a jako jediného soudce uznávala své vlastní svědomí. Pro Johanku se otevřel nový svět. V Němcové poznala enu hrdou, nezávislou bojující o dívčí vzdělanost a práva eny. Roku </FONT><FONT size=3><STRONG>1852</STRONG> se Johank</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>a vdala za Petra Muáka. Rok nato porodila dceru Boenku, její kmotrou byla Němcová. Avak Boenka hodně churavěla a za pár měsíců zemřela. Johanka se ze smrti dcery nedokázala vzpamatovat. Jako terapii si zvolila psaní. Duevní činnost ji doporučil i lékař, snoubenec její sestry ofie, dr. Podlipský. V tomto nelehkém období poznala i Podjetědí, kam jí manel odvezl na prázdniny na zotavenou. </P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Roku <STRONG>1858</FONT></STRONG><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3> vstoupila Johanka Muáková do literatury jako Karolína Světlá prací s názvem \"Dvojí probuzení\" v pam</FONT><FONT size=3>átném</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3> prvním ročníku almanachu Máj. Ocitla se ve společnosti Vítězslava Hálka, Boeny Němcové a předevím Jana Nerudy. Poslední jmenovaný jí ovlivnil nejvíc. Z lásky k němu, z proitého vnitřního dramatu, čerpala a do své smrti. </P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>S Nerudou se setkala pop</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>rvé jetě coby studentem, kdy oba chodívali do rodiny českého vlastence Hemerky. Johana byla vdaná, ale přes vechnu úctu k manelovi se necítila v manelství astná. Sama řekla:\"on byl rozum a pozitivnost, já fantazie a zas fantazie, on kráčel, já chtěla létat.\" Od počátku si s Nerudou rozuměla. Více se s ním ale stýkala, a kdy pracoval jako novinář a redaktor a ona působila na poli literárním. V té době Nerudu, básníka nikdo neznal. Jeho poezii lidé nechápali a první sbírku \"Hřbitovní kvítí\" tvrdě k</FONT><FONT size=3>ritika odsoudila</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>. Neruda se nacházel v určité básnické krizi. Světlá ho neúnavně nabádala k dalí činnosti. Snaili se vzájemně podporovat. Tím se víc sblíili a Neruda se do Světlé zamiloval. Své city vyjadřoval upřímně a vánivě. Světlá ho stále odkazovala do mezí přátelství. On přitom po ní touil. Chtěl nejen jejího ducha, ale i tělo. Viděl v ní enu, ji si postavil na piedestal a hluboce se jí kořil. Přitom Světlá, narozdíl od Němcové nebyla zrovna výjimečná krasavice. Ale lo o souznění duí dvou lidí, kteří by za jiných okolností a v jiném století mohli a měli ít spolu. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>V tomto okamiku se musím trochu zastavit. Světlé je vyčítána dodnes určitá chladnost k citům Nerudy. Je srovnávána s Němcovou, která své milence bláznivě milovala, manel, nemanel. Tehdy se ale Praha rozhodně neblíila k anonymnímu velkoměstu. Světlá moná milovala Nerudu, víc ne si byla ochotná připustit. Ten hluboký cit ji rozhodně překvapil. Téměř ve třiceti letech nic tak intenzívního asi ani nečekala. Bojovala sama se se</FONT><FONT size=3>b</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>ou. Nechtěla ublíit rodině, ale nechtěla se vzdát Nerudy, proto zvolila cestu přátelství. Doufala, e ho tím neztratí. Neruda ji přemlouval, aby s ním utekla. Němcová by ho na jejím místě poslechla, jenome Světlá měla jinou povahu. Cítila odpovědnost vůči manelovi, kdy mu dala manelský slib. Těko se nalézají slova jak celou tu vnitřní tragédii Světlé popsat. Okamik citového tápání najdeme v dopise sestře ofii, kde píe o naturelu svého mue, o vzájemném neporozumění a pak se zmiňuje o Nerudovi: \"Ta</FONT><FONT size=3> druhá povaha, která se mi tak znenadání</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3> v cestu vpletla, je mi příli podobná...\" Dále píe, e \"není mravní na ádný pád, vzdor pohlavní čistoty\". Kdyby prý chtěla mravně jednat, tak by \"se nesměla na nic ohlednouti a jít za citem svým a ten jí praví hlasem neomylným, e by osvítila zatemnělou dui a svému ivotu teprv pravého dala významu.\" A Neruda se Světlé vyznává: \" .... Tato láska ze mne učinila to, čím jsi Ty, jsme si teď rovni. Já Ti jí víc nabízeti nebudu, ale já si ji také zakázati nedám.\" V p</FONT><FONT size=3>o</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>sledním svém dopise z prázdnin Světlá Nerudovi píe, jak se těí na návrat a na něj. Bohuel k jejich shledání nedolo. </P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=right><A href=\"http://www.zivotopisy.cz/clanky.php?id=18\">http://www.zivotopisy.cz/clanky.php?id=18</A></P></FONT>', '2003-03-05 22:54:42', 310, 250, 110),
(131, 'Listy z Prahy', 'Jan Čulík', 'Článek z Britských listů o slubách v ČR. <A href=\"http://www.britskelisty.cz/brit/97/0804brit.htm\">http://www.britskelisty.cz/brit/97/0804brit.htm</A>', '<FONT face=\"Times New Roman\">\r\n<H3>Vojanova restaurace a dalí záitky</H3></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Zmínil jsem se, e jsme zasedli tento týden jeden den dopoledne v Praze s Ivanem Kytkou ke kávě v jedné malostranské restauraci. Nebylo to ale tak jednoduché. </P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Na jídelním lístku, s luxusními cenami, vystav</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>eném u vchodu, jsme se dověděli, e restaurace má otevřeno od 10.30. Bylo 10 hodin 25 minut. Restaurace byla otevřená a mezi stoly v zahradním areálu se pohybovali číníci. Vstoupili jsme tedy dovnitř a zeptali se, zda můeme k jednomu stolu usednout a za</FONT><FONT size=3> </FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>několik minut, a bude restaurace oficiálně otevřena, si objednat snídani. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Z Británie jsem naučen, e zákazník je vdycky pán a vdycky má pravdu - platí to zejména v zařízení, kde zaplatím za snídani několik stovek korun. Čím je podnik draí, tím je personál vstřícnějí. V Praze to ale zdá se neplatí, v draích podnicích se jen od turistů vybírají větí peníze, ale kvalita slueb lepí není. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Číníci nás z luxusní Vojanovy restaurace tři minuty před otevřením prostě víceméně </FONT><B><FONT size=3>vyhodili</B></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>, a si přijdeme, a bude oficiálně otevřeno. Vojanova restaurace měla zároveň otevřeno i zavřeno. </P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\">\r\n<H3>Zvyk v obchodech zákazníkům účtovat za taky z igelitu</H3></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\"Jdu koupit něco k snídani,\" řekl jsem ve středu ráno ve dveřích své matce. \"Prosím tě, vra se a vezmi si taku,\" odpověděla, \"nebo ti zas budou účtovat peníze za igelitový pytlík.\" </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Ano, přiznám se, e jsem naučen chodit nakupovat bez taek. V obchodě vám na Západě přidají k nakoupenému zboí tzv. carrier bag, igelitovou taku, zadarmo. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Proč vyadují v ČR v obchodem za igelitovou k útratě jetě dalích několik korun? Nebylo by pro ně vůči zákazníkům vstřícnějím a diplomatičtějím gestem, kdyby si cenu těch taek započítali do ceny výrobků? </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Sveřepost některých českých prodavačů je pozoruhodná. Jsou naučeni na to, povaovat zákazníka za nepřítele. Jako zákazník nemám zřejmě v některých obchodem právo na víc ne zaplatit. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Kupoval jsem na trhu na Havelském náměstí od nějaké eny z lidu u stánku asi pět kilo jablek. Z velikosti jablek vyplývalo, e nákup měl pět kilogramů a pět dekagramů. Řekl jsem: \"Vzhledem k tomu, e těch jablek tady kupuji vlastně docela dost, můete to zaokrouhlit dolů na celých pět kilogramů?\" Odpověď zněla urputně a tvrdohlavě: \"Ne\". A paní mně přesně účtovala za těch dalích pět deka o dvě koruny naví</FONT><FONT size=3>c. </P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Pak jsem kupoval v samoobsluze v iroké ulici potraviny asi za pět set korun. Prodavačka u kasy mi k tomu za igelitový pytlík připočetla tři koruny a taky nedala si říct, e vzhledem k velikosti nákupu by měla projevit určitou velkomyslnost. Zavolala vedoucího, tomu jsem se věc snail vysvětlit, podpořil ji a tvrdě vyadoval tři koruny za pytlík. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>K jiné scénce dolo v samoobsluze na náměstí v Nové Bystřici. Děti si chtěly koupit pár pohledů a kdy je přinesly k pokladně, doadovaly se k nim i známek. \"Známky nevedeme,\" zněla přísná odpověď. Ondřej Hausenblas, který zrovna el kolem pokladny, vtrhl do rozhovoru: \"Tak ty pohledy od nich tady v té samoobsluze nekupujte\", řekl mně i dětem. \"A se naučí, jak se vede obchod,\" dodal nekompromisně. To vyvolalo </FONT><FONT size=3>p</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>ozdviení: \"My se máme cosi učit?\" ozvala se pohorena prodavačka. </P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\">\r\n<H3>Rekordně nejdrzejí postoj k veřejnosti</H3></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Hned po vchodu do obchodu na pravé straně je velký nápis přiblině tohoto znění, v četině i v angličtině (cituju po paměti): </P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\"Zákazníci jsou povin</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>ováni pouít při nákupu vozíku. Vstupem do obchodu také dávají jeho zaměstnancům právo</FONT><B><FONT size=3> prohledat jejich soukromé taky</B>.\" </P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Je to pozůstatek policejního státu? Ano, vude se krade v obchodech. Doufám, e ten obchod zkrachuje. </P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\">\r\n<H3>O podvádění</H3></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Na rudolfinskou výs</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>tavu jsem el se sedmiletou dcerou. Kupoval jsem vstupenky na Praském hradě, děti do esti let měly vstup na výstavu zadarmo. Jene mé dceři je sedm let. Chtěl jsem tedy i vstupenku pro ni. Dívky, které mi lístky prodávaly, mi řekly, a ji nekupuju, prot</FONT><FONT size=3>o</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>e nikdo nepozná, e dítěti není est let, ale sedm. </P>\r\n<P align=justify>Co byste v takové situaci udělali vy? Má cenu těm slečnám u té pokladny ten problém vysvětlovat a přes jejich doporučení na koupi lístku pro sedmileté dítě trvat? </P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\">\r\n<H3>Jan Čulík, 4. 8. 1997</H3></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify><A href=\"http://www.britskelisty.cz/brit/97/0804brit.htm\">http://www.britskelisty.cz/brit/97/0804brit.htm</A> </P></FONT>', '2003-04-22 17:10:49', 326, 256, 20);
 which I think everybody is  because she is that argumentative. (comedian Adam Bloom)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<h5 style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 50.4pt\"><a name=\"_Toc41104230\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><em><font size=\"3\">Special cases</font>\r\n </em>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</h5>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Both languages also have a number of elliptical constructions which are lexical in nature and may or may not have an equivalent. For instance, </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">in these two examples which </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Grepl and Karlík </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">(1986, p. 341)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">cite as </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">equivalent examples of ellipsis in Czech, the first</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">cannot be expressed elliptically in English while the second can</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">: <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">23.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Otec musí </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">[] </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">na schůzi.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(My) father must </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">[go] to the meeting.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">24.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Sestra začala </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">[]</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">večerní kolu.</span>\r\n <span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\"><br />\r\nMy sister started [] night school.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">Czech has a whole range of systemic elliptical constructions following modal verbs (particularly in spoken language as exemplified in 23) which are not available in English. <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">A number of examples of differences in both directions can be identified which are a consequence of lexical or collocational meaning. This and other features described below can lead us to the concept which we might call cohesive prosody a twin concept to semantic prosody (different from cohesive harmony). Here are two examples: <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref41036011\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">25.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Domluvili jsme se [] na večer.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">We agreed [to meet/or do something clear from context] in the evening.</span>\r\n </a>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref41036528\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">26.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">The progress report <u>gives the clearest hint</u>\r\n yet that the 15-nation </span>\r\n</a>\r\n<place />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41036528\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Union</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</place />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41036528\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u>could</u>\r\n soon <u>be transformed</u>\r\n , and its population <u>[] expanded</u>\r\n from 370 million to 450 million.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41036528\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Postupová zpráva <u>naznačuje</u>\r\n dosud nejjasněji, e unie patnácti národů <u>můe projít transformací</u>\r\n a její populace <u>se můe zvýit </u>\r\nz&nbsp;370ti miliónů na 450.</span>\r\n </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">The Czech verb <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">domluvit se </i>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(to agree on) in example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41036011 r h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 25</span>\r\n , clearly contains enough meaning so that any further specification of what was agreed can be extracted from context or even cultural assumptions. The English <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">agree </i>\r\nwill elide much less willingly. Example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41036528 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 26</span>\r\n exemplifies not only the difference in the ellipsis of auxiliary verbs but also the different lexical approach of the two languages in both directions: English <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">gives hint </i>\r\nwhere Czech <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">hints</i>\r\n and English <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">is transformed </i>\r\nwhere Czech <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">undergoes a transformation</i>\r\n .</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">Sometimes a difference in ellipsis is a consequence of different valencies (both verbal and nominal) which while not significant in itself may cause severe disruptions in translated texts. The English passive is often another cohesive element which is elliptical from the perspective of Czech (although as the example below shows this works in the opposite direction as well). Other examples can be identified which are a consequence of lexical meaning. The following example combines all the above.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">27.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Doma se vechno nestihne. <br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">At home, everything cannot be managed/done [on time]. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The verb </span>\r\n<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">stihnout</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">meaning</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">to manage </span>\r\n</i>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">implies both timeliness and completion and is contextually strongly bound to housework. It is further passivized (passive reflexive) which makes the agent elliptical. It would be a brave translator however, who would offer this English equivalent: <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Its hard to get all the housework done. <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Although Czech does not posses lexical items to substitute for verbs (such as <i>do, so</i>\r\n ), it can and does use the negative particle with the verb elided which is questionable in English with the negative particle and impossible with the positive particle.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">28.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">We dont know which of them are harmful and which [are] not.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo14\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(a)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Nevíme, které jsou kodlivé a které ne.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(b)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Nevíme, které jsou kodlivé a které nejsou.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">29.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">We dont know which of them are harmful and which are [yes].</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo18\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(a)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Nevíme, které jsou kodlivé a které ano.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(b)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Nevíme, které jsou kodlivé a které jsou.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<h3 style=\"MARGIN: 12pt 0cm 3pt 36pt\"><a name=\"_Toc106526252\"></a>\r\n<a name=\"_Toc41104231\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc106526252\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Arial\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\"><font face=\"Arial\">1.2.2</font>\r\n <span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Arial\">Organic Grammatical Cohesion</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</h3>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The concept of organic cohesion was introduced by Hasan (1985) to serve as the opposition to componential cohesion. It covers all cohesive devices whose main function is to provide cohesive links.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<h5 style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 50.4pt\"><a name=\"_Toc41104232\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><em><font size=\"3\">Asyndetic connection</font>\r\n </em>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</h5>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Halliday and Hasan do not see asyndetic connection as a separate category but both the English Comprehensive and the Czech Academic grammars make a point of its status in the cohesive system. Although, syndeticity is clearly a matter of degree, it is a useful distinction for a contrastive study concerned not only with semantic but also formal relations. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Although both languages commonly use asyndetic connection as a cohesive device, there are some systemic differences which impact other aspects of the cohesive system. It would appear that Czech is much less tolerant of asyndetic connection in certain cases than English particularly in expository texts. Czech students of English frequently report a need for expressions to use to connect their sentences and are frustrated when certain asyndetic connections appear in a text. Their intuition receives some support from frequency studies of texts in both languages. </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Těitelová </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">et al. (1987) reports an average frequency of conjunctions in Czech of about 10%, whereas a similar analysis carried out on English by Johansson and Hofland </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1989)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">shows conjunctions (both coordinating and subordinating) at just over 5%. Neither study provides a list of conjunctions included in this number (although T</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ěitelová </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">et al. report 87 lexemes excluding relative pronouns and adverbs) but the result is quite telling. The importance of this disparity is further underscored when we look at the top 10 most frequent words in Czech and English. The English list will include only one conjunction (and as the third most frequent), whereas Czech boasts two conjunctions (a [and] at number one and </span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">e</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"> [that] at number seven) and a relative pronoun (kter</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ý</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"> [which] as number ten). This remains true even after we exclude analytic items such as the, of, a, and to.<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn26\" href=\"#_ftn26\" name=\"_ftnref26\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[26]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\nThe last piece of evidence to support the claim of Czechs reluctance for the asyndetic is the limited use of the semi-colon which does not even appear on the original Czech keyboard layout and the much less frequent use of the semi-colon.<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn27\" href=\"#_ftn27\" name=\"_ftnref27\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[27]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The thing that makes comparison of asyndetic constructions difficult is that first language accounts understandably focus on what is possible rather than what is likely to be precluded. So, for instance, an observation such as one that Czech is reluctant to use lists where the last item is not preceded by a (and) whereas English uses this feature quite frequently for emphasis are hard to provide conclusive evidence for either from literature or text analysis. The difference in the use of asyndetic connection will be significant however, because it impacts on the use of connectives (see below). </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<h5 style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 50.4pt\"><a name=\"_Toc41104233\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><em><font size=\"3\">Connectives</font>\r\n </em>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</h5>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Connectives have been dealt with most comprehensively within the domain of syntax as conjunctions or the domain of logic as logical operators. They are not as commonly treated as an element connecting broader segments of discourse. Halliday and Hasan include them in the cohesive category of Conjunction but sometimes it may be hard to draw a firm line between this and the category of Lexical Cohesion. I will argue that connectives are in fact often anaphoric in nature (similarly to <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nFrances</country-region />\r\n </place />\r\n Anaphoric nouns or Winters Vocabulary 3) and are subject to similar principles of accessibility as the more conventional anaphors.<a name=\"_Ref41274514\"></a>\r\n <a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn28\" href=\"#_ftn28\" name=\"_ftnref28\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41274514\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[28]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41274514\"></span>\r\nAs such, they can be best explained using the concept of image-rich and image-schematic mental spaces (Fauconnier 1985, 1997, Werth 1997, Lakoff 1987) and only an account that explains connectives as anaphoric and deictic phenomena can adequately deal with these issues as well. A more detailed analysis will be provided in the next chapter, what follows is only a sketch of the problems that arise. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Halliday and Hasan (1976, p. 226) set connectives apart from other cohesive devices: Conjunctive elements are cohesive not in themselves but indirectly by virtue of their specific meanings; they are not primarily devices for reaching out into the preceding (or following) text, but they express certain meanings which presuppose the presence of other components in the discourse. This is why in Hasans (1985) diagram, conjunction was classified as an organic rather than a componential cohesive device. Unlike, for instance pronouns, interpreting a conjunction does not initiate a search for an antecedent but rather a specification of the way in which what is to follow is systematically connected to what has gone before (Halliday and Hasan 1976, p. 227). One of the questions explored in the next chapter will be to what extent this holds when confronted with the actual usage in naturally occurring texts. For instance, Gutwinskis </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1976)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">treatment of <i>but</i>\r\n and <i>however</i>\r\n mentioned in footnote <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' NOTEREF _Ref41274514 h \'\">27</span>\r\n seems to suggest otherwise.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Here are some of the actual phenomena that have the function of connectives. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Hedges  </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">toti</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">, </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">snad</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">, </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">často</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">, actually, technically, supposedly</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Structural vs. lexical: the answer is, its X, that  </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">jene, prostě</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">u, a, vak, however, etc.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">parentheticals</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">vůbec, anyway</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">the above, latter, above all, concerning, pokud jde o, výe zmíněný, jeden z nich</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">predicate instrumental</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">-</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"> (as in který, </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">etc.)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListBullet\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 44pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-FAMILY: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">ˇ<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Anaphoric nouns, adjectives, adverbs, Vocabulary 3 items</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">As I mentioned earlier, Czech students of English overpopulate their texts with connectives such as <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">so, then</i>\r\n , etc. while English learners of Czech either under-use them or use them incorrectly. Apart from exceptional cases, this results in largely grammatical sentences which are judged to be unnatural. This suggest that finding direct correspondences between the two languages will be difficult because the source language imposes its connective conventions on the target language and translators are not challenged to establish consistent rules for interpreting both syndetic and asyndetic connections.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Even where there are straightforward equivalents, the connectives in each language exhibit a number of idiosyncrasies that come to the fore in a comparative account. For instance, Pechar </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1986, p. 11)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">reports that the Czech a (and) cannot be used to equate things not directly comparable. As in the English sentence: He was thin and tentative as he slid his birth certificate from <place />\r\nPuerto Rico</place />\r\n across the desk. where and tentative would have to be translated with a subordinate clause.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">But there are some more general differences which affect the way in which discourse is viewed. In Chapter 3, I will argue that referring to a mental space as a whole vs. referring to parts forming that mental space is an important dynamic factor in the use of connectives. Hedges, for instance, pick out, an attitudinal aspect of the mental image rather than any specific referential element. I hope to show that Czech connectives appear to include much of this modal information while English connectives restrict themselves to the expression of a logical relationship and that this aspect of a syndetic connection is what Czech readers miss in English texts. This claim is also consistent with evidence from language acquisition. Kesselov</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">á </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(2002)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">refers to Pa</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">č</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">esovás </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1979)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">research citing evidence that conjunctions are the last part of speech for children to master. She attributes this to the abstract relational meaning of conjunctions but it may also have something to do with their modality which is the last feature acquired in English </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(cf. Palmer, 1990)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">. Hypertrophy of and is typical of the early stages of development in both languages. Halliday and Hasan (1976, p. 233) attribute the discomfort of adults with this feature of child speech to the fact that children confuse the structural use of and with its conjunctive function. An complementary explanation might be that a child imbues her ands with modal meanings which are not part of the adult usage of the word. Yet, despite the similar perception of the child language deficiencies in both languages, unlike Czech, English can use this feature for emphasis as this translator of Kunderas <i>Book of Laughter and Forgetting</i>\r\n chose to do twice in the space of two paragraphs:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">30.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Poletoval sníh<u>,</u>\r\n bylo chladno <u>a</u>\r\n Gottwald byl prostovlasý.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">It was snowing <u>and</u>\r\n cold, <u>and</u>\r\n <span style=\"LETTER-SPACING: -0.05pt\">Gottwald was bareheaded.</span>\r\n </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">31.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\">Tu fotografii znalo z plakátů<u>,</u>\r\n učebnic <u>a</u>\r\n museí kadé dítě.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Every <span style=\"LETTER-SPACING: 0.2pt\">child knew the photograph, from seeing it on posters <u>and</u>\r\n in schoolbooks <u>and</u>\r\n mu</span>\r\n <span style=\"LETTER-SPACING: -0.25pt\">seums.</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The key problem that arises in this context is the difference between grammaticality, communicability and acceptability. It is expected of contrastive studies that they show clear limits of what is acceptable in one language and not in the other but this might prove impossible in this case. Very few uses of connectives in one language would be considered ungrammatical if translated literally. Barring blatant mistranslations, very seldom do we find an alteration of meaning on a scale sufficient to allow us to claim a major shift in the information conveyed. But we must remember that more than information (in the sense of purely prepositional content) is conveyed by connectives. It might be argued that the use of connectives is merely a matter of good style but this should not preclude us from looking at what the conventions are. Good style must follow some rules after all, even if they are not the same type of rule as those governing the sentence. What makes example <a name=\"_Hlt40680349\"></a>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlt40680349\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">21</span>\r\n </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlt40680349\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(b) an adequate translation of <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref40680151 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 21</span>\r\n ? I will argue that it is the need of the Czech language to express modal connections which results in cohesive prosody. A similar point was made by </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Levý</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">s </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1963, p. 103)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">who identifies words such as </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">pak, jen, kdy, tedy, přece, třeba</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">as </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">important for maintaining the fluency and naturalness of texts (particularly literary texts) translated into Czech. He calls them free of meaning but I will argue that they are in fact constructions whose meaning can be found in the modality of mental spaces and whose function is cohesive prosody (see example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41275301 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 47</span>\r\n on page <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' PAGEREF _Ref41275301 h \'\"><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes\">6</span>\r\n </span>\r\n).</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">If we accept their anaphoric nature, connectives also seem to stand outside the low-accessibility end of Ariels accessibility scale such as pronouns. As such, they might be better served by a more cognitive account such as van Hoeks or Langackers. Another question that will need to be answered is, whether the difference in use between connectives in Czech and in English can be ascribed to typological or conventional differences, or needs to be firmly dealt with as a difference in how each language conceptualizes the world. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<h3 style=\"MARGIN: 12pt 0cm 3pt 36pt\"><a name=\"_Toc106526253\"></a>\r\n<a name=\"_Toc41104234\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc106526253\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Arial\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\"><font face=\"Arial\">1.2.3</font>\r\n <span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Arial\">Lexical cohesion</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</h3>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">There are few differences between Czech and English in lexical cohesion that can be identified to be a consequence of language type. There seem to be, however, significant if subtle differences in the conventions on usage. Czech texts have somewhat stricter limits on repetition (referred to as elegant variation by English style manuals). This might be partly a consequence of the different conventions on the use of word similarity but it is also culturally socialized. </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Bečka </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(1948, p. 323)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">proposes the following dictum</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">: </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">The basic requirement of stylistic variability is not to repeat a stylistic element while its previous usage is still activated. Different elements require different length of text before they can be repeated and no exact rules are provided. Bečka does however propose a rule of thumb: The rarer the occurrence of the item the fewer repetitions. Grammatical elements can be repeated much more freely. Although this rule can be broken for emphasis, the example below shows that repetition for emphasis is not the same in both languages. The English sentence has three possible Czech equivalents but a quick survey on a translators discussion group elicited (a) as the most appropriate translation of the original. <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref40692491\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">32.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">The man had made the boy and now the man must wither.</span>\r\n </a>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo13\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(a)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Mu stvořil chlapce a teď musí uvadnout.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(b)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Mu stvořil chlapce a teď mu musí uvadnout.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(c)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Mu stvořil chlapce a mu teď musí uvadnout.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">It is interesting that similar injunctions against repetition as those expressed by Be</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">čka</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">can be found in all English style books for very much the same reason:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalQuote\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 14.2pt 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">I try to avoid starting two successive sentences with the same word unless I\'m deliberately creating parallelism. I also avoid starting two successive paragraphs with the same word. These aren\'t hard and fast rules, but I find them useful. They force me to vary my sentence structure, so I don\'t fall into plodding rhythms. (James Alan Gardner, 2001, A Seminar on Writing Prose)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Few such guides venture as far as to suggest actual set limits on elegant variation. Bečka admonishes against repeating certain unique or highly figurative expressions even within one document or an entire body of work on one end of the scale, and on the other advises not to repeat words with the same stem even within one sentence. But most style guides for both languages fall back on subjective measures such as rhythm and freshness. Despite these similarities, it is beyond any doubt that Czech is much more sensitive to repetition and many more examples similar to <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref40692491 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 32</span>\r\n can be found. It is another example of explicit culturally shared rules not resulting in the same patterns of usage as we saw in the case of third-person pronouns. One indication of this difference could be the frequent criticism of excessive elegant variation in English style guides<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn29\" href=\"#_ftn29\" name=\"_ftnref29\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[29]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\nwhich would be more difficult to find in a similar Czech publication.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">This difference could be partly a consequence of the grammatical type. As an analytic language, English is forced to repeat many more lexical items even within the same sentence (e.g. articles, auxiliaries, some prepositions) while </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">Be</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">čka</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\"></span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">advises even against repeating the same preposition in adjacent sentences in <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nCzech.</country-region />\r\n </place />\r\n<p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">There is a subtle difference between elegant variation and reiteration as described by Halliday and Hasan (1976). Again both Czech and English use the same range of devices (as do probably most languages) but their acceptability as something to express cohesive links varies greatly. Halliday and Hasan (1976, p. 279) identify three kinds of reiteration: (a) same word, (b)&nbsp;synonym, (c) superordinate and (d) general word giving these examples:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref41097692\"><span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">33.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">T</span>\r\n </a>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41097692\"><span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">heres <u>a boy</u>\r\n climbing that tree.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41097692\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Na ten strom leze nějaký chlapec.</span>\r\n </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41097692\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo12\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(a)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"CS\">The boy</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">s</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\">going to fall if he doesnt take care.<br />\r\n(a\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n*Jestli si chlapec nedá pozor, tak spadne.<br />\r\n(a\'\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n?Jestli si ten chlapec nedá pozor, tak spadne.<br />\r\n(a\'\'\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nJestli si nedá pozor, spadne.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(b)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"CS\">The lads</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">going to fall if he doesnt take care.<br />\r\n(b\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n??Jestli si nedá pozor, tak ten kluk spadne.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(c)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"CS\">The child</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">s going to fall if he doesnt take care.<br />\r\n(c\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n??Jestli si nedá pozor, tak to dítě spadne.<br />\r\n(c\'\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n?Vdy to dítě spadne! (Oh, the childs going to fall!)<br />\r\n(c\'\'\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nSlez dolů dítě! Spadne. (Come down child! Youre going to fall!)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(d)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"CS\">The idiot</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">s going to fall if he doesnt take care.<br />\r\n(d\')<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nJestli si nedá pozor tak ten hlupák spadne.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">We can see that only (d) allows for a straightforward reiteration. Similarly to example&nbsp;<span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref40692491 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 32</span>\r\n , the repetition of the same word is so unacceptable as to make (a\') ungrammatical without some definiteness marker (which could be zero in highly emphatic colloquial speech). Sentences (b) and (c) in example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref40692491 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 32</span>\r\n were merely awkward, however, because the repetition could be seen to serve a certain purpose of emphasis which is obviously missing in this case. (a\'\'\') is the only acceptable translation. The same holds for (b\') and (c\') which could be even interpreted as referring to a different child. The hyperonym becomes more acceptable as the mood changes. Interestingly enough, only the reiteration through a general word seems to be acceptable. Both Czech and English have a sufficient if not matching arsenal of these words but they have only been sufficiently described for English </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(cf. Francis, 1986)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">A twin concept to that of reiteration is something Halliday and Hasan (1976, p. 184) refer to as collocation. I consider Sinclairs term </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(see Partington, 1998, p. 67)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">semantic prosody</i>\r\n more appropriate, however, and will use it throughout this study. I will reserve the term collocability for instances where two or more words are likely to appear together and will consider it one of the devices used to achieve <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">cohesive prosody</i>\r\n (see below). Semantic prosody can be expected to appear in any language but the actual prosodic patterns will inevitably differ across different languages and even different genres. The following example will illustrate many of the possible differences.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref41101652\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">34.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1) In </span>\r\n</a>\r\n<place />\r\n<country-region />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41101652\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Iraq</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</country-region />\r\n</place />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41101652\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">today, our military is <u>focused</u>\r\n and <u>unwavering</u>\r\n . (2) We have an effective plan of <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">battle</i>\r\n and the flexibility to <u>meet</u>\r\n every <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">challenge</i>\r\n . (3) Nothing -- nothing -- will <u>divert</u>\r\n us from our clear <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">mission</i>\r\n . (4) We will <u>press on</u>\r\n through every <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">hardship</i>\r\n . (5) We will <u>overcome</u>\r\n every <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">danger</i>\r\n . (6) And we will <u>prevail</u>\r\n . (</span>\r\n </span>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.whitehouse.gov/news/releases/2003/03/20030326-4.html\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41101652\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">http://www.whitehouse.gov/news/releases/2003/03/20030326-4.html</span>\r\n </span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41101652\"></span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41101652\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">) <br />\r\n<br />\r\n(1) </span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41101652\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Nae armáda je dnes v Iráku plně <u>soustředěná</u>\r\n na svůj <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">úkol</i>\r\n . (2a) Máme připravený efektivní <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">bitevní plán</i>\r\n a (2b) jsme dostatečně flexibilní, abychom se mohli <u>vypořádat</u>\r\n se vemi <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">problémy</i>\r\n . (3a) Nic nás <u>neodvrátí</u>\r\n od naeho <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">cíle</i>\r\n , (3b) který je jasný. (4) Budeme <u>pokračovat</u>\r\n v naí práci přes vekeré <u>překáky</u>\r\n . (5)&nbsp;<u>Překonáme</u>\r\n vechna <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">nebezpečí</i>\r\n a (6) <u>zvítězíme</u>\r\n . (my translation)</span>\r\n </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Although it was possible for me as the translator to preserve both cohesive harmony and prosody (see below) of the English text, this could be done only at the price of lexical equivalence. One of the first issues is lexical availability and collocability, therefore for instance <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">focused and unwavering</i>\r\n became <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">focused on its task</i>\r\n and <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">prevail</i>\r\n was simplified as <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">win</i>\r\n . This also became an issue when translating <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">clear mission</i>\r\n which became <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">goal, which is clear. </i>\r\nThe lexical chains in the two texts can be summarized as follows:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p><table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: medium none; BORDER-COLLAPSE: collapse; mso-table-layout-alt: fixed; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-insideh: .5pt solid windowtext; mso-border-insidev: .5pt solid windowtext\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" border=\"1\"><tbody><tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid\"><td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: windowtext 1pt solid; WIDTH: 218pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"348\" colspan=\"2\"><p class=\"NormalTableHeading\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\"><strong>English</strong>\r\n </font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 218pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"348\" colspan=\"2\"><p class=\"NormalTableHeading\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><strong><font size=\"2\">Czech</font>\r\n </strong>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1\"><td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: windowtext 1pt solid; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><strong><font size=\"2\">predicative <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</strong>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">appelative <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">predicative <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">appelative <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid\"><td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: windowtext 1pt solid; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">focused / unwavering <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">meet <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">divert <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">press on <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">overcome <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">prevail <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">battle <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">challenge <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">mission <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">hardship <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">danger <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><p><font size=\"2\"></font>\r\n</p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><font size=\"2\"></font>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><font size=\"2\"></font>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><font size=\"2\"></font>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">focused <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">tackle <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">divert <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">continue <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">overcome <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">win <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 109pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" width=\"174\"><p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">task <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">battle plan <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">problems <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">goal <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">obstacles <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalTable\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">danger <p></p>\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">We see that only one predicate and one appelate were translated with full lexical and grammatical equivalence. It is clear, however, that both sets of lexical chains constitute a roughly equivalent semantic pattern including their interaction. The slightly more generic terms used in Czech are compensated for by the addition of an additional item and two subordinate clauses. We might then tentatively conclude that although much of the lexical identity is lost, the overall cohesive harmony of the text (as defined by Hasan 1985 and discussed below) is preserved. The same conclusion would be reached if we adopted Hoeys (1991) repetition matrices as an analytical tool. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">A different and broader view of repetition is presented by Hoey (1991) who sees it as the principal cohesive mechanism and therefore includes coreferential devices such as pronominal anaphora under the heading. According to Hoey, there are nine possible types of links of different weight that can exist between sentences in a text. They are in order of weight (p. 83, and p. 65): </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">1.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">simple lexical repetition (writer  writers)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">2.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">complex lexical repetition (writer  writings, happy  unhappy)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">3.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">simple mutual paraphrase (writer  author)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">4.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">simple partial paraphrase (writings  volume)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">5.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">antonymous complex paraphrase (author  plagiarist)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">6.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">other complex paraphrase (author  writings)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">7.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">substitution</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">8.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">co-reference</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">9.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">ellipsis</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Since Hoey is interested in exploring the cohesive patterns of complete texts of chapter to book length, his interest obviously lies in cohesive ties that span over paragraphs (or in some cases an entire book), particularly in non-narrative texts, and therefore purely coreferential devices such as substitution are outside his scope of interest. In fact, he mostly deals with relations which are outside the lowest point on Ariels (1990) accessibility scale which lends further support to the claim that accessibility is only one dimension of cohesion advanced in the previous??? chapter. Perhaps more importantly, unlike with Halliday and Hasans classification, we can find direct equivalents of Hoeys types of repetition in Czech texts without encountering the same problems as in example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41097692 r h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 33</span>\r\n above. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<h5 style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 50.4pt\"><a name=\"_Toc41104235\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><em><font size=\"3\">Discourse and text management</font>\r\n </em>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</h5>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; PAGE-BREAK-AFTER: auto\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Lexical availability is also an issue when we compare two languages. Each language has a variety of discourse management tools and what is available to the speakers (and writers). These include processes described by Hoey (1991, p. 172-3) as <i>discourse expansion</i>\r\n which includes some of the phenomena I refer to as discourse management. Hoey looks at universal phenomena that distinguish human processing of a text from that done by a machine such as reference to the first sentence which is understood to be the first sentence of a paragraph or interpreting the authorial we as referring to one person rather than a group or royalty. If we expand this group, we find that, despite significant overlap, there are also significant differences between the two languages. A brief look at Francis (1986) anaphoric nouns categories such as text nouns or verbal activity nouns, shows that although most of these are available in Czech, there are some notable and striking exceptions that will have a significant impact on the cohesion of texts written in some expository genres. Some of the words without an easy equivalent when used anaphorically are: account, corollary, plea, narrative, tenet, claim, pipedream, installment, volume, issue, matter and others (Francis 1986, pp. 12  16). Sometimes, it is only a question of Czech not having an equal number of synonyms such as account and claim which can be both translated as </span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">tvrzení</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"> or </span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">výrok</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"> as can statement or assertion. Others, such as corollary or plea have no direct equivalents in their anaphoric incarnation. There are inevitably cases where it is the English speaker who is left looking for an equivalent as with the word </span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">paragraf</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"> which refers to a section in legal texts and is difficult to always translate accurately into English.<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn30\" href=\"#_ftn30\" name=\"_ftnref30\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[30]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">When we expand this category to include other parts of speech such as adverbs we will see that English commands a much wider arsenal of discourse management tools. This allows for a richer conceptualization of discourse in English which results in easier non-technical communication about text.<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn31\" href=\"#_ftn31\" name=\"_ftnref31\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[31]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\nFor instance, Czech has no equivalent of latter/former other than the first/the last which makes some translations awkward. The grammatical requirements excluding adverbs from being used as adjectives in Czech make the use of above/below in phrases such as in the above argument more awkward, as well. Some phrases conceptualizing discourse, for instance, operative word or respective/ly are completely absent in Czech and frequently present a significant challenge to the translator.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Some of these differences are due to linguistic type and some to the way speakers conceptualize discourse. A significant number of these differences must also be attributed to the conventions of usage and cultural acceptability. A case in point is poetic language. For the most part, Czech has much stricter sanctions on rhyme (only euphony is allowed) and rhyme is the only figure of speech commonly recognized and perceived as euphonic<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn32\" href=\"#_ftn32\" name=\"_ftnref32\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[32]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n. This has implications for non-poetic language as well. For instance, most Czechs do not perceive alliteration to establish a strong connection between words even when more than two or three adjacent items are alliterated. In a recent childrens book series Lemony Snicket A series of unfortunate incidents, all thirteen titles were alliterated including Ersatz Elevator. Although the Czech translators tried to maintain this feature, they had to give up only after the second title because the distortions in meaning were too severe and not offset by euphonic gains.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">These differences spill over into what native speakers can say about their discourse. Again, it seems that English has more firmly established norms and conventions which have the corresponding common-usage terms such as elegant variation mentioned above. Figurative language is common to both Czech and English as a cohesive device but it seems that the conventions on its appropriateness vary with significant implications for the translator which transcend those that can be predicted from differences in shapes of words and collocability. As a reflection of this, non-technical Czech lacks some terms that are commonly used in English such as mixed metaphor or malapropism. A significant concept that is missing in Czech is that of pun. Although, pun can be translated into Czech as </span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">slovní hříčka</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"> (play on words) and much of Czech popular poetry and advertising revel in it, it is virtually absent from prose and especially from expository texts such as newspapers and academic writing. A quick look at an English language newspaper will reveal if not puns then highly figurative language in the headlines, such as <a href=\"http://www.timesonline.co.uk/article/0,,2-682606,00.html\"><span style=\"COLOR: windowtext; TEXT-DECORATION: none; text-underline: none\">Soundless music to make good vibrations</span>\r\n </a>\r\n in The Sunday Times of <date month=\"5\" day=\"18\" year=\"2003\" />\r\n18 May 2003</date />\r\n . Similar headlines are virtually unheard of in Czech newspapers and puns such as in the examples below would be completely unacceptable for a serious journalist to use.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref41067810\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">35.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">With his blond hair and blue eyes, Lieutenant General David McKiernan scarcely resembles any previous <u>caliph</u>\r\n of </span>\r\n</a>\r\n<place />\r\n<city />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41067810\"><u><span lang=\"EN-US\">Baghdad</span>\r\n </u>\r\n</span>\r\n</city />\r\n</place />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41067810\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">. Yet the task of <u>ruling</u>\r\n <country-region />\r\n<u>Iraq</u>\r\n </country-region />\r\nmay soon fall on the shoulders of a <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nUS</country-region />\r\n </place />\r\narmy commander whose main interest in <u>Arabian nights</u>\r\n is the cover of <u>darkness</u>\r\n they provide for his troops.</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref41067813\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">36.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">If </span>\r\n</a>\r\n<city />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41067813\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Sharon</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</city />\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41067813\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">blocks progress towards a Palestinian state, will <place />\r\n<state />\r\n<u>Washington</u>\r\n </state />\r\n</place />\r\nseriously attempt to whip a favoured ally into line? In short, if it threatens <u>his</u>\r\n Jewish support, will <u>Bush</u>\r\n come to shove? The Sunday Times, <date month=\"2\" day=\"9\" year=\"2003\" />\r\n9/2/2003</date />\r\n , Tony Allen-Mills</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Both example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41067810 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 35</span>\r\n and <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41067813 n h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 36</span>\r\n , show that the figurative language is clearly part of the cohesive chains established in the text. Neither can be translated because the idioms key to the connection are expressed differently in Czech and do not provide the same similarity to establish an effective link. Examples such as these, again, show the limits of the extent to which adequate translation is possible between the two languages, particularly such translation that maintains not only the meaning of the original but also its cohesive harmony and prosody.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<h3 style=\"MARGIN: 12pt 0cm 3pt 36pt\"><a name=\"_Toc106526254\"></a>\r\n<a name=\"_Toc41104236\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc106526254\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Arial\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\"><font face=\"Arial\">1.2.4</font>\r\n <span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Arial\">Preliminary corpus evidence</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</h3>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; mso-pagination: widow-orphan lines-together\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">A preliminary look at the sample of data seems to confirm the general tendencies predicted (although by no means identifying the specific details of the differences, or precluding alternative conclusions to be reached from a more representative and balanced sample). The pilot corpus consisted approximately of 8000 words (3881 of source language and 4140 of target language). The chart below outlines the structure of the corpus:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<div align=\"center\"><table class=\"MsoTableGrid\" style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-TOP: medium none; BOR