wrap=\"65535\" width=\"77\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-INDENT: 0cm; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: right\" align=\"right\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt\">3659 <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"BORDER-RIGHT: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-RIGHT: 5.4pt; BORDER-TOP: #e0dfe3; PADDING-LEFT: 5.4pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; BORDER-LEFT: #e0dfe3; WIDTH: 48pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; HEIGHT: 13.2pt; BACKGROUND-COLOR: transparent; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt\" valign=\"top\" nowrap=\"65535\" width=\"77\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-INDENT: 0cm; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: right\" align=\"right\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt\">4362 <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n</div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">It is obvious that when measured in words, Czech texts are significantly shorter (16%). There is, however, a marked difference depending on the direction of translation. Czech source texts are 20% shorter than the English translation while the Czech target texts are only 11% shorter. This is consistent with the results of Kr</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">álová </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(1995)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">reported below. Most of the phenomena discussed so far were represented in the sample. This section provides a brief summary of some of the findings.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The general constraints on <span class=\"NormalHeadingText\"><em>third-person pronominal anaphora</em>\r\n </span>\r\nseem to be very similar for both languages. No differences were identified in the sample for intrasentential anaphora and only a handful of differences for intersentential anaphora were found where, as predicted, Czech needed to reinstantiate a noun, possibly because the zero pronoun of its grammatical type was iconically insufficient for a reference across several paragraphs (i.e. the antecedent ceased to be accessible to the only anaphor available [the full pronoun is used for a different type of emphasis]). </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">37.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">The world has been very kind to this <u>book</u>\r\n . The <u>sales</u>\r\n of the English <u>editions</u>\r\n in their various forms have exceeded a million and a half. In Chicago I was assured many years ago, by an enterprising pirate now retired, that the <u>sales</u>\r\n throughout the United States had exceeded a million; and although, in consequence of<b> <u>its</u>\r\n </b>\r\nhaving been published before the Copyright Convention<br />\r\n<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">K této <u>knize</u>\r\n se svět chová velice laskavě. Jen z různých <u>edicí</u>\r\n vydaných v Anglii se u prodalo přes půldruhého miliónu <u>výtisků</u>\r\n . A v Chicagu se mi u před mnoha lety dostalo ujitění - z úst jistého podnikavého piráta nyní v. v. - e víc ne milión <u>výtisků</u>\r\n se prodalo ve Spojených státech; vzhledem k tomu, e tam <b><u>má kniha</u>\r\n </b>\r\nvyla jetě před mezinárodní dohodou o ochraně autorských práv <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Differences consistent with the predictions above were found in the use of <i>ellipsis</i>\r\n , <i>substitution</i>\r\n and <i>reiteration</i>\r\n . The majority of the cases occurred within the phrase, and as in the example below, where English elides both prepositional <i>or </i>\r\nphrases but Czech only the second, it is difficult to determine to what extent ellipsis and reiteration are used as obligatory features in each language: </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">38.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">the power of the federal government to arrest and [] detain citizens without trial or [] access to attorney, to search private homes without warrant or [] notice, to tap telephone and computer communications<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><br />\r\nmonosti federální vlády uvěznit či zadret občany <u>bez</u>\r\n soudu či <u>bez</u>\r\n monosti kontaktovat obhájce, prohledávat soukromé objekty bez varování či [] povolení, odposlouchávat telefony a sledovat elektronickou komunikaci</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">In the example below, the difference in translation may be attributed as much to the difference in the conventionalization of the metonymy BED = SLEEP as to rules governing ellipsis. This sentence also illustrates the greater tendency of Czech not to repeat words within the same sentence.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">39.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Every night before [] bed, I\'ve taken a broom handle and thrust it under my <u>bed</u>\r\n .<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Kadou noc, ne <u>jdu</u>\r\n do postele, pod <u>ní</u>\r\n ouchám násadou od smetáku.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Again, quite predictably, the lexical availability of substitutive items necessitates changes in the translation, which in the example below are combined with paraphrase of the verb (</span>\r\n <i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ospravedlnit  objasnit</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">)<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nto satisfy the no repetition requirement:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">40.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">It remains only to explain the merits justifying such an extraordinary success. I am <u>quite</u>\r\n unable to <u>do so</u>\r\n .<br />\r\n<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Zbývá jetě objasnit, jakými přednostmi se dá <u>ospravedlnit</u>\r\n tak mimořádný úspěch. Já <i><u>sám</u>\r\n </i>\r\n<u>to</u>\r\n <u>objasnit</u>\r\n nedovedu.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The translation of the second sentence further intensifies the connection with </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">sám</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(alone) and by omitting the hedge <i>quite</i>\r\n . This might be considered a perfect example of cohesive prosody discussed below.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Both syntax and lexis were used to express <span class=\"NormalHeadingText\"><em>definiteness</em>\r\n </span>\r\nin Czech as predicted. Also, similarly to the definite article the Czech demonstrative is used both to refer back to previously mentioned items and to introduce new ones. Quite frequently, the English translation uses the demonstrative. In the opposite direction, by far the most frequent translation of both the definite and indefinite articles was zero. Of course, all definite and indefinite items take part in the communicative dynamism of the sentence but the majority of their positions are unmarked. The indefinite article was translated lexically as </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">nějaký </span>\r\n</i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(some) only once in the pilot corpus.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Translating the definite article with the demonstrative is relatively rare in the anaphoric use but more common when used cataphorically. Nevertheless, this represents only a fraction of the total number of occurrences of the definite article:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">41.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Where I want to start telling is <u>the</u>\r\n day I left Pencey Prep.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Chtěl bych vám to začít vypravovat od <u>toho</u>\r\n dne, kdy jsem odjel z přípravky v Pencey.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The most (at least numerically) striking difference was in the use of <span class=\"NormalHeadingText\"><em>connectives</em>\r\n </span>\r\n. Czech, as predicted, used more semantically empty (see discussion of Lev</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ý </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">1965 above</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">connectives than English but it was not the case that every difference in usage included a full connective in Czech and zero in English. A more in-depth investigation of the data will confirm or disconfirm the preliminary hypothesis that Czech uses more connectives to refer to mental space as a whole or its modal (particularly causal) aspects while English prefers to refer particular elements in the space (and its temporal and spatial properties). For example, </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">toti</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">in the sentence below quite obviously refers not only to the preceding discourse but also to the speakers attitude as the translators decision suggests:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">42.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Doktor Vlach si <u>toti</u>\r\n <sub></sub>\r\nrozdělil lidi podle toho, jak se chovají v poloprázdné kavárně .<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"EN-US\">You have to appreciate</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">that Doctor Vlach classifies people according to their behaviour in a half-deserted café</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">As predicted, this need to express modality also works when the Czech translator encounters modalities which are unexpressed in English:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">43.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">It\'s been [] a nervous week. Every night before [] bed, I\'ve taken a broom handle and thrust it under my <u>bed</u>\r\n . Each time, I\'m waiting for <u>the</u>\r\n \"Oof!\" Osama [] is <u>under there</u>\r\n , I <u>just</u>\r\n know <u>it</u>\r\n . <br />\r\n<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">To byl <u>ale</u>\r\n nervózní týden. Kadou noc, ne <u>jdu</u>\r\n do postele, pod <u>ní</u>\r\n ouchám násadou od smetáku. Pokadé jsem čekal [<i>nějaké</i>\r\n ] Au! Je tam <u>toti</u>\r\n Usáma. <u>To</u>\r\n vím <u>jistě</u>\r\n . <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">By using <i>ale</i>\r\n (but), the translator was able to express the speakers attitude as well as exophoric reference to current affairs. In fact, the reverse translation of the first sentence could easily be What a week! This is repeated in the last sentence where </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">toti</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(?therefore) not only expresses the causal relations between the sentences but also intensifies the attitude.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">This attitude is part of the mental space established in the course of the development of discourse. The nature of such a hypothetical space (or the very usefulness of such a notion) will drive a substantial portion of data analysis in reported in chapter 3. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<h2 style=\"MARGIN: 12pt 0cm 3pt 28.8pt\"><a name=\"_Toc106526255\"></a>\r\n<a name=\"_Toc41104237\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc106526255\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Arial\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\"><em><font face=\"Arial\">1.3</font>\r\n </em>\r\n<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><em><font face=\"Arial\">Cohesive harmony and cohesive prosody</font>\r\n </em>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</h2>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; mso-pagination: widow-orphan lines-together\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">In this chapter, I have made references to cohesive harmony and prosody as two concepts that may be important in a contrastive study of cohesion. <i>Cohesive harmony</i>\r\n was introduced by Hasan who describes it as a phenomenon which brings together lexical and grammatical cohesive devices, subjecting them to semantic considerations of identity and similarity. (Hasan 1985, p. 94). In other words, cohesive harmony is the measure of how the cohesive chains interwoven through a text interact to create a coherent meaningful whole. Hasan argues that without such interaction, the cohesive chains alone would not be sufficient to provide texture: we need to include some relations that are characteristic of those between the components of the message. (p. 91) We saw this on a small scale for instance in example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41101652 r h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 34</span>\r\n but this phenomenon was also well-described on larger texts by Hoey (1991, 199X)?? and Martin (1992). Hoey (199X, p. 390) concludes that a cluster of cohesive ties connecting two sentences in a non-narrative text has the effect of relating the messages in a manner not exhaustively accounted for by the ties alone. Hoey, however, remains on the surface of discourse and presents the cluster of cohesive ties as two dimensional without recourse to mental representations. This has the advantage of dealing with what is directly accessible and indisputable but at the same time it fails to capture the full complexity of the cohesive patterns in a text which becomes obvious in a study of texts in translation.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">I suggested that a successful translator will have preserved the cohesive harmony of a text over lexical and grammatical equivalence. This also means that comparing the two languages on a level which is much more difficult to formalize, or to use Hoeys (1991) terms, on the level of patterns rather than on the level of structures. In accordance with this, I propose the concept of <i>cohesive prosody</i>\r\n to describe the conventions of patterning of texts to achieve cohesive harmony. The term <i>prosody </i>\r\nwas originally used in linguistics to describe suprasegmental features of speech and was later used to describe the influence of semantic connotations on the meaning of text. Partington (1998, p. 67) described the connotations of the verb to commit thus: The unfavourable connotation can be seen to reside not simply in the word <i>commit</i>\r\n but over a unit consisting of <i>commit</i>\r\n and its collocate (<i>offences</i>\r\n ,<i> serious crime</i>\r\n ,<i> foul</i>\r\n , etc.). The prosody of a word is said to color the neighboring items so to use Partingtons example the meaning of the phrase good times set in is likely to be interpreted through that coloring rather than as the function of the meanings of its components: </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleUnnumberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 38.55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 9pt; FONT-FAMILY: \" courier=\"\">set in (good times); set in = to be introduced</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Cohesive prosody is similar to semantic prosody in that it is suprasegmental and adds color to a series of connected items that transcends their purely logical relationship. For instance, the use of the word indeed in the example below is an example of cohesive prosody. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">44.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">When they had married, six years before, <u>the difference in their ages</u>\r\n had seemed unimportant. In itself it presented no great <u>obstacle</u>\r\n to fulfillment. Thomas Robertson was still healthy, he could still father children. And <u>whatever had to be endured</u>\r\n in the making, a child was worth it. <b>Indeed</b>\r\n , Tomas was the reward for that nightly <u>sacrifice</u>\r\n she had made of herself on the altar-bed. Tomas was what <u>it</u>\r\n had <u>all</u>\r\n been for, and without him it would have all been for nothing. The man had made the boy and now the man must wither. (Frank Shon)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Indeed does not link any specific items in the cohesive chain formed by the underlined words. In fact, it could be omitted altogether without any outward change in the meaning of the paragraph. However, I would argue that its omission would change the cohesive prosody of the text significantly with an impact on the modality of its mental representation. This will in turn greatly influence the translators decision making process when deciding on the cohesive harmony of the text. Cohesive prosody will become much more important in chapters 3 and 4?? where I will claim that Czech connectives (such as </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">toti</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">) have different prosodic properties from their English counterparts. I will argue that the interaction between the semantics and syntax of connectives can be best described using the ideas from construction grammar </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(Croft, 2001, Croft and Cruse, 2004, Goldberg, 1995)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The notion of cohesive prosody will also provide a link between cohesion and stylistics with which many authors seem to grapple. If we accept that the art of stylistics stems from our ability to find the appropriate form in every instance, and balance the need for cohesive expression with the need of clarity,<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn33\" href=\"#_ftn33\" name=\"_ftnref33\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[33]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(milauer 1972, 303) </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">we can continue to add that the choices we make in the process of selecting stylistically appropriate forms are influenced not only by their register and lexical meaning but also by their prosodic qualities.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<h2 style=\"MARGIN: 12pt 0cm 3pt 28.8pt\"><a name=\"_Toc106526256\"></a>\r\n<a name=\"_Toc41104238\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc106526256\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Arial\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\"><em><font face=\"Arial\">1.4</font>\r\n </em>\r\n<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><em><font face=\"Arial\">Cohesion in translation</font>\r\n </em>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc106526256\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><em><font face=\"Arial\">: transfer hypothesis</font>\r\n </em>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</h2>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">As a bridge between this and the following chapter, I will take a brief look at some of the issues of language in translation. Since most of the data in this study comes directly from translated texts or is at least interpreted with respect to translated texts, it might be worthwhile to reflect on how this affects the nature of the data available and the conclusions drawn from it. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Contrastive analysis should never enter into a description of a language as a primary system of classification. Historically this has stunted the understanding of many languages and this lesson is being repeated today when many linguists base their descriptions of natural language on the needs of the language of computer algorithm. This can be readily seen in the construction of examples in much of the literature on anaphora where questions are posed that would never occur were we not faced with the need for devising an algorithm excluding all possible combinations of items many of which would never occur as a potential problem to a native speaker such as <i>*Who<sub>i</sub>\r\n </i>\r\n<sub></sub>\r\n<i>did his<sub>i</sub>\r\n </i>\r\n<i>wife kill? </i>\r\n(</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Bílý </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">1981). </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">On the other hand, linguists describing individual languages only do not have access to the same range of concepts that are available as a result of a contrastive study. The study of translated texts can be beneficial particularly when discovering the level of grammaticalization in contrast to conventionalization<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn34\" href=\"#_ftn34\" name=\"_ftnref34\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[34]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n. The differences in cohesion in translated texts were dealt with in some detail by Blum-Kulka (2002 [1986]). Blum-Kulka starts from the premise that the search for coherence is a general principle in discourse interpretation (p. 298) which is in agreement with my claim that good translators seek to preserve cohesive harmony above all else. She goes further to define coherence as a covert potential meaning relationship among parts of a text, made overt by the reader or listener through the process of interpretation (pp. 298  299) in opposition to cohesion which she defines as an overt relationship holding between parts of the text, expressed by language specific markers (p. 299). She provides evidence that the act of translation necessitates shifts in both coherence and cohesion. Some of these shifts are due to stylistic preference but many are due to what Blum-Kulka calls the <i>explicitation hypothesis</i>\r\n :</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalQuote\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 14.2pt 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The process of interpretation performed by the translator on the source text might lead to a TL text which is more redundant than the SL text. (p. 300)<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn35\" href=\"#_ftn35\" name=\"_ftnref35\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[35]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">This hypothesis, however, is only partially consistent with example <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' REF _Ref41101652 r h \'\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\">&#8206;</span>\r\n 34</span>\r\n on page <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' PAGEREF _Ref41101652 h \'\"><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes\">6</span>\r\n </span>\r\nwhere focused and unwavering was explicitated into focused on its objective and prevail into win but the same text contains the reverse process whereby the connotations of mission and challenge are lost to goal and problem respectively. Another piece of evidence put forward by Blum-Kulka viz. that TL texts tend to be longer than SL texts can only be used when comparing languages of similar type. Kr</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">álová </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1995) reports that translations from western languages into Czech tend to be on average 10-15% shorter mainly due to the lack of analytical expressions, a tendency reflected in the pilot corpus (see above)<a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn36\" href=\"#_ftn36\" name=\"_ftnref36\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 11pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[36]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n. This does not invalidate the explicitation hypothesis completely if we reformulate it as <i>redundancy-shift hypothesis</i>\r\n :</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalQuote\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 14.2pt 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The process of interpretation performed by the translator on the source text <i>is likely to </i>\r\nlead to a TL text with <i>a different level of redundancy</i>\r\n than the SL text.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">This weakens the hypothesis with respect to its predictive value as to the direction of change but at the same time it makes it stronger with respect to the likelihood of such a change occurring in translation. It also becomes fully consistent with examples such as those quoted by Pechar (1986, p. 11) where the actual translation combining metonymy [M] and ellipsis [E] in (b) contrasts with the literal translation in&nbsp;(a).</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">45.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">He opened a desk drawer, took out cigarettes and offered them to Christine.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo10\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(a)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Vytáhl zásuvku ze stolu, vyndal cigarety a nabídl je Kristýně.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo10\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(b)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Vytáhl ze [M] stolu cigarety a nabídl [E] Kristýně. (</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">He pulled cigarettes out of [M] his desk and offered [E] Christina).</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Reporting on work from Russian translation studies by Komissarov, Pechar talks about a <i>conceptual equivalence</i>\r\n where the conceptual basis is preserved but the semantic organization of the utterance is changed and notes the bidirectionality of this phenomenon with the following example where only one, more explicit translation is possible.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">46.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">I saw there was a question asked.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Viděl jsem, e byl vznesen dotaz. (I </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">saw <i>that</i>\r\n a question was asked.)</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Similar points have been made by Le</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">vý </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(1965, p. 98) with respect to the translators effort to preserve artistic impact. </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Levý</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">talks about <i>intellectualization</i>\r\n </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(intelektualizace)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">which he classifies into three types: 1) making the text more logical (</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">zlogičování textu), </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">2) making the implicit explicit (</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">vykládání nedořečeného)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">, and 3) formal expression of syntactic relations (</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">formální vyjadřování syntaktických vztahů). </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">He uses the following example from the Forsyth saga to illustrate type 3 but it also exemplifies types 1 and 2:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><a name=\"_Ref41275301\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">47.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Aunt Hester, pass<u>ing</u>\r\n through the little, dark hall (she was rather short-sighted), had tried to shoo it off a chair, taking it for a strange, disreputable cat  <u>Tommy</u>\r\n had such disgraceful friends.</span>\r\n </a>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Ref41275301\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo17\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(a)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"CS\">Kdy</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">procházela teta Hester, <u>která</u>\r\n byla trochu krátkozraká, tmavou předsíní, chtěla to zahnat se idle, <u>poněvad</u>\r\n to měla za cizí, neslunou kočku. <u>Tommy</u>\r\n udruje takové kompromitující známosti.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo17\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(b)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">Teta Hester to chtěla cestou skrz erou předsíňku sehnat se idle. Byla trochu krátkozraká a myslila, e je to nějaká cizí kočka, poběhlice  <u>jejich kocour</u>\r\n míval velijaká ostudná přátelství.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleSub-numberedChar\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 72pt; mso-list: l9 level4 lfo17\"><span lang=\"CS\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">(c)<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"CS\">Jak</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\">la teta Hester setmělou předsíní, chtěla to sehnat ze idle. Byla trochu krátkozraká <u>a tak</u>\r\n si myslila, e to bude nějaká toulavá kočka. <u>Kdy</u>\r\n ten <u>jejich macek</u>\r\n míval takové podivné známosti.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Levý </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">points out that while neither translation is grammatically or stylistically incorrect in the target language, only (c) preserves the impact of the original while keeping to a natural flow. He calls the oversaturation of the text with logical dependencies (</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">přesycování slohu logickými závislostmi) as </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">exemplified in </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(a) </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">typical of the language of translation while complaining that (b) overcorrects for this by keeping strictly to the asyndetic approach of the original. The translation in (c) wins </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Levý</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">s approval by making the logical relations between the three propositions explicit and at the same time imbuing them with affective meaning, which I have called modality, thus making the text flow well. In my pilot corpus, I even found an example where the hedge </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">přesně</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(precisely)</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">was inserted in the translation to intensify the connection established by the anaphoric noun <i>fashion</i>\r\n  </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">způsob</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">. <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">48.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">The PATRIOT Act was passed in such a fashion.<br />\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Patriot Act proel <u>přesně</u>\r\n tímto způsobem.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">This further supports the modified version of Blum-Kulkas hypothesis since each translation results in a different level of redundancy with respect to the original.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">As Blum-Kulka points out, the shifts of cohesion when defined as overt processes observable in the text are open to statistical analysis and therefore the above hypotheses can be tested. However, since, for us, the distinction between cohesion and coherence is one of a scale rather than of complementary distribution, it is not immediately obvious what the quantitative data actually means. We must also ask ourselves what are the sources of these shifts.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Blum-Kulka (p. 313, her emphasis) suggests three possible outcomes of a large-scale study exploring shifts in translation:</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo11\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">1.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">cohesive patterns in TL texts tend to <i>approximate</i>\r\n the norms of TL texts of the same register</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo11\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">2.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">cohesive patterns in TL texts tend to reflect norms of SL texts in the same register, which may be due to processes of <i>transfer</i>\r\n operating on the translation</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"ListNumbered1\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt 50.15pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo11\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">3.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">cohesive patterns in TL texts are neither TL nor SL norms oriented, but form a system of their own, possibly indicating a process of explicitation.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">She notes that type of outcome also depends on the typological properties of the two languages involved in the process of translation. The differences discussed above indicate that different results may obtain not only for different types of languages but also for different types of cohesive devices. This allows us to formulate a language-specific <i>cohesive transfer hypothesis </i>\r\nfor Czech and English as follows.<i> <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalQuote\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 14.2pt 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Translations from Czech into English use more connectives than naturally occurring English texts while the reverse holds for Czech translations from English. Therefore the source language imposes its connective cohesive structure on the target language which is in contrast to other aspects of the same text such as cohesive correference or manner-salience in verbs (cf. Slobin 2003) where the target language largely dominates. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a name=\"_Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">I will investigate this hypothesis further in the following chapter. If this hypothesis is correct, we can consider it a strong possibility that the differences in cohesive patterns are due to the differences in how the two languages conceptualize text and how they construct mental representations. The implications of this will be considered in the final chapter.</span>\r\n </a>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNewSection\" style=\"MARGIN: 18pt 0cm 0pt\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\">Final caveat on translation accuracy:</span>\r\n </b>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">Even professional translations make errors but, even more frequently, what some would consider a mistranslation (factual, functional or stylistic) is perfectly acceptable for others. </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"ExampleNumberedBefore0pt\" style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt 55pt\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\">49.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<u><span lang=\"EN-US\">It</span>\r\n </u>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">happens like clockwork these days: A <u>significant</u>\r\n <u>piece</u>\r\n of legislation comes before Congress that was ostensibly drafted to help defend the nation against terrorism.<br />\r\n<br />\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><u><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Ve</span>\r\n </u>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">v těchto dnech lape jako hodinky: Do Kongresu dorazila <u>podstatná</u>\r\n <u>část</u>\r\n zákonů údajně navrených na pomoc obraně národa před terorismem.</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">In this example, the translator made two changes. The first is a case of specifitation. The neutral <i>it</i>\r\n or the original was replaced by the exophoric </span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ve</span>\r\n </i>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">(everything). Because Czech, expresses the impersonal subject by a zero pronoun, there was no other choice but commit to a reference for <i>it</i>\r\n and </span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ve</span>\r\n </i>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">is probably much less of a departure than something like </span>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">legislativní proces.</span>\r\n </i>\r\n</span>\r\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">The second change, however, is clearly a mistranslation and it is only by coincidence that the overall sense of the sentence is not affected. The translator mistook <i>piece of legislation </i>\r\nfor <i>part of the legislation</i>\r\n , thus effectively saying in Czech: <i>A significant portion of the laws allegedly proposed to defend the nation against terrorism arrived in Congress.</i>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Toc41104239\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Similar examples occur quite regularly even in published and edited translations and translations not intended for publication (such as promotional materials) are teeming with the most egregious errors due more often than not to incompetence rather than a lack of concentration as in the example above. Any parallel corpus consisting of translated texts will contain a significant number of such problems no matter what the source of the samples. This needs to be taken into account particularly when discussing quantitative data especially in light of the <i>cohesive transfer hypothesis</i>\r\n above. </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<h1 style=\"MARGIN: 12pt 0cm 3pt 21.6pt\"><a name=\"_Toc106526257\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Arial\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore\"><font face=\"Arial\" size=\"5\">2</font>\r\n <span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" times=\"\" new=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n<font face=\"Arial\" size=\"5\">Bibliography</font>\r\n </a>\r\n</h1>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Ariel, M. 1990. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Accessing noun-phrase antecedents</i>\r\n . London: Routledge.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Bečka, J. V. 1948. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Úvod do české stylistiky</i>\r\n : Kninice \"Kruhu přátel českého jazyka\"; sv.6. Praha: R. Mikuta.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Bilý, Milan. 1981. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Intrasentential pronominalization and functional sentence perspective (in Czech, Russian, and English)</i>\r\n : Lund Slavonic monographs; 1. Lund: Slaviska institutionen.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Croft, William. 2001. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Radical construction grammar: Syntactic theory in typological perspective</i>\r\n : Oxford Linguistics. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Croft, William, and Cruse, D. A. 2004. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Cognitive linguistics</i>\r\n : Cambridge textbooks in linguistics. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Dane, Frantiek. 1985. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Věta a text: studie ze syntaxe spisovné čestiny</i>\r\n : Studie a práce lingvistické; 21. Praha: Academia.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Emmott, Catherine. 1997. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Narrative comprehension: a discourse perspective</i>\r\n . Oxford: Clarendon Press; Oxford University Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Francis, Gill. 1986. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Anaphoric nouns</i>\r\n . Birmingham: English Language Research.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Goldberg, Adele E. 1995. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Constructions: A construction grammar approach to argument structure</i>\r\n : Cognitive Theory of Language and Culture. Chicago: University of Chicago Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Gutwinski. 1976. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Cohesion in literary texts: a study of some grammatical and lexical features of English discourse</i>\r\n : Janua linguarum. Series minor; nr.194. The Hague: Mouton.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Hajičová, Eva. 1975. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Negace a presupozice ve významové stavbě věty</i>\r\n : Studie a práce lingvistické; 11. Praha: Academia.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Hajičová, Eva. 1993. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Issues of Sentence Structure and Dicourse Patterns</i>\r\n : Theoretical and computational linguistics. Prague: Charles University.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Halliday, Michael Alexander Kirkwood, and Hasan, Ruqaiya. 1976. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Cohesion in English</i>\r\n . London: Longman.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Halliday, Michael Alexander Kirkwood, and Hasan, Ruqaiya. 1985. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Language, context, and text: aspects of language in a social-semiotic perspective</i>\r\n . Victoria: Deakin University Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Halliday, Michael Alexander Kirkwood. 1994. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">An introduction to functional grammar</i>\r\n . London: E. Arnold.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Hoey, Michael. 1991. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Patterns of lexis in text</i>\r\n : Describing English language. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Hrbáček, Josef. 1994. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Nárys textové syntaxe spisovné četiny</i>\r\n . Praha: Trizonia.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Hurford, James R., Knight, Chris, and Studdert-Kennedy, Michael eds. 1998. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Approaches to the evolution of language: social and cognitive bases</i>\r\n . Cambridge, UK; New York: Cambridge University Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Johansson, Stig, and Hofland, Knut. 1989. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Frequency analysis of English vocabulary and grammar: based on the LOB Corpus</i>\r\n . Oxford: Clarendon Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Kesselová, Jana. 2002. Spojky ako indikátor mylienkovych operácií dieaa mladieho kolského veku.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Klégr, Ales. 1996. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The noun in translation: a Czech-English contrastive study</i>\r\n : Acta Universitatis Carolinae. Philologica. Monographia; 127-1995. Praha: Univerzita Karlova.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Králová, Jana. 1995. Textová koheze v překladových textech. </span>\r\n<span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\">In <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Spisovná četina a jazyková kultura 1993: Sborník z olomoucké konference 23. - 27. </i>\r\n</span>\r\n<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">8. 1993</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">, 220-223. Praha: Filozofická fakulta Univerzity Karlovy.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Krámský, Jirí. 1972. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The article and the concept of definiteness in language</i>\r\n : Janua linguarum. Series minor; 125. The Hague: Mouton.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Levý, Jiří. 1963. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Umění překladu</i>\r\n : Edice dílna; sv.10. Praha: Československý spisovatel.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Martin, J. R. 1992. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">English text: system and structure</i>\r\n . Philadelphia; Amsterdam: John Benjamins Pub. Co.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Mistrík, Jozef. 1973. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Stylistika slovenského jazyka</i>\r\n . Bratislava: SPN.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Pačesová, Jaroslava. 1979. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Řeč v raném dětství</i>\r\n : Spisy Univerzity J.E. Purkyně v Brně, Filozofická fakulta; c. 219. Brno: Univerzita J. E. Purkyně.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Palek, Bohumil. 1988. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Referenční výstavba textu</i>\r\n . Praha: Univerzita Karlova.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Palmer, F. R. 1990. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Modality and the English modals</i>\r\n : Longman linguistics library. London; New York: Longman.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Partington, Alan. 1998. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Patterns and meanings: using corpora for English language research and teaching</i>\r\n : Studies in corpus linguistics; 2. Amsterdam: J. Benjamins.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Pechar, Jirí. 1986. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Otázky literárního překladu</i>\r\n . Praha: Československý spisovatel.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Pintarová, Magda, and Bednářová, Ivana. 1998. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Communicative Czech</i>\r\n . Prague.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Slezáková, Markéta. </span>\r\n<span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\">1999. Role ukazovacích zájmen <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">ten, ta, to</i>\r\n v mluveném dialogickém textu. In <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Komunikační a strukturní aspekty četiny a jiných jazyků</i>\r\n . Praha: Ústav českého jazyka a teorie komunikace FF UK. <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"PL\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\">Teitelová, Marie. </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">1974. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Otázky lexikální statistiky</i>\r\n . Praha: Academia.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Teitelová, Marie. 1987. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">O četině v číslech</i>\r\n : Malá jazyková kniznice; sv. 4. Praha: Academia.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Wales, Katie. 1996. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Personal pronouns in present-day English</i>\r\n : Studies in English language series. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; TEXT-INDENT: 0cm; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt 36pt; TEXT-INDENT: -36pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<div style=\"mso-element: footnote-list\"><br clear=\"all\" />\r\n<hr align=\"left\" width=\"33%\" size=\"1\" />\r\n<div id=\"ftn1\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn1\" href=\"#_ftnref1\" name=\"_ftn1\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[1]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">To my knowledge, there is no cross-linguistic evidence to either prove or disprove this claim. However, psycholinguistic evidence suggests that speakers seek to establish cohesive relations even where there are none (see footnote <span style=\"mso-field-code: \' NOTEREF _Ref40572725 h \'\">9</span>\r\n ).</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn2\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn2\" href=\"#_ftnref2\" name=\"_ftn2\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[2]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">This approach is also hinted at by Quirk et al. (1985, p. 1423) in their <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Comprehensive Grammar of the English Language</i>\r\n . The authors approach the final chapter From sentence to Text as a reinterpretation of grammatical processes the description of which takes up the bulk of the volume from the perspective of their role in both the interpretation of a text and in the construction of a text.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn3\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn3\" href=\"#_ftnref3\" name=\"_ftn3\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[3]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">In fact, Langacker (2001) makes precisely this argument, except, as an exponent of Cognitive Grammar relying on constructions, he does not use the concept of sentence structure in the traditional constituent-or-dependency-tree sense.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn4\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn4\" href=\"#_ftnref4\" name=\"_ftn4\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[4]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">It may be worthwhile to remind ourselves of Crofts (2001) claim that using the same label for similar phenomena in different languages, such as the zero pronoun in Czech and English, should be more a matter of convenience than an assumption of some deep-rooted similarity. </font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn5\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn5\" href=\"#_ftnref5\" name=\"_ftn5\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[5]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">This explain why David Dimbleby defending his position against a questioner on BBCs Question Time (25. 10. 2002) could point to sir Malcolm Rifkind and say He said that, <sn />\r\nnot</sn />\r\n <sn />\r\nI.</sn />\r\n  without causing offence but Princess Diana referring in such a way to Prince Charles in an example quoted by <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nWales</country-region />\r\n </place />\r\nwill be interpreted as animosity. Either case would be seen as a sign of extreme discourtesy if not insult in <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nCzech.</country-region />\r\n </place />\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn6\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn6\" href=\"#_ftnref6\" name=\"_ftn6\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[6]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">This is reflected in Halliday and Hasans (1976) treatment of what they call conjunctive relations which they divide into <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">additive, adversative, causal </i>\r\nand <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">temporal</i>\r\n (p. 242-3) although their illocutionary properties are also taken into account (as in <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">expository additives</i>\r\n )<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">. </i>\r\nIt is important to note, however, that they consider conjunction one of the marginal cohesive relations: Conjunctive elements are cohesive not in themselves but indirectly, by virtue of their specific meanings; they are not primarily devices for reaching out into the preceding (or following) text, but they express certain meanings which presuppose the presence of other components in the discourse. </font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn7\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn7\" href=\"#_ftnref7\" name=\"_ftn7\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[7]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Blum-Kulka (2000 [1986], p. 300) reports on a finding by Stemmer analyzing data from German students of English with the conclusion that conjunctions were markedly overrepresented in the learner data.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn8\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn8\" href=\"#_ftnref8\" name=\"_ftn8\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[8]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Strong echoes of Halliday and Hasan can also be found in the Comprehensive Grammar (Quirk at al. 1985) although its concept of cohesion is even broader since the authors aim is to basically reinterpret the grammatical features of English as cohesive devices (see footnote 1).</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn9\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn9\" href=\"#_ftnref9\" name=\"_ftn9\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[9]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Kehler (2002) gives these examples from <city />\r\nHobbs</city />\r\n : 1) John took a train from <place />\r\n<city />\r\nParis</city />\r\n </place />\r\nto Istambul. He has family there. and 2) ? John took a train from <place />\r\n<city />\r\nParis</city />\r\n </place />\r\nto Istambul. He likes spinach. Despite the fact that the texture of 2 is decidedly weaker, if it were to be accepted as a text, speakers must construct a context where the asyndetic connection will become meaningful, i.e. establish a cohesive link. One such possible context, is the assumption of a world where desirable spinach can be found in <place />\r\n<city />\r\nIstanbul</city />\r\n </place />\r\nand it is a worthwhile cause to justify making such a journey. Hallidays and Hasans (1976) definition of text as a cohesive unit of language (through the concept of texture) is another way of implicitly stating something similar.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn10\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn10\" href=\"#_ftnref10\" name=\"_ftn10\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[10]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">In their original account, Halliday and Hasan (1976, p. 29) defined cohesion basically as a non-structural textual device. What Hasan (1985) now calls <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">non-structural cohesion</i>\r\n was simply <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">cohesion</i>\r\n . This study will assume this new, broader definition of cohesion. </font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn11\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn11\" href=\"#_ftnref11\" name=\"_ftn11\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[11]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Cohesion as a concept has not, however, become as well-established in Czech philology as it is in English. For example, the main Czech philological journal </span>\r\n<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Nae řeč</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">has not </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">published a single article to cohesion in Czech as a phenomenon over the last thirty years and there have been only a handful of articles dealing with specific issues which come under the heading of cohesion.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn12\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn12\" href=\"#_ftnref12\" name=\"_ftn12\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[12]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Slovak is sufficiently typologically and lexically similar to Czech with the two languages being for the most part mutually intelligible to allow for comparisons at this level of analysis. Furthermore, Mistr</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">íks </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">approach was also successfully used on <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nCzech.</country-region />\r\n </place />\r\n</span>\r\n</font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn13\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn13\" href=\"#_ftnref13\" name=\"_ftn13\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[13]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">An explicit formulation of this can be found in </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">milauer (</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">1972, p. 303): Individual parts of suprasentential units, in particular of paragraphs, are connected through essentially the same thought relations as parts of a sentence. (</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Jednotlivé části nadvětných celků, předevím odstavců, spojují se v&nbsp;podstatě tými mylenkovými vztahy jako členy ve větě.) <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</font>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; TEXT-INDENT: 16.45pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">A useful comparison may be made with Quirk at al. (1985) who rather than viewing text as a structure built of sentences claim that a text  unlike a sentence  is not a grammatical unit but rather semantic and even a pragmatic one. A text is a stretch of language which seems appropriately coherent in actual use. That is, the text coheres in its real-world context, semantically and pragmatically, and it is also internally or linguistically coherent. (p. 1423) The authors use the term cohesion to refer to the internal and linguistic coherence of text. </font>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn14\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn14\" href=\"#_ftnref14\" name=\"_ftn14\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[14]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\"><cite>The American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language, 2000</cite>\r\n defines one of the senses of <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">instauration</i>\r\n as The institution or establishment of something.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn15\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn15\" href=\"#_ftnref15\" name=\"_ftn15\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[15]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">I am using</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">the</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">authors own</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">translations from the English summary even where a different translation might be appropriate.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn16\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn16\" href=\"#_ftnref16\" name=\"_ftn16\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[16]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Similar notions might be identified both in Paleks (1984) denotation and Haji</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">čová</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">s </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">(</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">1993) stock of shared knowledge which are however less amenable to be used for establishing and resolving cohesive relations.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn17\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn17\" href=\"#_ftnref17\" name=\"_ftn17\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[17]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The use of </span>\r\n<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ten</span>\r\n </i>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">as an anaphoric rather than deictic pronoun is present in other Slavonic languages, for instance in Russian.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn18\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn18\" href=\"#_ftnref18\" name=\"_ftn18\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[18]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Examples 4 and 6, seem to bring into question or at least seriously weaken the assertion made by Sanford and Garrod that (1980, p. 145) [p]ronouns can never be used to identify implied entities in the extended domain of reference. While this seems to hold for most sentences such as that given by Sanford and Garrod Mary dressed the baby. <i>They</i>\r\n (vs. the clothes) were made of pink wool.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial\">, </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">there are clearly instances where such reference is possible. Indeed, both languages under investigations seem to possess various devices for the disambiguation of such examples (see below) which in some instances appear not to be difficult to translate (see Czech translation of <i>Three men in a boat</i>\r\n ). It would not be surprising if the existence of devices with similar function turned out to be a language universal.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn19\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn19\" href=\"#_ftnref19\" name=\"_ftn19\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[19]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">This seems to be, at least in come cases, an issue of the convention of usage. The well-known difference between American and British English in referring to institutions by third-person singular or plural respectively. For instance, American English refers to <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">police</i>\r\n strictly as <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">it</i>\r\n while British English prefers <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">they</i>\r\n ; additionally, many borderline cases can be identified. This seems to be a productive difference, for instance, <i>Al Quaida</i>\r\n can be referred to as <i>they</i>\r\n in British English and not in American.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn20\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn20\" href=\"#_ftnref20\" name=\"_ftn20\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[20]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Unfortunately, I am not aware of any statistical analysis of English similar in depth and scope to that of </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Těitelová</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\">s work on <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nCzech<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">.</span>\r\n </country-region />\r\n</place />\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\"></span>\r\nIt is interesting, if perhaps not surprising, how the local linguistic tradition can influence </span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">not </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">only the interpretation of language data but also its availability.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn21\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn21\" href=\"#_ftnref21\" name=\"_ftn21\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[21]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Du Bois (1980, p. 207) mentions that 34% of 613 noun phrases in his spoken elicited corpus occurred as the initial mention of a particular character or object and that a substantial number of noninitial mentions are formally indefinite. A similar point is made by Epstein (2002, p. 333) who suggest that the notion of the accessibility of a discourse referent should replace that of familiarity or unique identifiability as a basis for describing the conditions for the use of the definite article in English.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn22\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn22\" href=\"#_ftnref22\" name=\"_ftn22\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[22]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Těitelová et al. (1987, </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">p. 26) reports that in spoken Czech texts </span>\r\n<i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ten </span>\r\n</i>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">becomes the most frequent word.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn23\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn23\" href=\"#_ftnref23\" name=\"_ftn23\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[23]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">For instance, Romanian will use the definite article in generic sentences such as I like coffee.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn24\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn24\" href=\"#_ftnref24\" name=\"_ftn24\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[24]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">For instance, one of the key handbooks of Czech syntax (Grepl and Karl</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ík</span>\r\n <span style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-GB\">1986) only devotes two pages to ellipsis focusing mostly on dialogic and aberrant examples. The importance of ellipsis was underscored by Haji</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">č (2002) </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">who points out that taggers working on the Prague Dependency Treebank found ellipsis to be by far the most common grammatical relation.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">This, however, also includes zero pronominal subject and ellided grammatical words in coordinated phrases (e.g. without me and John). <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</font>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn25\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn25\" href=\"#_ftnref25\" name=\"_ftn25\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[25]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Some aspects of substitution, namely repetition, were extensively studied by Hoey (1991). I will refer to his monograph when dealing with lexical cohesion.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn26\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn26\" href=\"#_ftnref26\" name=\"_ftn26\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[26]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Which and that make it to the top 20 in English, but T</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ěitelová at al. </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">stop at ten precluding further comparisons. It should be noted, however, that this only has illustrative value and can hardly be considered conclusive.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn27\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn27\" href=\"#_ftnref27\" name=\"_ftn27\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[27]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Kathryn Murphy (personal communication) points out that Kundera stands out from among the majority of Czech writers by his common use of the semi-colon. Problems with Kunderas language and its translation have been discussed in some detail by Wood (2001)?? who points out that much of Kunderas writing in exile was done by his own admission with the translator in mind. This might be particularly reflected in the larger number of asyndetic connections in his text. However, his texts still need to conform to basic cohesive principles of Czech and it would be a subject for an independent study to discover which were preserved.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn28\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn28\" href=\"#_ftnref28\" name=\"_ftn28\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[28]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Similar explanation of certain uses of connectives was given by Gutwinsky (1976, p. 157) who also talks about the scope of connectors noting for instance that <i>but</i>\r\n and <i>however</i>\r\n denote essentially the same connection but differ in scope. I will argue that this difference is at least partially due to their modality.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn29\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn29\" href=\"#_ftnref29\" name=\"_ftn29\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[29]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">This can be found as far back as Fowlers 1908 Kings English (http://www.bartleby.com/ 116/ 302.html). One critic of the excessive use of elegant variation in journalism likened the use of words such as <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">missive</i>\r\n for <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">letter</i>\r\n to a pronoun saying that journalists appear to have more pronouns at their disposal than normal speakers.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn30\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn30\" href=\"#_ftnref30\" name=\"_ftn30\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[30]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Cohesion of legal texts is a world unto itself but again, it is English, where it seeped into popular consciousness as can be seen from the ready hypostasis of certain items. Therefore, a sentence of a type You just need to learn your henceforths and forthwiths and youll be a proper lawyer commonly found in Wodehouses writings makes perfect sense in English but is impossible in <place />\r\n<country-region />\r\nCzech.</country-region />\r\n </place />\r\nBy the same token, the metonymy in the Czech sentence: </span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Pozor na paragrafy.</span>\r\n <span lang=\"EN-US\"> (Be careful of the law!, lit. Watch out for sections or laws/clauses!) is impossible to translate using the same figure of speech.</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn31\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn31\" href=\"#_ftnref31\" name=\"_ftn31\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[31]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">This is by no means to imply that discourse in Czech is impoverished but it does mean that the range of self-reflective cohesive devices available to translators working with English as the source language will be limited. Original Czech texts will of course not suffer from this since they are the expression of native conceptualization.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn32\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn32\" href=\"#_ftnref32\" name=\"_ftn32\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[32]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Some of these differences were comprehensively described by Pechar (1986) who also points out that many of these disparities are cultural and subject to change even within one language.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn33\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn33\" href=\"#_ftnref33\" name=\"_ftn33\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[33]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">Stylistické umění záleí právě v&nbsp;tom, abychom v&nbsp;kadém jednotlivém případě nali vhodnou formu a správně vyváili potřebu sevřeného vyjadřování s&nbsp;potřebou jasnosti a přehlednosti. <p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</font>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn34\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn34\" href=\"#_ftnref34\" name=\"_ftn34\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[34]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Im aware that for many authors since Labov (but cf. also Jespersen) these two terms are virtually interchangeable as they may be but for the purposes of comparison I am using them as separate concepts (see the discussion of sources of interlingual differences </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"></span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-field-code: \' PAGEREF sources_of_difference p h \'\"><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes\">on page 3</span>\r\n </span>\r\n).</span>\r\n </font>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn35\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn35\" href=\"#_ftnref35\" name=\"_ftn35\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[35]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">The hypothesis can be expanded to account for both cohesion and coherence using Blum-Kulkas own statement: Contrary to natural discourse, translation is a process by which what is <i>said</i>\r\n [cohesion] might become obvious and clear, while what is <i>meant</i>\r\n [coherence] might become vague and obscure. (p. 312, authors emphasis, my inserts in []).</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div id=\"ftn36\" style=\"mso-element: footnote\"><p class=\"MsoFootnoteText\" style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><a title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn36\" href=\"#_ftnref36\" name=\"_ftn36\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><span class=\"MsoFootnoteReference\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 150%; FONT-FAMILY: \" times=\"\" new=\"\">[36]</span>\r\n </span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</span>\r\n</a>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"2\">Although, should the tendency for the difference in text length to be significantly smaller when Czech is the target rather than the source language, observed in the pilot corpus, be confirmed on a larger scale, this would support Blum-Kulkas original hypothesis but also be consistent with the modified formulation.</font>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n', '2003-06-08 16:04:17', 7572, 14, 30);
: George Eliot and Prague', 'Susan Reynolds', 'Winning entry in the 2002 <a href=http://www.bcsa.co.uk>BCSA</a> essay competition.', '<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\" /><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">By Susan Reynolds</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</SPAN>It was in July 1858, a decade after the year of revolutions, that the novelist George Eliot (Marian Evans) and her partner George Henry Lewes undertook a journey that led from <st1:City><st1:place>London</st1:place></st1:City> to <st1:City><st1:place>Dresden</st1:place></st1:City> by way of <st1:City><st1:place>Munich</st1:place></st1:City>. Preoccupied with German culture, Eliot was eager to explore the delights of the Saxon capital, and wrote rapturously in her letters and diaries of the art treasures that she saw there. On the way, however, they travelled through <st1:City><st1:place>Vienna</st1:place></st1:City>, and spent a day and a night in <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City>, their first visit to the city. Immediately after breakfast, as Eliot explains in her journal, they went out to see as much of the grand old city as possible in one day.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</SPAN>The most interesting things we saw were the Jewish burial-ground (the alter Friedhof) and the old Synagogue. The Friedhof is unique  with a wild growth of grass and shrubs and trees and a multitude of quaint tombs in all sorts of positions looking like the fragments of a great building, or as if they had been shaken by an earthquake. We saw a lovely dark eyed Jewish child here, which we were glad to kiss in all its dirt. Then came the sombre old synagogue with its smoked groins, and lamp for ever burning. An intelligent Jew was our cicerone and read us some Hebrew out of the precious old book of the Law.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">After dinner we took a carriage and went across the wonderful <st1:place><st1:PlaceType>bridge</st1:PlaceType> of <st1:PlaceName>St. Jean Nepomuck</st1:PlaceName></st1:place> with its avenue of statues, towards the Radschin  an ugly straight-lined building but grand in effect from its magnificent site, on the summit of an eminence crowded with old massive buildings. The view from this eminence is one of the most impressive in the world  perhaps as much from ones associations with <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City> as from its visible grandeur and antiquity. The Cathedral close to the Radschin is a melancholy object on the outside  left with unfinished sides like scars. The interior is rich, but sadly confused in its ornamentation, like so many of the grand old churches  hideous altars of bastard style disgracing exquisite Gothic columns. () We got our view from a <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Damen Stift</I> (for ladies of family) founded by Maria Theresa, whose blond beauty looked down on us from a striking portrait. Close in front of us sloping downwards was a pleasant orchard; then came the river with its long, long bridge and grand gateway; then the sober-coloured city with its surrounding plain and distant hills.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</SPAN>In the evening we went to the theatre  a shabby ugly building and heard Spohrs Jessonda.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Despite her cool appraisal of what she perceived as the imperfections of some of <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City>s buildings, Eliot was obviously impressed with the beauty of its setting and the nobility and sense of history that she perceived in the city. Lewes was equally enthusiastic; in a letter to John Chapman of <st1:date Month=\"7\" Day=\"23\" Year=\"1858\">23<SUP>rd</SUP> July 1858</st1:date>, he called <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City> the most splendid city in <st1:country-region><st1:place>Germany</st1:place></st1:country-region>  a glowing if inaccurate description. Their journey had taken them through <st1:City><st1:place>Salzburg</st1:place></st1:City>, Ischl, <st1:City><st1:place>Linz</st1:place></st1:City> and <st1:City><st1:place>Vienna</st1:place></st1:City>, and unpardonable as it might seem to a modern reader, Lewes was simply including <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City> in a broad application of <st1:country-region><st1:place>Germany</st1:place></st1:country-region> which enveloped not only <st1:State><st1:place>Bohemia</st1:place></st1:State> but the Habsburg empire in general. Had either of them had any real awareness of the developing consciousness of Czech nationhood or Czech literature, he might not have made this mistake. This was, after all, just five years after the publication of Erbens <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Kytice</I> (1853) and three since that of Boena N&#277;mcovás <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Babička. </I>Karel Havlíček Borovský had died two years before, and the 1850s were a reactionary period in which journalism stagnated, as not only political and educational periodicals but literary ones were suppressed. But Jan Neruda had just brought out his <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Hřbitovní kvítí</I> (Graveyard Flowers) the previous year, and Vítězslav Hálek was working on his <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Večerní písně </I>(Evening Songs; 1858-59), similarly influenced by Heine. Within two years the revival of constitutional life in the Austrian empire would bring new vigour to Czech literary culture and allow the generation of young writers who took their name from the almanac <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Máj </I>to make their voices heard. None of this, however, can be detected in Eliots impressions of <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City>. Within the next decade, too, the theatre which she stigmatized as shabby and ugly (the Královské stavovské divadlo or Royal Estates Theatre, renamed in 1949 after Josef Tyl, who managed it from 1846 to 1851) would see the first performances of Smetanas operas instead of the now almost forgotten works of Spohr (<I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Jessonda</I> dated from 1823).<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Eliot, however, could know nothing of this. There is no indication that she had any contacts in Prague, unlike in Munich, where she met David Friedrich Strauss, whose <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Das Leben Jesu</I> she had translated, and Vienna, where she and Lewes visited the anatomist Joseph Hyrtl.<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>The only Czech-born scholar she is known to have met was Dr. Anton Springer, a Bonn-based academic who visited <st1:country-region><st1:place>England</st1:place></st1:country-region> in 1850 with his English friend Robert Noel, brother of her acquaintance Edward. His abandonment of his Bohemian Liberal principles after 1848 had made him unpopular with Czech nationalists.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </SPAN>Cultural links between <st1:country-region><st1:place>Britain</st1:place></st1:country-region> and <st1:State><st1:place>Bohemia</st1:place></st1:State> were relatively undeveloped at that time.<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>In the 1830s and 1840s several pieces of travel writing had appeared: Henry Reeve, editor of the <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Edinburgh Review, </I>had published an account of a visit to Prague in 1836 in the <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Metropolitan Magazine</I>, and three years later parts of it were included in the Rev. George R. Gleigs <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Germany, Bohemia, and Hungary, Visited in 1837</I>. In 1840 Peter Evan Turnbull brought out <st1:country-region><st1:place><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Austria</I></st1:place></st1:country-region>, a two-volume work which, although pro-Habsburg, recognized the continuing vitality of the Czech language.<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>There were also two anthologies which, had Eliot known of them, would have provided an introduction to Czech literature. In the same week in July 1858 when she and Lewes were in <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City>, John Bowring (1792-1872) was travelling through <st1:place>Pest</st1:place> and <st1:City><st1:place>Vienna</st1:place></st1:City> on the way home from government business in <st1:country-region><st1:place>Turkey</st1:place></st1:country-region>. Better known as a diplomat and politician, Bowring had developed an interest in Eastern European literature, corresponded with the poet Čelakovský and scholars afářik, Dobrovský and Kopitar, and in 1832 published <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Wýbor z Básnictwi Českého. Cheskian Anthology: Being a History of the Poetical Literature of </I><st1:State><st1:place><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Bohemia</I></st1:place></st1:State><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">, with Translated Specimens.</I> Čelakovský in particular was disappointed with this compilation, and in 1828 Bowring had aroused controversy with his article Bohemian Literature in the <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Foreign Quarterly Review</I>, which cast aspersions on Dobrovský and contained potentially compromising anti-Habsburg material. Bowrings translations appeared in Hankas 1843 edition of the <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Rukopis Královédvorský</I>. In the 1852 <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Polyglotta </I>edition they were replaced by versions by the Rev. Albert Henry Wratislaw (1821-92), Fellow of Christs College, <st1:City><st1:place>Cambridge</st1:place></st1:City>, and subsequently Headmaster of Felsted School, <st1:place>Essex</st1:place>.<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>In 1849 Wratislaw, whose knowledge of Czech was much greater than Bowrings, published <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Lyra Czecho-Slovanska</I>, a collection of Bohemian poems, ancient and modern, translated from the original Slavonic with an introductory essay, but there is no evidence in Eliots journals or letters that she had read either of these anthologies.<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </SPAN>There was simply no time, either, for her to make more than a superficial acquaintance with <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City> itself. </P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>However, the short time she spent there left so strong an impression on her that in the following year, 1859, she published a story, <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The Lifted Veil</I>, which incorporated her journal entry almost word for word. The only fiction which she ever wrote in the first person, it tells of a young man, Latimer, whose dubious gift of prevision causes him repeatedly to relive the moment of his approaching death. One of the earlier instances of this occurs when his father promises him a trip to the <st1:place>Tyrol</st1:place> and <st1:City><st1:place>Vienna</st1:place></st1:City>, returning through <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City>. The name induces in Latimer a vision of a city unrefreshed for ages by the dews of night, or the rushing rain-cloud; scorching the dusty, weary, time-eaten grandeur of a people doomed to live on in the stale repetition of memories, like deposed and superannuated kings in their regal gold-inwoven tatters. The city looked so thirsty that the broad river seemed to me a sheet of metal, and the blackened statues, as I passed under their blank gaze, along the unending bridge, with their ancient garments and their saintly crowns, seemed to me the real inhabitants and owners of this place, while the busy, trivial men and women, hurrying to and fro, were a swarm of ephemeral visitants infesting it for a day. Although he has never seen a picture of <st1:City><st1:place>Prague</st1:place></st1:City>, Latimers vision is minutely detailed, even including a patch of rainbow light on the pavement, transmitted through a coloured lamp in the shape of a star. When he reaches Prague, (like Eliot, en route to Dresden), and prepares to visit the Jewish quarter on a hot, dry August day, he hopes that a late start may prevent the party reaching the places which he had seen in his vision. But, as I stood under the blackened, groined arches of that old synagogue, made dimly visible by the seven thin candles in the sacred lamp, while our Jewish cicerone reached down the Book of the Law and read to us in its ancient tongue,-- I felt a shuddering impression that this strange building, with its shrunken lights, this surviving withered remnant of medieval Judaism, was of a piece with my vision. Those darkened, dusty Christian saints, with their loftier arches and their larger candles, needed the consolatory scorn with which they might point to a more shrivelled death-in-life than their own. He feels compelled to walk on under the archway of the grand old gate leading on to the bridge, gripped by chills and trembling, until he finds the very patch of rainbow light on the pavement that he had seen in his vision. This detail marks the beginning of the inexorable process of dissolutiona doomed marriage to Bertha, the fiancée of Latimers dead brother Alfred and the one person whose mind he cannot penetrate, an experiment in resuscitation which leads to a terrible revelation by a dead servant, and the growing curse of insight which leaves Latimer with no illusions about the true thoughts and intentions of others or his own inevitable end.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The Lifted Veil </I>was completed on <st1:date Month=\"4\" Day=\"26\" Year=\"1859\">26<SUP>th</SUP> April 1859</st1:date>, and appeared (anonymously, on Eliots publisher John Blackwoods advice) in the July issue of <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Blackwoods Edinburgh Magazine</I>, exactly a year after the journey which inspired it. In the intervening months Eliot had suffered family discord, the death of her sister Chrissey, and gossip attributing the authorship of her novels <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Scenes from Clerical Life </I>and<I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"> Adam Bede</I> to Joseph Liggins. Not surprisingly, these experiences coloured her view of human nature and even the memories of a city which had impressed her so strongly the previous summer, and are reflected in the brooding atmosphere of the story and the jaundiced outlook of its narrator. For Eliot/Latimer, Prague is a city imprisoned in its past, with no hint of national revival or burgeoning creative vitality, a revolution ten years before or the possibility of independent statehood sixty years later. Fascinated by mesmerism and clairvoyance, Eliot responded to Pragues traditions of mysticism, magic and alchemy, and its rich historical associations, which made it ideal as the setting for the pivotal episode in a story linking scientific experimentation with elements of the supernatural, and the dangers of foreknowledge.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Had she known Erbens <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">tědrý den</I> (Christmas Eve), Eliot might well have chosen its final lines as an epigraph to <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The Lifted Veil</I>:</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Better to dream on in hope mistaken,</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Sheer darkness before us, nothing to see,</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Than uncover the future, and to awaken,</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Knowing its terrible certainty! </P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<H1 style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><FONT size=3>BIBLIOGRAPHY</FONT></H1>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-tab-count: 1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Ashton, Rosemary <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">George Eliot: a life </I>(Hamish Hamilton, London, 1996).</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\">Eliot, George</B> <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Journals</I>, ed. Margaret Harris and Judith Johnston (Cambridge University Press, 1998).</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The George Eliot Letters (vol.2)</I>, ed. Gordon S. Haight (New Haven, Connecticut, 1954-5, 1978).</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The Lifted Veil</I> (ed. Sally Shuttleworth), (Penguin Books, <st1:City><st1:place>London</st1:place></st1:City>, 2001).</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\">Naughton, J. D. </B>The Reception in Nineteenth-Century <st1:country-region><st1:place>England</st1:place></st1:country-region> of Czech Literature and of<SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN>the Czech Literary Revival (unpublished PhD thesis, <st1:City><st1:place>Cambridge</st1:place></st1:City>, 1977).</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"><SPAN lang=PL style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\">Novák, Arne</SPAN></B><SPAN lang=PL style=\"mso-ansi-language: PL\"> <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Czech Literature</I> (tr. </SPAN>Peter Kussi), (Ann Arbor, 1976).<I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"><SPAN style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </SPAN><o:p></o:p></I></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The </I><st1:City><st1:place><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Oxford</I></st1:place></st1:City><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\"> Dictionary of Opera</I>, ed. John Warrack and Ewan West (Oxford University Press, 1994). </P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">The Travellers Literary Companion to Eastern &amp; </I><st1:place><I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Central Europe</I></st1:place> (ed. James Naughton), (Brighton, In Print Publishing Ltd., 1995.</P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\">Š Susan Reynolds 2002</P>', '2003-06-10 20:34:11', 2788, 28, 110);
</p>\r\n</b>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">This category might include regional collections, reference libraries and rare documents. A regional collection makes loans as a rule in cases where the library has a specific title in multiple copies. When a particular regional document is loaned out, a large role is played by an individual assessment of each request. All regional libraries receive regional legal deposit copies. They are the regional centres for inter-library services. </p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\"></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"><p align=\"justify\">V rámci regionálních funkcí má krajská knihovna napomáhat rozvoji základních knihoven a jejich veřejných knihovnických a informačních slueb. Co se týče kategorizace uivatelů - knihoven - v rámci meziknihovních slueb se zatím objevuje jen v náznacích, a to zejména v poplatcích. Knihovnám v rámci kraje jsou v ojedinělých případech poskytovány sluby zdarma. Rozdíly v rozsahu půjčování se neobjevují, zřejmě pouze nae knihovna zaujala ke knihovnám ve Středočeském kraji rozdílný přístup, ve smyslu téměř absolutního zrovnoprávnění absenčních slueb poskytovaných registrovaným uivatelům a registrovaným uivatelům knihoven Středočeského kraje. Projekt je zatím ve zkuebním stadiu. Předpokládám, e jsou i v jiných knihovnách vedeny na toto téma diskuze. Vzhledem k financování knihoven z krajských úřadů (teprve od letoního roku) se moná situace bude teprve vyvíjet a zcela nepochybně budou hrát roli konkrétní podmínky.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"><p align=\"justify\">As part of their regional functions, regional libraries are to assist the development of \'basic\'<a name=\"BMX12\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BM12\"><sup>12</sup>\r\n</a>\r\n libraries and their public library and information services. As for categorizations of users and libraries, within the framework of inter-library services these have hitherto only appeared indirectly in references, particularly as regards fees. Libraries are provided with services for free in individual cases within a particular region. Differences in the extent of lending do not appear. Apparently only our library has adopted a different approach to libraries in the Central Bohemian Region, in the sense of almost absolutely equal rights in loan services provided to our own registered users and registered users of libraries in the Central Bohemian Region. This project is currently in its trial stage and I presume that discussions on this subject are now under way at other libraries. As libraries are now funded from regional authorities (since last year) the situation may only now change and conditions on the ground will doubtless play a role.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"><p align=\"justify\">Jetě pár poznámek k placení za meziknihovní výpůjční sluby. Krajská knihovna je financována z krajských peněz. Tudí se dá říci, e v podstatě kadý obyvatel kraje má mít stejnou monost svoji krajskou knihovnu vyuívat. Je omezen přirozeně místem svého bydlitě, vzdáleností od místa sídla knihovny. Proto by měl mít monost meziknihovní slubou získat takové spektrum dokumentů jako uivatel bydlící v místě sídla krajské knihovny, ovem s přihlédnutím k potřebám vech uivatelů. V tom případě není zřejmě ani vhodné vybírat za potovné či vybírat jakékoli jiné poplatky ne od řádně zaregistrovaného uivatele. Uivatel v místě sídla krajské knihovny má zcela jistě mení potřebu vyuívat meziknihovní slubu. Z názorů mých kolegyň vyplývá, e preferují více otevřený a půjčovací přístup v oblasti meziknihovních slueb, ne ochranitelský. Zdůraňují, e nejsou problémy, týkající se ztrát, pokození nebo nedobytnosti výpůjček prostřednictvím této sluby. Stejně tak se shodnou na tom, e vybírání peněz za potovné znamená neúměrnou evidenci a pracnost ve srovnání s vynaloenými náklady.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"><p align=\"justify\">Another couple of comments on payment for inter-library loan services: Regional libraries are funded from regional money, so it can be said that basically all the inhabitants of the region have the same opportunity to use their regional library. They are naturally restricted by their place of residence and the distance from the place where the library is located. Thus they should have the opportunity to acquire the same range of documents through inter-library services as users living at the place where the regional library is located, while naturally, the requirements of all users are to be taken into account. In this case, it is clearly not even appropriate to collect postal charges or any other fees apart from those collected from ordinary registered users. Users at the place where the regional library is located quite clearly have less need to utilize inter-library services. The viewpoints of my colleagues indicate that they prefer a more open and pro-lending approach than a protective one when it comes to inter-library services. They stress that there are no problems involving losses, damage or uncollectibility of loans through these services. Likewise they agree that collecting money for postal charges means an amount of work and record-keeping that is out of proportion to the costs involved.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\"></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"><p align=\"justify\">A zcela na závěr - knihovny to jsou sluby obyvatelům a sluby by měly být vstřícné a rychlé. Uivatel potřebuje mít nejen dobře zpracovaný fond, kvalitní vyhledávací prostředky, ale i monost si dokument půjčit. </p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\"></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"><p align=\"justify\">And in conclusion, libraries are public services and these services ought to be rapid and readily available. Users need to have not only a well-organized library collection and high-standard search facilities, but also the opportunity to borrow documents.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\"></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM1\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX1\">1</a>\r\n The idea of \'management\' is much stronger here than the idea of an individual \'manager\'. \'Správce\' here does not allude to a person but to the role of an institution. </p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM2\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX2\">2</a>\r\n See Singular and Plural in the <a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/Athens/Forum/7953/Intro.html\">Czech&lt;&gt;English Translation Problems</a>\r\n page</p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM3\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX3\">3</a>\r\n See Signposting in the <a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/Athens/Forum/7953/Intro.html\">Czech&lt;&gt;English Translation Problems</a>\r\n page</p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM4\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX4\">4</a>\r\n The terminology used here is from the Czech&lt;-&gt;English <a href=\"http://www.nkp.cz/o_knihovnach/altnk.htm\">Glossary of Library Terminology</a>\r\n at the Czech National Library. Use was also made of the English version of the <a href=\"http://www.nkp.cz/o_knihovnach/English/ZakonAng257.htm\">Czech Libraries Act</a>\r\n, the translator of which has several decades of experience working in British libraries.</p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM5\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX5\">5</a>\r\n See Dominik Luke\'s comments on use of such signposting as \'former\' and \'latter\' in his <a href=\"http://www.bohemica.com/?m=catalog&s=14&a=132\">Overview of Cohesive Devices in Czech and English</a>\r\n. </p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM6\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX6\">6</a>\r\n \"Absenční půjčování\" is sometimes translated simply as \"loans\" or \"lending\", but here I feel the translator should intercede to bring out the full meaning. \"Absenční výpůjčky\" and \"prezenční výpůjčky\" are discussed in some detail in this <a href=\"http://groups.yahoo.com/group/Czechlist/message/21831\">Czechlist thread</a>\r\n. </p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM7\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX7\">7</a>\r\n See Translation 3) note 3).</p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM8\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX8\">8</a>\r\n It might appear unusual to leave \'republic\' standing on its own. \'Elsewhere in the country\' is another possible option, even though \'country\' might conceivably be taken to mean rural areas.</p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM9\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX9\">9</a>\r\n I find that \'potamo\' is an awkward little word which the dictionaries seem to avoid. Alternatives appreciated. <br />\r\nNOTE 1.8.03: Thanks to Paul Sinclair for suggesting \"by extension\", which is certainly applicable in some contexts.</p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM10\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX10\">10</a>\r\n \'Using\' is a handy device that can sometimes cover expressions like \'formou\' and \'prostřednictvím\'. It can act as a kind of pseudo-preposition in all kinds of contexts. </p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM11\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX11\">11</a>\r\n \'Populárně naučná literatura\' is usually translated simply as \'non-fiction\' [see 4)] but in this context I felt it was necessary to differentiate it from the broader category of \'literatura faktu\'.</p>\r\n<p><a name=\"BM12\"></a>\r\n<a href=\"#BMX12\">12</a>\r\n This is the term used in the Libraries Act [see 4)]. A case could also be made out for use of an equivalent such as \'local libraries\' or \'district libraries\'.</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>', '2003-07-27 22:15:45', 2077, 260, 127);
to rampant white-collar crime, a common ill response to the temptation inherent in Czech privatization.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Co je ale podstatné: dorůstají nové generace, generace lidí, kteří nabývali rozum u ve svobodě, a nejsou tudí zdeformováni ivotem v komunismu, lidí, kteří nové poměry přijímají u jako samozřejmost. Bylo by skvělé, kdyby jejich vstup do různých oblastí veřejného ivota vedl k tomu, e se budeme stále věcněji, důkladněji a bez předsudků zabývat vlastní minulostí, bez její otevřené reflexe nemůeme být dobře sami sebou, a e se budeme stále úspěněji rozcházet s mnoha nedobrými následky zhoubného díla, je předchozí reim napáchal v naich duích.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>The most important thing, however, is that new generations are maturing, generations of people who grew up free and are not deformed by life under communist rule. These are the first Czechs of our times who inherently consider freedom normal and natural. It would be great if the breaking through of these people into various parts of public life leads to our society more factually, thoroughly and impartially examining its past, without whose reflection we cannot be ourselves. I also hope it will lead to our successfully parting with many ill consequences of the work of destruction the communist regime wreaked upon our souls.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Váení přátelé, </P>\r\n\r\n<P>rok, který včera skončil, nám asi nesplyne nerozliitelně s těmi, které mu předcházely, protoe nám přinesl několik důleitých změn a událostí, na ně nelze zapomenout a z nich je opět třeba vyvodit či získat to nejlepí, co z nich vyvodit či získat lze.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>The year that ended yesterday will most likely not be just one more year for us, because it has brought us several unforgettable changes and events, out of which we should deduce and gain as much as we can.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Byl to předevím rok několikerých voleb. Můeme si stýskat nad malou účastí voličů i nad lecčíms jiným, ale jedno popřít nemůeme: tyto volby ukázaly, e vedle těch, pro ně je politika předevím účelovým manévrováním ve sféře moci a moc sama jen kořistí k rozdělení, mohou v ní působit i lidé otevření, ochotní naslouchat jiným, zkrátka ti, kteří vracejí správě věcí veřejných její nejvlastnějí smysl. A to nám dává novou naději.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>It was a year of several elections. We can complain at the small number of voters and other problems, but we cannot deny one thing: these elections have shown that politics need not be the domain solely of people for whom it is primarily about single-mindedly maneuvering in the sphere of power, and power itself but loot to apportion. There is also room in politics for people who are open, willing to listen to others, those who understand and prove the forgotten truth that public administration is about the public. And this gives us new hope.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Uplynulý rok byl zároveň rokem, kdy se spojenecká aliance, její jsme členy, sela na svém vrcholném zasedání poprvé za bývalou eleznou oponou právě u nás, v Praze. Toto zasedání bylo, jak víte, velmi úspěné a mělo téměř převratný význam: rozhodlo o dalích důleitých krocích k proměně Aliance v organizaci schopnou čelit zcela novému spektru hrozeb a připravenou obepínat celou Evropu, nikoli přijímat jen některé. Zároveň toto zasedání jasně připomnělo, e úkolem Aliance není slouit předevím politikům, generálům a výrobcům zbraní, ale naopak chránit vechny občany a slouit obecné bezpečnosti, míru a lidským svobodám. Nemusím zajisté zdůrazňovat, jak se toto ve - byť moná zvolna a nepřímo - obrátí v ná prospěch: vdyť jsme dodali Praze opět autoritu města, v něm se píe historie!</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Last year was also a year during which NATO, of which we are a member, met for the first time behind the former Iron Curtain--here in Prague. This Summit was, as you know, very successful and of almost ground-breaking importance: it decided on further important steps in the Alliance\'s transformation into an organization capable of facing a whole new set of threats and prepared to include all of Europe, not only certain of its countries. The Summit also clearly reminded us that the Alliance\'s goals are not to serve politicians, generals and arms producers, but to protect all citizens and serve the purpose of general security, peace, and personal freedom. I hope I do not have to remind you that all of this will turn to our benefit, even though it may do so slowly and indirectly: we have once again instilled Prague with the authority due any city where history is written!</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Neméně důleitá věc se loni stala krátce po tomto zasedání: nae mnohaleté úsilí bylo korunováno úspěchem a my jsme byli - spolu s devíti dalími evropskými zeměmi - přizváni do Evropské unie. Dík tomu máme anci přímo se podílet na historicky prvním pokusu Evropy zaloit svůj vnitřní pořádek na principu vzájemné rovnosti, společného sdílení demokratických hodnot a respektu k vůli vech svých součástí. Evropská unie nejsou samozřejmě jen solidární dotace bohatích chudím, i kdy i to má svůj význam: bezpočtukrát jsem se o tom přesvědčil, kdy jsem měl monost vímat si dynamického rozvoje zemí, které v době svého přijetí také nebyly nejbohatí. Evropská unie je ale předevím velkorysým projektem mírového souití na naem kontinentě a jeho mírového souití s jinými částmi světa, tedy projektem, který by měl definitivně ukončit dvoutisíciletou éru válek, konfliktů, vytváření zájmových sfér, zbabělých kompromisů se zlem a bojů o nadvládu mocnějích nad slabími, včetně Evropy nad světem. Pevně věřím, e referendum o naem přijetí se bude v tomto roce těit velkému zájmu občanů, kteří si uvědomí dosah svého rozhodnutí.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Another, no less important thing happened last year not long after the Summit: our long efforts were crowned with success and we were--together with nine other European countries--invited into the European Union. This will give us a chance to participate in the historic first attempt of Europe to base its order on the principles of equality, sharing of democratic values, and respect for the wishes of all of its parts. The European Union is not just about subsidies provided out of solidarity by the rich to the poor, even though this is important too: I was personally convinced of the importance of this aspect when I had the opportunity to observe the dynamic development of countries that were far from rich at the time of their accession. The European Union is a broad-minded project for our continent\'s nations to peacefully coexist among themselves and with other parts of the world. It should finally end a two-thousand-year era of wars, conflicts, spheres of influence, cowardly compromises with evil and struggles for the dominance of the strong over the weak, including that of Europe over the rest of the world. I hope that this year\'s referendum on our EU membership will meet with great interest among citizens, and that they will realize the reach of their decision.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Uplynulý rok nám přinesl ovem i jednu velikou, dosud nám v tomto rozsahu téměř neznámou zkouku: povodně. Velké uznání a dík patří vem, kteří zachraňovali lidské ivoty a majetek lidí, jako i vem, kteří pomáhali odstraňovat ničivé následky povodní a kteří na tuto pomoc přispívali. Spousta z nás ztratila mnoho, včetně po léta budovaných domovů, mnoho ztratila i společnost jako celek poničenými památkami či archivy. Ale zároveň se ukázalo něco nesmírně důleitého: kdy jde do tuhého, umíme tvořivě a pohotově spolupracovat a jsme schopni velké solidarity. Není tedy pravda, e ijeme v jakémsi eldorádu sobců. Ve společnosti je silná vůle k dobrým činům. Jde jen o to uvolňovat ji i tehdy, kdy nestojíme tváří v tvář katastrofě, a systematicky vytvářet klima, které jí bude přát.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>The past year also brought us one large test: floods. I would like to thank all who were out there saving human lives and property, those who helped to remedy the devastating effects of the floods, and those who sponsored these activities. Many of us lost everything, including homes built over many years; society lost many historical buildings and archives. The floods have also shown something else: in tough times, we can creatively and readily cooperate and are capable of great solidarity. Thus we can be sure: our country is no El Dorado for the selfish--our society is full of drive for acts of good will. The only complications lie in helping to release this pent- up drive even in times when we are not standing face to face with catastrophe, and systematically creating a friendly climate for it--one that is charitable towards the charitable.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Ale nejen to: podobně jako povodně v roce 1997, jsou i ty loňské významnou a svým způsobem varovnou zkueností pro vechny, kdo pečují o nai krajinu, o lesní a vodní hospodářství, o urbanistickou podobu lidského osídlení, jako i pro politiky, samosprávu a podniky, kteří by jim měli vytvářet nejrozumnějí podmínky k práci, i za cenu, e by dlouhodobému zájmu museli obětovat leccos ze zájmu okamitého. Nejnovějí vědecké poznatky a technické monosti bychom měli pokorně skloubit s praktickými zkuenostmi naich předků, kteří si je po tisíciletí předávali a kteří se moná méně trápili otázkou, proč je něco lepí tak či onak, ale o to víc respektovali to, co se prostě jako lepí osvědčilo. Bylo by prostě velmi dobré, kdyby nám tyto povodně pomohly lépe chápat, e nejsme pány světa, kosmu, zeměkoule a přírody, ale jen jejich součástmi, které by měly dobře váit, kde jsou hranice jejich zásahů do toho, co je obklopuje a bez čeho by nemohly vůbec být, toti kde se sluba krátkodobému a spočitatelnému prospěchu proměňuje v dlouhodobou a někdy i nenapravitelnou zkázu, její neblahé důsledky u nikdy nikdo nespočítá.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Similarly to the floods in 1997, last year\'s disaster is a significant warning for all who manage our natural landscape, forests, waters, and the landscapes of our towns and cities, as well as for politicians, public authorities, and companies, who all should create the best conditions for these people\'s work, even if that means sacrificing some short-term gain to long-term interests. We should humbly meld the latest scientific developments and technological possibilities with the practical experience of our predecessors, who handed such experience from generation to generation for thousands of years. They perhaps spent less time contemplating why one thing is better than another, but all the more simply respected whatever was proven as being better. It would be good if the floods helped us to understand that we are not the masters of the world, the universe, our planet and nature, but are only a part of them. We should carefully contemplate: where are the boundaries of our interventions into our surroundings, without which we could not exist? When does short-term and calculable profit become long-term and sometimes irrecoverable damage, whose effects can not be calculated?</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Milí spoluobčané, </P>\r\n\r\n<P>kdy jsem hovořil o rozdělení Československa jako o technické operaci, nabízela se otázka po tom, o čem tehdy bylo tak málo řeči: toti po ideji naeho státu.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>When I spoke of the division of Czechoslovakia as a technical operation, a question so little spoken of in those times struck my mind: upon what ideas does the Czech state rest?</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Nejsem si jist, zda Česká republika má nějakou svou zvlátní ideu, kterou lze jen tak říct nebo dokonce vyhlásit. Jsem si vak jist, e mnoho velkých muů a en naí historie různým způsobem formulovalo soubor moudrých principů vzájemného souití, k nim je dobré se vdy znovu vracet, které je třeba si připomínat, rozvíjet, ířit či promítat do svých ivotů a do své práce. K těmto principům patří odpovědnost nejen za sebe a k sobě, ale i za své spoluobčany a k nim, nejen za svou obec či stát a k nim, ale i za irí lidská společenství a k těmto společenstvím. Česká otázka je otázkou lidskou, říkával Masaryk. Myslím, e nemusíme mluvit v kadé větě o národu či národních zájmech, ale o to víc bychom se měli zabývat svými bliními, ať u v naí ulici či kdesi na druhém konci světa. Ano, naim národním zájmům vskutku asi nejlépe poslouíme, kdy se prostě budeme dobře chovat k sobě navzájem, k zemi, kterou obýváme, jako i k jiným národům, a kdy budeme nepředpojatě přemýlet o lidské historii, lidském údělu i lidském poslání na tomto světě. Ké by tedy pokora, zájem o druhé, lidská odpovědnost, smysl pro spravedlnost a solidarita byly tím, co lze nazvat ideou českého státu!</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>I am not certain whether the Czech Republic has its own special, fundamental ideas that could be named, let alone proclaimed. But I am certain that many great men and women of our history formulated a set of wise principles of coexistence to which we should always return, which should be remembered, built upon, propagated and reflected into our lives and our work. These principles include responsibility not only for oneself and to oneself, but also for and to one\'s fellow citizens, not only for and to a community or country, but also for and to the broader human society. \"The Czech issue is the human issue,\" said the first President of Czechoslovakia, T. G. Masaryk. I thus think that we do not have to use the words nation, or national interest in every sentence, but that we should concentrate instead on our neighbors, be they on our street or on the other side of the world. Yes, I am indeed saying that we will serve our national interests best if we simply treat each other well, treat the country we live in well, treat other nations well, and think of human history, human fate and our human mission in this world without prejudices. May humility, interest in others, responsibility for mankind, and a sense of justice and solidarity be that which can be called ideas underlying the Czech state!</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Dámy a pánové, </P>\r\n\r\n<P>demokratický vývoj v naí zemi je nezvratný. To ale neznamená, e nae úkoly končí. Znovu a znovu je třeba si připomínat, e demokracie nespočívá jen v určité struktuře institucí, ale té v duchu, lidském obsahu, smyslu a ideálech, jim má tato struktura slouit. V naem případě to mimo jiné znamená, e je třeba znovu a znovu stavět hráze bezohlednosti skryté za praporem tvorby zisku či pokusům vyvolávat v lidech strach ze svobodného projevu či svobodného sdruování, a znovu a znovu je třeba posilovat autoritu a nezávislost policie a justice, těchto praktických garantů spravedlnosti. Různá bratrstva lidí vdy připravených se obratně obohacovat na úkor ostatních a usilovat přitom o beztrestnost či dokonce politické krytí bohuel stále existují. Nesmíme tomu jen odevzdaně přihlíet!</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Our country\'s democratic development is irrevocable. That said, our work is not over. We must remind ourselves over and over that democracy is not just a certain institutional structure, but also a spirit, a human capacity, a purpose, and an ideal. The structure exists to serve these. In our case this means, among other things, that we must repeatedly build dams against ruthlessness claiming merely to be a search for profit and against attempts to inculcate fear of the freedoms of speech and assembly. Repeatedly, the authority and independence of the police and the judiciary, those practical guarantees of justice, must be strengthened. Unfortunately, there are still associations of people always ready to skillfully enrich themselves at other people\'s cost and to seek immunity or even political cover. We cannot just passively watch this!</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Ná Parlament bude brzy volit nového prezidenta republiky. Věřím, e se jím stane člověk moudrý, otevřený a odpovědný, který bude tvořivým partnerem politické reprezentace vzelé z nedávných voleb a v něm budou vichni občané dobré vůle spatřovat svého spojence.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>The parliament will soon vote for the new president of the Czech Republic. I trust that the new president will be a wise, open-minded, and responsible person, a creative partner to the political representation arising from the recent elections, one in whom all law-abiding citizens will see their ally.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Volba jednoho činitele státu, jakkoli se shodou okolností dnes stává velmi důleitou, není ale tím nejdůleitějím, co je před námi. Bez podpory občanů ani sebelepí prezident toho moc nezmůe. Přesně to jsem ostatně říkal před třinácti lety ve svém prvním novoročním projevu a dnes - kdy k vám mluvím na Nový rok naposledy - mi nezbývá, ne to opakovat.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>The election of one of the state\'s representatives, however important it may be at the moment due to its context, is not the most important thing before us. Not even the best president can do much without support from citizens. I said exactly that thirteen years ago in my first New Year\'s address and today--as I speak to you on New Year\'s Day for the last time--I cannot help but repeat it.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Váení spoluobčané, </P>\r\n<P>milí přátelé, </P>\r\n\r\n<P>ké by tato země byla u natrvalo a stále zřetelněji hezkým a sluným domovem nás vech, ké bychom na ni mohli být stále hrdějí!</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>May this country finally, permanently, and more and more distinctly be a fine and respectable home for all of us; may we be more and more proud of it!</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>Přeji vám vechno dobré v roce 2003 i v celém vaem budoucím ivotě!</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P>I wish you success in 2003 and in your entire life!</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n</TABLE>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P> </P>\r\n\r\n<P> </P></BODY>\r\n</HTML>', '2003-07-21 16:55:15', 4329, 260, 50);
 e si vydobyla mezinárodní uznání té jako podnětná redaktorka vědecké revue Byzantino-slavica, zejména tím, e se jí podařilo v tomto českém časopise soustřediti přední pracovníky vech národů, zabývajících se byzantinologií.</I></TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">The nomination for the election of M. Paulová was submitted on 16<SUP>th</SUP> March 1946 by Karel Stloukal. Amongst other things he wrote: \"<I>It is now finally opportune for a woman who is an internationally recognized scholar to gain membership of the Czech Academy.\" \"She has also gained international recognition as an inspiring editor of the scholarly review Byzantino-slavica, especially due to the fact that she has succeeded<A NAME=\"BMX8\"><A HREF=\"#BM8\"><SUP>8</SUP></A> in bringing together the foremost Byzantologists from all over the world in this Czech journal</I>.\"</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Druhým enským členem české akademie z oblasti vědy byla, jak uvedeno, <B>Marie Curie.</B> Návrh na její zvolení podali 1. května 1908 Bohuslav Raýman a Bohuslav Brauner. Vedle výčtu hlavních publikací, objevů a ocenění konstatovali, e: V době nejnovějí rozplaila důkladnou prací svou sensační experimenty Sir W. Ramsaye o přeměně mědi v lithium.. Volba proběhla 28. listopadu 1908, císařem potvrzena a 27. června. 1909. Bohuel, osobní sloka M. Curie neobsahuje a na pár materiálů k výročí objevení radia, ádné dokumenty. Koncept dopisu, v něm se jí a současně A. Meilletovi volba oznamuje, je uloen v Meilletově osobní sloce.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">As already mentioned, the other female member of the Czech Academy in the field of science was Marie Curie. The nomination for her election was put forward on 1<SUP>st</SUP> May 1908 by Bohuslav Rayman and Bohuslav Brauner. In addition to listing her chief publications, discoveries and awards they said that \"She has recently refuted<A NAME=\"BMX9\"><A HREF=\"#BM9\"><SUP>9</SUP></A> the sensational experiments performed by Sir W. Ramsay on the transformation of copper in lithium with her exhaustive work\". Her election took place 28<SUP>th</SUP> November 1908 and was not ratified by the Emperor until 27<SUP>th</SUP> June 1909. Unfortunately, the file on M. Curie does not contain any documents apart from a couple of items on the anniversary of the discovery of radium. A draft letter informing her and A. Meillet of their election is kept in Meillet\'s personal file.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">V německém protipólu České akademie, Gesellschaft zur Förderung deutscher Wissenschaft, Kunst und Literatur in Böhmen, byla situace obdobná. Vechny čtyři členky, kterými se mohla vykázat za celou dobu své existence, byly evidovány jako spisovatelky.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">The situation was similar at the German counterpart<A NAME=\"BMX6\"><A HREF=\"#BM6\"><SUP>6</SUP></A> of the Czech Academy, Gesellschaft zur Förderung deutscher Wissenschaft, Kunst und Literatur in Böhmen. All of the four female members that it had to show for its entire existence were registered as writers.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Po vzniku ČSR byly zaloeny dvě nové ústřední vědecké instituce: Masarykova akademie práce jako centrum organizace technického výzkumu a Československá národní rada badatelská jako spojovací článek mezi dosavadními vědeckými institucemi, ústavy a korporacemi a reprezentativní orgán československé vědy v zastřeujících mezinárodních vědeckých uniích. Masarykova akademie po celou dobu své existence nezvolila ani jedinou enu za svého řádného člena (oficiální označení odborní znalci), a pokud se v pramenech tohoto fondu jména en vyskytují, pak v této instituci působily pouze jako externí, tj. přizvaní členové (např. v Společnosti nauk zvolená A. Dratvová).</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">After the creation of the Czechoslovak state, another two central research institutions were established: the Masaryk Academy of Labour as a centre for organizing technical research and the Czechoslovak National Research Council (hereinafter \"Research Council\") as a connecting link between the existing research institutions, institutes and corporations and as a representative body for Czechoslovak science in international umbrella scientific associations. Throughout its entire existence, the Labour Academy did not elect a single woman as an ordinary member (officially designated \"specialist experts\") and where the names of women appear in its records at all, they merely have the status of \"external\", that is \"invited\", members (for example, A. Dratvová elected by the Learned Society).</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Rada badatelská měla ve svých řadách dvě členky, obě byly zvoleny a těsně po druhé světové válce v roce 1945. Podle jednacího řádu z 12. října 1935 volily odbory této instituce své členy prostou větinou, rozliení na eny a mue tu ovem formulováno nebylo. Volby se mohly konat kdykoliv v roce, nejpozději dva měsíce před generálním shromáděním, kdy mohl být proti nově zvoleným členům podán kadým členem protest; pokud byl schválen větinou hlasů přítomných členů, byla volba neplatná. Při výběru členů se mělo přihlíet předevím k vědecké kvalifikaci, dále také k tomu, aby v ČSNRB byly zastoupeny nejdůleitějí domácí vědecké instituce.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">The Research Council had two female members, both elected shortly after the Second World War in 1945. Under the Council procedural rules of 12<SUP>th</SUP> October 1935, members were elected by individual departments of this institution by a simple majority and there was no mention of any distinction between men and women. Elections could take place  at any time of the year but no later than two months before a General Meeting, where objections could be raised against newly elected members by any existing member. If an objection was approved by a majority of votes, the election was invalidated. When members were elected, their research qualifications were a primary consideration as was the endeavour to represent the most important research institutions in the country in the Council.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Z dokladů není příli jasné, které z uvedených kritérií převáilo pro to, e byla ji do třetice nominována <B>Milada Paulová</B>, zvolena v září 1945 do oboru historického. Oznámení o zvolení ji zastihlo v době jejího pobytu ve Velké Británii. Druhou členkou byla <B>Jiřina Popelová-Otáhalová</B>, zvolená v říjnu roku 1945 do oboru filosofického. V době volby byla docentkou Filosofické fakulty Masarykovy university v Brně. Kromě Paulové a Popelové-Otáhalové pracovalo ve výborech ČSNRB nejméně dalích est en, nebyly ovem řádně zvolenými členkami.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">It is not too clear from the records which of these criteria was of paramount importance when <b>Milada Paulová</b> was nominated and elected for a third time in September 1945 to the History Department. Notification of her election reached her while she was staying in Great Britain. The second member was <B>Jiřina Popelová-Otáhalová</B>,<B> </B>who was elected in October 1945 to the Philosophy Department. At the time of her election she was a senior lecturer at the Philosophical Faculty of Masaryk University in Brno. Apart from Paulová and Popelová-Otáhalová, at least six other women were working on Council committees, but not as ordinary elected members.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Podíváme-li se sumárně na období do roku 1952, tedy do vzniku CSAS, zjistíme, e v jeho průběhu se staly členkami ústředních českých vědeckých institucí pouze čtyři domácí eny-vědkyně a jedna ze zahraničí; ta také jako jediná byla zvolena před vznikem samostatného československého státu, tj. před rokem 1918. Prvenství mezi domácími členkami náleí jednoznačně historičce, profesorce University Karlovy v Praze Miladě Paulové, která byla postupně zvolena členkou tří ze čtyř ústředních vědeckých institucí. Dalo se tedy očekávat, e bude následovat její volba členkou nové Akademie věd.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">To summarize the period up to 1952 and the establishment of the Czechoslovak Academy of Sciences, we find that only four Czechoslovak women scholars and one woman from abroad became members of central Czech research institutions and the latter was the only one who was elected before the creation of the independent Czechoslovak state in 1918. A unique status among members here was enjoyed by the historian and professor at Charles University in Prague, Milada Paulová, who was elected over time to three of the four central research institutions. Hence it could also be anticipated that she would be elected as a member of the new Academy of Sciences.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Přenesme se nyní s předmětem naeho tématu do druhé poloviny 20. století; dalí členky ostatně ji v jeho první polovině nepřibyly. Z pohledu institucionální základny české vědy dolo na počátku 50. let minulého století k zásadním změnám, které byly součástí transformace společnosti podle komunistické ideologie. Pro své budovatelské aktivity komunisté často nalézali inspiraci u svého velkého východního vzoru, SSSR. To platilo i pro oblast vědy. Z úvah o nutné přestavbě institucionální základny vědy vzeel nakonec vítězně sovětský model jedné velké centrální akademie, do ní vplynula řada vědeckých institucí, spolků a společností, předevím ale tzv. předchůdci, mezi nimi byly v zákoně explicitně uvedeny Společnost nauk a Česká akademie a de facto sem přely jak zaměřením činnosti, tak částí členské základny i Masarykova akademie práce a Rada badatelská. Vplynutí předchůdců do nové CSAS neznamenalo té automatické převzetí jejich členů.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Let us now turn our focus on this subject to the latter half of the twentieth century; no more female members were elected in its first decades. Substantial changes took place to the institutional base of Czech scholarship and science in the early 1950s, which were all part of the transformation of society under Communist ideology. Czechoslovak Communists often drew inspiration from their great model to the east, the Soviet Union, for their own brave new<A NAME=\"BMX10\"><A HREF=\"#BM10\"><SUP>10</SUP></A> activities and this was also the case with science and scholarship. Deliberations over the necessary reconstruction of the institutional base for science and scholarship ultimately led to the victory of the Soviet model, with one great central academy swallowing up all the research institutions, associations and societies, the so-called \"predecessor organizations\", explicitly including by law the Learned Society and the Czech Academy, and in practice the areas of interest and some part of the membership bases of the Masaryk Academy of Labour and the Research Council were also taken over, for the inclusion of the predecessor organizations in the new CSAS did not mean the automatic inclusion of their members.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n<TR><TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Vedle jednoznačně politických nebo ideologických cílů měla nová Akademie věd naplnit také tuby po badatelské ne-univerzitní organizaci; která by poskytovala dobré a zajitěné podmínky pro vědeckou práci. Nový reim, který sám sebe označoval za demokratický a pokrokový na rozdíl od éry předchozí, hlásal nové, spravedlivé, vůči muům rovnocenné postavení en a podtrhoval jejich nenahraditelnou společenskou úlohu. Ačkoliv se oficiálně počítalo s větím zastoupením en v politice a ve společenských organizacích a s jejich větí samostatností uplatněním v profesích vyadujících speciální vzdělání, v realitě lo často o formální záleitosti. Dosti enormní zaměstnanost en v Československu u brzy v 50. a 60. letech, srovná-li se s poměry na západ od jeho hranic, vypovídá více třeba o potřebě zajistit finančně rodinný rozpočet. V rétorice předcházející zaloení CSAS ale prakticky zdůrazňování nastupujícího významu nové instituce pro eny nenajdeme.</TD>\r\n<TD WIDTH=\"50%\" VALIGN=\"TOP\">\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">In addition to its clearly political and ideological objectives, the new Academy of Sciences was also meant to meet the demands for a research-based organization outside of higher education to provide sound and secure conditions for research work. The new regime, which described itself as democratic and progressive in contrast to the previous era, proclaimed a new, fair, equal status for women and stressed their irreplaceable social role. Although greater representation for women in politics and in social organizations was officially anticipated, as was their greater independence within professions requiring special education, in reality this was often only on paper. The widespread employment of women in Czechoslovakia from the early 1950s through to the 1960s, when compared with conditions to the west of its borders, says more perhaps about the need to financially secure family budgets. In the rhetoric preceding the foundation of the CSAS, however, we do not actually find any emphasis on the prospective importance of the new institution for women.</TD>\r\n</TR>\r\n</TABLE>\r\n\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM1\"><A HREF=\"#BMX1\">1</A>\'Uplatněni\' might possibly be translated as \'involvement\' here as in \'Women\'s Involvement in Science and Scholarship\'. However, I feel there is often a difference in the way titles and headings in scholarly, scientific and other domains are characteristically dealt with in English and Czech. Czech titles will often include a \'lead-word\' or some other description of function and purpose, which might sound rather plodding and redundant in literal translation, e.g.:  </P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<br>Vymezení cílů výzkumného záměru - Research Plan objectives\r\n<br>Časový harmonogram řeení výzkumného záměru - Research Plan schedule\r\n<br>Personální zabezpečení - Personnel\r\n\r\n\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM2\"><A HREF=\"#BMX2\">2</A> \'Věda\' and \'vědecký\' are always to be handled with caution. The Czech word \'vědecký\' is closer to German \'wissenschaftlich\' than to the narrower English \'scientific\'. We might consider \'scientific\', \'scholarly\', \'research\', \'academic\', \'learned\' and in rare cases I have used \'systematic\' where the primary relevant connotation has been that of an arrangement of knowledge in a categorized, orderly manner. The following descriptions of <A HREF=\" http://www.bobnancy.com/glossary/wissenschaftlich.html\">\'wissenschaftlich\'</A> are perhaps useful:</P>\r\n\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">Wissenschaftlich is often translated as \'scientific\'. But in German the word is not as narrow in meaning as has generally become the case with \'scientific\' in English and can simply mean \'a systematic body of knowledge\' (cf. Wahrig, Deutsches Wörterbuch: \'geordnetes, folgerichtig aufgebautes, zusammenhängended Gebiet von Erkenntnissen\'). In this sense \'wissenschaftlich\' can be translated as \'systematic\'. Depending on the context, wissenschaftliche Erkenntnis, for example, can be translated as \'scientific knowledge\' (where the meaning is clearly intended), but also, in a wider sense, as \'systematic knowledge\'.</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">---</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A HREF=\"http://www.uni-trier.de/uni/fb2/anglistik/ Projekte/stubbs/termpapr.htm\">http://www.uni-trier.de/uni/fb2/anglistik/ Projekte/stubbs/termpapr.htm</A></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<br>science = die Naturwissenschaften (ie physics, chemistry, biology, etc)</P>\r\n<br>a scientist = ein(e) Naturwissenschaftler(in).</P>\r\n<br>Wissenschaftler haben herausgefunden, dass ...</P>\r\n<br>= Researchers have discovered that ...</P>\r\n<br>or Linguists have discovered that ...</P>\r\n\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">The term social sciences is used (for sociology, economics, etc, and possibly psychology).\r\n<br>die Geisteswissenschaften = the humanities or the arts. <br>Literaturwissenschaft = literary studies or literary theory. \r\n<br>FB2 Sprach- und Literaturwissenschaft = Faculty of Language and Literature.\r\n<br>The term Linguistic Science is hardly used in English. Just Linguistics.\r\n\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">In other words, Wissenschaft has a much wider meaning than science. The term scholarship might sometimes be used, but this has different connotations of \"knowledge, wisdom and reading books\", and seems to exclude research, experiments, etc.</P>\r\n\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">In the sense of the Prüfungsordnungen, eine wissenschaftliche Arbeit = an undergraduate dissertation.  Wissenschaftlich can often be translated as academic (an academic article).</P>\r\n\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">All of the above is purely terminological: calling a piece of work wissenschaftlich is often just a matter of claiming that it has prestige, which it may or may not deserve. The real question is what is meant: often the best term might be systematic (to study a problem in a methodical, rigorous and systematic way). (The underlying question is whether there are fundamental differences between the methods appropriate for studying the social and the natural world, but that is too big a topic for here.)</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM3\"><A HREF=\"#BMX3\">3</A> For \'vědecká instituce\' we might also consider scholarly institute/institution\' or \'scientific institute/institution\' if context permits, or even the rather outdated \'learned society\' in historical contexts or if a genteel style is really called for. </P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\">I have not been very consistent here in my use of  \'institute\' and \'institution\': the Academy as a whole is made up of \'ústavy\' = \'institutes\' so I reserve \'institution\' for the Academy itself, but elsewhere I have often allowed the immediate context or collocational considerations to decide. </P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM4\"><A HREF=\"#BMX4\">4</A> In many situations I find that \'fortunes\' plays the role of \'osudy\' surprisingly well. In this rather cheerless context I felt a slightly grimmer edge was needed, hence \'fates\'.</P>\r\n<B><P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n</B><P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM5\"><A HREF=\"#BMX5\">5</A> \"V titěné podobě, tak v elektronické, zprostředkované internetem\". Academic references to computers and the internet in Czech can sound terribly huffy and puffy in anything remotely like literal translation. Condense where possible. Sometimes I\'d be tempted to ignore words like \'nové\' and \'moderní\' in formal Czech references to IT, as they can come over as something like \"Mr. Logie Baird\'s new televisual equipment\".</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM6\"><A HREF=\"#BMX6\">6</A> \'Counterpart\' sounds more natural but the \'opposite pole\' idea conveys something specific and interesting about Czech-German relations as perceived by the author so I decided to calque with inverted commas in the first instance and use the more natural sounding word in the second. \'Equivalent\' might also be considered if we did not wish to stress the \'opposite\' aspect.</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM7\"><A HREF=\"#BMX7\">7</A> Alternative ideas appreciated. \'Antecedent organizations\' also exists but is much less frequently met with.</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM8\"><A HREF=\"#BMX8\">8</A> I often tread warily around \'podařit se\'. \'Manage to\' is another possibility but sometimes I will just miss the idea out completely (or compensate with \'actually\') because it can sound excessively fussy in English.</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM9\"><A HREF=\"#BMX9\">9</A> \'Rozplait\' is an unusual choice of words that baffles most Czechs. One occasionally comes across such idiosyncratic \'rogue\' usages and a little research is required. Still not totally happy about \'refute\'.</P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"></P>\r\n<P ALIGN=\"JUSTIFY\"><A NAME=\"BM10\"><A HREF=\"#BMX10\">10</A> I put out an appeal on Czechlist for ideas about \'budovatelské aktivity\' but drew a complete blank. Still open to alternative suggestions.</P>\r\n', '2003-12-06 06:09:40', 2322, 260, 127);
 Kdy Helena přila domů, viděla</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>She was a pretty woman, Helen. Helena byla teda fakt krásná enská </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>[Knittlová, ibid]</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<h4 style=\"margin: auto 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(64, 128, 128);\">PERSONALIZING PERSONNEL</span>\r\n <p />\r\n</h4>\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><i>Trvá soustředěná pozornost nastupující generaci <strong>kvalifikovaných dělnických profesí</strong>\r\n , kterých je na trhu práce váný nedostatek.<p />\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><em><p />\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><i>Attention continues to be concentrated on the upcoming generation <strong>of qualified tradesmen</strong>\r\n , of whom there is a serious lack on the labour market today.</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>I am told there has been a tradition in Czech bureaucratese going back even before the Communist era to speak of workers in inanimate terms. While this tendency can be seen aplenty in English corporatese too, there are occasions such as this when we have to animate things a little to get any sense at all. </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><em>Při provádění lékařských prohlídek musí být posuzujícímu lékáři sdělen pracovní podmínky, pro které je zdravotní způsobilost posuzována. </em>\r\n<i><br />\r\n<em>For the performance of a preventive examination the examining doctor must be informed by the track operator of the working conditions for which </em>\r\n<strong>the persons</strong>\r\n<em> state of health is being assessed. <p />\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>Pro Vae budovy nabízíme různé reimy zabezpečení, recepci návtěv počínaje a ostrahou mimo pracovní dobu konče<br />\r\nWe offer various security regimes for your buildings, from visitor reception to security guards outside working hours:</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<br />\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<h4 style=\"margin: auto 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(64, 128, 128);\">ELEGANT VARIATION</span>\r\n <p />\r\n</h4>\r\n<p>&quot;Never use the same word twice in the same sentence&quot; is advice that even technical writers seem to take deadly seriously. But for legal and technical texts, uniformity of terminology should normally be enforced - especially on technicians with a literary bent. </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<h4 style=\"margin: auto 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(64, 128, 128);\">REPETITION</span>\r\n <p />\r\n</h4>\r\n<p>With its well-developed system of endings denoting gender, Czech usually has less need to repeat a noun for the sake of clarity than English has. However, reverse situations can be found: <i><br />\r\n<em>31.12 roku následujícího po roce, po kterém byla výpověď doručena straně vypovídané.</em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>31st December of the year following that in which the notice was delivered to the counter-party.</em>\r\n</i>\r\n <em><p />\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>kompatibilita metodiky CRA s metodikou komplexního hodnocení světových agentur<br />\r\nthe compatibility of the Czech Rating Agency\'s methodology with that of the comprehensive ratings given by world-renowned agencies</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>A note on style from Fowler: A dozen sentences are spoilt by ill-advised avoidance of repetition for every one that is spoilt by ill-advised repetition.</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>Knittlová [ibid] also notes: Opakování lexikální jednotky v nezměněné podobě povauje česká stylistická norma za poněkud těkopádné, pokud nejde o součást autorova záměru, a dává přednost dalím monostem. Angličtina je v tomto ohledu méně citlivá.</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>V jedotlivých vědních oborech a jejich projevech vystačíme s poměrně skrovným výsekem lexikálního repértoáru, take index opakování je dost vysoký, podle průzkumu při porovnání s ostatnými funkčními styly vůbec největí. </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<h4 style=\"margin: auto 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(64, 128, 128);\">COMPONENTIAL ANALYSIS</span>\r\n <p />\r\n</h4>\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><lh>\r\nIt frequently happens in descriptive and expressive language that a low-frequency word has to be put through the following process:</lh>\r\n </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">ˇ</span>\r\n<span>&nbsp;</span>\r\nBreak it down into all its primary and secondary, conceptual and connotative meanings </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">ˇ</span>\r\n<span>&nbsp;</span>\r\nDecide which of these have any valency with the given context. </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">ˇ</span>\r\n<span>&nbsp;</span>\r\nPare these down to two words (or at very most three if they have cumulative effect and do not disturb the dynamic of the text). </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">ˇ</span>\r\n<span>&nbsp;</span>\r\nBracket off any remainder for possible later weighting of subsequent componential analyses. A couple of English-Czech examples:<em> <p />\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>dapper - upravený a čilý<br />\r\nfacetious - obhrouble nebo nejapně vtipný<br />\r\nquaint - přitalivý, starobylý a pitoreskní</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<h4 style=\"margin: auto 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(64, 128, 128);\">OR NOT TO BE</span>\r\n <p />\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>Důleitým zdrojem informací jsou dotazníky</i>\r\n<i><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><br />\r\n<kbd>!!!An important source of information are questionnaires</kbd>\r\n</span>\r\n <br />\r\n<em>Questionnaires are an important source of information</em>\r\n <br />\r\nWhere subject and complement are of different numbers, the English verb normally follows the number of the subject, whereas Czech allows for either.<p />\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><br />\r\n<kbd>!!!An important source of information are questionnaires</kbd>\r\n</span>\r\n <i><br />\r\n<em>Questionnaires are an important source of information</em>\r\n</i>\r\n <br />\r\nWhere subject and complement are of different numbers, the English verb normally follows the number of the subject, whereas Czech allows for either. </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>The Czech verb <i>být</i>\r\n is &quot;stronger&quot; than English <i>to be</i>\r\n , in that it can stand more easily on its own and carry the meaning of <i>exist</i>\r\n or <i>there is</i>\r\n (as in <i>brambory nejsou!</i>\r\n ). With the instrumental it can also have shades of <i>represent</i>\r\n , <i>act as</i>\r\n , <i>consist in</i>\r\n or even <i>make</i>\r\n . <em><p />\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>Podkladem pro úhradu dohodnuté ceny je daňový doklad vystavený agenturou<br />\r\nThe tax document made out by the agency acts as supporting documentation for payment of the agreed price. <em><p />\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>Ten les bude dobrá skrý.<br />\r\nThis wood will make a good hiding place.</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p /><p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p>In definitions:<em> <p />\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>Provozuschopností dráhy je technický stav dráhy zaručující její bezpečné a plynulé provozování</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>By track serviceability is meant that state of repair of a track which guarantees its safe and uninterrupted operation<p />\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p /><br /><p />\r\n<p />\r\n<h4 style=\"margin: auto 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(64, 128, 128);\">PRESENT HISTORIC</span>\r\n <p />\r\n</h4>\r\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm 0cm 0pt;\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><br />\r\nOccasionally crops up in journalistic style and usually needs to be transformed into the appropriate past tense. </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><em>Pro úplnost doplňme bronz Fučíka mezi mui a dva bronzy juniora kop - ten si jetě o místo polepil na ME 1995, kde ovem zůstal zcela ve stínu zlata a stříbra Petra Jelínka. Mezi mui se po roční pauze znovu na scéně objevuje Pospíil, a znovu úspěně - je to druhé místo těsně před Vaňkem, který si ve vynahrazuje triumfem ve druhém závodě. Dalí stříbro připojuje Pospíil v roce následujícím, kde jsou vak vedle dalího stříbra Sukeníka, ústředními postavami junioři - kromě bronzů Jelínka a Kotulána poprvé zlatě zazáří Jakub Oma. <p />\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>For the sake of completeness let us also mention the bronze won by Fučík in the mens and the two bronzes won in the juniors by kob, who improved on that by one place in the 1995 European championships but was still overshadowed by the gold and silver won by Petr Jelínek. After a year out, Pospíil came back onto the mens scene, again successfully, in second place just in front of Vaňek, who made up for everything with his triumph in the second competition. Pospíil added another silver the following year when apart from the other silver won by Sukeník, the central figures were the juniors: in addition to the bronzes won by Jelínek and Kotulán, Jakub Oma struck gold for the first time.</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><br /></p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<h4 style=\"margin: auto 0cm;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(64, 128, 128);\">KINDS OF</span>\r\n <p />\r\n</h4>\r\n<p>Sometimes needs inserting</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><em>Obvod dráhy u ostatních drah je vymezen svislými plochami <p />\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><i>Track precincts for other <strong>kinds of</strong>\r\n track are demarcated by vertical panels </i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n<p><br /> </p>\r\n<p />\r\n<p />\r\n', '2004-05-08 18:20:14', 2975, 258, 102),
(179, 'Czech-English Translation Problems - 3', 'MBC', '', '<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">CONTRASTS NEED TO BE BROUGHT OUT</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p>The reader can be helped along with discourse markers like \"but\", \"however\", \"nevertheless\" or even...<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><em>Úspěné obchodní společnosti se vyplatí poskytnout zaměstnancům nadstandardní platovou úroveň. Tento standard ovem náleí jen těm, kdo peníze svému zaměstnavateli vydělávají; to nejsou ani uklízečky, ani topiči, jene i oni se obvykle doadují vyího standardu. Správní společnost obsluným profesím platí (a Vám účtuje) jen tolik, kolik je obvyklé.</em>\r\n<i><br />\r\n<em>It pays for successful companies to give their employees above-average salaries but this rate is of course only due to those who actually earn money for their employers, which is not the case with cleaners and boilermen. But then they too will usually demand the higher rate. A management company, </em>\r\n<strong>in contrast</strong>\r\n<em> , will only pay its auxiliary staff the going rate (and charge you accordingly). </em>\r\n</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">END-WEIGHT VERSUS SUBJECT-BEFORE-PREDICATE</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><kbd><span style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt\">!!!The development of a young squirrel reared by its mother and that of an artificially reared one are described.</span>\r\n</kbd>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>At first sight this translation looks grammatically correct - so what is so awkward-sounding about it?The translator has slavishly followed the English rule of subject before predicate, but has ignored the fact that English prefers the longest noun phrase at the end of the sentence:<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>A comparison is made between a young squirrel reared by its mother and another one reared artificially.<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Similarly, predicates like <em>\"...are presented\"</em>\r\n or <em>\"...are studied\"</em>\r\n can be transformed into nominalized introductions like <em>\"A presentation is made of\" </em>\r\nand <em>\"A study is made of...\"</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">OTHER WORD-ORDER PROBLEMS - THEME AND RHEME</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p>A lot of theorists seem quite unabashed in taking English as a model for language use per se and then explain other usages in terms of this English model. Fortunately, we have our own world-renowned linguists with their own Czech bias just down the road here in the <a href=\"http://dec59.ruk.cuni.cz/~dlukes/plchome.htm\">Prague Linguistic Circle</a>\r\n . Some of its most famous participants made a special study of theme/rheme problems, i.e. issues involving the ordering of `that which is talked about\' and `the statement that is made about it\'. Such \'topics\' and \'comments\' can be of great importance to the translator from Czech because a) the order in which information is given can be one of the primary elements of a text to be conveyed and b) even if it isnt, failure to create a well-integrated information-order structure, whether following the original or not, can be the reason your grammatically sound text still looks somehow diffuse and unfocused. <p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://rafale.worldnet.net/~abdel/linguistics/prague.htm\">Vilém Mathesius</a>\r\n , commented that \"English differs from Czech in being so little susceptible to the requirements of <a href=\"http://www.ling.ed.ac.uk/~csporled/ilex_html/node4.html\">Functional Sentence Perspective</a>\r\n (FSP) as to frequently disregard them altogether.\" and \"In English, the lack of a differentiated morphemic system in many areas places heavy constraints on word-order patterns. In Czech, with its richer morphemic systems, word order can follow the FSP much more faithfully\". That is, in Czech the newly introduced information can always go at the end of the sentence whether as subject, object or whatever, whereas English is supposedly constrained by its hidebound subject-object word-order. <br />\r\nNow few English speakers will have stopped mid-sentence to bemoan this lack, perhaps because a) English has several syntactic devices (described below) for putting information to the end of a sentence (for whatever reason - whether because it is new or to link it to its conjunction; b) new information does not <i>have</i>\r\n to go to the end of the sentence in English because c) (my own pet theory) English takes a lot more advantage of intonation to express what is theme and what is rheme than does Czech with its comparative monotone, and this can be expressed in English writing by means of punctuation, (which compared with the strict rules of Czech punctuation is positively freestyle) or in some cases even by italic and bold type faces. <p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>This issue crops up a lot in the problem of fitting a word into a position immediately before the conjunctions \"who\" or \"which\":<br />\r\n<em>V soutěi drustev zvitězili Rusové, kteří měli ve vech esti kategoriích po dvou závodnících.</em>\r\n<i><br />\r\n<em>The winners of the team event were the Russians, who had two competitors in each of the six categories</em>\r\n</i>\r\n <em><p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Na činnost PT, a.s. má dopad rovně řada faktorů, které lze jen těko ovlivnit<br />\r\nPT, a.s. activity is under the joint influence of several factors which can only be controlled with difficulty</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Na činnost PT, a.s. má dopad rovně řada faktorů, které lze jen těko ovlivnit<br />\r\nPT, a.s. activity is under the joint influence of several factors which can only be controlled with difficulty<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Below are several other strategies to be considered when faced with word-order problems. <p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">WORD-ORDER - PASSIVE VOICE</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><br />\r\nThe most obvious choice. As Mathesius points out, the passive is used a lot more in English than in Czech. <br />\r\n<em>Janu navtívil Petr</em>\r\n<i><br />\r\n<em>Jane was visited by Peter</em>\r\n</i>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>(In Czech, by the way, the passive is sometimes used <br />\r\n1) where the active would confuse subject and object because of identical gender.<br />\r\nCinovec doprovází wolfram<br />\r\nCinovec je doprovázen wolframem<br />\r\nSo in such cases, the English translation need hardly follow suit<br />\r\n2) Where one subject undergoes a number of actions:<br />\r\nHoch, který po autu hodil kamenem, byl chycen majitelem auta, vláčen na policií, vyslýchán, odveden stráníkem do koly a pak břečící odevzdán rodičům<br />\r\nIn Czech, use of the passive can be journalistic or indicative of higher style, which themselves have to be accounted for). <p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">WORD-ORDER - ARTICLES</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p>The English indefinite article, introducing a new item of information, can often by rendered in Czech by final position in the sentence:<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Do místnosti vstoupila dívka<br />\r\nA girl entered the room<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Dívka vstoupila do místnosti<br />\r\nThe girl entered the room</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Do místnosti vstoupila dívka<br />\r\nA girl entered the room<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Dívka vstoupila do místnosti<br />\r\nThe girl entered the room</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Dívka vstoupila do místnosti<br />\r\nThe girl entered the room<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">WORD-ORDER - CLEFT SENTENCES</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p>New information can be emphasised in English by fronting it in a cleft sentence. In Czech the rheme goes to the end.<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Tom včera spravil Janin psací stroj v kanceláři<br />\r\nIt was at the office that Tom repaired Janes typewriter yesterday<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>It was with a screwdriver that Tom repaired Janes typewriter<br />\r\nTom včera spravil Janin psací stroj roubovákem</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Tom včera spravil Janin psací stroj v kanceláři<br />\r\nIt was at the office that Tom repaired Janes typewriter yesterday<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>It was with a screwdriver that Tom repaired Janes typewriter<br />\r\nTom včera spravil Janin psací stroj roubovákem</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>It was with a screwdriver that Tom repaired Janes typewriter<br />\r\nTom včera spravil Janin psací stroj roubovákem<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Or by fronting it in a pseudo-cleft sentence<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Pozoruhodné bylo, e to přiznala<br />\r\nWhat was remarkable was her admitting it.</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Pozoruhodné bylo, e to přiznala<br />\r\nWhat was remarkable was her admitting it.<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">WORD-ORDER - OTHER DEVICES</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><br />\r\nFirbas also lists a device he calls the \"possessive passive\" using the verb \"to have\" :<br />\r\n<em>He always has crowds attending his concerts - Na jeho koncerty vdycky chodí davy lidí</em>\r\n <br />\r\nAnd the \"perceptive passive\" using the verb \"to find\":<br />\r\n<em>\"Upon examination of these, I found a certain boldness of temper growing in me\" </em>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">WORD-ORDER - CONVERSE VERBS</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i><br />\r\n<em>Mezi zákazníky firmy X je např. Y, větina energetických podníků i armáda.</em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>X\'s customers include Y, the majority of power utilities and the army, </em>\r\n</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><em>Vzniknout </em>\r\ncan be rendered with a similar shift in perspective. <i><br />\r\n<em>Česká republika vznikla v roce 1993.</em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>The Czech Republic was established in 1993</em>\r\n</i>\r\n <em><p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Tento postup vychází z následujíciho filosofie:<br />\r\nThis approach is based on the philosphy that<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Nájemce se zavazuje pouívat předmět nájmu tak, aby pronajímateli nevznikla ádná koda<br />\r\nThe lessee undertakes to use the subject of lease in such a way as not to cause any damage to the lessor<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>V dohodnuté částce je obsaeno... <br />\r\nThe agreed amount comprises...<em>V kapitole 8 se hovoří o </em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>Chapter 8 discussesNa obr 4 je schematicky znázorněn </em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>Figure 4 showsV tabulce VII jsou uvedeny </em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>Table VII gives <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Converse verbs can be a very useful way of overcoming word-order problems. Shifts in perspective involving buy/sell, lend/borrow belong/own etc can be used to get a word to the end of a sentence for reasons of thematic structure.<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>emisním měřením bylo zjitěno, e<br />\r\nmeasurements of emissions indicated that</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>emisním měřením bylo zjitěno, e<br />\r\nmeasurements of emissions indicated that<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>If such departures from the original text make you nervous about losing sight of terra firma, you can always check your position with a <strong>truth-conditional</strong>\r\n approach to the problem. Put your converse translation through the following analysis:<br />\r\nExample: <em>Tento obchod patří mému bratrovi - [B] My brother owns this shop </em>\r\n<br />\r\n1. Ask yourself if there is any conceivable set of circumstances where one utterance is true while the other is false, i.e. this shop belongs to my brother but my brother does not own this shop.<br />\r\n2. Now ask yourself, does any such imagined situation a) stand in its own right or b) does it need an extra element to be made explicit, (i.e this shop \"should\" belong to my brother) for it to be meaningful.<br />\r\n3. If b) then the converse view is valid and in most cases this translation should be good.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>', '2004-05-08 18:22:20', 3527, 258, 103),
(180, 'Czech-English Translation Problems - 4', 'MBC', '', '<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">FIRBAS ON ASPECTUAL VERB FORMS</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>In <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0521373085/czechenglishtran\">\"Functional Sentence Perspective\"</a>\r\n , Jan Firbas says, \"it is worth noticing that an English adverb of indefinite time that has been framed in could be more readily rendered into Czech by an aspectual verb form than one that occurs outside the frame, serving as a setting or a specification<br />\r\n<em>v létě chodíval na dlouhé procházky</em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>He usually went for long walks in summer. </em>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">NOMINALIZATION AND VERBALIZATION</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>Bureaucratic texts can go rather heavy on the abstract nouns, which often need to be loosened up and recast in other forms<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Uzavření dodatku je podmínkou dalí platnosti nájemní smlouvy<br />\r\nThis lease contract only continues to be valid on condition this annex is concluded<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Bezdrátové LAN, které vyadují rychlou obnovu síových systémů, <strong>jejich nefunkčnost by mohla způsobit katastrofu</strong>\r\n .</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Wireless LANs involving the rapid restoration of network systems <strong>that could cause a disaster if not working</strong>\r\n <em><p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><strong><i>Pro zvýení atraktivnosti pro své klienty</i>\r\n</strong>\r\n<i> firma zavádí systém řízení kvality ISO 9001.<br />\r\nThe company is introducing the ISO 9001 quality control system <strong>to make it even more attractive to customers</strong>\r\n </i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Uzavření dodatku je podmínkou dalí platnosti nájemní smlouvy<br />\r\nThis lease contract only continues to be valid on condition this annex is concluded<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Bezdrátové LAN, které vyadují rychlou obnovu síových systémů, <strong>jejich nefunkčnost by mohla způsobit katastrofu</strong>\r\n .</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Wireless LANs involving the rapid restoration of network systems <strong>that could cause a disaster if not working</strong>\r\n <em><p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><strong><i>Pro zvýení atraktivnosti pro své klienty</i>\r\n</strong>\r\n<i> firma zavádí systém řízení kvality ISO 9001.<br />\r\nThe company is introducing the ISO 9001 quality control system <strong>to make it even more attractive to customers</strong>\r\n </i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Bezdrátové LAN, které vyadují rychlou obnovu síových systémů, <strong>jejich nefunkčnost by mohla způsobit katastrofu</strong>\r\n .<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Wireless LANs involving the rapid restoration of network systems <strong>that could cause a disaster if not working</strong>\r\n <em><p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><strong><i>Pro zvýení atraktivnosti pro své klienty</i>\r\n</strong>\r\n<i> firma zavádí systém řízení kvality ISO 9001.<br />\r\nThe company is introducing the ISO 9001 quality control system <strong>to make it even more attractive to customers</strong>\r\n </i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><strong><i>Pro zvýení atraktivnosti pro své klienty</i>\r\n</strong>\r\n<i> firma zavádí systém řízení kvality ISO 9001.<br />\r\nThe company is introducing the ISO 9001 quality control system <strong>to make it even more attractive to customers</strong>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>However it is more common in English than in Czech to personalize an action thus:<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Pracuje pomalu<br />\r\nHe is a slow worker</i>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Pracuje pomalu<br />\r\nHe is a slow worker<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>The Prague School has something to say on this matter too. Jan Firbas developed the idea that words with more specific meanings are more likely to function as rhemes while words with more general meaning tend to function as themes. The capacity of a word for assuming the rhematic function by virtue of its semantic specificity is by him called \"communicative dynamism\". It is viewed as a matter of degree: the elements of an utterance are no longer simply classed as theme or rheme, but are considered to have a given degree of thematic or rhematic function. <br />\r\nFirbas uses this concept to compare verbs and nouns in Czech and English in terms of communicative dynamism. He concludes that verbs in Czech have greater dynamism than verbs in English. This is shown by the frequent use in English of semantically vague verbs in construction with a more precise noun, where in Czech a single precise verb might be used, i.e.<br />\r\nkončí - comes to a conclusion<br />\r\nrozhoduje - makes a decision <br />\r\nIn Firbas\' opinion, this difference accounts for the much greater overall frequency of nominal constructions in English than in Czech.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">MODALS</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>A Czech technician student of mine once complained about the use of \"<em>should not</em>\r\n \" in English manuals. \"<em>You should not poke your finger into this socket</em>\r\n \" sounded to him somehow half-hearted and a provocation. If only they had used <strong>must</strong>\r\n ...\"<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">PREPOSITIONS BEFORE AND AFTER</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>V budově i okolo ní<br />\r\nIn and around the building<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Czech does not usually allow for more than one preposition before a noun except in the case of a few set expressions (<em>Modlitba za v Pánu zesnulé</em>\r\n ) <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">ATTITUDINAL SENTENCE MODIFIERS </span>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>As a general rule I would say play down English swear-words - and conversely, many common Czech swear-words need to be spiced up in translation so as not to sound twee and 1930s. Colloquial words with originally sexual meanings that can easily be used among friends in Britain and America would sound like something from out of the gutter in literal Czech translation.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Knittlová [ibid] surveys the various renditions of \'goddam\' in a translation of Salinger\'s Catcher in the Rye. She suggests that it is rarely conveyed by a one-to-one equivalent and various lexical and syntactic devices are used. <br />\r\nI\'m losing my goddam mind  začiná mi to lízt na mozek<br />\r\nHe didn\'t say one goddam word about Jane  o Hance neřek ani ň<br />\r\nWhat a goddam fool I was  e jsem to byl ale blázen bláznivá<br />\r\nIt is in my goddam blood  mám to v krvi<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Vcelku lze shrnout, e mezi anglickými a českými intensifikátory v překladu často nedochází ke korespondenci. Tendenci k tomu vykazuje anglická konstrukce s hell a české pekelný, pekelně. Překladatelé se o korespondenci tohoto typu ani nesnaí, spíe jím jde o vystiení odpovídající české stylistické roviny. Intensifikátory obdobně jako citoslovce a expletiva fungují předevím jako signály zexpresívňující celou výpověď.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>', '2004-05-08 18:24:11', 3016, 258, 104);
] tradičně tvrdí, e je oproti četině mnohem méně expresivní, vyuívá celkem málo expresivních prostředků. [Knittlová quoting Poldauf - ibid]<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Při srovnání angličtiny a četiny se zdá, e angličtina má chudí inventář inherentně expresivních prostředků ne četina, co vak neznamená, e by byl nutně méně účinný. Expresivita v angličtině je ve větím mnoství případů koncentrována do lexikálních výrazů, které jsou nositeli výhradně expresivních konotačních sloek a mají radiační schopnost, kdeto v českém textu je expresivita rozprostřena rovnoměrnějí na větí počet nositelů sloek denotačních i konotačních. [Knittlová, ibid]<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">JOINING SENTENCES</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>I find there is sometimes a need to fuse separate Czech sentences together in English translation, especially in texts for popular consumption (e.g. for tourists) in which a more graceful style has to be \"imposed\" on what might otherwise appear to be rather peremptory-sounding, jerky sentences .<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><i><br />\r\n<em>WaveLAN má také ojedinělý identifikační systém WaveLAN Network ID. Kadá deska se sama identifikuje pomocí tohoto tajného identifikačního kódu pro sí. Navzájem spolu mohou komunikovat pouze desky s identifickými kódy.</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><em><p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><i>WaveLAN also has its own unique WaveLAN Network ID system whereby each board identifies itself with this secret network identification code and only boards with identical codes may communicate with each other. </i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Proto or tak at the beginning of a sentence often signal the requirement to fuse sentences with so or even with an -ing form.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Část filtru byla zničena a do konce roku se filtr nepodařilo zprovoznit. Kotle ve Výtopně Krnov byly proto ve druhém pololetí provozovány pouze na původní mechanické odlučovače.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><i><br />\r\n<em>Part of the filter was destroyed and had not been put back into service by the end of the year so during the second half of the year the boilers at the Krnov Heat Generation Plant were only being operated with their original mechanical separators</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">SIGNPOSTING</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>The words and phrases that simply serve to indicate the order of ideas and their relationship to one another (<em>however, moreover, thus, in any case etc</em>\r\n ) can be the most expendable parts of a translation. <br />\r\n<em>Nejenom ....ale i</em>\r\n <br />\r\nVery often <em>\"not only... but also\"</em>\r\n is too strongly emphatic and a simple <em>\"both...and\"</em>\r\n is more appropriate, or even just a simple <em>\"and\"</em>\r\n . <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><i><br />\r\n<em>Firma X </em>\r\n<strong>ne</strong>\r\n<em> dodává </em>\r\n<strong>jen</strong>\r\n<em> kompletní technologii WaveLAN,</em>\r\n<strong>ale</strong>\r\n<em> je rovně systémový integrátor Lucent Technologies [...] WaveLAN má </em>\r\n<strong>nejen</strong>\r\n<em> řídicí programy pro sí, </em>\r\n<strong>ale</strong>\r\n<em> také diagnostický software, který Vám umoní snadno monitorovat sí WaveLAN</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><i><br />\r\nX supplies complete WaveLAN technology <strong>and</strong>\r\n acts as a Lucent Technologies systems integrator [...] WaveLAN has <strong>both </strong>\r\ncontrolling network programs <strong>and</strong>\r\n diagnostic software to enable you to monitor a WaveLAN network with ease<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><i><br />\r\n<em>This example is from a promotional text where such emphasis might well be desirable, but even here, the repetition of \"not only... but also\" would perhaps sound somewhat bombastic. <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Sometimes some radical retailoring is required:</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p style=\"tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt\"><em><span style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: \" courier=\"\">Předmětem dodávky je kromě hardwaru u vech komponent i potřebný software.<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nVedle instalačního softwaru, síových driverů, diagnostického softwaru jsou to i programy WaveAROUND a WaveMONITOR.</span>\r\n</em>\r\n<i><span style=\"FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: \" courier=\"\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<pre><i><p><font size=\"2\">&nbsp;</font>\r\n</p>\r\n</i>\r\n</pre>\r\n<p><em>Supplies include the hardware for all components and all the software you need: installation software, network drivers, diagnostic software, WaveAROUND and WaveMONITOR.</em>\r\n<i> <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Četina pouívá běněji ne angličtina konektory, které naznačují logický vztah mezi větami. Anglický text, který překládá vechny explicitní konektory, působí ztrnule, pedantický. [Knittlová, ibid]<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Nezanedbatelná je také asymetrie mezi angličtinou a četinou v tom, e četina více ne angličtina vyjadřuje postoj ke sdělované skutečnosti. [Knittlová, ibid]<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>See <a href=\"http://www.bohemica.com/admin/%20http:/www.bohemica.com/?m=catalog&s=14&a=132\">Dominik Luke\'s article on cohesion and connectors</a>\r\n .<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">POSITIVE INTRO TO NEGATIVE FACT BECOMES NEGATIVE INTRO TO POSITIVE FACT</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>Domnivám se, e jí na naem dobrém mínění nezáleí<br />\r\nI dont believe she cares about our good opinion<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Předpokládám, e jsme o moc nepřili<br />\r\nI dont suppose we have missed much<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Zdálo se, e mě neslyel<br />\r\nHe didnt seem to have heard me <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">SUMMARIZING ADJECTIVES</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>Adjectives in English can sometimes merely summarize a relationship with a noun, whereas Czech prefers adjectives which give an explicit description of this relationship. For example the expression <em>\"fast food\" </em>\r\nwould appear comical in literal Czech translation, evoking images of meals that have taken to their heels. <em>\"Churchill-sized cigar\"</em>\r\n would need a longer adjectival clause: <em>\"doutník tak velký jak je kouřil Churchill\"</em>\r\n . Or how about <em>\"She was a jet-setter Seattle lumber heiress.\"</em>\r\n in Czech? <br />\r\nTranslating into English, we should not be afraid to use this resource to summarize and condense.<br />\r\n<em>Mzda, která stačí k ivotu</em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>A living wage</em>\r\n <br />\r\n<em>Jídlo k okamitému podávání </em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>Ready-to-serve food</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Četina nemá tendenci k tak hutnému vyjadřování, jaké je pro mnohem nominálnějí angličtinu přirozené:<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Podle údajů světové zdravotnické organizace první místo mezi vemi přičinami úmrtí zaujímají srdeční choroby.<br />\r\nAccording to WHO statistics, heart diseases are the No. 1 killer. [Knittlová, ibid]<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p style=\"mso-outline-level: 5\"><b><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">TRANSLATION BY ITALICIZING<br />\r\n<p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</i>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><i><br />\r\n<em>It is not normal practice in Czech to intersperse a dialogue with italics to express emphasis. In many genres in English this is quite acceptable. Hanas comment on this is that Czech will express English italicized forms with suffixes, enclitics, diminutives, syntax or full pronomial forms. Hence we might very occasionally venture to render these in English with a change of typeface: <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Bude tak potěen <br />\r\nHe will be pleased</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Moc se nám to líbilo <br />\r\nWe have enjoyed ourselves<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Proč jsi mi nezatelefonovala? Přece jsem ti telefonovala /Ale já jsem ti telefonovala <br />\r\nWhy didnt you phone me? But I did phone you.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>You might like to have a look at a very detailed <a href=\"http://www.phil.muni.cz/angl/DIPLOMKY/1998/slancarova/slancarova.html\">online dissertation</a>\r\n comparing the use of italics in Czech and English.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">SINGULAR AND PLURAL</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>A singular can be used in Czech to denote the multiple occurrence of a single item. Here English usually prefers the plural. <br />\r\n<em>musí být stvrzeno </em>\r\n<strong>podpisem</strong>\r\n<em> oprávněných zástupců</em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>must be confirmed by the </em>\r\n<strong>signatures</strong>\r\n<em> of authorized representatives</em>\r\n <em><p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Pro tyto lázenské hosty bude klient zajiovat pobyty formou objednávky. <strong>Objednávka</strong>\r\n se stává závaznou po potvrzení klienta.<br />\r\nThe client shall arrange for the stays of these spa visitors by placing orders. <strong>Orders</strong>\r\n become binding upon confirmation by the client </i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>Cena za <strong>pobyt </strong>\r\nlázenských hostů byla stanovena dohodou<br />\r\nThe price for the spa visitors <strong>stays</strong>\r\n has been set by agreement<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Můete provést spojení <strong>sami</strong>\r\n a nemusíte čekat na technika...<br />\r\nYou can make a connection <strong>yourself </strong>\r\nand neednt wait for a technician...<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Pro tyto lázenské hosty bude klient zajiovat pobyty formou objednávky. <strong>Objednávka</strong>\r\n se stává závaznou po potvrzení klienta.<br />\r\nThe client shall arrange for the stays of these spa visitors by placing orders. <strong>Orders</strong>\r\n become binding upon confirmation by the client <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><p><i><p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">NOTES ON JOURNALISTIC STYLE</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Even in the most drily objective of Czech journalism, dialogue is often coloured with a rich palette of descriptive communicative verbs: <em>pravil, oznámil, pronesl</em>\r\n and so forth whereas in English-language dialogue, such forms are strongly marked for expressiveness, and it is quite conventional to repeat \"she said\" for entire stretches. I wouldn\'t recommend making a serious report sound like an interview with Peter Rabbit. <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>je třeba poznamenat, je nutno ukázat, nelze přehlédnout, atp<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Anglické ekvivalenty typu \"it should be noted\" apod by byly zcela na místě ve vědeckém pojednání, ale v urnalistickém stylu se dává přednost vyjadřování úspornějímu. [Knittlová, ibid]<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">AND/OR</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><em>\"And\"</em>\r\n<i> needs to be inserted in some lists:<em> <p></p>\r\n</em>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>Cena instalačního materiálu (kabely, vodiče, pomocný materiál)<br />\r\nPrice of installation material (cables, conductors and auxiliary material)</i>\r\n<i><p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><em>\"And\"</em>\r\n<i> needs to replace <em>\"or\"</em>\r\n in some lists<br />\r\n<em>Barokní umění reprezentují malíři jako K. kréta, P. Brandl, J. Kupecký a mistři rokoka, N. Grund či A. Kern. </em>\r\n<br />\r\n<em>Baroque art is represented by the painting of K. kréta, P. Brandl, J. Kupecký and masters of rococo, N. Grund and A. Kern.</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h4 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><i><span style=\"COLOR: #408080\">THE ETHOS OF ENGLISH</span>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h4>\r\n<p><i>In <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1853591912/czechenglishtran\">Paragraphs on Translation</a>\r\n Peter Newmark says, \"the English language, [is] less synthetic and more analytical (i.e. having a smaller grammar and a larger number of idioms)\" and so makes use of idioms more than some other languages do. He thus defends such translations as <em>a poco distanza - a stone\'s throw</em>\r\n or <em>il suo costo altissimo - its staggering cost.</em>\r\n <br />\r\nHana also points out that if she translated every English idiom with an equivalent form, the resulting Czech text might appear unduly conversational and trivial, so she will sometimes render an idiom with a more neutral expression. Conversely then, I feel there is sometimes a good argument for touching up long deadpan passages with a mildly idiomatic turn of phrase or two just to allay that \"seeping oddness\" feeling in a text :<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><em>připravujeme dalí projekty</em>\r\n<i><br />\r\n<em>further projects are in the pipeline</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><em>Celá oblast informačních technologií má vysokou akceleraci</em>\r\n<i><br />\r\n<em>IT as a whole is rapidly gaining ground</em>\r\n <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><i>However, we should exercise great caution in typecasting an entire language as being prone to this or that, relative to another entire language. A relative tendency may well be detected in a given genre, register or situation towards abstractness, information overload, alliterative effects, punning etc but we should be very wary of the mistake that I hope I haven\'t made too often here of ascribing the sins of an individual author to the language as a whole. The whole question of whether you convey or convert is, of course, decided in individual strategies for individual texts.<p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><strong><i>Except in the case of public material which has been included with the authors consent, examples are not authentic texts and are only inspired by real cases. </i>\r\n</strong>\r\n<i><p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<h3 style=\"MARGIN: auto 0cm\"><em>We would welcome comment from translators of Czech and other languages, from interpreters, teachers, students, proofreaders, copy-editors and anybody else involved in Czech-English translation, publication or editing.</em>\r\n<i> <p></p>\r\n</i>\r\n</h3>\r\n<div class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><em><span style=\"FONT-STYLE: normal\"><font size=\"4\"><hr align=\"center\" width=\"100%\" size=\"2\" />\r\n</font>\r\n</span>\r\n</em>\r\n</div>\r\n<p><i><a href=\"mailto:zehrovak@volny.cz\">zehrovak@volny.cz</a>\r\n </i>\r\n</p>\r\n', '2004-05-08 18:28:21', 3874, 258, 105),
(176, 'Czech-English Translation Problems - 1', 'MBC', '', '<p></p>\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n<BODY BGCOLOR=\"#FFFFFF\" TEXT=\"#000000\" LINK=\"#0000FF\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><CENTER><font size=\"3\"><h2><i>Czech-English translation tips</i></h2></font></CENTER>\r\n\r\n\r\n</CENTER><h3><P>It can sometimes be difficult to put your finger on exactly why a translation looks inadequate. A grammatically sound text can still have that indefinable raw feeling about it, where perhaps the translator has not made full use of the expressive resources of English or has missed a nuance in Czech and left the reader in linguistic limbo...</p>\r\n<P>This motley collection of notes addresses such problems and is aimed primarily at readers who already have a basic familiarity with the obvious pitfalls of Czech > English translation. We claim no special authority in presenting this compilation, and we welcome more seasoned or reasoned opinions from others.</p> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><H4><font color=\"#408080\">POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS</font></H4>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>There are many cases where a possessive pronoun needs to be recast in some other form, particularly where it acts as direct object of a verb: \r\n\r\n\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE><EM><p>Tento výčet deseti doporučení není jistě vyčerpávající, ale <STRONG>jejich důsledné uplatnění</STRONG> vdy výrazně změnilo průběh a předevím výsledky pravidelných porad sledovaných manaerských týmů. </BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE><p>This list of ten recommendations is certainly not exhaustive but the course and especially the results of regular meetings in the management teams under observation have always been significantly affected <STRONG>when they have been applied consistently</STRONG>.</EM></BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Mu, který na ni zautočil, tam byl \r\n<br>Her attacker was there.</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P><EM>Dnes obíhá v různých výkách na 550 satelitů. Pravděpodobnost jejich sráky s meteorem stoupá s jejich mnostvím\r\n<BR>Up to 550 satellites are currently orbiting at various heights. The likelihood that one of them may collide with a meteor rises in proportion to their number.\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>designation of the operated track and a description of it,\r\n<BR>označení provozované dráhy a její popis,</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Přehled a popis různých druhů ocúnů, rostoucích ve Střední Asii, doplněný o stručný klíč k jejich určování a uvedení <STRONG>moností jejich pěstování</STRONG> i u nás.\r\n<br>A summary and description of various crocus species growing in Central Asia with a concise classification key for identifying them and an account of the <STRONG>opportunities for growing them</STRONG> here.</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>This problem can sometimes be dealt with by repetition of the subject:\r\n<p><EM>podmínka pro provozovatele dráhy vydat vnitřní předpisy o organizování dráhy, a zajistit jejich dodrování. \r\n<br>a condition for the track operator to issue internal regulations on the organization of the track and to ensure adherence to these regulations</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>In very formal texts, use of <EM>therein, thereon</EM> etc might be considered\r\n<BR><EM>rekonstrukce dráhy nebo její části\r\n<BR>reconstruction of the track or a part thereof.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Touto koncesní listinou se nahrazuje původní výe uvedená koncesní listina včetně jejich změn\r\n<br>This licence deed supersedes the above original licence deed including the amendments contained therein</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><H4><font color=\"#408080\">OVERUSE OF PARTICIPLE FORMS </font></H4>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>English often prefers prepositional phrases to straight participles.\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>It is being reconstructed - it is under reconstruction\r\n<br>The observed team - the team under observation \r\n<br>The considered design - the design in question\r\n<br>The disputed matter - the matter in dispute</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Základem bezpečnosti je sama pouitá technologie\r\n<br>The basis for this security is the actual technique in use</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Tak můeme přidat bezdrátový segment do ji stávající klasické sítě\r\n<br>In this way we can add a wireless segment to a standard network that is already in existence.</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><H4><font color=\"#408080\">POST-POSITION ADJECTIVES</font></H4>\r\n\r\n\r\n<br>The position of an adjective before or after a noun can occasionally change its entire meaning in English. As you drive into Prague, why do the numerous roadside hoardings offering \"free rooms\" strike you as not quite right? Because an adjective before a noun can denote a permanent state and after a noun a temporary state. In this case, a \"free room\" could be a room without charge.\r\n<p>The questions involved are not necessarily involved questions and present shareholders are not forced to be the shareholders present! I have seen the latter mistake in a translation of a company memorandum, changing the entire company voting procedure with a single deftly-placed word.\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Poukazy jsou platné pouze <STRONG>na vyznačenou dobu</STRONG>\r\n<br>Vouchers are only valid <STRONG>for the period indicated</STRONG>.</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><H4><font color=\"#408080\">HYPONYMS AND HYPERONYMS</font></H4>\r\n<P>One of the differences I find between Czech and English in the lexical sphere is that of the greater or lesser degree of specificity used in referring to certain objects, i.e. the use of hyponyms and hyperonyms.\r\n<p>Consider, in your typical French thriller, when a robber enters a bank, bystanders shout, \"Attention, il a un revolver\". Now it would stand out a mile if you translated with the same degree of specificity. Of course, English has a word for revolver, but the more general word \"gun\" would be more appropriate. In Czech, we would be even less specific and shout \"Pozor, má zbráň\", or \"je ozbrojen\". \r\n<P>Likewise, if I need something to write with, in Czech my first reaction would be \"má propísku?\". English can certainly give an exact rendering - \"ball-point pen\" or \"biro\", but I would suggest that in most cases we would use the more general category of \"pen\". \r\n<P>Other examples that come to mind are \"automat\", used for any device from a cash dispenser to a fruit machine, perhaps \"práek\" where Anglophones would perhaps often use the brand-name of the medication (maybe this goes for a lot of other consumer goods too), \"curtains\", where a Czech would differentiate \"závěsy\" and \"záclony\", \"sign\" which covers everything from \"známka\", \"znak\" and \"znamení\" to \"stopa\" and \"odznak\", and then there is the classic case of <A HREF=\"http://www.geocities.com/Athens/Forum/7953/mush.html\">mushrooms and toadstools</A>. Any Czech would take one look at your average indeterminate fungoid growth and tell you exactly what variety of boletus it is. \r\n\r\n\r\n<p>In her excellent work K teorii i praxi překladu [Univerzita Palackého v Olomouci, 2000], Dagmar Knittlová points out that the most frequently occurring English words sometimes need a more specific Czech word, e.g. made of planks  stlučený z prken:\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\"Vcelku lze konstatovat, e české protějky anglických nejfrekventovanějích slovesných skupin jsou sémanticky bohatí, specifičtějí.\"\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Similar points which she raises:\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\"Přídavná jména obsahují někdy navíc v českém protějku jemnějí specifikaci, např. barevný odstín  red, rudý; brown, ořechový; yellow, zaloutlý (rozhodující je kolokace, často je vak volba zcela subjektivní). V některých případech je v českém protějku anglického adjektiva zahrnut výsledek předchozího děje, např. limp, zemdlený; puffy, napuchlý; dirty, zastříkaný.\"\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\"Substituce hyperonymem je ve vztahu angličtiny směrem k četině méně častá. Nejčastěji abstrahuje český protějek od specifického typu pojmenovaného objektu, není-li specifičnost přísluného druhu v četině známá, např. chestnut, katan; rangeland grass, pastviny.\"\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\"Za zvlátní případ specifikace je mono povaovat konkretizací. Přidaná je sloka, která je nositelem konkrétnosti, např. property  pozemek (specifický druh majetku konkretizovaný jako vlastněná půda), equipment  přístroje.\"\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><H4><font color=\"#408080\">THE EXPLICATIVE GENITIVE</font></H4>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>Sometimes you get the feeling that a subject is being explained to death, with a dozen words being used where none would do. In his <EM>The Noun in Translation</EM>, Ale Klégr gives one reason as: \"an interesting difference between Czech and English: the much-favoured construction in Czech, termed the <EM>expicative genitive</EM>, in which the head (a general or generic term) is specified by a narrower, lower-level term (e.g. a hyponym). [In translation, this] tends to be reduced to the modifying element (the narrower term):\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>Hence:\r\n<BR>z dob dětství - from childhood\r\n<BR>dokonávají dílo očisty - completing the great purge\r\n<BR>se stálo záleitostí běné praxe - became a routine procedure\r\n<BR>synonymum slova základ - the synonym for foundation \r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Elsewhere, there is no shame in a little judicious \"translation with the delete button\" if the text is getting bogged down in pleonasms or other excesses.\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Za dobu své existence, <STRONG>a to ji od počátku svého vzniku</STRONG>, firma X přispěla výraznou měrou ke sníení cenových relací\r\n<br>Ever since its very beginnings, X has contributed significantly to price reductions </EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Za tím účelem vydal X firmě Y pověření <STRONG>opravňující</STRONG> firmu zajiovat tyto instalační sluby. \r\n<br>For these purposes X granted Y authorization to provide such installation services </EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>Za tímto účelem <STRONG>realizuje výstavbu</STRONG> provozní laboratoře\r\n<br>For these purposes it is building an operations laboratory </EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>opatření na ochranu ovzduí s cílem <STRONG>zajistit</STRONG> bezpečné splnění zákonných emisních limitů\r\n<br> steps to protect the atmosphere aimed at safely complying with legal emission limits</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<EM><p>garantujeme vekeré nae úsílí <STRONG>směřující k </STRONG>zachování dalí těsné spolupráce\r\n<br>we assure you of all our efforts to maintain further close cooperation </EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P><EM>Odstraňování jiných překáek <STRONG>omezujících</STRONG> provozování drání dopravy\r\n<BR>Removal of other impediments to the operation of the track</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<P><EM>doklad <STRONG>prokazující</STRONG> odbornou způsobilost\r\n<BR>proof of professional competence</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<BR>údaje<STRONG> vztahující se</STRONG> k druhu dopravy a rozsahu slueb poskytovaných <STRONG>v souvislosti</STRONG> s přepravou osob a věcí</EM>\r\n<EM><P>information <STRONG>on</STRONG> the type of transport and the extent of services provided <STRONG>for</STRONG> the transport of persons and things,</EM>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>Additions do sometimes need to be made:\r\n<EM><br>Klient rovně odpovídá za to, e informace, které poskytne agentuře, nebudou zavádějící\r\n<br>The client is also responsible <STRONG>for ensuring</STRONG> that information provided to the agency is not misleading </EM>', '2004-05-08 18:14:04', 13986, 258, 101),
(186, 'Elizabeth Jane Weston: an English poet in Rudolf IIs Prague', 'Susan Reynolds', 'Essay by second prize winner of the 2004 <a href=\"http://www.bcsa.co.uk\">BCSA essay competition</a>\r\n.', '<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Far distant from my native lands,<p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">here in <state />\r\n<place />\r\nBohemia</place />\r\n</state />\r\ns heart<p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Elizabeth Jane Weston: an English poet in Rudolf IIs <city />\r\n<place />\r\nPrague</place />\r\n</city />\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n<b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoHeader\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: 36.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nThe skys inclemency stirs up the angry winds;</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">the watery clouds are soaking with ceaseless rain.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">The turbulent <place />\r\nVltava</place />\r\n, swollen with rainy waves,</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Bursting, impetuous, breaks through its river-banks</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nFor those who remember the floods which devastated <city />\r\n<place />\r\nPrague</place />\r\n</city />\r\n in August 2002, these lines may recall only too vividly the events of that summer. In fact, they were written in response to a similar disaster which occurred more than four hundred years earlier, as their title testifies:  ON THE FLOOD which arose in Prague from continual rain in the year 1596or, to quote the original, De inundatione for this poem, like all the others in the collection where it appeared, was written in Latin, the work of the English maiden, Elizabeth Jane Weston, who, when she witnessed those floods, was just fifteen years old.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nTwo questions immediately suggest themselves: how did this young Englishwoman come to be in Bohemia at that time, and how did she come by a knowledge of Latin which enabled her to write and publish a two-volume collection of verses in that language at a time when, as a letter from Elizabeth Albert of Kameneck to the poet Balthasar Exner declared, learning was considered not only useless for a maidens concern but actually shameful? There was clearly something exceptional not only in her achievements but in her circumstances which enabled her not only to publish her poetry but to move freely and gain recognition among literary circles in Rudolfine Prague. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nIt is only comparatively recently, with the publication of a modern edition of Westons complete writings in 2000, that the facts of her origins and attainments have become accessible. The parish records of the Oxfordshire <placetype />\r\nvillage</placetype />\r\n of <placename />\r\nChipping Norton</placename />\r\n note the marriage on 29 June 1579 of John Wessone and Joane Cowper, followed on 23 July 1580 by the baptism of their son, named after his father, and, in 1581, by that of <city />\r\n<place />\r\nElizabeth</place />\r\n</city />\r\n, daughter of John Weston. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\nWhen, many years later, she wrote a moving elegy in memory of her mother, Elizabeth Weston recalled that when she was barely six months old her father had died; his burial is recorded on 6 May 1582. Her mother, however, did not remain alone for long. Shortly before John Westons funeral Edward Kelley, occultist and scryer to the alchemist Dr. John Dee, claimed to have received messages from the Archangel Michael urging him to marry, which he did, his bride being Westons widow. When the couple accompanied Dee and his wife to <state />\r\n<place />\r\nBohemia</place />\r\n</state />\r\n in 1583 and eventually entered the service of Rudolf II, the Weston children were left behind in the care of their grandmothers. However, before long they too died, and<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nJohn Francis and Elizabeth were taken to join their mother and stepfather in <state />\r\n<place />\r\nBohemia</place />\r\n</state />\r\n. Kelleys brother Thomas had married Ludmilla, the niece of Jindřich of Písnice, deputy to the Imperial Chancellor Zdeněk Vojtěch Lobkovic, and mayor of Most, and when Dee went back to <country-region />\r\n<place />\r\nEngland</place />\r\n</country-region />\r\n in 1589 Kelley and his family stayed behind. He was subsequently raised to the rank of eques aureus or Knight of the Golden Spur by Rudolf, and proudly styled himself of the knightly kin and house called Imaymi in the <placetype />\r\ncounty</placetype />\r\n of <placename />\r\nConaghku</placename />\r\n in the <place />\r\n<placetype />\r\nkingdom</placetype />\r\n of <placename />\r\nIreland</placename />\r\n</place />\r\n. He had already enjoyed the patronage of the last of the Romberks, Peter Vok, who amassed a library of over 10,000 volumes at his court in southern <state />\r\n<place />\r\nBohemia</place />\r\n</state />\r\n and had summoned Dee and Kelley in 1586 to conduct seances at his castle in Třeboň. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\nIn this atmosphere of scholarly patronage Kelley, whom <city />\r\n<place />\r\nElizabeth</place />\r\n</city />\r\n recalled loved her like another father, was able to ensure that both his stepchildren received a first-rate education. In 1588 he employed John Hammond, gentleman, of <placename />\r\nTrinity</placename />\r\n <placetype />\r\nCollege</placetype />\r\n, <city />\r\nCambridge</city />\r\n as a tutor for them; later on <city />\r\n<place />\r\nElizabeth</place />\r\n</city />\r\n would address a poem to this noble and learned gentleman, thanking him for the excellent teaching which he had provided.<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nFor John Francis, this led to matriculation at the German <placetype />\r\nuniversity</placetype />\r\n of <placename />\r\nIngolstadt</placename />\r\n; <city />\r\n<place />\r\nElizabeth</place />\r\n</city />\r\n proved to be a gifted linguist and was attested by several contemporaries to have a fluent command of Czech, English, German, Italian and Latin. This last was the language not only of the universities and the Church but of international diplomacy, and to write in Latin was the surest way to reach a wide audience of humanistic scholars, far more so than writing in one of the less accessible modern languages. It was not, however, merely a stiff formal medium for stilted proclamations; Latin verse of the period throughout Europe abounds in playful, affectionate and witty addresses to friends as well as touching expressions of sorrow on the deaths of close companions. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nElizabeth Weston, or Westonia, as she came to be known, used Latin when writing to her student brother, a refreshingly normal young man despite his brilliance who enjoyed lighter pastimes such as playing the lute (the contemporary students equivalent of the guitar). Sadly, he also recorded problems with his health and consultations with<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nDr. Hollings, the English professor of medicine at <city />\r\n<place />\r\nIngolstadt</place />\r\n</city />\r\n, who suggested that his case required surgical treatment. This was in 1598; two years later he was dead, aged just twenty. </span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\nThe family was already in serious difficulties. The exact date of Kelleys death is uncertain, but his favour with the Emperor had declined after 1591, when he killed a courtier, and he was imprisoned first in Prague on Rudolfs orders, and then in the fortress of Křivoklát and elsewhere. Whatever the truth of the rumours surrounding his death, by 1597 his wife and stepchildren were destitute, with creditors distraining on their remaining property. It was now, with her brother away in <state />\r\nBavaria</state />\r\n, in poor health, and unable to be of much assistance, that <city />\r\n<place />\r\nElizabeth</place />\r\n</city />\r\n seized the initiative and put her talents to good use. She addressed a series of fluent appeals in verse to powerful patrons including Johann Anton Barvitius, Rudolfs Dutch advocate and counsellor, Jindřich of Písnice, and the Emperor himself, claiming that not only Kelleys property but her mothers had been snatched, their possessions auctioned off at deliberately low prices, and the mother and daughter cheated of their just means of support.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nNot surprisingly, she compares herself to Ovid, who, after a scandal whose precise details remain obscure, was banished by the Emperor Augustus to Tomis on the Black Sea and spent the rest of his life bewailing the harsh climate, the uncultivated ways of the local people and his exile from family and friends, and addressing impassioned but fruitless appeals for his recall. <city />\r\n<place />\r\nElizabeth</place />\r\n</city />\r\n was more successful, and was, in any case, certainly not deprived of congenial and literary company. It was the Silesian poet Georg Martinius von Baldhoven, three years her senior, who not only encouraged her to publish but, in 1602, brought out the first collection of her <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Poëmata </i>\r\nin two volumes and wrote tirelessly to literary acquaintances in support of her. Six years later, this collection was followed by a second, <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">ParthenicônLibri III</i>\r\n, which also included some of her correspondence.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\n<city />\r\n<place />\r\nElizabeth</place />\r\n</city />\r\n was not wholly pleased with this publication, claiming that material by other hands had crept in. In any case, the title, A Maidens Writings, was no longer entirely accurate. Her pleas to her patrons had included entreaties for funds to be released to supply a dowry, without which her chances of marriage would be seriously diminished. Book I of <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Poëmata </i>\r\n<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\ncontains verses to Johannes Leo, an imperial lawyer in the service of the Duke of Brunswick who was in charge of several of the delicate negotiations necessary in the final years of Rudolfs reign, including those associated with the invasion of <city />\r\n<place />\r\nPassau</place />\r\n</city />\r\n. She thanks him for gifts, but consents to be bound to him only in such wise as love binds the learned. In any event, in 1603 the couple married, possibly to the consternation of Baldhoven, who hints that he himself had aspired to her hand. The wedding, however, sparked off a cluster of lively poems in congratulation by fellow poets in praise of Leo and his Lioness, and a charming sequel by Balthasar Exner on the birth of their first child, Maria Polyxena, describing the Muses acting as nursemaid to the baby girl and hoping that a brother and eight sisters may follow to represent Apollo and the full complement of Muses.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nIn fact, over the next few years Westonia, equally apt in producing books and babies, gave birth to two more daughters and four sons. Childbearing was, however, a notoriously hazardous business in the seventeenth century, and in November 1612 she died, aged 31, and was buried in the <placetype />\r\nchurch</placetype />\r\n of <placename />\r\nSt. Thomas</placename />\r\n in <city />\r\n<place />\r\nPrague</place />\r\n</city />\r\n. Leo, left to care for three motherless young daughters (none of his sons had survived), did the expedient thing and, after a decent period of mourning, took a new wife, Johanna Seltenschlagius von Fridenfeld, in 1614.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nThe scope of Elizabeth Westons verse is, by any standards, impressive, encompassing not only vivid accounts of personal experiences, devout meditations on religious themes, heartfelt tributes to her mother and brother, and impassioned appeals on the familys behalf but colourful versions of Aesops fables and<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nspirited and witty epigrams that show her as a poet able to hold her own among the international community of poets who frequented Rudolfine Prague, and to command the respect of men as distinguished as Jiří Carolides, the leading Latin poet at court, Sir Stephen Lesieur, James Is ambassador to Rudolfs court, and Sir Edward Dyer, poet and Chancellor of the Order of the Garter, who visited Prague several times. The English Maiden addressed poems not only<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nto the Emperor but to King James I and VI on his accession-- a direct link with a monarch whose daughter, another Elizabeth, would in 1613 would marry Frederick, the Count Palatine, and in 1619 begin her brief reign as Bohemias Winter Queen.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\nIn her own way, Elizabeth Weston similarly bridged the cultural worlds of England and Bohemia; to the end of her life she proudly retained her awareness of her English birth as is clear from the verses which she wrote in August 1610 in a copy of <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Parthenica </i>\r\nnow in the British Library, signing herself Elizabeth Jane, wife of Johannes Leo, Agent in the Imperial Court and Englishwoman of the Weston Family. Queen of <state />\r\n<place />\r\nBohemia</place />\r\n</state />\r\ns learned poets, her short but brilliant career earned her the friendship and admiration of the highest and most erudite in both her countries.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%; TEXT-ALIGN: center\" align=\"center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">BIBLIOGRAPHY<p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\nElizabeth Jane Weston, <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Collected Writings</i>\r\n, edited and translated by Donald Cheney and Brenda M. Hosington (<place />\r\n<placetype />\r\nUniversity</placetype />\r\n of <placename />\r\nToronto Press</placename />\r\n</place />\r\n, 2000).</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Binns, J. W. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Intellectual Culture in Elizabethan and Jacobean <country-region />\r\nEngland</country-region />\r\n: The Latin Writings of the Age </i>\r\n(<place />\r\nLeeds</place />\r\n, 1990).</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Evans, R.J.W. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Rudolf II and his World: A Study in Intellectual History 1576-1612 </i>\r\n<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\n(Oxford, 1973; revised ed. 1984).</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Fučiková, E. (ed.) <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Prag um 1600 </i>\r\n(Freren/Emsland, 1988).</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Kolář, A. Humanistická basniřká Vestonia in: <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Sborník filozofické fakulty Univerzity Komenského v Bratislavě</i>\r\n 4, 40 (2) (<city />\r\n<place />\r\nBratislava</place />\r\n</city />\r\n, 1926), pp.16-19.</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Pelcl, F. M. <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">Abbildungen Böhmischer und Mährischer Gelehrten und Künstler</i>\r\n (Prag, 1777).</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 9pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%; mso-bidi-font-size: 10.0pt\"></span>\r\n</p>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-GB\"><p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"FONT-SIZE: 9pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 200%; mso-bidi-font-size: 10.0pt\">Š Susan Halstead 2004<p></p>\r\n</span>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n', '2005-02-12 14:52:51', 1860, 28, 100);

