(94, '', '<P><STRONG>Questions to guide the reading and discussion of Holý, Chapter 1: Nation against state, pp. 19  54. </STRONG></P>\r\n<P>\r\n<HR id=null>\r\n</P>\r\n<OL style=\"MARGIN-TOP: 0cm\" type=1>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: list 36.0pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo17\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">On p. 19 Hol</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ý </SPAN><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">talks of the separation of the public and private domains of life hitherto unprecedented in modern society and compares communist Czechoslovakia to paralyzed society. Can he reconcile that and his statements on p. 20 with the Marxist dictum that family is at the core of the state? Can further examples supporting or refuting his assertions be found (using examples from many countries)? Furthermore, can the following quote be taken at sociological face value? The omnipresent lie of the state has a devastating effect on morality in general. It establishes the norm of a lie being rewarded rather than punished. (p. 23) <?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\" /><o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: list 36.0pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo17\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">On pages 21 to 27, Hol</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ý </SPAN><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">describes certain societal and workforce abnormalities typical of the communist Czechoslovakia. Identify the assumptions he makes about the normal situation and compare them with real examples of current discourse on similar topics in todays English speaking corporate world (e.g. <A href=\"http://www.dilbert.com\">Dilbert </A>cartoons, <A href=\"http://www.house.gov/house/Contract/CONTRACT.html\">Contract With America</A> in the US or <A href=\"http://www.countryside-alliance.org\">Countryside Alliance</A> in the UK).<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: list 36.0pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo17\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Hol</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ý</SPAN><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> says: Not only are political actions for the most part symbolic but symbolic actions become political ones. (p. 33) How does he use the word symbolic? Is his explanation of the lack of popular support for political dissent in line with this statement?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: list 36.0pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo17\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Czechs conceptualize their nation as a natural entity that has existed for at least a millennium. (p. 38) Is this different from other nations? Can equivalent symbols of the magnitude of Praha, </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Sv. Václav, Jan Hus or Bílá Hora </SPAN><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">be found in other cultures (such as the recent Great Britons campaign by the BBC)? Is what Ho</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>lý</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> </SPAN><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">calls the polysemic we unique to the Czech construction of the national self?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: list 36.0pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo17\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">How does The truth prevails and demonstrators demands for that freedom which is the basic attribute of a person and a condition of ones humanity (p. 53) accord with the assumption that Czechs view their nation as a linguistic and cultural rather than a political entity(p. 49)? Is the assumption that material betterment was at the heart of the opposition to communism necessarily in contrast with the nationalist explanation of the phenomena?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: list 36.0pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo17\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Carol Leff Skalnik, 1993, Czech and Slovak Republics: Nation Against State (p. 28): Czechs have tended, from the inception of the state in 1918 to fuse their national identity with the larger identity of the state itself--Czechoslovakia. While this identification with the state might be perceived by Slovaks as the arrogance of the larger nation equating its own interests with <I>raison d\'état</I>, it is nevertheless true that it is hard to find a pattern of Czech national self-assertion comparable to that of the Slovaks: no Czech demands for a separate Czech Republic, and little sense that both Czechs and Slovaks would benefit from devolving additional power to the two individual republics so as to give freer expression to national identity and interest. a) How can this statement be reconciled with Hol</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ýs view</SPAN><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> that Czechs see the nation and the state as two separate entities? b) How is this comparable to the attitudes of other dominant nations in multinational settings. What other instances of dominant behavior similar to that of the Czechs can be found?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; tab-stops: list 36.0pt; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo17\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Create a question the answer to which will best summarize your understanding of the texts.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI></OL>', 183, 0, 100),
(95, '', '<hr>\r\nMůete si vybrat z několika moných básní. Snail jsem se vybrat různé typy básní od současných básníků píících různými styly.<hr>', 184, 0, 10),
(96, '', '<H3>Odpovězte na tyto otázky:</H3>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>Jaké je vhodné oblečení pro různé příleitosti?</LI>\r\n<LI>Je to, co nosíte dnes, stejné jako to, co nosili vai rodiče nebo prarodiče ve vaem věku? </LI>\r\n<LI>Změnila se role oblečení ve společnosti? Jak?</LI>\r\n<LI>Jak lidé pouívají oblečení, aby na sebe upoutali pozornost?</LI>\r\n<LI>Proč je dnes tak oblíbené značkové oblečení? Je to nový jev?</LI>\r\n<LI>Zajímají se dnes eny o módu víc ne mui?</LI></UL>\r\n<P>Vyberte si jednu z otázek a napite krátký esej. Ostatní si připravte ústně. Napite seznam slovíček a frází k módě a oblékání.&nbsp;</P>', 185, 0, 100),
(97, '', '<H3>Monday</H3>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>Doing things at the moment (meaning and conjugation of verbs)</LI>\r\n<LI>The two meanings of imperfective verbs (doing things now vs. doing things regularly</LI>\r\n<LI>Verbs <EM>znát </EM>(<EM>koho, co</EM>), <EM>vědět</EM> (, e; , kde; ,co; , kdo; , kdy; , kam; ,proč), <EM>umět</EM> (<EM>něco dělat</EM>) (Textbook pp. 36-37)</LI>\r\n<LI>Asking questions (Textbook p. 37)</LI></UL>\r\n\r\n<h3>Tuesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of making questions\r\n<li>Review of verbs, conjugations\r\n<li>Review of accusative (adding rules for Masc. ending in -a, and -CeC)\r\n<li>Listening to Unit 3 introductory dialogue and answering questions\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Wednesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review and translation of dialogue in Lekce 3: new words, phrases: <i>dost, docela, chtěl/a bych, <b>ně</b>jaký, <b>ně</b>co, <b>ně</b>kdo, <b>ně</b>kde, <b>ně</b>kdy</i>\r\n<li>Numbers 1 - 10 - exchanging telephone numbers, matriculation numbers and card license plate numbers\r\n<li>Czech children\'s song 1,2,3,4,5 (<a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/readings/sounds/jednadvetristyrypet.rm>Listen</a>)\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Thursday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review numbers: Answer <i>Kolik je hodin?</i>\r\n<li>Add teens (<b>-náct</b>) and tens (dva-, tři-, čtyři- <b>-cet</b>, pa-, e-, sedm-, osm-, deva- <b>-desát</b>)\r\n<li>Practice dialogue and shopping phrases on s. 43 (focus on correct intonation of questions\r\n<li>Learn to use weight measures: <i>100g = 10 deka, 200g = 20 deka, 300g = 30 deka, 500g = půl kila, 1 kg = kilo, 2 kg = 2 kila</i>\r\n<li>Create own dialogue using real Czech store booklets\r\n</ul>\r\n', 186, 0, 100),
(98, '', '<H3>Monday</H3>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>Workbook p. 14-15</LI>\r\n<LI>All exercises for Units 1 and 2 should be ready by Wednesday (books will be collected)</LI></UL>\r\n<h3>Tuesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Complete all exercises in Units 1 and 2 (including progress test at the end of Unit 2)\r\n<li>Answer questions about introductory dialogue in Unit 3, translate text\r\n<li>Learn numbers 1 - 10 from textbook\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Wednesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Use charts in textbook to learn the rules for plural\r\n<li>Using these rules do these exercises Workbook p. 17: 1, 2\r\n<li>Practice numbers 1-10\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Thursday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Practice plurals: Učebnice stana: 46-47, cvičení: 6, 7\r\n<li>Practice numbers: do listening and spelling exercise assigned to you in <a href=http://www.slavonic.arts.gla.ac.uk/czech/tuit/index.php>Online Coursework</a>.\r\n<li><i>Kolik stojí jídlo v Čechách?</i> Find out how much foods listed in Učebnice on s. 52 cost using food websites: <a href=http://www.multimarket.cz>Multimarket.cz</a> and <a href=http://www.carrefour.cz>Carrefour.cz</li>\r\n\r\n</ul>', 187, 0, 100),
(104, '', '<h3>Monday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>How much do foods cost? - Kolik to stojí? (numbers over 100, listening)\r\n<li>What do you buy in the supermarket every week? - Co kupujete v supermarketu kaý týden? (review of plural)\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Tuesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Reading and listening to text about shopping for gifts \r\n<li>Introducing possessive pronoun <i>svůj</i> p. 49, ex. 1\r\n<li>Pronouns as direct objects and following certain prepositions, p. 51, ex. 5</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Wednesday</h3>\r\nClass in computer lab.\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review numbers - listening exercise (<a href=http://www.slavonic.arts.gla.ac.uk/czech/tuit target=new>log in</a>)\r\n<li>Review food stuffs \r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Hrajeme PEXESO (<a target=new href=czechonline/games/foodpexeso>Click here to play!</a>)\r\n<li>Kolik stojí jídlo v českém supermarketu? (Compare <a target=new href=http://www.multimarket.cz>Multimarket.cz</a> and <a target=new href=http://www.carrefour.cz>Carrefour.cz</a>.)\r\n</ul>\r\n<li>Review of prepositions and pronouns (<a href=http://www.slavonic.arts.gla.ac.uk/czech/tuit target=new>log in</a> to do exercise).\r\n</ul>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Thursday</h3>\r\nReview of all new grammar in Lekce 3.\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Creating and answering questions about text on p. 47\r\n<li>Reviewing prepositions (p. 47, ex. 7)<br>\r\n<P><EM>BÝT KDE? na</EM> (<STRONG>on</STRONG>, in, at), <EM>v</EM> (<STRONG>in</STRONG>, at)<BR><EM>JÍT KAM? na </EM>(<STRONG>on</STRONG>to, into), <EM>do</EM> (<STRONG>in</STRONG>to)</P>\r\n<P><EM>FOR</EM>: <EM>pro</EM> (for a person), <EM>za</EM> (for a cost), <EM>na</EM> (for a purpose)</P>\r\n<P>Banány stojí 20 Kč za kilo.<BR>Kupuju auto za 20 000 Kč.<BR>Jdu na oběd. Jdu do restaurace na kávu. Potřebuju ampon na vlasy. <BR>Kukuju knihu pro kamaráda. Jdu do obchodu pro mléko a pro chleba.</P>\r\n<P><STRONG>Prepositions in context:<BR></STRONG><STRONG>na</STRONG> - těit se na, dívat se na<BR><STRONG>o</STRONG> - zajímat se o</P>\r\n\r\n<li>Reflexive verbs with <i>si</i> and <i>se</i><br>\r\n<B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Verbs with SE and SI</P>\r\n<P></B></FONT>\r\n<TABLE border=1 cellPadding=9 cellSpacing=1 width=738>\r\n<TBODY>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"50%\"><B><FONT face=Arial size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>SE</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"50%\"><B><FONT face=Arial size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>SI</B></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"50%\"><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>mýt se (kdy)<BR>dívat se (na co)<BR>dívat se (na co)<BR></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>těit se (na co</FONT><FONT size=3>)<BR>mít se (jak)<BR>jmenovat se (jak)<BR>zajímat se (o co)<BR>ptát se (koho na co)<BR></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>učit se (co</FONT><FONT size=3>)<BR>líbit se (kdo)</FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"50%\"><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>mýt si (co)<BR>dát si (co)<BR>vzít si (co)<BR></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>půjčit si</FONT><FONT size=3> (co)</FONT></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><B><FONT size=3></P>\r\n<P align=justify>Word order  SE,</FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3> SI, HO, JI, MĚ are always in the second position in the sentence</P>\r\n<P></B></FONT>\r\n<TABLE border=1 cellPadding=9 cellSpacing=1 width=738>\r\n<TBODY>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"43%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=right>Jmenuju<BR>Já<BR>Jak<BR>Na co</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"11%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=center>se</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"46%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Petr.<BR>jmenuju Petr.<BR>jmenuje?<BR>Karel dívá?</B></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"43%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=right>Petr a Jana</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"11%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=center>si</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"46%\"><B><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>chtějí koupit auto.</B></FONT></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><B><FONT size=3></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<TABLE border=1 cellPadding=9 cellSpacing=1 width=738>\r\n<TBODY>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"43%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=right>Petr </B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"11%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=center>má rád</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"46%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Janu.</B></FONT></P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"43%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=right>Petr</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"11%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=center>ji</B></FONT></P></TD>\r\n<TD vAlign=top width=\"46%\"><B><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>má rád.</B></FONT></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></P></B></FONT>\r\n<ul>', 192, 0, 100),
(105, '', '<h3>Monday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Practice numbers\r\n<li>Practive plural of nouns and adjectives (Textbook p. 45-46, ex. 1, 2, 3, 4\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Tuesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Direct object of pronouns: Workbook p. 17, 18 ex. 3 - 5, Textbook p. 51, ex. 4\r\n<li>Reflexive possessive <i>svůj</i>: Workbook p. 18, ex. 6, Textbook, p. 49. ex, 2\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Wednesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Complete prepositions: Textbook p. 47, ex. 7\r\n<li>Make questions about the text: Textbook p. 47\r\n<li>Complete appropriate forms of question words: p. 18, ex. 7\r\n<li>Add type of product to store: Workbook p. 20, ex. 14\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Thursday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Workbook, p. 19-20, ex. 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13\r\n<li><a href=http://www.slavonic.arts.gla.ac.uk/czech/tuit>Online exercises</a> reviewing prepositions (incl. difference between kde and kam), pronouns, and se/si.\r\n</ul>', 193, 0, 100),
(106, '', '<h3>Assignment description</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Prepare a 5-minute oral presentation for the rest of the class on the most interesting trip abroad you\'ve ever had. \r\n<li>Prepare 3-4 focus questions for colleagues to answer at the end of the presentation. \r\n<li>Also prepare a list of words with English translations to give to your listeners. \r\n<li>The presentations will be recorded on tape and reviewed later.\r\n<li>Your presentation should answer the following questions in one way or another but need not follow any particular sequence:\r\n<uL>\r\n<li>Proč jsem se rozhodl(a) na svou cestu jet?\r\n<li>Co jsem si musel(a) na cestu připravit?\r\n<li>Co mě na cestě nejvíc překvapilo. (Příjemně i nepříjemně).\r\n<li>Co jsem si z cesty přivezl(a) zpátky?\r\n<li>Co jsem po návratu nejčastěji vyprávěl(a) přátelům.\r\n<li>Proč bych jel(a)/nejel(a) na stejnou cestu znovu?\r\n<li>Co bych napodruhé udělal(a) jinak?\r\n</uL>', 194, 0, 100),
(107, '', '<H3>Slovíčka a fráze k tématu <EM>Móda</EM></H3>\r\n<H4>Adjektiva</H4>\r\n<P>formální oblečení je často nepohodlné<BR>sportovní oblečení je pohodlné<BR>domácí oblečení je pohodlnějí <STRONG>ne </STRONG>oblečení do koly<BR>módní není moderní<BR>tradiční oblečení je kroj</P>\r\n<H4>Slovesa</H4>\r\n<P>mít na sobě (něco) - <EM>Co má dnes na sobě?</EM><BR>vzít si na sebe (něco) - <EM>Petr si dnes vzal (na sebe) bundu.</EM><BR>nosit (něco) - <EM>Do koly nosíme čisté oblečení.</EM><BR>být oblečený (nějak) - <EM>Jak je dnes Jana oblečená?</EM><BR>oblékat se (nějak) - <EM>Jak se oblékají lidé v moderní době?</EM></P>\r\n<H4>Příleitosti a typy oblečení</H4>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>Sportovní oblečení</LI>\r\n<LI>Oblečení do koly</LI>\r\n<LI>Oblečení pro formální příleitosti (večerní oblečení)</LI>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>do divadla \r\n<LI>do restaurace \r\n<LI>na večírek \r\n<LI>na banket \r\n<LI>na promoci \r\n<LI>na pohovor </LI></UL></UL>\r\n<H4>Sportovní oblečení</H4>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>adidasky, tenisky, botasky</LI>\r\n<LI>tepláky</LI>\r\n<LI>bunda</LI>\r\n<LI>ortky</LI>\r\n<LI>tričko</LI></UL>\r\n<H4>kolní oblečení</H4>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>díny, kalhoty</LI>\r\n<LI>svetr</LI>\r\n<LI>kabát, bunda</LI>\r\n<LI>čepice</LI>\r\n<LI>detník, plátěnka</LI></UL>\r\n<H4>Večerní oblečení</H4>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI><STRONG>aty</STRONG></LI>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>blůza</LI>\r\n<LI>sukně</LI>\r\n<LI>lodičky nebo boty na podpadku</LI></UL>\r\n<LI><STRONG>perky </STRONG>ze zlata nebo ze stříbra</LI>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>prsten</LI>\r\n<LI>náunice</LI>\r\n<LI>náhrdelník nebo řetízek</LI>\r\n<LI>náramek</LI>\r\n<LI>hodinky</LI></UL>\r\n<LI><STRONG>oblek</STRONG> (smoking, frak)</LI>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>sako</LI>\r\n<LI>vestička</LI>\r\n<LI>koile</LI>\r\n<LI>kravata / motýlek</LI>\r\n<LI>klobouk (cylindr, buřinka)</LI></UL></UL>', 185, 0, 110);
 of the conduct of other Czechs they know, or of their perception of the differences in behaviour and attitudes between Czechs and non-Czechs.  As peoples experiences differ, so too do their perceptions of the typical Czech character.\" Is this knowledge \"cognitively different\" (p. 80) from knowledge of national traditions? </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>\"The official communist ideology emphasized the socialist character of Czechoslovak society. Any characteristics which might have been perceived as typical of Czechs were seen as unimportant.\" (p. 73) Read Josef Kainars </FONT><I><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>Český sen</I></FONT><FONT size=3> (Vybrané spisy, sv. 2, pp. 107-119, 154-164) and discuss this statement. Also compare Holýs own description of the co-opting of the Hussite symbolism for the purposes of international propaganda. </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>\"When Czechs talk about their traditions, they do not mean presumably time-honoured but in fact newly-invented customs  such as the various public ceremonies.\" (p. 76) Read Hobsbawns original article (in Hutchinson and Smith, Nationalism, pp. 76  82) and compare the invented traditions of the Czech state with his examples. </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Compare the contradictions associated with the concept of <I>kulturnost</I> as described by Holý with those implicit in <I>The Grandmother: A modest idyll</I></FONT> by Josef Jedlička (<A href=\"http://www.arts.gla.ac.uk/Slavonic/NemcovaJedl.html\">http://www.arts.gla.ac.uk/ Slavonic/NemcovaJedl.html</A>). How does the idea of an idyll fit into Holýs <FONT size=3>approach? How could it be further enhanced by reference to Isaacs basic group identity? </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Metaphor could be described as a projection from one domain of experience to another in order to make some sort of sense of the target domain based on what we know of the source domain. The projections are never complete but rather select a particular aspect of each domain. (cf. Lakoff 1992 - <A href=\"http://www.ac.wwu.edu/~market/semiotic/lkof_met.html\">http://www.ac.wwu.edu/~market/semiotic/lkof_met.html</A>) If the source domain is \"culture and nature\" (p. 103) and target domain are the Czech images of Slovaks, what are the mappings? \"People make sense of the political process mainly through its symbols\" (p. 100, paraphrase of Kertzer 1988). How can such metaphoric projections explain this process? </LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Create a question the answer to which will best summarize your understanding of the texts.</LI>\r\n<P></P></OL>\r\n<P align=justify></P></FONT>', 197, 0, 100),
(111, '', '<UL><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<LI>Zjistěte základní statistiky o obou městech a srovnejte:</LI></UL>\r\n<UL></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Velikost/Rozloha</LI>\r\n<P></P></FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Počet obyvatel</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Dějiny</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Veřejnou dopravu</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Sluby atd.</LI>\r\n<P></P></UL>\r\n<UL>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Srovnejte kvalitu ivota pro stálé obyvatele v&nbsp;obou městech</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Srovnejte kvalitu ivota pro krátkodobé návtěvníky</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Popite jeden zajímavý záitek v&nbsp;jednom městě. Mohlo by se to samé stát i v&nbsp;tom druhém? (200&nbsp;slov)</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Srovnejte reputaci obou měst a rozhodněte jestli je zaslouená nebo nezaslouená</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Pouijte věty jako jsou: <I>X je větí ne Y o kolik/kolikrát; v&nbsp;X je ., zatímco v&nbsp;Y je; ve srovnání s&nbsp;X má Y ; co se týče Z, je X  ne Y</LI>\r\n<P></P></UL></I>\r\n<P>Pouijte adverbia jako jsou: <I>daleko, nesrovnatelně, nepoměrně, mnohem, X-krát, bezkonkurenčně, o trochu, neznatelně,</I></FONT><FONT size=3> </P></FONT>', 198, 0, 100),
(112, '', '<h3>Monday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of grammar so far in preparation for progress test<br>\r\nExercises in Lekce 3, p. 50-51, ex. 3, 4, 5\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Tuesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of numbers (differences between Czech and Russian)\r\n<li>Reading and translating a text (Textbook p. 56)\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Scanning and reading for gist\r\n<li>Practicing pronunciation (reading out loud without eyes on paper)\r\n<li>Translation\r\n</ul>\r\n<li>Differences between <i>sem/tady, na, v, do</i> (introduced last week)\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Wednesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Progress test 2\r\n<li>Asking for and giving directions (na/v) - Kde je kola?, Musíte jít první/druhou/třetí ulicí doprava\r\n</ul>\r\n<h3>Thursday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of prepositions: Textbook, p. 61, ex. 3, 4\r\n<li><i>pů</i>jdu, <i>po</i>jedu - future tense of the verbs jít and jet\r\n<li>jít/jet + infinitive/noun and preposition: půjdu hrát tenis, půjdu na kávu\r\n</ul>', 199, 0, 100),
(113, '', '<h3>Monday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>All exercises up to the end of Unit 3\r\n<li>Online exercises practicing certain features\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Tuesday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Workbook, p. 22, 23 ex. 2, 3\r\n<li>Study for progress test\r\n</ul>\r\nNo homework on Wednesday.\r\n<h3>Thursday</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Complete exercises started in class in Textbook\r\n<li>Workbook, p. 22, ex. 1\r\n<li>Write what you will be doing tomorrow/at the weekend (five sentences each)<br><i>Co budete dělat zítra?/Co budete dělat o víkendu?</i><br> Use sentences like: <i>Zítra půjdu hrát tenis. O víkendu chci jít do muzea. A potom se chci učit.<i>\r\n</ul>', 200, 0, 100),
(114, '', '<h3>Zadání</h3>\r\nVaím úkolem je podívat se na jedno vydání českých televizních novin a jedno vydání jejich anglického ekvivalentu a vypsat:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Alespoň 10 typických novinářských frází v kadém jazyce (nemusí to být fráze ekvivalentní) - například spojovací fráze, úvodní věty, typické obraty\r\n<li>Alespoň 20 slovíček k tématu mezinárodních vztahů (např. kolize, summit), které se v tomto pořadu objeví.\r\n</ul>\r\nV knihovně jazykového centra najdete videokazetu s nahrávkou jedněch českých a jedněch anglických zpráv (poádejte knihovnici-na kazetě je napsáno Czech News/English News/L4 Translation Class). Pokud mono mi své seznamy polete emailem.<p>\r\n<b><i>V hodině pak budeme za pomoci společného seznamu frází a slovíček překládat na ivo anglické zprávy (jiné ne na trénovací kazetě)</i></b>\r\n<hr>\r\n<b>Zprávy najdete taky online například na těchto adresách:</b>', 201, 0, 100),
(115, '', '<B><FONT face=Arial><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Reading assignments to guide the reading and discussion of: Isaacs chap. 9, 10 pp. 171-219; </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Hobsbawn </SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">in Nationalism by Hutchinson and Smith </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>pp.</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">76  82;</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS> Hroch </SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">in Becoming National: A Reader edited by G. Eley and R. G. Suny 1996, pp. 60  77</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>, Krejčí </SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">and </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Malinovský </SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">in Ethnicity edited by Hutchinson and Smith, pp. 209 - 220</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>, Milton Bennet recording  </SPAN> <SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">of lecture </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Beyond Tolerance</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">: Intercultural Communication in a Multicultural Society presented at TESOL 1995<?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\" /><o:p></o:p></SPAN></FONT></B> \r\n<OL style=\"MARGIN-TOP: 0cm\" type=1>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Basic group identity comes into view most often dressed in national colors (p. 171) Does this mean that all things national are elements of basic group identity? Or in other words are there elements of nationhood which are <I>not </I>associated with basic group identity?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Compare Ho</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>lý</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">s discussion of </SPAN><I><SPAN lang=CS>odrodilec</SPAN></I><I><SPAN lang=CS style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> </SPAN></I><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">with </SPAN><?xml:namespace prefix = st2 ns = \"urn:schemas:contacts\" /><st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Isaacs</SPAN></st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> description of the different national identities (French, Japanese, American, Israeli). Could a similar notion develop in those cultures? What might be its equivalent? <o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Compare </SPAN><st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Isaacs</SPAN></st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> outlook with that of </SPAN><?xml:namespace prefix = st1 ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags\" /><st1:PersonName><st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Milton</SPAN></st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> </SPAN><st2:Sn><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Bennet</SPAN></st2:Sn></st1:PersonName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">. Focus in particular on the interplay between the notion of melting pot as opposed to pluralism? Identify them as two poles on a moral outlook and put them in the context of your own personal convictions.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">the word nation has been used interchangeably with tribe, people, ethnic group, race, religion, nationality, country, and state. (p. 175) How has it been used by </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Holý</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> </SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">and other authors so far? How would </SPAN><st2:Sn><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Isaacs</SPAN></st2:Sn><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> solve their dilemmas (choose at least two)? <o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Can Hobsbawns paradox between nation as an innovation and nation as a collective claim to ancient commonalities be identified in elements of current Czech identity as described so far? How could making this explicit elucidate </SPAN><st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Isaacs</SPAN></st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> paradox of concurrent globalization and fragmentation? (Analyze a specific example.) How can </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Krejčí and Velímský</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">s survey further support these conclusions?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">How do Hrochs structural phases of national movement and the consequent four types of national development apply to the current discussion of Czech identity as presented by Hol</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ý </SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">so far (particularly with respect to the role of history in national identity)? Does their application to actual nations arrive at a similar division of the European national space as </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Krejčí and Velímský</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">s more formal (quantitative) analysis? How could it be compared to </SPAN><st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Isaacs</SPAN></st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> more intuitive approach?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">How could be Krej</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>čí and Velímský</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">s assumption of the presence of national consciousness in all cases of well-established nations refined by </SPAN><st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Isaacs</SPAN></st2:GivenName><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> concept of basic group identity? How could it, on the other hand, refine Hol</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ý</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">s (and Hrochs) distinctions between types of national identities (i.e. Western vs. </SPAN><st1:place><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Central Europe</SPAN></st1:place><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> in Hol</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ý </SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">and four types of national movement in </SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>Hroch</SPAN><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">)? What role does the cultured elite capable of articulating national claims (p. 209) play in the different conceptions of nationality surveyed so far?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI>\r\n<LI class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt\"><SPAN lang=EN-US style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">What in the readings was new, what was surprising and what provoked disagreement? What was not clear? What could be said to be the common theme of all this weeks readings?<o:p></o:p></SPAN></LI></OL>', 202, 0, 100),
(124, '', '<B><FONT face=Arial size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>Questions to guide the reading and discussion of Holý, Chapter 4: National traditions and the imagining of a nation, and B</FONT><FONT face=Arial size=3>ělohradský 2000 Interview</P>\r\n<OL></B></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>How could the excerpt below be seen as the expression of current Czech nationalist feelings? What are its other representations mentioned by Holý? To what degree could the same be said about the feelings of any nation  Austrians, Italians, English (with minor modifications) and even to some extent Americans? Discuss in relation to Holýs \"The history of the Czech nation  is a history of rewriting history.\" (p. 118)</LI>\r\n<P></P></FONT><FONT size=1>\r\n<P align=justify>Seton-Watson: p. 157 \"The long history of the Czechs shows more tragedy than happiness, more fanaticism than measured judgement. The Czechs have shown more talent for music than for politics, for rhetoric than for action. Yet they have been a complex and contradictory people. To foreign visitors the modern Czechs appeared kindly reliable and rather prosaic; but important episodes in their past contradict that impression.\"  \"Nature has been kind to them, but man has not. Bohemia is a land washed for longer by deeper streams of native and foreign blood than almost any other; restored again and again by patient toil; adorned by some of the noblest buildings man has raised, the birthplace of much of his greatest music; often threatened, never secure; the very heart of Europe.\"</P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Compare these two examples of British national discourse with what Holý describes as the two Czech views of history. What are the similarities and what are the differences between the two nations use of symbols and generalizations to express feelings on national identity?</LI>\r\n<P></P></FONT><FONT size=1>\r\n<P align=justify>\"For this reason, it was the most natural thing in the world that this part of London should emerge as the place in Britain to try new approaches to the policing of drugs. A sensible society might have conceded that the war on drugs was not proceeding well, and waited to see what the results of Lambeth\'s year-long experiment might be.</P>\r\n<P align=justify>But we\'re not a sensible society. Instead we\'re an entrenched society, hopelessly embroiled in adversarial debate, in thrall to a media we claim to despise and hooked on sensational disclosure about the private lives of people we don\'t know from Adam. Mr Paddick\'s Brixton experiment need not have offered buckets of all of these undesirable, but much coveted components, but, as the story developed, it did.</P>\r\n<P align=justify>Since much of the British press is nastily reactionary, it doesn\'t take a genius to work out that a softening in drugs policing introduced by a gay officer would always be under suspicion.\" <I>Debora Orr, Oct 11, 2002, Independent </P></I>\r\n<P align=justify>\"Lord Nelson, Please forgive us as a nation, for we have forgotten you. We are nation that values Royal misfit media junkies and millionaire hippies, more than valour, duty, courage and compassion, only a few of the qualities that you possessed. I salute you Lord Nelson but England I\'m afraid, no longer expects.\"</P>\r\n<P align=justify>\"Churchill won and rightly so! An all rounder who fought the Boers, Mahdi and in the Great War, who crushed the IRA, who broke the general strike and won WW2, truly the greatest of Britons. Sorry that Cromwell didn\'t do better though.\" <I>Views expressed on the message boards of the BBC Great Britons web site </I></FONT><A href=\"http://www.bbc.co.uk/greatbritons\"><I><FONT size=1>http://www.bbc.co.uk/greatbritons</I></FONT></A><FONT size=1> </P></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Holý makes certain statements about the Czech view of history based on popular historical figures (p. 126, 135). \"References to history in popular discourse often take the form of a simple listing of the names of particular persons.\" (p. 26). Can his claims of Czech uniqueness be substantiated by a comparison with other nations lists. Compare the lists below. What conclusions can we draw from them?</LI>\r\n<P></P></FONT><FONT size=1>\r\n<P align=justify>\"Over the centuries, Scotland has produced many of the worlds greatest scientists, historians, doctors, musicians, inventors and actors.\" Suggested top 10: Robert Burns, Alexander Fleming, John Muir, William Wallace, John Logie Baird, Billy Connelly, Sean Connery, Alexander Graham Bell, David Hume, Robert Luis Stevenson</FONT><FONT size=1> </FONT><A href=\"http://www.thescotsman.co.uk/index.cfm?id=1155872002\"><FONT size=1>http://www.thescotsman.co.uk/index.cfm?id=1155872002</FONT></A></P><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify></FONT><A href=\"http://www.bbc.co.uk/greatbritons\"><FONT size=1>http://www.bbc.co.uk/greatbritons</FONT></A></P><FONT size=1>\r\n<P align=justify>Gallery of Czech heroes and villains: </FONT><A href=\"http://web.isp.cz/jcrane/Czech_hero.html\"><FONT size=1>http://web.isp.cz/jcrane/Czech_hero.html</FONT></A></P><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Holý cites Pynsent and Macura to say: \"The Czechs celebrate their suffering rather than their victories and the heroes of the Czech nation are its martyrs.\" He also mentions the fact that no Czech equivalent of Nelson or Napoleon can be found. How does this fit in with what Masaryk thought of the Slavs? What other Czech heroic figures could added to </FONT><FONT face=\"Times New Roman\" size=3>ika to </FONT><FONT size=3>enrich our understanding of the little Czech/great Czech nation dichotomy?</LI>\r\n<P></P></FONT><FONT size=1>\r\n<P align=justify>\"This national philosophy of the Slavic nations is finally a moral philosophy, a philosophy of history. It is an attempt to find the meaning of history, and therefore it has to have a moral and social orientation.\" <I>Masaryk 1902 Chapters on \'Slavonic National Thought\' p. 156</P></I></FONT><FONT size=3>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>Read <A href=\"http://www.ce-review.org/00/20/interview20_belohradsky.html\">Central Europe Onlines interview with </A></FONT><A href=\"http://www.ce-review.org/00/20/interview20_belohradsky.html\">Václav Bělohradský</A><FONT size=3>. Where does he agree with Holý? Where does he engage in an academic discussion and where is he just a part of the Czech mythmaking?</LI>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P align=justify>\r\n<LI>What in the readings was new, what was surprising and what provoked disagreement? What was not clear? What could be said to be the common theme of all this weeks readings?</LI>\r\n<P></P></OL></FONT>', 213, 0, 100),
