(141, '', '<h2>Pondělí</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of homework: <i>Co jste dělali o prázdninách?</i>\r\n<li>Dates: p. 97, 241; 2.12. - druhého prosince, or druhého druhý\r\n<li>Names of months: <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/reference/idiomatics/months_and_seasons.htm>see more here</a>.\r\n<li>Years: 1999 - devatenáct set devadesát devět (hundreds are counted), 2002 - dva tisíce dva\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h2>Úterý</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Start of term review (complete as homework)\r\n</ul>\r\n<h2>Středa</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Listen and read text in Unit 7\r\n<li>Genitive plural and its uses\r\n</ul>', 230, 0, 100),
(142, '', '<h2>Pondělí</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Complete exercises on p. 97\r\n<li>Describe one important event of world history for each month. e.g. <i>Osmého května skončila druhá světová válka.</i>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h2>Úterý</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of term 1 grammar and conversation topics (due 20.1.)\r\n</ul>\r\n<h2>Středa</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Exercises on genitive plural: pp. 104 - 106\r\n<li>Textbook p. 48: 1, 2\r\n</ul>', 229, 0, 100),
(144, '', '<h2>Pondělí</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of genitive plural\r\n<li>Time giving the hard way, Textbook p. 110-111\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h2>Úterý</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of time giving and TV schedules\r\n<li>Quantities and genitive (106: 9)\r\n<li>Genitive of pronouns (107: 2)\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h2>Středa</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Review of genitive of pronouns\r\n<li>Agreement of verbs with counted nouns (e.g. \"Ten men read the book\" \"Deset muů četlo knihu.\")\r\n</ul>\r\n', 233, 0, 100),
(145, '', '<h2>Pondělí</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Continue with genitive, term review\r\n<li>Textbook 110: 6\r\n</ul>\r\n<h2>Úterý</h2>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Finish exercises on genitive - 106: 8, 9\r\n<li>Genitive pronouns - Textbook 108: 1, 2, 3, Workbook: 48: 3\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h2>Středa</h2>\r\nAssignments for the exam week:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Exercises on agreement with counted nouns 112: 1, 2\r\n<li>Complete all exercises in workbook to the end of the Unit 7\r\n<li>Complete review translation (see below)\r\n</ul>\r\n', 234, 0, 100),
(146, '', '<H1 style=\"MARGIN: 12pt 0cm 3pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><FONT size=5><FONT face=Arial>Review Translation<?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office\" /><o:p></o:p></FONT></FONT></SPAN></H1>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">My name is Igor. I am from </SPAN><?xml:namespace prefix = st1 ns = \"urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags\" /><st1:City><st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Moscow</SPAN></st1:place></st1:City><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> from Russsia. Ive lived in </SPAN><st1:City><st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Prague</SPAN></st1:place></st1:City><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> for two years and therefore I speak a little Czech. Because I speak Russian and German, Czech isnt too difficult for me. I have a lot of Czech friends from many parts of the </SPAN><st1:place><st1:PlaceName><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Czech</SPAN></st1:PlaceName><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> </SPAN><st1:PlaceType><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Republic</SPAN></st1:PlaceType></st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">. My friend Pavel from Český Krumlov often tells me: Igor, you should come to my town. But I dont have enough time to travel because I work a lot. I like reading books and going to the theater. I have read several Czech books last year but I havent read anything from Kundera. I also havent seen any Czech opera but I have already seen all new Czech films by Jan Sv</SPAN><SPAN lang=CS>ěrák</SPAN><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">. <o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Today I have a Czech class in the evening and I mustnt forget to do my homework. I dont have much time but I need to practice. Not many people in </SPAN><st1:City><st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Prague</SPAN></st1:place></st1:City><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> speak Russian and I have many questions. What do Czech like? Who is the minister of culture? Where are some good classical concerts? When does the film festival start? How much do books cost? I also want to read Czech books in </SPAN><st1:country-region><st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Czech.</SPAN></st1:place></st1:country-region><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">I like </SPAN><st1:City><st1:place><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">Prague</SPAN></st1:place></st1:City><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> a lot. It has beautiful buildings and many nice restaurants. Restaurants are important for me because I often go there for lunch. I like Czech food but I dont eat it very often. Sometimes, when I dont want to eat in a restaurant, I go to the shop for some food that I can cook for myself. I dont like cooking but I cook well. In February, I invited some friends for dinner. It was my birthday on the twenty-first and I wanted to celebrate. I cooked them a Russian meal and bought some Russian vodka. My friends gave me nice gifts and flowers. I liked the roses from Jana because I like red flowers. Karel and Pavla like to sing Czech songs. They arrived at </SPAN><st1:time Minute=\"20\" Hour=\"8\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\">twenty past eight</SPAN></st1:time><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"> and then we started singing. We ate lots of food and drank lots of vodka and everybody was happy.<o:p></o:p></SPAN></P>\r\n<P class=MsoNormal style=\"MARGIN: 6pt 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN style=\"mso-ansi-language: EN-US\"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></SPAN></P>', 234, 0, 120),
