INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Welcome to Czech Language Forum', 'Hello all!\nWelcome to the Czech Language Forum. It is a discussion board intended to give you space to ask and answer questions about Czech Language. Did you find a word or phrase which you need help in translating? Are you wondering how Czech compares with other languages. Wondering about your Czech ancestry? Ask away, somebody will probably have the answer.\nHave fun!\nDominik Lukes<br> <a href=http://www.bohemica.com>http://www.bohemica.com</a>\n', '2001-04-23 19:01:38', 0, 0),
(2, 13, 'Josef Füllenbach', 'joschfue@gmx.de', 'České forum', 'Zdá se mi, e vekerá konverzace je tady v angličtině. Doufám, e toto forum můe také slouit ke konverzaci v četině mezi lidmi, kteří se učí český jazyk.\r\nJosef\r\n', '2001-04-26 03:14:19', 0, 0),
(3, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'How important are cases in Czech?', 'Here\'s a question that\'s been on the minds of many students of Czech. How important are cases? Could you speak the language without knowing them? How serious can be mistakes in case endings?\nAnybody would like to share their experience with learning and using cases?\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 05:07:45', 0, 0),
(4, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Re: How important are cases in Czech?', 'In my experience, cases are not nearly as important for basic everyday conversation. Czechs are much more forgiving of mistakes in case endings than things like aspect or even pronunciation.\nNot knowing how the cases work could be a proble when reading, though, where it is often the <i>case</i> what enables you to parse the sentence.\nAnd of course, anybody who wants to speak Czech well must try to use the correct endings. But whenever I teach beginner students, I try to pester them with using the correct endings as little as possible.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 05:12:09', 3, 0),
(5, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: české forum', 'Samozřejmě, e toto forum můete pouít pro otázky v četině. Protoe je ale větina studentů začátečníci, je větina diskuse v angličtině.\nPřidal jsem ale také nové forum, které je zaměřeno jenom na konverzaci v četině, které se jmenuje <a href=http://www.cuni.cz/cgi-bin/cgiwrap/dlukes/mb.pl?board=0005>Česká konverzace</a>.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 05:32:41', 2, 0),
(6, 13, 'Peter Dain', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'colloquial/formal verb forms, decimals', 'Ahoj,\nHope this is the right place to post grammar questions,\nWhen you read a sentence:\n  ...hraji...\nin czech, how do you know if this is:\n     formal czech singular 1st person\nor collequial czech (where 1st person singular is hraju)\n     third person plural\nand similarly for other verbs with varying forms.\nHow did this whole variation of forms originate historically?  Are the all the variants still used?\nAlso, what are the different ways of saying decimals in czech,\neg.  37.3%  or 37,3%\nPeter (learning czech in Milton Keynes).\n=============================\nMoved here from Student Forum\n=============================\n', '2001-05-05 01:08:31', 0, 0),
(7, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Re: colloquial/formal verb forms', '\"hraj<b>i</b>\" can only be formal first person singular (i.e. I play). For it to be third person plural, the <i>i</i> would have to be long, i.e. \"hraj<b>í</b>\". The only possible confusion in Czech conjugation is with the long í between third person singular or plural. You have to rely on context to resolve that, e.g. \"sedí\" could mean \'he/she sits\' or \'they sit\'. (Note: that doesn\'t apply if the \'í\' is preceded by \'j\'). Also remember, that both \'hraji\' and \'hraju\' are now acceptable written forms.\r\n', '2001-05-05 17:02:24', 6, 0),
(8, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Re: decimals in Czech', 'Decimals are expressed using the form \'celá\' (whole). Also note, that Czech uses commas and not points for decimals.\nFor example:\n1,7 = jedna celá sedm\n2,7 = dvě celé sedm\n. . .\n5,7 = pět celých sedm\nYour example would be: 37,3% = třicet sedm celých tři procent.\n', '2001-05-05 17:08:04', 6, 0),
(9, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: origin of colloquial Czech', 'The origins of colloquial Czech are described in the Reference section of <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline target=_top>Czech Online</a>.\nTo answer your question briefly, it has to do with the time of so called National Revival when the revivalists were looking for a model of Czech for literary purposes and chose the language of the Kralická Bible which was over two hundred years behind spoken Czech of the time.\n', '2001-05-05 18:32:02', 6, 0),
(10, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'How to say Czech?', 'I have received several queries about the word Czech. It is important because the awkward name makes it difficult for Czech Republic to be talked about without self-consciousness. David Short agreed to write a little about the issues. I would also like to hear from speakers of other languages how they deal with the name (especially after the split of Czechoslovakia).\n', '2001-05-10 01:13:49', 0, 0),
(11, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: How to say Czech in Czech?', 'You can read a little about how to say Czech in Czech in the <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/reference/referencetop.htm>Reference Trivia section on Czech Online</a>.\nIt is important to remember that where English makes do with one word, Czech needs five. A different one for man, woman and adjective. And two different ones for language. I can\'t tell you, how often I have heard foreigners say that they are learning a Czech woman.\n', '2001-05-10 01:19:11', 10, 0),
(12, 13, 'bart', 'sweinstein@barwol.com', 'Language', 'How do you say the following in Czech?  \"Hello, my name is Bart.  I live in New York.  How are you?  Good-bye.\n', '2001-05-13 02:03:53', 0, 0),
(13, 13, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol', 'Re: Language', '...Bart...ahoj, jmenuju se kevin, bydlim v londyne, jak se mate, nashledanou....pronunciation might be a problem...try it like this....ahoy, e-menu yu se...,bidleem f londyn-yuh, yak se maah-te, na skle da no and blur it up a bit\n', '2001-05-13 07:23:35', 12, 0),
(14, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Language AND culture-Introductions', 'This a very iteresting question. To translate all of these phrases is very easy:\n\"Dobrý den. Já jsem Bart. Bydlím v New Yorku. Jak se máte? Nashledanou.\"\nBut there are of course a variety of problems that spring up. It is interesting how the simplest phrase can be complicated if you want to use it not just grammatically but also culturaly appropriately.\n1. The most important problem is \'How are you?/Jak se máte?\' You can NEVER use that phrase when introducing yourself. You will not insult anybody but it will be as incomprehensible as saying \'Do you like your eggs poached or raw?\' The phrase \'Jak se máte?\' is generally used very rarely, mostly only when you really want to know how somebody is. It is becoming more frequent under the influence of badly translated American soaps but this definitely hasn\'t seeped into the introductions ritual.\n2. Next, you obviously need to decide in what relationship you are to the person you\'re introducing yourself to. If it is somebody older, more senior, or somebody the same age or younger you met in a formal situation. In that case you use the formal Vy form, given above. If, on the other hand, it is somebody you met through your friend in an informal situation, such as a party, you can use the informal Ty version: \"Ahoj. Já jsem X. Jak se má? Ahoj.\" (Of course, avoiding the <i>Jak se má</i>.) In general, you want to keep to the formal version unless you\'re certain you should use the informal one. Especially people over 30 will often use it with each other even in informal contexts. (Read more about the nature of friendship in the <a target=_top href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechculture>Culture Guide</a>).\n', '2001-05-13 15:10:30', 12, 0),
(15, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Language-Jmenuju se dilema', 'Although \'Jmenuju se\' is the correct translation of \'my name is\'. It is much better to use \'Já jsem ...\' (I am ...). It is simpler and much more common. Using \'jmenuju se\' sounds awkward unless you\'re introducing yourself to a formal group of people.\nIf you do want to go with \'jmenuju se\' I recommend pronouncing it as \'menu-yoo\'. The initial \'j\' is almost always dropped when followed by a consonant.\n', '2001-05-13 15:17:00', 13, 0),
(16, 13, 'Andrew', 'accelerator_14@hotmail.com', 'The Czech Verb \" to be \"', 'In Spanish there are two forms of to be and I was wondering how many forms of  to be there are in Czech.\n', '2001-05-17 00:25:03', 0, 0),
(17, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: The Czech Verb', 'Thanks for this interesting question. Czech only has one form of the verb \'to be\' itself but it can express the same as the Spanish \'ser\' and \'estar\' by the case of the noun with it. Namely, if you want to express an aquired quality, the noun that are part of the predicate  will be in the Instrumental case while a permanent quality uses the Nominative.\nFor example: <i>Petr je můj bratr.</i> (Petr is my brother) uses the Nominative, while <i>Petr je ministrem</i> (Petr is a minister) uses the Instrumental.\nUnlike Spanish, though, this is only used in written and formal Czech (you are most likely to see it in newspapers). In normal speech, you don\'t have to worry about this.\n', '2001-05-17 23:28:18', 16, 0),
(18, 13, 'Andrew', 'accelerator_14@hotmail.com', 'to do', 'I was wondering about the verb  to do  I know that in some languages they do not have that verb because basically it doesnt mean anything. So I was also wondering if the Czech language has that verb. Thanks\n', '2001-05-22 22:54:10', 0, 0),
(19, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: to do', 'I\'m not aware of any language that doesn\'t have the verb \'to do\' in the sense of performing some indeterminate action, but I wouldn\'t be surprised if there were quite a few that don\'t. Czech does have the verb \'to do\' in this sense. It is \'delat\' (imperfektive) or \'udelat\' (perfective). It is never used as an auxiliary as in the English \'do you know it?\'. It can sometimes be used to translate the English \'make\' as well.\nDominik\nPS: You may be confusing \'to do\' with \'to have\' or \'to be\' when used as copulas (i.e. connecting two words) which are absent in many languages such as Russian or Arabic (and others in Semitic family). For example in Russian you say, \'Peter doctor\' or \'there is a book by Peter\' instead of \'Peter is a doctor\' or \'Peter has a book\'. Czech, however, has both of these words.\n', '2001-05-23 16:17:19', 18, 0),
(20, 13, 'ione kramer', 'ionek@yahoo.com', 'Grundsmann', 'A relative in Moravia, in early 1850s part of Austria, was a \"Gruntsmann\" (I presume this is from the German Grundsmann). I think it indicates a title for holding land. Can\'t find it in German sources. What status does it indicate and what responsibilities went with it?\n', '2001-05-24 09:36:49', 0, 0),
(21, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Grundsmann', 'Ione, I\'m sorry this is a question I know nothing about. At a guess, I would imagine Gruntsmann was someone responsible for the upkeep of the land but your explanation is also possible. I could find no mention of the title on all of Czech internet (or indeed all of internet). Your theory seems to be supported by the fact that there is no Czech name \'Grundsmann\' or \'Gruntsmann\' (I searched the phone books on the internet) while there are plenty of Germans by that name. Many Czechs have German-derived last names but they are mostly based on their professions such as Schmidt or origin such as Bem or Bohm.\nI hope someone out there will have a better answer.\n', '2001-05-24 16:23:10', 20, 0),
(22, 13, 'Rowboat Jerry', 'jhughesgroupins@aol.com', 'Translate into czech', 'In czech, what phase/words would a father use to lovingly describe his daughter as \" little sweet?\"\nIf applicable, can you give me several variations. Thanx\nFor example, is \"malo or mola sladka\" correct?\n', '2001-05-26 01:38:45', 0, 0),
(23, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Translate into czech', 'If you mean \'A little sweet\', then the best translation would be \'drahousek\' /drah-hoe-sheck/ or \'zlaticko\' /zlah-tyeee-tchkoh/. Czech can use one word because of endings called diminutives used for endearing words instead of the English \'little\' or \'-ie/y\' as in \'dearie\' or \'matey\'. If you knew what you were doing, you could call her \'sladkustka\' /slut-koost-kah/ which is a neologism in Czech but could be quite cute in the right hands. Also remember, that when actually calling her something like this rather than just describing her, you must use the vocative ending. I.e. \'drahousku\', \'sladkustko\' (\'zlaticko\' stays the same). If you wanted to say \'my little...\' you could preface what ever term with \'ty moje...\'.\nYour suggestion \'malo sladka\' means \'not sweet enough\'. Not knowing what you actually want this for, this is the best I can do.\n', '2001-05-26 15:02:42', 22, 0),
(24, 13, 'Andrew', 'accelerator_14@hotmail.com', 'Re: to do', 'Yeah I can see how every language would have \'to do\'. The language i was talking about,Spanish,has the verb \'hacer\' which means \'to do\' or  to make  and i guess I should have been a little more specific in my question in like in Spanish when you want to ask \'Do you have to go?\' you would say \'?Tienes ir?\'and if you wanted to say \'You have to go\' you would put \'Tienes ir\' and the do is understood and that is how it is for about all the spanish verbs. And i was just wondering if that is what it was like in Czech. Thanks for answering my question.\n', '2001-05-27 04:56:14', 19, 0),
(25, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: to do', 'The short answer is yes. As I said, Czech never uses \'do\' as an auxiliary verb like in English \'don\'t go\'. In fact, the only true auxiliary in Czech is the verb \'to be\' using the present form in the past tense (udělal jsem - I did) and budu to form the imperfective future tense.\nAs in English, \'to do\' is used as part of many collocations, such as \'udělat chybu\' - \'make a mistake\', \'udělat skandál\' - \'make a fuss\', etc. It is also used with many prefixes (similar to English adverbs in phrasal verbs), e.g. \'vydělat\' - \'to earn\', \'dodělat\' - \'to complete\'. But in all these cases it plays a more or less crucial role to the meaning of the phrase.\n', '2001-05-27 05:23:30', 24, 0),
(26, 13, 'Richard Syed', 'richard.syed@btinternet.com', 'Czech grammar book', 'Do you know of a Czech grammar book, i.e. a formal reference grammar laid out in the traditional way? The text of the book preferably in English, Czech, German, or French.\n', '2001-05-27 15:20:19', 0, 0),
(27, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech grammar book', 'There isn\'t a complete modern Czech reference grammar available in English. David Short\'s section on Czech in Comrie\'s Slavonic Languages. You can buy it through the Bookstore here. There is also a new grammar of Czech by Laura Janda and Charles Townsend which you can get from Lincom Europa at <a href=\"http://home.t-online.de/home/LINCOM.EUROPA/lwm.htm\">http://home.t-online.de/home/LINCOM.EUROPA/lwm.htm</a>.\nThe materials in the CzechOnline Reference Section are fairly complete but aimed primarily at students of Czech as a foreign language at beginner to intermediate levels. I hope to expand them over time.\n', '2001-05-28 02:32:58', 26, 0),
(28, 13, 'Rowboat Jerry', 'jhughesgroupins@aol.com', 'Re: Translate into czech', 'Dominik,\nThank you very much re: \"little sweet.\"\nI believe \"sladkustka\" is perfect!!\nAppreciate your help.\nJerry\n', '2001-05-28 06:33:05', 23, 0),
(29, 13, 'Leontien', 'tinkerbell@myestate.com', 'How to write..', 'Who can help me how to write a boys-name?\nIt\'s pronounced as \"Laia\" or \"Laidja\".\nMaybe someone knows which boys-name I mean...\n', '2001-05-30 20:35:26', 0, 0),
(30, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: How to write..', 'I think you want <b>\'Láďa\'</b> which is most likely short for \'Ladislav\'. Do not be confused by the -a ending which is typically feminine but very common in Czech male nicknames.\n', '2001-05-31 03:27:11', 29, 0),
(31, 13, 'Leontien', 'tinkerbell@myestate.com', 'Re: How to write..', 'Thanks a lot,\nI would never have thought to write it this way!\nPerhaps you can also tell me the proper way to pronounce this name? I hope so.\nAnd another question is if this name is used a lot in Czech or not?\n', '2001-05-31 15:22:27', 30, 0),
(32, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: How to write..', 'The closest to English pronunciation would be something like / laaah-dyah /. Have a look at the Reference section on <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline>http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline</a>. There are examples of all Czech sounds there.\n', '2001-06-02 01:08:40', 31, 0),
(33, 13, 'Jerry Cizek', 'jcizek3rd@aol.com', 'What is \'nazdar\'?', 'As a child my grandparents used the word \"nazdar\" when they hoisted their pivo.  That was many years ago.  What does that word really mean?  I was told it was the bohemian or Czech equivalent to \"cheers\" or \"here\'s to you.\"  Can you assist?\n', '2001-06-02 01:28:02', 0, 0),
(34, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: What is \'nazdar\'?', 'Actually, <i>nazdar</i> is rarely used as the equivalent of \'cheers\'. The origins of this word can be traced to 1870s, when it was introduced by Miroslav Tyr as a greeting for his (still active today) physical education group <i>Sokol</i>. It means \'to success\' (i.e. \'na zdar\'). It is not used as a greeting very often any more except by older people, but it is a part of a \'soft curse\' \'no nazdar\' meaning something like \'now you\'ve done it\'.\n', '2001-06-02 01:35:36', 33, 0),
(35, 13, 'Andrew', 'accelerator_14@hotmail.com', 'Re: to do', 'This has nothing to do with the Czech language but do you know if there are any other languages that use the verb \'to do\' the same as we do in English. If so what are the ones you know. Thanks\n', '2001-06-02 07:11:38', 25, 0),
(36, 13, 'Andrew', 'accelerator_14@hotmail.com', 'Good Night and Good', 'hey. Since my grandma is czech(her grandma came from Czechoslovakia) and she doesn\'t remember Czech any more because it has been so long since she spoke it. But she does remember hot to say how are you and the numbers. I heard from a multimedia cd that you say good night \'dobre veicjar\' or something like that and she says  \'dobshey nOtes (the o\'u\' sound) and she says good as \'dobshey \' instead of dobre. Are these both correct or is this a result of living in the United States for a while. Thanks\n', '2001-06-05 08:44:30', 0, 0),
(37, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Good Night and Good', 'I\'m afraid, your grandma\'s Czech has been somewhat tainted by being handed down from generation to generation. \'Good night\' is \'dobrou noc\' pronounced / DOB-row nohts / there is definitely no \'oh\' sound in \'noc\'. The word for \'good\' has over a dozen different forms depending on the context it is use in. Have a look in the Reference section on Czech Online. The most common forms will be \'dobrý\', \'dobrá\', \'dobré\'. If you use \'good\' as an adverb, such as \'I\'m good\', then the form is \'dobře\'.\nIt sounds complicated but in fact, if you\'re learning Czech, it becomes obvious pretty quickly. Dominik\n', '2001-06-06 02:04:21', 36, 0),
(38, 13, 'Gloria Ptacek McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCOm.net', 'Re: Good Night and Good', 'Ahoj!\nMy Czech is only at the level of reading headlines.  So judge what\nI say with a grain of salt or three...\nAs far as I know \"dobry večer\" is \"good evening\"  and \"dobrou noc\" is \"Good night\".\n  You say the latter when leaving at night.\nWhen you part from someone you have met, you say\n\"Na shledanou,\"\n(Until I see you again...)\nGloria\n', '2001-06-09 22:11:17', 36, 0),
(39, 13, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Ptacek - Klima family from Praha', 'Ahoj-\nMy great great -grandparents came from Prague but no one here has kept up any\nconnection with the family.  They cane in the late 1880s or about 1890.  All we know\nis that he was Karel (Charles) Ptacek and she was Marie Klima.  Think that is right.\nMy mother is convinced that they actually were from Prague and not from a village.\nBut with so little to go on and so many Klimas and Ptaceks, there is probably no\nhope of finding them.\nWhen I asked my great-uncle Joe before he died how I might find the Ptaceks\nin Prague (I went there twice in the 1970s), he said,  \"look up in the trees...\"\nAt least I know some of these people are my relatives.\nNazdar!  (They probably said that in our family about then...Another thing I\nknow about them was that in Chicago at least, they were \"svobodomyslich\"\nor Freethinker Czechs who subscribed to this Freethinker newspaper,\n_Svornost_.)\nGloria\n', '2001-06-09 22:22:11', 0, 0),
(40, 13, 'Kvetina', 'Vladskvetina@hotmail.com', 'Happy Birthday', 'I was hoping, since I am so new to the language, that someone could help me with this?  I would like to write out the Birthday song in Czech for someone, but have no idea of how to do it.  Can anyone help me with this?  Is it sung in Czech at all?  Dekuji!\nKvetina\n', '2001-07-11 05:48:29', 0, 0),
(41, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Happy Birthday', 'The Czech text used to sing to the tune is:\n\'Hodne stesti, zdravi\nHodne stesti, zdravi\nHodne stesti, mily X\nHodne stesti, zdravi.\'\nDon\'t forget to put the name of X into vocative (e.g. Pavle, Tomasi, Dominiku, or Jano) and of course change \'mily\' to \'mila\' for women.\n(Sorry for writing without accents but I\'m at a library in England.)\n', '2001-07-11 21:25:45', 40, 0),
(42, 13, 'kevinku', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: Happy Birthday', 'i also have problems with my accent in english libraries..few more birhday tips...a co dostanu za dárek....wheres my present....hezký narozeniny...happy birthday....kolik je ti let...how old are you....co budeme pít....what will we drink...normally greetings etc are made the evening before the actual birthday.....kevin\n', '2001-07-12 04:02:14', 41, 0),
(43, 13, 'Shashika', 'shaskia_85@hotmail.com', 'Voice', 'Hey, Dominik. You have a lot of information in text but even looking at the pronounciation guide I can\'t even say it,on some other sites there are recordings of people saying it. probably too much of a project to do everything. Some things would help.\n', '2001-07-24 10:28:34', 0, 0),
(44, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Voice recordings on Bohemica.com', 'You can find sound on http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline in the following sections:\nReading: Unit 1: exercise Alphabet\nReference: Pronunciation: Consonant Clusters, Comparing English and Czech, Intonation\nReference: Spelling\nAll the sounds of the Czech language (and intonation) with examples are available there in Real Audio. They are presented in different contexts to illustrate their use. More sound will be added as the online course becomes available (hopefully before the end of the year).\n', '2001-07-24 15:04:04', 43, 0),
(45, 13, 'Peter', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'specifying dates', 'In czech, when indicating a date for staying at a hotel, I have seen a phrase like:\nv terminu 11.07.2001 na 1 noc\nIs this saying I have reserved for 1 night and am leaving on the morning of 11.07.2001? When reserving a hotel, is it normal in czech to specify the leaving date and the no. of days rather, than the arrival date and the no. of days as one might do in English?\n', '2001-07-29 14:25:59', 0, 0),
(46, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Not in my dictionary', 'Ahoj!\nI just thought I would ask what this verb is.\nzruit\nI was also wtaching the Kalendarium TV show and the host said\nLizst byl zadrace ditě.\nI also cannot find zadrace in my dictionary.\nSometimes when we foreigners only hear Czech, we have to guess what is one word and what are two words.\nFor all I know the above could be:   za drace\nDěkuji;\nGloria\n', '2001-07-29 20:47:02', 0, 0),
(47, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Wading in deeper to the language...', 'Dobry den,\nAfter putting in many hours of Zpravy (the news) and of\nPočasi (the weather) I do understand a word every few words.\nHere are the problems of oral comprehension, though.\nYou have a very large vocabulary being used quickly.  I\nhave found that the artsy radio station Radio Vltava speaks\nthe slowest and the news really races by.  The reason is\nbecause the classical music announcers always speak in a cultured voice, which tends to mean a leisurely pace.\nI understand far more on Radio Vltava than on the ČT1\nnews (Zpravy).\nWhile the images sometimes help, I find the increased speed offsets the advantage of images plus sound.\nIf I think of other tips for beginners, I will post.  I hope\nthat others will also tell where they are succeeding and how they manage it!\nNazdar!\nGloria\n', '2001-07-29 20:55:33', 0, 0),
(48, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Not in my dictionary', 'zruit - means \'cancel\'\n\'zadrace ditě\' is nothing but it could be either:\n\'ztracené\' (lost) or \'zázračné\' (miraculous, or precosious in the context) (I think the second is more likely, if you\'re talking about the composer).\n', '2001-08-01 20:18:31', 46, 0),
(49, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: specifying dates', 'I\'m not sure what is meant by the phrase \'v terminu\' (it sounds like something you might put on a contract, in which case, it should include both the start and the end date). Personally, when making a reservation, I give both the arrival and the departure date. I.e. I would say: \'přijedu 12. 8. a odjedu 18. 8.\', or I might say \'přijedu na dvě noci od 12. 8.\' but always giving the start date.\n', '2001-08-01 20:24:38', 45, 0),
(50, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Is \"Ahoj\" from sailors?', 'I have a Hungarian friend and she suggests that \"ahoj\" has\nnothing to do with the similar word in English, but rather\nis a completely different Czech word that merely sounds similar.\nIs this correct?\nGloria\n', '2001-08-02 02:23:31', 0, 0),
(51, 13, 'terdem', 'demaray@mcn.net', 'what would be a good Czech name on my license plates for a New Beetle', 'I would like to put a czech name on my license plates for my new green volkswagen beetle. I have been looking some up and found fazole (bean) vyjet si (go for a drive) and vozovka (drive - but I am not sure if this drive is a verb or a noun). I can only use seven letters and one space. Do these words translate into what I think they do or are they way off base? Any suggestions???  Thanks - Ter\n', '2001-08-03 02:09:52', 0, 0),
(52, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: what would be a good Czech name on my license plates for a New Beetle', 'vozovka is the same as silnice, both mean \'road\' (yes it is a noun)..\nbtw. silnice also has 7 letters..\n', '2001-08-03 15:43:03', 51, 0),
(53, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: what would be a good Czech name on my license plates for a New Beetle', 'Why not try \'brouk\' beetle or \'brouček\' - little beetle, or \'autíčko\' - little car.\n', '2001-08-03 17:36:06', 51, 0),
(54, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Ahoj and Ahoy', 'Your HUngarian friend is wrong. \'Ahoj\' is indeed the old English nautical greeting (although unlike that it can be used for saying goodbye, as well). It was introduced in the 1920s along with the \'tramping\' and \'scouts\' movements. It was first used by \'tramps\' canoeing down Czech rivers. Czechs are extremely self-conscious about not having a sea, so this is as close as they could get.\nNote: \'Tramp\' in Czech means a romantic roamer and nothing to do with poverty. In fact, the movement was very popular in the middle classes as well.\n', '2001-08-03 17:41:39', 50, 0),
(55, 13, 'Andrew', 'accelerator_14@hotmail.com', 'CD-ROM', 'Hey all, I bought a learning cd-rom from eurotalk the other day and it has helped me but it has very little information (definitley for beginners). Do you know of some other program that really teaches you the language and just doesn\'t have a few recordings of certain phrases. Thanks\n', '2001-08-07 08:02:35', 0, 0),
(56, 13, 'M.', '', 'Questions.', 'I\'m an American high school student, and I originally didn\'t set out this summer to try and learn Czech, but somehow it ended up that way. ;) I got two textbooks (\"Colloquial Czech\" and \"Teach Yourself Czech\") and now I\'m enjoying myself too much to stop.\nPronunciation is really messing me up though. I\'m especially having trouble with H\'s that come in front of a consonant (like \"hrad\"). Neither of the books I\'ve got covers it. Also -- I know that the accent is usually on the first syllable, but what if there\'s a vowel in an unstressed syllable? The only example I can think of offhand right now is \"Navrátil\", the last name of the oh-so-friendly Czech engineer in the dialogues in \"Teach Yourself Czech\", Zdenek Navrátil.\nLast question -- how do you say \"There is\" or \"There are\" in Czech?\nThank you!\nM.\n', '2001-08-11 13:23:43', 0, 0),
(57, 13, 'Fredrik', 'fredrik.andersson@kemisten.nu', 'Days of the week', 'I\'m curious about the etymology concerning the Czech words for \"Tuesday\" and \"Sunday\". Someone suggested that \"nedele\" means something like \"the day when nothing is being done\". Is that right?\nThanks!\nFredrik\n', '2001-08-15 15:35:58', 0, 0),
(58, 13, 'kevin handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: Days of the week', 'Fredrik, doing nothing on Sunday makes sense so I would assume hence nedele. I looked in my dictionary and have determined that Tuesday must have been a day for dusting. Im sure thats not really the case but it will be interesting to find out the origins of Tues and also Monday. You can see middle in Weds and 4th-5th in Thurs-\nFri... Saturday might be based on Sabbath as in Jewish but I dont know....look forward to answers ...kevin\n', '2001-08-16 01:48:13', 57, 0),
(59, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Days of the week', 'Doing nothing on sunday (nedele - nedelat) sounds good..\nAnd I think, that I\'ve heard that utery (Tuesday) comes from russian [Vtornik], which means \'second\', as we number days from Monday, Tuesday then being the second in the week..\n', '2001-08-16 13:01:27', 57, 0),
(60, 13, 'Fredrik', 'fredrik.andersson@kemisten.nu', 'Re: Days of the week', 'Thank you for the answer, Kevin! As far as I can tell, Monday is simply the day after (po) Sunday so if the \"Doing Nothing Day hypothesis\" is correct, Monday would simply be the day after Doing Nothing Day. I\'m looking forward to reading more thoughts about this./Fredrik\n', '2001-08-16 14:00:19', 58, 0),
(61, 13, 'Fredrik', 'fredrik.andersson@kemisten.nu', 'Re: Days of the week', 'Thank you, Micval! That\'s really an interesting suggestion, and makes a lot of sense since Thursday and Friday also are named after their positions in the week./Fredrik\n', '2001-08-16 14:04:54', 59, 0),
(62, 13, 'gloria mcmillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Slovo: mají', 'Ahoj!\nI am translating an article titled:\n\"Mají se spisovatele eniti?\"\nMy collaborator who is fluent says that this could be rendered as\n\"Should writers marry?\"\nBut \'should\' is another word:  musit.\nHow is it that \'mají\' means \'should\' here?\nCan you explain?\nDekuji,\nGloria\n', '2001-08-19 06:13:22', 0, 0),
(63, 13, 'M.', '', 'Saturday', '\"Saturday\" in Spanish is similar to in Czech: sabado. Maybe the common root has something to do with Sabbath, I don\'t know.\nM.\n', '2001-08-20 13:18:59', 58, 0),
(64, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Slovo: mají', 'Your translator was correct. \'Muset\' means \'must\' but the contruction \'mít\' plus infinitive is usually translated as \'should\'. You could also go with the more cumbersome \'are they supposed to\'.\n', '2001-08-20 18:03:18', 62, 0),
(65, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Meaning of all days of the week', 'To set the record straight:\npondělí - from \'po neděli\' i.e. day after Sunday<br> úterý - from old Slavic \'vtoroj\' - second<br> středa - from Czech \'střed\' - middle, center<br> čtvrtek - from Czech \'čtvtý\' - fourth<br> pátek - from Czech \'pátý\' - fifth<br> sobota - same origin as Sabbath<br> neděle - from \'nedělat\' - do nothing\nNote 1: Names of the days are never capitalized\nNote 2: Czech week starts with Monday not Sunday\n', '2001-08-21 14:03:55', 57, 0),
(66, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Days of the week', 'Czech \'úterý\' doesn\'t come from Russian \'vtornik\' but has the same origin in old Slavic for second \'vtoroj\'.\n', '2001-08-21 14:06:21', 59, 0),
(67, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: CD-ROM for learning Czech', 'The EuroTalk is really very basic more of a a glorified vocabulary practice which is the same for all the languages it is available for.\nThere is only one other CD-ROM available which is better, especially, if you\'re trying to learn to read historical texts. You can find a link to it in Bookstore on bohemica.com.\nWe\'re planning an interactive CD-ROM of bohemica.com with some more sound and video added. All I can say is watch this space but it will probably be a while before I have any good news to report (a rich investor would help).\n', '2001-08-21 14:10:37', 55, 0),
(68, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Questions-Pronunciation difficulties with H', 'It\'s great that you\'re having fun learning Czech. You\'ve put your finger on it with \'H\'. For an English speaker, it is the single most difficult sound to make.\nThe reason for this is that the English \'h\' is \'unvoiced\' and the Czech \'h\' is voiced. It means that they are as different as \'t\' and \'d\', \'p\' and \'b\', or \'f\' and \'v\'. The best way of learning this is to try to recreate the difference. If you put your finger lightly on your Adam\'s apple, you will feel it vibrate with \'d\', \'b\', and \'v\' but not with \'t\', \'p\', and \'f\'. When you try the English \'h\' you should feel no vibration but you will when you get the Czech \'h\' correctly.\n', '2001-08-21 14:17:20', 56, 0),
(69, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Pronunciation difficulties with stress', 'Yes, the stress is always on the first syllable in Czech (although, if with one syllable prepositions, it is on the preposition). It doesn\'t make any difference if the following syllable has a long vowel in it.\nThis is probably the most difficult thing for foreigners to get right if they want to reduce their accent. Stress and vowel length are independent. The vowels in Czech have always the same quality and are never reduced.\n', '2001-08-21 14:20:28', 56, 0),
(70, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: \'there is\' and \'there are\' in Czech', 'Czech doesn\'t have that construction and it is in fact one of the few things many Czechs never completely learn in English.\nYou simply use \'je\' or \'jsou\'. Sometimes you might use \'má\' or \'mají\'.\nFor example, \'there is a castle in Prague\' is \'v Praze je hrad\'\n\'there are problems in the USA\' is \'v USA jsou problémy\' or \'v USA mají problémy\'\nNote that sometimes you must add \'tam\' or \'tady\', as in:\n\'there\'s a car overthere\' - \'tam je auto\', \'tam máme/mají auto\'\n\'there\'s a car here\' - \'tady je auto\'\n', '2001-08-21 14:25:04', 56, 0),
(71, 13, 'M.', '', 'Thank you', 'Thanks so much for your help. Although I\'m still a little intimidated by certain words (and certain consonant-clusters), I think I\'m beginning to get it, pronunciation-wise.\n', '2001-08-25 12:57:01', 70, 0),
(72, 13, 'Andrew', 'accelerator_14@hotmail.com', 'Hunger and Cold', 'My questions are based on what i have learned in another language class (spanish). In Czech do people say \'I have hunger\'and \'I have cold\' (as in spanish). Or do they say like in English \'I am hunger\'or \'I am cold\'of course these would all be literal translations. Thanks for your time.\n', '2001-09-03 04:50:08', 0, 0),
(73, 13, 'LeRoy  L.  Hubl', 'LMHubl@aol.com', 'Form Letters', 'I would like to know where I can get private and public genealogy form letters written in Czech and English languages written on the the same form letter?  Please contact me:  LMHubl@aol.com\n', '2001-09-03 23:40:37', 0, 0),
(74, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Hunger and Cold', 'Well, in Czech hunger and cold both use a different system. Hunger is the same as in Spanish, i.e. mám hlad (I have hunger). The same goes for thirst (žízeň).\nHowever, for cold and warmth, you use a sentence pattern that had e.g. cold as the subject and the person perceiving it as a dative object. I.e. \'je mi zima\' = I\'m cold (it is cold to me), or \'je mi teplo\' - it is warm for me.\n<b>Note:</b> Never say \'jsem teplý\' because it means \'I\'m gay\'.\n', '2001-09-04 01:18:30', 72, 0),
(75, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Days of the week', 'A few more notes on Sunday:\n--there is a Czech saying, \"V neděli se nedělá\"--Nothing should be done on Sunday.\n--In bureacratic language, Sunday is grouped together with holidays (if I can take lexicographer Fronek\'s word on it) as a \"public holiday.\"\n--The Czech notion of the week starts on Monday, not on Sunday as in the US.\nHere\'s an extract of what Machek\'s Etymological Dictionary of the Czech Language (1997 reprint by the Lidové noviny publishing house) has to say on the remaining days:\nMonday -- it is indeed po+n(e)dělí -- \"after Sunday.\" Several other Slavic languages use the same pattern.\nTuesday -- see Luke\'s post. \"Vterý\" as \"second\" was replaced long ago in Czech with \"druhý,\" but remained in this word (and was used to create the word \"vteřina\"--\"one second\"--during the Czech Renaissance).\nWednesday--from \"středa\", the old word for \"middle\" (long since masculinized to \"střed\" in its main meaning). Hey, if the week starts on Monday, isn\'t the middle on Thursday? Machek states that this reflects the influence of German (which counts from Sunday), with its \"Mittwoch\"--\"midweek\"--on Czech.\nThursday--quite simply the fourth day (from the root <i>čtvr-<i>).\nFriday--quite simply the fifth day (from the root <i>pát-<i>).\nSaturday--Whew, that\'s a lot of abbreviations (Machek writes for etymologists, not human beings)! It comes from the Sabbath, that\'s all I could make of what he wrote.\nLong live etymology!\n', '2001-09-06 18:07:31', 57, 0),
(76, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: to do', 'Although I (sadly) never formally studied grammar on the university level, I am surprised by your statement that the only auxiliary in Czech is \"to be.\" What about--at the very least--\"to have to\" (muset) and \"to be supposed to\"/\"to be planned-to\"/\"to be expectable\" (mít)?\nThe American Heritage Dictionary states that an auxiliary verb is \"a verb that accompanies particular forms of the main verb of a clause to form a phrasal unit expressing the tense, mood, voice, or aspect of he main verb. <i>Have, may, can, must,</i> and <i>will</i> are some auxiliary verbs: <i>He will come. They must go.</i>\"\nSorry for being such a pedant; it\'s a \"professional deformation\" of many translators, I think.\n', '2001-09-06 18:17:44', 25, 0),
(77, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'The mnemonic for -jí vs. -ejí', 'Hello,\nThis one is for Dominik, I think. Years ago, a Czech acquaintance of mine mentioned a mnemonic for keeping straight (without looking at lists and dictionaries) which verbs should get -jí in 3rd p. p. and which -ejí. I\'ve long since forgotten it. Any thoughts on what that mnemonic is?\n', '2001-09-06 18:23:05', 0, 0),
(78, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Good Night and Good', 'The statement \"There is no \'oh\' in \'noc\'\" is a bit confusing. I think this is what Dominik means, I hope he will correct me if I\'m wrong:\nThe English long o is a diphthong--in plain English, that means a fusion of two vowels into something a tiny bit different than just the two of them put together. In the case of long o, the parts being fused are what I\'d call a \"clean long o\" and another sound, not sure if it\'s \"u\" or \"w.\"\nTherefore, our classical \"oh\" is not, strictly speaking, anywhere in Czech, because it uses a \"clean o\" -- the first half of our diphthong.\nThat, by the way, is how you tell if an English-savvy Czech is saying English \"no\" or Czech \"no\" (which means \"yeah\") to you -- our has the diphthong.\n', '2001-09-06 18:32:10', 37, 0),
(79, 13, 'M.', '', 'Zima', '\"Zima\" is the name of an American drink. I\'m not entirely sure what it is, but I\'ve seen commercials for it, and I felt incredibly smart when I learned that it meant \"cold\".\n', '2001-09-10 09:37:55', 74, 0),
(80, 13, 'M.', '', 'Politeness', 'I\'m having a little trouble with all the different ways to show politeness (keep in mind, I\'m an American, I\'m not used to it *smile*). Besides saying \"vy\", of course, there\'s \"mohou\" instead of \"muzou\" (sorry, I\'m having a little trouble with setting up the Czech keyboard), saying \"Neznate\" for \"Do you know...\" instead of just \"Znate\"... Am I supposed to use <i>all</i> these to speak to somebody with whom I\'m on <i>vy</i> terms?\nAlso -- does anybody have any hints for learning cases? Is it just rote memorization? Because I\'m completely lost, case-wise.\n', '2001-09-10 12:18:59', 0, 0),
(81, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: The mnemonic for -jí vs. -ejí', 'Well, I was searching for something good until I remembered that the only fail-safe mnemonic for native speakers is absolutely useless to any non-native learner of Czech.\nAll you have to do is ask yourself what the imperative form is. As you know, the best way to form the imperative is to strip away the ending from the 3rd person plural. Therefore, if the imperative form ends in -ej, like \'sázej\', you know that the third person plural is \'sázejí\' (bet, plant). But if it ends in another consonant such as \'bě\' (run), you will know that it is \'běí\'.\nThis only works for the \'trpět\' vs. \'sázet\' models, though.  Also, there is no ending \'-jí\' in the 3rd person plural, there are \'-í\', \'-ejí\' and \'-ají\'. \'-jí\' is conincidental in words like \'kryjí\' (cover), \'bijí\' (beat) where it is part of the present stem.\n', '2001-09-11 01:17:29', 77, 0),
(82, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Politeness', 'All the linguistic tools for politness you mentioned are fine. Another one is to use people\'s academic titles if they have one. A big booster for a successful relationship with a Czech is to use the correct form of their name when addressing them, i.e. the Vocative. For example, my name is Dominik, but people say \'Dominiku\' when they turn to me.\nYou might also add the conditional, i.e. \'nemohl byste\' rather than \'nemuzete\'? But that is true in most European languages.\nOn the other hand, the difference between \'mohou\' and \'muzou\' is more a matter of written vs. spoken rather than polite impolite. Although, the more formal the occasion, the more formal (i.e. writing-like) your speech should be.\n', '2001-09-11 01:23:12', 80, 0),
(83, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Hints for learning cases', 'It comes down to some form of rote but that doesn\'t mean that you shouldn\'t understand the meaning of the case because that could be helpful in learning not just what ending to use but also when to use it.\nThere is a great site for that run by Laura Janda at http://www.seelrc.org/casebooks that you may want to have a look at.\nThe big question is, should you learn all the cases at once (like people did with Latin), or should you go one by one. Most modern textbooks try to introduce the cases gradually but some people may prefer to learn the whole case paradigm at once. The rule here, as in all language learning, is: If it works for you, keep doing it and forget about theories.\n', '2001-09-11 01:28:43', 80, 0),
(84, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Zima', 'It also means \'winter\'. It only means \'cold\' when you say \'je zima\' (it is cold) or \'je mi zima\' (I am cold).\n', '2001-09-11 23:05:17', 79, 0),
(85, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: The mnemonic for -jí vs. -ejí', 'Useless or not, that seems to be the one that was mentioned to me back then. I see I\'ve been too long away from textbooks -- thinking up imaginary verb endings. Thanks.\nErik\n', '2001-09-12 19:05:59', 81, 0),
(86, 13, 'M.', '', 'How do Czech kids learn cases?', 'Or do they, even? Is it just something that comes as a baby learns to speak?\nWhat I\'ve been doing is just trying to remember particular sentences, and then applying that to other instances. Actually, I think I hit a wall a few weeks back with cases, and by now I\'m just starting to chop my way to the other side.\n', '2001-09-14 12:39:26', 83, 0),
(87, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: How do Czech kids learn cases?', 'That\'s pretty much how Czech children do it. They learn words in context.\nIn fact, cases aren\'t all that much of an issue. But it still takes kids lots and lots of time and effort (but no more than syntax does to English kids). You must remember that children do nothing but learn language day in and day out for six years.\nAn important parallel is that even children hit walls in their language development. Very often they start making mistakes in what they could previously do because they experiment with new rules.\nAdults have the advantage that they can expedite their language learning by using analytical rules. But at a later stage that starts to hinder them because they overanalyze. At some stage you must shed the rules and start speaking in acquired chunks.\n', '2001-09-15 02:55:51', 86, 0),
(88, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: to do', 'That\'s why I said \'true auxiliary\'. In fact, the extent to which a verb loses the meaning it contributes to a phrase varies. It is true that \'mít\' contributes very little of its meaning to the phrase \'máš to udělat\' (you should do it) so it is an auxiliary verb in that sense. I didn\'t mention it because it is part of a single idiom rather than a grammatical construction.\n', '2001-09-15 03:09:58', 76, 0),
(89, 13, 'Vessela', 'vesy@rice.edu', 'A tiny question', 'Hello,\nI have a little question about a Czech word I\'ve been trying to figure out.  What is the word \"brouku\" mean.  A Czech friend asked me in an e-mail \"Jak se mas brouku?\"  Sorry for the lack of the special symbols! I am quite new to this language, so I am trying to pay attention to everything now.\nThanks a lot!\n', '2001-09-17 23:19:42', 0, 0),
(90, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: What is \'brouku\'', 'Easy question, easy answer. \'Brouku\' means roughly the same as when you address someone \'sweetheart\', \'honey\' or \'baby\'. It is mostly used in jest in this form. A more endearing version is \'broučku\'.\nLiterally, it means \'beetle\' or \'little beetle\' (for \'broučku\'). Note that \'brouku\' is the form used when addressing . The dictionary form is \'brouk\' or \'brouček\'.\n', '2001-09-19 19:18:18', 89, 0),
(91, 13, 'David', '', 'what is rekovný', 'What does rekovný mean? It is not in my dictionary.\n', '2001-09-21 02:58:07', 0, 0),
(92, 13, 'Vessela', 'vesy@rice.edu', 'Re: What is \'brouku\'', '  Thank you so much for your reply!  Well, what can I say?  I am wondering a bit... Is this a common way to address a friend in the Czech Republic, or more like addressing your girlfriend (boyfriend) or may be a really good friend?!?!?!?!  I did get that e-mail from a Czech guy after all!\n  Many thanks again!\n', '2001-09-21 07:37:47', 90, 0),
(93, 13, 'Vessela', 'vesy@rice.edu', 'Re: What is \'brouku\'', '  Thank you so much for your reply!  Well, what can I say?  I am wondering a bit... Is this a common way to address a friend in the Czech Republic, or more like addressing your girlfriend (boyfriend) or may be a really good friend?!?!?!?!  I did get that e-mail from a Czech guy after all!\n  Many thanks again!\n', '2001-09-21 07:38:53', 90, 0),
(94, 13, 'kevin handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'rekovný', 'in my dictionary its listed as obsolete and jocular and means \"doughty\" i just wish i knew what doughty means\n', '2001-09-23 22:43:04', 91, 0),
(95, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: rekovný is doughty is brave', 'Doughty, as www.dictionary.com will tell you, means \'Marked by stouthearted courage; brave.\' There is a snooker player of that name but I never thought it meant anything.\nAnyway, \'rekovny\' means just that. It is derived from \'rek\' hero, so another translation might be \'heroic\'. It is truly obsolete and can be found in fables, mostly translations of Russian or Greek fairy tales or myths.\n', '2001-09-24 00:13:34', 94, 0),
(96, 13, 'David', '', 'Re: rekovný is doughty is brave', 'Thanks for your help.  I had never heard the word doughty before.  According to www.dictionary.com, it is \"seldom used, except in irony or burlesque.\"  My dictionary says that rek comes from German, and the common word for hero is hrdina or sometimes also bohatýr.  By the way, it also says that brambor comes from German, which I don\'t understand because I thought that potatoes are called Kartoffel in German.  Anyway, I found another Czech word that is not in my dictionary: víme.  What does that mean?\nOne more question: what is the difference between stěna and zed\'?\n', '2001-09-25 12:09:08', 95, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(97, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: stěna vs. zeď', 'Every \'stěna\' is \'zeď\' but not every \'zeď\' is \'stěna\'. \'Zeď\' is a wall as such, often meaning barrier. For instance, one standing in the middle of a field, or a wall of the house from outside. As well as wall from the outside. \'Stěna\' is a wall that is a part of a building from inside or a wall of a solid block from outside (like a cube). Rooms have \'stěna\'. It can also be used metaphorically, for example, \'stěna hory\' - slope of a mountain vs. \'zeď hor\' - the wall created by the mountains (as a barrier).\n', '2001-09-25 23:13:19', 96, 0),
(98, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: what is \'vímeť\'', 'I\'m not sure what that means without context. It is probably not a noun. It could be an archaic form of the verb \'vědět\' (to know) meaning \'because we know\'.\n', '2001-09-25 23:19:33', 96, 0),
(99, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: bohatýr - Russian tale hero', '\'bohatýr\' means \'hero\' only in the context of a Russian fairy-tale. It is never used in any other context except as irony.\n', '2001-09-25 23:21:54', 96, 0),
(100, 13, 'David', '', 'Re: bohatýr - Russian tale hero', 'Zase děkuji za pomoc.\n', '2001-09-26 12:49:14', 99, 0),
(101, 13, 'David', '', 'colloquial vs. literary Czech', 'I thought that your articles about colloquial vs. literary Czech were very interesting.  My question is not really a practical one, it is just something that is interesting for me.  How did the addition of v before o come about, e.g vokno?  It seems that this might actually be something that started in the language a long time ago, as there are some Czech words in the literary language that have vo whereas in Russian they have o: vous, vosa, and pavouk in Russian are us (usually plural - usy), osa, and pauk.  And coal in Polish is called węgiel.  So where did all of these extra v\'s come from?\n', '2001-09-26 23:22:08', 0, 0),
(102, 13, 'Naďa', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: colloquial vs. literary Czech', 'I am a Prague citizen and as a such I know that this phenomen of additional \"v\" in some words like e.g. okno=vokno, odjel=vodjel in informal spoken Czech is something very specific for Prague and the Central Bohemia region. It is part of its accent features. You would hardly hear a Moravian or Selesian person saying \"vokno\". \nSo personally I would think this \"v\" people started to use as they were \"lazy\" to pronounce some words properly. It is similar to making \"sme\" or \"sem\" from \"jsme\" \"jsem\".\n', '2001-09-27 20:49:25', 101, 0),
(103, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: colloquial vs. literary Czech', 'David, I had to look some of this up but I can still only give you a partial answer. \nThe \'v\' is a so called \'prothetic\' consonant which means simply \'added before\'. It is used to aid pronunciation and/or systematize it. \'Prothesis\' is common in many languages, the most common prothetic consonant in Slavonic languages is \'j\' as in \'jajco\' in Russian or \'jablko\' in Czech. \'v\' has started appearing in Czech since the 1300s, that\'s why it is not in Russian or Polish. \nMy best explanation for your examples is that this process is gradual and some words like \'vosa\', \'vousy\' had time to lexicalize, while others like \'okno\' did not (Jungmann, for example, in 1838, still lists both \'osa\' and \'vosa\' as possible - I don\'t have a proper historical dictionary of Czech at home for more detail and I\'m too lazy to go to the library). You must also remember that the colloquial prothetic \'v\' is mostly only in used in the Bohemian dialects but not in the Moravian dialects or in Slovak. \nI suspect that something else is going on in the Polish \'węgiel\'. My guess would be that the original old Slavonic bilabial \'w\' (similar to English \'w\') changed into \'u\' in Czech (although this mostly only happened when the \'w\' was originally a preposition as in \'úvod\') and into \'v\' followed by a nasal in Polish. But I could be completely wrong (especially, if \'uhlí\' has the same origin as the Latin \'ignis\') and I don\'t have the literature to back me up. The \'v\' never occurs before \'u\' in Czech, anyway.\nI seem to remember that Eisner has an entertaining chapter on this in \'Chrám i tvrz\' but again, I don\'t have a copy to hand.\n', '2001-09-28 03:22:10', 101, 0),
(104, 13, 'David', '', 'Re: colloquial vs. literary Czech', 'I know that sometimes pronunciation of words can change because it is easier a certain way, for example voiced consonants become devoiced at the ends of words, but is it really easier for you to pronounce a word beginning with vo instead of o?\n', '2001-09-28 03:22:55', 102, 0),
(105, 13, 'David', '', 'Re: colloquial vs. literary Czech', 'Ok, thanks for the info.  It is interesting.  I thought that you might be able to explain these things offhand, and of course I don\'t expect you to go to the library and do research just for me.\nWhat is \'Chrám i tvrz\' about?  Is there an English translation?\nAlso, there are a couple of more words that I can\'t find in my dictionary: hrotizánský and čuchnout.  Why do I keep finding words like this?  Am I using the wrong kind of books to learn from?  All of these words I found in Čapek. I know that you reccommend reading a \"trashy\" Czech novel, or something that I had already read in English, but I prefer to use something more interesting.\n', '2001-09-28 03:41:42', 103, 0),
(106, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: \'čuchnout\' a \'hrotizánský\'  - Čapek for learners', '\'čuchnout\' (pf.) is a collquial version of \'čichnout\' and it means \'to (take a) smell\'. It is often used in idioms in meanings like \'get a whif\' or \'get near something\'.\nI have no idea what \'hrotizánský\'  means. You would probably find the meaning in SSJČ (the biggest Czech dictionary) unless its a neologism which would not suprise me in Čapek. A context might help.\nThere\'s nothing wrong with reading Čapek to learn Czech. Anything that motivates you will do the trick. I recommned trashy books (and some people would include Čapek under that heading) because a lot of people don\'t think of them first and/or feel ashamed of reading them.\n\'Chrám i tvrz\' is a thick book consisting of a number of \'short\' stories about the Czech language. It was written by Pavel Eisner, a translator and philologist, for the common reader but it summarizes a lot of useful information from philology.\n', '2001-09-28 13:23:24', 105, 0),
(107, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: \'hrotizánský\' vs. \'hrozitánský\'', 'It just occured to me that your word could in fact be \'hrozitánský\' which would then mean \'terrible\' as in \'terribly large\'. It is an augmented colloquial version of \'hrozný\' (terrible).\n', '2001-09-28 17:20:49', 105, 0),
(108, 13, 'David', '', 'Re: \'hrotizánský\' vs. \'hrozitánský\'', 'Thanks again for the help, Dominik.  Yes, I checked again and you are right, the word is hrozitánský and not hrotizánský.  I just copied it wrong.\n', '2001-09-28 23:46:07', 107, 0),
(109, 13, 'Naďa', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: colloquial vs. literary Czech', 'Believe it or not - yes. But not the pronanciation of the single words as a such but the group of words in a sencence. It helps to link words together and enables speech to be faster. It is easier to say \"o vokně\" than \"o okně\", \"Jana vodjela\" than \"Jana odjela\". \nFor learners of Czech it is usually quite difficult to identify the \"single\" words in spoken Czech as Czechs link the words together very tightly when talking. What you hear is not \"mluvil o vokně\" but \"mluvilovokně\".\nThis is just my personal opinion how the additional \"v\" come into use in our language. There might be some scientific explanation which I dont know. :-)\n', '2001-10-02 12:47:56', 104, 0),
(110, 13, 'Mietek', 'szarynski@yahoo.com', 'Pronunciation of \"j\"', 'What is the correct pronunciation of \"j\" in words that begin with j + consonant, such as \"jsem\", \"jsou\", \"jmeno\", and \"jmenovat\"? \n', '2001-10-03 22:28:29', 0, 0),
(111, 13, 'David', '', 'Another obscure question', 'According to my Czech dictionary, the word tábor comes from Hebrew, but according to my Hebrew dictionary, a camp is called makhaneh, which is completely different.  The closest Hebrew word to tábor that I could find is tabur, which means navel.  What is going on here?  Is the Czech dictionary wrong?\n', '2001-10-03 22:38:10', 0, 0),
(112, 13, 'David', '', 'A more practical question', 'According to my Czech textbook, double consonants are \"not generally distinguished from single consonants,\" so denní is pronounced like dení.  My question is: does this rule apply to double consonants that are formed by the juxtaposition of root and prefix?  For example, is oddechnout pronounced like odechnout?  What about when the double consonant comes from a preposition and the following word, e.g. \"od toho\" = \"otoho\"?\nI have one more pronunciation question: In my textbook, the expression \"na shledanou\" has a footnote that says that, contrary to the regular rule of unvoiced consonants becoming voiced in front of voiced consonants, \"sh\" is pronounced like \"sch.\"  Is this a rule, or is it only in the expression \"na shledanou?\"  E.g. are \"shora\" and \"shrnout\"  pronounced like \"schora\" and \"schrnout?\"  If the answer is yes, then does this rule apply to a \"sh\" combination that is formed by the two adjacent words?  E.g. \"s husou\" and \"hlas husy\" = \"s chusou\" and \"hlas chusy?\"\n', '2001-10-03 22:59:48', 0, 0),
(113, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Pronunciation of \'j\' - not pronounced', 'No \'j\' is not pronounced when it is the first letter of word followed by a consonant. It is not \'incorrect\' to pronounce it but it is done only when enunciating. (Many Czechs don\'t realize that and insist on teaching it to students. It is in fact one of the hardest things to get used to as a teacher of Czech, i.e. to speak slowly, enunciate but not pronounce the \'j\'). \n<b>Note:</b> The \'j\' sound always comes back when you negate these verbs, because it becomes part of the syllable, i.e. neJsem, neJmenuju se.\n', '2001-10-04 00:24:31', 110, 0),
(114, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Pronunciation of double consonants', 'The answer is yes. That is, even when the double consonant comes about as a result of prefix+root, the pronunciation tends to be as a single consonant (as you indicate in your example). However, unlike with \'denní\', it is entirely permissible to pronounce them as two consonants (while with \'denní\' it would sound comical). I.e. you can get \'od-dechnout\' or \'od-toho\' as well as \'odechnout\' or \'otoho\'.\n', '2001-10-04 00:30:19', 112, 0),
(115, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Assimilation of \'sh\'', 'In fact, both \'zh\' and \'sch\' are possible exept in these three words: \'shora\', \'shůry\', \'shluk\' where it is always \'zh\'. \'zh\' is more common in Moravian dialects and \'sch\' in Bohemia.\nJust a note to my previous answer: even though the pronunciation of \'od toho\' as \'otoho\' is very common, it is not considered \'spisovná čeština\' - I know it\'s funny, but literary Czech has a spoken norm as well. What it means is that you shouldn\'t hear it from newsreaders on TV but sometimes you do.\n', '2001-10-04 01:59:15', 112, 0),
(116, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: tábor = camp - a word from Hebrew', 'Well, the dictionary is right but your interpretation isn\'t. The word does come from Hebrew but not as a translation of \'camp\' but as the name of a biblical mountain in Judges 4:6 (it\'s supposed to be a cone like hill, so for all I know, it may have meant \'navel\') which the Hussites used to name one of the mountains where they organized processions. On Mount (or rather hill) Tábor (now the name of a city around it) was an encampment of the most ferocious Hussite resistance group. From there, its meaning spread to \'military camp\' but now it means just \'camp\'. Apparently, such was the \'taborite\' military might that you can find echos of the word in the history of over 20 languages (including Polish, German, Turkish, Hungarian, Arabic, etc.) but I can\'t give you any examples (because I don\'t know them and the book I used to look this up doesn\'t list them).\n', '2001-10-04 02:19:05', 111, 0),
(117, 13, 'Naďa', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Pronunciation of \'j\' - not pronounced = hovorová čeština', 'Omlouvám se za příspěvek v češtině místo v angličtině, ale půjde mi to tak snáze:\nDominiku, nejsem si jistá, jestli tvoje odpověď na záležitost s \"j\" není trochu zavádějící. \nNení přeci pravda, že v mluvené češtině nemají být vždy tato \"j\" (ve slovech jako: jsem, jmenuji se) vyslovována. \nŘíkat \"menuju se .. a sem z ...\" si mohu dovolit pouze v hovorové češtině, když mluvím např. se svými přáteli či rodinou. Ale kdykoli při formálním styku, stejně tak jako v televizi či v rádiu, je správné vyslovování (tj. Jsem, Jsme, Jmenují se) naprostou samozřejmostí a nezbytností.\n', '2001-10-04 21:59:43', 113, 0),
(118, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Pronunciation of \'j\' - not pronounced = hovorová čeština', 'Milá Naďo, bohuel toto je běná miskoncepce. Je naprosto správné tato \'j\' nevyslovovat. Jejich výslovnost je povaována za hyperkorektní. Naleznete to v pravidlech výslovnosti. Stejně je ale lepí studenty učit jazyk, co nejblií tomu, co se říká. Respektive je nutné najít rovnováhu, tj. být na hranici spisovnosti a někdy ji v zájmu jednoduchosti i překročit. Nikdy to ale není jednoduché rozhodnutí. Dominik\n', '2001-10-05 19:32:48', 117, 0),
(119, 13, 'Antonio Rey', 'soutopenedo@hotmail.com', 'Comprehension of other languages by czechs', 'As I am Spanish, I can understand Italian and Portuguese fairly well, and can get easily enough training to understand French. I wonder how easily Czech people can manage to understand Polish, Russian, Bulgarian, the Balcanic Slavic languages, etc. And, of course, Slovak: can they understand spoken Slovak with a, say, 95% of correctness?\n', '2001-10-10 17:03:52', 0, 0),
(120, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Comprehension of other languages by czechs', 'That\'s a very interesting question. Let\'s start with Slovak. Any Czech who\'s about 25 and older will have been exposed to enough Slovak TV and radio (which used to be part of the old \'national\' broadcasts, so they will be able to understand well over 95% of written and maybe slightly less spoken Slovak. It wouldn\'t surprise me if the level of understanding dropped to below 80% for the generation now growing up and didn\'t get even lower with the subsequent generations. You must remember that some basic phrases and words are very different, e.g. Slovak \'ako sa vám páčí\' vs. Czech \'jak se vám líbí\' (How do you like.), while others are nearly identical \"Ako sa máte\" vs. \"Jak se máte?\".\nAn educated Czech can just about have some sort of conversation with a Pole or read a simple text. I\'d say the level of understanding is well below 30% for the population in general. (You must realize that there is relatively little contact between the two languages-i.e. no Polish is seen/read by Czechs).\nCzechs can understand individual words in Russian but not much more. Thus it is easier for me to guess at meanings of some words but often the word is completely different, or worse a similar sounding word means the oposite (e.g. čerstvý in Czech means \'fresh\' and \'čjorstvyj\' in Russian means \'stale\').\nIt\'s similar with other Slavonic languages like Ukrainian, Croatian and Slovenian and even worse with Bulgarian.\n', '2001-10-11 02:56:01', 119, 0),
(121, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Takzvaný -- is the dictionary wrong or am I going nuts?', 'Hi,\nI always assumed that \"takzvaný\" lacked the negative and ironic feeling of \"so-called\" in English. After all, I constantly see Czechs use \"so-called\" in inappropriate cases. But when I checked the Slovník spisoné četiny today out of curiosity, I found:\n\"usually (but at the same time unexpertly, too-informally, or erroneously) so called\": akumulatorový vozík, t-á. jetěrka; <i>iron.</i> t-é dobré úmysly.\"\nNot one mentioned meaning is neutral! Why, then, do Czechs write \"so-called\" as if it were non-judgmental? And isn\'t e.g.\n\"Hrávali ve větích městech, kde si pronajímali takzvané krejcarové boudy.\"\n(from a book I\'m translating) also non-judgmental?\nErik\n', '2001-10-11 06:54:26', 0, 0),
(122, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Takzvaný -- is the dictionary wrong or am I going nuts?', 'I\'d say you\'re correct. It can be non-judgemental and non-ironic in all these cases. So the best translation in your sentence would be \'..., as they were called.\' \n\'Krejcarové\' could be \'two-bit\' if it were for the American audience or \'ha\'penny\' for the British.\n', '2001-10-11 21:50:21', 121, 0),
(123, 13, 'rod', 'rdasdar@aol.com', 'what is the meaning of this word?', 'What is the meaning of \" carevnka\" in english?? thanks \n', '2001-10-13 01:09:17', 0, 0),
(124, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: carevna or červenka?', 'I\'m not sure what word you mean. \'carevna\' would be the Tsar\'s wife or basically the Russian equivalent for \'queen\'.\n\'červenka\' is either some small bird or sometimes some cattle disease (something like \'swine fever\'). I\'m not too clear on the terminology but a dictionary and context should clear it up.\n', '2001-10-13 04:28:07', 123, 0),
(125, 13, 'David', '', 'Re: carevna or červenka?', 'Tsarevna in Russian is the tsar\'s daughter.  The tsar\'s wife is tsaritsa.\n', '2001-10-13 11:35:40', 124, 0),
(126, 13, 'David', '', 'neznámá slova', 'What do umolousat and tístek mean?\n', '2001-10-13 11:40:58', 0, 0),
(127, 13, 'Rod', 'rdasdar@aol.com', 'Re: carevna or červenka?', 'Thank you for reply. So \"carevnka\" doesnot mean anything in Czech language as a word or term?    \n', '2001-10-14 13:09:29', 124, 0),
(128, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: carevna - tsar\'s wife in Czech', 'That\'s right. But in Czech \'carevna\' is the tsar\'s wife. At least in the Russian fairy tales I know. Catherine the Great would be called that as well.\n', '2001-10-14 21:26:33', 125, 0),
(129, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: \'umolousat\' a \'tístek\'', 'Well, you certainly know how to pick them. Something is \'umolousaný\' when it slightly dirty and sticky at the same time. Like a piece of paper you\'ve held after eating a greasy saussage. I can\'t think of an English word for it.\n\'tístek\' on the other hand is either someone or something that is lucky or will bring luck. Context will be essential.\n', '2001-10-14 21:29:42', 126, 0),
(130, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Takzvaný -- is the dictionary wrong or am I going nuts?', 'Hiya,\nOh, I\'d already solved that sentence before I wrote that submission. I used my typical device for translating tzv. -- just quotes, which are neutral, brief, and unobtrusive. \"Krejcarova bouda\" became \"penny booth,\" a term which actually exists in US English (they\'re e.g. shoot-for-a-prize booths; you can find them at fairs and festivals, though it\'s been a long time since they cost a penny).\n', '2001-10-15 07:22:43', 122, 0),
(131, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Name etymology -- Šantrůček and Nekola', 'Hi,\nI\'m doing a fan translation (so far just šuplíkový) of Už jsme doma\'s first album, and I\'ve run into two names in the lyrics that I am too lazy to research. Bad Erik!\nŠantrůček -- this is the title of a song with no lyrics but \"Kobyla / Šantrůček\". I know \"zašantročit\" is something like \"appropriate for oneself\"; are there any more connotations?\nNekola -- (from \"Sousedská\": \"Soused ing Nekola / Soused je ing\") Mr. Unwheels or Mr. Uncola -- is he anything more?\n', '2001-10-15 07:30:06', 0, 0),
(132, 13, 'masi', 'masi_cz@centrum.cz', 'Re: Name etymology --  antrůček and Nekola', 'Hi!\n    let\'s have a look for following URL:\nhttp://www.noise.cz/ujd/ .. and I think you will get what it means ..\n    And .. I\'m going to concert of this band today .. it\'s really bad you can\'t go .. :-)\nMasi\n', '2001-10-16 17:27:14', 131, 0),
(133, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Name etymology --  antrůček and Nekola', 'Hi Masi,\nI\'ve never been satisfied with the noise.cz (and CD-book) translation. Šantrůček, for example, simply becomes \"Shantrooczech\", which is a pointless pun that has been cheap for at least ten years, and gives the listener no idea that Šantrůček is a name, and doesn\'t play with \"zašantročit\". \"Kobyla\" is a mare, not (necessarily) a hack. \nNekola is left untranslated (it could be \"Nring,\" \"Nround,\" \"Wheelnt,\" or many other fine words). The translator didn\'t try to work with the bilingual pun \"Sou se d-je-ing\". And so on.\nThe translations don\'t even attempt to convey the rhythm of the original.\nAnd so on.\nI\'m heading to UJD when they come to Brno; I can\'t wait.\n', '2001-10-16 23:29:04', 132, 0),
(134, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Name etymology --  santrůček and Nekola', 'According to Jungmann\'s dictionary (an essential household item), \'santrok\' used to be essentially synonymous with \'lichva\' i.e. \'usury\'. It could also mean a \'thief\' or \'robber\', not all the contexts are clear. If you want something that scans, go for \'petty thief\', if that\'s \'co tím chtěl básník říci\', that is.\nActually, \'zasantrocit\' in my idiom, means simply \'to mislay something\'.\nNekolný as an adejctive used to mean something like \'rude\', \'disagreeable\' or \'cantankerous\'. I doubt anyone is still aware of that meaning. I would imagine they just like the sound. But it is definitely not \'unwheels\' even though, the lyrics writer may have had that in mind.\nUz jsme doma is a great band, do share some of your work, when you\'re done.\n', '2001-10-17 04:20:46', 131, 0),
(135, 13, 'Natalie Casper', 'ncasper929@mac.com', 'Case & Proper Nouns', 'My friend, Petr, once tried to explain to me how the spelling of his name would change with the various cases.  I am still confused and would like to appear like I know just a bit more about his language by using his name correctly.  How exactly would his name be changed for the various cases, and when would each case be used?  Thanks!\n', '2001-10-20 10:29:23', 0, 0),
(136, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Case & Proper Nouns', 'If you communicate in English, the only form you could really use in a conversation is the addressing (vocative) form. I.e. you use it when you\'re addressing him by his name rather than talking about him. It is:\n<b>Petře</b> pronounced with the soft \'r\'. You can use it in an English conversation and still make sense. E.g. Hi, Petře, how are you? Petře! Come over here.\nThe other forms are:\nPetr has a house.\nI see Petra.\nI am at Petra.\nIt belongs Petrovi.\nWe were talking about Petrovi.\nWe went there with Petrem.\nBut as you see, it makes very little sense.\n', '2001-10-20 21:48:05', 135, 0),
(137, 13, 'mariemann', 'mariemann@altavista.com', 'Re: A tiny question', 'brouk means beetle, brouku is a 5th case\ndiminitive is brouc^ek, brouc^ku and it is a nice way to call cute, dear people (my mom writes me sometimes, Muj mily brouc^ku.\n', '2001-10-22 23:37:47', 89, 0),
(138, 13, 'Sean Wagle', 'Sean.Wagle@mindspring.com', 'Gender of nouns ending in \'e\'', 'The books I\'m using kind of gloss this over, and say that words ending in \'e\' could be any gender. But I still need a better system for figuring this out. My dictionary doesn\'t give genders for most words (I guess the authors assumed Czechs already know this!) \nSo I\'ve been trying to work out something that will get it right most of the time, and these are my conclusions:\nSome words are obvious by their meaning like soudce (judge) or tchyně (mother-in-law).\nMost words ending in \'ce\' (usually \'ice\') are feminine. There are many of these, and maybe half or more of all words ending in \'e\' are this type.\nMost words of small living things (vejce,stěně) are neuter.\nWords that end in \'ie\' (amnestie,anarchie,galarie) keep their feminine gender from the donor language of French or Latin.\nMany combinations of \'e\' like \'fe\' and \'he\' don\'t even exist in Czech (or have so few words, you don\'t need to worry about them.)\n================\nSo... this leaves 5 remaining endings that are relatively common, that I\'m still not sure about: \'je,le,se,ze,e\'\n\'je\' is a soft sound, as in bóje (bouy) or kóje (booth). Usually feminine? That\'s what I\'d guess, if I had to.\n\'le\' is a tough one. Some words like chvíle (moment) are feminine and some like pole (field) are neuter. Some other examples (which I don\'t know) are boule (bulge) cedule (notice) cibule (onion) datle (date). Usually neuter? Usually feminine? What\'s the best guess?\n\'se\' and \'e\' are soft sounds and I suspect they are usually feminine. (Right?) Many of these are foreign loan words. Deprese (depression) diskuse (discussion) galoe (one galosh/shoe)\n\'ze\' also contains a lot of foreign words. exploze (explosion) fúze (merger) iluze (illusion) koroze (corrosion). Normally feminine? Normally neuter?\nFinally \'e\' is fairly common. But more of these words look Czech than as if borrowed from elsewhere. Drae (coated pill) gae (salary) kue (skin) loue (puddle). Again, what gender is most common... usually?\n', '2001-10-26 23:22:51', 0, 0),
(139, 13, 'Mera', '', 'Tense times', '(Sorry. I couldn\'t resist a cheap joke.)\nI\'m having enormous amounts of trouble with aspect. I know that the imperfective is supposed to be used when an action is either repeated or unfinished, but whenever I think I have a grip on it I come across some sentence in my book that seems, to me, to be in the wrong aspect. (I know this is a problem with my Czech, not with the book. I trust David Short\'s judgement much more than my own.)\nI spent time at the library going through as many books on sundry Slavic languages as I could, but none of them were any clearer on aspect. Is it really just \"repeated or unfinished\" and should I just plow forward? Or am I missing something? I feel like I\'m missing something, but, frankly, I\'ve felt that way since I started Czech. \nDoes anything like a table that corresponds English and Czech tenses exist?\nThanks so much.\nMera\n', '2001-10-27 14:16:41', 0, 0),
(140, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Aspects of tense times', 'This is a very good question. I\'ve been long wondering how to make aspect more accessible to students. You are indeed correct, there seem to be more exceptions than regular uses of aspect. On the other hand, the correspondence to English tenses is not very reliable. However, I do think that things can be made easier if you convert the Czech aspect into tenses, thus getting:\nPast imperfective\nPast repetitive\nPast perfective\nPresent imperfective\nPresent repetitive\nFuture imperfective\nFuture repetitive\nFuture perfective\nYou can then asign these \'tenses\' functions without worrying about aspect in general. I\'m working on this and will post the results of my work on this site. You can also look for equivalent tenses for individual functions of each aspect/tense rather than an equivalent to the form. For example, the function of the present imperfective expressing actions happening at the moment, is translated into English as present continuous (progressive). On the other hand, the function of present imperfective expressing things continuing from the past into the present is translated into English as present perfect.\nI.e. Dívám se teď na televizi. = I\'m watching TV now. BUT\nDívám se na televizi od 8 hodin. = I\'ve been watching TV since 8 o\'clock.\nYou will still find certain things are hard to translate but your job as a student should be made easier.\n', '2001-10-27 19:20:23', 139, 0),
(141, 13, 'Gloria Ptacek McMillan', 'gloria@bohemica.com', 'The meaning of -ník', 'What does -ník do when added to a noun?\nThe evening show on Ceska Televize 1 is called \"Večerník\"...but what does\nit mean?  How does it change \"večer\"?\nDoes it mean \"evening stuff\" or \"evening show\"?\nIn general what does the -nik do to a noun?\n', '2001-10-28 02:36:13', 0, 0),
(142, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: The meaning of -ník', '-ník as a suffix changes the meaning of the noun to something akin to: \'that which of...\' or \'that which is based on/is in/is for ...\'. It can also mean the doer similar to English -er (in Czech it is one of many such suffixes).\nE.g.\ntýden (week) - týdenník (a weekly [bulletin, paper])\nprávo (law) - právník (lawyer)\nšaty (dress) - šatník (wardrobe)\nmíle (mile) - milník (milestone)\nnoc (night) - nočník (potty, chamber pot)\nThe link may not always be obvious, see:\nhora (mountain) - horník (miner) - although \'hora\' used to mean \'mine\'\nkřižovat (to roam, to tack) - křižník (destroyer)\n', '2001-10-28 03:31:41', 141, 0),
(143, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Gender of nouns ending in \'e\'', 'You don\'t ask for little, Sean. To be honest, your analysis went deeper than I ever did (or felt the need to). The reason there is fairly little information about gender of nouns from this perspective is that native speakers seem to know the gender (except in rare case) and few learner can actually profit from such detailed set of rules. Most prefer to memorize the gender or learn it in context. This is what I could come up with:\n-je - yes, I would say it is usually feminine \n-le - is a toss up, I would say that words of foreign origin like tabule, fabule, míle are feminine (although then there\'s the All-Slavonic \'píle\') while the native ones (pole, tele, vole, pískle) are neuter. There seem to be more of the feminine ones.\n-se/še - yes, I agree they seem to be mostly feminine even ones of Czech origin - výše (height), chýše (hut)\n-ze - is mostly neuter even in Czech words like příze (something for weaving)\n-že - is mostly feminine (I can only think of páže [page] for neuter; there\'s also kníže and hrabě which have masculine meaning and agreement but follow the kuře model). Be careful, dražé has a long é at the end, but it is still neuter.\nYou should also keep in mind that there are two neuter models ending in -e - moře and kuře (so more trouble there).\nThis reminds me of another common ending for -e nouns:\n-ře which is (probably) always neuter (kuře, zvíře, tygře).\n', '2001-10-28 03:31:42', 138, 0),
(144, 13, 'nanno', 'debnan@rendo.dekooi.nl', 'translation', 'What are the czech words for kiss and how do I say: \"I kiss you\"?\n', '2001-10-28 17:15:10', 0, 0),
(145, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: translation of kiss', 'a kiss = pusa, polibek (more poetic)\nto kiss = dát pusu, líbat (more poetic)\nI kiss you/I\'m kissing you = líbám tě\n', '2001-10-28 21:38:34', 144, 0),
(146, 13, 'Andrew', 'none@none.com', 'Books', 'Well I am a beginner at this langauge and I have been searching for a book that can help me with all the grammer stuff. Like verb conjugation and word order. What do you think is the best book for beginners?\n', '2001-11-09 03:12:35', 0, 0),
(147, 13, 'Ron', 'stormborne@hotmail.com', 'Please help with translation problem', 'I am sorry to ask for the favor of a translation, but I don\'t have many resources to work with where I live and I want to send a card to two acquaintances who was just relocated unexpectedly because of work. How would I say this to them?: \"I miss seeing my two bicyclists. How is the new job? I hope all is well with you, and we see each other again soon.\"  \nI\'ve tried the online English-to-Czech translator at InterTran but I don\'t trust the results. I compared words to another online dictionary and came up with different meanings to my words. \nThank you very much,\nRon\n', '2001-11-10 03:53:35', 0, 0),
(148, 13, 'christina', 'Christina.Moulis@dewrsb.gov.au', 'moravian music', 'I have some great cds Čechomor, Mezi Horami by Českomoravské Hudební Společnost.\nbut I can\'t understand the words very well. they seem to be using non standard czech, looks a bit like slovak according to my slovak friend.  Is there some special moravian dialect they use for their lidová hudba?\n', '2001-11-12 10:42:26', 0, 0),
(149, 13, 'christina', 'Christina.Moulis@dewrsb.gov.au', 'Re: Tense times', 'Some of David shorts book definitely uses the wrong aspect, it happens after he introduces the past tense, but before he introduces the perfective grammar explanations, so I would only trust from chapter 11 onwards. eg chapter 9 dialogue 1 should be -\nIvan zná ředitele a ten slíbil (not sliboval)\n', '2001-11-12 10:49:13', 139, 0),
(150, 13, 'christina', 'christina.Moulis@dewrsb.gov.au', 'conjugation of verbs', 'when I was at summer school in Prague, we were talking about verbs (class IV in your complete conjugation chart ending in í) and the third person present plural.\nMy czech friends and tutors seemed to think that it was acceptable to use -í all the time in present-day speech, you didn\'t have to worry about which vebs use -ejí, except in a very small subset of verbs, about 5 , although no-one could agree on a list.\ndo you agree with this. If so, what is the small subset of verbs that you must use -ejí?\n', '2001-11-12 11:15:43', 0, 0),
(151, 13, 'NK', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: moravian music', 'Your guess is right. There are severe regions in the Czech Republic where various dialects are still alive. These dialects sometimes vary not only from region to region but sometimes even within one region, e.g. within Moravia.\n', '2001-11-12 18:53:49', 148, 0),
(152, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: conjugation of verbs', 'I think your tutors were right. To be honest, I cannot think of any \'sázet\' IV class verbs where a simple -í would not be enough. The irregular chtějí requires it but that is something different. The problem comes in the imperative where you need to know the correct 3rd pers. pl. form to be able to form the imperative. So even though you can say \'sází\' the imperative will still be \'sázejí\' and not \'sáz\'. \nYou can also look at it another way. You can adopt the colloquial ending -ej which can be used with all group IV. models. But you will then need to lean the imperative pretty much word by word.\n', '2001-11-13 03:18:01', 150, 0),
(153, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Translation problems', 'The problem with a lot of texts people write to be translated is that they try to be too personal, or put as much of their personal feelings into it as they can. I\'m not criticising you or anybody else but I\'ve had problems translating so many short personal messages so I thought I\'d share some of my frustrations. The trick is: Be boring. Use simple sentences otherwise you will be misunderstood. Avoid idioms. It is hard to guess what would be seen as idiomatic from the point of view of another language but that is always the problem. \nHere\'s how I would translate your sentence but without context I can only say \'I miss my two bicyclists\' and \'How do you like your new job?\'. I also have to assume that you\'re talking to two people.\n\'Stýská se mi po mých dvou cyklistech. Jak se vám líbí v nové práci? Doufám, že se vám daří dobře, a že se zase brzo uvidíme.\'\nThat\'s why you cannot trust a machine to translate. And that\'s why a human translator needs so much background information. A lot of people just ask how do you say this, but without context you have a fifty percent chance of not getting the right equivalent. In a way, that\'s how you can tell a good translator. It\'s the one that asks lots of questions. The bad ones just take the money and run.\n', '2001-11-13 04:08:02', 147, 0),
(154, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Books', 'The best advice I can give you is go to the Bookstore section on Bohemica.com. It lists all the books I know about and what I think about them. Some you can even buy through Amazon but many you can\'t.\n', '2001-11-13 04:15:50', 146, 0),
(155, 13, 'Erik', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: moravian music', 'Since you like Čechomor, perhaps you\'ll like this bit of trivia about their name: while the word doesn\'t, strictly speaking, mean anything, \"Čech\" means \"Czech\" or \"Bohemian\" (i.e. not Moravian) and \"mor\" means \"plague.\" So you could take it as \"a Bohemian plague\" or perhaps \"a plague upon Bohemians\" or even \"a plague from Bohemia.\" Dominik could tell you which of the interpretations would feel most natural for a Czech. THE NAME\'S A JOKE, OF COURSE -- the group is, as it name suggests, mixed Bohemian/Moravian.\n', '2001-11-13 22:50:45', 148, 0),
(156, 13, 'Mera', '', 'More trivia', 'Does anybody know how artsy people became known as \"Bohemians\"? I\'ve been wondering this for awhile.\n', '2001-11-18 13:39:14', 155, 0),
(157, 13, 'Mera', '', 'She tried to save the boy', 'I\'ve been even more confused aspect-wise as of late and was looking on the internet for more resources. I came across this really great Russian site -- I wish I remembered the address. It had a whole section on aspect that included something about \"successfully completed\" and used this story to illustrate: Varya sees a little boy fall into the Moika Canal in St. Petersburg. She throws herself into the water but by the time she gets to the place where she saw the boy he\'s disappeared and drowned. When she talks about this she won\'t say \"I tried to rescue the boy\" but just uses the imperfect form for \"rescue\".\n(I thought that was a cute story.) Anyway, first off, is this the way it is in Czech? Second off, that looks really ambiguous to me, and suggests that she\'s still in the water looking for the boy. \nAnother aspect question: My book says that \"Delala jsem chyby\" means both \"I made mistakes\", \"I kept making mistakes\", and \"I used to make mistakes\". This is probably the most confusing thing I\'ve read about aspect. What? Czech always seemed to me to be an incredibly precise language, what with things like \"mrtev/mrtvy\" (In English, of course, if you\'re dead, you\'re dead) and verbs like \"domluvit\" and \"premluvit\" when in English you say things like \"come to an agreement with\" and \"con someone into\". Again, this looks like one particular phrase could mean several different things. Is English just persnickety about tenses?\nI\'ve looked through all the Czech books I can find and even scoured every book on Slavic languages at the library (for some reason, my little underfunded library has an awful lot of \"Teach Yourself...\" books -- there\'s \"Teach Yourself Serbo-Croat\" and \"Bulgarian\" and \"Slovene\" and \"Polish\" and \"Russian\", in case I ever got bored) and none of them are any clearer on aspect. I\'m starting to get the feeling I\'m making this too complicated.\nA slightly less important question: Does \"i\" mean the same thing as \"a\", namely, \"and\"? When do you use \"i\" and when do you use \"a\"?\nThanks so much. I\'m full of questions.\n', '2001-11-18 14:37:27', 0, 0),
(158, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: \'i\' vs. \'a\'', 'There is a significant differrence between i and a. \'i\' means basically \'as well as\' while \'a\' means \'and\'. Sometimes their meanings overlap slightly but there\'s always a subtle change in perspective.\n\'Byl tam Petr a Pavel\' vs. \'Byl tam Petr i Pavel\'\n\'Petr and Pavel were there.\' vs. \'Petr and Pavel were both there.\'\n\'I Petr tam byl\' - \'Even Petr showed up\'/\'Petr was there, as well\'\nYou can even have them as part of the same clause in spoken language.\n\'A i Petr tam byl\' - \'And Petr was there, too.\'\n', '2001-11-18 18:43:09', 157, 0),
(159, 13, 'Mera', '', 'Re: \'i\' vs. \'a\'', 'Thanks. That makes sense.\nMera the small, blood-sucking insect (I just learned that my name means \'flea\' or \'louse\' in Czech. Great.)\n', '2001-11-19 11:43:20', 158, 0),
(160, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Bohemians', 'The use of the term \'Bohemian lifestyle\' aparently goes back to the 1400s when a lot of Czech Gypsies known for their free way of living migrated from the Czech lands on the back of the Hussite exploits. But I don\'t have any more details or reliable sources.\n', '2001-11-20 03:13:49', 156, 0),
(161, 13, 'macronyx', 'macronyx@centrum.cz', 'Re: Comprehension of other languages by czechs', 'me, as a 16 year old Czech without any theoretical linguistic knowledge of any kind can understand Slovak very well.  Although there are quite many words which differ, e.g. the names of months (Slovak uses western-like names for them - oktober - rijen etc.), many words in common Slovak are from foreign origin (mur in Slovak - zed in Czech (Mauer in German, (wall)), names of plants and animals etc. etc., every inteligent person can guess the meaning and understand without significiant problems. The Czechs can listen to Slovak radios, TVs, read Slovak books, travel round Slovak etc. without problems (thought I wanted to read a book in Slovak (Ladislav Mnacko) and after few pages rather decided to read it in Czech, it showed as very uncomfortable.\nI was also trying to understand spoken Polish and when the Poles spoke very slow, it was possible, thought I didn\'t understand everything.\n', '2001-11-27 00:12:40', 120, 0),
(162, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: what is Mera', 'I don\'t know how you pronounce your name but I imagine that it is pretty far from \'blecha\' (flea) or \'ves\' (louse). In fact I cannot think of any Czech word (insect name or otherwise) that would be close to Mera. The nearest I believe is \'mura\' which is a generic term for night butterflies (moths). Then there is \'mira\' which means \'measure\' or a short name for Miroslav. \n', '2001-11-27 03:32:10', 159, 0),
(163, 13, 'Mera', '', 'Re: what is Mera', 'Hmm, wow, it said in the dictionary at slovnik.cz that it meant \"flealouse\". I\'m glad to know that I\'m not a flea.\n', '2001-11-27 13:05:15', 162, 0),
(164, 13, 'Christina moulis', 'Christina.Moulis@dewrsb.gov.au', 'Verb Aspect', 'Dominic, could you tell us how russian aspect is different from czech?  \nFirstly, is there this sense of completion/round trip.\neg In russian,  \n  îíŕ őîäčëŕ íŕ ęčíî - She went to the cinema (and came back).\n  îíŕ ďîřëŕ íŕ ęčíî - She went to the cinema (and is still there).\n  îíŕ řëŕ íŕ ęčíî - She was on her way to the cinema ....(when something happened)\nFor the verbs of motion, it seems to me that in russian is is a unidirectional vs multidirectional distinction, but in czech it is frequency which is important.\nAnd what about the non verbs of motion, does a sense of completion or a sense of an action being done/undone matter like it does in russian?\nThe reason I am asking is that there are heaps of books about russian aspect and verbs of motion written in English, but I have only seen the most superficial treatment of these subjects in books about czech grammar written in English, it is very frustrating.\nHow much can I rely on russian grammar books to teach me about czech aspect?\nAlternatively, do you know of a book on czech grammar written in english, which gives an exhaustive treatment of aspect and verbs of motion?\n', '2001-11-28 10:34:01', 0, 0),
(165, 13, 'Christina Moulis', 'Christina.moulis@dewrsb.gov.au', 'Re: Verb Aspect', 'Sorry, you need to change the encoding of the russian to cyrillic\n', '2001-11-28 10:35:14', 164, 0),
(166, 13, 'David', '', 'Re: Verb Aspect', 'That is a very good question.  I think that the aspects of most verbs are used in more or less the same way in Russian and Czech, but there are a few common verbs that are somewhat different.\nVerbs of motion is one category in which there are definite differences. The Russian \"ona pola v kino\" can be translated, depending on the context, to \"she went to the movies,\" \"she has gone to the movies,\" \"she had gone to the movies,\" \"she set out for the movie theater,\" etc.\nBut in Czech there is no such word as \"pola\" (or would it be půla?). The perfective form of verbs of motion is only used in the future tense, and never in the past, infinitive, or subjunctive.  Another difference is that with adverbs meaning \"sometimes\" the determinate form is used in Czech, while the indeterminate form is used in Russian: \"Jde časem do kina\" - \"Inogda ona chodit v kino.\" By the way, in Russian it should always be \"_v_ kino\", and not \"_na_ kino,\" and the word kino is indeclinable in Russian but declinable in Czech.\nBesides that there are probably several other common differences in use of verbal aspect between Czech and Russian, but as far as I know, there are no more rules that can be applied to a large class of verbs.  You have to deal with each word separately.  In Czech it is perfectly normal to say \"budu moct,\" whereas in Russian you must say \"smogu,\" and never \"budu moč\'.\"\nAn in-depth article about aspect usage in Russian can be found at http://www.websher.net/srl/asp.html.\n', '2001-11-29 02:46:51', 164, 0),
(167, 13, 'christina', 'christina.moulis@dewrsb.gov.au', 'Re: Verb Aspect', 'Thanks, I already have heaps of books on russian aspect, I need a detailed treatment of czech aspect.\nYes, of course it\'s v kino, I haven\'t studied russian for 5 years so I\'m a bit rusty.\n', '2001-11-29 06:49:55', 166, 0),
(168, 13, 'Renee', 'rstarek@hotmail.com', 'Translate Phrase', 'How do you say Happy Birthday in Czech?\n', '2001-11-29 19:58:07', 0, 0),
(169, 13, 'Renee', 'rstarek@hotmail.com', 'Is this right?', 'I think it is \"Vse nejlepsi k narozeninam\", but I don\'t know how to write it with the accents.  Let me know if this is not correct.  Please help!\n', '2001-11-29 20:06:20', 0, 0),
(170, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Is this right?', 'Yes, this is correct ascii version. With accents it\'s<br><br>\nVe (or vechno) nejlepí k narozeninám..\n', '2001-11-29 22:44:30', 169, 0),
(171, 13, 'laura', 'juicee_juice@yahoo.com', 'pronunciation help, prosim!', 'Ahoj,\n    I am looking for a good guide with phonetic pronunciations, does anyone have suggestions?\nIn the mean time, can someone tell me how to pronounce this prhase?\n\"Hodne stesti\"\nIt does translate to good luck, correct?\nprosim,\nlaura\n', '2001-11-30 12:54:05', 0, 0),
(172, 13, 'foxík', 'littlefox@atlas.cz', 'Re: Translate Phrase', 'Happy Birthday! = Ve nejlepí k narozenám! or Krásné narozeniny!\n', '2001-11-30 19:33:44', 168, 0),
(173, 13, 'Cassandra', '', 'haha', 'Can any one post a sound wave of how to say I love you in czech for me thank you!!\nCassandra Suchy-Mabrouk\n', '2001-12-12 08:44:02', 0, 0),
(174, 13, 'Gratefull for any help', '', 'Would someone be kind enough to translate ?', 'Could someone pleaase translate the follwing?\n\"tato verze je upline nova a opet lepsi nez ta predch ozi\"\nNot sure if this is actually important, but it might be, and I\'d be very thankfull for assistance.\n', '2001-12-13 13:59:47', 0, 0),
(175, 13, 'NK', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Would someone be kind enough to translate ?', 'It says: \"This is the latest version which is again better than the previous one.\"\n', '2001-12-13 14:56:54', 174, 0),
(176, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: haha', '<a href=http://ctknews.com/tutor/tutor1.htm>http://ctknews.com/tutor/tutor1.htm</a>\nThis site was recently brought to my attention and it contains a few interesting phrases in Czech including \'I love you\' or the pronunciation of the names of some important Czech politicians.\n', '2001-12-15 04:29:42', 173, 0),
(177, 13, 'Cassandra', '', 'Re: haha', 'Oh thank you thank you so much you are so kind!!! \nCassandra\n', '2001-12-16 03:58:31', 176, 0),
(178, 13, 'Veronica', 'veronica@mail2goddess.com', 'Direction for a beginner?', 'Ahoj!\nMy name is Veronica and I would be very grateful if someone would kindly answer a few questions that I have. I would like to ask the forum if anyone had a few book recomendations? I have decided to take Czech as a second language and I\'m really not sure where to start. I looked at the book listings in the Bohemica site and I am wondering if they are well suited to someone who is a complete beginner. I would like to find a couple of books and tapes that could show me the basics, beginning with letter and word pronunciation, and then vocabulary, phrases and grammar. I hear Pimsleur Czech is supposed to be good, although it is abridged. I would also like to know if it is possible to learn some Czech from dictionary and Czech newspapers and magazines, and if there is any books I should stay away from. Any opinions would be helpful and greatly appreciated.\nDekuji Vam,\nVeronica\n', '2001-12-17 09:26:02', 0, 0),
(179, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Direction for a beginner?', 'You didn\'t say whether you have a teacher or not. The only two complete teach-yourself textbooks are David Short and James Naughton. The others are either books that require a teacher or are not very good introductions into the language for the serious learner. If you just want to learn a few phrases for your trip to Prague, any book with a tape will do. Most learners end up with several books before they settle on the one that works for them. Plus, there\'s no one book that\'s perfect in all aspects, anyway.\nYou need a basic knowledge of grammar to learn any Czech from books or newspapers. Using a dictionary is not enough because many words change their forms significantly based on the change in meaning.\n', '2001-12-18 01:46:04', 178, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(180, 13, 'Veronica', 'veronica@mail2goddess.com', 'Re: Direction for a beginner?', 'Ah, I am afraid that there are no teachers of that sort where I am. I am unfortunate to live in a small town in Eastern Kentucky where finding a French teacher is very difficult, let alone someone who is qualified to teach Czech. I am indeed planning a trip to Prague in the spring, to see the city and visit a few of my (Czech) friends, but I really would like to learn the language, because I might consider living there someday. However, I will definately look at the authors that you have mentioned and check out a few phrasebooks and tapes. Perhaps it might be best if I bought several books and audio tapes, and piece together my own lessons through them.\nThank you very much for your response, Dominik. If you have any other suggestions, I would be very grateful to hear them. \nVeronica\n', '2001-12-18 12:08:40', 179, 0),
(181, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Directions for a beginner-Do the usual', 'My usual suggestion is take an intensive month-long Czech course in Prague, Brno or anywhere in the Czech Republic (see a list in Links). You\'ll learn a lot, meet lots of Czechs and have a good time. Believe me, it will come out cheaper than coming out here on your own (they usually provide accommodation cheaper than any hotel or hostel in town). If you have the time (and the cash to come anyway), do it!\n', '2001-12-19 04:15:05', 180, 0),
(182, 13, 'Veronika', 'veronica@mail2goddess.com', 'Re: Directions for a beginner-Do the usual', 'Oh, that is a great idea! I didn\'t realize that Prague offered extensive class courses in Czech, but I suppose it would make sense now, wouldn\'t it? I think I will purchase a few books and tapes anyway to get a feel of the language and learn some \"survival\" Czech, and then I\'ll see if I can book a course in advance.\nThank you for the good advice, Dominik!\nVeronica\n', '2001-12-19 04:48:41', 181, 0),
(183, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Veselé Vánoce a Stastný Nový rok', 'I\'d like to wish all Czech Forum participants a great year 2002 and lots of Czech in the head. Here\'s an online card for everybody:\n<a href=http://www.bohemica.com/2002>http://www.bohemica.com/2002</a>\nDominik\n', '2001-12-27 03:05:14', 0, 0),
(184, 13, 'Jorge Garate Bartik', 'jogabar@yahoo.com', 'Alguien me puede ayudar atraducir del espańol al Checo.', 'Hola:\nTengo familiares en la Republica Checa y no puedo comunicarme por el problema del idioma, ellos no tienen acceso al espańol.\nAlguien me puede ayudar ?\n', '2001-12-28 00:51:23', 0, 0),
(185, 13, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Why Sylvestr?', 'I was wondering why the Czechs call their New year celebration \"Sylvestr.\"  What does this name mean?\nDekuji,\nGloria\n', '2001-12-28 22:41:10', 0, 0),
(186, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Why Sylvestr? or Silvestr', 'Silvestr happens to be the name of a saint (a fourth century Pope) who died on January 31 and when that later became the last day of the year, it just happened to be his name that was on the calendar. So Czechs just refer to that day by the name of the saint on the calendar. There are other significant days like \"Josef\" or \"Vaclav\" that are celebrated (although only by people who know a Josef or Vaclav - which is pretty much everybody).\n', '2001-12-29 04:49:35', 185, 0),
(187, 13, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom', 'Re: Why Sylvestr? or Silvestr', 'Thanks for the explanation!  The only Sylvester we\nknow here in the US is that cartoon cat who chases\nTweety Pie in the Warner Brothers cartoons.\nI was fairly certain it wasn\'t named for him!\nHappy Silvestr!\nGloria\n', '2001-12-29 22:58:17', 186, 0),
(188, 13, 'peter', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'diary/calander', 'Does anyone know a place that sells diaries (or calanders), czech language editions, by mail order or over the internet.\nI want to use one all through this year so I will really learn the czech names of the days and months, but I am not visiting the czech republic till the summer.\n', '2001-12-31 03:08:05', 0, 0),
(189, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'imperfect aspect', 'Aspect really is (as Dominik has pointed out somewhere on this site) the hardest part of Czech to master--all in all, it\'s even harder than declensions, because even though declensions are complex, they are pretty much out there in the open, and relatively straightforward to describe. Aspect runs deep, and even after eight years of speaking Czech, I still feel I am on thin ice as far as aspect is concerned.\nSo in other words--don\'t worry, you\'re not alone.\nStill, you\'re basically on the right track. Note, however, that for things that \"used to be\" or \"always used to happen\", a common variant is the \"-avat\" ending (changes to \"-áva-\" during conjugation IIRC, here\'s hoping IRC because I dang well better after 8 years...). There\'s a great little song using the \"-avat\" ending that is good to sing when you\'re drunk (it\'s even better, I suppose, if you\'re male, over 50, and like to wear silly old-fashioned hats: (Note: bejvat = bývat)\n\"Bejvávalo, bejvávalo, bejvávalo lepší\nBejvávalo, bejvávalo, bejvávlo lepší\nZa naších mladších let\nBejval svět jako květ\nBejvávalo, bejvávalo, bejvávalo lepší!\"\nThere might be more to the song, but if so, I don\'t know it.\n', '2002-01-03 06:48:30', 157, 0),
(190, 13, 'Diane', 'ourseatree@aol.com', 'Translation Help, long!', 'My apologies for posting a need for help with such a lengthy translation...I am traveling to Prague in a few days time and received this email.  As I have several reservations for my stay, I am unsure if this email relates to  my travels or not.  I have tried to translate it word by word with no luck.  Much must be getting lost in the translation, as what I have translated makes no sense! So, if you have some time, and a bit of kindness :) I would greatly appreciate your help!\nMany thanks in advance!\nNazdarek vsicni Doufam\nze  se  nikdo  neurazi,ze  pisu  hromadne s chzbama a bez hacku,no nic noviho.Jenom  toliko vam chci ydelati,ye  se nedostavam ted  casto  na name@seynam.cz a  tak do konce unora budu  na brigade,kde  neni intranet,jen  pocta  tak  tam  sedim u pocace s adresou name@intrans.cz.Jinak  bardzo dobrz rok 2002, hodne vseho a  malo  niceho. Zdravi (name). \np.s. Kdo vi  neco o nejakym  plese?(at  mi  radsi  posle sms) \nDiane\n', '2002-01-05 22:48:54', 0, 0),
(191, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Translation Help, long!', 'All right, I think I have a few spare minutes, so here\'s my translation (hope I got everything right, note that there are several words like \"chzbama\" or \"seynam\", where <b>y</b> and <b>z</b> are swapped):\n<i>\nHi everybody, I hope not to offend anyone writing with errors and without diacritic marks. There\'s nothing new. I just want to tell you, that I don\'t have access to name@seznam.cz and till about end of February I will be doing a job, where there\'s no internet, just email - name@intrans.cz.\nI wish you good year 2002, lots of everything, few of nothing..\nGreetings.. (name).\np.s. Does anyone know anything about any ball? (better send sms).\n', '2002-01-07 02:57:01', 190, 0),
(192, 13, 'Diane', 'ourseatree@aol.com', 'Re: Translation Help, long!', 'Thank you so much for taking the time to translate, although I now how absolutely no idea what this email is about!  Seeing it in English didn\'t help at all.  But, I am relieved, as I leave today for Prague, that this is not a travel notice.  You are too kind!\nMy very best,\nDiane\n', '2002-01-07 20:18:46', 191, 0),
(193, 13, 'Jay Dowman', 'jayd31@ntlworld.com', 'Where can I hear Czech?', 'Hi,\nDoes anyone know a good site that I can type a word into and listen to the correct Czech pronounciation?\nThanks in advance\nJay\n', '2002-01-09 01:21:12', 0, 0),
(194, 13, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: Where can I hear Czech?', 'I don\'t know of any such site. But I was able to learn Czech pronunciation from listening to Czech music, especially Uz Jsme Doma (who got me into Czech culture in the first place). You can download many of their songs from Audiogalaxy and find their lyrics at http://www.noise.cz/ujd/texty.htm\nOf course, this option presumes your tolerance for Residents-influenced proggy art-punk.\n', '2002-01-16 09:11:53', 193, 0),
(195, 13, 'Jay Dowman', 'jayd31@ntlworld.com', 'Re: Where can I hear Czech?', 'Thanks for your reply, I\'ll see whether I can bear it! I did find a site that has pronunciation on if anyones interested, its www.travlang.com and click on the Czech flag, it has some basic words on there.\nAhoj\nJay\n', '2002-01-17 00:17:12', 194, 0),
(196, 13, 'Briana Jones', 'Brijo61808@aol.com', 'report', 'I am doing a report on Czech and i cant seem to find much information on it.so,where are some good sights that i could go to ,to find out more about this country?\n', '2002-01-17 05:46:08', 0, 0),
(197, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: report', 'See Links section on this site.\n', '2002-01-17 12:05:43', 196, 0),
(198, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Where can I hear Czech?', 'How about this site. Go to the Reference section on http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline and you will find many sound files under spelling and pronunciation.\n', '2002-01-17 12:07:23', 193, 0),
(199, 13, 'Antonio', 'soutopenedo@hotmail.com', 'Translation of Jorge\'s request above ^^^', 'Could somebody help me translate from Spanish to Czech??\nI have some relatives in the Czech Republic, and I cannot communicate with them because of language problems: neither I speak Czech, nor they speak Spanish... Could somebody help me?\n', '2002-01-17 15:34:05', 184, 0),
(200, 13, 'gloria mcmillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Czech verb books or lists', 'Ahoj!\nZnate nejake knihy o ceske \"verbs\" (fully conjugated)?\nDekuji,\nSlava (Gloria)\n', '2002-01-18 23:53:03', 0, 0),
(201, 13, 'Farimah', 'daftary1@yahoo.com', 'Re: what is Mera', 'Actually, not to spoil your joy to discover your name does not mean flea in Czech (which it doesn\'t)maybe the person was thinking of \"mrcha\" which is an insect and can be used in slang to refer to an annoying person? \n', '2002-01-22 21:49:10', 163, 0),
(202, 13, 'Farimah', 'daftary1@yahoo.com', 'Re: Where can I hear Czech?', 'Not to mention the fact that half of the lyrics of Uz Jsem Doma are actually made up rather than real Czech words...They also sing really fast. I love them but to learn Czech I would also suggest more traditional stuff, e.g. those of Jaroslav Hutka (although probably many of his songs might be Moravian) or Karel Kryl (most popular 60s singer). \nYou can listen to Czech radio, for example Radio Limonadovy Joe (Lemonade Joe) or Radio 1 by selecting these radio stations from the following website:\nhttp://windowsmedia.com/radiotuner/\n(select Czech Republic first).\n', '2002-01-22 21:54:58', 194, 0),
(203, 13, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Tricky sentence', 'How close is this to correct, prosim?\n-------------------------------------\n(para 1)	V Americe jsou jen dve tridy zen, ktere jsou k spolecenskym snaham hluche; jen dve tridy zen, ktere nic pro spolecensky vyvoj necini; jen dve tridy zen, ktere posud upeji v staroveke necinnosti, a ty jsou prosluly, maly newyorsky krouzek millionarek, ktere ziji jen proparadu, jako voskove sochy ve politovani hodny lidsky vyhrel, obeti muzske vasne--ubohe zeny polosveta.\n	In America there are only two classes of women who are attempting to make some noise socially; there are only two classes of women who aren\'t developing anything socially, only two classes of women who still languish in old-style inactivity and these are the famed little circle of New York millionaires who live only for display like wax figurines in showcases--and the second group are the scorned, rather lamenable mass of people--underdogs--the seriously victimized by men--the unfortunate women of the hemisphere.\n', '2002-02-03 00:59:32', 0, 0),
(204, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Tricky sentence', 'Not too bad. Here are a few problems:\n\'wax figurines\' are \'voskové figuríny\'\n\'hemisphere\' is \'polokoule\'\n\'old-style\' is \'staromódní\' (\'staroveke\' is \'ancient\' as in the Roman period)\n\'pro parádu\' is two words\n\'mass of people\' is \'masa lidi\'\n\'scorned\' is better as \'odsuzovany\' (\'politovanihodny\' is more \'sorry\')\nBut to be honest even the English original (assuming English was the original) seems a little confused to me. \n', '2002-02-03 23:14:41', 203, 0),
(205, 13, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Tricky sentence', 'Hi, Dominik and all!\nActually the original was in Czech as you have it there\n(except for propoard, which I ran together...)\nIt is 1903 Czech, so may sound funny at times.\nI have so-called translated over 25 pp. of this 80 pp. book\nwith dictionary painfully in hand.  \nJosefa Humpal-Zemanova\'s _Amerika v pravem Svetla_ (1903).\nI keep telling myself there\'s \"svetle\" at the end of the tunnel!\nGloria \"Slava\"\n', '2002-02-05 05:11:38', 204, 0),
(206, 13, 'Lindsey', 'lindseyduncan@graffiti.net', 'Format of a CV/resume', 'What is the correct format for a CV/resume in Czech?  I believe there are differences from country-to-country.\n', '2002-02-08 16:43:50', 0, 0),
(207, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Format of a CV/resume', 'This is an interesting question. The traditional resume in Czech is unstructured. A sort of free essay describing your life from the time you were born up to the present.\nBut I doubt any company would appreciate that now. Most expect a structured resume which would pretty much follow the accepted standards in the US. \n', '2002-02-12 23:12:12', 206, 0),
(208, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', '', 'Re: Czech verb books or lists', 'To the best of my knowledge there are only two books dealing with Czech verbs only. One is by Helena Conforciová and was published by SPN in 1991. It is called \"Seznam nejfrekventovanějších českých sloves\" and contains about 1000 verbs. It has all the conjugation and aspect information plus exaples of use. However, it is hopelessly out of print and would be very hard to get.\nThe other one is by O. Parolková and I only saw it in passing some time ago so I don\'t know much about it except that the publisher only sells in the Czech Republic. Nothing that could be bought internationally. If anybody knows about something like that, I\'d like to know.\nPS: I started working on a list myself but as usual I got sidetracked. I will post results if any on this site.\n', '2002-02-16 15:08:11', 200, 0),
(209, 13, 'Andrew', '', 'Help with a song', 'The other day my grandma remembered a Czech song from when she was little. I don\'t know how to spell any of the words and she doesn\'t remember what they mean. It started like \'Pivo! Pivo! Pivo Chervena. Groto pivo bhudda pit. Groto pivo bhudda mit. Pivo! Pivo! Pivo Chervena.\' If you have ever heard the song could you please tell me what it means. She said it was some kind of drinking song. \nThanks\n', '2002-02-18 09:48:29', 0, 0),
(210, 13, 'fox', 'littlefox@atlas.cz', 'Re: Help with a song', 'I have never heard this before song but if it helps can tell you the right spelling of the words and the \"rough\" meaning of the song.\nPivo, pivo, pivo červená. Kdo to pivo bude pít? Kdo to pivo bude mít? Pivo, pivo, pivo červená.\nBeer, beer, beer turns red. Who will drink it? Who will have it? Beer, beer, beer turns red ...\n', '2002-02-18 17:30:14', 209, 0),
(211, 13, 'Andrew', '', 'Re: Help with a song', 'Thanks for the translation and the meaning. Sorry for horrible spellings that I gave you of the the words.\nTHANKS!\n', '2002-02-19 08:47:59', 210, 0),
(212, 13, 'Glo McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Czech verb books or lists', 'Hi, again!\nMany of us struggling students will much appreciate your hard work on the verb list.\nOne verb I cannot get: dostat.\nGlo\n', '2002-02-24 00:56:42', 208, 0),
(213, 13, 'Glo McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'DOSTAT\'s many meanings and forms', 'Greetings, conjugation fans!\nMy most recent struggles are with that ever-useful verb\ndostat.\nMy Poldauf dictionary says it can mean:\n1) receive\n2) experience\n3) feel\nThose are the main ones.\nIt is one of those unfriendly-to-foreigner verbs\nthat has a weird format.\nUsing meaning (1), I am trying to say:  \nI receive a letter.\nDostanu (jsem) se dopis.\nMeaning (2). I am saying:\nI experienced Disneyland in Anaheim.\nDostala (jsem) se Disneyland v Anaheima.\nMeaning (3).  I am saying:\nI feel well.\nDostanu (jsem) se dobre.\nCould somone tell me how close these are to right?\nDekuji,\nGlo\n', '2002-02-24 01:02:38', 0, 0),
(214, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: DOSTAT\'s many meanings and forms', 'Sorry, none of these are correct. 1. is correct except it\'s not reflexive, i.e. \'dostanu dopis\' - I will receive a letter.\nIf reflexive, it means to get to a place:\n\'dostanu se do Prahy\' - I will get/make it to Prague\n\'dostanu se na universitu\' - I will get into the university\nDostat on its own never means feel or experience so your examples 2 and 3 make no sese whatsoever.\nDostat could mean experience or feel only in the following idioms:\n\'dostat ránu\' - to get hit\n\'dostat šok\' - to get a shock\n\'dostat přes hubu\' - to get beaten up (lit. to get [one] over the trap [mouth]\nor even in \'dostat chřipku\' - to get a flu\nYou could also use it to translate an idiom like \'you got me\' as in \'I don\'t know the answer\' or \'you found me\' as \'dostal jsi mě\' or \'tos mě teda dostal\' (you got me, there).\n', '2002-02-24 22:28:15', 213, 0),
(215, 13, 'gloria mcmillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Mystery verb', 'I am stumped.  My Poldauf holds no clues as to the \"bee\" and \"bernou\" in this sentence\"\n  Proto leccos, co evropský intelligentní čtenář beře za bernou minci a amerického novin--nemá nejmeniho vlivu na Americkeho čtenáře delnik, který odebira si treba nekolik listu protivných politických stran a z těch pak vyhladedává si svou střední cestu.\n', '2002-02-24 22:43:29', 0, 0),
(216, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', '', 'Re: Mystery verb-archaic and idiom', 'They are both forms of the verb \'brát\' (to take). \'beře\' is archaic for \'bere\'. \'brát za bernou mince\' is an idiom meaning something like \'take for granted\' or \'assume to be true unconditionally\'.\n', '2002-02-25 23:34:21', 215, 0),
(217, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Welcome to Czech Culture Forum', 'Hello all!\nWelcome to the Czech Culture Forum. It is a discussion board intended to give you space to ask and answer questions about Czech Culture. Are you planning a trip to the Czech Republic and are wondering about the dos and don\'ts? Do you live in the Czech Republic and are you puzzled by some seemingly strange behavior of the Czechs you interact with? Would you like to know about the meaning of special events and holidays? Or would you like to share stories or anacdotes? This is the forum for you.\nEnjoy the discussion!\nDominik Lukes<br> <a href=http://www.bohemica.com>http://www.bohemica.com</a>\n', '2001-04-23 19:01:38', 0, 0),
(218, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Is Czech food good?', 'Hi! I thought I\'d start the discussion off with this very important question. <i>Is Czech food any good?</i>.\nThis is not as frivolous as it may sound, because the quality of the food is important to many cultures and everybody appreciates having their national cuisine complimented.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 04:52:14', 0, 0),
(219, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Is Czech food good?', 'I started the questions so I might as well be the first one in with an answer.\nI always use a quote from Pavel Eisner (a great Czech nationalist) who said that the greatness of Czech food is one of the great self-delusions of the Czech nation. He said that compared to French or Asian cuisines (and this was in the 40s), anything Czech cooks can come up with is bland. I also often jokingly say that the main Czech spices are salt and pepper. I must declare an interest, though, as a vegeterian, I can hardly enjoy the staples of Czech nutrition - pork and beef, although I do like the occasional knedlik. Czech cuisine is also accused of being extremely unhealthy which it undoubtedly is if consumed in large quantities. But I guess if you have a helping of <i>knedloveprozelo</i> once a week (like the Czech forefathers did) will do you no harm. And actually the really traditional Czech specialities use lots of quite delicate spices, so even that isn\'t too bad.\nWhat do others not raised on the <i>knedlik</i> think? Good or bad?\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 04:59:50', 218, 0),
(220, 14, 'theprophe', 'tradehard20@hotmail.com', 'Re: Is Czech food good?', 'well, i never knew nothing about czech food until i tried it, and i have to say it is wonderful!! My favorite meal now is veprova adn i just love those dumplings, i even order the czechoslovak cookbook designed for north american people (cause it gives ingredients substituions for our grocery stores)\n', '2001-04-28 23:37:12', 218, 0),
(221, 14, 'kevin handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: Is Czech food good?', 'Although not a full blown vegetarian, not being particularly keen on meat I found the thought of Czech food pretty daunting. My only experience of eastern europe before the ninety\'s was a skiing trip to Bulgaria. Even though the holiday ranks among the best I ever had.(Andropov died the day before we flew there, leading to some incredible mad rantings by our tour guides on mourning behaviour and consequential loss of nerve by many fellow travellers....all of which caused some great confrontations and the skiing was brilliant not a lift queue in sight) The basic food consisted of a lump of gristle in gravy I had a real job surviving the week on a diet solely of alcohol.. Czech food has been a revelation to me the only downside being my mother-in-laws belief that all men should have the appetite of a horse (and so early...lunch at 12.00 etc). my favourite dinner would consist of smazeny syr (fried cheese in breadcrumbs served potatoes and tartar sauce it\'s just a brilliant combination I\'d only had tartare sauce with fish before) follwed by fruit dumplings (these are served with a whey cheese and cream and are exquisite) on top of that it\'s without fail accompanied with pivo...Overall the food might not be as exotic as Indian or say Italian but it is not bad at all and some of the puddings are really tops\n', '2001-04-29 04:35:36', 218, 0),
(222, 14, 'John', 'johnfgough@hotmail.com', 'Re: Is Czech food good?', 'Czech food and more importantly, Czech beer is excellent in both quality and value. I\'ve visited the Czech Republic twice so far this year and I am far from being a culinary expert, but I know what I like and I just wish the quality and variety of the food and drink was as good, even half as good in the UK.\nFrom my limited experience, it seems the main fare is pork, cooked in a vast number of different ways, my favourite being the roast pork served with saurkraut and dumplings which is available from the smallest of bars to the fanciest of restaurants all over the country.\nAs for the beer, well, where do I begin? Again the variety is large, the price cheap and the quality outstanding. Most of the brews come in two versions: the light, lager type and the darker, bitter type. My advice; try them all for you won\'t be disappointed. Prices range anywhere from 13 - 25 Kc for .33l, but at an exchange rate of 54Kc to Ł1, even the most expensive beer is great value compared to the UK.\nGotta stop talking about this subject now as it\'s making me want to go back there.....and I only returned from Bohumin yesterday!\n', '2001-04-29 19:28:24', 218, 0),
(223, 14, 'kevin handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'things czech', 'i\'m surprised theres been little activity on this site....if you\'re learning the language it must follow you are interested in the people and how they think...maybe you feel culturally a bit thick like i often do....however i thought i\'d throw in a couple of bits of trivia...the music from the hovis adverts with the delivery boy going up that steep hill is Dvorak...the word dollar originates from czech...going on one of the sweetest things i find on my trips to the czech countryside is the almost universal addiction to mushroom picking...everyone seems to really get off on it, getting up early and hunting out the good spots....then racing home to prepare and cook them (after showing the best off to the neighbours)....what do you think of the czech national anthem i think it\'s a lovely piece (in the recent film Pelisky set in \'68 an old man who had fought as a pilot in the 2nd world war plays it mournfully as the russians invade...it was beautiful and touchingly sad)....what about Lada, Smetana, castles, cesky raj, whats Moravia like (i\'ve never been there), and what about Easter beating ??\n', '2001-05-13 07:06:29', 0, 0),
(224, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: things czech', 'Thanks Kevin for stiring things up in this discussion. You brought up a lot of important cultural tid-bits. Personally, I believe that culture is as important to speaking a language as grammar. And it is tid-bits like these that will make it easier for you to interact with Czechs. I will try to follow up on some of things you mentioned in separate messages.\n', '2001-05-13 15:22:53', 223, 0),
(225, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: things czech-origins of the dollar', 'Just a small correction on the Czech origins of the dollar. The word itself comes from the German \'Tahler\' meaning \'valey-er\' and it refers to coins minted using silver from the mines of Jáchymov valley roughly from the 14th century on. It was good quality silver and soon any new coinage being introduced was named after this to make it credible. Over the centuries it has defined the name of dozens of currencies.\nThe word comes out of German rather than Czech because silver mines in Bohemia and Moravia were run by Germans who has been invited there very early on because of their mining skills. They were there until 1945 when they were deported after the war.\n', '2001-05-13 15:30:42', 223, 0),
(226, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: things czech-the story of the Anthem', 'I\'m glad that you like the Czech anthem. I just want to remind you, that it is not nearly as popular with Czechs as you might think. It is not sung or played as often as it might be. It is partly because it is associated with the 40 years of communism when it was always played side by side with the Soviet anthem and the International. It is also too soppy and emotional so Czechs might be embarrassed to sing it in public. But if you take it on its own, it is a nice tune and the words definitely go to the heart of Czech national pride.\nIn fact, ever since it appeared in a play by J. K. Tyl in the 1830s, it was as much criticised (by among others Jan Neruda) as admired. But it became so popular with the nationalists that it became the anthem in 1918. You can see the lyrics and score in the Reading section on <a target=_top href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline>Czech Online</a>.\n', '2001-05-13 15:53:55', 223, 0),
(227, 14, 'Kayla Robinson', 'Lerobinson@na.ko.com', 'Holidays', 'What and When  are the main Holidays in Czech Republic and how are they celebrated?\n', '2001-05-16 22:33:40', 0, 0),
(228, 14, 'John', 'johnfgough@hotmail.com', 'Re: things czech', 'Only just found out about Easter beating. We (my Czech girlfriend and I) were over there for Easter and for obvious reasons she refused to tell me about it until we were back in the UK. Sounds like a lot of fun (if you\'re male) and should be introduced over here post haste.\n', '2001-05-18 19:49:48', 223, 0),
(229, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Holidays', 'This is a very broad question. Like in most of the world influenced by Christianity the main holidays are Christmas, New Year\'s Eve and Easter, all of which have both Christian and pagan undertones. Czechs add St. Nicholas (Mikulá) on December 5 and some more.\nSome of the important other state holidays are:\nMay 8 - Liberation of Prague in 1945\nJuly 5 - Cyril and Methodius brought literacy to Czech lands in 9th century\nJuly 6 - burning of John Hus in 1415\nSeptember 28 - St. Wenceslas (of Christmas carol fame)\nOctober 28 - founding of Czechoslovakia in 1918\nNovember 17 - \'Velvet\' revolution in 1989\nCzechs also like to celebrate:\nApril 30 - \'Čarodějnice\' burning of witches on the eve of May Day\nMay 1 - První máj - day of love/labor holiday\nNovember 2 - \'Duičky\' - rememberance of the dead\nThis is all off the top of my head. You can find more info on some of the sites listed here under Links.\n', '2001-05-19 05:17:53', 227, 0),
(230, 14, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: things czech', 'Continueing with my half baked knowledge of things Czech...and english spelling..., I thought I\'d see what you all think of becherovka... I\'m actually researching some now. For those of you who\'ve never drank it, it\'s a distilled herbal concoction made just outside Prague...sounds horrible and at first it is but after awhile it grows on you (at least it has on me). The recipe goes back some time and was invented by the Czech Jan Becher (please don\'t tell me he was German!!). Anyway the drink has a unique taste and packs quite a punch as few hangovers have taught me. Did you know the colloquial Czech for being hungover is to \"have a monkey\" (má opice). Lastly anyone out there want to tell how to use Absinth from what I can gather it should be good for a gorilla... ahoj kevin\n', '2001-05-21 05:11:31', 228, 0),
(231, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'how do they.....?', 'When a guy likes a girl, how do Czechs act? I want to know how they are, like the way they view things. I rally would like someone to answer.\nArlette\n', '2001-05-24 00:43:16', 0, 0),
(232, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: how do they.....?', 'Arlette, I\'m sure that question puzzles many Czech women too. I hope some will help answer you. Being a Czech guy who has spent quite a bit of time in different cultures I can\'t honestly say that there is one overriding difference between Czech and other men in their attitude to women. Maybe, if you were a little more specific. Of course, Czech women on the whole are not as liberated as those in the West, so a lot of Czech men have fairly traditional views (some downright sexist). On the other hand, very few women subscribe to feminist ideals, partly through successful male propaganda. I think if you imagine the West in the fifties or sixties you get about the right (if oversimplistic) idea.\nThere are of course many details of courtship that may be different from country to country, so if you have any specific questions about that, don\'t hesitate to ask.\nDominik\n', '2001-05-24 16:06:23', 231, 0),
(233, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: how do they.....?', 'What i mean is for example in latin cultures men are very jelaus about their women, if your his girlfriend they spect you to be more conservative with other men for example not huging a friend or not giving them cheek kisses, you know?\nI wounder how they spect a women to be? or how they are? like in their friendship they hug and cheek kiss with their females friends or their very conservative. I just need to know stereo typing Czech males. Can you tell me?\nThank you,\nArlette\n', '2001-05-24 23:42:42', 232, 0),
(234, 14, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: how do they.....?', 'Arlette, my czech wife informs me that czech men sometimes buy their partners flowers, say \"i love you\", say how their day went rather than I\'m to tired too talk now. Although I think this might be some underhand snipe at me it\'s probably true that czech men are a little less reserved than Englishmen such as myself. Overall I doubt you\'ll find czech men much different to the rest of us, I think as Dominik says they are perhaps more like we were 20 years back. More likely to get down the pub (hospoda in czech) etc. than your typical nineties (whats a two thouandish) man. As someone who\'s married to a foriegner and gone through most of the difficulties involved...enjoying some and being exasperated as well...I think you should (assuming you\'ve met a czech man you like) carry on as you would with any other person you like...both of you without a doubt will have to adjust...but this is true even if you come from the same backgrounds. I doubt you will find getting on with a czech lad much different to normal...parents however I think can be more testing but as I\'m still trying to fully converse with Stana\'s I can\'t help much (give \'em the spect they deserve and I\'m sure it\'ll work out}....good luck...kevin\n', '2001-05-25 03:21:21', 233, 0),
(235, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: how do they.....?', 'Thank you, Kevin\nI really didn\'t know how to react infront of him especially because of the difference. Are they very freindly with women or just when they feel something else?\nthank you,\nArlette\n', '2001-05-25 06:10:14', 234, 0),
(236, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: hugs and kisses', 'Arlette, here\'s a fairly important tip regarding hugs and kisses. It is very rare for Czechs to greet each other or say good-bye by hugging and/or kissing. If it happens it is generally perceived as an affectation.\nIt may seem paradoxical, then, when you\'re in Prague, you see so many people engaging in, what Americans charmingly or prudishly call, public displays of affection. It has to do with the difference of private/public space between Czechs and Anglosaxons. Have a look in the culture section on this site.\nAs to jealousy, I find it hard to perceive any difference there, although the more traditinal Czech men may expect their girfriends to not socialize with other men without them, but I\'m not sure how common that is or if it signifies any difference between the two cultures.\n', '2001-05-25 15:05:27', 233, 0),
(237, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: saying I love you', 'Kevin, buying flowers is indeed something Czech men do, but I doubt they do it all that often. But I\'m fairly sure that they are much more reserved than their American, French or even English counterparts in saying \'I love you\'. First, Czech language has two many shades of saying this. Steer clear of \'miluju te\' which is very strong (and may sound insincere) in favor of \'mam te rad\' which is a little more neutral. But neither would be used as part of a regular conversation, e.g. when saying good-bye. Czechs are immensely puzzled by American soap operas where people seem to be saying I love you to each other all the time. I have a feeling, that this phrase is almost always used as a subordinate clause rather than a complete sentence. But I may be wrong. Hope some Czech men and women will join the discussion.     Dominik\n', '2001-05-25 15:13:20', 234, 0),
(238, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: hugs and kisses', 'Thank you Dominik,\nI always wounder how they were, to be honest since there are a lot of differences between a culture I was scare to offend someone or to make look like I was flirting, that\'s why I ask. How do you say Accounting in Czech?\nThank you,\nArlette\n', '2001-05-25 19:28:44', 236, 0),
(239, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: accounting', 'Seems a little off the subject (unless I\'m missing something) but \'accounting\' in Czech is \'ucetnictvi\' pronounced /OO-chet-nyitst-vee/ an \'accountant\' is \'ucetni\' (same for male and female). There is no one easy verb \'to account\', though, it always has to be translated according to the context.\n', '2001-05-25 22:06:48', 238, 0),
(240, 14, 'Joe', 'bft_98@yahoo.com', 'American man dating a Czech woman', 'Hello all! I am curious about the dating culture if the Czech people. I met a wonderful Czech woman, and we are seeing eachother. I just want any advice that anyone can offer so that I don\'t do or say something wrong. There are a differences between American and Czech cultures, and I just want to be sensitive to this.\nThanks for any help!\n', '2001-05-31 20:07:07', 0, 0),
(241, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: American man dating a Czech woman', 'I don\'t think that the differences are all that great if you talk about \'dating culture\'. However, there are lots of small differences based on different values and habits. The best way to go about it is to talk about it together and try to discover where the cultures may clash. This in itself is subject to cultural difference as Czechs are a little more reluctant to discuss their feelings but it is not an insurmountable obstacle.\nIt is hard to come up with any more general advice. You may want to have a look in the <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechculture>http://www.bohemica.com/czechculture</a>. The list of Dos and Don\'ts and discussion of private and public space should be most helpful.\nFeel free to ask about any specific issues.\n', '2001-06-02 01:20:10', 240, 0),
(242, 14, 'Christina', 'christina.moulis@dewrsb.gov.au', 'Re: saying I love you', 'How do I differentiate between I love you and I really like you a lot when talking to my lover, I don\'t want to say I\'m in love with him, but I\'m very fond of him, and I don\'t know whether mám tě rada would be too strong.\n', '2001-06-08 11:42:28', 237, 0),
(243, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: saying I love you', 'Well, that can be pretty hard in any language. Context is important and also typically two people establish a code which defines the import of certain messages. \'Mam te rad(a)\' can be very strong but in the right context it may be used as no more than \'I\'m fond of you\'. And also, as I said before, you should keep in mind, that Czechs are not given to direct pronouncements like that and that the words \'Mam te rad(a)\' would likely be a part of a bigger sentence such as \'chci ti rict, ze te mam rad\' (I want to tell you, I love you). It is also unlikely that they would be used very often in the meaning \'I love you\'.\nIf you\'re looking for a more neutral way of saying \'I\'m fond of you\', try descriptive sentences such as \'jsi prima\' (you\'re cool/great), \'libi se mi s tebou\' (I like being with you).\nBut the bottom-line is, there\'s more than language and culture at play. Plus, as a \'afraid-of-commitments\' male, I may be completely off the mark on my     ysis (although probably not too far off).\n', '2001-06-15 15:24:52', 242, 0),
(244, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Czech Humor', 'Hi all, I was wondering what people out there think about Czech humor. How have you reacted to Czechs making jokes? Did you laugh or just shake your head? Did you like Svejk, or do you understand Jara Cimrman?\nHow does Czech humor compare with your national humor? Or is there even such a thing as Czech humor?\nBy the way, there plenty of Czech jokes in the Reading section at <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline>http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline</a> and some in the Culture Guide on <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechculture>http://www.bohemica.com/czechculture</a>.\n', '2001-06-19 21:08:15', 0, 0),
(245, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech Humor-No stand-up comedy', 'It may be questionable to identify something as typically Czech, when it comes to humor, but there is definitely a difference in how it is delivered. For example, Czechs do not have stand-up comedians. There\'s not even a word for it. Popular actors sometimes put on shows which are not all that different from a stand-up act but they would be probably classed as raconteurs in the English speaking world. Some of the old school names would be Miroslav Hornicek, Jiri Sovak, Vladimir Mensik, or more recently Miroslav Donutil or Halina Pavlovska and many more. But I cannot think of a single person who was a stand-up comedian first and foremost. There is Milan Markovic who is very popular in the Czech Republic, but he is Slovak.\nSketch shows, on the other hand, have always been popular and abundant. There is a long tradition of so called \'Small Theater\' (Divadlo malych forem) that dates back to early 1900s. Some of the most famous ones were based on a double act (Voskovec a Werich, Suchy a Slitr, Simek a Grossman, Vodnansky a Skoumal, etc.) and have significantly influenced Czech culture of the 20th century. But most of these people put on real plays or sketch shows which may have included elements of stand-up comedy but were still classed as theater.\nI wonder if people from cultures other than the English speaking have similar problems in finding an equivalent concept.\n', '2001-06-19 21:25:15', 244, 0),
(246, 14, 'mike', 'saintswithbert@aol.com', 'asking a czech girl out for date with american gur', 'i met a really wonderful czech girl. i have visited with her and we seem to like each other. i took her to church with me and now i\'d like to ask her out for a regular date.she\'s only known me for two days. how should i go about it?\n', '2001-07-10 04:38:15', 0, 0),
(247, 14, 'Bob', 'rcbrown@rochester.rr.com', 'cost of living comparison', 'I was thinking about going to the CR, and I was interested in knowing the cost of living compared to the USA. Can anyone help me out with finding information? I am interested in housing, food, and clothing costs.\n', '2001-07-10 22:20:34', 0, 0),
(248, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: cost of living comparison', 'Generally, living in the CR is cheaper (but you earn a lot less). A one bedroom apartment in Prague should be less than $300 a month and another $200 should be plenty for food and transportation (more if you only frequent expat places and eat out a lot). You should also expect higher initial costs. If you get a job as an English teacher and work hard (you must aquire soem qualifications before you do), you can make about the equivalent of $500 a month if you work hard. Other jobs (waitressing, etc.) are scarce and anything more will require a good level of Czech.\nOne thing that is much cheaper in the US is electronics so buy any laptops, etc. there. Clothing and footwear can also be slightly cheaper in the States for some stuff.\n', '2001-07-11 21:33:34', 247, 0),
(249, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: asking a czech      out for date with american gur', 'Well, church. That\'s pretty forward. You don\'t say whether this is in the CR or elsewhere. But basically do the same thing you would in your country. The Czech word for date is \'rande\' and the phrase is \'jit na rande\' but you may just want to stick to inviting her to dinner or a movie. For more details you\'d have to be more specific.\n', '2001-07-11 21:36:51', 246, 0),
(250, 14, 'SR', 'lite512000@yahoo.com', 'Re: cost of living comparison', 'Hi Bob;\nI  flew to Prague last November (best time of year, no tourists) and loved it; don\'t listen to all the negative bullshit you\'ve probably read on the internet....everybody does not have their hand in your pocket.\nCabs were good to me, I think the mini-van ride from the airport was only $6.  When you\'re there, just hook up with someone and walk.....the only way to see Prague\nYou can probably see all of Prague in 3 days so prepare for a real communication breakdown at the train station when you\'re ready to leave.  This was the only place I had language problems....But trains are cheap, $8,$10 to Brno\nI really can\'t help you with lodging  or hostels, as I stayed in a rather expensive hotel that I booked from here\nMost everything is cheap in CK, just higher in Prague.\nSo, dive in and don\'t worry about a thing. I\'m going back in October or November to take the train to Moscow. See ya there            SR\n', '2001-07-18 05:05:06', 247, 0),
(251, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Czech food', 'What is the type of food that Czech people eat?\nThank you,\nArlette\n', '2001-07-25 00:33:34', 0, 0),
(252, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech food', 'We had a discussion on whether Czech food is any good but that was for those who have tried it and formed an opinion. Now we can try talking about what it is.\nI think that when you talk about national cuisines, you need to look at three (intersecting) contexts. 1. What people eat at home. 2. What people eat in restaurants. 3. What people are given to eat in public eateries (company canteens, school cafeterias, etc.). I would like others contributing to this discussion to think along those lines when making more general statements.\n1. The most common food at home is meat with some sort of sauce and potatoes or \'knedliks\'. Rice and pasta of all shapes are also fairly popular. The traditional meat is pork with beef a close second. Today, chicken is very popular (note that Czechs make a strong distinction between \'kure/chicken\' [young] and \'slepice/hen\' [adult]). Lamb and fish are fairly rare, mostly eaten at special occasions. Compared to French or Asian cuisines, Czech food may seem bland. I often joke, that the two main Czech spices are \'salt and pepper\'. But in fact, traditional Czech dishes use a lot of aromatic herbs and spices such as carraway, dill, marjory, and many more. You can find Czech recipes on the net in various emigrant newsgroups, or you can buy a few Czech cookbooks in English through the Bookstore section on Bohemica.com.\n2. To start with, eating out is not something Czechs do very often (although that is changing). Most pubs will offer pickled saussages, \'beer\' cheese or head cheese as something to have while drinking. Then there are restaurants that offer the usual range of foos from Italian to Chinese. But as more Czechs eat out, a great many of \'traditional\' pubs have adopted a more extended menu. The great staple of pub food are deep-fried things. Cheese, mushrooms, Wiener-schnizel with French fries or potatoes. Some of the more tradiational Czech dishes are not as common in restaurants with short menus, but can be found in a growing number of them.\n3. We all remember the school cafeteria fare dished out by the powerfull to the defenceless and many of the likes and dislikes of adults have their roots in the school cook\'s skill or lack thereof. Perhaps the most interesting feature of cafeteria eating for foreigners is that often as frequently as once a week, the main dish is something sweet which would be considered desert by people from other countries. Most fof these dishes are impossible to translate and hard to describe quickly. It can be doughnuts with jelly and cream, little cakes with thin custard, a thing called \'zemlbaba\' (rolls and apples baked with cheese and lots of sugar), or rice pudding.\nA word about combinations. There are certain things that simply don\'t go together for Czechs. Chicken or anything deep-fried with rice, \'knedlik\' or pasta seem strange. Only certain types of knedlik go with certain meat dishes. Mashed potatoes with gravy are also not accepted.\nThat\'s all I can think of at the moment. Please contribute with your observations.\n', '2001-07-26 16:24:36', 251, 0),
(253, 14, 'Josephine Hutchinson', 'josephine.hutchinson@gta-travel.com', 'Re: cost of living-more detail needed', 'I represent Gulliver\'s Travel Associates and we are currently preparing a number of online travel guides to inspire people to travel to certain countries and cities. I am currently researching the Prague Guide and need to find out about the cost of living in Prague and the Czech Republic. I need to know the cost - in local currency - of,\n] Cup of Coffee\n] Can of Coke\n] Pint/ glass of\n] Bottle of\n] Bus journey in city centre\n] Pack of\n] Litre of Petrol\nI also need to know\n] How much does mail cost to send and how long does it take ] to reach within Europe and outside Europe?\nAnd while I\'m here, are there any times of the day or night when you can make cheaper off-peak telephone calls and what is the most expensive time of day to make calls.\nAny help you can offer will be greatly apreciated as I really need to publish these facts correctly.\n', '2001-07-27 18:13:31', 247, 0),
(254, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: Czech food', 'Thank you for your answer and what food will you recomend to eat in a restaurant.\n', '2001-07-28 01:20:11', 252, 0),
(255, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech food in a restaurant', 'Depends entirely on what you like. Some people swear by Czech food, others swear at it. I recommend you take the time and try everything. Unless you go to someplace ridiculously expensive, Czech restaurants are cheap enough for this kind of research. If you have a limited budget, time or stomach space, you should definitely try something that comes with dumplings (knedliky), such as \'vepro-knedlo-zelo\' (pork, dumplings and saurkraut) which is the quintessential dish for all cholesterol thrill-seekers. And no Czech restaurant experience would be complete without having a go at \'smazeny syr\' (deep-fried cheese), which in some restaurants is about all you can have if you\'re a vegetarian (like myself). But the green-muncher\'s lot is improving and most places now have a \'Meatless\' section on the menu (beware, though, some of these items may contain ham or salami). You could try \'sopsky salat\' if you want some vegetables of varying degree of freshness. As dessert, \'palacinky\' (mostly translated as \'pancakes\' but they\'re  crepes really) are certainly worth considering.\nHave fun and let us know how you liked it (if you survive to tell the tale, that is).\n', '2001-07-28 16:41:47', 254, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(256, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'how is the.....', 'I would like to know how is the wheather in the Czech Republic? I don\'t know if somebody can tell me.\n', '2001-08-02 00:58:47', 0, 0),
(257, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: how is the.....', 'at the moment the sun shines and it\'s pretty hot out there..\nand according to the <a href=\"http://pocasi.chmi.cz\">weather forecasts</a> it should stay like this for a few more days..\n', '2001-08-02 13:47:54', 256, 0),
(258, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: how is the..... (one day after..)', 'heavy clouds (at least I see them from my window) and it should rain in the afternoon.. even some storm could come..\n', '2001-08-03 15:45:57', 257, 0),
(259, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: how is the..... (one day after..)', 'Thank you, where you live is it nice all the time or when in the year is really cold? is it to cold to go outside? because I sure will like to go there when it\'s cold.\n', '2001-08-05 01:06:28', 258, 0),
(260, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: how is the..... (one day after..)', 'HOW NICE!DIRECTLY FROM THE CZECH REPUBLIC, THANK YOU. WHAT I MEAN THE FIRST TIME IS WEATHER NOT HOW PUT IT, AND THE SECOND TIME I MEAN TOO COLD NOT TO COLD. I\'M ASKING THAT IF WHERE YOU LIVE WHEN IS IT COLD? AND HOW COLD? SINCE I WROTE IT TOO FAST I DIDN\'T WRITE IT GOOD.\n', '2001-08-05 19:02:49', 259, 0),
(261, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: how is the..... (a little climatology)', 'the first time I thought that you were perhaps going to CR, so were asking about the weather now.. but now..\nthe weather differs from place to place thanks to the orography of the CR. The hottest month is July, the coldest is January.\nI don\'t remember average temperatures, but on a \'good\' year, in winter the temperature can drop to -20 Celsius not\nonly in the mountains.. You cannot expect such low temperatures in cities like Prague, but in the small town where I live,\nwhich is in a small valley, if the sky is clear, the morning can be really frosty..\nin the last few years, we didn\'t have much snow, at least compared to what I remember from my childhood i-))\nmore information about the climatology can be found on the web page of Czech Hydrometeorological Institute\n<a href=\"http://www.chmi.cz/meteo/ok/infklim.html\">eg. here</a>\n', '2001-08-06 13:01:59', 259, 0),
(262, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Another question about Czech culture', 'I would like somebody to tell me, if you go out on a date\nwho is the one who pay, the female or the male.\n', '2001-08-11 00:38:50', 0, 0),
(263, 14, 'Bob Brown', 'rcbrown@axiomnet.net', 'finding work in CR', 'I am planning on moving to the CR next year and would like any assistance in finding work in or near Ostrava. Can anyone direct me to agencies or websites so that I can submit my resume? Also, can anyone offer any advice?\n', '2001-08-14 21:14:49', 0, 0),
(264, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: finding work in CR', 'I know about a few websites like\n<a href=\"http://www.jobs.cz\">Jobs.cz</a>\nor\n<a href=\"http://www.jobpilot.cz\">Jobpilot.cz</a>.\nThere are more of course, links to others can be found (and searched) on <a href=\"http://job.centrum.cz\">job.centrum.cz</a>.\n', '2001-08-15 12:12:47', 263, 0),
(265, 14, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: Another question about Czech culture', 'Arlette, Not sure who pays is really part of culture. Unfortunately for you females and perhaps fortunately for us men, Czech people generally seem to be on a par with western europe, in as much as the person who can either most afford it or whos turn it is tends to pay. At least this was my experience when dating my now wife. On the weather front to cheer you up...assuming you would like it hot....today at 5pm where my wife and daughter are...about 50km SE of Prague it was 36 degrees C. ...regards kevin ...ps nice to see you still pursueing your interest in czech culture\n', '2001-08-16 01:30:06', 262, 0),
(266, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: Another question about Czech culture', 'Thank you, I would like to know, I don\'t know if you can help me with this or I don\'t known if somebody else would like to help me. Please tell me if the CR has a basketball team or any famous basketball team. Also tell me a pretty common female name.\nThank you.\n', '2001-08-17 01:24:05', 265, 0),
(267, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Who pays for dinner', 'Arlette, you raised an interesting point which brings up some cross-generational issues.\nAs a just-turned-30 Czech male, I feel compelled at least to offer for my liberated female co-diners and they mostly feel compelled to refuse. That applies only if there are just the two of us. In a larger group everybody pays their share (note you can ask the waiter to pay separately). It is of course possible for a male to invite a woman to dinner with the proviso that he will pay but the reverse is equally acceptable (although still a bit rarer).\nIt is different in Poland where the male always pays (I hear).\nI know that for people a generation older, the male is expected to settle the bill, even if the woman pays her share. It is even possible for a woman to pick up the tab and give the man the money to pay. This may be that she can\'t be bothered but I believe that it is to preserve the man\'s face. My generation is caught somewhere in the middle, but I don\'t think the younger generation has any such hang-ups.\nI would be interested to hear about your real experiences.\n', '2001-08-22 20:25:01', 262, 0),
(268, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Re: Who pays for dinner', 'I really haven\'t go out with somebody from the CR, but I was intresting for two reasons: couriosity and if one day I have the oportunity to have the plesure of going out with my crush. In my culture the men never pays and if he lets you pay that\'s defenely not a good men, but that\'s in my culture that\'s why I ask. I\'m sure I won\'t be bother if I invite him and I have to pay. I defenely wouldn\'t invite him to be honest and is not only my culture but my personality. How about Czech women, do they ask men out? I would like to know. I also would like to know a pretty female name that is common. I always ask so I won\'t be embarased.\nThank you.\n', '2001-08-24 01:11:10', 267, 0),
(269, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: finding work in CR', 'Bob, teaching English is your best bet until you learn enough Czech to be able to work in your field. But these days being native speaker is not enough so you must get a TEFL certificate. You can try one based in Prague on http://www.itc-training.com. This, like most TEFL schools, will help you find a job, although the options in Ostrava may not be as open as in Prague where you can start working pretty much within a week. Learn more about the field in general at http://www.eslcafe.com.\n', '2001-08-24 01:57:13', 263, 0),
(270, 14, 'Diane', 'samuelson_diane@hotmail.com', 'Two-Tailed Lion', 'Why does the lion on the Czech emblem have two tails?  When I was in Prague, I was told a story about it, but cannot recall.\n', '2001-08-29 02:55:59', 0, 0),
(271, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Two-Tailed Lion', 'I had to look this up, too, but it seems that the symbol was originally a one-tailed lion (or sometimes an Eagle). The second tail was added during the rein of Přemysl Otakar I. which was reputedly a gift from the Emperor Otto II. as was reported  in the Dalimil Chronicle (which however is not always a reliable historical source). It was also in line with the fashion of the day because you can find two-tailed lions in other heraldic symbols around Europe.\n', '2001-08-30 04:50:40', 270, 0),
(272, 14, 'Petr Chudoba', 'pchudoba@rochester.rr.com', 'Bohemian Christmas Customs', 'I am hoping that you can help me on two topics that I am trying to research regarding medieval Christmas customs in Bohemia. I believe both of them were also practiced in other European lands during that time.\nThe first is the custom of electing a boy bishop (in honor of St. Nicholas) who would preside at the church. According to one source, \"The custom of electing a boy-bishop on the feast of St. Nicholas dates from very early times, and was in vogue in most Catholic countries, but chiefly in England, where it prevailed certainly in all the larger monastic and scholastic establishments, and also in many country parishes besides, with the full approbation of authority, ecclesiastical and civil. The boy-bishop was chosen from among the children of the monastery school, the cathedral choir, or pupils of the grammar-school. Elected on St. Nicholas\'s day (6 December), he was dressed in pontifical vestments and, followed by his companions in priest\'s robes, went in procession round the parish, blessing the people. He then took possession of the church, where he presided at all the ceremonies and offices until Holy Innocents\' day (28 December).\" One source stated that this was practiced in Bohemia, but I am unable to find any specific mentions of it.\nThe second custom pertains to Christmas caroling in Bohemia at that time. The boys would carry a pole with an illuminated star at the top of it while they were caroling. This represented the Star of Bethlehem. I know that this custom was also practiced in the Ukraine, but mentions of it in Bohemia are limited. Each year I host a Czech Christmas party where we carry out all of the old customs. I would like to construct such a pole and add the custom to the festivities this year. Obviously, I would need drawings of the object in order to re-create it.\nMaterial and/or images of any other Bohemian Christmas customs from the Middle Ages would also be appreciated, as I am researching them as well.\nI hope that you\'ll be able to help me with any materials, images, or leads on these two topics.\nPlease e-mail me directly as well.\n', '2001-09-01 06:34:32', 0, 0),
(273, 14, 'Nickie', 'swimgirl_nov@yahoo.com', 'just a little question', 'i\'ve been wondering whats a czech mans mentality is about dating non-czechs?\nAnd also i\'am trying to learn Czech but the best way (i think) to learn it is to find a real teacher but i cant find hide or hair of one around where i live, which is Yorktown, V.A.\nthanks\n', '2001-09-05 05:47:17', 0, 0),
(274, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czechs dating non-Czechs', 'I don\'t think there is much of a mentality there. There are certainly no cultural proscriptions. So some Czechs will date non-Czechs and others won\'t. You should be aware, though, that Czechs born and bred do not to have much awareness of cultural differences and therefore may seem to be judgemental. But there are probably as many exceptions to that rule as there are examples.\nBut, yes, the best way to learn the language is to get a real teacher. Learning through a relationship sometimes works but more often it doesn\'t because one party gets more out of the exchange and couples tend to settle into the language that is more convenient to them. In fact, in-laws can be a much more of a motivating factor, because they often do not speak English and don\'t care. Anyway, hopefully soon, we\'ll be able to help you with Czech Online but that is some time away, still.\nDominik\n', '2001-09-06 00:50:32', 273, 0),
(275, 14, 'Vessela', 'vesy@rice.edu', 'summer job in Praha', 'Hello! I am new to this forum and I am very happy that I found it.  I am very excited about Prague and the Czech Republic, and about learning Czech and also about dating and meeting people from that country.\nThis summer I went to the Czech Republic for the first time and I decided that I would love to go back there next summer again and for a much longer period of time.\nMy question is, and I would greatly appreciate your help on this, What are my chances of finding a summer job in Praha?  And how do I look for a job?  Can I find one in advance, before going back to Praha?\nI am also thinking what kind of a job I could do?  I am from Eastern Europe myself, in my early twenties and I have been living, studying and working in the US for the past 6 years.  I have no problem with English, but I don\'t really know Czech.  I am, however, willing to study it very hard over the course of the year.  I would really appreciate any thoughts and helpful information!\nThank you!\n   Vessela\n', '2001-09-09 07:12:30', 0, 0),
(276, 14, 'Dominik Lukeš', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: summer job in Praha', 'Well, Vessela, if your English is good, as it seems to be, teaching it is the first thing you should think about. For that, though you would need to get a TEFL certificate which is a one month affair. Have a look at http://www.eslcafe.com to get a feel for that world. You wouldn\'t have a problem with longer term jobs but may find it more difficult to find one just for the summer (but it\'s not impossible with enough advanced planning).\nSince you don\'t mention what you can do, your other option is to come here and hope to get a temp job as a bartender somewhere. There are enough expat places to do that but I\'m not sure how stiff the competition is for that kind of employment. Local pubs/bars may not employ you without a work permit and/or good knowledge of Czech (on the other hand, what better way to learn).\nThen, there are a variety of internship opportunities that are not paid or only modestly paid. Try having a look at http://www.jobs.cz for some listings of employment opportunities. You could also go directly to some of the multinationals\' webpages and contact them directly. All the big companies are in Prague, just put the .cz in the link or go to their international web page.\nThat\'s about all <i>i</i> can think of. Maybe there\'s someone else out there who has a better idea...\n', '2001-09-09 18:39:19', 275, 0),
(277, 14, 'Vessela', 'vesy@rice.edu', 'Re: summer job in Praha', '     Thank you so much for your reply.  I think taking the TEFL certificate is a great idea.  I read all the information on that matter...And I will probably try to do that.  However, I was really thinking more of getting just a small job for the next summer since I would have to come back and complete my degree in the US.  A bartender or a waitress or a shop assistant is actually exactly what I had in mind.  I wonder how hard it is to get a permit for this kind of work?!?  And where do I go about getting one?\n     As for what I do,  I have been working on my Master\'s in Piano Performance and Piano Technology and teaching piano and music theory lessons.\n     When it comes to learning Czech, I am deffinitely willing to try my best.  Fortunately, the language has a lot of things in common with my native Bulgarian and Russian which all of us in Eastern Europe had to study throughout school.\n      Thank you!\nVessela\n', '2001-09-10 00:14:36', 276, 0),
(278, 14, 'Vessela', 'vesy@rice.edu', 'Renting a place in Praha', '   Here I am again,\nI have another question about renting a place in Praha. If I want to spend 1, 2 or 3 months in the city next summer, could I find an apartment or a room that I could rent just for that period of time? What is the approximate cost or price range? Is renting a place a cheaper solution or staying at a youth hostel is?\n    Thanks again, I do appreciate your help a lot.\n      Vessela\n', '2001-09-10 00:24:09', 0, 0),
(279, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Renting a place in Praha', 'Well, the news here is mixed. While it is possible to find an apartment for that period of time most of them rent by the day so that would become prohibitively expensive. You could pay about $500 per month if you got lucky (quite a bit less for a long term lease). The problem is that finding an apartment is much easier when you\'re actually in Prague and can get to know the scene, go to expat hangouts, laundromats, etc. Hopefully, someone may have a better answer.\nMost of the summer schools do find apartments for their students at about $300 a month. So taking a course (see Links on Bohemica.com) could be killing two birds with one stone.\nDominik\n', '2001-09-10 03:25:35', 278, 0),
(280, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: summer job in Praha', 'I don\'t think most people would get a work permit for a short-term waitressing job. Especially, if it is in an expat hang-out. The immigration police are more worried about Ukrainian construction workers than a few bartenders from Western Europe. But I may be wrong so it might be wise to contact a prospective employer in advance.\nYou can also try posting a question on http://www.americansinprague.com or having a look at http://www.praguepost.cz, or http://www.neweuropepromotions.com.\nI would be grateful if you could report the results of your research on this board as well.\nAlso, you might be able to get a job teaching music and/in English.\n', '2001-09-11 01:44:56', 277, 0),
(281, 14, 'R.H. Mezger', 'mezger5@hotmail.com', 'WAS: Czechs dating non-Czechs', 'Warning! Ameri-centric view.\nI guess I have a little experience here.  My girlfriend (who is Czech) told me once that she would *never* date an American.  Why? Many reasons. Some based on the stereotypes of the typical American and more importantly, I believe, is the reaction of her friends.  Lets face it.  It has become a stereotype for American men to go to the Czech Republic and have a \"summer fling\".\nWe have been dating for about 5 months now and her friends still think that we will not last as they feel that I will get tired of being here (in Prague/CR) and one day just leave.  It has taken a lot for me to assure her that this IS NOT the case.  Naturally she still has a fear of the day when I call to say \"I\'m in the states\".\nFriendship in the Czech Republic is much more \"real\" than the friendships I have found in the US. They are very important in the lives of Czechs (this is my opinion btw). They are family. If her friends would not accept me then it would put a strain on our relationship.  Lucky for me they seem to want to give me the benifit of the doubt and have been kind enough to invite me out for drinks and various activities. I am well aware they are being accepting for the benefit of my girlfriend, which I think is a very noble thing.  They try to make me feel comfortable just to make my her happy. That is not to say they do not like me.  I just think that they are more concerned about her happiness.\nI\'m getting way off the subject here I think ;-)\nYes a real teacher is best.  My partner is very helpful when it comes to teaching me Czech.  She has told me what she thinks I should learn first (the basic verbs and all of the variants, esp. být, mít, chtit, etc). If it wasn\'t for her my pronunciation would probably not be as good as it is. But it can put a strain on a relationship if that\'s all she/he becomes is a teacher.\nIn my case, I can not afford a teacher. I have been lucky enough though to meet some Czechs that really want to help me learn the language. Dominik has said that one of the best things you can do is to get out of Prague and I agree strongly with this.  Every weekend I go to a little village and \"hang out\" with the locals.  Since most of the people there (Kropacova Vrutice btw) don\'t speak English it is up to me if I want to communicate with them. *I* have to make the effort or else sit in silence.\nLet me steer the conversation back to friends and the comment made about in-laws.  I agree that the parents are a motivator for learning Czech. I want to be able to say something to them.  I want to show them that I am going to stay here, and the best way, at least the way I look at it, is to make an effort to learn the language.\nI think that the friend factor comes into play here to.  We have gone to many parties with her friends and at times it was a bit isolating when everyone around (including the signifacant other) is having a grand time talking in Czech (quite naturally of course ;-). It sure as hell makes me want to know the language! At any rate... I am rambling on here.  I just wanted to add my experience here.\nBefore I part I want to comment on the quotes made by some of my fellow country men (http://www.bohemica.com/czechculture/worksheets/helping_other_foreigners.htm).  My experience is just the opposite of these views especially with the second opinion. I have found the Czech people to be VERY friendly and QUITE helpful. Perhaps The person who made these comments was frequenting the ex-pat bars like the Marquis De sade or the infamous Chapeau Rougue.  Yes I do believe that Prague Czechs are different than the rest of the country but not so much as to justify such a harsh statement. It makes me laugh the statement \"...in a small international group of my friends\". So this guy/gal was hanging out with all the \"ex-pats\".  It is obvious.  I have met people who are of this opinion and 99% of the time these people only go to English speaking bars and make no effort to learn the language! They may as well be at home.\nWell I\'m glad to get that off my chest.\nTo wrap it up... I think that learning Czech is hard but not impossible by any means.  It just takes a lot of time, practice, and perseverence. I am far from being able to converse but I am trying.  I will learn!\nNa schledanou!\n', '2001-09-12 13:38:13', 274, 0),
(282, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Bump: Becherovka and absinthe', 'Just had to respond on this one -- it made me smile...\nAs far as my Czech wife and I know offhand, becherovka is made in Karlovy Vary (sometimes called \"Carlsbad\" in English, in line with the German name \"Karlsbad\" -- which means, more or less, \"Karl\'s Hot Springs.\" The similarity with \"bath\" should be no surprise, as these are used throughout Europe for bathing cures.). Karlovy Vary are in the northwest, near the German border, and thus is, in the national context, not close to Prague at all. (Being an American, I might find even Berlin to be \"close\" to Prague... but that\'s another matter. :-))\nLike wine coolers, it is indeed easy to drink too much Becherovka. Even if the unmixed drink is too strong to tempt one to drunkenness, there is a variety of weaker, \"dangerous\" mixed drinks, like \"beton\" -- Becherovka and tonic water. Otherwise, the word means \"concrete.\" It\'s a word imported from French, I think.\nAs far as absinth (absinthe?) is concerned -- this drink, which AFAIK is banned in the US, is permitted in its weaker form in the Czech Republic. This weak form has, I\'ve heard, too little of wormwood, the ingredient that makes true absinthe what it is, to bring the true--though potentially dangerous--experience. There are places where you can buy \"white\" absinthe, the stronger kind, but you have to be lucky (?) and/or ask around. I\'ve never tried white absinthe, so can\'t say if it\'s worth it. SURGEON GENERAL\'S WARNING: In extreme cases, consumption of absinthe (respektive of wormwood) can allegedly make you blind and nuts. Have fun, but be careful.\nThere is a tradition here of drinking absinthe in the following manner: order a spoon and a packet of sugar with your absinthe. Pour the sugar onto the spoon. Dip it in the absinthe (or have someone less drunk do so, so you don\'t spill part of your expensive absinthe on the table). Get a lighter. Set the absinthe-soaked sugar on fire. Let it burn for a while until it starts to caramelize. DIP THE SPOON IN YOUR ABSINTHE AND START FLAMBEAUING IT. Let it flame for a while. DRINK THE @#$#@ STUFF and the sugar at the bottom. Congratulate yourself for having risked your life in the name of science.\nVisit Traubka (Traubova street no. 6) in Brno and, if you\'re lucky, you\'ll be served your absinthe in a lab beaker.\nMy theory is that the mentioned technique is supposed to strengthen the effect of the actual wormwood. But I really don\'t know.\n------\nContinueing with my half baked knowledge of things Czech...and english spelling..., I thought I\'d see what you all think of becherovka... I\'m actually researching some now. For those of you who\'ve never drank it, it\'s a distilled herbal concoction made just outside Prague...sounds horrible and at first it is but after awhile it grows on you (at least it has on me). The recipe goes back some time and was invented by the Czech Jan Becher (please don\'t tell me he was German!!). Anyway the drink has a unique taste and packs quite a punch as few hangovers have taught me. Did you know the colloquial Czech for being hungover is to \"have a monkey\" (má opice). Lastly anyone out there want to tell how to use Absinth from what I can gather it should be good for a gorilla... ahoj kevin\n', '2001-09-12 19:48:58', 0, 0),
(283, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: WAS: Czechs dating non-Czechs', 'Thanks for the thoughtful comments. I think you\'re right on the money regarding the importance of friendship.\nI can see one more motivation for a Czech woman not to go out with an American and that is not to be seen as an opportunist who is after an easy life in the US. Remember that for lots of Czechs, America is still the land of dreams. There\'s still that image of an au-pair (partially based on reality) going abroad to get married but it\'s receding as more and more go and come back.\nHow accepting people are of foreigners also varies by the circles you move in. I have so many friends dating/married to foreigners that in my circle it seems perfectly normal (most of them live in Prague, too).\n', '2001-09-15 02:48:02', 281, 0),
(284, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Bump: Becherovka and FERNET', 'You should not neglect Fernet in your research. It is a stronger, more bitter version of Becherovka and like this, it is an acquired taste.\n', '2001-09-15 03:22:01', 282, 0),
(285, 14, 'nickie', 'swimgirl_nov@yahoo.com', 'Re: WAS: Czechs dating non-Czechs', 'Thanks everyone for repliying!\n', '2001-09-15 23:04:41', 283, 0),
(286, 14, 'Nickie Cross', 'swimgirl_nov@yahoo.com', 'just a few questions', 'i was wondering (yet again) about american sterotypes, what is the american sterotype in czech?\nAnd my second question is does anyone know where this is?\nBroumov 4\nkrinicke sildliste 211\n55001\nCzech republic\nand i coulnt put any of the hotchecks or anything in it\n(no czech keyboard^_^)\nthanks for your time!\nja nai!\n-nickie\n', '2001-09-15 23:09:43', 0, 0),
(287, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: finding Broumov on a map', 'The best place for finding locations on a map is http://mapy.atlas.cz. If you type in 55001 in to the PSČ search box (that is the post code or ZIP) you will be able to select Křinice and display it on a map.\nYou will find that Broumov is a small town in North-East Bohemia near the POlish border.\n', '2001-09-16 18:13:22', 286, 0),
(288, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech stereotypes about Americans', 'Unfortunately, the Czech stereotypes about Americans are not very positive. While the Czechs hold America as a concept (country) in high regard and aspire to many of American achievements (from democracy to high-tech), the Czech view of Americans is heavily and negatively influenced by the number of American TV shows on Czech television. These are often badly translated and badly dubbed. The translators often use literal translations where more free but idiomatically accurate equivalent is required (e.g. \'how are you\' translated as \'jak se máš\' in a variety of contexts). On top of everything the actors doing the dubbing mimic English intonation which sounds exagerated and affected in Czech.\nThus Americans are considered shallow (you don\'t ask \'how are you\' unless you really want to know how the other person is), primitive (speaking with child like overexcited intonation) and uneducated. The last one is typically the result of poor knowledge of European geography on the part of the Americans. The fact that when compared on the same scale, Europeans know even less about America, is not considered.\nThose are the stereotypes. But I think you would find that individual Czechs will be very accepting of individual Americans. They may be slightly condecending in some areas and slightly subservient in others. But the Americans will suffer from the same duality.\n', '2001-09-16 18:22:13', 286, 0),
(289, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Where to eat Czech food in Prague', 'This may seem a funny question but there are lots of people who visit Prague but never get to taste real Czech food. It\'s because most pubs only serve a very limited menu of Czech dishes and that mostly during Czech lunchtime (12-13:00). On the other hand, the few \'traditional\' Czech restaurant compose their menu mostly of \'old\' Czech dishes that are as exotic to modern Czech as to a foreign visitor.\nBut all is not lost. I have found the place. It is a self-service restaurant called Havelská Koruna and can be found on Havelská ulice opposite St. Havel Church. (A two-minute walk from Můstek metro station at the bottom of Wenceslas square). They serve all the great Czech dishes my generation grew up on and can identify with. The food there is reasonably cheap but, most importantly, it is good. Another important factor is that it is open daily from 9 am to 8 pm. The place gets a little crowded around lunchtime but even the long lines are pretty fast and you can usually get a place to sit.\nAs you would expect, it is a heaven for meat-eaters but even vegetarians like me can eat well there. I usually get some sort of \'knedlík\' and \'špenát\' or \'omáčka\'. Of the omáčka\'s, my favorites are \'koprová\' and \'houbová\'. But the carnivors should not let \'svíčková\' pass them by.\nBut most mouth-wateringly, they have a sweet dish counter called \'Sladká jídla\' where such things as \'ovocné knedlíky\', \'bramborové šišky s mákem\' and \'dukátové buchtičky\' can be had, again and again. Remember these are eaten as a dish in its own right not  a dessert, but the greedier among us (like me again) can go for a \'malá porce\' (small portion) even after the main course of something with lots of salt.\nIf you take your study of Czech quisine seriously, you should stop by the \'Racionální výživa\' (Health food - or literally, \'rational nutrition\') counter. There, they have all of  the typical Czech \'bufet\' (cafeteria, diner) salads, some of which are good and some of which are not.\nThey have decent beer (Gambrinus) to drink but there\'s is also the usual range of non-alcoholic beverages.\nYou don\'t have to like Czech food. (And it may not be anything special). But before you condemn it as inedible sludge, good at best for a cholesterol suicide, you should visit Havelská koruna a few times and try their fare. If you don\'t like that, Czech food and you are probably never going to be friends.\nPS: I really don\'t own any shares in the place (but maybe I should because it\'s full most of the time).\n', '2001-09-17 00:37:49', 0, 0),
(290, 14, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: just a few questions', 'Nickie, you\'re probably better off without hotchecks (or hot czechs) as what you should be using are hačeks...the c with the haček over it being pronounced ch. You dont know Arlette do you ...kevin\n', '2001-09-17 03:33:18', 286, 0),
(291, 14, 'Nickie', 'swimgirl_nov@yahoo.com', 'Re: just a few questions', 'nope i dont know Arlette.\nhehe.....i didnt know how to spell hacek because i\'ve only heard it before.\nand thanks to Dominik for telling me where in the heck Broumov was!I looked it up at the site and found it.\n-nickie\n', '2001-09-17 04:29:51', 290, 0),
(292, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Becherovka and SLIVOVICE', 'At the risk of speaking in cliches...\nOther important research topics are SLIVOVICE (plum brandy from Moravia... you will need a lot of money or good Moravian friends to get truly high-quality slivovice, but it\'s worth it) and BOROVICKA (liquor with the flavor of pine trees... much better than it sounds).\n', '2001-09-17 17:37:15', 284, 0),
(293, 14, 'Evelina', 'evelina_woodruff@hotmail.com', 'Woodruff', 'What czech people think about russians now? I am going to Prague for vocation and I speak russian. Shoul I speak or better not? I know how the czech people suffered under the \"soviet occupation\". Do they still hate russians?\n', '2001-09-18 00:22:10', 0, 0),
(294, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech views of Russians', 'That is not an easy question. In general, I would say, people have lost the hatred of all things Russian, that they might have had in the 1980s. Especially in Prague, I believe that no one would have a problem with your speaking in Russian. I hear Russian a lot around Prague (there are even several Russian newspapers) and I never noticed anybody being hostile to a tourist just because of that (like it happened to me in Latvia). On the other hand, few people will be able to speak Russian so you may be better off using English.\nThere are still some people with strong anti-Russian feelings but I doubt they would take it out on a Russian tourist. Even during the Russian/Czech ice-hokey match at the winter Olympics finals in Nagano, there wasn\'t nearly as much anti-Russian sentiment as there was in the 1970s when any win of the Czech team would be interpreted as national victory, overcoming the forces of evil. Now, it\'s more of a source of petty nationalism and comforter patriotism.\nAny other views are welcome.\n', '2001-09-25 23:13:28', 293, 0),
(295, 14, 'sally', 'sally_ng@hotmail.com', 'Czech national symbols (flower, flag)', 'What does the colour of the flag symbolise?\nDoes Czech have any flower that represent the country?\n', '2001-09-25 18:06:29', 0, 0),
(296, 14, 'Naďa', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech national symbols (flower, flag) - FLOWER', 'Czech Republic doesnt have a flower that represents the country but a plant - basswood tree.\r\n', '2001-09-26 12:41:58', 295, 0),
(297, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech national symbols (flower, flag)', 'To be honest, I don\'t know what the colors of the flag mean. There used to be a saying to help you remember which color is on top in the flag: \'Red into the house. White from the house.\' I have no idea what that means. I looked around but couldn\'t find any more info. Maybe someone more knowledgeable will help you. I imagine the meaning of the colors will be the same as in most national flags that use red, blue and white, though. \r\nThere\'s no national flower, but the national tree is the linden tree.\r\nSome of the sites you may look at are http://www.czech.cz, http://www.muselik.com or many more listed under Links on Bohemica.com.\n', '2001-09-26 13:31:06', 295, 0),
(298, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Czech national symbols (flag)', 'the only thing we were told about the flag at school, back in the times when we were Czechoslovakia was that the red and white stripes were the \'original\' czech flag and the blue thing represents a \'contribution\' of Slovakia..[br][br]\r But when the republics separated in 1993, Czech republic kept the former Czechoslovakian flag and it can now mean something else..\n', '2001-09-26 14:52:31', 295, 0),
(299, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech national symbols (flower, flag) - FLOWER', 'The Czech name of this tree is \'lípa\' and in the context of the Czech national symbol it is typically translated as \'linden-tree\'. \r\nThis is what Dictionary.com has to say about it:\r\n\"linˇden (lndn) n.  Any of various deciduous shade trees of the genus Tilia having heart-shaped leaves, drooping cymose clusters of yellowish, often fragrant flowers, and peduncles united into a large lingulate bract. Also called basswood, lime.\"\r\n', '2001-09-27 01:07:12', 296, 0),
(300, 14, 'Naďa', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech national symbols (flower, flag) - FLAG', 'I discussed the flag colours with my parents and they both remember to be told at schoold that:\r\n- red, which is the colour of blood, symbolizes suffering and wars in the past of our country\r - white symbolizes freedom and peace. White strip is above the red one which symbolizes that freedom and peace always overrule wars.\r - the blue triangle is a symbol of Slovakia from the times of Czechoslovakia - the peaks of Tatra mountains \r\n', '2001-10-02 13:08:42', 297, 0),
(301, 14, 'Christina', '', 'Re: saying I love you', 'Well, now somethings changed,I need to know, if he\'s started calling me moje milá (he called me moje milenko before), what does that mean?\r\nChristina.\n', '2001-10-12 09:24:01', 243, 0),
(302, 14, 'Mom', 'darkness@speedofnight.com', 'Romance?', 'I need to give background for my question, which concerns a potential romance.\r\nMy daughter is a lovely young American girl in her mid-teens but is terribly shy. She has attracted the interest (and the interest is returned) of a very nice young Czech man who speaks very little English. Sometimes he is bold, following her when she is shopping and smiling. Or once he managed to say \"You are very beautiful\" and another time \"I like you much\", but both times he turned red and walked away quickly before she could say anything back. Other times he seems as shy as she is and ignores her. Perhaps because of a lack of English? I don\'t know, but between the two of them, they are managing not to have a relationship for 7 months. \r\nI am sure part of the problem is a combination of youthful uncertainty and the language barrier, but is there something she can say or do that would indicate her interest? As I said, she is very shy.\r\n\"Mom\"\n', '2001-10-28 23:17:26', 0, 0),
(303, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Romance? Dating? Forthrightness?', 'I\'m pretty sure I\'m a very good person to answer (being a childless single 30 something) but a few points certainly jump to mind. I\'m certain you\'re right in attributing the lion\'s share of the problem to youth and language. \r\nShyness is fairly universal, although different cultures are shy in different contexts. For example, a Czech man may feel shy about expressing his feelings, but not shy about approaching a woman. In fact, typically, it is the Czech men, who are taken aback by the forthrightness and independence of American women. So part of the problem maybe an expectation on the part of the boy that American women initiate contact (since this difference between Czech and American women may have been talked about in his circles).\r\nAnother issue could the difference in the American and Czech concepts of dating. Date (rande) in Czech, is slighly different from the English meaning (and the slighness of the difference makes for a lot of confusion). In Czech, the implications are more straightforwardly \'relationshipy\' (yes, this is a technical cross-cultural term:-) while in English the definition is more open. It seems to me, that the Czech term implies more commitment. In other words, he may feel quite strongly about her but not strongly enough to ask her out, because it implies more commitment. (But this is pure conjecture. You may have a non-stereotypical Czech man and a non-stereotypical American woman on your hands, while both expect sterotypes from each other. A right mess.)\r\nSo my advice, for the little it\'s worth. Invite him over for tea or some sort of party and let things develop (remember Czechs expect to be invited and the invitation may have to be repeated - since it is polite for [some] Czechs to refuse once or twice). A good thing to talk about is the Czech Republic, learn a few facts and dazzle him and then ask more questions (would work on me;-). \r\nI\'m sure somebody out there has a better idea. Dominik\n', '2001-10-29 04:00:14', 302, 0),
(304, 14, 'Mom', 'darkness@speedofnight.com', 'Re: Romance? Dating? Forthrightness?', 'Your advice is very helpful and I have shared your post with my daughter. She especially appreciated the knowledge that it may be polite for him to refuse an invitation once or twice. She has been thinking about inviting him to a small gathering (assuming she can gather the courage), and so won\'t be crushed if he first refuses. She tells me \"I think I can do this\", but we will see ;) Thanks again,\r\n\"Mom\"\n', '2001-10-29 21:28:48', 303, 0),
(305, 14, 'Martin Howlings', 'howlings@europe.com', 'Prague community for expats', 'I would like to extend an invitation to all Prague expats or for those who will be living in Czech.\r\nhttp://www.expats.cz\r\nAn online community! Please come and introduce yourself. Help, advice and humour for finding your feet.\r\n-Martin\n', '2001-10-30 06:00:17', 0, 0),
(306, 14, 'Hannah Kleinova', 'haniik@post.cz', 'Re: Czech food', 'Dear Arlette,\r it depends on where you live. Town or village, Bohemia or Moravia. People from Moravian villages eat more meat (they are more connect with nature - I mean moravian people usually own fields and some domestic animal and eat it. Town inhabitants eat usually sweets and pastry. Students are usually vegetarians...\r Typical czech breakfast is tea or coffee, roll with butter and salami or cheese or roll with yoghurt. \r Typical dinner: vegetable soup, some meat with dumpling and sauce (typical sauces: tomato, horse-radish, dill...)\r Typical supper: tea or cofee or milk, some bread and butter.\r yours sincerely Hannah K. from Brno in Czech republic\n', '2001-11-02 00:21:14', 251, 0),
(307, 14, 'dennis mcmasters', 'joshonskis@hotmail.com', 'relocating to czech and dogs...', 'Does anyone know the rules regarding moving to the czech republic with a dog (or two)?\r\nthanks\r d\n', '2001-11-06 08:12:51', 0, 0),
(308, 14, 'Mera', '', 'Slang, idioms, and other such fun things', 'Supposedly language is a reflection of the culture that produced it. I\'m not sure what idioms like \"Bite me\" say about English, but I did read that American English has more synonyms for \"angry\" than any other language: annoyed, peeved, furious, enraged, livid, mad, worked up, upset, etc. \r\nWhat are some Czech slang words and idioms? There\'s very little information about this on the internet and almost none in any of the books I have, and I\'m curious. Also -- there\'s a site <a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/Athens/Forum/7953/word.html\">here</a> that lists a bunch of Czech words that don\'t translate into English (I liked \"nedovtipa\", I know some of those people). What are some English words that don\'t translate into Czech? \n', '2001-11-18 15:14:18', 0, 0),
(309, 14, 'Nicholas Meade', 'nicholas_meade@hotmail.com', 'Christianity in Czech Republic', 'I have recently read that over 50% of the population of the Czech Republic is atheist.  In response, I am concerned on this matter and would like to ask a few questions.  Firstly, I would like to know if any instances have occured where Christians have been persecuted or discriminated against?  I would also like to know if the Czechs are tolerant or open-minded to religious values, beliefs, etc.  (For example, if I were to pray before a meal what problems could I encounter?)  Basically, what I\'m asking is would I have any problems if I were to visit the Czech Republic being a practicing Christian? \n', '2001-11-26 21:13:30', 0, 0),
(310, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Christianity in Czech Republic', 'The number of atheists in the Czech Republic is actually closer to 60%. I include myself in that number so read the following with that in mind.\r\nThe different faiths experienced a brief surge of interest afte 1989 but were not able to sustain it. \r\nI believe that all discrimination based on religion has ceased. It was quite strong under communism, of which some members of my family were the target. It is interesting to note, though, that there never was a separation of church and state (like in Russia). If discrimination still exists it is more a lack of respect of the outer signs of worship. Few employers will make allowances for this, for example. But religious freedoms as such are fairly well established now. For example, religious education is allowed and teachers are free to subscribe to any faith they wish as long as they don\'t push it on their pupils (unless part of religion classes or church schools).\r\nCzechs are fairly tolerant of religious believes but also very disdainful of religion. You will not be discriminated against if you pray before a meal but if you do it publicly or ostentatiously, people will certainly look at you funny. \r\nYou don\'t have to suppress your believes but you may find people will not respect you as much, since devoutness is not considered a sign of strong character but rather as closer to fanatism even among many of the Christians. \n', '2001-11-27 02:54:23', 309, 0),
(311, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Slang, idioms, and other such fun things', 'I would be extremely wary of making sweeping statements about cultures based on their language. You can certainly make some specific claims such as lack of words for concepts but I doubt that the number of words for anger is all that significant on its own unless you also research other parts of the language. \r\nI would say that there are more words that are difficult to translate than the easy ones. You can read about some of the problems in my essays on this site http://www.bohemica.com/essays. \r\nYou should also remember that idiomacity represents a whole range of phenomena from collocations to proverbs. Even the fact that you have direct object after \'to believe\' in English and an indirect object in Czech is an idiom of sorts, as is saying \'every cloud has a silver lining\'. \n', '2001-11-27 03:23:16', 308, 0),
(312, 14, 'Robert Draznik', 'gfoxx@msn.com', 'first visit', 'I made my first visit to the Czech Republic last year (2000) and had a wonderful time. May I suggest that one can find an excellent \"bed and breakfast\" in the town of Krivoklat which is about 30 miles west of Prague. You can rent a car in nearby Rakovnik if you like but you can also \"catch\" the train in Krivoklat and go anywhere you want to. Of course, if you like you might find a guide to help you but if you get lost you can always find your way back. And...don\'t forget to drink lots of dark Krucevice(sp?) beer and a little plum brandy. Bob\n', '2001-12-03 23:55:36', 0, 0),
(313, 14, 'Diane', 'ourseatree@aol.com', 'Shoes and Handshakes', 'I will be visiting Prague for the first time next month.  I am wondering about the removal of shoes before entering a home, is this really done?  Also, is a handshake a proper way for a woman to introduce herself?  Is it true about men entering restaurants first, does this apply to casual dining spots as well?\r Any other tips would be welcome.\r Diane\n', '2001-12-05 04:33:05', 0, 0),
(314, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Shoes and Handshakes and Entering restaurants', 'Yes, it\'s all true. I can sense your disbelief but really, do take your shoes off when you enter the Czech house. You should be quick about it because you will be enjoined not to do so by your polite hosts, but the right thing is to take your shoes off anyway (this may not apply when the floor is obviously not fit for walking on barefoot or in slippers - or if you see your hosts wearing shoes).\r\nWomen shake hands in exactly the same way as men. There\'s no special rules. It may be of interest that in Russia only men shake hands and they do it all the time. Czechs are much more reserved about touching people so their handshakes are firm but brief and not repeated unduly.\r\nMen will typically enter restaurants first (women will often wait for them to do that), although otherwise the correct ettiquette is for men to let the woman to be the first through the door. If a woman enters the restaurant first, no cultural tragedy occurs (at worst the Czech man might feel slightly uncomfrotable, but will blame himself not the woman for breach of ettiquette - more often than not, it will pass unnoticed). This applies pretty much to all public dining establishments (i.e. not school/work cafeterias or fast food outlets).\r\nFor other tips, go through the Culture section on this site (http://www.bohemica.com/czechculture).\n', '2001-12-06 03:15:24', 313, 0),
(315, 14, 'Diane', 'ourseatree@aol.com', 'Dekuju mockrat!', 'I appreciate your timely response to my questions, although you may regret it as I am filled with a thousand and one questions!  I did thoroughly review the Czech Culture section and found it very helpful, and also slightly intimidating.  I am a 29 year old woman who will be visiting on my own and was discouraged to read that it might be difficult for me to meet local people.  I am visiting Prague to mainly experience local culture not view architecture (though I am interested in this as well!)  I am wondering if you have any advice on how and where I can achieve this?  Also, I will be in Prague during the Anniversary of Jan Palach\'s Death, I have had a difficult time finding information on this holiday.  How and where is it celebrated?  Finally, I am visiting in January.  What should I expect as far as tourist crowding, and potentially closed attractions?  Will it be shockingly cold?  I have read that smog is also heavy this time of year...\r Diane\n', '2001-12-07 20:22:34', 314, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(316, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: meeting Czech people - celebrating Palach', 'It is not all that easy to meet Czech people. I teach at a program for young Americans who find it extremely hard to meet Czechs mostly because they are not willing to modif their schedule (going out early and being home by 11 pm seems too much for some). But it is NOT impossible\r\nYour best bet is to find work (as a teacher of English, for instance). Your second best bet is joining a Czech class. Both should give you enough of a chance to get in a with a Czech group. You can always just randomly meet people and hope for the best, but chances are they won\'t be typical Czechs (but unless you\'re doing anthropological research, you don\'t care). Hopefully, there are some Czechs reading this who have better ideas than mine. You definitely shouldn\'t feel intimidated, if you\'re open-minded and commited enough (and you seem to be), I believe you\'ll be successful.\r\nDon\'t worry about overcrowding in January. Most of the tourist attractions remain open, though. It may or may not be bitterly cold but it certainly won\'t be warm. Below zero Celsius is pretty much a given (although who know with this global warming thing).\r\nThere may be some sort of meeting to commemorate Palach\'s death, a few newspaper editorials, but don\'t expect anything ostentatious. If you don\'t look for it, you could easily miss it. \n', '2001-12-08 04:56:10', 315, 0),
(317, 14, 'Antonio', 'soutopenedo@hotmail.com', 'Communism, Spanish, powerful Prague', '1. I have three Czech pen-pals since some years ago, and I have found a slight reluctancy on talking about the communist period of their country. Is it a taboo topic for an average Czech? Should I not to ask about it? Approx., what percentage of Czechs feel hate or shame, or simply nastiness about that period?\r 2. Is there a significant interest in the Czech Republic about learning the Spanish language?\r 3. There are some countries in Europe where the capital takes all the attention from foreigners, and this makes the other citizens angry, because they feel their areas are as interesting as the capital. For instance, France, Portugal, Italy and UK have more or less this situation; however, I don\'t think foreigners equal Spain = Madrid. I wonder if the inhabitants of Brno, Ceske Budejovice, etc, feel forgotten, in the comparison with the brilliant, world-known Prague.\r\n', '2001-12-08 22:33:59', 0, 0),
(318, 14, 'Cassandra', '', 'Re: relocating to czech and dogs...', 'Actually I believe that you are not allowed to relocated animals plants and other things of that sort because of the chance your animal/plant is carrying a diease that could cause problems in the other country because they don\'t have a restance to it as we do.  \n', '2001-12-17 00:44:42', 307, 0),
(319, 14, 'nickie cross', '', 'Re: Christianity in Czech Republic', 'heh....i woudnt care about respect im going to pray no matter what^_^ \r also im probably going to czech as an exchange student!!!\r *dances around* yay...but in 2004.....oh well i still might get to go.^_^\n', '2001-12-20 08:28:24', 310, 0),
(320, 14, 'nickie', 'swimgirl_nov@yahoo.com', 'more wierd-arse questions from nickie!', 'ok lotsa questions^_^ also the main reason for asking is becuase i might be going to czech as an exchange student!![--if all goes to plan that is.\r\n 1) when i do go in the summer of 2004 i chould have either completed french 3 or german one...i\'d like to know which languege whoudl help me the most. i think german whould out of logic, but who knows?\r\n2) what do czech teenagers think about ear peircing, i have five holes.....currently.\r\n3)what about guns and the mentality towrds them? like ive been shooting since i was four and i also own a CZ 80 the guns really nice, three dot system, .9 mm, has the magazine capacity of 10 rounds and the sights are fixed ^_^if youre wondering it was made in  the czech republic.\r  so will the other teens think its wierd that i whould have good knowlegde of guns and how they operate or should i just not say anything about guns what so ever? becuase even in the states some people will get egdy around the mention of guns.\r\n4) i chould use some advice about living arrangents while im with my host family, like with my family we dont really talk to each other or really stay at home for long periods of time...so are alot of czech familys close knit? and whould i just be a kind of interferance into the ruotine of the host family? im really clueless, so anything will help!\r thanks!\r -nickie\n', '2001-12-22 07:07:40', 0, 0),
(321, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: other languages in CR, piercing, guns, host family', 'Nickie, the very fact, you\'re asking questions, shows you\'re likely to enjoy your stay abroad. But I think your questions are extremely relevant to your experience, which bodes even better for it.\r\n(1) Of course, the best language for your stay  in CR is Czech (the more the better, but some better than none). French is an entirely lost cause but German should come in handy (even though it is on a par with or even sloghtly overshadowed by English these days). Any random Czech you may meet is likely to speak much more German than you can learn Czech in a semester (i.e. numbers, basic questions, greetings). But if your only options are French or German, German is the obvious choice.\r\n(2) Piercing causes no more raised eyebrows in the Czech Republic than it does in the US. Teenagers think it\'s cool otheragers think it\'s not, but tolerate it within bounds (e.g. if you can still recognize somebody\'s face). There\'s even a professor at one Art School, who\'s pierced and tattoed all over his body.... So there.\r\n(3) There is very little stigma attached to guns in the Czech Republic (still a major export after all). Your shooting experience and skills are more likely to be envied than censured-particularly if you own a Czech-made gun. Few Czechs your age would have any real gun experience (other than the fairly popular airguns - my granny even has one and shoots it all the time). The gun laws are fairly strict compared to the US but fairly loose compared to Western Europe. Because there\'s comparatively little gun related crime, there\'s no active gun-control lobby outside the usual human rights campaigners.\r\n(4) Czech families may tend to be a little more closely knit than American ones but the variation is so great that you may find a Czech family very similar to yours or one that is more of a unit. In general, I would say that the Czech family would try to do as much together as possible, visit each other more often and help each other out--including more remote relatives. But as always, this varies greatly.\r\nHistorically, you may say that the Czech family belongs to a Western model (more urban and industrial based than agricultural) - so you will also find differences based on whether the family comes from the city or from the country.\r\n', '2001-12-28 00:23:03', 320, 0),
(322, 14, 'nickie', 'swimgirl_nov@yahoo.com', 're: public schools!!', 'well thats good to hear about the gun situation here its like \"youre 14 and own a pistol?! *gasp, shock*\"  but it really depends on who youre talking to... but a bad thing aboiut going to czech for a full year is that i cant shoot my gun ^_^\r well the differnt languegs that are avialble to me next year (9th grade) are french 3, german 1, spanish 1 and latin 1....so i might take german if it helps any.\r\nand i have another question ^_^;;\r  what are the czech public schools like?\r AFS ( the program im going with) will most likly put me in a smaller village, not praha or olomouc....\r well i\'d like a comparision to a normal US highschool...even my *middle* school has drug dealing,kids popping pills in the bathroom and the building is old and kinda falling apart. but yeah im pretty much curious about life outside of during the summer (ive been to czech twice already but only for a few weeks each).\r so thanks!\r -nickie\r\n', '2001-12-29 02:42:07', 321, 0),
(323, 14, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 're: public schools!!', 'Czech public high schools are fairly organized places. I\'m sure there\'s drugs and bullying going on but it isn\'t as widespread as you describe (particularly outside Prague).\r\nThe biggest difference is that you take all your subjects with the same people (i.e. the graduating class isn\'t all people in one year but only about 20-30 people who do everything together). \r\nAlso you have virtually no control over what classes you take. \r\nYou will probably also find that the assignments are more passive (little or no research or projects).\r\nLife outside school is probably very similar: camps, hanging out, going to movies...\n', '2001-12-29 19:07:31', 322, 0),
(324, 14, 'David', '', 'dietary supplements', 'Is it possible to buy dietary supplements in the Czech Republic?  I heard that in Germany and France it is necessary to obtain a prescription for vitamins, minerals, certain herbs, amino acids, etc., and I was wondering if such a restriction exists in the Czech Republic as well.\n', '2002-01-03 16:04:20', 0, 0),
(325, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: dietary supplements', 'Dietary supplements can be purchased without a prescription, talking about vitamins and minerals. Even my mother was selling this (Finclub). I\'m not sure about aminoacids, but I think you generaly need prescription for stuff like.. body building and this sort (maybe it\'s even completely forbidden, but you can get it, if you know who to ask..)\n', '2002-01-05 14:19:01', 324, 0),
(326, 14, 'Glen', 'gmilleriii@hotmail.com', 'compliments', '  I have recently started seeing a wonderful Czech woman, and we seem to be handling my lack of Czeck knowledge and the slight problems with the English language she has.  I have started trying to learn the czech language and trying to learn things about the Czech republic(which REALLY impressed her), but my problem is the words that don\'t translate.  I would really like to say something that would knock her socks off in Czech, but I am having trouble locating something like this.  In English we have TONS of words for beautiful and sweet and etc... so I am sure they are there in Czech too.  I unfortunately cannot find them.  Any ideas? Thanks for the help.\r\nGlen\n', '2002-01-11 23:12:12', 0, 0),
(327, 14, 'Dan', '', 'Re: dietary supplements', 'David\r\nYou can whatever you want.\r\nVitamins and minerals - Pharmacy - Buy Czech products, cheaper,same quality as US\r\nAmino acids - Sport nutrition shops - Buy American products (Twinlab, ...) fairlysuperior to European\r\n', '2002-01-16 22:01:16', 324, 0),
(328, 14, 'Farimah', 'daftary1@yahoo.com', 'Re: Shoes and Handshakes', 'If you smoke you are also expected to let your male companion light your cigarette, they tend to be very disappointed if you light your own cigarette...\r\nI never had any problems meeting Czechs when I fist went there because I just stepped into the first pub I saw and actually met some interesting people but those were the old days. You can barely find any Czechs in pubs in the center of Prague (if you can find a real pub at all!).\r\nProbably your best bet is to go on week-end trips outside of Prague, for Prague is like any other capital: people don\'t usually talk to strangers. But you can also offer conversation exchanges to meet some people and once you meet a few people, Czech hospitality takes over and you will probably be introduced to many more.\r\nP.S. Remember not to wear socks with holes in them in case you are visiting someone at home...\n', '2002-01-22 22:25:47', 313, 0),
(329, 14, 'Miguel', 'miguel.cuenca@wanadoo.fr', 'language exchanges', 'Hello\r\nI\'ll be on training in Brno from 3/2/02 to 15/2/02. I would like to meet czech students to practise languages. I am Spanish and I live in France. So we can exchange Spanish (or French)and Czech languages.\r\nMiguel\n', '2002-01-28 13:30:56', 0, 0),
(330, 14, 'CJ', 'drmorf@yahoo.com', 'Pivo prices', 'ahoj!\r\nI\'m going to be in Plzěn (attending the international language school at ZCU) and Praha (practicing what I have learned) this summer. What is a fair price range for 0.5L of pivo in a pivnice or hospody? I\'ve heard that the \"touristy\" places charge at least 2X what the \"local\" places do. Any thoughts?\r\nCJ\n', '2002-01-29 10:48:25', 0, 0),
(331, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Pivo prices', 'Maucta,\r\nWhat you\'ve heard is true. in a common pub, the price <b>should not be</b> more than 20 for standard 0.5L 10-12 grade beer..\r but you can pay even more than 50 in a hotel or luxurious restaurant (or pub trying to look like luxurious restaurant in the places attractive for tourists..) maybe this doesn\'t apply to Plzen, I haven\'t been there ..\n', '2002-01-29 14:54:31', 330, 0),
(332, 14, 'CJ', 'drmorf@yahoo.com', 'Re: Pivo prices', 'Dějuki vám!\r\nThanks for the info. I\'m getting thirstier by the minute...\n', '2002-01-30 07:10:31', 331, 0),
(333, 14, 'CJ', 'drmorf@yahoo.com', 'Pivo in Praha 6', 'I will be staying in Prague at Villa Koslovka on Koslovska in the Hanspaulka neighborhood (near Evropska and the Techincal university-Metro stop=Dejvicka). Any worthwhile pubs in the area? Any to avoid? \r\nCJ\n', '2002-01-30 11:36:49', 0, 0),
(334, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Pivo in Praha 6', 'I don\'t come to Praha 6 much, so I cannot tell, but students of the Tech. Un. will know. Just ask any of them, as they come out of school i-)\r\n(on one bbs, I read a post about pub \"U Zivalu\" in Dejvice, which the user recommended..)\n', '2002-01-30 17:23:06', 333, 0),
(335, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'questions, more questions...', 'I\'m not try to ask because I\'m getting marry or something I\r just want to know,if anybody will tell me, how are weddings\r in CR, like the traditions I\'m asking for a typical and a \r general way. Will somebody tell me,\r Arlettte\n', '2002-02-09 08:31:49', 0, 0),
(336, 14, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: questions, more questions...', 'Well typically - talking about what I have seen from very far - don\'t expect anything shocking. Bride in white dress, groom in black suit, witnesses, bridesmaids, families, wedding feast..\r car ride with horns blowing..<br><br>\r The only thing that might seem strange is the ceremony taking place in city hall - but there should be no problem making it in church, in fact I think it\'s rather popular doing it in a castle or sort..\n', '2002-02-12 16:12:24', 335, 0),
(337, 14, 'Cassandra', '', 'a simple question', 'I was wondering when the Czech Repbulic and Slovakia spilt did the citzens of Czechlaslovakia get to choose which country they wished to be a citzen of?(I am sorry for any miss spelted words^^)\n', '2002-02-13 08:37:54', 0, 0),
(338, 14, 'christina', '', 'Re: Pivo in Praha 6', 'U černého vola, it was very cheap, full of czechs, not tourists.  The pivo was only 17 Kč.  It\'s actually in Praha 5, but I went there often when I lived in Praha 6.\n', '2002-02-22 10:03:01', 333, 0),
(339, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Welcome to Czech Student Forum', 'Hello!\nWelcome to the Czech Student Forum. It is a discussion board intended to give you space to share your impressions from learning Czech. Moan and whine if you wish. Offer your colleagues encouragement. Share useful learning strategies you found worked for you.\nGood luck!\nDominik<br> <a href=http://www.bohemica.com>http://www.bohemica.com</a>\n', '2001-04-24 00:26:09', 0, 0),
(340, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Is Czech difficult?', 'Hi All! To kick the discussion off, I want to start with a question about how difficult the Czech language is to learn. Any ideas?\nDominik\n', '2001-04-24 00:29:07', 0, 0),
(341, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Czech is easy', 'I don\'t think Czech is any more difficult to learn than any other language. I always say that \'Czech is easy, it\'s learning Czech that is difficult.\' I think we are seduced by the seeming ease with which people seem to learn English. In fact, as somebody once said, \'English is an easy language to learn badly.\' To learn Czech well takes as much effort as to learn Spanish well. It is the about the first ten lessons that are more difficult and unfortunately, most people seem to quit before they get to the next stage.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-24 00:33:20', 340, 0),
(342, 15, 'kevin handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'is czech easy', 'no it\'s not, to speak and understand well is hard after 3 years of trying and with a czech wife who has waltzed english and already speaks russian i should know....but i\'m really enjoying learning...whats the point of learning the easy languages such as french and german...furthermore it will give the chance to see bohemia and moravia beyond prague (wonderful town that it is) there is so much more to the country...also as a foreigner speaking czech you earn a huge amount more respect....so hopefully it will prove worth the effort and perseverance\n', '2001-04-25 05:49:40', 0, 0),
(343, 15, 'Jochen', 'jochen_steinmetz@yahoo.com', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Kdyz je cesky jazyk tak jednoduchy, proc je tady na te strane vsechno napsano v aj.??\nMyslim si, ze mohli bychom mluvit - promin napsat - alespon tady v cestine.\nCo se tyce me, jsem presvedcen ze pro \"zapadaky\" cestina je docela tezka. Trva docela dlouho nez se vubec muzes domluvit. Ale za to i maly uspech stoji za to, protoze Cesi vetsinou umi to vazet, kdyz umis se zeptat nejen po pive.\n', '2001-04-25 20:10:49', 341, 0),
(344, 15, 'Josef Füllenbach', 'joschfue@gmx.de', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Souhlasim s Jochenem: proc se tady vlastne skoro nepise cesky? Aby to slo jeste lepe: je tady mozne psat s hacky a carkami? Například takhle? Zůstanou háčky a čárky, kdy to čtete na vaích obrazovkách? A co krouek? Kdy ovem píeme s háčky atd., bude komunikace v četině jetě komplikovanějí.\nTaké souhlasím, e četina je docela teký jazyk. Ale dá se zvládnout, nejlépe, kdy člověk ije delí dobu v českém prostředí.\nTak prosím, dejte mi vědět, jestli tento text byl čítelný.\n', '2001-04-26 03:27:28', 343, 0),
(345, 15, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Jen komentar k hackum a carkam - pokud pisete ve Windows a tedy v kodovani CP-1250, pak\nje to v poradku a zobrazi se to <i>správně česky</i> snad vsude, i na unixech, kde se pouziva\nspise iso8859-2 kodovani, ale treba netscape pod linuxem to umi zobrazit spravne. Horsi by bylo, pokud by nekdo psal z unixu, pak by byl problem,\nprotoze cp-1250 je sice vetsinou kopie iso8859-2, ale treba \'s\' a \'z\' s hackem maji jine kody.\nCoz je ostatne duvod, proc ja pisu radsi bez hacku a carek.\nA jestli je cestina tezka? Nekdy ano, i my Cesi mame dost casto potize napriklad s i/y, mě/mně,\ni kdyz to je problem jen pri psani, stejne jako carky v souvetich. Napriklad v tomto prispevku si jejich spravnosti nejsem rozhodne 100% jistej.\n', '2001-04-26 04:35:40', 344, 0),
(346, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Mily Jochene, má samozřejmě pravdu. Problém je, e tyto stránky jsou určeny i pro začátečníky, pro které je psát jen česky velký problém. Pro ty, kdo chtějí diskutovat česky, jsem přidal novou diskusní skupinu <a href=http://www.cuni.cz/cgi-bin/cgiwrap/dlukes/mb.pl?board=0005>Ceská konverzace</a>. Byl bych rád, kdybys do ní také přispíval.\nDěkuji, Dominik\n', '2001-04-26 07:07:59', 343, 0),
(347, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: is czech easy', 'I know people who have lived in Prague for a decade and can barely order off a complicated menu. But even if you don\'t speak a word of Czech in Prague you can still survive. I also know a lot of foreigners living outside Prague whose Czech is just what you would expect it to be. I suggest it\'s all down to motivation. Which is what research suggest is the prime factor in learning a language anyway. On top of not needing Czech in Prague, a lot of expats don\'t feel that knowing Czech would help their careers back home like French and Spanish would, thus reducing their motivation even further.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-26 23:32:17', 342, 0),
(348, 15, 'kevin handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Yeah I agree with Dominik if you want write in czech, haceks and all what help are you going to be to students wrestling with the the language. Vy lumpove.\n', '2001-04-27 03:10:17', 346, 0),
(349, 15, 'theprophe', 'tradehard20@hotmail.com', 'Czech is the hardest!', 'saw in the book of lists, that czech was the hardest language in the world, and the hardest sound to make was that r with the accent, sorry but my computer is screwed up and i can\'t access my czech keyboard\n', '2001-04-28 23:30:48', 0, 0),
(350, 15, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech is the hardest!', 'I wonder where this list comes from. It is certainly wrong Czech is definitely not the hardest language in the world. <b>Ř</b> which Czech shares with at least two other languages is comparatively easy compared to, for instance, tones in Vietnamese. Nor is it the most complex language morphologically. For example, African Bantu languages (e.g. Swahili) have many more possible endings and more than 12 categories (instead of 3 Czech genders). But my point was that the number of endings doesn\'t make a language difficult to learn. Latin has as complex grammar as Czech and yet it was the lingua franca for well over a thousand years.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-29 03:07:42', 349, 0),
(351, 15, 'Chris Garbett', 'chrisgarbett@blueyonder.co.uk', 'Re: Czech is the hardest!', 'Hi. I certainly don\'t think that Czech is the hardest language to learn.  After 3 years of self-study Ican make myself understood fairly well, although I still struggle with verb aspect! and I\'m starting to get to grips with endings - meaning that I find that I get the right ending most of the time - and some of them are becoming automatic.\nBut reading and writing Czech I find very difficult, I think probably because of a limited vocabulary. I need to read with a dictionary at hand (Fronek of course)  My wife and I have visited the Republic 4 times and are planning next visit, and have been to Haviřov, Ostrava, and Čáslav, and briefly visited Kutná Hora = and can certaínly confirm that the ability to speak even a little Czech is appreciated by Czech people, and they are very helpful.  Prague less so = so many tourists? and we found shop assistants to be very short on patience.\nCan\'t wait to get to grips with the language properly!\nRegards. Thanks for your hard work Dominik.\n', '2001-05-02 16:26:09', 349, 0),
(352, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech is the hardest!', 'Thanks for sharing Chris. I often say that aspect is the only really difficult thing in Czech. It\'s because it presents a completely new way of looking at actions. In that it is similar to articles in English. It certainly takes anybody learning English more than three years (even of fairly intensive study) to get their the\'s and a\'s sorted out. Cases, on the other hand, are laughably easy. All you have to do is practice until you\'ve drilled them into your head. No more difficult than getting the word order in English down. Often, it\'s drudgery but that\'s been part of language learning since time immemorial.\n', '2001-05-02 18:17:46', 351, 0),
(353, 15, 'John', 'johnfgough@hotmail.com', 'Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'As a complete novice, can anybody recommend a good book / cd-rom to get started learning the language?\n===================\nMoved here from Language Forum\n===================\n', '2001-05-04 19:11:36', 0, 0),
(354, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'Have a look in the Bookstore at Bohemica.com. Most of the textbooks available are annotated there. Personally, I think that Communicative Czech is probably the best all round textbook for classroom instruction. For self study, you have to choose between Teach Yourself Czech and Colloquial Czech. I recommend the former but being a colleague of the author, I may be biased.\n', '2001-05-05 18:04:42', 353, 0),
(355, 15, 'Chris Garbett', 'chrisgarbett@blueyonder.co.uk', 'Re: Czech Textbooks', 'Thanks, Dominik, for the opportunity to \"bounce\" questions and answers re the language, and learning in particular.  I would like to know what textbooks other students have found helpful.  I started with Czech In 3 Months (laugh), and James Naughton\'s \"Colloquial Czech\", and I still dip into them occasionally - but I mainly now use \"Do You Want To Speak Czech\" and \'Chcete jetě lépe mluvit Česky\' - both of which I find to be very good.  After learning a point in these books, I use the exercises in the other books to augment the practice of it.\nWe also try to read as much as we can in simple Czech - I found a book in a second-hand shop \"Pohádky a Povídky pro malé čtenáře\" which is also helpful in practicing.\nWhat suggestions does anyone else have to offer?\n', '2001-05-02 16:48:59', 353, 0),
(356, 15, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech Textbooks', 'I\'ve used \'Do You Want to Speak Czech\' once as part of a course and I am of two minds about it. While it contains many good exercises its structure is a little chaotic. Plus, it is evidently based on the German version (or written with German in mind). For example, in the first section there\'s a whole page of sentences like \'the book lies on the table\' which is compulsory in German but optional in both Czech and English. But the second one is definitely full of useful exercises for more advanced students.\n', '2001-05-06 05:22:36', 355, 0),
(357, 15, 'kevin handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'Dear John, I have found over the three years that i\'ve been learning Czech theres no absolute best book (or even approach)...each time i started on with a new book/cassette pack i\'ve found fresh stimulus] I started out with David Shorts Teach Yourself Czech...This got me of the ground and has quite a cleverly funny style (assuming you have similar humour to me/David Short...my next purchase was James Naughtons Colloquial Czech (the old version not whats out now)....I never got on as well with this at the time (but having subsequently lost it I find I quite miss it)...the problem with this book following Shorts is I feel their angle on Czech is to similar...anyway next was Czech in Three Months (much needed as it was now ten months since I\'d started learning).....I think this is a pretty dull book and would not suggest you start with it, however at my point of learning at the time it was ideal being far more succint and easier to follow than the previous two. It concentrates on grammar and I think to begin with would lack enough fun/interest to make you persevere...I\'ve also got Chcete Mluvit Cesky which is no bad but hard to track down over here and lastly we use Communicative Czech at the Czech Centre in London where I now attend a course (they have a web site I think its Dominik\'s links) Com. Czech is very good and might just be the way to start but I personally would go for Shorts Teach Yourself.....Whatever you get stick at it and don\'t stick with the one book get another when you find you\'re not making headway (and get the accompanying cassetes if you can afford them)....lastly try and join a live course the Czech Centre\'s excellent if you live near enough.....couple more points books are far cheaper to buy in Czech Republic so if can get them there...actually that was the only left point sorry if i\'ve rambled on i must go my dinners ready...ahoj kevin\n', '2001-05-07 02:01:33', 353, 0),
(358, 15, 'Dora Hranac', 'jcandme@prodigy.net', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'I was trying to find a place to buy the \"communicative Czech\" book, workbook, tapes. I am having trouble finding them to order.\n', '2001-05-08 07:11:18', 354, 0),
(359, 15, 'John', 'johnfgough@hotmail.com', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'Thanks for the tips, Kevin. As I just need to \"get off the ground\" for now, I\'ll give D. Shorts \'Teach Yourself Czech\' a go if I can find it.\nThanks again\nJohn\n', '2001-05-08 16:53:07', 357, 0),
(360, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'You can find it in the <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/bookstore.htm>Bookstore</a> on Bohemica. It will point you to a copy on Amazon.com or Amazon.co.uk.\n', '2001-05-10 01:02:33', 359, 0),
(361, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'I don\'t know of any way of buying it online and I doubt you will find it in a bookstore outside CR. Your best bet is having someone bring it over from Prague. The <a href=http://www.dumknihy.cz>Kanzelsberger</a> bookstore on Wenceslas square have it in stock. It is conceivable that they will mail it to you for a fee.\n', '2001-05-10 01:08:33', 358, 0),
(362, 15, 'Dora Hranac', 'jcandme@pordigy.net', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'Thanks for the infomation.  A big help.\n', '2001-05-10 03:21:15', 361, 0),
(363, 15, 'Jaroslava Lapáčková', 'jarkalap@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Textbooks', 'Mily Dominiku, k diskusi o ucebnicich - zkusil jsi nekdy pouzit ten druhy dil ucebnice Chcete jeste mlepe mluvit cesky v pracxi se studenty? Ja ano, a myslim, ze ta kniha je jeste vic neprehledna a nez ta prvni.  Daleko lepsi pro pokrocilejsi studenty je nedavno vydana J. Bishofova a kol., Cestina pro stredne a vice pokrocile, UK , Praha 1999.\nJarka\n', '2001-05-22 21:32:38', 356, 0),
(364, 15, 'Jaroslava Lapáčková', 'jarkalap@hotmail.com', 'Re: Learning Czech from a Textbook', 'Glasgow Unviersity pouziva od tohoto roku oficialne Communicative Cz pro studenty 1. a 2. rocniku a od pristiho roku by je melo objednavat knihkupectvi John Smith and Sons, myslim, ze tak se to jmenuje.\nVic by asi vedela kolegyne Ilona Bilkova.\nJL\n', '2001-05-22 21:35:13', 358, 0),
(365, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech Textbooks', 'Nezkousel, ale prohlizel jsem si ji a prislo mi, ze se v ni tezko orientuje. Na druhou stranu je v ni spousta dobrych jednotlivych cviceni a vim, ze ji nekteri ucitele pouzivaji jako zdroj.\n', '2001-05-23 16:20:11', 363, 0),
(366, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Dictionaries for learners of Czech', 'I would like to hear what dictionaries have been found useful by students of Czech. Personally, I think the only one worth its salt is the latest edition of the Fronek\'s dictionary. Its disadvantage for daily use, though, is its relative bulkiness. I wonder which of the pocket dictionaries have been found most useful.\n', '2001-06-02 01:26:07', 0, 0),
(367, 15, 'Student of Czech', 'cb@bohemica.com', 'Thoughts on learning Czech', 'I have a few thoughts on learning Czech that I\'d like to share with you, arising out of my own experiences, and would love to have your feedback. I believe you haven\'t touched on all of these thoughts. Forgive me if you have :-)\nCzech is probably not so difficult as I initially thought, the reasons for which you have already outlined, and for other reasons that I\'d like to present to you:\n-some words look similiar to English words: kino, inteligentni, skola, etc.\n-99 per cent of the consonant sounds are recognizable and pronounceable by English speakers\n-The vowel sounds are really not that difficult at all, for an English speaker\nWhat IS difficult:\n-I\'ve noticed that a single Czech word can take an ending with i, e, y, and their numerous perumutations: i with the accent i without the accent, e with an accent, e without the accent, y with the accent, y without the accent, a with the accent, a without the accent....at some time or other, depending on whether (the very same \"word form\") is a noun, an adjective and an adverb, etc. There are many possiblities arising out of the agglutination of these endings, which make for laborious learning.\nLeave to the autodidact to figure out the system/method! Truly, I\'d rather assimilate this by just hearing people speak and repeating stock phrases, while in the country, but for now I\'m stuck with memorizing tables, and trying to commit to my memory rules concerning what is a hard stem and what is a soft stem, so that I can attach the right ending.\nAnother thing which seems odd to me, at this point in my learning, is Czech word order.\nCzech syntax really strikes me as peculiar, especially when I see side by side translations into English in my textbooks. I know that Czech is supposedly a SVO type language, but I often question this, particularly when you include the syntax of interrogatives. Even English seems susceptible to variations from SVO word order. Also \"se\" (ex.: To se...) and \"si\" (Cte si dal..) are very bizarre. They are very frequent in Czech sentences, but I don\'t think you can easily translate them into English.\nFinally, certain consonantal cluters are rather difficult. \"Hodne stesti\" is a hard one, and all the permutations of Chtit: Chci, ches... cause me anguish! However, I don\'t think I could make it through the day without telling someone what I \"want\"! If I could think of a synonym which avoids the unpleasant \"ch\" plus consonant combination, I would be delighted to call on it instead!\nMy method of practice: listening to good Czech rock bands who sing in Czech, gathering around me a group of Czech acquaintances who are willing to talk Czech with me, at least for part of the time, watching CT1 and Czech TV ads on the internet, and so on.\n', '2001-06-02 13:00:55', 0, 0),
(368, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'News: Czech TV/Radio on the web', 'Hi all, I received this from a student and thought it might be of interest to students of Czech:\nNova TV has finally changed their website, and we can now watch entire Czech programs broadcast live over the web. Unfortunately, they use Real Player, and a low grade, inferior version of it too.\nBBC TV (BBC World) on the other hand, has upgraded their Quick Time broadcast, and their live streaming news is undoubtedly the best live TV on the Web.\nCT1 broadcasts delayed TV news, but it is reliable. It surprises me that the quality of their Real Player TV transmission is better even than that of the French webcasts.\n', '2001-06-03 04:00:33', 0, 0),
(369, 15, 'Jason', 'none@noe-mail.com', 'Re: Czech is the hardest!', 'HI, I would like to say that I am learning Czech and Spanish right now and I would like to tell you something that has worked for me while trying to learn the verb conjugations and other parts of the language. I try to think of myself as someone that doesn\'t speak any language or a newborn child. This helps me because when I hear how to say something i think and try to commit to memory what I just learned. I don\'t ask any questions why it has to be that way. I just think if i need to ask this I know how to say it.This might sound like the worst way in the world to learn a language or exactly what everboyd else is doing but it has helped me so much and I am hoping it will help someone else.\n', '2001-06-06 01:53:35', 349, 0),
(370, 15, 'Jason', 'none@no-email.com', 'Re: News: Czech TV/Radio on the web', 'microsfts windows media also has radio. All you have to do is right click on the go button and click radio but i don\'t know about netscape. Then click on radio stations and then click radio stations guide and it will take you to a site. then find by language and you can listen to the radio in many different languages instead of CZECH!!!\n', '2001-06-06 01:57:53', 368, 0),
(371, 15, 'Gloria', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Hearing Zpravy  in Czech', 'Hi, all!\nI have been listening and viewing Czech Zpravy or News for days now since\nschool let out.  I am using this knowledge for research on a czech immigrant\nwoman that I am studying for my dissertation.  I do have a native speaker who\nis helping me, but I want to do some team translating with her.\nThis Zpravy goes by so fast that I catch about two words per sentence.  I feel\nas if drowning in incomprehensible words.  I try writing some down, but those\ndon\'t seem to come up regularly.\nI think radio and TV for children would be more useful for real beginners because\nthey speak slowly to kids, and they repeat words a lot.\nAre there any sites and times for children\'s shows that you know?\nDekuji,\nGloria\n', '2001-06-26 19:59:43', 0, 0),
(372, 15, 'Nazrul', 'naz27@pacific.net.sg', 'Study in Czech Republic (general language course)', 'Please send me more information on Czech language course for international student and How many Instutute conducting language course?.\n', '2001-06-26 23:00:32', 0, 0),
(373, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Study in Czech Republic (general language course)', 'See links in the Links section on Bohemica.com. Dominik\n', '2001-06-28 18:31:39', 372, 0),
(374, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Hearing Zpravy  in Czech', 'Dear Gloria, I highly recommend you use http://www.radio.cz for your listening practice. It is the internet version of Czech Radio broadcasting for international audience. That means that you can listen to the same thing in English and then Czech (they also do Spanish, German, French and Russian). What\'s more the broadcat transcripts are also available (in all the languages) and you can even have them delivered daily by email for free. To make your listening easier, you can even download the news bulletins in MP3 format so that you can listen to them offline.\nI don\'t know of any children\'s radio but I\'m sure there are some shows for kids on Radio Praha. In general, however, children\'s programs are more rather than less difficult. Although the language is slower and more enunciated, the vocabulary (and sometimes grammar) is not appropriate/useful for adult learners.\n', '2001-06-29 18:05:47', 371, 0),
(375, 15, 'Gloria', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Hearing Zpravy  in Czech', 'Thanks for your post, Dominik.  I have spent many, many hours listening to Czech radio and watching Czech TV on the tine screen.  I can now follow the weather reports because\nafter all there are only so many things that can happen in weather.\nThis encourages me.  I will check out the transcripts at\nthe URL you give and keep up my work on gaining some fluency.\nMy favorite show is now \"Kalendarium\" on Czech TV1 (CT1).\nThis has many US figures as featured persons.  I get more\nthan with radio because i can watch the context.\nHere is how learning Czech feels on July 1, 2001:  It is a stream of words I only understand when another word is already being said, and many I just don\'t recall.  I think of my growing fluency as little islands in a stream of words I can\'t understand.  I love to find whole phrases.  They are bigger islands.\ndekuji,\nGloria\n\"Pocasi zitra bude jasny...\"\n', '2001-07-01 22:39:44', 374, 0),
(376, 15, 'Gloria Ptacek McMillan', 'glomc@dakotaCom.net', 'POEM: Learning a New Language', '<i><b>Learning a New Language</b></i>\nI hold my breath and dive in.  Over my head<br> Sounds race by, purring and laughing as they<br> Chase each other all around my ears.\nI cannot speak to them.  They glide along,<br> Hum songs as I gasp for air, feel the water<br> Close above my head, and grab a branch.\nBrrm--hrrm--zrrm. I long for the shore<br> And never to come again into the streaming,<br> whirling cauldron of sound.<br>\nOne whole phrase jumps out and shakes<br> Me by the shoulder and I turn around.<br> Zackit -- stodil -- mokryk?<br>\nEach day I hold my breath and dive in.<br> Over my head sounds race by,  a few stop<br> And speak to me.\nGloria McMillan\nJuly 5, 2001\n', '2001-07-10 23:32:02', 0, 0),
(377, 15, 'allison', 'alucas9@pacbell.net', 'u.s. student looking to study abroad in prague...', 'anyone out there with helpful information in this department? i\'m speculating at this time.  input in regards to schools or programs would be greatly appreciated. thanks :)\n', '2001-07-23 08:53:36', 0, 0),
(378, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: u.s. student looking to study abroad in prague...', 'Try http://www.chp.cz, they organize good courses for US undergraduates from selected schools (they might also be able to point out other options). You can also have a look at http://www.ff.cuni.cz (Charles University Faculty of Arts) to see what they have to offer.\nIf you just want to learn some Czech, I recommend a summer school (see a list under Links section here).\n', '2001-07-23 21:01:18', 377, 0),
(379, 15, 'claudio', '99twister@gmx.de', 'week-end courses : cz for foreigners', 'Hello!\nI´m looking for a week-end course \"czech for foreigners \" in Praha or Plzen or.... (?)\nHas somebody infos about that???\nThanks\n', '2001-09-14 16:40:03', 0, 0),
(380, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Re: week-end courses : cz for foreigners', 'I can only point you to the Links section where there are links to summer schools. Many of these also offer other types of courses all year round.\nAnother place to look is Teacher Bank here on Bohemica.com. It lists both private tutors and language schools that teach Czech.\n', '2001-09-15 03:18:06', 379, 0),
(381, 15, 'Erik', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Dictionaries for learners of Czech', 'I\'ll agree that the first Czech-English dictionary I\'ve ever seen that comes near the standards of quality for other languages\' translation dictionaries is Fronek\'s (I assume you mean Velky cesko-anglicky slovnik). It\'s the first dictionary I\'ve seen that has the word (and meaning of the word) I\'m looking for 9 times out of 10. It also has superb declension and conjugation references, and helps beginners with separate headwords for things like \"oci\". But no, it\'s definitely not something to take in your small backpack. Also, that dictionary can be confusing for speakers of US English who are not yet experienced with British English, as it tends to treat US English as second-class and often leaves out \"American\" words completely--if you don\'t know your \"lifts\" and \"lorries\", you can end up not much the wiser.\nI used to use Fronek\'s (?) old red-white-blue Cesko-anglicky slovnik, which was smaller, but too poorly bound to handle being carried in a backpack and too incomplete to be reliable. In offices I often found ancient dictionaries more complete than that one, but I also seem to remember them containing errors, especially false friends (aktualni-actual) and uncommented half-false friends (kontrolovat-control). I never found any of the pocket dictionaries to be much better than the rest--there\'s only so much you can fit in such a small space.\nOn the \'net, I use the dictionary at Moravia Translations (www.moravia-it.com), although its lack of maintenance means it is full of false friends and false information (it uses user contributions).\nAs an absolute beginner, I found the dictionary in the back of Colloquial Czech the best.\n', '2001-09-20 05:02:14', 366, 0),
(382, 15, 'Dan Becker', 'DanBecker@hotmail.com', 'Czech Tutors in CA or NY', 'Hi all,\nI\'m looking for a Czech tutor in the White Plains, NY or Orange County, CA area.\nI am committed to learning to speak at an beiginner/intermediate level within 6 months.  However, at the rate my self study is proceeding I think a tutor is needed.\nIf anyone can assist or forward on information regarding where to find available tutors, please contact me at DanBecker@hotmail.com\n', '2001-09-19 02:18:14', 0, 0),
(383, 15, 'Vessela', 'vesy@rice.edu', 'David Short\'s textbook', 'As a complete beginner in Czech language, I bought David Short\'s textbook, Teach Yourself Czech.  So far I have been doing very well with it.  It seems to be really well organized when it comes to inroducing new grammar...\nOne question I have though is, how updated are the expressions and the vocabulary being used in the text book.  I am asking that because I happen to say something in Czech to a Czech girl, using the exact word and question as it was shown in the textbook and she smiled a little and said: \"We don\'t really say that in Czech. \"\nSo, here I am, really willing to make an effort and learn the language with all its complexities and peculiarities, but at the same time I want to be sure that when I go back to the Czech Republic I will be able to carry on a simple conversation using simple but common vocabulary and expressions.\nSo, any thoughts on that or how to learn more vocabulary would be much appreciated.\nThanks a lot!\n', '2001-09-22 20:33:57', 0, 0),
(384, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Dictionaries and verbs', 'Hi, I\'m learning Czech coming off of Italian, and I figured a good start would be to drill the verb conjugations into my head. Problem one is that different authors divide up the Czech verbs in different ways:\nMichael Heim:\n1: -jí verbs\n2: -í verbs\n3: -ou verbs\nDavid Short:\n1: a-conjugation\n2: i-conjugation\n3: e-conjugation\n4: uje-conjugation\nElisabeth Billington:\n1: \'dělat\' verbs (-at or -át)\n2: -ovat verbs\n3: \'mluvit\' verbs (-it, -et, or -ět)\n4: \'číst\' verbs (all the rest)\nThis variety is a far cry from the universal -are, -ere, -ire division of Italian. But this shouldn\'t be a big deal, as I can just divide up the verbs according to what seems most helpful to me (so far Billington\'s seems best); I don\'t expect textbooks to think the way I do.\nThis segues to problem two. I can\'t find a single online Czech-Czech dictionary, which I would expect to have verb conjugation tables (akin to www.garzanti.it). So I can\'t even verify my theories about which verbs are conjugated alike, which is pretty important information if you plan on dividing up verbs by conjugation.\nAny ideas?\n', '2001-09-22 21:34:14', 0, 0),
(385, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: David Short\'s textbook', 'You raised an interesting point. I know David personally and he\'s a brilliant linguist and teacher who knows Czech perfectly. So all the phrases he presents are well researched and still in use. Unfortunately, sometimes they are too idiomatic (not colloquial), and I have noticed that Czech speakers react to them unfavourably when used by a learner.\nThe problem is that, if you ask for common vocabulary and expressions and learn them, you will not be appreciated by Czechs, unless you are near perfect. It is the same in English, you expect learners to speak in a certain way and if they use advanced idioms in otherwise broken English, you look askance at them.\nAnother problem with the Czech in David\'s book is that it describes Czech in situation in which most Czechs do not think it shows its best side. For a lot of young people, it may be language their parents have used and for some it may not be the literary standard they would like to present to the world. The trouble with Czech is that there aren\'t enough learners of it for most Czechs to fully realize what that involves.\nMy advice is perseverance. Use the language from the book (which contains lots of good phrases) but listen to your friends\' speech as well and try to mimic the way they speak. To learn a language well takes lots of time and effort and you must expect lots of little setbacks like these along the way.\n', '2001-09-24 00:26:55', 383, 0),
(386, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Dictionaries and verbs', 'The funny thing about Czech verbs is that even though there are only four officially irregular verbs, most of them display some kind of irregularity for the learner.\nIn fact, none of the systems you mention describes the Czech verb in all its complexity (nor does it try to). They try to find a way of presenting the verb to a student who is likely to learn the infinitive first. The \'real\' classification of the Czech verb, however, relies on 5 basic categories of 1 - 3 models each, providing us with a total of 14 models (with additional exceptions and oscillations, as usual). I have made an overview of these for my students recently and will post them in the Reference section in the near future (at the moment it only presents the Billington system). I\'m also working on a Czech verb dictionary which will appear on Bohemica when it\'s ready (anywhere between a month and two years from now).\nWhat defines these models is how they differ in the third person singular (-e, -ne, -je, -í, -á) and plural (-ou/-í, --ejí, -ají) and first person singular (-m, -i/-u, -ji/-ju). There is no easy regular link between the models and their infinitive. That\'s why none of the textbooks can give a complete picture. The full 14+ model system is too cumbersome to be presented to students but if you\'re looking for a complete picture, that\'s where you\'ll have to go.\nI know of no online dictionary that even mentions the classes of verbs. The best dead-tree dictionary, though, is the one by Fronek which has several dozen models (including nearly all the exceptions) and each verb has a reference to it. It is simple the best all-round dictionary for students on the market (the only one worth mentioning really).\n', '2001-09-24 03:00:41', 384, 0),
(387, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: Dictionaries and verbs', 'The 14+ model system that I should consult: is there anywhere it can be found? Or does every available Czech grammar book shy away from teaching Czech grammar?\n', '2001-09-26 06:06:55', 386, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(388, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: New verb chart on Bohemica', 'OK, you jolted me into action and I posted an overview of the 14 model verb system plus a list of a couple dozen irregular verbs. You can find it under <b>Czech Online/Reference/Verbs/Categories</b>. None of the grammars for learners present the system in that way because it may not be of much use to people who don\'t already know how to use it. But for completeness\' sake, I feel you should know what it looks like.\nIn theory, you end up with four different words in Czech, for every English one. Present and past stem and perfective and imperfective. The past stem is reflected in the infinitive and the present stem in the conjugated form of the verb. Very often, they are quite similar, e.g. to shop is koupit/koupím (pf.) vs. kupovat/kupuju (ipf). But in rare cases, there really are four words the learn, e.g. to take is brát/beru (ipf). vs. vzít/vezmu (pf.). The five categories/14 submodels apply only to the formation of the present stem (i.e. the conjugated form of the verb - I don\'t like the term \'present stem\' because for perfective verbs, the conjugated form has a future meaning).\n', '2001-09-28 02:37:38', 387, 0),
(389, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: New verb chart on Bohemica', 'Thank you much. I haven\'t had a chance to give this a thorough read yet, but I will very soon. Then I\'ll probably have more questions!\nThanks again!\n', '2001-10-01 08:51:35', 388, 0),
(390, 15, 'jenn', 'jennjezek@icqmail.com', 'learning czech', 'i am interested in learning the czech language but i have no idea where to start..any tips??\n', '2001-10-05 07:44:09', 0, 0),
(391, 15, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: learning czech', 'Hopefully soon, my answer will be here. But the online Czech course we\'re working on (and off) isn\'t ready yet. Bohemica.com may be a good place to start anyway. Look at some basic exercises and grammar explanations in Czech Online. Read about Czech culture. Go to the Bookstore section and buy a textbook. See if there is a teacher near you in the Teacherbank. Look in Links for Czech schools and other sites of interest.\nBut if you really want to learn, the answer is find a teacher or take a course and buy a book. It\'s best to have the teacher before the book (he/she will probably have one that they know) but it\'s not essential. People typically end up acquiring more than one before they find the one that suits them. You can always use one of those Teach Yourself books. You will also need a good dictionary (Fronek listed in Bookstore/Reference is the best).\nAnd finally, prepare yourself for a lot of hard work. I hope someone else will be able to share their beginnings to smooth the path for you and other beginners. I started as a baby and have no recollection of what must have been a demanding but rewarding time.\n', '2001-10-05 19:42:42', 390, 0),
(392, 15, 'jenn', 'jennjezek@icqmail.com', 'Re: learning czech', 'well i think i have done all that i can do for now, i went to the public library here where i am and took out some books on czech culture, and also some phrase books and dictionaries, i am hoping that these will start my journey on learning the language\n', '2001-10-06 02:38:26', 391, 0),
(393, 15, 'Sean Wagle', '', 'Learning from others', 'I spent about 3 months studying Czech on my own. After a while I found that I wasn\'t learning much more, because it seemed like the earlier material was \"falling out\" of my mind, as I moved to the later chapters.... But I have met a few Czechs in my local area, and paid them a small fee to read the books with me and correct mistakes when I speak. I find that this helps my retention a great deal.\nThis shouldn\'t be a big surprise-- all languages were verbal before they were written. Our minds are preprogrammed to learn conversationally.\nThose of you who really want to learn Czech, I\'d advise that you seek out some native speakers, and overcome any hesitancy to show your clumsiness. You don\'t need a professional $40/hr translator to read vocabulary lists, and fix word endings so they \"sound right\". A retired person or an Au-Pair, who might make $8/hr in a normal job, is quite qualified to get you through the basic language, at a more affordable rate.\n', '2001-10-13 12:46:55', 0, 0),
(394, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Learning from others', 'You are certainly correct. As long as you don\'t expect too much of such a person, they should be enough to help you go through a teach-yourself book.\r\nYou should be aware, however, that even educated Czechs (just like educated native speakers of all languages) have great gaps in their knowledge of the Czech grammar at the learner level. There also a huge number of myths about \'good\' Czech you should be wary of. But as a conversational partner, any native speaker will do. In fact, there is no other way, than to use \'any\' native speaker.\n', '2001-10-14 21:40:23', 393, 0),
(395, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Verb chart (Categories III-V)', 'Hi, I\'m struggling to understand the verb chart at http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/reference/verbs/categories.htm\r\nI\'ll start with the simplest category, category V. It applies to some (but not all) verbs whose infinitive ends in -at or -át. Conjugation follows \'dělat\':\r\ndělám, dělá, dělá, děláme, děláte, dělají; dělal\r\nSo \'začínat\' presumably conjugates:\r\nzačínám, začíná, začíná, začínáme, začínáte, začínají; začínal\r\nIs that correct? I am especially concerned about the apparent rule that the long-a is present for everything but the 3rd person plural and the past participle. Also, does every member of category V lose only the \'t\' to form the proper stem? Or do stems vary?\r\nNow to category IV. It applies to all -et and -it infinitives, some -ět infinitives, and some -ít infinitives. The first sub-category (IVa) applies to all -it and some -ít infinitives. Conjugation follows \'prosit\'; but I don\'t know how to conjugate \'prosit\'. Presumably, it goes:\r\nprosím, prosí, prosí, prosíme, prosíte, [?]; prosil\r\nBut, as indicated, I don\'t know the 3rd person plural. Is it \'prosí\'? \'prosejí\'? \'prosějí\'? Presumably it is the first, since \'mluvit\' is part of IVa and \'mluví\' is <i>its</i> 3rd person plural. But the chart does not say for sure. I also do not know if stems follow the apparent \"drop the \'t\'\" rule or if they vary. And I do not know if the long-i appears in all but the past participle.\r\nNow to IVb. It applies to all -ět infinitives and some -et and -ít infinitives. Conjugation follows \'trpět,\' which presumably goes:\r\ntrpím, trpí, trpí, trpíme, trpíte, trpí; trpěl\r\nI do not know how to form the past participle for an -ít Category IVb verb. I also do not know if the past participle for an -et verb would receive the haček. I do not know if stems follow the \"drop the \'t\' or the \'et\'\" rule or if they vary. Finally, I do not know why \'rozumět\' is not in a separate category, since its 3rd person plural, \'rozumějí\', does not follow \'trpět\'.\r\nNow to IVc. It applies to some -et and some -ít infinitives. Yet, if we look at the list of examples, we find an -ět infinitive, smět. I do not know why. Conjugation follows \'sázet,\' which presumably goes:\r\nsázím, sázi, sází, sázíme, sázíte, sázejí; sázel\r\nI do not know how to form the past participle for -ít Category IVc verbs, e.g., znít. \'znel\'? \'zníl\'? Nor for -ět Catgeory IVc verbs, e.g., smět. \'smel\'? \'směl\'? Stem rule puzzlement as before.\r\nNow to Category III. It applies to all -ovat and -ýt infinitives and some -ít, -out, -ět, and -át infinitives. The first sub-category (IIIa) applies to some -ýt infinitives and some -ít, -out, -ět, and -át infinitives. Conjugation follows \'kryt\', which presumably goes:\r\nkryju, kryje, kryje, kryjeme, kryjete, kryjou; kryl\r\nIt is unclear if verbs other than -ýt verbs really follow \'kryt\'. For instance, \'obout\':\r\nobyju, obyje, obyje, obyjeme, obyjete, obyjou; obyl ?\r\nor\r\nobouju, obouje, obouje, oboujeme, oboujete, oboujou; oboul ?\r\nor\r\nobuju, obuje, obuje, obujeme, obujete, obujou; obul ?\r\nThis uncertainty extends to all other verbs in IIIa.\r\nIIIb concerns -ovat verbs, which are purported to be exceedingly regular. So \'kupovat\':\r\nkupuju, kupuje, kupuje, kupujeme, kupujete, kupujou; kupoval\r\nPresumably stem formation is regular, following the \"drop the \'ovat\' rule\".\r\nI\'ll get to my questions about the first two Categories later.\n', '2001-10-23 11:47:10', 0, 0),
(396, 15, 'Martin Howlings', 'howlings@europe.com', 'Are you about to move to Prague? Live there?', 'Meet others like you. You are not alone.\r\nhttp://www.expats.cz\r\nLinked with all the major parts of Prague.\r\nM.\n', '2001-10-30 06:01:55', 0, 0),
(397, 15, 'macronyx', 'macronyx@centrum.cz', 'other language sites as this one', 'Dominik, I must admire your work on this server, you have done a great job. I (as a native Czech from Hradec Kralove)  am rather looking for some such-good-as-your\'s advanced foreign languages site, do you or anyone from readers know about any? I\'m very interested in Scandinavian languages, mainly in Danish, but wont to know about all others too.\r tx, macronyx\r macronyx@centrum.cz\n', '2001-10-31 22:06:30', 0, 0),
(398, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: other language sites as this one', 'To be honest, I don\'t know of any other sites like this for other languages. I would like to have a look at some, though, if anybody can find them. There are language sites and culture sites for many languages, but I don\'t know of any combining all this material.\n', '2001-11-07 03:23:23', 397, 0),
(399, 15, 'Annie J. Pinder', 'ajp-czech@leguin.org.uk', 'Czech keymap', 'Hello, I am learning Czech and I was wondering if anyone has any idea where to get a czech keyboard map for Windows 2000. I talk a lot with some czech people on line, and I think it would help me if I could practice.\r Thankyou in advance,\r Annie\n', '2001-12-20 19:59:45', 0, 0),
(400, 15, 'Annie J. Pinder', 'ajp-czech@leguin.org.uk', 'Re: Czech keymap', 'Hello again.\r After some digging around, I worked out how to do it. It appears to be differant from Win98/ME in that you must:\r * Go to control panel, and open \"Regional Options\"\r * Tick \"Central Europe\", and chose \"czech\" from the pull down menu.\r\nThis worked for me, but now I have a comma as my decimal delimiter, even though the rest of my windows is English.\r Hope this is useful.\r -- Annie\n', '2001-12-22 18:24:12', 399, 0),
(401, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech keymap', 'You can find all the relevant info on Czech keyboard on http://www.bohemica.com/online.\n', '2001-12-22 19:15:31', 400, 0),
(402, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Recognizing Declension', 'Hi, I\'m the guy who posted a really long series of nitpicky questions a while back, who didn\'t bother to thank Dominik for putting up a verb chart, and since then, has been too embarrassed and guilt-ridden to post on this forum. What follows is a short series of simple questions.\r\nI\'m trying to come up with some rules for recognizing noun declensions. The idea is that, by observing the appearance a noun, you can figure out how to decline it. Here are my theories. The examples for each declension are from this site\'s case chart. I\'d appreciate any feedback on whether these theories are accurate.\r\n-ta, -sta --&gt; masculine hard-a declension (e.g., policista)\r -ce, -dce --&gt; masculine soft-e declension (e.g., poradce)\r -o --&gt; neuter hard-o declension (e.g., pivo)\r -itě --&gt; neuter soft-e declension (e.g., moře)\r -ň --&gt; feminine consonant -e declension (e.g., daň)\r -nost --&gt; feminine consonant -i declension (e.g., noc)\r ná??í --&gt; neuter -i declension (e.g., nádraí)\r\nFinally, I know this rule isn\'t always true:\r -e --&gt; feminine soft-e declension (e.g., rýe)\r\nThat\'s because \'kníe\' is in the neuter et/at declension along with e.g., kuře. But is that rule usually true? That is, is the percentage of -e nouns in the feminine soft-e declension a very high percentage? Or is it middling, with -e nouns found in other declensions just as often?\r\nThanks!\n', '2002-01-06 14:55:49', 0, 0),
(403, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: Recognizing Declension', 'Oops! those \'--]\' symbols were supposed to be arrows. Some weird ASCII business, no doubt.\n', '2002-01-06 15:11:12', 402, 0),
(404, 15, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Recognizing Declension', 'I\'m sorry for this incovenience, but the &gt; symbols have been exchanged for ] in order to strip html tags. I changed it back manually in the file, just to keep our forum nice at the time. I think I will change the behaviour in the hopefully-coming-soon version. Until then please use <strong>&amp;gt;</strong> instead of &gt; and <strong>&amp;lt;</strong> for &lt;\n', '2002-01-07 03:11:47', 403, 0),
(405, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Soft-e Feminine nouns', 'I am trying to find out the proper endings for soft-e feminine nouns. The <a href=\"http://seelrc.org/casebooks/appendixcz.pdf\"Appendix[/a> of the <a href=\"http://seelrc.org/casebooks/\">Czech Case Book</a> by Janda & Clancy uses \'růe\' as a paradigm noun, declining thusly:\r\n  sg   pl\r N růe růe\r G růe růí\r D růi růím\r A růe růe\r V růe růe\r L růi růích\r I růí růemi\r\nHowever, the <a href=\"http://www.xrce.xerox.com/research/mltt/demos/czech\">Xerox Czech Morphological Analyzer</a> says it declines instead thusly:\r\n  sg   pl\r N růe růe\r G růe růí\r D růi růím\r A růi růe\r V růe růe\r L růi růích\r I růí růemi\r\nNote that the accusative singular ending is -i instead of -e.\r\nMichael Heim\'s Contemporary Czech uses \'země\' as a paradigm noun. This book claims (and Xerox confirms) the following declension:\r\n  sg   pl\r N země země\r G země zemí\r D zemi zemím\r A zemi země\r V země země\r L zemi zemích\r I zemí zeměmi\r\nHeim uses \'část\' as the paradigm noun for the feminine-consonant-i declension, claiming the following declension:\r\n  sg    pl\r N část  části\r G části částí\r D části částem\r A část  části\r V části části\r L části částech\r I částí částmi\r\nNow, in section 14.45 of the Heim book, it is claimed that \"[a] number of feminine nouns follow the <i><b>část</i></b> pattern except in the oblique plural, where they follow the <i><b>země</i></b> pattern. Among the most common are <i><b>chut\'</i></b>, <i><b>le</i></b>, <i><b>moc</i></b>, <i><b>mysl</i></b>, <i><b>nemoc</i></b>, <i><b>noc</i></b>, <i><b>pomoc</i></b>, <i><b>půlnoc</i></b>, <i><b>stat\'</i></b>, <i><b>ves</i></b>.\"\r\nSince the oblique plural consists of genitive, dative, locative, and instrumental, we should expect these nouns to decline thusly:\r\n  sg pl\r N    i\r G i  í\r D i  ím\r A 0  i\r V i  i\r L i  ích\r I í  emi\r\nBut, according to Xerox, both <i><b>noc</i></b> and <i><b>půlnoc</i></b> take -e, not -i, in the genitive singular and accusative plural! To complicate matters, the all case chart on this site says that both Gsg and Apl take -i. When I try Google searches, \'noci\' is supported.\r\nSo:\r (1) Is Xerox wrong to say that \'noc\' and \'půlnoc\' take -e in the genitive singular and accusative plural or is Heim wrong in 14.45 to say that all those words decline alike, since \'noc\' and \'půlnoc\' are exceptions?\r\n(2) Is the accusative singular ending for \'růe\' -i (Xerox) or -e (Janda & Clancy)?\r\nThanks!\n', '2002-01-14 15:14:05', 0, 0),
(406, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: Soft-e Feminine nouns', 'That didn\'t work at all. Here is a link to what was supposed to happen: <a href=\"http://cmitchell.freeservers.com/soft-fem.html\">http://cmitchell.freeservers.com/soft-fem.html</a>\r\nThanks again.\n', '2002-01-14 15:28:42', 405, 0),
(407, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Neutral nouns?', 'Billington\'s <i>Czech in Three Months</i> and Harkins\'s <i>A Modern Czech Grammar</i> both assure me that the Czech consonants can be divided as follows:\r\n   Hard: t, d, n, r, k, g, h, ch\r    Soft: t\', d\', ň, ř, , , č, c, j\r Neutral: p, b, m, f, v, l, s, z\r\nI took this to mean that any noun whose stem ends in a hard consonant is hard, that any noun whose stem ends in a soft consonant is soft, and that there are both hard and soft nouns whose stems end with neutral consonants. That is, one cannot tell from the stem alone whether a noun is hard or soft if the stem ends in a neutral consonant.\r\nHowever, Billington implies that there are neutral nouns, saying:\r\n\"Here are some examples.\r\nHard: most (bridge); hlad (hunger)\r Soft: věc (thing); řeč (language)\r Neutral: pes (dog); vůz (car; wagon)\"\r\nBut presumably \'pes\' and \'vůz\' are both either hard or soft, right? Especially since the <a href=\"http://www.xrce.xerox.com/research/mltt/demos/czech\">Xerox Czech demo</a> says that they are both masculine, and the declension of masculine nouns depends heavily on being hard or soft.\r\nSo, which is true?\r\n1. Nouns whose stems end in neutral consonants are either hard or soft. It\'s just that we cannot tell whether they are hard or soft merely by looking at the stem.\r\n2. Nouns whose stems end in neutral consonants are neither hard nor soft.\r\nAnd if option 2 is true, then how on earth are we supposed to decline these \'neutral nouns\'?\n', '2002-01-17 09:03:15', 0, 0),
(408, 15, 'Julek', 'woodjulian@seznam.cz', 'nejlepší kurz pro pokročilé v Praze??', 'ahoj\r\nucim se čestinu už dlouho\r ted hledám kurz soustreden na ustní jazyk, protože už za pět měšíců začnu studovat na FF.\r tak, co- UJOP, FF nebo něco jiného?\r\nvelmi rad od tebe dostávám zprávu...\r Julek\n', '2002-01-19 01:02:25', 0, 0),
(409, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Neutral nouns?', 'By and large your first assumption is true. But you (or your sources) are confusing spelling rules with conjugation rules. \'p, b, m, f, v, l, s, z\' can only be soft with respect to being followed by \'i\'. There are lists (much hated by Czech students) of words in which these consonants are followed by \'y\'. This only applies to word stems and prefixes (vil vs. vyl - he wove vs. he howled). Otherwise the y/i is controlled by grammar: e.g. psi (dogs) vs. vozy(carriages) - both hard masculines but one animate and the other inanimate.\r\nFor the purposes of assigning a noun to a declension model, the list of soft consonants is everything with a hacek plus \'c\' and \'j\'. Everything else is hard with some individual exceptions (for specific models, cases or even words).\n', '2002-01-19 18:58:31', 407, 0),
(410, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Soft-e Feminine nouns', 'The answer is very simple.\r\n1) No, Xerox are not right (at least not fully). Both \'noc\' and \'půlnoc\' add -i for genitive singular. -e is possible in some dialects, though. Heim is roughly right about the plural (although there is a lot of variation in how individual speakers use the plural dative for these nouns - e.g. both \'nocím\' and \'nocem\' is conceivable, although only the first is codified as correct).\r\n2) The -e in Jana and Clancy is obviously a typo. Good on you for noticing.\n', '2002-01-19 19:09:24', 405, 0),
(411, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Recognizing Declension', 'Cole, the best I can do for you is make an informed guess (based on introspection). My guess is, that you are broadly right. I couldn\'t come up with any exceptions to your rules although that doesn\'t mean there aren\'t some. \r\nRegarding the \'-e\' rule, my guess is that there are only a few non-feminines in that group. \'kníe\' is one as is \'páe\'. Note that most Czechs would consider \'kníe\' masculine while declining it according to a neuter model - thus \'dobrý kníe\' but \'dobrá kníata\' or \'kníe A a kníe B byli...\'\n', '2002-01-19 19:16:23', 402, 0),
(412, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: Recognizing Declension', 'OK, thanks. I don\'t quite understand \'kníe A a kníe B byli...\', but that\'s a small concern, and my main question is resolved. Thanks.\n', '2002-01-20 09:09:15', 411, 0),
(413, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: Soft-e Feminine nouns', 'Thanks, that\'s exactly what I wanted to know.\n', '2002-01-20 09:11:08', 410, 0),
(414, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: Neutral nouns?', 'There is much of this that I do not understand. I\'ll try to recapitulate what you are saying.\r\nYou say that many words whose stems end with \"the neutral consonants\" are considered soft. In all of these words, the neutral consonant is followed by \'i\'. By this, I assume you mean that, for example, a noun whose nominative singular form ends \'pi\' or \'bi\' would likely be soft. Or, that for any soft noun whose stem ends in a neutral consonant, that soft noun\'s nominative singular form will end \'pi\' or \'bi\', etc. Then, whenever one finds a noun whose nominative singular ends like that, you can make a good guess that the noun is soft.\r\nThough I can easily see that nouns are soft or hard, I do not understand how prefixes could be soft or hard. And I do not understand how <i>vil</i> and <i>vyl</i> could be soft or hard; these two words are masculine past participles, are they not? How could past participles be hard or soft? Perhaps the fact that I am asking these questions betrays misunderstanding on my part. But it seems to me that you are saying that prefixes and past participles obey this \"if a soft word\'s stem ends in a neutral consonant, then that neutral consonant is followed by an \'i\'\" rule. And I do not understand how that could apply to prefixes and past participles (neither of which even have stems, as far as I know).\r\nI do understand why <i>pes</i> pluralizes into <i>psi</i> and <i>vůz</i> pluralizes into <i>vozy</i>. The first is following the \"drop the final e\" stem change rule and then declining according to the masculine hard animate paradigm. The second is following a \"shorten the vowel\" stem change rule and then declining according to the masculine hard inanimate paradigm.\r\nYou close by saying that most nouns whose stem ends in a neutral consonant are hard nouns. The exceptions to this rule are soft nouns -- some obey peculiar declension paradigms, some have irregular soft declensions in certain cases, and others are just irregular exceptions in general. Of course, examples of these phenomena would help make them clearer, but you only have so much time and you\'ve already answered quite a bit.\r\nThanks.\n', '2002-01-20 09:35:36', 409, 0),
(415, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Neutral nouns?', 'OK, let\'s simplify it. None of the b, f, l, m, p, s, v, z are soft and nouns ending with them are always considered hard. They only differ from h, ch, k, r, and d, t, n, in that they can be followed by both \'i\' and \'y\'--but that is a purely spelling rule not impacting conjugation. (Note: when d, t, n are followed by \'i\', they are really d\', t\', n\'.) All the other consonants are soft.\r\nIf you find an exception to the above rule, learn it as such. \n', '2002-01-21 22:12:27', 414, 0),
(416, 15, 'Christina', 'Christina.Moulis@dewr.gov.au', 'Re: Soft nouns?', 'OK, but what about slangy nouns like čiča(kitty cat), uča (teacher)?  You would expect a noun ending in a to have a hard stem usually, wouldn\'t you?  How do you conjugate these, should you use the soft or hard endings?\n', '2002-01-24 08:38:19', 415, 0),
(417, 15, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', '-ást verbs', 'Can someone tell me how to conjugate verbs like krást, třást, mást? They are supposed to follow the -e model, but the ever-fallible <a href=\"http://www.xrce.xerox.com/research/mltt/demos/czech\">Xerox Morphological Analyzer</a> finds nothing for my guesses (like for mást 3pSg: mase, máse, mae, máe).\r\nThanks!\r\n', '2002-01-25 21:51:02', 0, 0),
(418, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Soft nouns?', 'The soft/hard only applies to models ending in a consonant not in a vowel. Thus all the nouns you list, are conjugated using the \'voda\' model for feminines or \'policista\' for masculines. \r\nDominik\n', '2002-01-26 13:47:52', 416, 0),
(419, 15, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: -ást verbs', 'Here are some examples of this subpattern:\r\nkrade, třese, mate, pást - pase, klást - klade\n', '2002-01-26 13:51:35', 417, 0),
(420, 15, 'Randy Stein', 'randystein@onemain.com', 'Czech Teacher In Portland Oregon', 'I\'m looking for a teacher for a beginning student.  If interested, send me your name, qualifications, and rates to the email above\n', '2002-02-18 04:51:35', 0, 0),
(421, 16, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Vítejte v diskusní skupině pro učitele', 'Dobrý den a Ahoj!\nVítám vechny učitele četiny jako cizího jazyka do této diskusní skupiny zaměřené na jejich problémy a otázky. Máte problém se nějakým studentem nebo si říkáte jak nejlépe učit pády nebo vid? Toto fórum je pro toto ideální.\nPříspěvky můete psát česky i anglicky s háčky a čárkami nebo i bez nich. Důleité jsou mylenky.\nPříjemnou diskusi.\nDominik Luke<br> <a href=http://www.bohemica.com>Bohemica.com</a>\n', '2001-04-23 19:01:38', 0, 0),
(422, 16, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Jak učit česká slovesa?', 'Ahoj vichni učitelé. Abych nějak odstartoval diskusi o problémech učení četiny otázkou <i>Jak učit česká slovesa?</i>.\nRád bych věděl jaké jsou vae názory.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 01:21:37', 0, 0),
(423, 16, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Re: Jak učit česká slovesa?', 'Po letech experimentace jsem dospěl k názoru, e nejlepí způsob jak učit slovesa v četině, je začít minulým časem. Má to mnoho výhod. 1. Minulý čas je velice jednoduchý morfologicky. 2. V minulém čase se můe často obejít vid. 3. Studenti mohou mluvit o více věcech.\nSamozřejmě potřebují i konjugaci základních sloves, ale ne se uvede přítomný čas, tak se můou konjugaci asi 10 nejdůleitějích sloves naučit nazpamě. Zkusil jsem to u v několika kurzech a opravdu to funguje.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 01:25:34', 422, 0),
(424, 16, 'Stijn Croes', '', 'Re: Jak učit česká slovesa?', 'Vazeny pan,\r\nTed\' uzivam \'Ceska slovesa v praxi\' (Praha, Academia, 1997). Jedna se o valencni slovnik nejcastejsich ceskych sloves. Zpusob knihy je divny: pracuji z hlediska jejich valence, tj. schopnosti vazat na sebe jine vyrazy a mj. tak zakladat vetne struktury. Bohuzel myslim ze ta kniha je prilis tezka :(. Stoji asi 300kc.\r\nLehci je \'Ceska Slovesa\' (Parolková, Praha, Bohemika, 1997). Je kniha s cvicenimi, ktere jsou koncipovana tak, aby mluvili predevsim studenti. Stoji 82kc (nebo 6$ do Ameriky). \r\nMej se hezky,\r\n', '2001-09-21 03:26:04', 422, 0),
(425, 16, 'Jaroslava Lapáčková', 'jarkalap@hotmail.com', 'unvoiced/voiceless', 'Obracim se do fora s dotazem - jak se do anglictiny preklada neznela (souhlaska). Ve Zlatych strankach na bohemica.com se pouziva unvoiced, kolegyne me presvedcuje, ze to nikde neslysela a ze spravny termin je voiceless. Predpokladam, ze to jsou synonyma. Je to tak? \r\nKe komentarum - je odpoved zasilana take na email nebo je mozne odpoved najit jen na strankach bohemica.com?\r JL\n', '2001-10-24 16:51:50', 0, 0),
(426, 16, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: unvoiced/voiceless', 'Oboji je mozne (viz David Crystal - Dictionary of Linguistics and Phonetics). Uznavam, ze voiceless je asi beznejsi, ale pres paralelismus s cestinou - znely/neznely -jsem se ustalil na unvoiced (mozna, ze je nekde i voiceless).\r\nDominik\r\nPS: Dostavat odpovedi na email jeste mozne neni, ale brzy by melo byt. Jak se rika: Watch this space!\r\n', '2001-10-25 01:49:53', 425, 0),
(427, 16, 'Fabrice CARBONNE (French)- 23 years.', 'fabochili@hotmail.com', 'Nation building in Bohemia', 'Hello, \r\ni am sorry to write in English but i still dont know the Czech. I am in the third year of a French Institute of Political Sciences and i am writing a \"mémoire\"  (100 pages). I have chosen to write about the role of the past (and more specifically History) in the building of the Czech Nation in the XIX. I have seen how Palacky or Dobrovsky by their researches have in a way built the Nation or to be more precise the national feeling thanks to the identification of the Czech people to their heroes. The point that interests me is more specifically the way some nationalist historians use falsifications or wrong interpretations of national History in order to build the national feeling... For instance, Vaclav Hanka or Zalud-Vysokomytsky. I am looking to any documents or ur personal comments on the topic that would help me in my research. Thanks to write me in English, at my address : fabochili@hotmail.com Thank you very much for ur help. \n', '2001-11-30 20:04:23', 0, 0),
(428, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Ahoj od Dominika', 'Ahoj!\nVítám vás v diskusi pro studenty. Je to diskuse pro začátečníky i pokročilé.Můete tady diskutovat o tom, jak se vám líbí nebo nelíbí Česká republika, český jazyk, české filmy nebo česká hudba. Můete si taky psát otázky o cestování v Čechách a na Moravě.\nJestli máte Windows, můete psát i s háčky a čárkami, jinak bude lepí psát bez háčků a čárek.\nHodně těstí,\nDominik\n<a href=http://www.bohemica.com>Bohemica.com</a>\n', '2001-04-23 19:01:38', 0, 0),
(429, 17, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Můj oblíbený český film', 'Ahoj. Tady je první téma diskuse. <b>Jaký je vá oblíbený český film?</b> Můete taky psát, proč se vám ten film líbí, kdo tam hraje, kdo byl reisér a tak.\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 01:12:13', 0, 0),
(430, 17, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', 'Můj oblíbený český film z 90. let je Akumulátor I od reiséra Jana Svěráka. Jan Svěrák taky natočil film Kolja, který dostal Oskara, ale já si myslím, e Akumulátor je lepí. Je to parodie na akční filmy, která má velice subtilní humor. Hlavní roli tam hraje reisérův otec Zdeněk Svěrák, stejně jako v Koljovi.\nByl jsem na představení Akumulátora v Londýně a vem se ten film moc líbil.\nViděli jste ten film taky? Jak se líbil vám?\nDominik\n', '2001-04-27 01:19:35', 429, 0),
(431, 17, 'matilda handley', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'muj oblibeny cesky film', 'ahoj vsichni kdo se ucite cesky, chteli bychom prispet do rubriky muj oblibeny film...minuly rok jsme si v cechach koupili kazetu s filmem Pelisky a vsem doma se moc libil hudba je hezka a dobre pro tancovani.....od matisek (dcera kevina) 2 roky\n', '2001-04-27 03:37:26', 429, 0),
(432, 17, 'Chris Garbett', 'chrisgarbett@blueyonder.co.uk', 'Můj oblibený české město', 'Mám rád Prahu. Moje manelka a já byli čtyřikrát v Praze, a to je krásné město. Vloni, jeli jsme na Výstavitě, (a viděli jsme Křiíkovou Fontanu),  a Vyehrad. Máme rádi Obecní dům, a Plzeňská restaurace dolů. Je výborná restaurace, a levná!  Take, Restaurace Mucha je výborná.\neli jsme take na \'AghaRTA\' jazz klub, Krakovská 5, a byl výborný.\n', '2001-05-02 16:40:21', 0, 0),
(433, 17, 'Jochen', 'jochen_steinmetz@yahoo.com', 'Ceska televize', 'Ahoj vsem,\nrad bych se dozvedel, jestli nekdo z Vas ma nejake zkusenosti s ceskou televizi v internetu.\n1.) Vyzkousel jsem uz www.nova.cz ale do zahranici nevysilaji zive a po urcite dobe uz ani nevidim zadny obrazek nybrz jenom zvuk.\n2.) CT1 najdete na www.ct1.cz . Vysilaji jen zpravy ale za to i zive.\nCo Vy, mate vice uspeche nez ja?\nJochen\n', '2001-05-03 21:13:08', 0, 0),
(434, 17, 'Michal Belda', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Ceska televize', 'Zdravicko,\npokud vim, tak Nova vysila po internetu jen nektere porady (vysvetluji to snad autorskymi pravy). A vzhledem k tomu, ze spojeni ze zahranici asi nebude vzdycky nejlepsi, tak je docela mozne, ze problemy s obrazem jsou zpusobene prave nedostatecnou rychlosti, pripadne propustnosti linek.\n', '2001-05-04 15:45:48', 433, 0),
(435, 17, 'Peter Dain', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', 'Neznam titul ale muj oblibeny fil o lidech kdo kradou knofliky od pohovky kdyz navstiviji lidi.\nVidel jsem ten film v kine ICA (london).\nMoc se mi libi filmovy festival v Karlovy Vary, doufam ze jdu tam opet tento rok.\n', '2001-05-05 02:03:17', 429, 0),
(436, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Ceska televize + radio', 'Televize opravdu vysílá jen zprávy, ale české rádio vysílá skoro vechno. Pro studenty je moc dobré Radio Prague, protoe vechny zprávy mají na webu i jako text (a to nejen česky, ale i anglicky, německy nebo rusky). Najdete ho na <a href=http://www.radio.cz>http://www.radio.cz</a>.\nSeznam vech českých rádií, která vysílají na internetu, najdete na <a href=http://www.radia.cz>http://www.radi<b>a</b>.cz</a>\n', '2001-05-05 16:43:12', 433, 0),
(437, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Moje oblíben<i>é</i> české město', 'Já mám taky rád Prahu. il jsem tam skoro deset let a je to kadý rok lepí. Ale Praha není jediné zajímavé české město. Například Liberec, kde jsem se narodil, je hezké město v Jizerských horách. Je ideální pro lidi, kteří rádi chodí na tůry. V zimě je zase výborné pro lyaře.\n', '2001-05-05 16:47:21', 432, 0),
(438, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', 'Ten film se jmenoval Knoflíkáři. Myslím, e vyhrál nějakou cenu. Mě se moc nelíbil, protoe byl příli jednoduchý. Ale bylo tam několik humorných scén.\n', '2001-05-07 06:01:52', 435, 0),
(439, 17, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', 'Mne se nezdal prilis jednoduchy, naopak docela promysleny s podobne provazanou povidkovou strukturou jaka se stala popularni po Pulp fiction a podobnych filmech.\n', '2001-05-07 17:50:19', 438, 0),
(440, 17, 'flute', 'flute@yahoo.com', 'flute', 'ahoj --\njsme v parie\na taky studujeme česky. ěčř\n', '2001-05-11 21:46:12', 0, 0),
(441, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: flute-četina v Paříi', 'Ahoj v Paříi! Na jaké kole studujete? Jakou učebnici pouíváte? Je vá učitel Čech nebo Francouz?\nDominik\n', '2001-05-12 01:05:12', 440, 0),
(442, 17, 'Kevin Handley', 'handlekevinka@aol.com', 'Nový český film', 'Ted´ v Praze dávají nový český film Tmávomodrý svět. Chtěl bych vědět, jestli je ten film tak dobrý, jako Svěrákovi minulé filmy? Nevíte, kdo tam hraje, já jsem jenom slyel o Ondřeji Vetchém? Myslím, e to bude film zajímavý pro lidi co se učí česky a má Čechy rád. Budu rád, kdy mi napíe, kdo ten film viděl.... díky a ahoj Kevin\n', '2001-05-21 04:44:58', 0, 0),
(443, 17, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Tmavomodrý svět', 'Jeste jsem ten film nevidel, ale cetl jsem nektere recenze a byly vetsinou pozitivni.  Cinema.cz mu dala 100% a jeji ctenari 90%. Nekteri kritici, ale rikali, ze byl ten film tak trosku nudny.\nJe to prvni film Zdenka a Jana Sveraka, ktery neni komedie. Pojednava o osudech ceskych pilotu, kteri bojovali za druhe svetove valky v Bitve o Anglii a byli pak v padesatych letech perzekuovani. Urcite bude alespon historicky zajimavy.\nOpravdu tam hraje Ondrej Vetchy. Blizsi informace naleznete na <a href=\"http://www.tmavomodrysvet.cz\">http://www.tmavomodrysvet.cz</a>\n', '2001-05-23 16:42:01', 442, 0),
(444, 17, 'Gloria Ptacek McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', '              Můj oblíbený český film je _Obchod na Korze_.\n       Ten film byl velmí smutný ale také komický. Tono Bričko\n       je přátelský, chudý člověk.  Stará skladníka (Ida Kaminska)\n       bylá věrně tragický.\n', '2001-06-10 03:47:10', 429, 0),
(445, 17, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Nový český film - recenze', 'Na Tmavomodry svet vysla v Anglii v novinach The Guardian recenze v patek 6. cervence. Muzete si ji precist na <a href=http://www.guardian.co.uk/Archive/Article/0,4273,4216422,00.html>http://www.guardian.co.uk/Archive/Article/0,4273,4216422,00.html</a>.\n', '2001-07-09 15:30:00', 442, 0),
(446, 17, 'gloria mcmillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Není nic nového?', 'Ahoj!\nMám rada mluvit česky...\nKde narodil jste?\nNarodila jsem v Chicagu. A ty?\nChicago byl velky Česke mesto.  Antonin Čermak byl\nstarosta v 1930s. Čermak byl v Florida nabíjet.\nSrdečne pozdravy,\nGloria\n', '2001-08-19 06:25:19', 0, 0),
(447, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Kde jsem se narodil', 'Ahoj Glorie, jsem rád, e má ráda četinu. Mě se četina líbí, protoe je to poetický jazyk.\nJá jsem se narodil v Liberci. To je město asi 100 kilometrů na sever od Prahy. Liberec byl industriální město, kde byl hlavně textilní průmysl. Před válkou byl Liberec hlavně německé město a jmenoval se Reichenberg.\nNerozuměl jsem, co dělal starosta Čermák na Floridě. Můe to zkusit říct jetě jednou?\nDěkuju, Dominik\n', '2001-08-24 02:02:08', 446, 0),
(448, 17, 'gloria mcmcillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Kde jsem se narodil', 'Ahoj, Dominik!\nTvuj dopis měl Rumunský pismeny. (??)\nStarosta Čermák byl na Floridě chodit na Democratic\nConvention s President Rooseveltu.  Vrah chcel Pres.\nRoosevelt zabit. Starosta Čermák řekl chvile zemřel,\n\"Lepi ja ne ty\"!\nRozumi ten dějinný anekdoa (\"factoid\")?\nMůj město, East Chicago, Indiana, byl takě industriální město. Můj otec, Edwin Ptacek, pracoval v Inland Steel.\nDěkuju a hezky den,\nGloria\nPS Je ...s Pres. Rooseveltu v Locative?\n', '2001-08-26 02:53:15', 447, 0),
(449, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Kde jsem se narodil', 'Ahoj Glorie, má pravdu, můj dopis měl nějaká divná písmena. Nevím proč to je. U jsem to opravil.\nStarosta Čermák byl na Floridě s prezidentem Rooseveltem (tj. je to instrumental).\nPozor! Slovo \'chvíle\' je jenom substantivum, nikdy konjunkce. Take musí říct, \'řekl kdy umíral\'. Také někdy můe pouít slovo \'zatímco\'. Například, \'zatímco v Praze prelo, svítilo v Brně sluníčko\'.\nDominik\n', '2001-08-28 02:22:59', 448, 0),
(450, 17, 'peter', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'řídit v levo', 'Slyel jsem e v roce 1930 člověk řídil v levo v československu.\nJe pravda?\n', '2001-08-30 01:00:08', 0, 0),
(451, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: řídit vlevo', 'Ano, je to pravda. Před válkou se v Československu řídilo vlevo. Myslím, e to změnil a Hitler, ale nejsem si teď jistý. V té době se řídilo vlevo v mnoha Evropských zemích, které neovlivnil Napoleon.\n', '2001-08-30 01:29:06', 450, 0),
(452, 17, 'Naďa Katzerová', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Nový český film', 'Pokud ještě stále chcete vědět něco více o filmu, tak\njeho oficiální i-netové stránky jsou k nalezení na\nadrese www.tmavomodrysvet.cz\nPříjemné počtení a pokoukání! :-)\nNaďa\n', '2001-09-25 14:43:29', 442, 0),
(453, 17, 'Naďa', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Jak nejlépe pomoci cizinci naučit se česky?', 'Ahoj vem!\nRáda bych vás poádala o radu. Můj kamarád (Američan) by se moc rád naučil česky a poádal mě o pomoc. U zvládá základy gramatiky i trochu té slovní zásoby. Já teď nevím, jestli já, jako rodilý mluvčí, mu pomůu nejvíce tím, kdy s ním budu hodně mluvit, aby mohl procvičit a naučit se nová slova, nebo jestli by bylo lepí, zaměřit se na gramatiku a postupovat podle učebnice.\nCo myslíte? Co by bylo lepí?\nDěkuji za odpověď a zdravím vechny, co se učí česky! :))\nNaďa\n', '2001-09-25 15:49:12', 0, 0),
(454, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Jak nejlépe pomoci cizinci naučit se česky?', 'To je dobrá otázka. Ti z nás, kteří se učením cizinců ivíme si ji klademe taky. Nejlepí odpověď je, zkusit vechno. Kadý člověk je jiný, někomu vyhovuje gramatika, jinému v učení překáí. Neexistuje jediný způsob výuky, který by fungoval pro vechny. Je ale pravdou, e naprostá větina lidí trochu gramatiky potřebuje, ale zároveň je příliný důraz na gramatickou správnost zdruje. Rozhodně je dobré se vyvarovat gramatických zajímavostí, pokud o ně student vysloveně nemá zájem. Je dobré pouívat hry, ale nikdy se nesmí ztratit ze zřetele, e naučit se jakýkoli  nový jazyk zabere spoustu času a úsilí.\n', '2001-09-30 20:59:10', 453, 0),
(455, 17, 'Lucie', 'lucie.heclova@seznam.cz', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', 'Muj oblibeny film poslednich let jsou Knoflikari. To neres, po nicem nepatrej a sedni si na svoje misto jsou fraze, ktere zevsednely pro spoustu lidi. Je to film slozeny z ruznych povidek a musi se videt, neda se vypravet. Jestli vam muzu doporucit, tak prave bezi Michalkovo Babi leto v hlavni roli s Brodskym a Zazvorkovou. Je to velice zajimavy pohled dnesnich duchodcu na svet a na zivot. Jinak je to herecky koncert hlavnich protagonistu.\n', '2001-10-29 19:45:14', 429, 0),
(456, 17, 'erik@sky.cz', '', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', 'Ahoj!\r\nAsi nejsem moc náročný, mně se Knoflikáři také líbili. :-) Nejlíp se pamatuji scény s Japoncama (tedy Japonci), jak se snaží říct americký sprostý slova se správným élanem.\r\nNemám však jenom jeden jediný (?) oblibený český film, spíše několik. Kromě Knolikářů jsou to <i>Šeptej</i> (s jemnou a příjemnou hudbou kapely Colorfactory), Svěrákova <i>Jízda</i>, a Zelenkoví <i>Samotáři</i>. Prostě vůbec mám raději \"malofilmy\" než \"velkofilmy\" (s vyjímkou <i>Pelíšků</i>). (\"Velkofilm\" je film s velkým rozpočtem.)\r\nZ českých filmů, které jsem neviděl, mně nejvíc zajímá Švankmayerův <i>Don Šajn</i>. Viděl ho někdo?\r\nDominiku -- je lepší se v tomto foru vyhýbat používání hovorové češtiny?\n', '2001-11-06 05:30:48', 455, 0),
(457, 17, 'Dasha', 'konsulat@stauffacherlaw.ch', 'The unique \"r\"', 'I just read your comment on the czech special letter r (the one in \"rericha\" - as my keyboard doesn\'t recognise czech signs!) There are additionally two dialects in India which use the \"czech\" r and also a rare language (or even dialect) in the far off south of South America. At least I this information was given out by Zikmund&Hanzelka in the sixties. It would be interesting to know, whether those languages have survived.\r\nGreetings\r Dasha\n', '2002-01-23 21:09:43', 0, 0),
(458, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'New board format', 'Dobrý den a ahoj,\r\n\r\nthose of you who come here often will have noticed our new design which is made possible by a new server. Now you can actually limit the messages displayed to just those in the last few days and you can be notified if somebody responds to your question.\r\n\r\nPlease let us know how you like the new system. It is only the first step in overhauling the way this site operates.\r\n\r\nDominik\r\n\r\nDominik', '2002-02-28 05:22:01', 0, 1),
(459, 18, 'Dominik Luke', 'lakoff@bohemica.com', 'Vítejte do diskuse', 'Váení kolegové,\r\n\r\nvítám vás do diskusního prostoru k naemu semináři. Na této vývěsce můete dikutovat nad problémy, na ně narazíte při četbě knihy. Budu zde také vyvěovat různá oznámení a upozornění, semináře se týkající.\r\n\r\nS pozdravy,\r\n\r\nDominik', '2002-02-28 05:34:03', 0, 1),
(463, 15, 'christopher-blake', 'critt821@yahoo.com', 'long/short vowels', 'One of my biggest problems concerning Czech pronunciation (even more so than the infamous r\') is the difference between long and short vowels. Even in simple words like \"dobré,\" I find myself wanting to put the accentuation on the \"é\" if I am to hold it longer. It is extremely difficult for me to say DObrééé (I say dobrEEE). Can you offer me any suggestions? \r\nDekuji,\r\nChris\r\n\r\nPS-I speak AmEng.', '2002-03-01 05:39:28', 0, 0),
(464, 18, 'Dominik Luke', 'lakoff@bohemica.com', 'Zbývající kopie textů a worksheetů', 'Na katedře lingvistiky najdete jetě několik dalích textů a worksheetů k přítímu semináři naleznete na katedře lingvistiky.', '2002-03-01 07:09:35', 0, 1),
(465, 15, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: long/short vowels', 'I\'m sorry but there\'s nothing much you can do but keep trying. A good rule of thumb is to try to pronounce everything longer than you feel necessary. If you feel stupid while saying it, you\'re probably saying it right.\r\n\r\nAnother good trick is to clap hands on the first syllable to help you with the stress. You can also try mispronouncing English words as if they were Czech words. Record yourself and listen if you can discern the mistakes.\r\n\r\nBut practice and perseverance are the key. There\'s no magic formula on this.', '2002-03-01 20:47:14', 463, 1),
(466, 18, 'Dominik Luke', 'lakoff@bohemica.com', 'Atomismus', 'Toto je jedno téma, které na semináři nebylo zmíněno a myslím, e bude jetě důleité se mu více věnovat. Kolega Novosad není zatím přesvědčen Lakoffovým tvrzením o nedostatečnosti atomismu jako popisu kognitivního systému. Říká: \"pojem se taky od někud vzal, resp. je také výplodem mysli. tudí musí existovat něco meního ne pojem, co je zároveň v mysli a zároveň atomistické.\" Lakoffovi vak jde spíe o to, jestli je moné tvrdit, e význam je výsledkem součtu částí (tedy dále nedělitelných). Tuto pozici zastávají třeba Kamp a Partee v Theory of compositionality. Je to doplňokové čtení, třeba by se toho někdo rád ujal. Rád bych si přečetl nějaké dalí argumenty.\r\n', '2002-03-01 21:27:54', 0, 1),
(467, 18, 'Dominik Luke', 'lakoff@bohemica.com', 'Jetě k tělesnosti a klasické kategorizaci', 'Rád bych se vyjádřil jetě k několika bodům z naí poslední diskuze, které se objevily i na worksheetech. Kolega Jeřábek se ptá \"Pokud jde o tvrzení \'Mysl je oddělená od těla a nezávislá na něm.\' - \'nezávislá\' myslí L. co do existence nebo co do vlivu působení. To druhé neříká ani Descartes.\" Lakoff zde závislostí na tělesné zkuenosti nemyslí něco tak triviální jako, e obsah kategorie reflektuje nai zkuenost. To by asi nikdo nepopíral. Jde mu o to, e i to jak s nimy manipulujeme není abstraktní od těla oddělený proces. Porovnáváme-li tedy tvrzení \'Kriket je nudná hra.\' a \'Fotbal je zábavná hra.\' nestačí nám nějaká abstraktní charakteristika nudnosti a zábavnosti. Naopak potřebujeme odkazovat ke zkuenosti se zábavou, idealizovaným scénářům zábavnosti promítnutým na zkuenost hry nebo jejího sledování. Klasická teorie (kterou se implicitně snaí modelovat počítačové modely) se snaí pouze o jednu věc a to o určení jestli hra kriket náleí do kategorie nudných věcí nebo ne. Je ale naprosto zřejmé, e toto není klasická kategorie, ale naopak odstupňovaná a neostrá kategorie. (A to vůbec nebereme v potaz nějakou intencionalitu nebo subjektivitu.)\r\n\r\nStejně tak lze odpovědět na otázku k Austinovu příkladu: \"\'Být součástí kriketové hry\' není vlastnost?\" Lze samozřejmě postulovat kruhově definovanou kategorii entit, na ně lze pouít přívlastek \'kriketový\'. Tento přístup nám dá pouhý výčet, ale opomíjí fakt, e se rozhodnutí jestli se toto adjektivum pro daný objekt pouije nebo ne bude zaloeno na úplně něčem jiném, ne \'dotazu\' do databáze vlastností. Lakoff bude dále argumentovat, e je tento popis nedostatečný nejen z praktického ale i z teoretického hlediska.', '2002-03-01 21:28:33', 0, 1),
(468, 18, 'Dominik Luke', 'lakoff@bohemica.com', 'Termín \'embodiment\'', 'Několik z vás si vimlo, e pojem \'vtělesnění\' jako překlad anglického \'embodiment\' je poněkud za ui tahající. Záměrně jsem se ale vyhýbal pojmu \'vtělení\', které má tu nevýhodu, e narozdíl od anglického implikuje dokonavost a neopakovanost. Také má dobrý protějek v \'odtělesnění\' tj. \'disembodied\'. \r\n\r\nBěhem diskuse na semináři ale několik z vás pouilo slovo \'tělesnost mylení\'. Moná, e to by byl dobrý kompromis, protoe tam není tolik náboenských implikací. Jaký na to máte názor?\r\n', '2002-03-01 21:29:17', 0, 1),
(469, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Preposition trouble, declining foreign words, and so on.', 'I think I\'ve grasped the ins and outs of Czech prepositions (dear me, there are a lot of them) except for two: \"for\" and \"from\".\r\n\r\nI know \"from\" is \"z\" when it\'s used in the \"coming from a place\" sense, as in \"Jsem z Ameriky\", and I know it\'s \"od\" when it\'s the \"away from\" or \"coming from a person\" sense, as in \"A od koho, Daso?\" (That cartoon cracked me up.) But what about in the sense of \"He doesn\'t know *beep* from Shinola\" or \"I learned Czech from this book\" or \"From this sample, we can tell...\"?\r\n\r\nAnd I know \"for\" is \"pro\" when it means \"for the benefit of\", and \"na\" when it means \"used for\", and \"protoze\" when the \"for\" really means \"because\". How do you translate the \"for\" in \"I went for the butter\", or \"I don\'t know what all this is for\"?\r\n\r\nHow does Czech decline foreign names? Like, say, \"Massachusetts\"? Or \"Tallahassee\"? Or \"Goethe\"?\r\n\r\nI\'m sort of in Czech Learning Limbo now, as I finished \"Teach Yourself Czech\" but am very frustrated to find that I still get lost in the jungle of particles, pronouns, and interrogatives, and Czechs still laugh at me in chatrooms.\r\n\r\nAlso: Does Fronek\'s dictionary have slang and colloquial terms? If it doesn\'t, where can I find a resource for looking them up, either online or in print?', '2002-03-02 12:44:13', 0, 0),
(470, 18, '', 'olina.stehlikova@email.cz', 'Re: Termín \'embodiment\'', 'Coz takhle prostě ztělesnění? Je to slovo běné slovní zásoby, ve slovnících jsem ho nala jako běný překlad slova embodiment a přestoe má \"frekventovaný\" význam mírně odliný od Lakoffova záměru, myslím, e by bylo v kontextu chápáno správně. Navíc soudím, e ztělěsnění-odtělesnění je opoziční dvojice ne vtělení-odtělesnění...\r\nPouití navrhovaného sousloví tělesnost mylení mi přijde problematické i zavádějící.', '2002-03-02 15:37:28', 468, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(471, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Preposition trouble - z vs. od', 'The key difference between z and od is that of coming from inside and leaving from just ouside. Thus, if you say: \'Jsem z New Yorku\', it means I\'m from inside New York but if you say \'Jsem od New Yorku\', you mean you\'re from some little place just outside New York. Consequently, if to say \'I got it from my friend,\' you must say, \'Dostal jsem to od kamaráda\'. If you say \'Dostal jsem to <b>z</b> kamaráda.\' it will mean you got it \'out of him.\' The interpretation of your examples follows: \'he doesn\'t know X from Y\' - \'nepozna X od Y\' - surface appearance. \'I learned it from the book\' - \'naučil jsem se to z knihy\' - from the inside - but still \'naučil jsem se to od kamaráda\' - because you only have access to the outside of your friend. The same goes for \'from this example we assume\' - \'z tohoto příkladu usuzujeme\' - from the merit (inside) of the example.', '2002-03-04 22:48:04', 469, 0),
(472, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Preposition trouble - na and pro and k', 'There is a third preposition that can mean \'for\' as in something is good for something. It is similar to \'na\' in meaning. Thus, \'I don\'t know what it\'s for.\' will be either \'Nevím k čemu to je.\' or \'Nevím na co to je.\' There\'s only a very slight difference - k - implies general usefulness while \'na\' a specific use (such as a tool).\r\n\r\n\'I went for the butter\' is \'el jsem pro máslo\'.', '2002-03-04 22:52:42', 469, 0),
(473, 13, '', '', 'Re: Declining foreign words', 'There are some rules but they are not always unambiguous. Many volumes have been written about this. Place names like Tallahassee or Massachusetts are typically left alone because they don\'t obviously fall into some pattern. They are typically considered neuter singular in those cases. Of course, those ending in -a, -o, or a consonant other than b, f, l, m, p, s, v, z will follow the feminine, neuter, or masculine declension respectively. Examples are: New York - do New Yorku, Chicago - do Chicaga, Florida - z Floridy. The rest are not declined unless they are commonly known and have acquired a model.\r\n\r\nProper names like Goethe are even more complicated. Many follow a mixed pattern of some sort and many have no one accepted conjugation. Goethe\'s name would go: bez Goethea, Goetheovi/Goethemu, na Goethea, o Goethem/Goethovi, s Goethem. It is a mixture of the kamarád and poradce models with some adjectival influence thrown in. Some of the rules are explained in the reference section here.\r\n\r\n', '2002-03-04 23:04:54', 469, 0),
(474, 13, 'Silver', 'silverborne@hotmail.com', 'Advice: Did I translate this correctly?', 'I want to tell a Czech friend about the difficulty a mutual friend had getting paid for a job, in the Czech language. Would this be correct?\r\n\r\nEnglish: Janet waited a month for her wages from the restaurant.\r\nCzech(?): Janet cekal mesicne jeji mzda z restaurace.\r\n\r\nI think I am using English phrasing ~I hope not the wrong words too :(\r\n\r\nThanks for any advice!', '2002-03-05 10:14:36', 0, 0),
(475, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Advice: Did I translate this correctly?', 'Just replace \'měsíčně\' (monthly) with \'měsíc\' (month) and the meaning should be clear.', '2002-03-05 23:03:39', 474, 0),
(477, 13, 'Martin Howlings', 'martin@expats.cz', 'A website for expatriates of Prague - www.expats.cz', 'I would like to introduce a new website called http://www.expats.cz\r\nIt is a site dedicated to expats who live in the Czech Republic and for those that will be relocating here. I offer a personal invite to anyone who would like to be a member of our community.\r\n\r\nPlease visit me at http://www.expats.cz or email me martin@expats.cz\r\n\r\nWith regards,\r\nMartin Howlings\r\nSite Founder', '2002-03-09 20:44:08', 0, 1),
(478, 15, 'www.expats.cz', 'expatscz@yahoo.co.uk', 'A site for expats in Czech', 'I would like to introduce a new website called http://www.expats.cz\r\nIt is a site dedicated to expats who live in the Czech Republic and for those that will be relocating here. I offer a personal invite to anyone who would like to be a member of our community.\r\n\r\nPlease visit me at http://www.expats.cz or email me martin@expats.cz\r\n\r\nWith regards,\r\nMartin Howlings\r\nSite Founder', '2002-03-09 20:45:16', 0, 0),
(479, 14, 'monte schulz', 'montecms@earthlink.net', 'Re: Czech views of Russians', 'dominik,\r\nwhat\'s interesting is how so many czechs really admire the russian hockey players while despising the nation they came from. now, of course, so many of them play together in the nhl that petty national hatred seems silly. look at pittsburgh where robert lang and martin straka play on a line alexei kovalev. they would seem to be proof this.\r\n\r\nmonte', '2002-03-13 04:02:11', 294, 0),
(480, 15, 'jason', '', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Czech in not a language that can be easily learned without prior exposure to learning other languages.I feel sorry for the English speaker who thinks that they can learn Czech without prior experience learning another language. I lived in Prauge for some time and the whole time I was there i met only one person from an English speaking country who was able to speak the language well. Most people are at first drawn to the mysterious language, and are determined to learn it. But once they start 99% soon thereafter give up when they find out just how difficult it is. I have been learning czech for about 3 years and have an advantage becuase my wife is Czech. Also, i am certain that i would not be as far along with the language had it not been for my proficiency in German and Dutch. German grammar especially eleviated 50% of the pain of learning czech grammar. Czech is a gramatically intense language, and the ultimate challenge for language learners.Although, i have heard that finnish and hungarian make czech look like cake in comparison! If you do not understand the gramatical aspects of your own language, then czech is by no means for you.\r\n\r\nWhat i would like to see is a gramatical overview of the czech language for speakers of English. I remember when i was learning German, this in particular book helped me immensely.     ', '2002-03-21 11:48:30', 343, 0),
(481, 13, 'Iva', 'ivast@yahoo.com', 'Re: Advice: Did I translate this correctly?', 'I doubt that my comment is needed. But if you wanted the right Czech, it\'d be: Janet čekala měsíc na svou mzdu od té restaurace. Well, maybe we would rather  say it another way. Like: Janet čekala měsíc, a jí ta restaurace vyplatí mzdu. But I\'m just making it more complicated for you, I\'m sorry.   ', '2002-03-23 00:20:15', 474, 0),
(482, 13, 'Judy Orlicky', 'jbavasi@aol.com', 'How do you say \"All are welcome\" in Czech', 'My mother\'s church is having a multicultural Sunday. Parishioners are asked to bring finger foods from their culture and share the language.  I volunteered to make a Czech cookie I remember my grandmother making and to find out how to say \"All are Welcome\". Can someone give me a Czech translation?  \r\nJudy Orlicky Bavasi ', '2002-03-23 05:59:13', 0, 1),
(483, 13, 'strnecka', 'strnecka@seznam.cz', 'Re: How do you say', 'I am not sure about the English phrase, I think you would like to offer cookie to everyone? Something like \"help yourself\"? Well, you have to say: \"Prosím, nabídněte si!. \r\nExact translation is: Vichni jsou vítáni. But we use first sentence, which could be translated as: offer yourself.', '2002-03-26 19:31:07', 482, 0),
(484, 13, 'Dorota', 'dorota.hesse@polishmarket.com', 'pronounciation of y & i', 'How to pronounce short and long y and short and long i?\r\n\r\nMy little Polish book on Czech says it is all like Polish \'i\' (when short) or \'ii\' (when long), so like \'ee\' in the English \'meet\', just sometimes short, and sometimes long. But I don\'t think they are right... My Czech friend has tried to teach me these sounds but somehow he didn\'t manage :) Anyway I think he was trying to tell me that the Czech sounds are not just like the Polish \'i\' or \'ii\'. I just still don\'t know what they really are... :))))\r\n\r\nWhoever will answer, thank you in advance! :-)', '2002-03-26 20:48:15', 0, 1),
(485, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: How do you say', 'Another option is to say: \"Poslute si!\" but I think a simple \"Prosím\" might do the trick.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2002-03-27 06:15:53', 482, 0),
(486, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: pronounciation of y & i', 'Sorry to say, but you little Polish book is right. \'y\' and \'i\' are pronounced exactly the same - which applies to their long variants as well. There are many Czechs who claim they can hear the difference but they are wrong. I think the problem lies in the difference in pronunciation of this vowel sound between Polish and Czech. ', '2002-03-27 06:19:57', 484, 0),
(487, 13, 'Dorota', 'dorota.hesse@polishmarket.com', 'Re: pronounciation of y & i', 'Dominiku!\r\n\r\nYou don\'t have to be sorry! :))\r\nThis is great news, actually, as it makes my studying easier :)))\r\n\r\nDekuji.\r\nDorota.', '2002-03-27 11:37:40', 486, 1),
(488, 13, 'Ben', 'walshb@kbcfp.com', 'Re: pronounciation of y & i', '\r\nsorry to disagree, but... in my experience czech people can definitely tell the difference between \"y\" and \"i\". for example, i had an interesting conversation in which i mispronounced \"prirozeny\" as \"prirozeni\" (look it up!)\r\n\r\nas far as pronunciation goes, \"y\" is easy - just like \"ee\" in english. \"i\" is easy to pronounce but hard to explain. the closest i can think of is in the film \'monty python and the holy grail\' - the knights who say \"ni\" - that\'s the \"i\" sound you\'re looking for. not the best example i admit :-)\r\n\r\ncheers\r\n\r\nben\r\n', '2002-03-28 22:22:26', 487, 0),
(489, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: pronounciation of y & i', 'sorry to disagree i-)\r\n\r\nyou are talking about different pronounciation of \"n\" before \"y\" and \"i\" (and also \"d\" and \"t\") - it is the \"n\" (\"d\" and \"t\" resp.) that is pronounced as \"ň\" (\"ď\", \"\") before \"i\".\r\n\r\nand as for the knights who say \"ni\", you can definitely write \"ni\" or \"ňy\", and you won\'t be able to tell the difference if you say it (at least if you don\'t try hard to pronounce the latter as \"ň&uuml;\")', '2002-03-29 13:09:31', 488, 0),
(490, 14, 'Dorota', 'dorota.hesse@polishmarket.com', 'Czech in K-k', 'Does anyone know where I could find something like a Czech centre /meetings /etc in Krakow?\r\nThanks,\r\nDorota.', '2002-04-02 17:06:39', 0, 0),
(491, 14, 'Kaetter', 'kaetter@hotmail.com', 'Attitudes to work and work roles', 'For a school project I am trying to find out more about the Czech culture: I would like to know the Czechs attitudes to authority, their attitudes to work and work roles, and also their attitudes to trade unions and industrial action ..........\r\n\r\nThanks!', '2002-04-02 20:33:58', 0, 0),
(492, 13, 'Tomek', 'tomek.koziel@polishmarket.com', 'The Beatles', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nI would like to know if there is a \'Czech\' name of this band. For example, in Poland we call them \'Beatlesi\' or \'Beatelsi\' (the first one is actually correct).\r\nThank you,\r\nTomek', '2002-04-03 19:25:27', 0, 1),
(493, 13, 'littlefox', 'littlefox@atlas.cz', 'Re: The Beatles', 'The Czech translation of \"The Beatles\" is \"Brouci\".\r\nCzechs use both the names, but prefer the English original.', '2002-04-03 20:53:11', 492, 0),
(494, 13, 'Dorota', 'dorota.hesse@polishmarket.com', '\'ci\', \'si\'', 'Does \'i\' soften \'c\' and \'s\' in Czech? As far as I have noticed it doesn\'t but please tell me if I am right.\r\nThanks.\r\nDorota', '2002-04-04 18:20:17', 0, 1),
(495, 13, 'littlefox', 'littlefox@atlas.cz', 'Re: The Beatles', 'The Beatles are also called \"Bítlsáci\" here :)', '2002-04-05 13:54:33', 493, 0),
(496, 13, 'Dorota', 'dorota.hesse@polishmarket.com', '\'L\' sound', 'How do you pronounce \'L\' in Czech? For me it sounds as you have to put your tonge somewhere back (I don\'t know how to explain this as I am not a linguist...) and sometimes it even sound like \'R\' to me... Why?', '2002-04-05 22:31:20', 0, 1),
(497, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: \'ci\', \'si\'', 'The short answer is no it doesn\'t. It only softens d, t, n into ď, , ň.', '2002-04-06 04:40:01', 494, 0),
(498, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: \'L\' sound', 'The Czech \'l\' is different from Polish. The tounge forms sort of a \'u\' and touches the palate. It is very similar to \'r\' except the tongue doesn\'t vibrate.', '2002-04-06 04:43:07', 496, 0),
(499, 14, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: a simple question', 'Sorry, it took this long to answer. I actually didn\'t know for certain and only now have come across this information. Yes, Slovaks had to decide which citizenship they wanted. There was some grace period but ultimately, they had to make the choice.', '2002-04-06 04:45:20', 337, 0),
(500, 14, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Attitudes to work and work roles', 'Interesting question. The Czech attitudes to all the issues you mention are mixed. Authority is built in into the language to a certain extent and there is a certain level of unquestioningly following the boss but at the same time bosses have little inherent respect (i.e. the employess often make fun of them - just like you know from most office cultures - but the official deferrence may run a little deeper here -- just an educated guess). I\'d say imagine America of the 1950s and you\'d be very close in terms of work roles and observing authority. But it\'s all changing fast and more and more Czech companies adopt a corporate culture of their trading partners and the multinationals doing business here.\r\n\r\nFinally, about trade unions. Most people look down on them and don\'t trust them (partly because of links to communists in the past, when unionazation was basically compulsory but rather than giving you any bargaining power it was used as another means of controlling the workers. But now in times of rampant capitalism the unions are becoming more of an accepted force. That is in the minds of most people. In fact, they have been pretty powerful all along since they played a role in privitazation. E.g. they have effectively blocked the privitazation of the Czech railways.\r\n', '2002-04-06 04:53:07', 491, 0),
(501, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', 'lovermann@volny.cz', 'Re: conjugation of verbs', 'If you have troubles with conjugations of verbs, you can visit my site. I`m sorry, it`s in russian, but you are to understand there all.\r\nconjugation of verbs is placed here: http://www.czech.h1.ru/gram.konj.html\r\nIf you want, I can translate it in english.\r\n\r\n---------\r\nbest regards\r\nDmitry Lovermann', '2002-04-06 05:38:49', 150, 1),
(502, 13, 'Anna', 'norfolk_lass2000@yahoo.co.uk', 'help me!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!', 'Hi!!! If you can translate czech to english, i need your help!! it\'s only 2 sentences but I need to know what they mean because the person who wrote them won\'t translate them for me so I assume they\'re not nice!! I don\'t know!!! But I need to know by next week. Anyway, this may not be written correctly but this is what they are. \"Ma\'m zasimaron tendenci ztracet holky...!? Pakto/paleto stoji spoustu udmalya vysledek se 0%\".       PLEASE HELP ME!!!!!!!!!!! thanks.', '2002-04-07 07:00:00', 0, 1),
(503, 13, 'Dorota', 'dorota.hesse@polishmarket.com', 'Re: \'ci\', \'si\'', 'It\'s me again... :) I hope there\'s no limit for asking questions... :)\r\n\r\nCan someone tell me in what other situations \'d\', \'t\', nad \'n\' get softened? I think my Czech friend was once telling me that it happens when they are followed by \'e\', and when they are at the end of the word... The problem is I don\'t really remember if I am right, and maybe there are also some other cases when these consonants should be softened.\r\n\r\nThank you in advance.', '2002-04-09 14:35:14', 497, 1),
(504, 13, 'Dorota', 'dorota.hesse@polishmarket.com', 'transgressive', 'One more question... :)\r\n\r\nWhat is present and past transgressive? Can someone give me an example of how and when to use this?\r\n\r\nI try to compare this to Polish and it seems that, for instance:\r\n\'berouc\' (feminine and neutral form of present transgressive of \'brát\')  sounds like \'bioršc = taking\', while \'minuvi\' (feminine and neutral form of past transgressive of \'minout\') sounds like \'minšwszy = having passed\'. But then I don\'t know why those transgressives have different forms for masculine, feminine and neuter, and plural... In other words, I am lost.\r\n\r\nCan someone help?\r\nThank you.\r\n\r\nDorota.', '2002-04-09 17:34:30', 0, 1),
(505, 13, 'Dawn Hill', 'dhill006@baker.edu', 'Zilla', 'I would like to know how Zilla is spelled in Prague. or if there is a different spelling for it I need it to continue my ancestery search but my great grandfather changed our name when he came to the USA from Prague?\r\nPlease if anyone can help me email me back. ', '2002-04-12 10:47:49', 0, 0),
(506, 15, 'mike', 'nihilism@the-stock-market.com', 'Re: long/short vowels', 'someone can try to correct me on this, and i may have trouble explaining it properly, but i some point i decided that the key to Stress lay in the consonant(s) at the end of the syllable more than the involved vowel.. that is, that one had to pay attention to the consonants for stress, and the vowels for long vowels.', '2002-04-14 09:00:00', 463, 0),
(507, 13, 'Chris', 'chrisgarbett@blueyonder.co.uk', 'idioms', 'Could anyone suggest a book of Czech/English idioms?\r\n\r\n', '2002-04-15 18:30:23', 0, 1),
(508, 13, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Glo\'s vety April 19, 2002', 'I have a couple \"vety\" I think I may have mistranslated:\r\n\r\nCZ:  Kolikrat vyslechtl jste tenhle CD?\r\nEN:  How many times did you listen to that CD?\r\n\r\nCZ:  Apaches zastavali sve (jejic?) reservation.\r\nEN:  The Apaches held onto their reservation.\r\n\r\ndekji,\r\n\r\nGloria', '2002-04-19 22:21:31', 0, 0),
(509, 13, 'Gloria Mcmillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Glo\'s vety April 19, 2002', 'Oops. Make that \r\n\r\n(jejich)\r\n\r\nand\r\n\r\nDekuji,\r\n\r\nGloria', '2002-04-19 22:23:13', 508, 0),
(510, 14, 'Vania', 'v_dankova@yahoo.com', 'what tourist are Czechs?', 'Hello!\r\n\r\nI am seeking for some information about what tourists are Czech people? \r\nThanks in advance!', '2002-04-21 02:20:39', 0, 0),
(511, 14, 'Arlette', 'caro245@aol.com', 'Please answer', 'HI! I WAS WOUNDERING DOES LADIES IN THE CR GO VISIT GUYS. IF SO DOES\r\nTHAT LOOKS BAD?', '2002-04-21 08:25:04', 0, 0),
(512, 14, 'Carolyn DeLoache', 'Caroline32669@yahoo.com', 'Food and Culture', 'I am interested in the role food plays in the Czech culture. What are some foods that are unique to Czechs?\r\nThanks.', '2002-04-21 20:47:09', 0, 0),
(513, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', 'lovermann@volny.cz', 'Re: idioms', ' I think it could be better to create a page, where idioms will be laid out. Do someone have any ideas on this topic? As for me, I have, but just a few ones.\r\n\r\n-----------\r\nbest regards\r\nDmitry Lovermann', '2002-04-21 23:15:09', 507, 1),
(514, 14, 'Dmitry Lovermann', 'lovermann@volny.cz', 'Re: Please answer', 'Sure, It`s not bad :))', '2002-04-21 23:25:35', 511, 0),
(515, 14, 'Dmitry Lovermann', 'lovermann@volny.cz', 'Re: Please answer', 'Sure, It`s not bad :))', '2002-04-21 23:25:41', 511, 0),
(516, 14, 'Pavel tébl', 'pavel.stebl@seznam.cz', 'Re: what tourist are Czechs?', 'I should think Czechs as tourists love to travel cheap which comes as no surprise. That makes us avoid tourist traps.', '2002-04-22 05:23:09', 510, 0),
(517, 14, 'Pavel tébl', 'pavel.stebl@seznam.cz', 'Brno', 'If you happen to be in Brno which for many reasons seems more conducive to learning Czech, don\'t hesitate to contact me. One thing I like doing is to show people round Brno, which helps collapse the myth that \'there\'s nothing worth visiting there\'. If you ever hear this, this is just Prague spite.', '2002-04-22 05:29:51', 0, 0),
(518, 13, 'Chris Garbett', 'chrisgarbett@blueyonder.co.uk', 'Re: idioms', 'Byl jedno jeden idiom, ale pokud jde o mne, nerozuměl jsem! V Londyně prel kočkami a psy, ale v Praze neprel?\r\n\r\nIdioms do not easily translate!', '2002-04-23 16:06:30', 513, 0),
(519, 13, 'littlefox', 'littlefox@atlas.cz', 'Re: idioms', '\r\nV Praze neprí kočky a psi, ale lije jako z konve ... případně \r\nse venku vichni čerti ení .. :-))\r\n\r\nIdioms are fun but cannot be translated word for word.', '2002-04-25 13:06:16', 518, 0),
(520, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Glo\'s vety April 19, 2002', 'Hi, \r\n\r\nYou write so formally! (In using the \"vy\" form.) But budí (\"so be it\"), as the Czechs say... when speaking very formally.\r\n\r\nI\'d suggest: \"Kolikrát jste si poslechl tohle CD?\" Explanation:\r\n\r\nAuxiliary verbs AFAIR must always be in a sentence\'s \"second position,\" though that\'s not necessarily the second word (i.e. sometimes it\'s later). Here, it IS the second word: \"Kolikrát jste...\" \r\n\r\nNow, the verb is AFAIK (I\'ve been speaking Czech 8 years, but that doesn\'t mean I get everything right) actually \"poslechnout si\". The \"si\" is more-or-less because we\'re speaking of an action someone does for his/her own benefit/pleasure. AFAIR when a \"si\" verb is appropriate because it\'s for one\'s own pleasure and it\'s a short activity, this is called in Czech, for some odd reason, a \"moral\" reflexive verb.\r\n\r\n(For comparison: \"poslechnout koho/co\" would be \"to obey\" him/her/it--you might say that since you\'re not doing it \"si,\" not doing it \"for yourself,\" you are a mere puppet, condemned to OBEY [insert evil laughter].)\r\n\r\n(For a different sort of comparison: there are many giving-yourself-a-treat verbs of this sort: pokouřit si, zajít/zaskočit si na pivo, dát si čaj, etc.)\r\n\r\nVyslechnout is a different sort of listening to someone. A few examples: \r\n     vyslechnout koho -- listen to someone [who is asking you a question]\r\n     vyslechnout koho -- interrogate/question sb\r\n     vyslechnout svědka -- hear/examine a witness\r\n     vyslechnout koho do konce -- hear sb out\r\n     náhodou co vyslechnout -- overhear sth.\r\n     vyslechnout si (!) zprávy -- listen to the news (but AFAIK not a CD--I may be wrong though).\r\n\r\n     In \"vy\" verbs, there\'s often a feeling of \"drawing something out of something\"--in this case, drawing meaning out of sound. Meanwhile in \"po\" verbs, there\'s often a feeling of \"po dobu,\" for a time--especially a short time, typically with a cleanly definable beginning and ending. \r\n\r\nAgain AFAIK, acronyms are nearly always neuter in gender, because when a sole letter is used as a noun, it\'s neuter. (The main exceptions being some acronyms that have been made feminine--can\'t recall offhand.)\r\n\r\n-----------------\r\n\r\nThe second one is tougher, I\'m not sure how I\'d say it myself, i.e. what I suggest below may be way off.\r\n\r\nI\'ll chicken out and suggest either \"Apačové nepřili o svou rezervaci\" or \"Apačové o svou rezervaci nepřili.\"\r\n\r\nThis is chickening out because \"o něco nepřijít\" doesn\'t mean \"to hold onto something,\" but rather \"to not-lose something.\" I have no clue how to say the former. But then again, in real foreign-language communication, \"chickening out\" is an important part of being able to communicate at all.\r\n\r\n(Zastávat = to stick up for something, to stand for something, to represent/champion/espouse/etc. sb/sth [as a lawyer/activist/etc.], or to hold... a function: \"Zastávám funkci primátora\" = I hold the post of mayor [of a large city].)\r\n\r\nYou were right with \"svou\" (and considering you didn\'t know what gender \"reservation\" is, you can hardly be blamed for using the neutral \"své\"). It links reservation back to the subject of the sentence, which is exactly what \"sv-\" possessives do.\r\n\r\nI offered the two word-order variants for the sentence because they each correspond to a different \"point\" for the sentence (this is officially called by some bland name, I think it\'s \"communicative dynamism\"). \r\n\"Apačové nepřili o svou rezervaci\" roughly = \"The Apaches held on to their RESERVATION.\" (For example: they held onto their reservation, but they lost something else.)\r\n\"Apačové o svou rezervaci nepřili\" roughly = \"The Apaches HELD ON to their reservation.\" (For example: nobody thought it was possible, but they did it, they held on to it.)\r\n\r\nBy the way: thankfully for Czech-speaking US fans of Native Americans, there is a huge \"cult\" of the Indians in this country, so there\'s no need of explanations or complicated terms to talk about Indians--as you can see in that sentence. (Start by thanking \"sauerkraut Western\" author Karl May... and the Normalization-era Communists.)', '2002-04-28 04:04:29', 508, 0),
(521, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: idioms', 'Basically to translate them, you have to cross your fingers and hope that\r\n  a) there is an equivalent or near-equivalent idiom in the target language, and\r\n  b) you find it! (Hint: good dictionaries, and good e.g. Czech friends!)\r\n\r\nAnd if you\'re speaking English as a foreign language, you have to \r\n  c) hope you\'re not using an American idiom with a Britisher or vice versa!\r\n\r\n(Hope I\'m not \"carrying coals to Newcastle\"...)', '2002-04-28 04:08:02', 518, 0),
(522, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: transgressive', 'I can\'t tell you much about the transgressive, but I can tell you one piece of good news:\r\n\r\nYou can use Czech in real life for years and years, you can work professionally as a translator for years and years, and NEVER HAVE TO USE IT.\r\n\r\nSo my recommendation is that you focus on something less frustrating (unless you like reading pre-1960s literature).\r\n\r\nBTW transgressives have different forms for masculine, feminine, and neuter, because some grammatician thought it would be a good idea, or that it reflected something in people\'s actual speech. In the end, though... the whole transgressive dropped out of people\'s actual speech... and everyday writing.', '2002-04-28 04:12:23', 504, 0),
(523, 13, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Verb questions (again)', '1. What verbs ending in -ět are in the -je category? In http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/reference/verbs/categories.htm, it says that there the -je catgeory includes some verbs ending in -ět. But what are those verbs?\r\n\r\n2. Does a -řít ending guarantee inclusion in the -e category? All the -ít verbs I know of that are in the -e category end in -řít. Is this a good rule? And are there any -řít verbs that are *not* in the -e category?\r\n\r\n3. Is vzít the only verb that ends in -ít and that is in the -me category? It\'s the only one I know of. If it is the only one, isn\'t it just an irregular verb?\r\n\r\n4. Is there any rule that verbs ending in -íst tend to follow? I know of číst, příst, and jíst -- they all seem to do their own thing. Are there any other -íst verbs and if so, how do they behave?\r\n\r\n5. Is snít the only -ít verb that goes ít, í, il? If so, is it just an iregular verb?\r\n\r\n6. How does one conjugate sníst and páchat?', '2002-04-28 08:53:26', 0, 0),
(524, 14, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Food and Culture', 'Food plays an important role in Czech (as in any) culture. Bread was very important traditionally with much lore associated with it but that has all but disappeared and people know it from literature only. \r\n\r\nCzech food could be (and has been) described as bland but in fact it doesn\'t have to be. Some of the traditional recipes use lots of spices - but they only add flavor and almost never make the food hot. But most Czech cooking is pretty bland and that\'s how Czechs like it. Fat and high in cholesterol is considered good. Moreover most Czechs think that Czech food is great and everybody who tries it will fall in love with it and will never want anything else. And a lot (but not all) of foreigners like it or learn to like it. Because after the initial shock it isn\'t really so bad. But it IS NOT the best in the world. Just something we\'re used to.\r\n\r\nThere are lots of meals that are unique to Czech cuisine in their details or the way they are prepared but the only one I can think of as being exclusive to it is \'knedlík\' which is usually translated as dumpling but no dumplings I\'ve seen anywhere else are like that. There are many varieties of knedlík from plain dough or potato dough to ones filled with anything from fruit or jam to saussages to lard. They also come in a variety of shapes from sliced bread-like loaves to balls (small or large).  You can get a recipe or even buy the ingredients from http://www.knedlik.com.', '2002-04-28 12:34:34', 512, 0),
(525, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Zilla', 'I just realized it could be íla which also means vein. But that\'s just the closest Czech word that could also be a name today - he might have made more substantial changes. Or it might have been some German name (common for Czechs) that is hard to recognize.', '2002-04-28 12:38:26', 505, 0),
(526, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'A few answers', 'Hi, \r\n\r\nI\'m a poor grammarian, so I can only answer a couple of your questions.\r\n\r\nBy the way, Fronek gives FORTY-FIVE classes of verbs, though most differ only in minor details. So don\'t feel depressed that they seem a bit confusing. They are... but nobody\'s gonna kill you for getting it wrong by a letter or so at the start.\r\n\r\n3. I\'m not sure what you mean by the \"-me\" category, but certainly: sníme, jíme...\r\n\r\n5. No; \"Znárodnit\" (to rob from everyone and give to the State) is another example of this type.\r\n\r\n6. Sníst is like jíst:\r\nsním         sníme (snězme!)\r\nsní (sněz!) sníte (snězte!)\r\nsní          snědí\r\n\r\n...and snědl, snězený. Not sure if there\'s a substantive (like \"jedení\" for \"jíst\").\r\n\r\nPáchat is listed in Fronek\'s Really Big Dictionary and Car Wash as having two patterns, and he should know, but keep in mind that the -ám pattern is practically AFAIK the only one ever used:\r\n\r\npáu/páchám             páeme/pácháme (páchejme!)\r\npáe/páchá (páchej!)  páete/pácháte (páchejte!)\r\npáe/páchá              páou/páchají\r\n\r\npáchal, pachaný, pachání', '2002-04-29 03:08:02', 523, 0),
(527, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Food and Culture', 'Perhaps as a vegetarian, Dominik forgot to mention the context in which knedlík is usually found: \"vepřo-knedlo-zelo,\" which is pork, [let\'s say] dumplings, and sauerkraut. Not sure on the order of the 3 words--not sure the Czechs are sure.\r\n\r\nThere is one kind of bread that is still a major part of the culture: \"liquid bread,\" i.e. beer. Beer is for old men, young men, old ladies, college girls, everyone; nearly everyone drinks at least some, and there is no lack of beer alcoholics. (They are especially visible in rural areas, though I don\'t feel competent to say the alcoholism rate is higher there.)', '2002-04-29 03:15:28', 512, 0),
(528, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: what tourist are Czechs?', '...unless that tourist trap is named \"Croatia.\" That is, I\'m under the impression that prices have gone up considerably there in recent years, but Czechs keep on visiting in droves all the same.\r\n\r\nErik', '2002-04-29 03:16:32', 516, 0),
(529, 13, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: A few answers', '3. By the -me pattern, I mean verbs that go mu, me, me, meme, mete, mou; examples include pojmout, vujmout, and přijmout. Notice that vzít, bizarrely, follows this pattern: vezmu, vezme, vezme, etc. My question is whether there are any other -ít ending verbs that, like vzít, follow this -me pattern.\r\n\r\n5. Thanks.\r\n\r\n6. Thanks.', '2002-04-29 05:57:02', 526, 0),
(530, 14, 'Antonio', 'toncxjo@hotmail.com', 'Re: Brno', 'Your message is very interesting. I wish to know why you say Brno is a better town to listen to and learn the Czech language.', '2002-04-29 17:56:36', 517, 0),
(531, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: A few answers', '3. I\'m not sure, but considering that Fronek grants it the status of a pattern on his verb table (whereas he tends to handle single-case exceptions through notes by their dictionary entries), it would seem that not vzít is not alone.\r\n\r\nThe only verb I can think of that follow this pattern can hardly be counted: převzít.\r\n\r\nErik', '2002-04-29 23:33:51', 529, 0),
(532, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Brno', 'That one\'s easy; perhaps Pavel felt it was too obvious to mention :-).\r\n\r\nPrague is renowned... or notorious... as a city where you can rent an apartment, buy food, wash your clothes, go out to eat, find as many friends as you want, deal with a certain percent of the bureaucrats, and so on without speaking a word of Czech. In Brno, you can\'t.\r\n\r\nFurthermore, Brno Czechs are more desirous to / WILLING to converse with you in Czech. I\'ve been living here for eight years and work profesionally as a translator, and there are still Praguers who, because of their good English, de facto refuse to speak with me in Czech (i.e. they don\'t say it outright, but through their actions). That\'s the willing part, the desirous part is that they feel less of a burning urgency to milk you for every free tidbit of English they can get (sorry, Praguers).\r\n\r\nErik', '2002-04-29 23:42:33', 530, 0),
(533, 14, 'Pavel tébl', 'pavel.stebl@seznam.cz', 'Re: Brno', 'Thanks, Eric, for putting it so well. Many of my English speaking colleagues--I am a practising English teacher--share in this experience of bigger exposure to Czech culture, or its Brno variant.', '2002-04-30 02:26:41', 532, 0),
(534, 14, 'Pavel tébl', 'pavel.stebl@seznam.cz', 'Brno II', 'Brno is definitely not in for tourists insofar as it cannot match the architectural splendour of Prague, Telc or Kutna Hora. Still, it does have a lot to offer to \r\npeople interested in history and it just has an inimitable flavour to it once you \r\nget in touch with the people and open up to the difference. I know a couple of expats who got caught by its \r\nleasurely lifestyle and homegrown philosophy. As a city of six universities, it has the highest density\r\nof student population in the Czech Republic, and as a result, lots of clubs, \r\ncinemas, restaurants are available at Czech prices. In other words, it\'s not \r\na classic tourist trap like other \'hot\' Czech cities.\r\n\r\nIf you ever come this way, I\'ll be happy to exchange some notes as I\'ve \r\nnow made Brno my home and resisted offers to trade it for Prague.', '2002-04-30 02:44:36', 517, 0),
(535, 13, 'Cole Mitchell', 'cole_mitchell@hotmail.com', 'Re: A few answers', 'Ok, well thanks for the effort anyway. Hopefully, this summer I\'ll be able to get a copy of Fronek\'s book.', '2002-04-30 03:22:10', 531, 0),
(536, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Brno II', '> I know a couple of expats who got caught by its leasurely lifestyle and *homegrown* philosophy. \r\n\r\nLook up the word \"homegrown\" and its common contexts on Google and you might think twice about using it... unless you wanted to express something very subtle and/or subtly. There is na tom a certain appropriateness in that good wine country is also, um...\r\n\r\nBTW Brno is not *entirely* devoid of \"valuable\" (to say it in Czenglish) architecture. From the periods people associate with sightseeing (i.e. Baroque etc.) there\'s the castle and the cathedral (pílberk and Petrov). But the fine functionalist work should hardly be ignored -- like the Tugendhat village by Mies van de Rohe, \"Europe\'s Frank Lloyd Wright,\" and to a certain degree like the Komerční banka building.\r\n\r\nAs for young folks\' nightlife -- I can confirm Pavel\'s statement. It can be harder to find than in Prague until you get in with the Czechs... but then that\'s kind of the point.\r\n\r\nErik-with-a-k', '2002-05-01 04:41:25', 534, 0),
(537, 13, 'Arick McNiel', 'mcniela@pom-emh1.army.mil', 'HELP - Computer Dictionary', 'Hello All,\r\n\r\nI am wondering if anyone knows of a Czech / English electronic dictionary (or any programs) that run on DOS or WindowsCE 2.0\r\n\r\nThanks.  Please respond to my email address.\r\n\r\nArick', '2002-05-01 08:12:49', 0, 1),
(538, 14, 'Chris', 'chrisgarbett@blueyonder.co.uk', 'Re: Brno II', 'My wife and I are considering a visit to Brno maybe later this year.  We have visited Prague (been there, done that!) about five times and have many friends there, now.  We have also been to Čáslav and Kutná Hora, Ostrava, Havířov and have visited parts of Slovakia.  We have been trying to learn Czech for about ř years, but here in England it is not easy without opportunities to speak to Czechs.\r\n\r\nWe are interested in seeing the architecture of Brno and the caves north of the city.  Do tours run there year round or only for the summer?\r\n\r\nWhat sights would you recommend?', '2002-05-10 15:44:29', 536, 0),
(539, 13, 'Chris', 'chrisgarbett@blueyonder.co.uk', 'Dát se', 'Can someone explain just what is the meaning of dát se?  and how does it differ from dát si?  What is its relationship to dát?\r\n\r\nPlease help me.....', '2002-05-11 16:27:01', 0, 0),
(540, 13, 'gloria mcmillanova', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Glo\'s vety April 19, 2002', 'Dekuji!', '2002-05-12 03:46:06', 520, 0),
(541, 13, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: Glo\'s vety April 19, 2002', 'What are AFAIR and AFAIK?', '2002-05-12 03:56:42', 520, 0),
(542, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Dát se', 'This word is hard to interpret without context. Here are a few possibilities:\r\n\r\n* dát se (někomu) - to give oneself (into) someone\r\n* dát se na něco - to take up (join a life course) e.g. dát se na cigarety (to take up a smoking)\r\n* dát se do něčeho - to start off (doing something) e.g. dát se do práce (start working)\r\n\r\n* dát s<b>i</b> almost always means to have (as when ordering food - dám si pivo - I\'ll have a beer)\r\n\r\nBoth are related to dát formally and historically but are really separate words with their own meanings. Analytically they mean: dát si (to give to oneself) and dát se (to give oneself).', '2002-05-12 12:52:48', 539, 0),
(543, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Announcing new essays on Czech', 'Ahoj,\r\n\r\nErik Piper offered to post some of his essays on Czech on Bohemica.com. You can read the first installment on <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/czechessays_top.htm>Czech Essays</a>\r\n\r\nI also added a new one by me which appeared in the Prague Post earlier this year.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2002-05-12 12:58:43', 0, 0),
(544, 14, 'Colby', 'array@ii.net', 'Work in CZ', 'hi.. I\'m planning to travel to Czech sometime in the next year or two, and I\'m wondering if anyone can tell me what sorts of temporary (a few months) work are popular for foreigners, or are easy for us to get, and which leave adequate time to see & experience the country. My Czech language is far from conversational at this point - just starting to make simple sentences, with no grasp of grammar - but I have a number of Czech friends here in Australia who are helping me learn.\r\n\r\nthanks\r\ncau, C.', '2002-05-12 19:12:14', 0, 1),
(545, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Glo\'s vety April 19, 2002', 'AFAIK = \"as far as I know\"<br>\r\nAFAIR could be \"as far as I remember\"<br><br>\r\nFor explanation of other acronyms you can look <a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/SouthBeach/Lagoon/9819/acronyms.html\">here</a>.', '2002-05-13 17:44:17', 541, 0),
(546, 13, 'Peter', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'gramatical prefixes and looking up in dictionaries', 'When trying to translate czech to english, I often get stuck, not being able to find words in dictionary because of gramatical prefixes.  \"Ne\",  \"ně\", and perfective \"po\"/\"u\" I can guess, but last week I was really stumped by \"superlative\" nej..., I couldn\'t work out what nejpůvabněji meant because I tried looking up under \"nejp...\" and \"jp...\" not \"puv...\".\r\n\r\nDoes anyone know of a list somewhere of all the possible gramatical prefixes, Are there other common ones apart from ne, ně, po, u, nej_\r\n', '2002-05-14 03:51:16', 0, 1),
(547, 14, 'Cassandra', 'cassandrasm@subaru.zzn.com', 'just wondering', 'I found out recently that in romainea(sorry don\'t know how to spell it) people refer to one another not by first names but by middle names do they do this in the czech republic also? also how closely related is czech culture and language to polish language and culture?', '2002-05-15 09:47:01', 0, 1),
(548, 14, 'Lynn', 'boots_and_jeans@yahoo.com', 'Czech vs. American \'Personal Space\'', 'I have a question about Czechs and their \'personal space\'. I read that \"Czech private space is smaller but its boundary is firmer. This is especially obvious when compared with Americans who require larger personal space but are much more willing to let people get into it.\" Could any of you Czechs out there give me your opinion of whether you think it is ok for a woman to give a Czech man a hug when greeting and departing if we are just friends. Would this be weird for him? Thanks for any help! ', '2002-05-17 07:49:50', 0, 1),
(549, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: gramatical prefixes and looking up in dictionaries', 'Here\'s a very quick the-Czech-Telecom-clock-is-ticking response:\r\n\r\n-- You can find a very detailed Czech discussion of prefixes and of \"slovotvorba\" in general in the back of the Slovnik spisovné četiny pro kolu a veřejnost (Academia, Prague 1998).\r\n\r\n-- Almost every Czech preposition has a related prefix: do/do-, od/od-, za/za-, etc.\r\n\r\n-- Some prefixes come in two variants, one with a short vowel, and one with a long vowel: vy/vý-, za-/zá-, etc. Of course this is not so important when using a dictionary... I\'m not sure why the variants exist, but I can say that the long-vowel variants are rarer.\r\n\r\n-- Prefixes often make the difference between a perfective verb and an imperfective one.\r\n\r\nCheers\r\nErik', '2002-05-23 22:40:15', 546, 1),
(550, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Brno II', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nI\'d recommend the Tugendhat Villa, as well as, of course, the Jakubské church and the Petrov Cathedral, as well as the pilberk castle. If you like morbid stuff, the Kapucíné have a crypt in the center of town. To the south there is the Lednice-Valtice estate complex (two mansions, a beautiful park, an aquarium, and more).\r\n\r\nYou should be warned that for the caves you generally need to buy your ticket in advance -- a week might be OK, two weeks always has been for me. Also note that there is no direct public transport there; you will have to go by train or bus to Blansko and walk about 10 km or take a bus. I\'m afraid I don\'t know about their season. I do know they were open in early May. The hours should still be OK in September if they work like most Czech tourist sites.\r\n\r\nCheers\r\nErik', '2002-05-23 22:46:27', 538, 0),
(551, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: just wondering', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nFirst names are the norm here. Czech and Polish are quite similar in their Slavic roots, and both languages are somewhat understandable (but also silly-, obscene-, or archaic-sounding) to speakers of the other language. They share a Slavic culture and a Communist \"heritage\" and have both been influenced by the (ethnically) Germanic cultural space, but Czech was more influenced by Austria-Hungary and Poland by Germany and ex-Prussia. Poland is AFAIK more Catholic and less atheist. Both consider themselves to be \"Central Europe\" and closer at heart to the West than to the East.\r\n\r\nHope that helps!\r\n\r\nErik', '2002-05-23 22:51:17', 547, 0),
(552, 14, 'Tommie', '', 'Birthday greetings', 'hello! can someone please tell me an appropriate Czech birthday greeting for a guy to give to a female Czech friend. I mean a written greeting, for a card, if it is different from verbal greetings.\r\n\r\nthank you\r\nTom =)', '2002-05-27 20:43:15', 0, 0),
(553, 14, 'Scott', '', 'Re: just wondering', 'Also, very seldom do you hear a native born Czech with a middle name.  It is not common for them.  ', '2002-06-02 11:37:23', 551, 0),
(554, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Meaning of \"Těí mě.\"', 'What is the meaning of this phrase when you are introducing yourself or someone else?\r\nThank you,\r\nScott', '2002-06-03 10:56:56', 0, 0),
(555, 13, 'scott', '', 'Re: Meaning of', 'Sorry, \r\nNevermind, I found it in a dictionary.  Thank you anyway.', '2002-06-06 03:42:32', 554, 0),
(556, 14, 'allison', 'alucas9@pacbell.net', 'traveling with a visa', 'hi there :) \r\n\r\nis it possible to travel easily in and out of the czech republic w/ a visa, or is wise no to do so?\r\n\r\nthanks,\r\nallison.\r\n\r\nbtw. this is such a great site; it\'s extremely informative....thank you ;)', '2002-06-09 11:32:32', 0, 1),
(557, 14, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: traveling with a visa', 'Depends where you\'re from. Most English speaking countries do not need a visa for stays under 90 days and then it\'s just a matter of stepping across the border and back. The exception is Australia and I\'m not sure about South Africa. You can find more info on this on http://www.czech.cz. ', '2002-06-12 18:24:17', 556, 0),
(558, 14, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Birthday greetings', 'The simplest and most universal greeting is:\r\n\r\n\"Vechno nejlepí k narozeninám.\" (All the best for your birthday.) You could also add \"Hodně těstí a zdraví\" (Lots of luck and health).', '2002-06-12 18:27:39', 552, 0),
(559, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: pronounciation of y & i', 'Sorry to disagree,\r\n\r\n\"i\" in \"ni\" (\"ti\",\"di\") is slightly different from \"y\" in \"ny\" (\"ty\",\"dy\"). It is due to (lack of) palatalization of previous consonant.', '2002-06-20 18:19:41', 489, 0),
(560, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: transgressive', 'The transgressives (přechodníky) are rare, but still living (particularly present transgr.).\r\n\r\nSome examples:\r\n\r\nPřechodník přítomný:\r\nsoudě (m), soudíc (f,n), soudíce (pl) from soudit (oni soudí):\r\n\r\nSoudě (soudíc) z vaeho výrazu, mám pravdu. = Z vaeho výrazu soudím, e mám pravdu. (nebo Z vaeho výrazu lze soudit, e ...)\r\n\r\nPřechodník minulý:\r\nminuv (m), minuvi (f,n), minuve (pl) from minout (on minul).\r\n\r\nMinuvi cíl kulka se zaryla do stěny. = Poté co minula cíl, kulka se ....\r\n\r\n\r\n(Don\'t confuse it with adjectives: soudící, minuví.)\r\n', '2002-06-20 20:16:47', 522, 0),
(561, 13, 'Joshua D. Hyrman', 'qaswix@hotmail.com', 'Does Hyrman Mean Anything?', '  My ancestors came to America from Czechoslovakia and I have been doing some research into my history. I am wondering if the family name Hyrman has any meaning in the Czech or Slovak language (as the Hebrew names did). Perhaps it describes the family profession.\r\n  I have also considered the possibility that the name was corrupted or changed, maybe a letter added or removed somewhere.\r\n  I am also wondering if Czech is a root language or if it is taken from another language (eg., Latin is a root language, but English is derived from German).\r\n\r\n  Thank you for your time and help.', '2002-06-24 23:35:53', 0, 1),
(562, 13, 'Beth', 'Alabeff@aol.com', 'Quote Translation Please!', 'NAPAM&#1025;T V&#376;RAVY C-(WITH ~ ABOVE IT)ESKE OBCE SOKOLSKÉ SUCHARDA', '2002-06-25 04:15:58', 0, 0),
(563, 14, 'Elena', 'tonkich.export@tescoma.com', 'Re: Czech vs. American \'Personal Space\'', 'Hallo!\r\n\r\nI suppose, you\'re write. I\'m not neither american no czech, but I\'d the same problem from the very beginning. In generaly, they\'re conservative and only shake hands while greeting and departing. Only if you are closer friend it will be taken normaly. Or in the companies with foreigners - it\'ll be OK for everybody if you\'re stranger. But don\'t be affraid, woman are more emotional and can afford it :-) For some czechs even kisses are normal (but only for some!)  \r\nElena', '2002-07-09 16:34:21', 548, 0),
(564, 13, 'Michael', 'm.srba@centrum.cz', 'Re: Meaning of', 'Literally it means that it is giving you joy: in other words \"it\'s a pleasure (to meet you)\" (to put it in more correct English)\r\n\r\nm', '2002-07-10 22:18:17', 554, 0),
(565, 13, 'Michael', 'm.srba@centrum.cz', 'Re: Does Hyrman Mean Anything?', 'First of all it seems to be (by origin) a German name - probably Czech-ized. (By German I mean either originating from the German minorities, which Czechoslovakia had a considerable share) but could also be derived from Yiddish.\r\n\r\nThe (ethymological) origin could be multifold, not knowing the background of your ancestors. The closest, however, I could currently imagine is from the Germanic word Her(r)man(n). Where is the old word for \"Heer\" (i.e. army). perhaps someone, who served in the armies of Frederick the Great or Napoleon, and got the nickname \"the one who served as a warrior\". Perhaps quite a romanticised theory, but who knows...\r\nFrom Her(r)mann it could have been misspelled in the course of time into Hyrman (which would phonetically still sound like a distant \"Hermann\")\r\n\r\nWith names it\'s sometimes very hard to fathom the exact depth of a name\'s meaning, but perhaps this helps slightly into a certain direction\r\n\r\nmichael ', '2002-07-11 01:13:05', 561, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(566, 13, 'Michael S', 'm.srba@centrum.cz', 'Re: Where can I hear Czech?', 'If you have free access to internet, you could also op for listening to the on-line broadcastings by teh Czech Radio (Česky Rozhlas).\r\nMaybe in teh beginning you might be lost, but bit by bit you should be able to catch more and more words. A matter to get used to it.\r\n\r\nmichael', '2002-07-11 01:22:06', 193, 0),
(567, 13, 'Josie', 'grandmotherpeace@cs.com', 'grandmother', 'I would appreciate knowing the word for \"grandmother\" in Czechoslovakian. My ancestors are from Czechoslovakia and I want to have my grandson call me the Czechoslovakian word for grandmother.  Thanks, Josie', '2002-07-15 07:18:28', 0, 1),
(568, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Quote Translation Please!', 'I think it reads \"Na pamě výpravy České obce sokolské Sucharda.\" and it means \"To the memory of the Czech Sokol movement expedition [signed] Sucharda.\" The Sokol movement was akin to YMCA but not aimed at exclusively at youths or boys. If you tell us more about where it\'s from somebody may have a little more of an idea what this refers to.', '2002-07-16 00:28:02', 562, 0),
(569, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Does Hyrman Mean Anything?', 'Another Czech name Hyrman could be derived from is Heřman. I don\'t know much about it\'s etymology off hand. It could be a Czechified form of the German Hermann or it could be derived from the Czech word for camomile which is \'heřmánek\'.', '2002-07-16 00:31:02', 561, 0),
(570, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: grandmother', 'Strangely enough, this is quite a tall order. First, there is no such language as Czechoslovakian (although in the 1930s some people were trying to claim there was). There is Czech (spoken in the Czech Republic) and Slovak (spoken in Slovakia). They are fairly similar but NOT the same. And as it happens the word for gandmother is different in both of them. The Slovak word is \'stará mama\' and the Czech is \'babička\'. (see Czech ONline for pronunciation) In Czech, however, it is further complicated by the need to change the word ending when talking to someone. Your grandson should then address you \'babičko\' and use \'babička\' only when talking about you. A short version \'babi\' [bah-bee] is even more common though and it should be easy enough for him to learn too.', '2002-07-16 00:41:55', 567, 0),
(571, 17, 'Michael', 'mterpstra@centrum.cz', 'Jakto e nikdo nemá zájem?', 'Nedávno jsem objevil tuto stránku (asi trochu pozdě...), ale jakto e letos nikdo nenechal aspon malou zprávičku? \r\nSám iji u 34 let v Nizozemí (jsem ale o pár roku starí), a na \"staré kolena\" jsem se konečně dal do studia Bohemistiky. Dokonce moje milá partnerka se teď pilně učí celý měsíc četinu v Praze. Čekáme toti rodinu, take a se narodí nae dítě budeme mít monost spolu mluvit česky.  :-)) \r\n\r\nDoufám e občas se někdo ozve aby jsme se mohli bavit o českým jazyce nebo jiné aspekty \"českého\" ivota.\r\n\r\nS pozdravem / čauvec\r\nmichal\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n', '2002-07-19 02:49:20', 0, 1),
(572, 16, 'Michael', 'mterpstra@centrum.cz', 'Re: Jak učit česká slovesa?', 'Určitě to hned zkusím a se moje partnerka vrátí z Prahy, kde zrovna ma intenzivní jazykový kurs. Sice bude u mít nějakou základu sloves, ale určitě jetě tento tip přijde vhod..\r\n\r\nmichal (v NL)\r\n', '2002-07-19 03:10:04', 423, 1),
(573, 13, 'Jessica', 'biddybear1@hotmail.com', 'How Do You Pronounce My Ancestor\'s Name?', 'My ancestor\'s last name is Koczelu from Bohemia in the 1700\'s. How do you pronounce his name? I don\'t know if that is the original spelling since it came from the archive in Graz. My ancestor was born in a little town called Plav but later he moved to Austria. I don\'t know if there were any accent marks. If you can pronounce my ancestor\'s name, please spell it phonetically for me.\r\n\r\nThanks\r\nJessica', '2002-07-20 08:52:54', 0, 1),
(574, 13, 'Michael', '', 'Re: How Do You Pronounce My Ancestor\'s Name?', 'Hi Jessica,\r\n\r\nThe spelling is slightly changed and originaly it would have been \"Koeluh\" (pronounced: Ko-zhe-looh). The English translation is \"Tanner\" \r\nTalking of the 1700`s, a Bohemian composer called Leopold Antonín Koeluh (1747-1818) who became a famous composer and pianist at the imperial court in Vienna. In fact he succeeded W.A. Mozart. His works vary from Cantatas, piano concertos, symphonies and arias.\r\n\r\nMichael', '2002-07-20 12:51:54', 573, 0),
(575, 13, 'Michael', '', 'Re: HELP - Computer Dictionary', 'Arick,\r\n\r\nThere are electronic dictionaries, but personally i am using an on-line dictionary (would that be an option for you too?)\r\n\r\ntry \r\nhttp://www.slovnik.cz/bin/ecd\r\n\r\nrgds\r\nmichael', '2002-07-20 12:56:45', 537, 0),
(576, 13, 'Michael', '', 'Re: pronunciation help, prosim!', 'Hodně těstí\r\n\r\npronounced: hod-nye shtye-styee  [approximately...]\r\n\r\nmichael', '2002-07-20 13:16:27', 171, 0),
(577, 13, 'PeterC', 'pc@lancaster-pc.demon.co.uk', 'Re: Where can I hear Czech?', 'Michael\r\n\r\nI\'d appreciate it if you could give the web address of on-line broadcaste by cesky rozhlas.\r\n\r\nDekuju\r\n\r\nPC', '2002-07-31 01:14:19', 566, 1),
(578, 13, 'Cherie', 'kticf@africa.com', 'Czech and Russian', 'Are the Czech and Russian languages similar when spoken?  Would I be able to understand a little Russian if I was able to speak Czech,  would being able to speak Czech made learning Russian easier?  ', '2002-07-31 14:21:25', 0, 0),
(579, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Czech and Russian', 'I`m a native russian speaker, but now i can speak czech very well, and.. I think the similarity is, but if you don`t speak czech VERY GOOD, you won`t understand russian. I have to say, that there`re a lot of words in both of two languages, but the meanings of these words are opposite. For example, \"rodina\" means \"family\" in czech, but \"native country\" in russian and so on. Hm, but generally, you can understand russian, but i guess, it`ll be very difficult.', '2002-07-31 17:57:10', 578, 1),
(580, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: gramatical prefixes and looking up in dictionaries', 'If you want, we can correspond, and I will answer all your questions about czech language. Sometimes, I need a english netive speaker to control my translation. So, we can help each other :)', '2002-07-31 18:00:26', 549, 1),
(581, 13, 'Mirka', 'subluster@mail.ru', 'potřebuji pomoc', 'Chtěl bych vědět, jak se sklonuje podstatná jména stůl a sůl, dík.', '2002-07-31 18:04:17', 0, 1),
(582, 16, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Vítejte v diskusní skupině pro učitele', 'Umím dobře český, ale nejsem ádným učitelem. Občas mám otázky, tykající se výuky četiny. Můu se tázat tady, nebo mám psát do fóruma \"Language forum\"?', '2002-07-31 18:10:41', 421, 1),
(583, 17, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Jakto e nikdo nemá zájem?', 'No, já mám zájem a proto občas nechávám zprávičky nebo otázky v tomto fórumu. Rád se pobavím o českém jazyce :)', '2002-07-31 22:03:30', 571, 1),
(584, 17, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'jak bude správně...?', 'Dominiku, můu se zeptat, proč jste napsal \"byl industrialní město\"? Ja bych pouil sedmý pád a řekl bych \"byl industrialním městem\". Jaký je rozdíl?', '2002-07-31 22:10:17', 447, 1),
(585, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: potřebuji pomoc', 'Stůl (masc.)\r\n\r\nSg. stůl,stolu,stolu,stůl,stole,stolu,stolem\r\nPl. stoly,stolů,stolům,stoly,stoly,stolech,stoly\r\n\r\nSůl (fem.)\r\n\r\nSg. sůl,soli,soli,sůl,soli,soli,solí\r\nPl. soli,solí,solím,soli,soli,solích,solemi', '2002-08-01 14:37:11', 581, 0),
(586, 16, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Vítejte v diskusní skupině pro učitele', 'Latinská slova (neutra) zakončená na -um se skloňují jako \"slovo\", ale koncovky se připojují ke kmeni fór-, tedy \"do fóra\".', '2002-08-01 14:55:35', 582, 0),
(587, 16, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Sklonování slov z latiny.', 'Patří sem latinská slova: centrum, muzeum? Můete mi tu napsat sklonování jednoho z těch slov. Ja jsem to zkusil udělat sam, ale měl jsem problém s mnoným číslem. Dík.', '2002-08-01 16:31:09', 586, 1),
(588, 17, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: jak bude správně...?', 'Pravidlo lze nejlépe vysvětlit na příkladech:\r\n\r\n1. Tygr je elma. (1. pád)\r\n2. Bratr je učitelem. (7. pád)\r\n\r\nTygr je a vdy bude elma svou podstatou, zatímco \"být učitelem\" je vlastnost získaná a (potenciálně) dočasná. Bohuel, některé sporné případy vyadují hlubí analýzu a \"filosofický\" pohled na věc, jazykový cit zde nepomáhá.\r\n\r\nV případě Liberce bych pouil:\r\n\r\nLiberec je (malé, velké, hezké atd.) město.\r\nLiberec byl industriálním (česky lépe průmyslovým) městem. (dnes je správním, universitním atd. střediskem kraje)\r\nLiberec byl německým městem (kdy a v jakém smyslu?). (dnes je českým městem, za 50 let bude moná cikánským městem, kdo ví?)\r\n\r\n', '2002-08-01 16:35:45', 584, 0),
(589, 16, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Sklonování slov z latiny.', 'Centrum (před -um je souhláska):\r\n\r\nSg. centrum,centra,centru,centrum,centrum,centru,centrem\r\nPl. centra,center(vloené \"e\"),centrům,centra,centra,centrech,centry\r\n\r\nMuzeum (před -um je samohláska):\r\n\r\nSg. muzeum,muzea,muzeu,muzeum,muzeum,muzeu,muzeem\r\nPl. muzea,muzeí,muzeím,muzea,muzea,muzeích,muzei (pl. jako pole)\r\n', '2002-08-01 16:53:05', 587, 0),
(590, 17, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'gramatika a mluvnice', 'Zajímá mě, jaký je rozdíl mezi těmi dvěma slovy: gramatika a mluvnice. Ja se domnívám, e jde jen o cizí slovo \"gramatika\". A \"mluvnice\" - původní české slovo. Nemýlím se náhodou? ', '2002-08-01 17:35:16', 0, 1),
(591, 17, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'gramatika a mluvnice', 'Zajímá mě, jaký je rozdíl mezi těmi dvěma slovy: gramatika a mluvnice. Ja se domnívám, e jde jen o cizí slovo \"gramatika\". A \"mluvnice\" - původní české slovo. Nemýlím se náhodou? ', '2002-08-01 17:42:34', 0, 1),
(592, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Stůl - oprava.', '\r\nStůl - 6. pád jed. č.: (na) stole\r\n\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\r\nObecně 6. pád jed. č. slov typu \"hrad\" má koncovku\r\n\r\n             -ě (-e)   nebo   -u\r\n\r\nnapř.: hradě i hradu, dubě i dubu\r\n\r\nSlova zakončená na -l, -s, -z mají zpravidla koncovku -e\r\n\r\nnapř.: na stole, v dole, na voze, v lese;  ale: na plese i plesu;\r\n\r\nSlova zakončená na -k, -g, -h, -ch, -r mají koncovku -u\r\n\r\nnapř.: v lesíku, o dialogu, na břehu, o hrachu, v míru, na severu;\r\n       ale: v jazyku i jazyce, na papíru i papíře;\r\n  ', '2002-08-02 14:22:58', 585, 0),
(593, 14, 'Peter', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'I.P.Pavlova', 'Who was I.P.Pavlova, who seems to have so many streets named after him/her in the Czech republic?', '2002-08-03 04:34:57', 0, 0),
(594, 13, 'Lughaidh', 'gaelbhach@minitel.net', 'Translation', 'Ahoj !\r\ni\'d like to translate this sentence please, can u help me?\r\n\r\n\"Loving without being loved back (or getting love back?) is such a torture, that even the gods didn\'t impose it (this torture) on the damned ones of the abysses (of hell)\"\r\n\r\nSorry if my english isn\'t fine, it\'s not my mothertongue !\r\n\r\nThanx a lot in advance\r\n\r\nLughaidh', '2002-08-03 05:00:53', 0, 1),
(595, 13, 'Graham', 'grahamlaming@hotmail.com', 'Help please with a Czech greeting ....', 'Ahoj.\r\n\r\nCan someone please tell me how to write a \'GET WELL\' card in Czech? My greeting is.....\r\n\r\n\"We both wish you a very quick recovery from your operation. May good health return soon for you.\"\r\n\r\nThat is it! Thanks in anticipation of your help!\r\nGraham', '2002-08-03 16:37:10', 0, 0),
(596, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Help please with a Czech greeting ....', 'my version of translating of \"We both wish you a very quick recovery from your operation. May good health return soon for you.\"  is:\r\n\"My oba přejeme ti aby ses rychle uzdravil z operace. Doufáme, e se tvé zdráví brze vrátí k tobě.\"\r\n', '2002-08-04 07:15:55', 595, 1),
(597, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Opět potřebuji pomoc', 'Měl jsem problém se sklonováním těchto slov: album, kriterium.', '2002-08-04 07:17:44', 0, 1),
(598, 13, 'Graham', '', 'Re: Help please with a Czech greeting ....', 'Děkuju mockrát, lovermann. Vsechno nejlepsi!\r\n\r\n', '2002-08-04 12:12:47', 596, 0),
(599, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'jak se časuje sloveso \"dít se\"', 'Ví někdo, jak se časuje sloveso \"dít se\". V ivotě jsem slyel jenom dvě formy tohoto slovesa: dít se, a děje se.', '2002-08-04 17:11:40', 0, 1),
(600, 16, 'lovermann', 'loverman@seznam.cz', 'album, kriterium', 'Jak se sklonují podstatná jména kriterium a album, dík', '2002-08-04 17:33:59', 0, 1),
(601, 13, 'lovermann2', '', '', '', '2002-08-04 17:35:15', 0, 0),
(602, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'co znamená slovo \"mermoc\"?', 'Můe mi někdo pomoc přeloit tuhle větu: \" Pokud toti mermomocí chceme, jsme schopni utvořit různá extrémně dlouhá slova tak, aby neodporovala pravidlům české slovotvorby a pravidlům ohýbání, a tvrdit, e jsou to česká slova\".\r\nVechno je mi jasně, kromě slova \"mermoc\".. ', '2002-08-04 17:38:03', 0, 1),
(603, 13, 'Michael', '', 'Re: co znamená slovo', '\"Mermomocí\" znamená e se například něco chce za kadou cenu. (\"at any price\")', '2002-08-04 23:56:50', 602, 0),
(604, 14, 'Michael', '', 'Re: I.P.Pavlova', 'Ivan Petrovich Pavlov was a Russian scientist (1849 - 1936) who won the 1904 Nobel Price for Physiology/Medicine. Perhaps you have heard of the \"Pavlov reaction\" or \"Pavlov reflexes\". As he experimented with dogs on their physiological reflexes when subjected to habitual patterns, this is to the broad masses the famous well known experiment which is associated with his name.\r\n\r\nPS \"Pavlova\" is the genitiv (possesive) form of his name...', '2002-08-05 00:12:18', 593, 0),
(605, 13, 'anon', '', 'why are they called \"hard\" and \"soft\"', 'In some Czech textbooks, whole \"words\" or \"consonants\" are referred to as \"hard\" and \"soft\", but does this refer to the impression upon our ears made by the sounds (an auditory impression), i.e. the subjective impression of the sound, or does the term \"hard\" refer to the \"hardness\" of the physical movements made by the tongue, or lips, or something else i.e. physical facts concerning the \"pronunciation\".\r\n\r\nIn the grammar guide, in discussing the \"hard\"/\"soft\" distinction, Dominik refers us to the section entitle \"Pronunciation\", implying that the etymological origins of \"hard/soft\" might have to do with pronunciation.  ', '2002-08-05 04:48:26', 0, 0),
(606, 13, 'Katie Mac', 'katie@katiemacalister.com', 'Please, oh, please, oh please!', 'I need two words translated for a book I\'m writing, and would be eternally grateful if anyone could tell me how to say \"little warrior\" in Czech. \r\n\r\nAnyone like to give it a shot and keep me from yanking out my hair in frustration? Thanks much!\r\n\r\nKatie', '2002-08-05 20:23:23', 0, 1),
(607, 13, 'Chocapic', 'gaelbhach@minitel.net', 'Is my sentence too difficult to translate ?PLEASE!', 'Ahoj ! \r\ni\'d like to translate this sentence please, can u help me? \r\n\r\n\"Loving without being loved back (or getting love back?) is such a torture, that even the gods didn\'t impose it (this torture) on the damned ones of the abysses (of hell)\" \r\n\r\nSorry if my english isn\'t fine, it\'s not my mothertongue ! \r\n\r\nThanx a lot in advance \r\n\r\nLughaidh/Chocapic', '2002-08-05 22:08:52', 0, 0),
(608, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@mail.ru', 'Re: Please, oh, please, oh please!', 'little warrior is  \"malý vojín\"', '2002-08-06 00:12:36', 606, 1),
(609, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@mail.ru', 'Re: Is my sentence too difficult to translate ?PLEASE!', 'It`s really hard to translate this sentence, but I will try...\r\n\"Milovat a nebýt milováným je takové trapení, e ani bohové nemohli přenést ho na čerty z samého pekla\".\r\n', '2002-08-06 00:29:19', 607, 1),
(610, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@mail.ru', 'Re: co znamená slovo', 'Díky moc. Stačí napsat česky :)', '2002-08-06 00:32:34', 603, 1),
(611, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@mail.ru', 'jak se sklonuje slovo \"bůh\"', 'subj.', '2002-08-06 00:35:48', 0, 1),
(612, 13, 'Katie Mac', 'katie@katiemacalister.com', 'Re: Please, oh, please, oh please!', 'Oh, you\'re an angel, many thanks for translating that for me! \r\n\r\nKatie', '2002-08-06 00:50:42', 608, 0),
(613, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: jak se sklonuje slovo', 'Stejně jako vechna maskulina CůC, tj. boha, bohovi/bohu, boha atd.', '2002-08-06 01:45:22', 611, 0),
(614, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Please, oh, please, oh please!', 'Also depending on context, it might be \'malý válečník\' or \'malý bojovník\'. \'Malý vojín\' is more of a \'small soldier\' or \'small private\'.', '2002-08-06 01:47:36', 606, 0),
(615, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: jak se časuje sloveso', 'V podstatě jiný kontext ne \'děje se\' neexistuje. Ale bylo by to \'děju\' \'děje\' ... \'dějí/dějou\'. Dá se to najít třeba ve slově \'nadít se\' - např. \'ani se nenaděje a přijedeme\' (before you know it, we\'ll be here).', '2002-08-06 01:50:18', 599, 0),
(616, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: gramatika a mluvnice', 'Je mezi nimi také stylistický rozdíl. \'Mluvnice\' je trochu archaické v kontextu \'učím se anglickou mluvnici\', ale můe se objevit v názvu knihy \'Mluvnice četiny\', ale ne \'Moderní česká mluvnice\'. Moná ale pro to mají jiní lidé jiný cit.', '2002-08-06 01:57:04', 591, 0),
(617, 16, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: album, kriterium', 'alba, albu, album, albu, albem, --  kritéria, ..., kritériem\r\n\r\nAle vyskytuje se i \'albumu\' (např. \'dám tu fotku do albumu\'), ale nikdy ne \'kritériumu\'.', '2002-08-06 01:59:19', 600, 0),
(618, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Sloveso \"dít se\"', 'Běné je i mno. číslo  \"dějí se\" (hovor. \"dějou se\")\r\na minulý čas  \"dělo se\" i \"dálo se\", \"děly se\" i \"dály se\" (věci)\r\na rozkazovací způsob  \"děj se\".\r\n\r\nSloveso odvozené \"nadít se\" má význam ve vech osobách (pozor na příč. min.):\r\n\r\nnaději se (hovor. naděju se),naděje se, ... , nadějí se (hovor. nadějou se)\r\nnadál (-a, -o) jsem se, ...\r\nrozkaz. zp. naděj se, nadějme se, nadějte se\r\n\r\n', '2002-08-06 20:08:12', 615, 0),
(619, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Album, kritérium.', '', '2002-08-06 20:16:13', 597, 0),
(620, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Album, kritérium.', 'Zajímavějí je mnoné číslo:\r\n\r\nalba,alb,albům,alba,alba,albech,alby\r\n\r\nkritéria,kritérií,kritériím,kritéria,kritéria,kritériích,kritérii\r\n', '2002-08-06 20:20:36', 597, 0),
(621, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Meanings of \"u\"--NOTE: your text box won\'t expand in Netscape', '', '2002-08-06 22:34:22', 0, 1),
(622, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'past participale of \"to eat\"', 'Could someone point me to a hcart conjugating \"jist\"\r\n(to eat)?\r\n\r\nDiky,\r\n\r\nGlo\r\n\r\nBTW, on my screens \"send post\" is so small as \r\nto be almost unreadable.  Consider increasing\r\nyour font size.\r\nText box works in IE.\r\n\r\n', '2002-08-07 00:10:08', 0, 0),
(623, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@mail.ru', 'Re: past participale of', 'http://jazyk.prag.ru/gram/gram.konj.jist.html\r\nMy site is in russian, but czech text is in czech language :))\r\nSet windows-1251 encoding and you will see czech letters.\r\nIf you have another verbs to conjigate, vizit \r\nhttp://jazyk.prag.ru/gram.konj.html', '2002-08-08 23:28:09', 622, 1),
(624, 13, 'glo mcmillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'uses of \"u\"', 'I hear people saying \"u\"...\"u\"  all the time in Czech when I\r\nlisten to them speak.   It ounds as frequent as \"tak\", so what\r\ndoes it mean when used--as it seems to be--as a particle or an\r\nintrojection?\r\n\r\nDekuji,\r\n\r\nGloria\r\n\r\n\r\nI still had trouble using Netscape--no text box.', '2002-08-10 19:36:10', 0, 1),
(625, 13, 'Glo McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Re: past participale of', 'Dekuji!  That helps.', '2002-08-11 05:19:48', 623, 0),
(626, 13, 'Vladimír', '', '\"U\" = \"ji\" (already).', '\"Ji\" (in colloquial Czech \"u\") means \"already\" (\"yet\" in questions).\r\n\r\nE.G.\r\n\r\nji - already\r\nji - yet\r\nji (kdy) - as early as\r\n\r\nji byl - had already been\r\nji zakoupil - has already purchased\r\nji přeplněný - already crowded\r\nji uplynul týden - week is already gone\r\nji dlouhou dobu - long since\r\nji dávno - long ago, long since\r\nji dříve - before, before now, once before\r\nji ne - no any longer, no longer, no more, not any longer, not any more\r\nji ne- - not longer\r\nji nebyl - was no longer\r\nnení ji vyráběn - no longer produced\r\nji nikdy - never again\r\nji od (o ceně) - from under\r\nji od 30 liber terlinků (o ceně) - from under 30 GBP\r\nji předtím - ere this\r\nji v 9. století - as early as the 9th century\r\n', '2002-08-12 14:51:40', 624, 0),
(627, 13, 'glo mcmillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', '\"U\" = \"ji\" (already).--Thanks for this help.', '', '2002-08-12 18:48:06', 0, 0),
(628, 14, 'Jarda', '', 'Re: Czech vs. American \'Personal Space\'', 'I found this question very funny.\r\nWell, I think Czech guys love a hug very much although they dont like showing their feelings in public.\r\nAnyway this is only my personal opinion maybe I am wrong.\r\n\r\nJarda', '2002-08-14 11:48:28', 548, 0),
(629, 13, 'J-B Blanc', 'blancjb15@hotmail.com', 'v.s.', 'Does anybody know what means the abbreviation v.s.?\r\nThank you', '2002-08-17 02:54:22', 0, 1),
(630, 13, 'Michael S', '', 'Re: v.s.', 'in which context??\r\n\r\nthere is \"VS\" which stands for \"valné shromádění\" (= general assembly) or \r\nthere is \"V\" which stands for \"vysoká kola\" (= higher vocational education)\r\n\r\nlet me know if this is applicable \r\n\r\ns pozdravem\r\nMichael', '2002-08-17 16:52:24', 629, 0),
(631, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: v.s. - Vysoká kola = University, College', 'Sorry, but \'vysoká kola\' has nothing to do with \'vocational\' education. It can only be translated as \'university\' or \'college\' with any degree of accuracy. \'higher vocational education\' is \'vyí střední vzdělávání technického typu\' or even \'učilitě\' - depending on the English context.', '2002-08-17 18:04:23', 630, 0),
(632, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: v.s.=Veřejná Společnost?', 'When seen after the name of an organization, v.s. could mean \'veřejná společnost\' or public company - but this, as far, as I know has no equivalent in the Czech law and I could only find it on one web site.', '2002-08-17 18:09:51', 629, 0),
(633, 13, 'lotte pattyn', 'lottepattyn@hotmail.com', 'diffiult language?', 'Hello everyone,\r\n\r\nI\'m considering to learn Czech and Russian at university next year. I was wondering is Czech a really hard language? Is it similar to other languages? Normally I speak Dutch.\r\n\r\nIf anyone is willing to answer my questions, please send me a mail!\r\nThank you in advance!\r\n\r\nLotte', '2002-08-17 22:31:04', 0, 0),
(634, 13, 'Jean-Baptiste Blanc', 'blancjb15@hotmail.com', 'Re: v.s.=Veřejná Společnost?', 'That\'s the context where I found this abbreviation:\r\nProsím uhradte objednavku na ná ucet, v.s. je cislo objednavky.', '2002-08-18 05:19:54', 632, 1),
(635, 13, 'Jean-Baptiste Blanc', 'blancjb15@hotmail.com', 'Re: diffiult language?', 'I can tell you from my own experiences that Czech is not so difficult. I think it\'s the same difficulty as German. Compared to Hungarian, Czech is really easy!', '2002-08-18 05:25:04', 633, 1),
(636, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: v.s.= Variabilní Symbol', 'Well, in fact, the answer is that \'v.s.\' means Variabilní Symbol which is something that you need to inlude in an electronic money transfer to somebody\'s account. That will tell them that the money is for you. There is also \'specifický symbol\' which describes the type of payment. Usually you don\'t have to worry about either but sometimes recipients require you to do it - usually the utilities, tax and social security people, etc.', '2002-08-18 14:20:07', 634, 0),
(637, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: diffiult language?', 'Czech is more difficult in the beginning but if you persevere it is no more difficult than any other language. But you must always expect a lot of hard work and frustration (as with any language).', '2002-08-18 14:21:57', 633, 0),
(638, 13, 'Peter', '', 'Why are word endings referred to as \"hard\" or \"soft\"?', 'Why are Czech consonants or word stems (or \"word endings\") actually called \"hard\" and \"soft\"? Is it the subjective impression of the sound, which seems \"hard\" to our ears, or does it have to do with the \"hardness\" of the physical movements made by the tongue, or lips, i.e. physiology of the vocal movements involved in pronunciation?\r\n\r\nThanks!', '2002-08-19 00:11:24', 0, 0),
(639, 14, 'matilda', 'handleys@volny.cz', 'Re: I.P.Pavlova', 'So why are so many places named after Pavlov in Prague. Is it the high regard czechs hold for Russians?', '2002-08-20 02:54:29', 604, 0),
(640, 15, 'matilda', 'handleys@volny.cz', 'lack of students', 'Is there no students out there. I\'ve been struggling with czech a few years now and although I have made progress, whenever I visit I find it almost impossible to hold a conversation (of any length). The other problem I have is with word order, I\'ve now started reading books in the hope of picking up a rhythum (if I could spell it). I,m finding this very heavy going...sometimes a paragraph seems to take forever. Any tips for making a leap forward from the everday/phrase book stage I\'ve been stuck in since the first year.', '2002-08-20 03:16:00', 0, 0),
(641, 15, 'Michael', '', 'Re: lack of students', 'Most of the threads are somehow posted on the \"Language\" chapter.\r\n\r\nAs for your question, that`s not easy to answer... Where do you study Czech (or where do you live)?\r\nAs for me, I grew up in a Czech environment (but living abroad almost all my life) so it`s a different situation obviously. \r\n\r\nThe old fashioned way - reading books - is of course an option, but perhaps dull and dry in the end... (I am assuming that you live outside the Czech Republic, and that this is so far your only tool)\r\nIf you have unlimited internet access, maybe listening frequently to Czech Radio stations could be a welcome alternative. Via satelite dishes you could even catch Czech TV (at least in Europe) which is more entertaining, and giving a more variety of \"practice\" of all sorts of speech in Czech.\r\n', '2002-08-22 23:19:15', 640, 0),
(642, 13, 'Ashlinka Kaberle', 'ashlinka_kaberle@hotmail.com', 'Hey! Canadian..here my fav hockey player is  from Czech, and I wanna learn the language!', '', '2002-08-23 23:04:35', 0, 0),
(643, 14, 'Michael', '', 'Re: I.P.Pavlova', 'I wouldn\'t necessarily classify this as \"high esteem for Russians\" per se. \r\nLet\'s put it on the fact that this was an important scientist. Russians are, needless to say, like the Czechs also belonging to the Slavic people, and that\'s perhaps a \"pre\". The high esteem for Russians as you put it has somehow faded since the events in the latter part of the 20th century... \r\n(just to stress before somebody might misunderstand me: that personally I do not have anything against individual Russian people)\r\n\r\n', '2002-08-24 17:59:42', 639, 0),
(644, 14, 'junny', 'junja@lovemail.com', 'recourse', '1. What is the most distinctive features of Czech\'s communication both\r\n> verbal and non-verbal communication?\r\n> 2. For examples about one or more speech event\'s patterns of Czech.\r\nWould you please help me ? I have to do paper about them.\r\nThank you \r\n', '2002-08-24 19:07:11', 0, 0),
(645, 13, 'Rob', 'DigitalPDX@hotmail.com', 'Re: v.s.', 'versus?\r\n\r\nLike Mike Tyson v.s. Evander Holyfield?\r\n\r\nMedieval Latin, towards, against, from Latin, adverb, so as to face, from past participle of vertere to turn\r\n\r\n1 : AGAINST\r\n2 : in contrast to or as the alternative of <free trade versus protection>\r\n\r\nwww.webster.com', '2002-08-27 02:49:52', 629, 0),
(646, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: v.s.', '\'Versus\' is not used in Czech. Therefore neither is the abreviation. It wouldn\'t be v.s., anyway but vs. or v.', '2002-08-27 04:00:49', 645, 0),
(647, 15, 'Kirsi', 'Kitti_malmi@hotmail.com', 'The language', 'In 1997 Prague became my obsession, and I spent all my free time searching Czechs\' history and culture. Also went to work to Prague and studied the language in Charles University. In 2001 I completed my thesis about Czech Republic. Two years ago I moved from Finland to UK. As while writing my thesis in Prague I met my English boyfriend!!! 1 year ago we moved from Brighton to Glasgow and I found that here we have an excellent Slavonic Department for PHd research students, and during that 4 years time you can learn Czech language !!!I am so happy as it is very hard to find a place to learn Czech. Now I just hope I\'ll be granted a scholarship to be able to join the doctor\'s degree, otherwise,...snif.  ', '2002-08-28 13:46:57', 0, 0),
(648, 14, 'Michael', '', 'Re: Czech vs. American \'Personal Space\'', 'I would agree to that.... it depends on the indiviuals (and the occasion/environment)\r\n\r\nMía', '2002-08-30 00:17:43', 628, 0),
(649, 13, 'John', 'jnottle@internode.on.net', 'translation, please', 'Can someone translate the following for me, please. (Thankyou very much) za milou pozornost. I think I have roughly translated this but would like confirmation.\r\nBTW, I was in Cesky Krumlov just prior to the devastating floods. South Bohemia is very beautiful and historically fascinating...the huge Rozmberk fishpond, castles and abundant evergreen landscape are just poetical.', '2002-08-30 07:22:54', 0, 0),
(650, 13, 'Zdenek Drstka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: jak se sklonuje slovo', 'Bůh (or bůh - it depends; if you mean the only one God - as in christian, jewish or islamic religion - you are to write Bůh):\r\nNom. Bůh\r\nGen. Boha\r\nDat. Bohu\r\nAcc. Boha\r\nVoc. Boe\r\nLoc. (o) Bohu (locativ needs preposition in Czech)\r\nIns. Bohem\r\n\r\nPl. nom. bozi/bohové\r\ngen. bohů\r\ndat. bohům\r\nacc. bohy\r\nvoc. bozi/bohové\r\nloc. (o) bozích\r\nins. bohy\r\n\r\nPossesive adjective: Boí/boí\r\n\r\nGenerally, if you find letter \"ů\" (pronounced \"ú\") in nominativ (ex. prefix as \"pů-\" or \"prů-\"), you may be quite sure that i other cases \"o\" occurs in its place.', '2002-08-31 18:50:14', 611, 1),
(651, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: Please, oh, please, oh please!', 'Well, and what about \"vojáček\"? This is a deminutive form.', '2002-08-31 18:52:40', 606, 0),
(652, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: Opět potřebuji pomoc', 'Album - except the nom., acc. and voc. sg., it is the same as the word \"město\".\r\nKriterium - except the nom., acc. and voc. sg., it is the same as the word \"moře\".', '2002-08-31 18:55:50', 597, 1),
(653, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: Translation', 'I think it could be: \"Neopětovaná láska je takovým utrpením, e ani bozi jí netrýzní zavrené v podsvětí.\"', '2002-08-31 18:57:53', 594, 1),
(654, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Těí mě', 'Well, it is quite similar to \"How do you do?\" in English.', '2002-08-31 19:01:43', 554, 1),
(655, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: \'ci\', \'si\'', 'Written \"di\", \"ti\" or \"ni\" are pronounced softened - \"d\'i\", \"t\'i\" or \"n\'i\".\r\nAs well \"dě\", tě\" or \"ně\" are pronounced softened - \"d\'e\", \"t\'e\" or \"n\'e\".\r\nOr, if you hear \"d\'i\", \"t\'i\" or \"n\'i\", you may be sure you are to write it \"di\", \"ti\" or \"ni\". (And the same way with \"d\'e\" etc.)', '2002-08-31 19:09:45', 503, 1),
(656, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: moravian music', 'Yes, there are still three main groups of Moravian dialects, which are used in music and quite widely spoken (and, to be honest, we have to say that they are really nearer to the OFFICIAL Czech language than what is spoken in Prague and central Bohemia).\r\nThere is not enough space for it, but if you would like, I may send you the text of the song \"Mezi horami\" both in original and in English; and a little list of widely spoken Moravian dialects.', '2002-08-31 19:31:46', 148, 1),
(657, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: The meaning of -ník', '...but it always written with one \"n\" - týdeník, večerník, deník...', '2002-08-31 19:34:33', 142, 1),
(658, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: translation, please', 'za milou pozornost = for the smart attention..', '2002-09-01 19:14:05', 649, 0),
(659, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: diffiult language?', 'I`m native russian speaker and I know czech too. If you want, we can correspond :) my mail is lovermann@seznam.cz', '2002-09-01 19:16:59', 633, 0),
(660, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: diffiult language?', 'I`m native russian speaker and I know czech too. If you want, we can correspond :) my mail is lovermann@seznam.cz', '2002-09-01 19:17:18', 633, 0),
(661, 13, 'Zdeněk Drtka', 'drstka@phil.muni.cz', 'Re: Why are word endings referred to as', 'Consonants, which must be - but in written form only - followed by \"y\" (also called \"hard y\"), are sometimes called \"hard\"; these consonants are d, h, ch (pronounced as in word \"loch\"), k, n, r, t.\r\nConsonants, which must be followed by \"i\" (\"soft i\"), are sometimes called \"soft\"; these consonants are c, č, ď (but written \"di\"), j, ň (written \"ni\"), ř, ,  (written \"ti\"), .\r\nOther consonants (b, f, l, m, p, s, v, z) can be followed by both \"i\" and \"y\"; the pronountiation is the same, but the meaning is changed.\r\nBoth the letters \"i\" and \"y\" represent the same sound \"i\" (except some dialects).', '2002-09-01 23:07:11', 638, 1),
(662, 13, ' HOSTINA    CONSTANTIN', 'HOSTINA_13@YAHOO.COM', ' WHAT DOES \"HOSTINA\"  IN CZECH MEANS?', '   I have found 5330 sites through yahoo, which contained the name of \"Hostina\".  I`m very curious what it means because it is my name. All the sites are in czech and i don`t understand. I will be very grateful if  you will answer me if \"Hostina\" means something. Thank u very much.', '2002-09-02 01:21:13', 0, 1),
(663, 13, 'Michael', '', 'Re:  WHAT DOES', 'Hostina means literally a \"feast\" or \"banquet\" (eventually; symposium}\r\n\r\n', '2002-09-02 02:58:52', 662, 0),
(664, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Hostina.', 'Eventually \"guest\" (in case of surnames).\r\nStem \"host\" means \"guest\" + suffix -ina.', '2002-09-02 15:46:39', 663, 0),
(665, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Why are word endings referred to as', 'Otázka spíe zněla na souvislost mezi hláskami a stupnicí tvrdosti. To, zdá se, nikdo neví.', '2002-09-05 14:10:57', 661, 0),
(666, 13, 'Barbi Volper', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Czech sign language', 'I always wanted to know more about what deaf culture is like in the Czech Republic.\r\n\r\nWhat I know of is that they have their own form of sign language, no technology like TDD to talk on telephones or captions on tv.  This is probably the least question asked about Czech culture.  But I would really like to know.\r\n', '2002-09-07 00:13:34', 0, 0),
(667, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech sign language', 'The facilities for deaf and hearing-impaired people are really not as advanced as in the US (or even UK) but they\'re not too far behind. As far as I know, there is some captioning on TV and some news programs have interpreters into sign language (I\'ve been to lectures with such interpreters as well - there\'s a special program for the hearing-impaired at the Charles University). \r\n\r\nThere is also a strong tradition of trying to teach lip-reading instead of sign language but it is not as popular a solution as it once was. There has been a lot of original research done in this area by Czech linguists. See Czech Studies department at Charles University for more details: <a href=http://ucjtk.ff.cuni.cz>http://ucjtk.ff.cuni.cz</a>\r\n', '2002-09-07 00:20:03', 666, 0),
(668, 13, 'Krysztof', 'krysztof@noos.fr', 'Czech Dictionary', 'Hello!\r\n\r\nI am (desperately) looking for a czech monolingual dictionary (preferably on-line).  Actually looking for a tool that can tell me whether a verb is perfective or imperfective, what the gender of a noun is, etc.  I was using the \"Czech Morphological Analyzer\" but it does not seem to work anymore...\r\n\r\nHas anyone any idea?  Or can anyone recommend a useful book/dictionary?\r\n\r\nThanks for your help.  K.', '2002-09-07 09:35:52', 0, 1),
(669, 13, 'visla', 'jar.nov@volny.cz', 'meaning', 'What the word \"hejtman\" does mean? For example \"okresni hejtman\", \"hejtmanstvi\" etc. ', '2002-09-07 16:47:25', 0, 0),
(670, 14, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: recourse', 'Junny, This is quite a difficult one for me to answer as I at best only know Czech at an intermediate level, also the question a bit all encompassing. However I\'ll make a few points from my head. The Czech alphabet has some more letters (than the english) including a compound one \"ch\" and I think a fairly unique sounding one R with a hacek \"ř\" (this is pronounce rrzzzhh roughly). The language has 6 cases which alter the endings of nouns etc.. Nominative...subject of sentence, Accusative...the direct object, Vocative..for addressing people, Dative, Locatve and Instrumental which are a bit more difficult to explain. Lastly Czech has two forms High Style which is used in writing (formally) and Low Style which is everyday spoken. There\'s many more difference\'s and some of what I\'ve said needs clarifying (and may not be exactly what you\'re looking for. I suggest you do some ground work and then come back with some more pointed questions, there\'s plenty on info on the internet inc this site and books etc (not the shop). It\'s either that or you\'re going to need a small piece of paper!! Perhaps this might prompt some of the others who visit to give you some more succinct/linguistic info which I\'m struggling to do...come back with ?\'s and I\'ll look up what I can', '2002-09-07 22:00:04', 644, 0),
(671, 14, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: I.P.Pavlova', 'I don\'t necessarily think Matilda was being to serious when she mentioned the Czechs high regard for Russians (she might even have been being sarky). I thought the esteem for Russians ranked alongside that for Germans or even perhaps Americans if they jump into someone\'s personal space. What I don\'t know is the connection Pavlov had specifically with Prague/Czechoslovakia. I\'d be interested, I\'ve not found a huge lot about him on the internet, was it just his international renown.', '2002-09-07 22:12:25', 643, 0),
(672, 13, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: meaning', 'Hejtman translates as captain, hejtmanstvi,captainship/captaincy. An okresni hejtman is a district administrator and in this context hejtmanstvi could be the district executive/council', '2002-09-09 03:04:21', 669, 0),
(673, 14, 'Michael', '', 'Re: I.P.Pavlova', 'Hi - I\'m sure Matilda\'s remark wasn\'t perhaps that serious indeed. Don\'t worry. I though pethaps to specify my answer, just in case someone might misunderstand (you never know)\r\nAs for Pavlov\'s fame ... I am not too sure if there was a connection (e.g. that he lived there, visited Prague or whatever). Have to find that ourt still. Never too old to learn, eh?\r\n\r\nm', '2002-09-11 21:38:34', 671, 0),
(674, 13, 'Scott', 'schoctaw16@hotmail.com', 'Past Tense of Byt', 'Hi, I was wanting to know you, Domonik, or one of you others could tell me what the past tense of byt is. (or rather tenses) For example the change from \"Thats great=To je fajn!\" to \"That was great\"=To _____ fajn. Do any other parts of speech in the sentence change prefixes. What would these forms of byt change to for the past tense: jsem, jsi, je, to je, jsme, jste, and jsou.  I have one more question for you guys. How hard is it for me to purchase a czech keyboard and how much will it cost me.  I am tired of not being able to use the right alphabetical structure in my czech and want to switch to a czech keyboard.\r\n    Dekuji moc!\r\n       Scott', '2002-09-13 09:32:59', 0, 0),
(675, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Meaning of', 'Thanks, this phrase came in quite useful when I was meeting my future in-laws. As did the other tid bits of Czech I learned over the last year.  ', '2002-09-13 09:35:20', 554, 0),
(676, 15, 'Scott', 'schoctaw16@hotmail.com', 'A few tips to learn pronunciation.', 'I know that when I first started to learn czech the hardest part for me was understanding how to pronounce the words (as I am sure it is the same for most who try).  It affected the way I learned the words because I was learning them with the wrong pronunciations, so when I spoke to people in czech I was laughed at because my pronunciation was funny and very \"American\" as it was put to me.  The best and only cure I found for this was to go and spend 2 months ( wish I could have been there longer ) in CZ.  While there I was surrounded by my fiance\'s family and all the rest of Czech.  I could only speak to her and her sister in English and so I picked up on czech very rapidly as it became necessary for me to learn to communicate.  After a few weeks I had the pronunciations down and so therefore my studies took leaps and bounds.  So what I am trying to say in not so few words is that the best way to learn how to speak czech properly is to hear it spoken regularly.  For anyone seriously wanting to learn the language I suggest going to czech and staying with a family ( out of Prague ) where only one or two people speak English.  This is the best and quickest way to learn it.  Also listening to czech music, tv, and etc.. are very good ways to pick up on it.\r\nGood luck!\r\nScott', '2002-09-13 09:50:08', 0, 0),
(677, 13, 'scott', '', 'Re: Past Tense of Byt', 'sorry for the mispelling Dominik.', '2002-09-14 11:22:29', 674, 0),
(678, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Past Tense of Byt', 'So, \r\nTo je fajn,\r\nTo bylo fajn, (past)\r\nTo bude fajn. (fut.)\r\n\r\nBut the verb \"být\" is like an english \"to be\", the conjugation you can read here:\r\nhttp://jazyk.prag.ru/gram/gram.konj.byt.html\r\nThe site is in Russian, but you\'ll understand the czech part of it.', '2002-09-15 06:38:56', 674, 1),
(679, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'sklonování slova téma, fór.', 'Prosím vás o pomoc, jak se sklonují adjektiva tíma a fór', '2002-09-15 06:42:43', 0, 1),
(680, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Past Tense of Byt', 'Thanks Lovermann! I remember hearing that now.  It was killing me trying to find it.\r\nScott', '2002-09-15 11:12:21', 678, 0),
(681, 17, 'Linda', 'puppy_hi@hotmail.com', 'Re: gramatika a mluvnice', 'my opinium for that is that \"mluvnice\" is the way you talk or we czech people talk. \"gramatika\" is the way you write. as chech language is what you see you read this too words have the same meaning in czech. at least that is how i explain it to myself and will explain it to my children.', '2002-09-17 01:34:08', 591, 0),
(682, 13, 'Christinamoulis@rocketmail.com', '', 'Re: moravian music', 'I would love this, I already have the words in czech from the internet, but not the english.  To my ignorant eye, the words on Čechomor CDs look more like slovak.\r\n\r\nChristina.', '2002-09-17 12:57:36', 656, 0),
(683, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: skloňování slova téma.', 'Téma (starí pravopis thema) je neutrum:\r\n\r\nSg. téma, tématu, tématu, téma, téma, (o) tématu, tématem\r\nPl. témata, témat, tématům, témata, témata, (o) tématech, tématy\r\n', '2002-09-17 13:45:19', 679, 0),
(684, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: skloňování slova fór.', 'Fór se skloňuje jako \"hrad\" (jen 6. pád sing. je pouze -u, viz poznámka ke skloňování \"stůl\"):\r\n\r\nSg. fór, fóru, fóru, fór, fóre, (o) fóru, fórem\r\nPl. fóry, fórů, fórům, fóry, fóry, (o) fórech, fóry', '2002-09-17 14:01:56', 679, 0),
(685, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech Dictionary', 'The only dictionary that\'s any good at all is Fronek\'s Velký česko=anglický slovník. It will have all the information you need. You can buy it through the Bookstore on Bohemica.com.', '2002-09-20 17:31:07', 668, 0),
(686, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Sklonování podstatných jmen', 'Moc děkuji vem, kdo mi pomáhá s těkou českou gramatikou. Teď bych chtěl vědět, jako se sklonují tato podstatná jména: dveře, práce, peníze.', '2002-09-20 22:51:59', 0, 1),
(687, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Sklonování podstatných jmen', 'Moc děkuji vem, kdo mi pomáhá s těkou českou gramatikou. Teď bych chtěl vědět, jako se sklonují tato podstatná jména: dveře, práce, peníze.', '2002-09-20 23:12:46', 0, 1),
(688, 13, 'Gloria McMilllan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Verb: doručit', 'Ahoj!\r\n\r\nI am trying to use the verb doručit.\r\nIt is supposed to mean, acc. to Poldauf,\r\nto deliver a thing to a person.\r\n\r\nI will try a past tense:\r\n\r\nKdo doručil ten reč vcera?\r\nAnglicky:  Who delivered that speech last night?\r\n\r\nA future tense:\r\n\r\nAnglicky:  Bude nase prezident doručit reč zitra?\r\n\r\nHow far off are those sentences?\r\n\r\nDekuji pro vase pomoc!\r\n\r\nGloria\r\n\r\n', '2002-09-22 21:21:02', 0, 0),
(689, 13, 'Gloria Ptacek McMillan', '', 'Zpravy', 'Ahoj!\r\n\r\nHow do you say in Czech,\r\n\r\n\"I will be defending my dissertation on October 7th, and,\r\nif I pass, I will be taking my PHD degree in December?\"\r\n\r\n(And one of the three immigrant women whose writing I\r\nrhetorically analyze in that dissertation was a Czech woman \r\nwriting in Czech at times.)\r\n\r\nAno, that\'s my news.  I am living those Franz Kafaesque\r\njoys ot the last minutes before the defense itself.\r\n\r\nYour good wishes are much appreciated!  \r\n\r\nGloria', '2002-09-22 21:26:36', 0, 0),
(690, 14, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@DakotaCom.net', 'Norbert Fabian Capek screenplay project', 'Hi, all!\r\n\r\nI would like to begin a screenplay on the Czech liberal tehologian Norbet\r\nFabian Capek who died in dachau once I finish this dissertation defense\r\nand doctoral program here.\r\n\r\nI have been writing everywhere I could imagine to see if anyone else \r\nalready was writing such a screenplay.\r\n\r\nIf anyone on this list has any knowledge of another project already\r\nunderway or if you would like to collaborate on this project for which\r\nI need someone actually in Prague and CR to interview the remaining people \r\nwho knew Capek, let me know.  Even in the unlikely event that I get\r\nany travel monies for a trip to research Capek\'s milieu, I would like\r\nto have someone there in prague confirm the settings, too.\r\n\r\nDekuji,\r\n\r\nGloria McMillan\r\n', '2002-09-22 21:33:01', 0, 0),
(691, 17, 'těpánka Pfeiferová', 'rekat@volny.cz', 'Re: Jakto e nikdo nemá zájem?', 'Oracím se na vás s prosbou. Můj letitý přítel z Egypta(ijící tamté) je nucen se naučit Česky dokonaleji, ne v současné době zvládá a poprosil mě o pomoc. Potřebuje kazety s výukou Četiny v Arabtině, případně v Angličtině. Bohuel jsem zatím nebyla úspěná v hledání tohoto produktu a tak se obracím s prosbou na Vás. Pokud víte o takovém jazykovém kurzu, prosím kontaktujte mě na uvedeném E-mailu. Předem děkuji těpánka Pfeiferová ', '2002-09-23 00:10:17', 583, 0),
(692, 16, 'těpánka Pfeiferová', 'rekat @volny.cz', 'pomůe mi prosím někdo??', 'Obracím se na Vás s prosbou. Můj letitý přítel z Egypta( ijící tamté) je nucen se naučit Česky dokonaleji, ne v současné době zvládá a poprosil mě o pomoc. Potřebuje kazety s výukou Četiny v Arabtině, případně v Angličtině. Bohuel jsem zatím nebyla úspěná v hledání tohoto produktu a tak se obracím na Vás. Pokud o takovém kurzu víte, kontaktujte mě prosím na e-mail: rekat@volny.cz\r\nPředem děkuji        těpánka Pfeiferová', '2002-09-23 00:29:32', 0, 0),
(693, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Skloňování podstatných jmen.', 'Dveře, en. rod (pomnoné!):\r\ndveře, dveří, dveřím, dveře, dveře!, (o) dveřích, dveřmi.\r\n\r\nPráce, en. rod:\r\npráce, práce, práci, práci, práce!, (o) práci, prací;\r\npráce, prací, pracím, práce, práce!, (o) pracích, pracemi.\r\n\r\nPeníze, mu. rod, mn. číslo:\r\npeníze, peněz, penězům, peníze, peníze!, (o) penězích, penězi.\r\n\r\nPeníz (= jednotlivá mince), mu. rod, skloňuje se jako STROJ:\r\npeníz, peníze, penízi, peníz, penízi!, (o) penízi, penízem;\r\npeníze, penízů, penízům, peníze, peníze!, (o) penízích, penízi.', '2002-09-23 16:45:28', 687, 0),
(694, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Verb: doručit', 'to deliver a letter = doručit (odevzdat, předat) dopis\r\nto deliver a speech = pronést řeč\r\n\r\nKdo pronesl včera tu řeč?\r\nPronese zítra ná president řeč?\r\n', '2002-09-23 17:02:27', 688, 0),
(695, 17, 'Dominator', '', 'Re: jak bude správně...?', 'ja myslim ze lide zapomenili na to ze jazk je ziva vec a ze my jenom zbirame pravidla aby pomohli nasim snaham ji ucit a pri denim puoziti vetsina techto pravidel jsou ignorovani. Jako Prazak ja rikam vetsinou Vopravdu misto Opravdu', '2002-09-24 22:55:45', 588, 0),
(696, 17, 'Dominato', '', 'NEJLEPSI FILM', 'Muj obilebny cesky film je Cisaruv Pekar z Janem Werichem, a take mam rad Ucho, nevim kdo byl reziser a kdo v nem hral ale je to fakt spicka. \r\nAle abych pravdu rek nejradsi mam rad Medvidky, Potkali se u Kolina. Ted zrovna nevim kdo to udelal, je to od bratri v tricku. Ale je to produkt \"maleho naroda\" (Slova Capka) a muzeme na to byt hrdy. \r\nNi Zjisk Ni Slavu', '2002-09-24 23:00:59', 429, 0),
(697, 17, 'micval', 'micval@centrum.cz', 'Re: NEJLEPSI FILM', 'Myslim, ze v Uchu hrali hlavni role Radoslav (?) Brzobohaty s Jirinou Bohdalovou..', '2002-09-25 01:52:03', 696, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(698, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Zpravy', '\"Sedmého října budu obhajovat svou disertaci, a jestli ji obhájím, obdrím v prosinci titul PhD.\"\r\n\r\nHodně těstí!', '2002-09-26 21:22:52', 689, 0),
(699, 16, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: pomůe mi prosím někdo??', 'Vechny kurzu a materiály, o kterých vím, můete najít v sekci Bookstore na Bohemica.com.', '2002-09-26 21:24:29', 692, 0),
(700, 13, 'loverman', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'pomozte mi, prosím, přeloit..', 'Nemůu nidke najít, co znamená slova: \r\n\r\n1) brazolety, \r\n2) hobrový cynik, \r\n3) za faguli ivenou  západem slunce\r\n4) uzpívané keble\r\n5) Pejzatý\r\n6) rozparek od Kniete  \r\n7) s početným komonstvem\r\n8) jdu domů voháknout\r\n9) Jeho hogerovsky měkký hlas ( co znamená hogerovský )\r\n\r\nsěkuji vem za pomoc', '2002-09-28 16:25:33', 0, 1),
(701, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: pomozte mi, prosím, přeloit..', 'Brazoleta (z franc. bracelet) = náramek; brazolety = náramky, t.j. pouta (argot)\r\n\r\nHobra = deska lisovaná z dřev. zbytků (zkr. podle výrobce Holzindustrie Braunau), spojení s cynikem nejasné\r\n\r\nFagule nebo fakule, z lat. facula, to je zdrobnělina z fax = pochodeň, louč; za fakuli ivenou ...  nejspí nějaká básnická metafora\r\n\r\nUzpívaný, patrně novotvar podle vzoru \"ukřičený\", \"upovídaný\"; uzpívané keble = neustále (moná a protivně) zpívající mli\r\n\r\nPejzy (z hebr.) = vlasy na skráních ortodoxních idů; pejzatý = patrně nějaký ortodoxní id\r\n\r\nRozparek (od slovesa \"rozpárati) = rozpáraná (často úmyslně neseitá) část vu oděvu, spojení s kníetem nejasné\r\n\r\nS početným komonstvem (archaicky) = s početnou druinou\r\n\r\nJdu se domů voháknout = (argot) jdu se domů (pěkně) obléci\r\n\r\nHögerovsky (s umlautem) = jako Karel Höger, český herec\r\n', '2002-10-01 14:16:01', 700, 0),
(702, 14, 'hillbillymama', 'hillbillymama@msn.com', 'Lived in czech', 'I meet someone that was born in the czech republic and we meet in 1992\r\nhis mom lives in europe i am happy i found thid site shes not doing good and i want to get a nice card and in my own words i want her to know i love her she knows germany but i want to do the card in czech i was there for 6 years i even have photos and just got a scanner for my birthday so i hope to get some up on my web page well i hope you that have been there enjoyed it as much as me and the ones that well visit there its very nice and peacefull nothing like being in the us not at all ', '2002-10-01 14:59:14', 0, 1),
(703, 17, 'nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: NEJLEPSI FILM', 'Příběhy medvídků, kteří se potkali u Kolína, byly moje oblíbené, kdy jsem byla malá ... a mám je pořád stejně ráda, i kdy u jsem starí. :)))\r\nSprávný název pohádek je \"Pojďte pane, budeme si hrát!\" a natočil je reisér Břetislav Pojar.\r\n\r\nZdravím vechny, kteří mají stále rádi pohádky!! :)) ', '2002-10-02 20:00:06', 696, 0),
(704, 13, 'Craig', 'sapphyre12@aol.com', 'Czech Bible', 'DOes anyone know where I can get a Czech Bible', '2002-10-05 12:39:01', 0, 0),
(705, 13, 'Dominik Lukes', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech Bible', 'You can download the text for free at <a href=http://www.theophilos.sk>http://www.theophilos.sk</a>. You get several translations of the Bible (old and modern) and the ability to compare King James with other translations. Very flexible software.', '2002-10-05 13:41:36', 704, 0),
(706, 14, 'Kim', 'bigbear@globalccs.net', 'Shipping Vehicles', 'Hi!  Does anyone out there have any recommendations about shipping a vehicle from the U.S. to either Amsterdam or Germany?  A friend of mine will be moving home (Czech Republic) in two weeks and I need to find a reliable and reasonably priced company.  Any help or advice would be appreciated!\r\n\r\nThanks.\r\n\r\nKim', '2002-10-11 23:08:39', 0, 1),
(707, 13, 'Lorenzo', 'exilems72@hotmail.com', 'Překlad', 'I need a little help translating one simple sentence into Czech:\r\n\"I\'d like to write about his new song\"\r\nI have had a try at translating it myself. So here\'s what I have come up with:\r\n\"Chtěl bych psat o svůj novy píseň\"\r\nCan anyone tell me if this is correct?\r\n\r\nThank you for your help!\r\n\r\nLorenzo', '2002-10-12 22:50:43', 0, 0),
(708, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Překlad', '\"svůj\" = reflexive possesive pronoun\r\n\r\nChtěl bych psát o SVÉ nové písni. = ... about MY new song.\r\nChtěl bych psát o JEHO nové písni. = ... about HIS new song.\r\n\r\nNow I have discovered that I cannot translate into English:\r\n\r\nOn by chtěl psát o SVÉ nové písni. He\'d like to write about ??? new song.\r\nOn by chtěl psát o JEHO nové písni.          -\"-            ???   -\"-    ', '2002-10-14 14:40:14', 707, 0),
(709, 14, 'nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Shipping Vehicles', 'Hi,\r\nI have seen this topic to be discussed at Expats forum recently. \r\nTry to have a look there, the address is: www.expats.cz\r\n\r\nRgds,  Nadka', '2002-10-14 20:12:40', 706, 0),
(710, 13, 'Lorenzo', '', 'Re: Překlad', 'Thank you Vladimír!\r\nNow it\'s clear! I think you can translate \"svůj\" as \"one\'s own\".\r\n\r\nOn by chtěl psát o SVÉ nové písni. He\'d like to write about HIS (own) new song. \r\nOn by chtěl psát o JEHO nové písni. -\"- HIS -\"-', '2002-10-16 00:00:09', 708, 0),
(711, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Překlad', 'Mr. X wrote Mr. Y about his new song.\r\n\r\nWho is the author of the song?\r\n\r\nIn Latin and Slavic languages:\r\nreflexive possesive pronoun suus/svůj - Mr. X (subject)\r\n3rd possesive pronoun eius/jeho - Mr. Y (object of the sentence)\r\n\r\n', '2002-10-16 16:37:41', 710, 0),
(712, 13, 'marjolein', 'marjoleinkloet@hotmail.com', 'can someone translate for me please?', 'In the summer of 2001 I went for a holiday to the Czech Republic and I\r\nvisited Praha. In Praha I found in a little old bookstore a kind of saving\r\naccounts book of a pawnbroking business or a savings bank, I don\'t know. This book was from Ruzena Nováková and this book was from 1924 to 1945. I\'ve heard this kind of bussiness was illegal during the comminist period, that\'s maybe why this book stopped at 1945. \r\n\r\nOn the front of the book is printed Vkladní knízka. Vseobecné zálozny\r\nv Karlíne. Could someone translate that and tell me if it is a savings bank book or book of the pwnbraking business\r\nI found out that Karlín is a quarter in Praha. \r\n\r\nYours \r\nMarjolein\r\n', '2002-10-17 16:48:44', 0, 1),
(713, 13, 'V.P.', '', 'Re: can someone translate for me please?', 'Savings (depositor\'s) book of General savings bank in Karlín.', '2002-10-17 17:52:20', 712, 0),
(714, 14, 'MJ Sharp', 'mjsharp@earthlink.net', 'Czech artist info?', 'I spent a month in Prague a year ago and while there I acquired a tiny watercolor sketch that I  found in a used/antique bookstore. It was done in 1960 by Zdenek Holub. I would love to find more information about him.  I was able to find out that he was a student of Fisarek and that he did the stained glass window at the Cheb railway station. He lived from 1927-1995. I\'ve looked at libraries here for any more information about him, and while I\'ve found some books on different periods of contemporary Czech art, I haven\\\'t found anything about him.  Does anyone out there know of any resources that I could access about 20th century Czech artists? I\\\'m located in the U.S. but have a good internet connection.  Any thoughts?  Thanks very much!\r\n MJ\r\n', '2002-10-18 19:22:35', 0, 1),
(715, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'skonování podstatných jmen muského rodu', 'Vím, e vedle koncovky -ích, bývá v 6. p. mn. čísla koncovka -ách u zdrobnělých podstatných jmen zakončených na -ček, např. v balíčcích - v balíčkách, a u některých podstatných jmen na -ek, např. v kouscích - v kouskách. \r\n\r\nA můj dotaz je jak s tím v rodě středním? Jak bude správně: (městečko) - v městečkách nebo ve městečcích?', '2002-10-20 16:37:54', 0, 1),
(716, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Skloňování podstatných jmen středního rodu.', 'Ve středním rodu (i rodě) je tomu jinak:\r\n\r\nZdrobnělá podst.jm. stř.rodu na \"-ko\" mají v 6.p.mn.č. koncovku \"-ách\", nezdrobnělá mají \"-ích\" i \"-ách\".\r\n\r\nNapř.:\r\njablko - jablkách i jablcích\r\nstředisko - střediskách i střediscích\r\noko - okách (duál očích!)\r\nale\r\njablíčko - jablíčkách\r\nočičko - očičkách\r\nměstečko - městečkách\r\n\r\nKoncovku \"-ách\" mají také slova na \"-cho\", \"-go\" (asi také na \"-ho\"):\r\nroucho - rouchách (6.p.jedn.č. rouchu i roue)\r\nucho - uchách (duál uích!)\r\ntango - tangách\r\n(slovo zakončené na \"-ho\" mě nenapadá :-)\r\n\r\n\r\n', '2002-10-22 19:15:09', 715, 0),
(717, 13, 'john', 'jnottle@internode.on.net', 'Polednice', 'Does anyone know where I can find an English translation, on the Web, of Polednice (The Noon Witch) by J.Erben.', '2002-10-23 12:05:28', 0, 0),
(718, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: Polednice', 'web.telecom.cz/antoni/kytice/poled.htm', '2002-10-23 16:20:40', 717, 0),
(719, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: Polednice', 'Sorry, it is in Czech, of course. It seems the English version is unavailable.', '2002-10-23 16:30:53', 718, 0),
(720, 13, 'Lee', 'gurn11@aol.com', 'Translateion', 'Can some one please help me?  I would like to be able to say in Chech the following phrases:\r\n\r\nI love you.\r\nI like you very much.\r\nYou are very beautiful.', '2002-11-04 04:03:08', 0, 0),
(721, 13, 'Shawn', 'euroaddiction@hotmail.com', 'CD-Roms', 'This has to be the first constructive site on this language \r\nI have found. Is there a timeline for when the cd rom will be out from this site can anyone else reccomend books etc i can get in canada. I just married a czech girl in september and i DESPERATELY want to learn the language. I would really like to take the online course and many other courses if possible if people would e-mail me links etc that would be great. Thanks for you help and answers. ', '2002-11-04 10:00:38', 0, 0),
(722, 15, 'shawn', 'euroaddiction@hotmail.com', 'Re: A few tips to learn pronunciation.', 'Good tips scott I recently married a girl from Decin I myself am from canada i spend alot of time in czech republic and the alphabeta pronunciation has come second nature now only if i could learn the rest. I have a beginners book but i want something more than that. I am dying to find a proper cd-rom and i am hoping that this website will put out something that is totally interative to learn vocabulary...structure...conjugation everything !!! I need alot of help on this issue reading books just doesn\'t seem to stick in my head.....interactive learning has alway been better for me.', '2002-11-04 10:15:36', 676, 0),
(723, 15, 'shawn', 'euroaddiction@hotmail.com', 'Re: A few tips to learn pronunciation.', 'Good tips scott I recently married a girl from Decin I myself am from canada i spend alot of time in czech republic and the alphabeta pronunciation has come second nature now only if i could learn the rest. I have a beginners book but i want something more than that. I am dying to find a proper cd-rom and i am hoping that this website will put out something that is totally interative to learn vocabulary...structure...conjugation everything !!! I need alot of help on this issue reading books just doesn\'t seem to stick in my head.....interactive learning has alway been better for me.', '2002-11-04 10:40:04', 676, 0),
(724, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: A few tips to learn pronunciation.', 'Shawn,\r\nThat is awesome to know that I am not the only crazy \"North American\" marrying a Czech girl :). What I would suggest is that you get your wife to teach you.  Ask her questions about everything (if she is willing to answer).  That is the quickest way I have found to learn.  I also am trying to learn from a book and it has proven to help me learn grammer, but when it comes to the actual language I think it is much easier to be brave and use what you know and then ask questions about what you dont. Anything you see that you have heard a few times or anything that you are just curious about write down and then ask her what they mean.  Keep a list of these words and phrases and it will help you learn the every day language easier.\r\nGood luck and God Bless,\r\nScott.', '2002-11-04 10:47:05', 723, 0),
(725, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Translateion', 'Please excuse my keyboard not being able to use the Czech alphabet:\r\n\r\nI love you: Miluji te!\r\nYou are very beautiful: Ty jsi moc krasny!  \r\n\r\nI would write \'I like you very much\', but I am not completely sure how to say it.  I could take a guess, but I dont want to tell you something wrong.  Maybe someone who is farther along can help better.\r\n', '2002-11-04 11:04:10', 720, 0),
(726, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Translateion', 'I do believe it is said like this but someone might want to check me:\r\nLíbí se mi ty mockrat! \r\n\r\nSomeone please check my spelling. Thanks.', '2002-11-04 11:09:17', 725, 0),
(727, 13, 'Lee', '', 'Translate', 'Can someone help, please?\r\n\r\nCan you trnaslate from English to Czech:\r\n\r\nI love you.\r\nYou\'re so beautiful.\r\nYou\'re so pretty.\r\n\r\nThank You.\r\n\r\n', '2002-11-04 23:51:49', 0, 1),
(728, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Translateion', 'I`ll check you:\r\n\"Libí se mi mockrát\", but I think that the correct translation of the phrase \"I like you very much\" is \"Mám tě moc rád\"', '2002-11-05 02:57:55', 726, 1),
(729, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Translate', 'I love you: Miluji tě.\r\nYou are beautiful: Jsi krásná.\r\nYou are so pretty: Ty jsi ale roztomilá.\r\nBTW, other phrases you can find here: \r\nhttp://czech.h1.ru/razg.en.html\r\nhttp://czech.h1.ru/razg.en2.html', '2002-11-05 03:08:12', 727, 1),
(730, 13, 'peter', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'hard and soft nouns', 'I think I have understood that, gramatically in czech, all nouns are either hard (tvrdy) or soft (mekky).  And that consonants are either hard, soft or ambiguous (obojetne).  And what usually determines whether a noun is hard or soft is the character of the last consonant (hard or soft).  But what I can\'t work out is how you tell, or how you look up in a dictionary, whether a noun ending in an ambiguous consonant (b,f,l,m,p,s,v,z) is hard or soft.  For instance, \'l\' is obojetne, so how am I meant to work out that ucitel is a soft noun?  Can someone help?  Or have I completely misunderstood something?\r\n', '2002-11-07 05:11:25', 0, 0),
(731, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'jak se sklonuje \"privilegium\"?', 'jak se sklonuje \"privilegium\"? Vím, e ve druhém pádu je koncovka -gia, ve 2.pádu mn.č. končí se na -gií, a v 7.pádu mn.ě. má koncovku -gii. Co s ostatními pády? dik', '2002-11-07 12:10:42', 0, 1),
(732, 13, 'eva', '', 'Re: Sklonování podstatných jmen', 'dveře, dveří, dveřím, dveře, dveře, dveřích, dveřmi (vzor: růe)\r\npráce, práce, práci, práci, práce, práci, prácí (vzor: růe)\r\npeníze, peněz, penězích, peníze, peníze, penězích, penězi', '2002-11-07 15:23:32', 686, 0),
(733, 13, 'eva', '', 'Re: jak se sklonuje', '1.  privilegium     privilegia\r\n2.  privilegia      privilegií\r\n3.  privilegiu      privilegiím\r\n4.  privilegium     privilegia\r\n5.  privilegium     privilegia\r\n6.  privilegiu      privilegiích\r\n7.  privilegiem     privilegii', '2002-11-07 15:55:16', 731, 0),
(734, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: jak se sklonuje', 'Lovermanne, nedává ve kole pozor. U ses ptal na \"kritérium\" ;-)\r\n', '2002-11-07 18:02:40', 731, 0),
(735, 15, 'Francesco', 'francesc_fe@iol.it', 'Buying textbooks', 'Hi,\r\ncould anyone tell me if there are Czech on-line bookstores where I can purchase 1) Czech Step by Step (with workbook) and 2) Communicative Czech (Elementary and Intermediate)? It would be very kind if you could answer to my direct e-mail: francesc_fe@iol.it\r\nThank you', '2002-11-08 19:30:13', 0, 1),
(736, 15, 'Francesco', 'francesc_fe@iol.it', 'Buying textbooks', 'Hi,\r\ncould anyone tell me if there are Czech on-line bookstores where I can purchase 1) Czech Step by Step (with workbook) and 2) Communicative Czech (Elementary and Intermediate)? It would be very kind if you could answer to my direct e-mail: francesc_fe@iol.it\r\nThank you', '2002-11-08 19:46:33', 0, 1),
(737, 13, 'Jana', '', '', '', '2002-11-16 00:38:19', 0, 0),
(738, 13, 'Gris', '', 'Tranlate, prosim', 'How do you say \"I miss you\" in Czech?\r\nDekuji.\r\n', '2002-11-16 00:41:31', 0, 0),
(739, 14, 'Steven Nowak', 'smnowak@rocketmail.com', 'the town of Chlumany', 'I\'m interested to know if there is anyone who will be able to help me \r\nwith any information regarding the town of Chlumany,in Prachatice region, which is where my Great-Grandmother\'s family came from in 1907.  The family name is Tvrzicky and I would just like to know something about the town that my anscestors came from.  Also I would like to know if there are any cultural traditions that are particular to that region of Czech Republic.\r\nI have tried numerous times to contact the mayor of that city, and haven\'t received any reply. ', '2002-11-16 03:40:22', 0, 0),
(740, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Tranlate, prosim', 'Stýská se mi po tobě.', '2002-11-16 05:09:27', 738, 1),
(741, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'sklonování', 'Pomůe mi někdo. Rád bych věděl, jak se sklonuje slovo rukojmí. Nemůu najít ádný vzor, podle kterého bych sám to udělal. Dík.', '2002-11-16 05:13:17', 0, 0),
(742, 13, 'lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'sloveso plácat', 'Jestli rozuím tomu slovu správně: Je to mluvit nesmysly, vanit?', '2002-11-16 05:23:29', 0, 1),
(743, 13, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: Tranlate, prosim', 'I am not the best authority on Czech but I think \'chybis mi tu\', literally you are missing to me here, you could use vsude (everwhere) instead of tu (here). Sorry don\'t have czech keyboard on aol so both S\'s have haceks missing (as does the hacek). ', '2002-11-17 04:39:49', 738, 0),
(744, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: skloňování - rukojmí', 'Rukojmí je buď mu. rodu: rukojmí, rukojmího, rukojmímu ... (jako Jiří)\r\nnebo stř. rodu (jako stavení).', '2002-11-18 14:19:45', 741, 0),
(745, 13, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: sloveso plácat', 'Ano, v přeneseném významu (obrazně): plácat (nesmysly), plácnout (nesmysl)\r\n\r\nJinak: plácat = chlopa, lëpa, pleska\r\n       plácnout = chlopnu, ...', '2002-11-18 14:41:37', 742, 0),
(746, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: sloveso plácat', 'Jen tak na okraji: také zajímavé je <i>plácat se v tom</i>, znamená to topit se, jednat bez kladného výsledku. Příklady:\r\n\r\nhttp://216.239.39.100/search?q=cache:ERjGWQv7fPkC:stupid.cz/+%22pl%C3%A1c%C3%A1m+se+v+tom%22&hl=en&ie=UTF-8\r\n\"Občas se člověku stane, e potká někoho komu celkem bezmylenkovitě dá naprostou větinu svého já. No a taky se občas stane, e se po čase zjistí e to nebyla ta pravá osoba, co je zjitění moc moc nepříjemné, úměrně době kterou jste s dotyčnou osobou byl. Pak přijde doba kdy se hádají city s rozumem. U mě po čase zvítězil rozum a celkem dobře se mi ho dařilo podporovat. No a pak přila doba, kdy se provalila jakási hráz mlčení a ze vech stran se na mě začala valit minulost. Jedním s těchto \"útoků\" jsou i tyto fotky, které jsem cvaknul půjčeným přístrojem, vrátil i s ním a po nějaké době jsem zapomněl. O to zákeřnějí bylo kdy se ke mě po několika letech dostaly, probudily vzpomínky a náladu tehdejích dnů, zasáhly a bodaly. No a plácám se v tom znova, naprosto nepřipraven čelit útokům.\"\r\n\r\n    Nevím, jak začít. Problémů mám víc, ale myslím, e spolu úzce souvisejí.\r\n\r\nhttp://www.ccsystem.cz/duvera/od_2001.htm\r\nMám problémy se svým sebevědomím. Nevím proč, ale poslední dobou je to čím dál horí. Nevěřím si u skoro v ničem. Vdycky jsem byla spí nesmělejí, ale teď mám chvíle, kdy si nevěřím vůbec v ničem. Mám tudí problémy ve kole, ale i s navazováním a udrením si přátelství. \r\nMám často deprese (někdy i denně), kterých se nedokáu zbavit. Střídavě si nadávám a střídavě se lituju. \r\nA taky jsem straně odtrená od světa, cítím se straně sama. Nemám skoro ádné přátele a ty, co mám, snadno ztrácím. Teď například bojuju o svou nejlepí kámoku. Je na mě ze záhadného důvodu natvaná. Teda vím o spoustě důvodů. Jene který je ten pravý??? \r\nJsem často nemocná, podle taky mi chybí pohyb a čerstvý vzduch, jene jít mezi lidi se nemůu přinutit. Mám vdycky pocit, e na mě zírají jako na debila. Teď jsem právě viděla film \"Návrat idiota\" a mám pocit, jako by to bylo o mně. Ale zpět k těm nemocem. Jsem silný alergik, mám často záněty dutin nebo průduek, chřipky a nachlazení. Jak říká mamka, stačí kdy mě ofoukne a já hned marodím. \r\n    Vím, e to zní asi trochu scestně, ale plácám se v tom u asi 3 roky a nevím, jak ven. ', '2002-11-20 21:44:39', 742, 0),
(747, 13, 'Dippie', 'jan@hotmail.com', 'vertaling', 'how do you translate:\r\n\r\nI\'m sorry pretty girl, but you are the girl of my dreams.', '2002-11-26 00:49:59', 0, 0),
(748, 13, 'dippie', 'jan@hotmail.com', 'Re: vertaling = translation', 'that\'s all\r\n\r\nvertaling = translating. thank you', '2002-11-26 00:51:45', 747, 0),
(749, 13, 'Prins', 'dewannes@hotmail.com', 'translation please', 'Death is in a small cornor.\r\n\r\n\r\nthanks', '2002-11-26 01:59:17', 0, 1),
(750, 13, 'kevin', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: translation please', '', '2002-11-26 05:22:53', 749, 0),
(751, 13, 'fred', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: translation please', 'Should that be corner or coroner', '2002-11-26 05:25:10', 750, 0),
(752, 13, 'prins', 'dewannes@hotmail.com', 'Re: translation please', 'it should be corner.  (french = coin    dutch= hoek)', '2002-11-26 23:42:00', 751, 0),
(753, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: translation please', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nnot sure why you\'d want to say that, but translated word for word (by me, a non-native-speaker), it\'s:\r\n\r\nSmrt je v malém koutě.\r\n\r\nIf you are trying to say something metaphorical, you might also want to provide the plain-English sentence for what you\'re trying to express--this would improve the translation.', '2002-11-28 17:44:49', 749, 1),
(754, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@NOSPAMsky.cz', 'Re: vertaling', '(Why is it 3/4 of the posts here ask for translations of pick-up lines and love letters?)\r\n\r\nLituji, holčičko hezká, ale jsi holkou mých snů.\r\n\r\nSounds rather strange, but that can be charming some of the time...\r\n\r\nErik', '2002-11-28 17:47:17', 747, 0),
(755, 13, 'Prins', 'dewannes@hotmail.com', 'translation', 'hi\r\n\r\ncan somebody please translate this sentence? I\'m inlove with this lovely woman and i like to tell her this in 7 different languages:\r\n\r\nIf I could give you as much money as love, \r\nyou could buy the whole planet.\r\n\r\n\r\nthank you', '2002-11-30 20:59:33', 0, 1),
(756, 15, 'Gene Attwood', 'gene@genesenglish.com', 'Re: Buying textbooks', 'Hi, you can find  both of these titles at amazon.co.uk\r\ngood hunting.\r\n', '2002-12-01 01:25:17', 735, 0),
(757, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: vertaling', 'I think it is because people have nothing better to do than trying be sly and tell there girlfriends in every language imaginable that they love them.  My question is - with no offense to anyone - why do some people ask for a traslation into Czech, when they use terrible English to ask the question. You would think that they would learn to speak English (atleast to a near correct extent) before they try to learn words in another language? Go Figure!\r\n\r\n-Scott', '2002-12-03 01:00:26', 754, 0),
(758, 15, 'Hil', 'Rhenium3@yahoo.com', 'When?', 'Just wondering when this site will actually be open for courses!  I\'m so eager to learn, I think this will be perfect. Plus, what will the cost be?  geez, I wanna learn! Arg!\r\n', '2002-12-08 01:55:35', 0, 0),
(759, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotnmail.com', 'Re: translation', 'Hi,\r\nThe translation for your lucky lady:\r\n\r\nKdybych Ti mohl dat tolik penez jako lasky,\r\nmohla bys si koupit celou planetu.\r\n\r\n(sorry, don\'t have Czech keyboard/alphabet here but I\'m sure she\'ll get it anyway)', '2002-12-08 18:30:42', 755, 0),
(760, 15, 'Mike', 'kubanekm@email.cz', 'Re: A few tips to learn pronunciation.', 'Hi\r\n\r\nI have dropped into this page only by accident whenn looking for some material for my exam in linguistics. I am a Czech student stduiyng English at the Palacky University in Olomouc. I find the idea of helping people to learn Czech very nice. To this topic, there is nothing else to add. Scott has said almost everything. Be brave, try to speak as much as possible, get to contact with other people - chatting in a typical Czech pub could bring you a good deal of information and new skills. GOOD LUCK\r\nMike', '2002-12-09 14:06:03', 724, 0),
(761, 15, 'Urszula Skrzekut', 'ulaska2@poczta.onet.pl', 'seeking a teacher of Czech', 'I am an English teacher wishing to learn Czech language.As I am a Polish woman of origin ,living very close to Czech border ( near KRNOV)I am looking for  a person able to help me learning Czech from this very area.I can help with English in exchange.\r\nThank you,\r\nUrszula Skrzekut', '2002-12-09 16:23:04', 0, 0),
(762, 13, 'matilda', 'handleykevinka@aol.com', 'Re: vertaling', 'To be fair Scott a lot of the writers are not English as indeed you are not. Correct spelling in English english is offence (at least i hope it is), none intended of course. I think as the site is generally in english, people try to write in it, albeit sometimes poorly. They are actually doing as you might say \"pretty good\" considering. Anyway whats vertaling mean.', '2002-12-17 03:07:01', 757, 0),
(763, 14, 'Nadka', 'nadak@volny.cz', 'Re: the town of Chlumany', 'Lets try Tourist Information Centre in Prachatice - they should be able to tell you more about cities and towns in Prachatice area, I suppose.\r\n\r\nInformační centrum\r\nVelké nám. 1, 383 01 Prachatice\r\nTel. +420 388312563, 388310589, Fax. +420 388312563\r\ne-mail: icentrum@prachatice-info.cz\r\nhttp://www.prachatice.cz\r\n', '2002-12-17 21:33:18', 739, 0),
(764, 13, 'Da Prins', 'dewannes@hotmail.com', 'translation for newjyear', 'can someone please translate:\r\n\r\n\r\nHappy newyear to all my friends.\r\n\r\n\r\nthanks', '2002-12-31 20:33:40', 0, 1),
(765, 13, 'Michal Belda', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: translation for newjyear', 'astný nový rok vem mým přátelům.', '2003-01-01 16:06:06', 764, 0),
(766, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'New Year, New Plans', 'Veselé vánoce a astný nový rok: <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/2003>http://www.bohemica.com/2003</a>.\r\n\r\nI hope the the year 2003 will bring you a lot of happiness and finally a real online course on bohemica.com. Plans are afoot.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-01-02 02:10:18', 0, 0),
(767, 13, 'Lisa Varin', 'Varin1925@aol.com', 'Need translation please', 'I have a beautifully embroidered pillow case from my Grandmother that has the following text on it.  Pozdrav z Vlasti  If I am correct it reads Greetings from Vlasti.  I do not know what Vlasti means.  Is it a person name?  Sure hope someone can help me.  Thank you.', '2003-01-02 07:34:07', 0, 1),
(768, 13, 'shawn s', 'crazy_horse69@msn.com', 'need to know info of language', 'i am doing a report on czech rep.. i had ancestors that lived in prague. I need to know more about the language of cz rep.  I only know that the language itself is called czech. Does anyone know anything else?? if you do... please email me at crazy_horse69@msn.com', '2003-01-03 03:31:46', 0, 1),
(769, 15, 'Fiona Macleod', 'fmacleod@eastman.com', 'Looking for a teacher in teeside', 'Hi,\r\nI am looking for Czech lessons/simple conversation from \r\na native Czech speaker in the Teesside area (Darlington). Eastman, my employer, has a large site in the Czech Republic and I work with several \r\nengineers there. I speak some Russian (rusty). No need to be a \r\nqualified teacher, just a patient native speaker. \r\nThanks \r\nFiona \r\n \r\n', '2003-01-03 03:34:13', 0, 0),
(770, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Need translation please', 'vlast = fatherland, country, patria\r\n\r\n(vlasti is genitive case)\r\n', '2003-01-03 16:11:16', 767, 0),
(771, 13, 'Megan', 'loserloserdoubleloser@msn.com', 'report at school', 'Hi,\r\n\r\n', '2003-01-03 19:22:19', 0, 0),
(772, 13, 'lovermann', '', 'Re: need to know info of language', 'I don`t understand people like you. Is it hard to find information about the language in internet or you want to get all info on your mail without trying to find by yourself? Look up via Google, I`m conveinced that you`ll get a lot of links.', '2003-01-04 22:35:35', 768, 0),
(773, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: need to know info of language', 'Try <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/reference/czechtrivia/czech_trivia.htm>This link on Bohemica.com</a>. It should answer most basic questions. There are also some essays you might be interested in on <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline>http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline</a>.', '2003-01-05 00:45:26', 768, 0),
(774, 13, 'Da Prins', 'dewannes@hotmail.com', 'translation pls', '\r\n\r\nI love you from the day we met. Do you love me too?\r\n\r\nthank you.', '2003-01-05 20:13:24', 0, 1),
(775, 13, 'Mike', '', 'Re: translation pls', 'Miluju tě ode dne, kdy jsme se setkali. Miluje mě také?\r\n\r\nyou are welcome', '2003-01-07 03:18:15', 774, 1),
(776, 15, 'Ales', 'aljosa@freemail.ru', 'Re: Looking for a teacher in teeside', 'Hi Fiona,\r\nhow can I help you? I am native Moravien who know czech language.', '2003-01-10 01:54:11', 769, 1),
(777, 13, 'Pwyll', 'gaelbhach@minitel.net', '\"See you tomorrow\"', '\r\nHow would a Czech say \"see you tomorrow\" ?\r\nIs there some sepcific expression for to say \"sleep well\" or sthg like that, other than \"dobrou noc\" ?\r\n\r\nDe^kuji\r\n\r\nPwyll, králík Anic^ky :-)', '2003-01-10 04:05:54', 0, 1),
(778, 13, 'Pwyll', '', 'Re: translation', 'I can give it in Breton, in Irish and in Scottish gaelic if u want:\r\nbreton :  \"mar gellahen roi&#241; dit kement ag argant ha ma r&#224;n karantez,\r\ne c\'hellahes preni&#241; ar blanedenn a-bezh.\"\r\n\r\nirish: \"dá mb\'fhéidir liom a oiread airgid a thabhairt duit agus a bheirim grá, thiocfadh leat an pláinéad seo go léir a cheannacht.\"\r\n\r\nscottish gaelic: \"nam burrainn dhomh uiread airgid a thoirt dhut agus a bheir mi gaol, burrainn dhutsa n phlanaid gu léir a cheannachd.\"\r\n\r\n\r\n(you should write it to your beloved but don\'t try to pronounce it, u\'d never manage to guess how a native speaker would pronounce if u don\'t hear any...)\r\nGood luck\r\n\r\nPwyll\r\n\r\n', '2003-01-10 04:27:07', 755, 0),
(779, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: c u 2morow', 'Basically, there is no direct equivalent (at least one I knew of) but if you are quite formal: Naschledanou (no time ref, if you need you could add: Uvidime se zitra = we\'ll see each other tomorrow). If this is more for friends you could abreviate it to: Tak zitra.\r\n\"Dobrou noc\" is the most common but \"Dobre se vyspi\" = \"sleep well\" is used as well.\r\nProsim.\r\nJana', '2003-01-10 20:05:00', 777, 0),
(780, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'phatic language', 'Hi,\r\nHas someone done/seen a comparison or a study on the differences in use of phatic language (English vs Czech)? \r\nI\'ve searched the net without much luck and I don\'t find it very easy to get my hands on Czech related materials here in London (I was sooo looking forward to this assignment but now I seem to got stuck, somehow).\r\nThanks a lot,\r\nJana', '2003-01-10 20:12:25', 0, 1),
(781, 13, 'scott', '', 'Re: New Year, New Plans', 'Thanks for all the hard work you guys have been doing Dominik! Over the last year and a half this site has helped my studies so much. It is just a big encouragement, if nothing else, to know that there are other people who are trying to tackle this feat of learning Czech. \r\nHave a great year!\r\nNaschla\r\nScott', '2003-01-11 02:35:00', 766, 0),
(782, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: vertaling', 'You are right, Matilda, about the use of English on this site. I suppose I never thought that some of the people were not native English speakers, thus resulting in bad spelling and grammar. In one sense, however, I am English. I was born in England and grew up for the first 5 years of my life speaking \"British\" English before I moved to America. Oh, and yes offence is also a correct spelling. Both \'offense\' and \'offence\' are variations of the word. Sorry that I tackled the whole situation a little briskly. I like how you distinguished that we Americans would say \'pretty good\'. I suppose I never have heard it said by any of my English friends or family :). Anyway, have a great day and good luck in your studies.\r\nScott', '2003-01-11 02:50:49', 762, 0),
(783, 13, 'Ken Vesely', 'kdvnkv@aol.com', 'Received from CR', 'Soubory jsou poipojeny a poipraveny k odesiani s touto zpravou.  (The o\'s in the two words starting with poip have a / through them.)', '2003-01-13 05:12:40', 0, 0),
(784, 13, 'Mike', '', 'Re: Received from CR', 'There should be probably letter ř instead of o. Is there any other problem?', '2003-01-13 14:48:34', 783, 0),
(785, 13, 'Jimmy', '', 'Translation Please', 'Hey sugar, I\'m good. I\'m trying to come back to Prague in the summer, see you then hopefully.\r\n\r\n\r\nThanks to anyone who can help!', '2003-01-13 20:10:23', 0, 0),
(786, 13, 'nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation Please', '\r\nAhoj drahouku, mám se dobře. V létě se pokusím přijet zpět do Prahy a\r\ndoufám, e se uvidíme.', '2003-01-14 21:21:59', 785, 0),
(787, 13, 'nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Received from CR', 'This is a message automaticaly generated by the email program your Czech partner uses when any enclosure is involved. The message says: \"the files are ready to be attached and send together with this (email) message\"', '2003-01-14 21:24:39', 783, 0),
(788, 14, 'Sarah', 'sarah_shoeshine@yahoo.com', 'Expat Czech Novel Published', 'Hi...I just finished an excellent expat novel set in the Czech town of Plzen, and thought that others who have visited Bohemia might like to know that it is out there, and filled with a whole host of crazy characters and drinking holes like \"The Devil\'s Happy Lap.\" All the things that make us visitors so attracted to the great Slavic country. You can find it at: \r\n\r\nhttp://www.burnhillwolf.com/bookgallery\r\n\r\nSarah', '2003-01-17 04:14:30', 0, 0),
(789, 14, 'Sarah', 'sarah_shoeshine@yahoo.com', 'Re: Expat Czech Novel Published', 'Ooops, I forgot to tell you the title. It\'s called Steffi\'s Club!', '2003-01-17 04:16:55', 788, 0),
(790, 13, 'Sandi', 'sfigster@aol.com', 'writing', 'Could you please tell me how to write home sweet home in czech language?', '2003-01-18 03:10:18', 0, 1),
(791, 13, 'Mike', '', 'Re: writing', 'I think we use almost literal equivalent: Domove, sladký domove', '2003-01-19 02:43:56', 790, 0),
(792, 14, 'Christoph', 'kupitiaulaya@yahoo.fr', 'czech partners for European cultural project', 'Currently I am preparing a cultural project for a French organisation. It is supposed to take place in 2004 in 8 European countries - also in the Czech Republic. The idea is, roughly,  a portrait of European cultural landscape between Torun and Toledo by 200-300 different authors. Therefore I am looking for contacts to interested organisations or groups in each country to deal with the organisation. Czech Republic and Poland are particular difficult for me, as I cannot speak the languages (which is also limiting the possibilities of internet research, off course). More information available by sending back an e-mail to kupitiaulaya@yahoo.fr   Every reference is very welcome!\r\n\r\nbest regards', '2003-01-21 14:41:04', 0, 1),
(793, 13, 'Cindy', 'dane3@charter.net', 'help!', 'My boyfriend is from Czech Republic and I\'m from the USA. His parents are coming to the states in about 6 weeks. I hardly know the language and they don\'t know any English.  I will be with them for several days by myself to entertain them while my boyfriend is on a business trip.  What suggestions does anyone have on a dictionary or book that I can buy that can help me communicate with them. Also would it be a good idea for them to get a Czech to English dictionary to bring with them? Does anyone know anything about translators and how well they work. I would be willing to spend the money for one as I will be going there this summer for several week.  Thanks!!', '2003-01-26 02:18:07', 0, 0),
(794, 13, 'John', 'jnottle@internode.on.net', 'Translation please', 'If anyone has the time I would be very greatful for a translation of the following poem sent to me by a friend in a Christmas card.\r\n(Sorry, my computer can\'t do inflections.)\r\nHodiny tise naslapuji\r\n   ve snehu,\r\nstaci par kroku-rok\r\n   se uzuvira...\r\nSkonci cas starych lasek,\r\n   starych pribehu,\r\n   rodi se predsevzeti\r\n   pruzracna a cira.\r\n\r\nSpokojene vanocni svatky\r\n   a mnoho stesti\r\n  v novem roce preje\r\n ', '2003-01-26 12:32:49', 0, 0),
(795, 13, 'Nikki Patnode', 'nikkipatnode1515@hotmail.com', 'Czech Dress', 'Hi, My name is Nikki.  I am 50% Czech and I am curious as to what Czech people wear.  I am trying out for Miss Czech of Minnesota and I need to wear something that shows culture.  I have a dress that my grandma made (she is part of a czech singer\'s group in New Prague,MN) but I am curious what younger people my age (21 yrs old) wear.  Is their clothing Americanized like some other European cultures or is it their own designs?  Is there a place online or in books that I can see images of traditional clothing styles and contemporary styles of younger people?   Thank you!\r\nNikki Patnode  ', '2003-01-27 09:35:43', 0, 1),
(796, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation please', 'John, I\'m not quite sure what the right balance between literary and \'meaning\' translation is (poems are rather difficult, I think) but I\'ll try to make sense. Pls let me know if you wand more precision.J.\r\n\r\nHodiny tise naslapuji     Hours are treading quietly\r\nve snehu,                 in snow,\r\nstaci par kroku-rok       few steps is enough - the year\r\nse uzuvira...             is closing...\r\nSkonci cas starych lasek, Ends the time of old loves (meaning relationshios),\r\nstarych pribehu,          old stories,\r\nrodi se predsevzeti       resolutions are being born\r\npruzracna a cira.         clear and pure.\r\n\r\nSpokojene vanocni svatky  Content Christmass Holidays (= Merry Christmass)\r\na mnoho stesti            and losts of happiness (stesti=1happines,2good luck) \r\nv novem roce preje        in the New Year from\r\n', '2003-01-27 18:40:32', 794, 0),
(797, 13, 'j', '', 'Re: Czech Dress', '', '2003-01-27 18:41:03', 795, 0),
(798, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: help!', 'Hi Cindy,\r\nRule 1: do not panic! \r\nRule 2: pen and paper is a necessity!\r\nRule 3: be creative! You\'ll be amased what you can communicate without words!\r\nI personally would not suspect anyone to go traveling without a proper dictionary but they might be relying on your boyfriend to interpret - tiring for him, frustrating for you. Make sure you have at least a pocket dictionary and some clue about Czech phonology - it will help you with spelling, especially vowels can be confusing.\r\nAs for a translator, my dad gave me one once (I was in similar situation to yours, his German wife spoke no Czech, I spoke no German), well to cut the story short: confusing, impractical, user unfriendly = waste of time and money.\r\nOne more think, language is one thing but culture is another - if they haven\'t traveled much, expect culture differences (maybe shock). Most of the comments on this web are quite good indication, although as any generalisation need to be taken with a bit of flexibility.\r\nLet me know if you need some more specific info.\r\nGood luck!\r\nJana', '2003-01-27 19:11:10', 793, 0),
(799, 14, 'Ingrid', 'ingrid.olsson@donnergymnasiet.se', 'youth cultural exchange', 'Hi!\r\nI\'m a Swedish girl aged 18 who are working with my friend on a youth cultural exchange between students in Prague and students on Gotland (an swedish island in the baltic sea)We are going to applyfor money so that we hopefully can make a trip to Prague in the beginning of May-03. But we need a group of students aged 16-18 living in Prague that we can have this exchange with. \r\n   We want this two groups of students to get together and learn to know each other and the different culturals. We are interested in the similarities and the differences between being young in Prague and being young on Gotland. \r\n  If anyone is interested or have any suggestion of who to contact please write me back...this is a serious project and we need a serious partner.', '2003-01-27 23:56:07', 0, 1),
(800, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: introductory Czech', 'All the things you mention are good. A small tourist dictionary and an English/Czech phrasebook will be of help. You can find some through <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/bookstore>http://www.bohemica.com/bookstore</a>.\r\n\r\nYou can also find some introductory Czech here\r\n<a href=http://www.bohemica.com/czechintro>http://www.bohemica.com/czechintro</a>.\r\n\r\nYou can also find some online dictionaries and info about the language in the Links section on this site.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-01-28 01:14:28', 793, 0),
(801, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Czech Dress', 'Well, it\'s as you guessed. Young people dress in the globalized manner. A really \"hip\" person will be able to spot many differences. Try http://www.expats.cz. Someone might be able to help you there.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-01-28 01:25:31', 795, 0),
(802, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Czech Dress', 'I am not sure if there is much about traditional Bohemian dress, but I bet if you were to try a search for traditional Valachian or Moravian dress that you would get many results.  It is pretty neat, and in Moravia they are still into dressing in this traditional dress for special occassions and events.  Good Luck!\r\n\r\nScott', '2003-01-28 03:24:32', 795, 0),
(803, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: help!', 'One of the best and most useful Anglicko-česky, česko-anglický dictionaries that I have found online is www.wordbook.cz . I think it will be of some help. It also has access to the Czech alphabet for those who do not have a Czech keyboard. \r\ntěstí = good luck!!!\r\nScott', '2003-01-28 03:37:53', 793, 0),
(804, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Capitalization', 'Dobre den,\r\nI have finally gotten a czech keyboard and need to ask one question. How do I capitalize the accented letters that are on the numbers row? Also, where is the @ symbol located on the czech keyboard?  Děkuju mockrát!!\r\nHezky den,\r\nScott', '2003-01-28 03:45:28', 0, 0),
(805, 15, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re: Capitalization', 'Hi, you will have to switch keyboard back to english to write @.\r\n\r\nAnd to capitalize accented letters you must use a tiny \"hack\" -> switch to czech keyboard, press + (shift and =, not + on numeric keyboard!), and then e.g. C - you get Č (Ř, , , Ď, , Ň and Ů). This applies to consonants. For vowels press just = and then A to get Á etc..', '2003-01-28 05:39:37', 804, 0),
(806, 13, 'john', 'jnottle@internode.on.net', 'Re: Translation please', 'Dear Jana,\r\nThank you very much for the translation. Your interpretation makes the poem, and it\'s sense, understandable to me.\r\nJohn', '2003-01-28 11:33:00', 796, 0),
(807, 15, 'Vladimír', 'bohaemus@email.cz', 'Re: Capitalization', 'You need not to switch keyboard back to ENGLISH.\r\n\r\nTo write \"@\" press right ALT (\"Alt Gr\" on IBM keyboard) + \"v\"\r\n\r\n  \\     AltGr+q\r\n  |     AltGr+w\r\n  @     AltGr+v\r\n  #     AltGr+x\r\n  $     AltGr+ů\r\n  [     AltGr+f\r\n  ]     AltGr+g\r\n  {     AltGr+b\r\n  }     AltGr+n  etc.\r\n\r\nIf you have got an original IBM keyboard (with IBM logo) with Czech layout, you can see these chars on the keys.\r\nYou can also write all East European Latin letters, e.g. ß Ł Đ ż   š ę ş ţ ă.\r\n\r\n', '2003-01-28 21:24:06', 805, 0),
(808, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Capitalization', 'Super! Děkuju pro tvůj pomoc! ', '2003-01-29 10:20:20', 804, 0),
(809, 13, 'Robert', 'rass445@aol.com', 'Books in Czech', 'I am trying to find a copy of Kundera\'s The Unbearable Lightness of Being (Nesnesitelna Lehkost Byti) in Czech language.  I understand it was originally published in Czech by a Canadian publisher, but that it is now out of print. A bookseller told me Kundera refuses to reprint the novel in Cz, although I don\'t understand why.  I live in the US.  Can anyone give me leads on how I could buy this book?  I couldn\'t even find it in Prague bookstores last month.  Might there be used bookstores in Prague where it could be found? Or, are there online book dealers who might have it?\r\nThanks.', '2003-02-01 18:53:11', 0, 1),
(810, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Kundera in Czech', 'Kundera does indeed refuse to allow the book to be reprinted in CZ; he had various conflicts in this country and, perhaps out of spite, has not allowed reprints for some years now. The early books were published in Czech, but I have strong doubts about Unbearable Lightness.\r\n\r\nErik Piper', '2003-02-05 19:38:15', 809, 0),
(811, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: writing', 'Actually, I\'ve never heard \"Domov, sladký domov\" here. I would suggest the nearest equivalent phrase, \"Vude dobře, doma nejlíp\" ([Life is] fine everywhere, [but it\'s] best at home).', '2003-02-05 19:43:22', 790, 0),
(812, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: phatic language', 'I\'m not sure how easy it is to access the archives of Lidove noviny, but I know they published an article a couple of years back that, while it didn\'t mention phatic language specifically, definitely explored that very topic. One of the most interesting statements was that English is less open to negative or even non-enthusiastic responses to questions like \"How are you doing?\" than Czech is. There wasn\'t much support for the argument, if I recall correctly, but it\'s interesting enough on its own.\r\n\r\nI\'d guess I\'d also note that phatic language is more perhaps clearly divided into formal/informal sets in Czech than in, at least, US English. (E.g. it is almost shocking in some circumstances in the US to address someone - which I guess would be phatic - by last name.)', '2003-02-05 19:52:42', 780, 1),
(813, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'XP-Languages', 'I am currently trying to install Czech fonts on an XP operating system, but I cannot find the language options under the keyboard as usual, and the search revealed no results. Can someone who has done so on XP please assist me.\r\nDekuju a hezky den,\r\nScott', '2003-02-06 01:33:33', 0, 0),
(814, 14, 'Caro', 'caro_o@juno.com', 'Please help me!', 'I need a page where I can find information on Kost Castle or a project\r\nbefore or after 1930. I need it in English.\r\n\r\nThank you, \r\nCaro', '2003-02-06 05:25:53', 0, 0),
(815, 14, 'paul meder', 'cookinla@dellepro.com', 'Napoleonic history', 'Hello,\r\n  I am trying to contact any Czech organization which is looking for volunteer assistance in the research and recovery of Napoleonic era artifacts.  Please contact me.\r\n  Paul,', '2003-02-06 09:46:11', 0, 1),
(816, 14, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Please help me!', 'These may help,\r\n\r\nhttp://fmv.vse.cz/cz/castles/kost.htm\r\n\r\nhttp://www.czechcastles.com/kost.asp\r\n\r\nhttp://oasis.fortunecity.com/ozarks/170/desk.htm\r\n\r\nhttp://www.thomasgraz.net/gl-534.htm\r\n\r\nhttp://www.zamky-hrady.cz/2/kost-e.htm\r\n\r\nhttp://www.infolbc.cz/en/okoli/hzte.html\r\n\r\nThese were all found on google.com.  If you want more try going to a search engine and typing in Kost Castle.', '2003-02-07 01:39:58', 814, 0),
(817, 15, 'kevin', '', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'Scott, I don\'t have XP although its on my wifes laptop but thats away at the moment. However I can\'t recall any problem swopping languages though its a czech version of XP. I wonder if all you need to do is download czech menu\'s etc from windows update page, might be worth ', '2003-02-09 18:52:26', 813, 0),
(818, 15, 'scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'Thanks! That might work, but what is interesting to me is that with every other OS all you have to do is go keyboard in the control panel and the option of languages is there. As far as I can tell it is not with XP (unless of course I missed it). I tried to search for language properties, but the only thing that was sent back was the Microsoft Office language selector.  So,I am not sure....', '2003-02-09 22:55:48', 817, 0),
(819, 13, 'Da Prins', 'dewannes@hotmail.com', 'translation please', 'can someone please translate this poem. thank you\r\n\r\nyou are my sun, warm on the inside and beautiful on the outside.\r\n\r\nthanks', '2003-02-11 02:19:52', 0, 1),
(820, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: translation please', 'you are my sun = jsi me slunce,\r\nwarm on the inside = ??? zevnitr (what do you mean by \'warm\'? - kind? gentle? friendly? neither of those? in Czech \'warm\' would most likely relate either to temperature or, in slang, to homosexuality)\r\nand beautiful on the outside = a krasna (for female, \'krasny\' for male)zvenci\r\n(This is why I admire anyone who is brave enough to go into poerty translation.)\r\nPls, let me know what exact message are you trying to convey for unambiguous translation.\r\nJana', '2003-02-11 21:33:28', 819, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(821, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: phatic language', 'Thanks, Erik!\r\nI\'ve had to hand it in already but thanks anyway - I might use it for my thesis later.\r\nPls let me have our e-m address in case you are interested in my findings (it hasn\'t been a big project and I did my best to avoid mixing UK with US, both linguistically and culturally, so i\'m not sure how relevant it might be to you)\r\nPlease do let me know if you happened to come across more materials (I mean Czech - UK cultural/linguistic comparisons in general, I suspect the phatic concepts are going to be one of the major differences but there will be others as well - proxemics, spacial references, handling time and formality in relationships and many more I can\'t think of yet - just starting the research now).\r\nThanks a lot!\r\nJana', '2003-02-11 21:47:10', 812, 0),
(822, 13, 'da prins', '', 'Re: translation please', 'i\'m sorry, with warm i meen \"hot\". warm is the word in dutch', '2003-02-11 23:46:54', 820, 0),
(823, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: translation please', 'This may help a little.  As Jana said it sounds kind of funny to say \'warm\' on the inside in Czech.  You might want to say something like bright and warming or just bright.', '2003-02-12 01:45:01', 822, 0),
(824, 13, 'Da Prins', '', 'Re: translation please', 'yeah more like that, it\'s a poem for a girl and i want to tell her how much i really love her and how I see her. (you\'re like the sun, warm (bright) on the inside (so i meen in the body, in her mind, with her feelings and stuff like that) and beautiful on the outside, because it is a very attractive girl.\r\n\r\nthanks', '2003-02-12 03:55:04', 823, 0),
(825, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: translation please', 'Ok, I understand what you mean but to be honest I don\'t know how to say it in Czech. The closest I can get would be:\r\nJsi jako slunce =you\'re like the sun\r\nzaris vnitrni krasou =you shine with your inner beauty\r\na osnujes vnejsi =and daze with outer (beauty implied)\r\n(rather imprecise but with romance, I think)\r\nj.', '2003-02-12 17:44:32', 824, 0),
(826, 13, 'Ulla', 'ulla_82@yahoo.com', 'OBECNA CESTINA', 'Hi\r\nI\'d be very grateful if You can give me e-addresses of OBECNA CESTINA (oops..unfortunatelly there is no Czech alphabet on my computer)\r\nthx', '2003-02-14 15:49:10', 0, 0),
(827, 13, 'Barbara', 'sevilleb@isecfdeo-emh1.army.mil', 'History', 'I would like to know what this means - jsa biskupem holomucky.\r\nMy name in English is Holomuzki.  My grandfather\'s name was holomucky or holamucky.  I found this phrase in a history paper, in the year 1597.  I would truely like to know what this means and find out if this is a name with a title or greeting?\r\n\r\nTHANKS\r\nBarbara', '2003-02-14 19:53:18', 0, 1),
(828, 13, 'Crazy4Horses', 'haflingergirl@hotmail.com', 'Translation (Czech to English)', 'Hi everyone, I was wondering if anyone could translate this for me, thanks:\r\n\r\n\"Ty koniku, Ty mysaku, prijedu brzo!\"\r\n\r\nthanks, Kyanna', '2003-02-15 10:55:46', 0, 0),
(829, 14, 'Paul', '', 'Czech Legend', 'On my last visit to Prague is saw a strange statue in a shopping arcade and would like to know more about it. It is located in an arcade called \'Lucerna\' that is on Vodichkova just off Wenceslas Sqaure. It depicts a knight on horse- back. However, the strange thing is that the horse is upside down! Why? I\'m told it portrays a legend. Can anyone help?\r\n\r\n', '2003-02-15 17:55:47', 0, 0),
(830, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: History', 'As I suppose, Holomicky can be the old form of olomoucký. Olomoucký = adjective of the word Olomouc - a town in Czech rep.  Maybe your grandfather was a biskup in Olomouc .. ', '2003-02-15 19:16:41', 827, 1),
(831, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', 'lovermann@seznam.cz', 'Re: Translation (Czech to English)', 'The meaning is something like that:\r\n\"Hi, dude, I`ll come soon\"\r\n:)))', '2003-02-15 19:18:29', 828, 1),
(832, 13, 'Crazy4Horses', 'haflingergirl@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation (Czech to English)', 'Thanks Dmitry!', '2003-02-16 11:48:26', 831, 0),
(833, 13, 'Crazy4Horses', 'haflingergirl@hotmail.com', 'Another Translation (English to Czech)', 'Hi everyone, I have a question: how would I say \"good boy\" in Czech if I was wanting to say to my horse \"good boy\". yes, I know I am weird for talking to my horse:)\r\nThanks a bunch,\r\nKyanna', '2003-02-17 01:31:25', 0, 0),
(834, 13, 'Crazy4Horses', 'haflingergirl@hotmail.com', 'Conjugations in Czech???', 'Hi everyone(again)\r\nDoes the Czech language have conjugations of verbs like Spanish? if so, what are they? Are they like spanish and have different ones for AR-ending verbs, IR ending verbs and ER ending verbs? \r\nthanks for any info,\r\nKyanna', '2003-02-17 01:34:36', 0, 0),
(835, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: History', 'Jsa biskupem holomuckym = being bishop of Olomouc (old spelling Olomúc, Holomúc, ...)', '2003-02-17 22:30:28', 827, 0),
(836, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', '', 'Re: Another Translation (English to Czech)', 'I don`t really know, whether exists a special expression for horses, but \"good boy\" will be \"dobry kluk\" [`dobri: `kluk]', '2003-02-18 04:00:02', 833, 0),
(837, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', '', 'Re: Conjugations in Czech???', 'Tako a look on my old page (encoding windows-1251 or iso-8859-2 to view czech letters correctly), and you`ll see conjugations of some verbs. It`s in russian, so you`ll be to find out the meanings of the words in http://slovnik.seznam.cz :)', '2003-02-18 04:05:54', 834, 0),
(838, 13, 'Crazy4Horses', 'haflingergirl@hotmail.com', 'Re: Conjugations in Czech???', 'Ok, what is the url of your website? Thanks a lot!\r\nKyanna', '2003-02-18 13:47:26', 837, 0),
(839, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: OBECNA CESTINA', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nI\'m not sure exactly what you mean. Obecná (\"common\") četina is a type of Czech - it is a dialect-group. It is considered the standard spoken Czech, and thus is distinct both from many dialects and a few dialect-groups, and from standard written Czech, known as \"spisovná četina\" . Does this help?\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-18 17:27:48', 826, 0),
(840, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Another Translation (English to Czech)', 'When speaking with animals and small children, the word for \"good\" (or \"nice\") is \"hodný\". I\'m not sure that \"boy\" is ever used for horses; you\'ll have to ask a Czech on that. Here\'s an actual quote from an article on horses where \"koník\" (horsie, pony, little horse) is used instead:\r\n\"Jsi hodný koník!\" (http://www.equinet.cz/14volnycas.htm, first yellow box on the page.)', '2003-02-18 17:34:22', 833, 1),
(841, 16, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Nic se tady neděje :-(', 'Ahojte!\r\n\r\nChtělo by to trochu tady oivit. (Things around here could use some livening up.)\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-18 17:37:32', 0, 0),
(842, 14, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Napoleonic history', 'You might want to get in touch with those involved in the Austerlitz 2005 project, prepared in anticipation of the upcoming anniversary of the battle of Austerlitz. Here is a link to a page with information on them:\r\nhttp://www.austerlitz2005.com/en/projektausterlitz/index.php\r\n\r\nHope that helps,\r\n\r\nErik Piper', '2003-02-18 17:47:59', 815, 0),
(843, 16, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Nic se tady neděje :-(', 'Ja vím! Mě taky!\r\nScott\r\n\r\np.s. Jak se řekne anglický neděje?', '2003-02-20 09:37:36', 841, 0),
(844, 15, 'Scott', '', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Ahojte! \r\nI was just wondering when the course would be open for people to sign up. I have tried a few times with no response.\r\n\r\nScott', '2003-02-20 09:48:06', 0, 0),
(845, 15, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Dear Scott,\r\nthe opening of the course is just getting postponed and postponed. The technology is in place but it\'s hard to find people who would actually sit down and finish the job. I\'m doing many different things so I don\'t quite have time to be the person to do it. However, a lot more is available now than there used to be. Why don\'t you try <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/publicaccess>http://www.bohemica.com/publicaccess</a> to get a bit of a look at what the future may hold. If you\'ve signed up, you\'re on our mailing list and the minute something more is available, you will know!\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-02-20 14:17:04', 844, 0),
(846, 16, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Nic se tady neděje :-(', 'To záleí jenom na vás. Jestli máte nějaké zajímavé téma, napite sem o něm a snad se rozvine nějaká diskuze!\r\n\r\nDominik\r\n\r\nPS: Rád bych vám oběma poděkoval za enthusiasmus a pomoc, kterou poskytujete lidem na tomto foru.', '2003-02-20 14:19:36', 841, 0),
(847, 16, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Nic se tady neděje :-(', 'Ahoj Scotte!\r\n\r\n\"Neděje se\" znamená doslova \"(it) is not happening\", ovem přesný překlad bude vdycky záleet na tom, co vyaduje daná situace a věta.\r\n\r\n(Even assuming I used correct Czech, that was a bit complicated, so: \r\n\r\nWord-for-word, \"neděje se\" means \"(it) is not happening\". However, the precise translation will always depend on what the given situation and sentence demand.\r\n\r\nNapříklad:\r\nNic se tady neděje - Nothing\'s going on around here.\r\nNeděje se něco, miláčku? - Is... something... wrong, dear?\r\nDějou se tady velký věci - Big stuff is happenin\'/goin\' on around here.\r\nCO SE DĚJE?! - WHAT\'S HAPPENING?! AArrghHH\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-20 16:21:49', 843, 0),
(848, 16, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: zabloudilá konverzace', 'Mimochodem... původní příspěvka byla do fora \"Česká konverzace\", zabloudila se sem kvůli bugině (ehm, programové chybě). Nechci ourat, jenom chci říct, e ta\'y to nepíu do \"teachers\" schválně.\r\n\r\nP.S. Díky Dominike, e tak dobře snáí mojí lenost ohledně těch článku. Ars longa vita brevis... \r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-20 16:24:53', 847, 1),
