(852, 16, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Nic se tady neděje :-(', 'Eric,\r\nDěkuju moc pro pomoc!Moje česka je patný protoe ja nestuduju čestinu dost. \r\nI need a teacher bad!!!!!!!\r\n\r\nI can learn vocabulary, but I dont understand sentence structure because there is no one to tell me why it is the way that it is.\r\n\r\nScott\r\n', '2003-02-21 02:05:21', 847, 0),
(853, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Another Translation (English to Czech)', 'Too bad there\'s no <a href=\"http://www.netscape.co.uk/help/faqs/newsgroups/emoticons.htm\"emoticon</a> for blushing!\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-21 15:24:05', 850, 0),
(854, 16, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Czech sentence structure', 'I think the number one barrier to understanding Czech sentence structure is its fundamentally different philosophy, called (or perhaps related to something called - dominik??) topic-focus perspective. In plain English, this means that instead of using sentence structure to show parts of speech (i.e. what\'s a noun, what\'s a verb, etc.), Czech uses sentence structure to show what information we expect the listener to already know, and what we are stressing is new/exciting/important.\r\n\r\nAt its most simple, the idea is: new information goes at the end (of the sentence or of a group of words that belong together). There are lots of exceptions, but you have to start with the rule :-). The only exception I\'ll mention is - sometimes na potvoru the new/stressed information goes naopak at the beginning.\r\n\r\nHere are a couple of examples to get you started:\r\nLenka podala solničku Honzovi - Lenka passed the salt to Honza (English-like sentence structure - in my experience, this usually means that no special emphasis is being placed on anything).\r\nLenka podala Honzovi sůl - Lenka passed the salt (and not something else) to Honza.\r\nLenka tu solničku Honzovi jenom podala. - Lenka only <i>passed</i> the saltshaker to Honza (what someone might say if speaking to someone who claimed that Lenka had thrown it).\r\nHonzovi tu solničku podala. - This is one of those nasty cases where the new information goes at the beginning - perhaps because Honzovi-at-the-end is already occupied by the \"English-like\" structure. Meaning: Lenka gave the saltshaker to Honza, and not somebody else (and, as always, the speaker is assuming this is new information for the listener).\r\nTu solničku Honzovi podala Lenka. - Lenka, and not someone else, passed Honza the saltshaker. (I may have the word order wrong on this one; hopefully Dominik will help me out on this one.)\r\n\r\nThe real question here is, why do I feel forced to include \"tu\" before \"solničku\" in some of the sentences? I\'d be writing another half hour on that, and I\'m already cheating my employer out of my working hours :(. But in short - adding the demonstrative pronoun \"to\" before a noun is quite common in everyday speech when a person is excited/riled up - and these various new-information structures tend to involve a cranky or excited speaker.\r\n\r\nOf course, real life sentences tend to be even more complicated than this case, but it\'s a good start. Well, one more thing:\r\n\r\nthe same principle works inside of groups of highly related words, most notably nouns and their adjectives:\r\núspěné projekty - successful projects (no special emphasis)\r\nprojekty úspěné - the successful projects (as opposed to the other ones)\r\n\r\nNote that this is one of the ways in which Czech makes up for its \"lack\" of a/an/the.\r\n\r\nThere is a related phenomenon that I feel is the same one, but Dominik says it\'s not, and that\'s the nearly-universal placement of the most specific word/phrase within a proper noun at the end, even if it\'s an adjective (which it often is):\r\nHotel Forum, Restaurace U Měděné panve, Divadelní ústav Praha... this often gets carried over into Czenglish, e.g. \"Polymer Institute Brno\".\r\n\r\nHope this has given you a good start in understanding sentence ', '2003-02-21 15:50:44', 852, 1),
(855, 15, 'Paul', 'paulgc69@hotmail.com', 'Looking for a teacher in Nottingham', 'Help! I\'m looking for someone who can teach me to speak Czech in Nottingham on\r\na one-to-one basis. Any recommendations or guidance would be much appreciated.\r\nPaul\r\n', '2003-02-21 16:51:04', 0, 1),
(856, 13, 'Ulla', '', 'Re: OBECNA CESTINA', 'Yes,I mean common -spoken- Czech.', '2003-02-21 16:54:37', 839, 0),
(857, 13, 'susie may', 'susie@wmay77.fsnet.co.uk', 'kovetik', 'I\'ve seen the word \'kovetik\' with umlaut over the \'o\' in a posting on a chatboard referring to Robert Patrick the actor (terminator 2, X Files) and have checked Hungarian dictionaries - a reference to the Czech language was made on one site I visited and wondered if it was Czech - can anyone help please?', '2003-02-22 03:43:34', 0, 0),
(858, 13, ' Penny', '', 'tombstone', 'Zde v panu odpociva  Zemrela L.P.  1895   v Stari 7 HO  Mesice   SPI  Sladce Drake Robatko   SRDCIM  NASIM  ODEBRANE      NAVECNOSTI  SE   SHLEDAME    ODPOCIVEJ V POKDJI', '2003-02-22 04:35:06', 0, 0),
(859, 13, 'Penny', '', 'tombstone', 'ZDE  V PANU  ODEPOCIVAJI    MARTIN REZAC  ZEMREL  ONE 8 HO RIJNA, L.P.  1899  V STARI 67 ROKU A JEHO  MANZELKA, ANTOINE   ZEMRELA ONE 27 HO PROSINCE L.P. 1890   V  STARI 58 ROKU SPETE SDADCE DRARY OTCE AUATI (OR AMATI) NASE U BRAN NEBESKYCH DEJ BOZE STASTNE SHLEDANI                      Sorry do not have a czech keyboard.  Would appreciate your help in translating this.', '2003-02-22 04:43:30', 0, 0),
(860, 13, 'Penny', '', 'Brozik and Rezac pronounciation', 'Can anyone tell me the phonetic pronounciations of my surnames.  We came from Mohelnice and Nepomuk Czech.  Any other info is welcomed also.', '2003-02-22 04:56:27', 0, 0),
(861, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: követik', '\"követik\" is a Hungarian verb, not Czech in any case.', '2003-02-24 15:46:30', 857, 0),
(862, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: tombstone', 'Zde v panu odpociva = She rests here in our Lord\r\nZemrela L.P. 1895 v stari 7ho mesice = She died in the year of our Lord 1895 at the age of 7 months\r\nspi sladce = sleep sweetly\r\nDrake Robatko = Robatko (?!?!?) Drake (i.e. in Czech, last names and first names are often inverted in official contexts - without a comma between them as in English.... BUT I have never heard of anyone being named \"Robatko\")\r\nSRDCIM NASIM ODEBRANE / NA VECNOSTI SE SHLEDAME / ODPOCIVEJ V POKDJI = Taken away from our hearts / We will meet again in eternity / rest in peace\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-24 17:10:59', 858, 1),
(863, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: tombstone', '\"drahé robátko\" = dear child', '2003-02-24 17:22:06', 862, 0),
(864, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: tombstone', 'ZDE V PANU ODEPOCIVAJI MARTIN REZAC = Here lies Martin Rezac...\r\n...now the question is, what are the diacritics on this one? I checked several variants on Google for their frequency:\r\nRezáč - 28 hits\r\nŘezáč - 5,410 hits\r\nŘeáč - 6 hits\r\nReáč - 1 hit (Italian citation of a Czech name, so maybe in error)\r\n\r\nOther variants either yielded no Czech hits or didn\'t seem worth the bother. So statistically speaking, his last name is probably Řezáč, but it\'s hard to be sure.\r\n\r\nZEMREL ONE 8 HO RIJNA, L.P. 1899 V STARI 67 ROKU A JEHO MANZELKA, ANTOINE ZEMRELA ONE 27 HO PROSINCE L.P. 1890 V STARI 58 ROKU = He died ??? [ONE doesn\'t make sense no matter what diacritics I add] on ??? the 8th of October in the year of our Lord 1899 at the age of 67, and his wife Antoine died on the 27th of December in the year of our Lord 1890 at the age of 58.\r\n\r\nThe \"ONE\" might be related to the word \"ono\", very roughly translatable in certain contexts as \"that fateful\" (on that fateful day/date/occasion)...\r\n\r\nSPETE SLADCE = sleep sweetly\r\n\r\nDRAHY OTCE AUATI (OR AMATI) NASE = Dear father ???? of ours. Perhaps A TATI - \"and daddy\"\r\n\r\nU BRAN NEBESKYCH DEJ BOZE STASTNE SHLEDANI = May God grant us a happy reunion at the gates of Heaven.\r\n\r\n', '2003-02-24 17:23:23', 859, 1),
(865, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Brozik and Rezac pronounciation', 'Brozik is almost certainly Broík. The only difficulty in this name is the rolled r - the same as in Spanish, certain dialects of French, and allegedly in Scottish. If you don\'t know any of these... try trilling the r, i.e. vibrating the front of your tongue against your teeth as you say it.\r\nBRO - zheek. \r\n\r\nThe \"zh\" is the sound found in the middle of \"pleasure.\"\r\n\r\nRezac is probably Řezáč. r-with-háček, the first sound in the word, is extremely difficult to pronounce correctly without practice, and it\'s not worth giving a lesson here. So use this pronunciation instead:\r\nRZHE - zahch.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-24 17:30:21', 860, 1),
(866, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: tombstone', 'Zemřel dne ... (den = day)\r\n(or Zemřel onoho ... (onoho is genitive of \"onen\"))\r\n\r\nDrahý otče a máti ... = Dear father and mother ...\r\n', '2003-02-24 17:38:35', 864, 0),
(867, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: tombstone', 'Thanks, Vladimir, for catching the problems with the transcription - I was a little too \"gullible\" in the case of \"drake\"... I was actually believe that this was a person with an English surname who had died in the CR! Your version makes a million times more sense...\r\n\r\nI never would have caught the archaic word robátko. My vocabulary is broad, but thins out a lot when it comes to archaic words, as you can see.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-24 22:35:23', 863, 0),
(868, 16, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: Czech sentence structure', 'Dekuju Moc! To je super!! I actually understand now. Finally someone was patient enough to take the time to explain it. \r\nHezky den!\r\nScott', '2003-02-25 01:51:32', 854, 0),
(869, 14, 'nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Legend', 'Have you seen the statue in the top part of the Wenceslaw Square? That is a statue of \"sv. Václav\" (St. Waclaw), patron of the Czech country.\r\nThe \"up-side-down\" statue in Lucerna is sort of a parody of the famous statue on the Wenceslaw Sq.', '2003-02-25 20:09:42', 829, 0),
(870, 13, 'Penny', 'nickel@gwtc.net', 'Re: Brozik and Rezac pronounciation', 'Thank you so much for your help with the pronounciations and the translating of the two tombstones.  I have some czech grammar books and course learning books but still could not figure them completely out.  I want to someday learn the Czech language and am acquiring books to do that.  Three branches of my family have originated from Czech and I wish to visit the country and continue to study the family history there, but that may not be for some time.  Thanks again for your information.  ', '2003-02-25 21:16:06', 865, 0),
(871, 13, 'Penny', 'nickel@gwtc.net', 'Re: tombstone', 'Thank you so much for filling in the missing information on my two tombstone translations.  The pictures I had of the tombstones were unclear in places and it was hard to read the letters.  I also could not add the correct diacritics  when writing them without a Czech keyboard but you managed to figure them out anyway.  I want to learn the Czech language so I can continue researching family history because we have acquired all our info in the US and everything we need to look for will now be in Czech.  I have acquired some books to help.  Than you again for your help.  Penny Novotny', '2003-02-25 21:26:40', 866, 0),
(872, 15, 'Jan Rezek', 'azer@seznam.cz', 'Question', 'I´m a native Czech from Prague. I´m looking for some job in the US (during the summer 03). Because i need to improve my English. I´m in the WAT program with GTSint. But I need to sign a work contract to get a J-1 visa. You can help me with my English and I can help you with yor Czech. I´m 21 and I study College of Economic Studies. If you can give me some tip or idea (where and how to find a job) please e-mail me to azer@seznam.cz   thanks', '2003-02-27 05:01:12', 0, 1),
