(875, 17, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Česká hudba', 'Druhý pokus o napsaní příspěvky do sekce \"Česká konverzace\" a nikdy jinak...\r\n\r\n(\"a nikdy jinak\" is a bad joke... it\'s a reference to the often-repeated phrase in Communist times \"Se Sovětským svazem na věčné časy a nikdy jinak.\")\r\n\r\nOsobně se velice zdráhám kupovat novou hudbu, a tak mám vkus poněkud zastaralý - českou alternativní hudbu z devadesatých let, nespí ze začátku devadesatých. Předevím U jsme doma, Znouzectnost, Zuby Nehty, Sto zvířat, a DG 307. Z toho mála novějích věcí, co znám, moc se mi líbily alba od Čechomor a málo známých kapel Neočekaný Dychánek a Chorchestr.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-27 18:14:45', 0, 0),
(876, 15, 'Pavel', 'pavel.cerny@strath.ac.uk', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'I have English version of XP at home and I can change the keybord layout like in 98 etc. You have to install support for Czech language (no neeed for menus and help in Czech). Then you can add Czech keybord. The language selector bar (in XP the equivalent to keybord selector) can be either down where the clock is or somewher else on the screen. Hope this will work... Cheers', '2003-02-28 16:41:48', 818, 0),
(877, 15, 'Pavel', 'pavel.cerny@strath.ac.uk', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'I have English version of XP at home and I can change the keybord layout like in 98 etc. You have to install support for Czech language (no neeed for menus and help in Czech). Then you can add Czech keybord. The language selector bar (in XP the equivalent to keybord selector) can be either down where the clock is or somewher else on the screen. Hope this will work... Cheers', '2003-02-28 16:43:37', 818, 0),
(878, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@hotmail.com', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'Thanks, I actually figured it out a while back but forget to post it.  Thanks for the concern and though,\r\nScott', '2003-03-01 01:14:36', 877, 0),
(879, 13, 'Mike Allen', 'youthchaos@hotmail.com', 'Re: Another Translation (English to Czech)', 'Might I just add to this thread that it is extremely satisfying to speak to animals in Czech.  I truly feel they understand it better than English.', '2003-03-04 06:01:56', 850, 0),
(880, 15, 'Dale', 'dalem@marshall-tufflex.com', 'Re: When?', 'Good Question!\r\n\r\nI have been trying to register for over a year now, but I never even get a courtesy reply.\r\n\r\nFor the last 4 years I have been teaching myself Czech from various text books. The best one I believe is CZECH in THREE MONTHS. Obviously it takes a lot longer than 3 months but there are plenty of exercises to enforce the learning.\r\n\r\nGood luck\r\nDale', '2003-03-08 15:36:35', 758, 1),
(881, 13, 'Caitlin', 'Ovation226@aol.com', 'How do you say...', 'Does anyone know how to say passioniate in Czech and if you could spell it with all the accents and stuff cause i am getting a tattoo that says passioniate love and the passionate part will be in czech.', '2003-03-09 08:07:26', 0, 0),
(882, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: How do you say...', 'Vánivá.\r\n\r\n(Vánivá láska.)\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-03-10 16:32:57', 881, 0),
(883, 13, 'm.m.allen', '', 'za svetla i za tmy', 'Is there a generalised instance where \'za\' takes the genitiv, or is it idomatic only to these two terms?\r\nthanks,\r\nmike', '2003-03-17 00:11:30', 0, 0),
(884, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilvoejesus.net', 'Re: When?', 'Hi, I asked Dominik the same question a few weeks ago.  He informed me that the program is not finished yet and therefore no one can use it yet.  However, if you have signed up then you are on the list, and as soon as it is up and running, you will be contacted.  I feel your anticipation!\r\nScott', '2003-03-17 09:34:51', 880, 0),
(885, 13, 'Carol Damioli', 'cdamioli@praguepost.com', 'Important Czech words', 'Does anyone know where I can find a list of the 1,000 most commonly used Czech words? It would help me focus my studying. Thanks.', '2003-03-17 17:10:26', 0, 1),
(886, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'No, \'za\' is used with the genitive always when it means \'during a period of time\' after a name for the period. E.g. \'za vlády Klause\' (during Klaus\' reign), \'za komunistů\' (during the communist times) but NOT e.g. \'za středověku\' (during the Middle Ages).', '2003-03-17 19:15:11', 883, 0),
(887, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'I don\'t know of any such list on its own. (There are some but they mostly describe literary language.) Try <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/dictionary>http://www.bohemica.com/dictionary</a> for a list of words used on this web site.', '2003-03-17 19:21:46', 885, 0),
(888, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Česká hudba', 'Zajímavé ukázky českého roku. Vůbec neznám DG307 a Chorchestr. Odkud jsou a jaký typ hudby hrají?\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-03-17 19:28:53', 875, 0),
(889, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Just out of curiousity, in which ways are \'od\' and \'na\' used, and in which cases are they.\r\n', '2003-03-18 01:55:19', 886, 0),
(890, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Just out of curiousity, in which ways are \'od\' and \'na\' used, and in which cases are they.\r\n', '2003-03-18 01:55:32', 886, 0),
(891, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Sorry for the repetition, my computer froze.\r\n..... I understand their meanings, but get confused sometimes when using them.  In which other ways can \'od\' be used than saying \'from\' and \'na\' used than saying \'on\'.\r\nThanks, Scott.', '2003-03-18 02:04:27', 889, 0),
(892, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Hi there,\r\n\r\nI just wasted a LOT of work time compiling the below, so forgive me for not explaining it in too much detail. Just a couple of notes:\r\n\r\nMethodology: I first searched for \"od\" in the top 30 pages in Google\'s listing overall for the word \"od\" within the .cz domain. I then switched to just the domain pes.eunet.cz for 10-15 more pages and continued searching for \"od\". I probably should have restricted the search to pes.eunet.cz from the start, as a whole-web search is always highly skewed towards the language of business, marketing, and computing. \r\n\r\nI haven\'t done \"za\" yet.\r\n\r\nEach slash is one instance of od, except where it was used many times in exactly the same way on one page, in which case I counted it only once for the group of occurrences, or sometimes twice if I was feeling nice. The subcases (dots) are not counted separately from the rest, that is, if you see twenty instances and ten dots for subcases, that\'s still twenty instances, not thirty.\r\n\r\nThis search surely didn\'t dig up all uses of \"od\", and in some ways a dictionary search would be much better, but I chose this method because it will draw your attention to what meanings are most important. Well, and because it was fun.\r\n\r\nAs silly as this might sound: NEVER believe that any Czech word \"means\" any English word (\"does \'od\' always mean \'from\'\"?). You\'ll only be slowing yourself down in learning. View it as a \"correspondence\" instead. I say this not as a word game, but as a way to take off your English-language glasses. \r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n\"od koho\" [source] ///////////////////\r\n	subcase od [author] . \r\nod čeho [source] ///////////////////\r\n	subcase (untracked) @@$#$ DOG KENNELS! - highly idomatic - they just HAVE to be named \"od [core of name]\", like pubs start with \"u\"\r\n\"od [point in a temporal, spatial, financial etc. scale]\" ///////////////////////////////////\r\n	subcase \"od a do z\" (idiom) ..\r\n	subcase \"od [letter of the alphabet] ...\r\n	subcase \"od X korun\" .\r\n	subcase \"od roku X\" ...\r\n	subcase \"od dd/mm/yyyy\" ...\r\n	subcase \"od X hodin\" ........\r\n	subcase \"od [event]\"..\r\n	subcase \"od [time-noun, e.g. ode dneka]\" .....\r\n	subcase \"od [course of action] do [course of action]\" .\r\n	subcase \"od [description at start of process] do [description at end of process] (e.g. \"od fanouků k barbarům\") ..\r\n	subcase \"od [thing] a po [fundamentally different comparable thing]\" (e.g. od novorozeňat a po bábu Dymákovou) .\r\n	subcase \"od [age] do [age]\" .\r\nNarozdíl od (or: na rozdíl od) čeho/koho (unlike sth./sb.) ////\r\nod počátku čeho (from the start of sth.) /\r\nbýt odstíněn od čeho (??? příp. řekne DL) /\r\nna [direction] od [place] /\r\n[distance] od [place] //\r\npočítat od [number] (to start counting from x)/\r\nosvobození od čeho (liberation from sth, waiving of sth) /\r\nmít co od [source of a bad smell, appearance, etc.] // (e.g. boty měl od bláta)\r\nod [place of origin] ///\r\nčas od času (from time to time) /\r\nod podlahy (rip-roaring, in-a-rip-roaring-way) /\r\n[verb of motion] od [place] // (e.g. odsouvat od sebe, odlepit se od povrchu země)\r\nlíit se od čeho (to differ from sth) / \r\nodliný od čeho (different than/from sth) /\r\nod [owner or \"owner\"] / (e.g. muzeum od Madame Tussaud, holka od Petra]\r\nrvat od úst (to force (words, food, etc. out of someone\'s mouth) /', '2003-03-18 16:55:58', 891, 1),
(893, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'OK, here\'s a somewhat organized collection of instances of \"na\". All were collected from about 7 pages of pes.eunet.cz, which means that their value as a scientific sample is diddleysquat.\r\n\r\n\"Na\" is always 4th case (literal or figurative motion) or 6th case (location).\r\n\r\nBy \"nominalized verbs\" below, I mean verbs turned into nouns, e.g. \"arrest\" (from \"to arrest\") \"skiing\" (from \"to ski\"). I may be using this word too broadly... In any case, I always grouped, um, really similar words together to save space (hledět, pohledět, pohled, etc.)\r\n\r\n[stated or implied \"být\" or similar verb or nominalized verb] na [thing or location that is, or can be compared to, a surface or one that is being used more as a concept than a particular space]: ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////// (all 6th case)\r\n		CONCRETE PLACES\r\n	studovat, být,  pracovat, etc. na [school] ......\r\n	na záchodě (in the bathroom) .\r\n	na stránce (on a/the page) ..\r\n	na adrese ...\r\n	na [ship] .\r\n	na [planet], na povrchu (the surface of) [planet]  ...\r\n	na náměstí (on the square).\r\n	[presentation, concert etc.] na [presentation space] .\r\n	na dovolené (on vacation) .\r\n	na světě (in the/this world) ..\r\n	na Moravě . (and na Slovesnku, but v Čechách)\r\n	na posteli, na luku (in bed) ...\r\n	na zemi (on the ground) .\r\n	na jizdárně (at the riding hall) .\r\n	na [body part or whole body] ..\r\n	na chodbě (in the hall) .\r\n	na scéně (on the stage) ..\r\n	na ulici X (on X street) .\r\n	na pracoviti (at a/the workplace) .\r\n		ABSTRACT \"PLACES\"...\r\n	být na tom [dobře/patně etc.] (to be doing well/poorly etc.) .\r\n	zvat/pozvat koho na [event] (to invite sb to [event]) ...\r\n	na [meeting] (e.g. na poradě) .\r\n	na Internetu ..\r\n	na počátku/začátku/sklonku/konci (at the beginning/end) ....\r\n	na [station, e.g. ČT1, ČRo] ...\r\n	řikat na rovinu (to say straightforwardly) .\r\n	být na denním pořádku (to happen all the time) .\r\n	účast na čem (participation in sth) .\r\n	na podzim, na jaře (in fall, in spring) .... (CAREFUL! v zimě, v létě)\r\n	\r\n[stated or implied verb or nominalized verb of motion] na [location that is a surface or one that is meaningful more as a concept than a particular space] ////////////////// (all 4th case)\r\n	school .\r\n	poznámka na okraj (side note) .\r\n	na [planet] ....\r\n	na zem (to the ground) ..\r\n	na jizdárnu (to the riding hall) .\r\n	na procházku (for a walk) .\r\n	na kopec (onto the hill) .\r\n	na dráhu (on the track (...of [career]), onto a track, onto the track) .\r\n		ABSTRACT DESTINATIONS\r\n	dán na pospas čeho (to be at the mercy of sth) .\r\n	odkázat koho na co (to point sb towards sth, also to leave sb with no choice but sth) .\r\n	co připadnout na koho (for sb to end up getting sth) .\r\n	odsunout na vedlejí kolej (to siderail) .\r\n\r\n[the directing of literal or abstract \"vision\"] /////////////////\r\n	pohled na co (a view/look at sth) ..\r\n	myslet na (to think of) .\r\n	zaměřit se na co (to focus one\'s attentions on sth) ...\r\n	vzpomínat se na koho/co (to recall, to reminisce about sb/sth) ......\r\n	zapomenout na koho/co (to forget sb/sth) .\r\n	mít co na svědomí (to have sth on one\'s conscience) .\r\n	divat se na (to look at) ..\r\n	ukazovat na co (to point to, point out sth) .\r\n\r\n[purpose/goal/desire] ///////////////\r\n	mít anci na [accomplishment, 4th case] (to have a chance at sth) .\r\n	táhnout na [place, 6th case] (to head to attack [place]) .\r\n	těit se na co (to look forward to sth) - ...\r\n	moci na koho/co (to be able to have a go at sb/sth) .\r\n	[thing] na [purpose] (e.g. znalostní soutě na rozehřátí - warmup trivia contest, grant na hru - a grant for a play) ...\r\n	přístup na (access to) .\r\n	[source] na [product] (e.g. lom na zelenou břidlici) .\r\n	trvat na čem (to insist on sth) .\r\n	rezervované na (reserved for) .\r\n	být dost [quality] na [purpose] (to be [quality] enough for [purpose]) .\r\n	dělat [activity] na zkouku (to do something as a test run) .\r\n\r\n[manipulation and things resembling manipulation] ////////////\r\n	[thing] na [thing to be manipulated] (e.g. mikrovlnka na mokré knihy, testy na jezdce, kladivo na čarodějnice) ..\r\n	pracovat na čem (to work on sth) ..\r\n	simulovat co na čem (to simulate sth on sth) .\r\n	ovlivnit, mít vliv na co (to affect/influence sth) .\r\n	vyzrát na (to get the better of) .\r\n	být připraven na co (to be prepared for sth) .\r\n	čekat na (to wait for) ...\r\n	mít co na starosti (to have sth as one\'s responsibility) .\r\n\r\n[result/outcome] //\r\n	být na mrtvici z čeho (to be about ready to have a stroke \'cuz of sth) .\r\n	na povaenou (enough to make you shake your head, literally \"to make you consider\") .\r\n\r\n[reply] //////\r\n	říkat na [statement or situation] [statement] (to say [statement] in reply to [statement or situation]) .\r\n	odpovědět na (to reply to) ....\r\n	reakce na co (reaction to sth) .\r\n\r\nna druhou stranu (on the other hand) /\r\nbýt zaloen na čem (to be based on sth) //\r\nna [round number] (almost [round number]) /\r\nna rozdíl od (unlike) / \r\nna téma čeho (on the topic of sth) /', '2003-03-18 19:18:43', 891, 1),
(894, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Great job Erik. Google is a great way of getting the info you need. Just to add another tool to everybody\'s linguistic armory here\'s a link to the public access point of the Czech National Corpus <a href=http://ucnk.ff.cuni.cz/verejny.html>http://ucnk.ff.cuni.cz/verejny.html</a>. There you can get a more manageable overview from a balanced corpus (samples from different genres and registers). It only shows 20 examples with a brief context but if you choose \'náhodně\' (random) as the search parameter, you can get different examples with different searches. If you are researcher you can sign up for full access which is not as sluggish as the public access (which is also occasionally inaccessible).', '2003-03-18 19:29:31', 893, 0),
(895, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'The only frequency list I\'ve ever found on the Internet lists only three sets of thirty-most-common words (from three different years), and is located at http://www.czech-language.cz/words/slova-nej.html. This would be a good place to start. Here is a copy of the most recent of the three lists (from the 1983 Frekvenční slovník věcného stylu, that is, the Frequency Dictionary of Practical Style):\r\n\r\na  \r\nv,ve  \r\nbýti  \r\nna  \r\nten  \r\nkterý  \r\ne  \r\nze  \r\nten  \r\nse  \r\no  \r\nk,ke,ku  \r\ni  \r\nmíti  \r\npro  \r\ndo  \r\non  \r\nnebo  \r\nmoci  \r\nsvůj  \r\njako  \r\nza  \r\npři  \r\nvechen  \r\npráce  \r\nale  \r\nná  \r\naby  \r\nu  \r\n\r\nIn a modern list, \"práce\" would, I suspect, drop off the list and another word would become number 10 - that is, I suspect its high frequency is an artifact of the Communist era, when it was sort of a buzzword.\r\n\r\nFor information on viewing these words (and any others) in context, see Dominík\'s post on \"za světla i za tmy\".\r\n\r\nIf you don\'t mind a highly skewed list and you don\'t mind getting an Excel file, contact me by e-mail - my curiosity was piqued by your question, and I\'m gradually putting together a 1000-word list based on a hand-built, web-pages-based corpus - which by its nature is not very representative, but which costs only my labor (práce) :-).', '2003-03-19 14:57:08', 885, 1),
(896, 17, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Česká hudba', 'DéGéčka byli odnoem Plastic People, hráli tam předevím Milan Hlavsa a Pavel Zeman. Existovali na dvě poměrně krátká období - v sedmdesátých a v raných devadesátých. Slyel jsem jen ty \"novějí\", které měli texty spíe básnického rázu od Zemana. Hudební styl měli mírně psychedelický a někdy hodně experimentální, např. poslední písničku z alba Uměle ochuceno schválně nechali ukončenou jakoby v půlce. Texty často spí odříkávali ne zpívali.\r\n\r\nChorchestři jsou funky kapela, hrával tam jeden můj kamarád a dodnes hrají vzdálení známí (?). Spousta dechových nástrojů, sem tam revivaly Jamese Browna, atd. V poslední době pouívají mnohem více sytezátorů ne dříve, moná proto je kamarád opustil.', '2003-03-19 15:06:16', 888, 0),
(897, 13, 'daniel', 'bustamad@wabash.edu', 'Advice', 'Hi:\r\n\r\nI need to learn Czech language as fast as possible. I\'ve got 2 months to do so, otherwise I\'ll miss a great Educational opportunity in the Cz.Rep.\r\n\r\nI must pass an exam with basic knowledge of the language.I\'m travelling to podebrady next week.\r\n\r\nWhich methods/books/techniques/onlineresources/exercises, etc would you recommend me?\r\n\r\nThanks', '2003-03-20 01:03:00', 0, 1),
(898, 13, 'Carol Damioli', 'cdamioli@praguepost.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'Yes, I\'d like to see your 1,000-word list when it\'s finished, even if it\'s an Excel file. Thanks.', '2003-03-20 16:59:39', 895, 1),
(899, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Advice', 'Hi Daniel,\r\nMy best advice to you would be to try to get a 1-to-1 teacher for the speed - Czech phonology can be quite tricky to a native English speaker and if you go for schoolarly sort of excersises, the amount of grammar might be quite off-putting (that\'s how I started with English and, frankly, after 5 years of various courses was quite lost in London for months after arrival). \r\nHow much info do you have about the exam? I mean, are you expected to have basic survival level of mainly spoken command or is it the equivalent of the English exam for academic purposes (ie. heavy reading and comprehention)?\r\nHolding my thumbs (=fingers crossed, I\'m actually not sure why we prefer that but there you go - good luck),\r\nJana', '2003-03-20 17:14:46', 897, 0),
(900, 13, 'Mike', '', 'Offering help', 'Hi everyone,\r\n\r\nI am a Czech university student of English. I find the idea of this web site quite interesting. I would like to offer help with learning Czech. Here is my ICQ number\r\n\r\n177329836\r\n\r\njust contact me and we can start talking about almost everything and in the meantime we can teach each other new language skills. Just dont be shy:)', '2003-03-24 02:45:57', 0, 0),
(901, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Advice', 'See also <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/bookstore>http://www.bohemica.com/bookstore</a> on this site. If you\'re really commited, David Short\'s teach yourself is pretty good. Of course taking part in a summer school or some other intensive course is your best (if not only) bet. I assume that\'s what you\'re going to be doing in Poděbrady, anyway. \r\n\r\nA course that offers a serious kick off is one based on suggestopedia (sometimes called alpha learning). But they\'re expensive (upwards of $500 for a week-long intensive) and hard to find for Czech.\r\n\r\nA technique that help me get on a more advanced level of a French course was literally transcribing the first six units of a textbook (but that worked for me). ', '2003-03-24 03:48:42', 897, 0),
(902, 17, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', '17 pádů českého jazyka', 'Tohle jsem dostal nedávno emailem. Tak mě napadlo, e by to studentům mohlo pomoci porozumět tomu, jak fungují české pády.\r\n\r\n\r\n\"17 pádů českého jazyka\" \r\n\r\n\r\nČeský jazyk má v současné dob&#236; 17 pádů. Mezilidské vztahy v naí \r\nspolečnosti si vyádaly (kromě původních sedmi) dalích 10 pádů: \r\n\r\n\r\n8. pád  kdo z koho  korytativ  \r\n9. pád  koho, jak  buzerativ  \r\n10. pád  koho, kam  delimitativ  \r\n11. pád  kdo, koho  likvidativ  \r\n12. pád  jak na koho  intrikativ  \r\n13. pád  komu, co  podmazativ  \r\n14. pád  co za co  korupcional  \r\n15. pád  jak komu  protekcional  \r\n16. pád  kdo s kým  kopulativ  \r\n17. pád  co na co  prezervativ  \r\n', '2003-03-24 23:11:18', 0, 0),
(903, 13, 'Mark', 'jrs@bigvalley.net', 'Czech Heritage?', 'Hi, I\'ve been researching my family tree for a project I\'m doing in my history class. The farthest I can date back to is through my grandmother\'s side to a man named John Huss. All I know about him is that he lived a little before the time of Martin Luther in his hometown of Prague in Czechoslavakia. However, I\'m curious as to whether he may have been Czech at all. I don\'t have a typical taste of what Czech names are like, but could Huss be one of them? i really am very curious about this unknown heritage! Might anyone be able to help me? Any help would sure be appreciated! Thanks.', '2003-03-26 00:45:11', 0, 0),
(904, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Heritage?', 'Hi Mark,\r\nI\'m no expert in history (used to hate it at school) but the reference might be to Jan Hus changed by translation (Jan is quite common Czech name and Hus might have been altered to help the pronunciation, Czech \'u\' never sounds like \'kjuu\' in e.g. \'cute\') in which case you\'ve good really quite famous ancestry! Have a look on http://mujweb.cz/www/historycz/\r\n(by the way, Czechoslovakia existed as a one country for historically quite short period of time, so it might help and narrow your search but would mean that John Huss can\'t be the Jan Hus who lived around 1400AD)\r\nIn any case, good luck with finding out,\r\nJana', '2003-03-26 20:38:57', 903, 0),
(905, 13, 'Jonathan Kosarev', 'jk190778@hotmail.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'The Primsleur Approach sounds very promising. There are actually supposed to be 2500 most frequently used words (in any language) that would give you communicative capability in that language. If you try it tell us how it turns out.\r\nhttp://www.pimsleurapproach.com/em.asp?learn=Czech&v=CZEGo01-01', '2003-03-26 23:27:52', 885, 1);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(906, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'Hello Carol,\r\n\r\nI\'m working on this, but it will take a really long time. It appears that no publicly available version (like SYNEK) of the Czech National Corpus enables frequency analysis (finding the frequency of a word, let alone a dictionary-headword), and building one\'s own corpus with usable size and quality is sheer hell.\r\n\r\nIn a few months, a frequency dictionary of Czech will be published, and will have by its nature a wordlist of much better quality than what I could ever produce in the few hours per week I can devote to this. I feel a little silly, therefore, for trying.\r\n\r\nIn the meantime, here are two frequency lists from papers called Frekvence vzorů českých sloves and Frekvence vzorů českých substantiv by Klára Osolsobě, Karel Pala, and Pavel Rychlý. They show the top 50 verbs and the top 100 nouns. The numbers you see below are numbers of appearances in the DESAM corpus, and were too much trouble to remove from the list.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nErik\r\n\r\nVERBS:\r\n\r\nbýt  33726\r\nmít (se)   6154\r\nříci    4768\r\nmuset    1708\r\nchtít    1313\r\naby    1152\r\njít    1147\r\nstát (se)     856\r\ndát (se)      705\r\nvědět      697\r\nzačít      663\r\nuvést          660\r\ndostat      625\r\nzískat      609\r\nplatit      503\r\nznamenat     593\r\nhrát (si)     493\r\npřijít      488\r\nříkat      487\r\nvidět      478\r\nvést      468\r\npatřit      431\r\nexistovat     402\r\nnajít      385\r\npracovat     382\r\npotřebovat     363\r\npovaovat     359\r\nzůstat      356\r\nrozhodnout     353\r\ndojít      348\r\ndokázat     331\r\nzměnit      318\r\npřipravit     315\r\nmyslit (si)     307\r\nstačit     296\r\ndělat (se)    295\r\nčekat     292\r\ntvrdit     291\r\npodařit se    290\r\njednat     289\r\ndosáhnout    288\r\ndávat     281\r\nobjevit    277\r\nvytvořit    276\r\nznát (se)    273\r\npočítat     272\r\ntvořit     271\r\nzdát se     263\r\ndodat     262\r\nnabízet     261\r\n\r\nNOUNS:\r\nrok	              2654\r\nPraha	              1958\r\nčlověk	              1646\r\nauto	              1361\r\ndoba	              1341\r\ncena	              1165\r\nstrana	              1143\r\nkoruna	              1063\r\nzemě	              1061\r\nspolečnost            1008\r\npřípad 	              982\r\npráce	              971\r\nzákon	              952\r\nden	              943\r\nmísto 	              936\r\nvláda	              891\r\nsystém	              887\r\nsvět 	              827\r\nmonost	              799\r\nvětina               770\r\ntrh	              763\r\npodnik	              751\r\nproblém	              737\r\nčást 	              730\r\nprogram	              719\r\nskupina	              715\r\nrepublika             679\r\nprocento              672\r\nměsto	              652\r\nministr	              622\r\nEvropa	              615\r\nčas	              605\r\notázka	              603\r\nvýsledek              603\r\nzměna	              599\r\nřada	              584\r\nsituace	              577\r\noblast	              577\r\nsluba	              560\r\nsmlouva	              556\r\nprezident             554\r\nzpůsob	              544\r\ninformace             536\r\npočet	              533\r\nkontakt	              531\r\nsnímek	              527\r\nčlen	              522\r\nhodina	              518\r\nministerstvo          514\r\ndítě	              510\r\nvěc	              510\r\nvoda 	              505\r\nčinnost               504\r\nzájem	              501\r\nobjekt	              500\r\nmu	              499\r\nvolba	              488\r\ncesta	              487\r\njednání	              484\r\nměsíc	              482\r\nfax	              482\r\núřad	              479\r\nnávrh	              477\r\nfunkce	              477\r\npodmínka              476\r\nslovo	              473\r\nvýroba 	              472\r\ndům	              468\r\npeníze	              467\r\nena	              444\r\nhráč	              444\r\nředitel	              439\r\npředseda              436\r\ntýden	              435\r\nzařízení              435\r\nprostředí             431\r\nstav 	              419\r\nposlanec              417\r\nhodnota	              412\r\nosoba	              410\r\npracovník             408\r\nJan	              407\r\nnabídka	              405\r\npočítač	              403\r\ntyp	              402\r\nkola	              399\r\nmilio/ón              396\r\nnáklad	              396\r\nlékař	              395\r\nnázor	              394\r\npodnikatel            393\r\ndůvod	              382\r\nzápas	              380\r\núroveň 	              378\r\ndaň	              376\r\ntým	              374\r\nbanka	              374\r\nzáklad	              369\r\nbod	              363\r\npolitika              362\r\ntext	              357', '2003-03-27 19:30:35', 898, 1),
(907, 17, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: 17 pádů českého jazyka', '...spí \"jak funguje česká společnost\" :-)\r\n', '2003-03-27 19:55:11', 902, 0),
(908, 13, 'Matt Blewett', 'matthew.blewett@tiscali.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'I had about two months to learn the language, but I didn\'t have to take a test...  if you have high speed internet access and need to learn communication, I recommend tuning into cz radio stations to get a feel for the speed and sound of the language.  Many links to online stations can be found at www.radia.unas.cz  hodně těstí = good luck.\r\n\r\n', '2003-04-02 00:52:20', 885, 1),
(909, 13, 'Miss Prompan Nam', 'r_lim@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation (Czech to English)', 'Hello INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(971, 13, 'Penny', 'nickel@gwtc.net', 'Re: Need help translating', 'Thank you again with your help with my translations.  Am doing family history and it is hard sometimes when there is a language barrier.', '2003-05-09 03:23:22', 945, 0),
(972, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Well, try to imagine how I feel trying to fit my wobly Czech mind into the English structure with all those \'auxiliaries\' and tenses and phrasal verbs and not just preposition but virtually anything having tons of meanings ...:))) \r\nTaky z toho silim!', '2003-05-11 23:13:03', 967, 1),
(973, 13, 'Jakub', 'xjacob@yahoo.com', 'Re: Woe is me', 'I fully understand your despair. Right now, trying to learn Hebrew, I can see how the language can twist and spill out of the bounds of my own czech+english mind frame. \r\nYou do, indeed, have to be very open-minded in learning Czech. The words can mean billions of things at the same time, but once you manage to learn a finite amount of these basic expressions, you can put them together in any way you desire to construct virtually any sentence - as opposed to English, where the grammar is set in stone, but to write a good sentence, you have to know a lot of fancy idioms. \r\nYet, concerning your question, be aware that \"Kde jsem se to vlastne vzal\" is an idiomatic expression. \"Vzit\" means \"to take\", while its reflexive form \"vzit se\" is rendered \"to marry\" - there are no more common generic meanings for \"vzit\", as far as I know. \"Kde jsem se tu vlastne vzal?\" is an idiom meaning \"How did I get here afterall?\". In feary tales, you can encounter \"Kde se vzal, tu se vzal\", meaning \"And out of nowhere, there he was/appeared\" - I wouldn\'t attempt to derive the meaning of such idioms from the dictionary definitions of individual words - all you can do with idioms is learn/memorize them. Cheers, Jakub ', '2003-05-13 07:22:45', 967, 0),
(974, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Hi Jakub,\r\n\r\njust a short note - \"How did I get here afterall?\" sounds quite unnatural; \"after all\" is an idiom meaning \"contrary to what was expected\" or \"you know\", as in:\r\n\r\nHe said he would be late, but he came after all.\r\nIt\'s a small world after all.\r\nLooks like it\'s not going to rain after all.\r\n\r\nAlso, watch out for that steel-tight English structure in \"Out of nowhere appeared X\" - although this is one of the few cases where subject/verb inversion doesn\'t look TOTALLY wacky, you should still be cautious. In general, I think it\'s better for Czechs to be too cautious about inverting English subjects and verbs than not cautious enough.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-13 15:30:12', 973, 0),
(975, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hi,\r\nCould anyone please shed some logic into such a basic question as \'a\' or not to \'a\'?\r\nWhat I\'m trying to understand is where the heck do need to put it; well obviously in front of nouns but in my Czech logic,\'a\' is totaly redundant, plurals are usually marked quite sufficiently. I\'ve finally managed to tame the bloody tenses in most of the times, I hope, but this really escapes me.\r\nThanks a lot,\r\nJana\r\nEric, should this provoke your linguistic tendencies, may you please try to restrict yourself to succint and fullproof comments? (Only joking, I thing your linguistic knowledge is adorable!)', '2003-05-13 21:51:24', 0, 1),
(976, 13, 'Jakub', 'xjacob@yahoo.com', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Good point. I was just trying to find the cloesest expression possible that would express the Czech idiom.\r\nRegarding the \"after all issue, I would leave that unchanged. \"Kde jsem se tu vlastne vzal?\" is an expression that implies some dose of surprise, after all... :-) Although your translation \"How the heck did I get in here?\" might be better.\r\nAnyway, you are right, Czechs love to mess up the word order in just about any kind of English sentence. Do you have any idea what the most common mistakes are? In the years I have been living in English-speaking world, I am afraid I established my own Czech-English language, which is perfectly understandable to everyone, but is never corrected (people are simply too polite).\r\nI guess, other than simple grammar mistakes, word order is the no.1 fault, is it not?   ', '2003-05-14 11:04:08', 974, 0),
(977, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Succinctly, OK.\r\nI\'ll leave details to others.\r\nThe \"grammatical\" names of the two types of articles really do say something about them.\r\n\r\n\"a/an\" is, roughly speaking, indefinite - vague, unknown, previously unmentioned, unsure.\r\n\"the\" is definite - sure, already-mentioned, known, a known topic, a topic assumed known.\r\n\r\nYou MIGHT even think of it this way. If your sister comes into the room and says \"I found a cat,\" it means \"Nala jsem kočku\". If she says \"I found the cat,\" it means \"Nala jsem... tu kočku; tu nai kočku; kočku, co jsme o ní včera mluvili; kočku, co vichni víó*, o kterou jde\" etc.\r\n\r\nOne last hint in your quest. Learn what a <i>determiner</i> is. Articles are AFAIK only needed for singular nouns with no other determiner. There\'s five types, can\'t remember \'em all.... articles, possessives, demonstratives, maybe partitives, and thingamajigs.\r\n\r\nThat wasn\'t succinct. Sorry.\r\n\r\nErik\r\n* well maybe not YOUR sister... that was my Brno-ish voice speaking there.', '2003-05-14 16:49:47', 975, 0),
(978, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Wordlist - apology', 'Hello all,\r\n\r\nI\'m simply NOT going to be able to compose and give to anyone any sort of \"top 1000 czech words list.\" I\'ve found it\'s not possible for one person working alone (and busy with many other things). My apologies to those who were eagerly awaiting it.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-14 16:53:41', 0, 0),
(979, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Hmm... if you mean \"is word order the no. 1 fault for Czechs in general?\" I\'d say it\'s pretty well tied with article errors, capitalization errors, and the \"of of the of of the of\" error, which is theoretically just stylistic, but when you mix it with the other two, you get incomprehensible sentences like this (which I used to have to correct a LOT in the old days when the societal standards for translators were lower than today):\r\n\r\n\"Since 1990 increases quickly health of the Nature of mountains of region of Broumovsko thanks to realizing establishment of improved system of defended territories of the Nature.\"\r\n\r\nActually, that sentence (my own invention anyway) was still almost understandable. I\'ll have to dig up one of those torturous sentences from the bad old days.\r\n\r\nAnyway:\r\n1. Most of the time, people really are too polite to correct any but the grossest errors. Anyone willing to correct you is a treasure.\r\n2. Word order is the most alien and meaning-destructive fault, so it\'s very important to tackle. Man bites dog is not the same as Dog bites man. (<-- articles omitted here reflect newspaper-headline usage)\r\n3. Hard to say if it\'s the most common, really.\r\n\r\nBy the way, \"after all\" doesn\'t work with that sort of questions, which I guess is how I should have explained the problem. However, you can ask questions like \"He did come after all, didn\'t he?\" (in which case \"after all\" means \"přece\").\r\n\r\nErr... sorry for spamming this board, folks! I just wanted to answer Jakub!\r\n\r\n[slinks away]', '2003-05-14 17:07:36', 976, 0),
(980, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Well, exactly, what do you need the \'a\' for? If my sister found more than one (poor her), it would be \'cats\' and if it was her Micinka, it would by \'the cat\'.*\r\n\r\nAnyway, what actually induced this was that in some respects my essays got on a relatively decent level and although I now and then misspell (just realised how dependent I am on a spellchecker), mix some prepositions, twist collocations or miss the odd \'the\' (I mean definite article), the \'a\' seems to be the one most commented on by my lecturers.\r\n\r\nSo, please share with me if you have any practical tricks for not leaving them (= indefinite articles, how can one plularise an \'a\'?) out. So far I\'ve tried to watch out for cases where I\'d say \'one\' (of the whatever) but didn\'t help. And the trouble with me and grammar is (besides of being leathaly boring) that I find it quite difficult to apply the rules on pieces of writing where I\'m desperate to make sense (and impress my markers by the content.)\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\nJana\r\n\r\n*not sure but don\'t think she ever had one and finding several would probably feak her out', '2003-05-14 17:33:58', 977, 0),
(981, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hello Jana,\r\n\r\n<b><i>Well, exactly, what do you need the \'a\' for? If my sister found more than one (poor her), it would be \'cats\' and if it was her Micinka, it would by \'the cat\'.*</b></i>\r\n\r\nWhat, exactly, does one need genders for? English gets by with implied gender except for a few words (pronouns) where it can be easily implied. What do we need rigid sentence structures for? Czech gets by (mostly) without them. What do we need the use of sentence structures to express old and new information for? English gets by (mostly) without it. Czech meanwhile gets by without articles at all (but replaces them to some degree with sentence structure). Why mark the subject\'s number and gender in the verb? In adjectives? We already noted them once!\r\n\r\nLanguages include redundancy, partly because they\'re created by humans and not computers (thank God), and partly because language is above all spoken, and redundancy means you can afford to miss a bit.\r\n\r\nAnyway - just keep in mind that if a singular noun doesn\'t need \"the\" and has no other determiner, then it needs, <i>needs</i> \"a\" or else it sounds alien. (I say \"alien\" because article omission is rare among speakers of West European languages, the foreign speakers we (Americans) know best, as mnost or perhaps all of these languages have articles.) Note that plural nouns get indefinite articles too -- it\'s called having nothing there. It makes linguistic sense, since most plural nouns are indefinite, whereas the ratio of a\'s to the\'s is much more balanced. Here the English language (and probably others, can\'t think right now) chose simplicity over redundancy.\r\n\r\nFor a quick check of what the other types of determiners are besides articles, see e.g. <a href=\"http://www.clas.ufl.edu/users/rthompso/gramdet6.html\">http://www.clas.ufl.edu/users/rthompso/gramdet6.html</a>. For more pages on determiners, you might try this google search: <a href=\"http://www.google.com/search?q=determiners+possessives+demonstratives&ie=ISO-8859-2&hl=cs&lr=\">http://www.google.com/search?q=determiners+possessives+demonstratives&ie=ISO-8859-2&hl=cs&lr=</a>.\r\n\r\n<b><i>So, please share with me if you have any practical tricks for not leaving them (= indefinite articles, how can one plularise an \'a\'?)</b></i>\r\nLike this:\r\na\'s.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nErik', '2003-05-15 00:04:49', 980, 0),
(982, 13, 'Scott Brown', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nWhen you asked this question it really boggled my mind.  I am so used to using articles to express how many or what type something is that I have had to completely try to forget them being natural and think of them as a Czech would.  I know one trick you might try is just accepting that they have to be there.  I had to do this with Czech.  It is weird for me to not specify something with a word, but by the way it is said.  I had to just stop thinking of why it is this way and just start accepting that this is how it is.  Lets say you want to state something e.g. I have a dog, I would like a cat, I would like the cat, I need a cat, than it is imperative to have them.  \r\n\r\nI have thought of it like this:  \r\nLets say you want to talk about getting a cat OK. If you say <B>\"I got cat\" </b> or <b>\"I picked up cat\"</b> it does not specify how many or what type of cat has been picked up.  It almost sounds like you have ordered some to eat :) haha.  Anyway, when you say it like this without any articles it is open to interpretation as to what kind or how many there are, but to a native speaker it sounds like you have made cat plural without adding an \'s\', or rather it sounds like you are making a generalization to all cats or a group of cats.  However, if you say <B>\"I picked up the cat\", \"I picked up a cat\", \"I got some cats\", \"I bought several cats, \"I received two cats\",</b> and so on... Than you have specified how many cats have been picked up, therefore the articles or other adjectives are modifiers of the word \'cat\'.  In English when you diagram a sentence the articles are placed underneath the word just as any other modifier would be (adjectives, adverbs, phrases, etc.).  So, just as you would use any other modifier to describe or further support the noun or object you need atricles to do the same.  I think if you think of an article as a reinforcer of a word that has to be there to specify what type it is or how many there are, then it will benefit you greatly in this endeavor.  Almost always (atleast I can\'t think of any examples that say otherwise) if you have a noun in a sentence that is receiving some sort of action or of some type of object than there should always be an article.  If you say <b>\"A dog barked\"</b> it is not being specific as to which dog.  It is just saying there was some dog that barked.  However, if you say <B>\"that dog\"</b> or <b>\"the dog barked\"</b>, you have specified which one did it.  In making generalizations you can use <b>\"a or an\"</b>(indefinate articles) but if you want to be more specific say<b> \"the\"</b>(definate article) or use pronouns like <B>\"that, this, his, her\".</b>\r\n\r\nBasically articles are just fillers and must be in place so you know what the noun is referring to.  I am sorry I cannot be of more help or if I have not answered the question directly.  This is a very hard topic for me to explain.  Try this website: <a href=\" http://owl.english.purdue.edu/handouts/esl/eslart.html\"</a> and see if it can help.  I will ask my fiancé, who is Czech, how she understands articles and try to gain some more insight as to how you might feel about the topic.  I think if I can get her to explain what it is like coming from your point of view, than I might be able to be of some more help.  Also, try going to google and looking for \"article explanations on English grammer\" and similar wordings and I think you find some useful information.\r\n\r\nI hope this serves as some help.\r\nHave a great day!\r\nScott', '2003-05-15 01:00:57', 975, 0),
(983, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hi, Scott!\r\nThanks a lot! I think that\'s exactly what its about - so natural for you, so \'can\'t-get-used-to-them\' for me. I\'ll try the web but I\'m much clearer already - thanks to your brilliant explanation! The distinction between \'a cat\' as one particular (although unknown/unspecified) animal and \'cat\' as refering to feline predators as a specie might work for me. \r\n...hope my essays will be \'a/an\'ed to perfection now :)\r\nYou\'ve been great help, much appreciated - anything I might try to help you with in return? (Well, I know, you probably have much better sources at hand and I\'m just starting to get the \'feel\' for English and how different the concepts are in comparison with Czech but would be happy to try my best.)\r\nThanks again,\r\nJana', '2003-05-15 18:05:29', 982, 0),
(984, 13, 'Scott Brown', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hi Jana,\r\n\r\nYou are very welcome.  I am just glad I was able to help a little.  Actually I do not have very good sources.  I am currently in Texas until August, and in the area in which I live there is no one who is interested in anything Czech.  :) In fact many people do not even know that Czech and Slovakia are two seperate countries, or rather they choose to stay ignorant and not learn that fact, even after I tell them a thousand times.  That becomes very annoying after two years of hearing that mistake from the same people :).  I appreciate the offer and may take you up on it sometime.  Right now I cannot think of anything.  I am currently trying to get a good grasp on the accusitive case.  The hardest part is trying to remember all the rules!!!!  \r\n\r\nBy the way, here are a few more links for you to check out:\r\n<a href=\"http://webster.commnet.edu/grammar/determiners/determiners.htm\">Articles and Determiners</a><BR><a href=\"http://grammar.englishclub.com/adjectives-determiners-the-a-an.htm\">English Club</a><BR><a href=\"http://www.pixi.com/~creativs/reptgakp/common.htm#1articles\">A good explanation of articles</a>\r\n\r\nSorry, I haven\'t had much time to search for more sites, but the last one I think will help the best.  Hondně těsti!!!\r\n\r\nScott', '2003-05-17 01:00:56', 983, 0),
(985, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Well, if it helps, it\'s not that much different here in London and that\'s Europe and +/- 2hrs on a plane. Don\'t get upset, it must have somenthing to do with the ocean :)\r\nThanks, good luck with the cases (so glad I don\'t have to learn them via grammar rules, tried German ones and it was confusing enough) and brilliant time in sunny Texas (that\'s how I picture it in torrential London),\r\nJana', '2003-05-19 18:24:56', 984, 0),
(986, 17, 'Katrin', 'seine_ratte@web.de', 'Mám otásku o Praze', 'Jsem studenka a chci studovat v Praze.\r\nMusím jeste hledat dobrá univerzita. Ted\' studují cestinu a anglictinu v první roce v Nemecky. \r\nMoje otáska je: Jak muzeme nacházet nedráhý byt v Praze? Jak je studentské kolej v Ceské republice???', '2003-05-21 01:07:43', 0, 1),
(987, 13, 'mike', 'youthchaos@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'I don\'t know if it\'s any help, but here\'s my succint but philosophical view on the indefinite article.  There is a mass of \"cat\" floating around in the universe.  We can think of it as the Platonic \"form\" of \"cat\".  Using the indefinite article summons up a single cat out of this general idea of cat...not a cat that we are neccessarily familiar with, but one single cat...', '2003-05-24 00:29:55', 980, 0),
(988, 13, 'Vlastik', 'va_nh@yahoo.com', '', 'Koukam ze je tady zhava debata.Kdyby nekdo potreboval pomoc,jsem z cech a ziji v usa.Moje zena je americanka tak by mi pripadne ten preklad do anglictiny opravila.Zatim cau Vlastik.', '2003-05-25 01:54:10', 0, 0),
(989, 13, 'Vlastik', 'va_nh@yahoo.com', '', 'Koukam ze je tady zhava debata.Kdyby nekdo potreboval pomoc,jsem z cech a ziji v usa.Moje zena je americanka tak by mi pripadne ten preklad do anglictiny opravila.Zatim cau Vlastik.', '2003-05-25 01:55:18', 0, 0),
(990, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Thanks, Mike, sounds logical, hope it\'ll help (I\'ll do my best to keep it in my mind when essay-wrestling).\r\nOn the other hand, how exactly do we actually deal with this distinction in Czech? Interesting... feels like Czech cats don\'t float in the universe but that must be wrong. I guess we would just use plural for the general idea or maybe modify the word (thanks god for Czech morphology, almost anything seems solvable by it).\r\nTHX,\r\nJ.', '2003-05-28 15:47:09', 987, 0),
(991, 15, 'petra', 'petrasmale@yahoo.com', 'looking for a czech teacher in Amsterdam, the Netherlands', 'I am living in Prague now, but will be back in Amsterdam next week. I would like to continue my lessons Czech in Amsterdam, in the Netherlands. If you know a teacher email me at Petrasmale@yahoo.com\r\n', '2003-05-28 16:42:47', 0, 0),
(992, 13, 'dan', 'woodspirt60@hotmail.com', 'translation', 'Can anyone give me a translation of the following?\r\nPotoeboval az k oici nico Eech', '2003-05-29 10:55:38', 0, 1),
(993, 13, 'dan', 'woodspirt60@hotmail.com', 'translation', 'Can anyone give me a translation of the following?\r\nPotoeboval az k oici nico Eech', '2003-05-29 10:56:39', 0, 1),
(994, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: translation', 'If \"Potoeboval az k oici nico Eech\" is the text you got, then it\'s been damaged by some problems with \"character sets\" - in other words, most of the letters with accent marks have been changed. I\'ve seen a lot of damaged texts in the past, so I can make some guesses here, but none of them make sense!\r\n\r\nPotoeboval - definitely \"Potřeboval\" (for safety\'s sake: \"Potreboval\") - \"He needed\"\r\naz - no clue. Definitely not \"a\", since the person was writing with accent marks.\r\nk - most likely means \"for\" in this case.\r\noici - maybe \"říci\" - \"rici\" - \"to say\"\r\nnico - probably \"něco\" - \"neco\" - \"something\"\r\nEech - probably \"Čech\" - \"[from] Bohemia/the Czech Republic\"\r\n\r\nEven when you take into account Czech\'s free word order, it doesn\'t make much sense.', '2003-05-29 14:23:31', 992, 0),
(995, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'IMHO\r\n\r\n\"Potřeboval a k říci něco Čech\"\r\n- Translate it literaly back to English (it seems to be \"translated\" (bleah :-O...) from English originaly) to get the meaning:\r\n\"He needed -?- to say something Czech\".\r\nI\'m not sure with the original word that has been translated as \"a\".\r\n\r\nWhat do you think about this hypothesis?', '2003-05-30 04:03:14', 994, 0),
(996, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'Correction: - from English or similar analytic language originaly -', '2003-05-30 04:04:31', 995, 0),
(997, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'Sorry for writing another correction:\r\n\"az\" could be \"a\" in non-damaged text.', '2003-05-30 04:07:01', 995, 0),
(998, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: translation', 'Actually, this hypothesis occurred to me, but it seemed to crazy to bring it up. Still, your analysis in detail shows it has merit. I thought about the \"az = a\" hypothesis too, but actually, if we start with the \"really bad translation from English\" theory, then \"to\" could easily have become \"a k\".\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-30 14:55:58', 995, 0),
(999, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'Infinitive \"to say\" was probably translated badly (literaly) as \"k rici\" instead as only \"rici\", because the author\'s or translator\'s mother tongue is not English and his English is really bad. (olny a hypothesisis)', '2003-05-30 23:08:48', 998, 0),
(1000, 15, 'Anton', 'anton_spierenburg@yahoo.co.uk', 'Czech teacher wanted, Southampton UK area', 'I am looking for someone to give me guidance and a push with learning Czech language. I have the basics and the possibility to practise immediately what I have learnt. I am living in Southampton and would be interested if there was something available in the area (max. 20 mile radius).\r\n\r\n', '2003-06-02 22:04:48', 0, 1),
(1001, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Or like on that list of bad Czech-English translations, \"where she married herself, there she married herself\"? Below \"prostorekost\" - \"simplegreekness\" and \"Boleslav Polivka\" - \"Paincelebrate Soup\". ', '2003-06-06 11:11:14', 973, 0),
(1002, 15, 'Majda', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Práci?', 'Erick\r\n\r\nJust a few corrections: \r\n\r\n- not OTELEFONOVAT but OBTELEFONOVAT\r\n- not OKOUŘIT but VYKOUŘIT (okouřit does not mean anything,\r\nvykouřit then mean exactly what Monica did :o)\r\n\r\nTo lick itself clean - animals = OLÍZAT, not OMÝVAT \r\nOMÝVAT/OMÝT = to wash off .. e.g. ovoce před jídlem pořádně omyjte or\r\nránu na noze musíte 2 x denně omývat studenou vodou or Vltava omývá \r\npraská nábřeí \r\n\r\nAhoj a četině zdar! :)\r\n\r\nMajda', '2003-06-06 19:09:12', 950, 0),
(1003, 15, 'Majda', 'littlefox@atlas.cz', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'V CENTRU\r\n\r\n\"V\" becomes \"F\" ... but say quickly f c f c f c f c .. and what\r\nyou hear is \"sort of\" SSSS :)) \r\n\r\nMajda', '2003-06-06 19:18:12', 917, 0),
(1004, 15, 'Carolyn Trachtova', 'trachtova@hotmail.com', 'Czech teacher needed in Atlanta, GA', 'I am desperately seeking a Czech teacher here in Atlanta. I lived in Prague for almost six years and the little Czech I know is deteriorating at a rapid pace.  My level used to be intermediate-ish, with horrible grammar.  My husband is Czech and  I want my kids to be bilingual, but if I don\'t start working on my own Czech soon I\'ll be asking them to translate for me!\r\n\r\nThanks \r\n\r\nCarolyn Trachtova', '2003-06-10 00:25:55', 0, 1),
(1005, 16, 'Carolyn Trachtova', 'trachtova@hotmail.com', 'Czech teacher needed in Atlanta, GA', 'I am desperately seeking a Czech teacher here in Atlanta. I lived in Prague for almost six years and the little Czech I know is deteriorating at a rapid pace. My level used to be intermediate-ish, with horrible grammar. My husband is Czech and I want my kids to be bilingual, but if I don\'t start working on my own Czech soon I\'ll be asking them to translate for me!\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n\r\nCarolyn Trachtova', '2003-06-10 00:31:04', 0, 1),
(1006, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', '', 'sklonování', 'Byl bych vděčen tomu, kdo napíe, jak se správně skolonuje podstatné jméno VEJCE. Dík :)', '2003-06-10 20:29:14', 0, 0),
(1007, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: skloňování', 'Sg. vejce, vejce, vejci, vejce, vejce!, (o) vejci, vejcem\r\nPl. vejce, vajec, vejcím, vejce, vejce!, (o) vejcích, vejci', '2003-06-11 13:35:22', 1006, 0),
(1008, 13, 'pjm', 'vl_509@hotmail.com', 'phonetic pronunciation of \"vl\" in Czech words?', 'Can someone please type in phonetically how to pronounce the Czech word \"vlcak\" (wolf) for example and is it the same \"vl\" sound as \"Vladimir\" for example? Thanks so much for any enlightenment!', '2003-06-12 18:22:10', 0, 0),
(1009, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: phonetic pronunciation of', '1) \"vlčák\" doesn\'t meen a wolf, but an Alsatian (German) shepherd dog. Wolf is \"vlk\".\r\n\r\n2) The pronounciation is simple, but quite strange for foreigners: First sylable \"vl-\" is said [vl:] or [vl@], second \"-čák\" is normal. It\'s the same as \"vla-\", but instead of [-a] there is [-@]*.\r\n\r\nOr as in English second sylable\'s pronunciation in \"evil\" [i:vl@]. Try to say \"evil charm\" but without the [i:] at the beginning and with [-k] instead of [-(r)m].\r\n\r\n* @ = 180 degrees turned \"e\", a character that is not in common fonts.', '2003-06-12 22:03:36', 1008, 0),
(1010, 13, 'J', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Ad', 'vlčák [vl@.tSa:k]\r\nvlk [vl@k]\r\n\r\n@ = 180 degrees turned \"e\", a character that is not in common fonts;\r\nS = also not present in common fonts, the phonetic symbol used to mark the pronounciation of eg. English \"sh\" or Czech \"\".', '2003-06-12 22:12:26', 1009, 0),
(1011, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: phonetic pronunciation of', 'sorry, not quite sure how to do it with this keyboard but will try to describe.\r\n\r\nyou\'re right about the \'vl\' in vlcak = \'vl\' in other words such as Vladimir, \r\nthe c is actually pronounced as \'ch\' in chocolate \r\nthe \'a\' is long as in marble\r\n(the \'k\' is the easy one: as \'c\' in cat)\r\n\r\nthe stress is on the long \'a\'\r\n\r\nhope this helps', '2003-06-13 22:32:00', 1008, 0),
(1012, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: phonetic pronunciation of', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nIf you are wanting to say to vlk (wolf) insead of vlčák than this is how I would pronounce it, being American :).  When I heard people say it this is how it sounded to me.  The \"vl\" is indeed pronounced as Vladimir.  As for the whole word together, though, this is how I would think of it from a native English speaking mind.  Pronounce \"v\" as in \"vermine\", \"vault\", \"venice\"...  As for the \"lk\": think of the word \"old\".  Now take just the first two letters \"ol\"; say them aloud.  Now pronounce the \"l\" in \"vlk\" as if it had that same deeper \"ol\" sound.  Then the \"k\" is just a normal k sound.  So, when sounded out, it would sound like this (V-ol-k).  Now say it faster so that it loses that initial o sound.  When said fast it sounds similar to Volkeswagon only with a little more stress on the L as in Vladimir.  You don§t pronounce the O so it sounds like Vlkswagon.  Sorry for the long definition.  I thought I would put every piece of my mind into it.  Hope I didn\'t confuse you :).\r\n\r\nHope this helps as well.\r\n\r\nScott ', '2003-06-15 11:52:19', 1008, 0),
(1013, 13, 'pjm', 'eastwick66@att.net', 'pronunciation wolf/vlk', 'Thanks to everyone who replied. Several online dictionaries gave \"vlcak\" as the word for wolf, not \"vlk\" so thanks for the clarification. Also, it gave \"vlcek\" as \"little wolf\" so I am entirely confused. I imagine the only way to learn a true pronunciation is to actually HEAR it! I will not be studying the Czech language because it is beyond my ken even though I have a healty IQ, I can not forsee comprehending it. My accolades to anyone who has mastered this enigmatic language!!!', '2003-06-15 20:39:53', 0, 0),
(1014, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', '', 'Re: skloňování', 'Děkuji moc :) Vdy mi pomáháte s tou četinou :)', '2003-06-16 02:07:23', 1007, 0),
(1015, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: pronunciation wolf/vlk', 'I understand what you mean... One thing I have come to do is never trust dictionaries.  Get info from them, but when translating the words from one language to another, the definition given for the word might lead you to think it means something, but it may in fact have a totally different meaning from what you are trying to say or how you are trying to say it.  In czech one thing that you do when you want to say something is little or small you add ček, sek, etc. (can\'t think of the other endings or spellings off the top of my head; maybe someone else could clarify)  Like in my name for example: In english you would say Scottie for a nick name or meaning little Scott, but in czech you would say something like Scottiček.  I also know a little boy whose nickname is Matísek (sounds to me like there is an accent on the i?), meaning little Matthew. Another example is doma or dům (House,Home) which would be domeček for little house.  \r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nScott ', '2003-06-16 10:50:15', 1013, 0),
(1016, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: pronunciation wolf/vlk', 'Both \"vlčák\" and \"vlček\" are words derived from \"vlk\" (wolf, lat. lupus, gr. lykos, from proto-indoeur. ulkos) but with different meanings. \"Vlčák\" is a common colloquialism for Alsatian dog/German shepherd. The correct term is \"německý ovčák\" (literally German shepherd, \"ovčák\" is derived from ovce = ewe, lat. ovis). \"Vlček\" means a little wolf.', '2003-06-16 16:31:36', 1013, 0),
(1017, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Just to show my brilliant expertise', 'The backwards e is called a schwa. ;)', '2003-06-18 09:16:47', 1010, 0),
(1018, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Just to be a horrible pedant', 'The latinism \"ad\" is not used in English. We use \"re:\" instead.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-06-18 14:03:06', 1010, 0),
(1019, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Ad!', 'There is no schwa after/before L in VLK and VLČÁK. The L is syllabic.\r\n\r\nAlso [i:vl@] (with a schwa at the end) is questionable.', '2003-06-18 14:50:48', 1010, 0),
(1020, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Ad!', 'There is no schwa after/before L in VLK and VLČÁK. The L is syllabic.\r\n\r\nAlso [i:vl@] (with a schwa at the end) is questionable.', '2003-06-18 15:25:37', 1010, 0),
(1021, 13, 'pjm', 'vl_509@hotmail.com', '\"cek\" or \"sek\" at end of first names?', 'Scott replied to my earlier question on this subject but, again, I\'m a bit unclear.  If I wanted to call a man or boy named Vladimir, for example, little Vlad....would I add cek or sek?  Vladicek or Vladisek?  And does this also apply to womens names?\r\nAlso, why do I notice that most Czech females last names end with \"ova\"...for example, if her last name is Hasek, is she called Hasekova and her brother is just Hasek???  Thanks for all replies! ', '2003-06-19 18:19:41', 0, 0),
(1022, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re:', 'I am not sure if you wanted to call a guy Vladimir little Vladimir how you would say it.  I am not a native speaker and do not have enough experience to hear that.  Especially since it is not a Czech name.  My name Scottiček was made up by some Czech friends because that is how they thought it should sound.  As for the \"Ova\" question:  Yes, when referring to any Slavic language, you will add and \"ova\" to the end of most names when the last name belongs to a female.   For example:  My fiancés last name is Bařáková.  Her fathers last name is Bařák.  A very few names do not change.  I cannot think of any off the top of my head.  Maybe a someone else could help with that.  \r\n\r\nPretty easy concept eh?\r\nScott', '2003-06-21 13:24:51', 1021, 0),
(1023, 13, 'Christian', 'Chris17jaar@hotmail.com', '\"czo to za wiocha ja k mozna taka piosenka tak zeszpacic KASZANA wy dziady \"', 'can anyone translate that sentince? \"czo to za wiocha ja k mozna taka piosenka tak zeszpacic KASZANA wy dziady\" I don\'t understand it.', '2003-06-21 15:57:35', 0, 0),
(1024, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re:', 'I am not an expert in Polish, but I would that it looks like Polish to me. ', '2003-06-22 13:04:21', 1023, 0),
(1025, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re:', 'about \"Little Vladimir\": you can say Vládíček (there you have it, it\'s \"ček\", but don\'t ask me why, i\'m just a native speaker i-)), Vladimírek, Vládík, Láďa..\r\n\r\nand speaking about women\'s surnames, in most cases you use \'-ová\', there are some exceptions like Petrů, Janů..\r\n', '2003-06-22 14:38:40', 1022, 0),
(1026, 13, 'micval', '', 'Re:', 'Oh and you don\'t call a woman Haeková, but Haková.. Another good example could be Havel - Havlová..', '2003-06-22 14:40:24', 1021, 0),
(1027, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re:', 'I haven\'t studied Czech morphology (quite complex thing, really, and I\'m Czech ;)) but I suspect that there are several rules (and even more exceptions, as it normally comes :)) \r\n1. gender: feminine, e.g. -enka, -icka, masculine e.g. -cek, -nek, neutrum e.g. -cko, -tko and 2. preceeding letter: e.g. Petr, Petricek but Ivan, Ivanek.\r\n\r\nI can imagine that the gender concept does not make the rich morphology any easier for an English speaker but at the same time the variety in options allows to espress the precise nuance just by a slight adaptation of the word.\r\n\r\nWithout going into too much grammar (boring!) I\'d say put -ik to the end (Vladik, Petrik, Skotik) or just soften the ending (Vlada, Peta, Scott doesn\'t work - foreign names can be \'Czehnglisezed\' only to a certain extend) and you should be fairly safe. Actually, maybe I should be more precise, -ik is not as strong as -cek/sek. How I feel it, is Vladicek would probably be a baby/small boy, Vladik a schoolboy and Vlada an adult (possibly a teanager, in his nice moments). It\'s not just the degree of affection you\'re expressing but also kind of relationship. \r\n\r\nIf you really want to go into this in detail, lots of grammar would be unavoidable, I\'m afraid. Maybe if there are specific names or words (it applies to objects as well and relates to kind of \'smallness\' and \'cuteness\' of the thing e.g. stul, stolecek) let me know the words; general explanations are more difficult and complicated by trying to cover the whole area.\r\n\r\nHope this helps a bit,\r\nJana', '2003-06-23 15:53:19', 1021, 0),
(1028, 16, 'Jennifer Keating', 'jennifer@hansetstainless.com', 'Hledám učitelkou cetiny v Portlandu, OR', 'I lived in the Czech Republic for 6 years and would like some help with grammar.\r\nDik!\r\nJennifer Keating', '2003-06-24 03:53:05', 0, 0),
(1029, 15, 'Jennifer Keating', 'jennifer@hansetstainless.com', 'Hledám učitelkou cetiny v Portlandu, OR', 'Mluvit umím.  Potřebuju pomoc s gramatikou, prosím.\r\nDejkuji za pozornost.\r\njk', '2003-06-24 22:25:27', 0, 0),
(1030, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Diminutives, female names', 'Scott replied to my earlier question on this subject but, again, I\'m a bit unclear. If I wanted to call a man or boy named Vladimir, for example, little Vlad....would I add cek or sek? Vladicek or Vladisek? And does this also apply to womens names? \r\nAlso, why do I notice that most Czech females last names end with \"ova\"...for example, if her last name is Hasek, is she called Hasekova and her brother is just Hasek??? Thanks for all replies!  \r\n   \r\nHi,\r\n\r\nmost of your questions have already been answered as best as is possible without a lot of grammar. Here is a little bit more to fill in the gaps:\r\n\r\n<b>Diminutives:</b> The -cek, -ik, etc. at the ends of nouns are called diminutives. Nine years ago -- the last time I studied Czech in a school -- they spoke of two degrees of diminutives for most words, with some only being able to receive one. I like Jana\'s note, uninfluenced by textbooks, that you can get a sort of weak diminutive just by \"softening\" the ending, without ending the \"official\" diminutive endings. If I had known that, maybe I wouldn\'t have been so surprised and confused when my acquaintance called my friend \"Honziiii!\"\r\n\r\nDiminutives say either that something is small, that we like it, or we are being ironic and we don\'t like it at all, or we just have a close relationship to it. \r\n\r\nWhen talking to a baby, you would tend to use diminutives even for things that you\'d otherwise talk about normally. This can even lead to weird expressions like \"obrovitansky balvanicek\" (a nice little enorm-a-lormous boulder). Trust me, I\'m a parent.\r\n\r\nSometimes diminutive forms have become so tied to a certain meaning that to say that meaning, you use a diminutive even though you\'re not really trying to say something is \"small or dear\". For example, even though a \"vkladni knizka\" (some kind of banking thing, not sure how to translate it, literally a \"deposit book\") really is generally small, even a Guinness-sized 3 foot by 3 foot vkladni knizka would still be a vkladni knizka and not a vkladni kniha.\r\n\r\nIf it\'s a noun, it can be made into a diminutive generally. Women\'s names are no problem; they just have a different set of endings, generally with -ka as the \"end of the ending\". \r\n\r\nMany verbs and adjectives can be made diminutive too; some have special diminutive forms based on \"baby talk\" that are far from the original (jist -- papat), and are used even among adults talking among each other who need those words\' nuances.\r\n\r\nEven really abstract things can be made diminutive -- e.g. problemky, starustky (little worries) -- if there are cases where they are \"close to a life\" and/or if they are somehow \"little\" or someone wants to \"belittle\" them, e.g. statecek -- little state.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nJust like in English, women are linguistically \"owned\" by their father, and then by their husband in Czech... just more so. That is, \"Haskova\" means \"Hasek\'s\" (wife, daughter). Although as Czech linguist Pavel Eisner pointed out, not even Czech is as bad as old-fashioned English usage - they would never call Robert Piper\'s wife anything like \"Mrs. Robert Piper\"!\r\n\r\nBesides the \"Petru\" etc. that Jana mentioned, there are the \"adjective\" last names, which receive a female ending, but not one indicating their ownership - just the female form of the given adjective. For example, pani Zridkavesela, pani Brzobohata.', '2003-06-25 15:02:14', 1021, 0),
(1031, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re:  Pár poznámek ke zdrobnělinám.', '1) V četině není přípona -sek. Ve slově Matýsek je přípona -ek, souhláska \"s\" patří ke kmeni: MateS + ek -> Matýsek (kmenové e se pravidelně prodlouilo a zúilo: e -> é -> í/ý, jako plech + ek -> plíek, flek + ek -> flíček).\r\n\r\n2) Toté platí pro domnělou příponu -ček, souhláska \"č\" je kmenové \"č\" nebo kmenové \"k/c\" změněné na \"č\" a patří ke kmeni, přípona je opět -ek:\r\n\r\nmíč + ek -> míček\r\ndrak + ek -> dráček\r\npták + ek -> ptáček\r\nvlk + ek -> vlček\r\nstřevíc + ek -> střevíček\r\nměsíc + ek -> měsíček\r\n\r\n3) Dalí běná přípona deminutiv muského rodu je -ík:\r\n\r\nbič + ík -> bičík\r\npes (ps-a) + ík -> psík\r\nvůz (voz-u) + ík -> vozík\r\n\r\n4) Příponu -ek lze připojit i k deminutivu. Tak vznikají sloené přípony ek + ek -> eček, ík + ek -> íček:\r\n\r\nhrad + ek -> hrádek + ek -> hrádeček\r\ndům (dom-u) + ek -> domek + ek -> domeček\r\nnos + ík -> nosík + ek -> nosíček\r\n\r\nněkdy deminutiva nahradila původní slova:\r\n\r\n(je) + ek -> jeek + ek -> jeeček\r\n(krt) + ek -> krtek + ek -> krteček\r\n\r\n5) Deminutiva en. rodu se tvoří příponou -ka:\r\n\r\nstrana + ka -> stránka\r\nkost + ka -> kůstka/kostka (s jiným významem)\r\nzahrada + ka -> zahrádka\r\nruka + ka -> ručka\r\n\r\nSloené přípony: ce (stč. cě) + ka -> ečka; ice (stč. icě) + ka -> ička;\r\n\r\nzahrada + ce -> (zahrádcě) + ka -> zahrádečka\r\n(ov-) + ce -> ovce + ka -> ovečka\r\nruka + ice -> (ručicě) + ka -> ručička\r\nhlava + ice -> hlavice + ka -> hlavička\r\n\r\n6) Deminutiva stř. rodu mají příponu -ko a sloené přípony -ečko, -íčko: \r\n\r\nslovo + ko -> slůvko\r\nzelí + ce -> (zelíce) + ko -> zelíčko\r\n\r\n', '2003-06-25 18:09:23', 1021, 0),
(1032, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re:  Pár poznámek ke zdrobnělinám.', 'Thanks for the help Vladimir.  That makes a lot more sense.  I still have a long way to go before I get the feel of this language.  I know living there is going to help immensely.  Thanks for all the input everyone, as you have answered some of my brooding questions as well.  \r\n\r\nScott', '2003-06-26 11:36:17', 1031, 0),
(1033, 13, 'Zuzana', 'snablova@post.cz', 'Re:', 'Hello, I am czech and I want to answer your question. Vladicek you can use for a small boy, but for a grown-up you just say Vlada. Vladicek is too childish.', '2003-06-26 16:59:40', 1021, 0),
(1034, 13, 'Zuzana', 'snablova@post.cz', 'Re:', 'It is Polish, not Czech. And sorry, i can not understand to it. Only few words.  ', '2003-06-26 17:02:01', 1023, 0),
(1035, 13, 'James', 'jburton1@cfl.rr.com', 'Czech language diacritic codes--Prosím vás!', 'Can some,kind \"Someone\" out there please tell me where I can find a table for Czech language diacritic codes.  I can compose e-mails readily now in most western European languages.  But my search for consistently usable diacritic codes for Czech language has been unsuccessful.  I have experimented and found a few, but many (especially of the lower case letters using the háchek {sp} ) are inferior in quality and often will actually change font size in the middle of a sentence (with irreversible results). I am also unable to find the letter \"u\" with the angstrom diacritic mark (like a degree sign). Pomoc! Pomoc, prosím prátele (sp)!  Many thanks.  Dekuji (sp) pekne (sp).  JAMES  ', '2003-07-08 06:35:25', 0, 1),
(1036, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech language diacritic codes--Prosím vás!', 'Despite being a techie and having been involved in Czech for years, there\'s not much I can say from a distance and in a hurry. What I CAN note, however, that might be little-known to other people who will in other respects give you more useful replies, is that: <b>Outlook will often mess up accented Czech characters in a way resembling what you mention</b>. The bug is apparently in the core code, as I worked for the company that localized Outlook into Czech, the problem was reported to Microsoft, and they didn\'t do anything about it.\r\n\r\nI don\'t have time to explore the problem to give a guaranteed-accurate answer, but here are some general, <b>probably</b>-true statements about the matter:\r\n- what\'s actually happening is that, upon hitting an accented character, Outlook is switching to an entirely different font, can\'t remember which one but as far as I can remember, it\'s some font Asians use that\'s capable of displaying European letters. Yes, I know that\'s weird.\r\n- the problem doesn\'t occur while writing the mail, but rather while reading it and re-using it\r\n- it appears even when writing plain-text (where fonts should theoretically be irrelevant)\r\n- Especially after all these years since the testing where this was probed in detail, I have no idea what a recipient, let alone a non-Outlook recipient, sees when such a mail is sent out; considering that this problem occurs even when writing plaintext, there\'s plenty of cause to believe the problem will actually be irrelevant for the end recipient (coz snad potom umoznuje odpoustet Microsoft to, ze se na dany problem podle vseho zhluboka vykaslali)\r\n- on my machine right here (Czech Windows 98 SE) you can convert the weird-looking text into good-looking text simply by launching Notepad, cutting the text, pasting it into Notepad, cutting the text again, and pasting it back into Outlook. \r\n\r\nMaybe this is related to your problem, maybe not.\r\n\r\nIt\'ll probably help if you tell what environment (operating system(s) and computer program(s)) you want to be writing in, and where you want it to be correctly displayed.\r\n\r\nBy the way, most Czechs just buy their Czech Windows (or their computers with Czech windows installed), and... go. And when writing for any medium where they know accent marks will likely be damaged, they write \"cesky\", i.e. \"tsesky\", i.e. without accent marks. Or they stubbornly refuse.\r\n\r\nGenerally speaking: every Unicode font should normally have support for all characters -- and not in \"weird\" versions. That\'s what makes me suspect that you are running into ye auld Outlook bug or something similar.\r\n\r\n> I am also unable to find the letter \"u\" with the angstrom diacritic mark (like a degree sign).\r\n\r\nTry this page:\r\nhttp://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/czechkeyboard/\r\n\r\nCheers\r\n\r\nErik\r\nerik at NO SPAM sky dot I MEAN IT DAMMIT cz', '2003-07-08 15:51:35', 1035, 0),
(1037, 15, 'Brandon Sowers', 'stewardoftheearth@yahoo.com', 'Re: Czech teacher needed in Atlanta, GA', 'I am in Athens, GA, about 60 miles away, and I also would like a short-term Czech language tutor before I leave for a student exchange in late august.', '2003-07-11 01:47:37', 1004, 0),
(1038, 20, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Welcome', 'Miredita:\r\n\r\nWelcome to the PST forum. If you have been invited to come to Albania as a Peace Corps Volunteer, this is a place for you to ask questions of PC Albania staff and/or discuss any pre-arrival issues you may have. Albania RPCVs are also welcome to contribute.\r\n\r\nPlease note that, although we will try to respond as accurately as possible to any queries, we are preparing the first PST in Albania after 5 years so some policies and details may change at the last minute. Also, be reminded that this is not an official Peace Corps website.\r\n\r\nDominik Lukes\r\nPre-Service Training Director\r\n\r\n', '2003-07-14 19:29:41', 0, 0),
(1039, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Internet Provider', 'I was wondering if any of you who live in Czech or who travel there quite frequently might know what the best free internet provider is in Cz.  I am going to search on google as well, but I figured that many of you might be able to tell me from personal experience.  \r\n\r\nThanks a million!\r\nScott', '2003-07-17 23:26:48', 0, 0),
(1040, 13, 'John', 'Tellman12@aol.com', 'the months', 'I read that every Czech month literally means something from that month, for example, that January is \'leden,\' which is from the Czech word \'led\', which means \'ice.\' Can someone please give me the etymology for all 12 Czech months? This will be greatly appreciated. -John', '2003-07-18 00:24:57', 0, 0),
(1041, 20, 'Robbie Hammer', '', 'Hello', 'I just wanted to say hi. My name is Robbie Hammer and I will be part of the new group of PC volunteers arriving in September. I look forward to learning more on this site.', '2003-07-18 10:31:22', 0, 0),
(1045, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: the months', 'John, I don\'t think it\'s that straitforward (there seem to be some links and spotable origins but not all are clear to me). This is my best guess:\r\nLeden - led=ice\r\nUnor - not a clue\r\nBrezen - brezi=pregnant (for animal/mammals only)\r\nDuben - not sure, dub=oak (the tree, not for wood)\r\nKveten - kvet=flower, kvest=blossom\r\nCerven - not sure, cerveny=red (rippening fruit?)\r\nCervenec - don\'t know\r\nSrpen - srp=grass-hook (a hand instrument used to cut the grass)\r\nZari - zarit=shine/glare\r\nRijen - rije=don\'t know the English equivalent, its when stags (or other wild animals) fight for females (includes the actions that follows carried out by the winners ;))\r\nListopad - list=leaf, padat=fall\r\nProsinec - prosit=plead/ask/beg\r\n\r\nIt looks pretty much as if the names kind of reflect what was significant in that particular time (nature observations and seasonal rural work).\r\nHope it helps a bit,\r\nJana', '2003-07-18 18:21:31', 1040, 0),
(1046, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Woo-hoo!', 'I just found the link to this in the Albania blog... and I can\'t wait to look through to see what info is available here. I read through the job postings for the positions to help us with PST. May the right people come forward to join the team! I looked up Lezhe on my map immediately, and found it right next to the \"place of touristic interest\" called Lissus. How nice!\r\n\r\nI\'m sending out the info about this site to the others that I am in contact with. I look forward to being in communication with the team in Albania already, as well as with my fellow wannabe PCVs!\r\n\r\nEva', '2003-07-19 03:05:44', 0, 0),
(1047, 17, 'Raist', 'raistilin@centrum.cz', ':o)', 'Ahoj. Pěkná stránka(y). Alespoň konečně vidím, e se neučíme jen my cizí jazyky, ale e se také někdo učí i tu nai četinu. :D', '2003-07-20 03:10:51', 0, 0),
(1048, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: the months', 'The English equivalent of \"říje\" is \"rutting\".  We also say that rutting season is mating season.', '2003-07-20 11:52:22', 1045, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1049, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: the months', 'Thanks, Scott. I thought \'mating\' didn\'t neccessarily involve the fighting bit, does rutting? The 1st immage that springs to my ming for \'rije\' are 2 stags pushing each other with their antlers locked. Also, is \'rutting\' specific to stags or can it be used to all animals?\r\nTHX,\r\nJ.', '2003-07-20 21:54:15', 1048, 0),
(1050, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: the months', 'Únor has the root -nor-, meaning is not clear, related words are:\r\n      nořit (se), ponořit (se) = to immerse, to dive, to submerge\r\n      ponurý = gray, dark, darksome, gloomy\r\n\r\nZáří is not derived from \"zářit\". The Old-Czech form is \"zářuj\" from \"za řujě\" (= za říje, in rutting season).\r\n\r\nProsinec is probably derived from \"siný\" (= grey, gray) as an oppositum to \"červen/červenec\" (červený = red).\r\n\r\nBtw, \"červený\" is derived from \"červ\" (= worm) and originally means \"(cloth) dyed by means of worm\".\r\n', '2003-07-21 15:45:16', 1045, 0),
(1051, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: the months', 'This is the definition I got from dictionary.com:\r\n\r\nAn annually recurring condition or period of sexual excitement and reproductive activity in male deer. \r\nA condition or period of mammalian sexual activity, such as estrus.\r\n\r\n-- Yes it is mainly associated with the fighting side of it when people are talking about rutting in every day speech, but it does also include the sexual side that occurs afterwards, as this is the main reason the staggs react in such a way.\r\n\r\nScott', '2003-07-22 07:06:08', 1049, 0),
(1052, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Looking good!', 'I emailed out the July 21 update to the people whose email addresses I have, and referenced them to this site.  The photos are fun to look at, too!  I\'m sending that info out to my family to take a look at.  Thank you for spending the time to put these things together for us!', '2003-07-23 21:02:35', 0, 0),
(1054, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Shortwave Radio? and other questions', 'Do you recommend that we bring a shortwave radio?\r\n\r\nHow about winter coats and boots? Bring\'em or get\'em there...?\r\n\r\nCan we send M-bags to the Peace Corps office? If so, would you please confirm the address?\r\n\r\nIs Albania a fragile environment for cameras? An RPCV in Turkmenistan told me that she went through four cameras (mostly a problem of dust, I think) until she finally settled in with a water-proof, underwater-type camera. I\'m thinking Turkmenistan is a harsher climate, in that sense, than Albania...\r\n\r\nIs there anything else in particular that you would give guidance on, either to bring or not to bring?\r\n\r\nMany thanks for your advice!\r\nEva', '2003-07-25 08:02:58', 0, 0),
(1055, 20, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Looking good!', 'Hi Eva (and all),\r\nI\'m glad you like the site. I didn\'t expect people to find it so soon, so I\'ve been a little remiss in regular updates. I\'ll try to do better. Right now we are busy looking for training facilities in Lezha. Very soon we will start looking for host families in our five training satellites: Laç, Mamurras, Shënkoll, Shëngjin and Balldren - when I have more pictures I will post them too.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-07-25 13:29:33', 1052, 0),
(1057, 20, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Shortwave Radio? and other questions', 'Eva, I cannot answer all your questions fully just yet but I\'ll do the best I can.\r\n\r\nShort-wave radios are always a good idea even though at least Tirana is also well-served by FM stations, including the BBC Worldservice which broadcasts in both English and Albanian. But since you will be spending very little time in Tirana, this may not be of much use. Albania is a mountainous country and there are many places where only shortwave reception may be available.\r\n\r\nWe haven\'t decided on a clothing recommendation list and mailing policy yet but when we do, I\'ll post it here - it will also get to you through official Peace Corps channels. The way things are at the moment, however, don\'t count on being able to mail things to the Peace Corps office, it\'s not equipped to handle packages.\r\n\r\nI\'ve been using my Minolta digital camera here for three weeks now and it hasn\'t suffered from the dust (which is everywhere in the summer) and I haven\'t heard of any problems from people who\'ve been here longer. But I never had any camera problems even when I worked in Central Asia which does have more dust than Albania. But as always, the more durable a camera you, the better. A decent camera bag should also help.\r\n\r\nHope this helps. More info coming when available.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-07-25 16:10:15', 1054, 0),
(1058, 20, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Hello', 'Hi Robbie,\r\nI\'ve put more information on the site and more is forthcoming. I hope to update at least once a week.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-07-25 16:30:51', 1041, 0),
(1059, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Internet Provider', 'Hi Scott,\r\n\r\nI don\'t know who\'s the BEST, but Volny is certainly the most famous and among the oldest. Their address is of course http://www.volny.cz.', '2003-07-28 14:39:18', 1039, 0),
(1060, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Internet Provider', 'Thanks Erik.  I will check them out.', '2003-07-31 01:30:20', 1059, 0),
(1061, 17, 'Xema', '', 'question', 'Can somebody explain me one thing about Czech language?', '2003-07-31 19:12:00', 1047, 0),
(1062, 17, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question', 'Which thing?', '2003-08-04 15:59:50', 1061, 0),
(1063, 17, 'Val Policky', 'vpolicky@cox.net', 'Help with phone calls', 'I need to make several personal calls to the Czech Republi but my Czech language skills are poor. I need to conference someone who can assist me with the language. I am willing to pay for your time. Can anyone help? my email is vpolicky@cox.net Thanks', '2003-08-08 00:56:16', 0, 1),
(1064, 20, 'Carlos Terrones', '', 'Money', 'Hello:\r\n\r\n     I am one of the invitees who will be arriving this September. Question?  How accessible are the ATM\'s and Credit Cards in Albania. Is it better to bring extra cash or travelers checks? \r\n\r\nThanks, \r\nCarlos', '2003-08-13 22:43:42', 0, 0),
(1065, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Water Quality', 'Hello again! About six of us who are going to be coming to have gotten into contact with each other. It seems like we\'ll have a fun group, with a whole range of diverse backgrounds. :)\r\n\r\nWhat can you tell me about the water quality in Albania? Have you determined recommendations for treating drinking water?\r\n\r\nHow about this: Any idea what Albania women wear to sleep in / what will be appropriate nightwear for living with a host family?\r\n\r\nI\'ve been watching the weather in Tirana... looks like you\'re staying a good 10-15 degrees ahead of Minneapolis, though with somewhat lower humidity. \r\n\r\nThanks much for any info on my questions!\r\nEva', '2003-08-14 04:36:07', 0, 1),
(1066, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Re: Money', 'Hello, Carlos! I\'d love to draw you into the web of other folks signed up to go. Would you mind emailing me at email4eva@yahoo.com? -- Eva', '2003-08-14 05:39:44', 1064, 1),
(1067, 20, 'Sabina Malik', 'subeans@hotmail.com', 'What\'s available in Lezhe?', 'Hi\r\n\r\nMy name is Sabina.  I am also an Albania PCV who will be arriving in September.  \r\n\r\nI was just wondering if you could give us some idea of the type of things, that are available in Lezhe or in the areas of Albania in which we will be training.  For example bath and beauty products (shampoo/conditioner, razors, etc) and any of the other minor things, which many of us are probably used to having access to on a daily basis.\r\n\r\nI just want to try and figure out, which things that I should bring along with me, based on what is not available in Albania.\r\n\r\nI\'m also interested in the banking situation in Albania.  If we bring traveller\'s checks, will be there be somewhere were we can cash them?  Can we open up chequing accounts?\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\nSabina', '2003-08-14 08:06:28', 0, 1),
(1068, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Re: What\'s available in Lezhe?', 'Hello, Sabina! Seven of us have made contact now, Carlos comes in at number eight and you at nine. Why don\'t you email me at email4eva@yahoo.com so that I can connect you in? -- Eva', '2003-08-14 23:40:04', 1067, 1),
(1069, 20, 'Enejda Gurja', 'egurja@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Hello', 'Hello to you all and welcome.\r\n\r\nEnejda Gurja\r\nTraining Manager', '2003-08-15 19:08:53', 0, 0),
(1071, 20, 'Enejda Gurja', 'egurja@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Water Quality', 'Hello Eva,\r\nHere are some answers to your questions that I hope can help you prepare.\r\n\r\nWhat can you tell me about the water quality in Albania? Have you determined recommendations for treating drinking water?\r\n\r\nWater quality in Albania varies from place to place. However bottled water is available anywhere and upon your arrival PC will provide each of you with a water filter which you will use during your stay in Albania.\r\n\r\nAny idea what Albania women wear to sleep in / what will be appropriate nightwear for living with a host family? \r\n\r\nWell, nightwear in Albania is similar to anywhere else gowns, pijamas, shorts and T-shirt. However when you are going to live with another family and you are not sure how they will be, it is better to be on the safe side and choose simple nightwear. Normal pijamas, or pijama pants and t-shirts would be fine. Winters are cold in Albania so perhaps flannel pijamas might be a good option for that time as well. \r\n\r\nSee you in September.\r\n\r\n', '2003-08-15 19:20:40', 1065, 0),
(1072, 20, 'Enejda Gurja', 'egurja@al.peacecoprs.gov', 'Re: Money', 'Hello Carlos,\r\n\r\nThere are very few places in Albania (mainly in Tirana only) that will take a credit card and ATM\'s are not common. However you can use them (CC\'s or bank cards) through a few banks to make withdrawals. \r\nYou can cash traveler\'s checks but they will not be cashed as easily as in the US. In most places(banks usually) there is a commission fee to be paid and it might take up to 7 business days for the transaction. \r\nThe best solution will be to bring cash and a bank card (or CC) just in case you travel out of the country or for emergencies. \r\nPlease feel free to ask more specific question if you need to I will be glad to help. ', '2003-08-15 19:38:55', 1064, 0),
(1073, 20, 'Enejda Gurja', 'egurja@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: What\'s available in Lezhe?', 'Hi Sabina,\r\n\r\nYou can find a lot of beauty products almost all over Albania and quite a variety I might say. If you are not picky about bath and beauty products then you can get most of them here (such as shampoos/conditioners, razors etc.) Howere if there are any particular products that are specific (face creams, products, hypo-alergenic lotions, or any skin specifis products) then you might want to bring some with you. Whereas you can find all kinds of product in Albania the brands and types of products will not be the same as in the US. \r\n\r\n\r\nAs for the banking situation you can cash traveler\'s checks at most banks but  it will be done at a fee and probably the transaction will take a few days. \r\nAs for bank accounts Peace Corps will set up accounts for you in Albania (there are no checking accounts in Albania as checks are not commonly used) these accounts are \'current accounts\'.\r\n\r\nI hope this helps but feel free to ask about anything else that might help you prepare.\r\n', '2003-08-15 19:52:37', 1067, 0),
(1074, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Re: Hello', 'Thank you, Enejda, for helping us prepare for our time in Albania! \r\n', '2003-08-16 03:27:45', 1069, 0),
(1075, 20, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Money', 'Carlos,\r\none more note on ATMs and money. I\'ve been trying (albeit not very persistently) to withdraw money from a bank using my Mastercard for quite a while. I\'m told that there is one in a Greek bank in Tirana but I simply haven\'t had the time to do it. The reason I\'m sharing this story is that training is a busy time for both trainers and trainees and you will not have much time either. Chances of being able to take a break during business hours during training and go to Tirana (about 90 minutes away) are slim (maybe once or twice) and since the ATMs are reportedly often closed/broken/out of money, you\'re not very likely to be able withdraw money using a credit card during training. And after training you\'re going to your site which (since there will be no placements in Tirana) is very unlikely to have an ATM, at all.\r\n\r\nTo cut a long story short, if you want to bring money you want easy access to, bring cash. You will be able to give your cash and cards to the Admin Officer for safe keeping but it will reduce the ease with which you can get to it.\r\n\r\nOne more thing: The few places that accept cards are Visa. I\'m told there are a few spots where you can use Mastercard but I have yet to see one.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-08-16 12:21:23', 1064, 0),
(1076, 20, 'Teri', 'pa2albania@yahoo.com', 'Re: What\'s available in Lezhe?', 'Thanks for the tips about personal products.  \r\n\r\nWhat about hair salons and color? ;-)  Only your hair dresser knows in the US -What about over the counter products and availability of hair salons for cuts and color, in various locations?  This is Very, Very important you know!  \r\n\r\nGosh - I was expecting more \"older volunteers\" and here I am, I think the most \"senior\" with this question!  (I\'ll be 46 the day before we all meet at stagin)!  and then you can guess about the color!\r\n\r\nThanks.', '2003-08-18 20:16:24', 1067, 1),
(1077, 17, 'Milan Bro', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: Help with phone calls', 'If you want me to help you,I\'ll do it and it\'ll be free.I\'ll be pleased to speak to you and practise my conversdation skills again.Send me your e_mail please where you\'ll write what you want.Thanks a lot.\r\nBye Milan from the Czech republic', '2003-08-20 01:43:12', 1063, 0),
(1078, 17, 'Val Policky', 'vpolicky@palpro.com', 'Re: Help with phone calls', 'Milan:\r\n\r\nDekuju pro vas email.\r\n\r\nThat is pretty much the extent of my Czech language capability.\r\n\r\nMy email address is vpolicky@palpro.com.\r\n\r\nMy situation is that I am trying to contact several people in the south central part of the Czech republic to try to find a missing link in the ancestry on my father\'s side. I am not a genealogy nut or anything like that, but I am attending a reunion of my family this summer and there are about 2000 descendants from one person named Karel Policky who came to the United States in the mid 1800s and we would love to find the link to the old country. He was born in a village called Semikovice, near Rouchovany.\r\n\r\nI would like to contact these people and just talk with them, but my Czech skills make it frustrating for both sides.\r\n\r\nI have visited the Czech Republic on two different occasions and totally enjoyed it.\r\n\r\nIf you send me your phone number, I will be happy to call you direct to see if this can work.\r\n\r\nWhat city do you live in?\r\n\r\nHow did you learn English?\r\n\r\nAnxious to talk with you.\r\n\r\nHave a nice day.\r\n\r\nVal \r\nWork: 1-949-766-5342\r\nCell: 1-949-338-7900\r\n', '2003-08-21 18:50:11', 1077, 1),
(1079, 20, 'mark kritz', 'mkritz20@scad.student.edu', 'hello', 'Hello my name is mark kritz I will be heading to Albania as a PCV in September and just wanted to make contact. ', '2003-08-21 19:39:32', 0, 0),
(1080, 20, 'Enejda Gurja', 'egurja@al.peacecorp.gov', 'Re: hello', 'Hello Mark and welcome. I hope you find the information in this forum useful and if you don\'t please feel free to ask questions that you think  might help you prepare for Albania.\r\n\r\nAll the best\r\n\r\nEnejda Gurja\r\nTraining Manager', '2003-08-21 21:42:09', 1079, 0),
(1081, 20, 'JP Piorkowski', 'jpiorko1@excite.com', 'Signing in from Portland, Oregon', 'Greetings all!  I\'ll be joining the group this September and just wanted to get in the mix on the pre-service chat.  I look forward to getting in touch and learning more about your backgrounds.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nJP\r\n', '2003-08-24 06:49:15', 1068, 0),
(1082, 13, 'Mario', 'Suitbefit@aol.com', 'Meeting Czech People', 'My fiancee will be moving to the states soon. I do not speak Czech well she does not speak English well. We both want to learn the others respective language. I am also looking for Czech people to meet in the Atlantic City area. Please someone contact me . Thanks', '2003-08-24 07:07:53', 0, 1),
(1083, 20, 'Nathan Lew', 'natelew@hotmail.com', 'shoes', 'Hi I was wondering about the footwear that we should bring.  I was planning on bringing a pair dress shoes, sandals, sturdy walking shoes, and was thinking about getting a pair of hiking/winter/waterproof boots.  I\'ve got the first three but havent decided on the boots. Do you think we should be needing them?\r\n\r\nNate\r\n', '2003-08-26 04:03:26', 0, 0),
(1084, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'Power Supply / Battery Rechargers', 'Hello!\r\n\r\nPerhaps some of this info is on the CD we\'re getting, but I haven\'t seen that yet...\r\n\r\nDo you have any recommendations on battery rechargers? I talked to someone who was recenty living in Albania (though I don\'t know where) who had an average of about 2 hours of electricity each day. Does that sound typical? \r\n\r\nAny recommendations on battery rechargers?\r\n\r\nHow about this... is it likely that we might transfer information from our laptops (letters, digital photos) to another computer to email back home (from the PC office, or an Internet cafe)? \r\n\r\nI was at Circuit City recently and discovered that they have these nifty devices for transferring data... you pop them into the USB port to use them, and they store something like 128 MB of data on something the size of a keychain. Do you think something like this might be useful?\r\n\r\nSuch things are more high tech than my norm here, but it did seem like an option that would be lightweight and NOT overtly theft-prone.\r\n\r\nThanks much for any info!\r\nEva', '2003-08-27 04:10:30', 0, 1),
(1085, 15, 'buba', '', 'czech words used in everyday american life', 'does anyone know what czech words we use now in everydayh american life', '2003-08-27 08:47:12', 0, 0),
(1086, 20, 'Dominik Luke', '', 'Re: shoes', '', '2003-08-27 11:20:20', 1083, 0),
(1087, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'robot... (from Karel Capek\'s work R.U.R.)\r\n\r\nOther than that there are none, at least none of which I can be sure.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-08-27 20:31:28', 1085, 0),
(1088, 20, 'justin parmenter', 'xhasti@hotmail.com', 'Re: shoes', 'i know you weren\'t asking me, but i just wanted to mention that my boots became my best friends when i was a peace corps volunteer in albania.  if you\'re planning on getting out and seeing the country, especially villages, you will be doing lots of walking in the mud.  spend a hundred bucks on a comfy pair of boots and you won\'t be sorry.  lots of good deals at campmor.com and sierratradingpost.com\r\n\r\njustin', '2003-08-28 02:48:48', 1086, 0),
(1089, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Meeting Czech People', 'So, what is the question you are trying to ask?  Are you trying to ask which ways would be the best to teach eachother your native language?  I think that she will probably learn English much faster than you will learn Czech, because she will be in a position where she is forced to use it on an every day basis.  As far as meeting other Czechs in the Atlantic City area goes, I am not sure.  I know that many people have asked for help in certain areas, but I have never seen a reply post to their inquiry.  You might want to try looking around the community and asking questions as to if there are any clubs, meetings, or just if anyone knows of any Czech natives that live there.  You might want to contact the chamber of commerce.  I am not sure if they could help, but they may be able to.  \r\n\r\nGood luck!\r\n\r\nScott\r\n\r\n', '2003-08-28 03:33:46', 1082, 0),
(1090, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: shoes', 'Sorry about the empty message earlier (my internet connection got in the way). There isn\'t much I can add to Justin\'s advice. I was just visiting some prospective host families and those who have their own houses often live off dirt roads with lots of rocks which with turn muddy as soon as it starts raining. The bottom line is, get good shoes. Another thing to consider as you pack is you can get dress shoes and sandals here (albeit not very cheaply) but I haven\'t seen any good hiking boots anywhere. \r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-08-28 10:45:11', 1088, 0),
(1091, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Power Supply / Battery Rechargers', 'Eva, the answer to your questions is: yes, yes, yes. \r\n\r\n1. Some of the questions you\'re asking are answered on the CD.\r\n\r\n2. Your informant was accurate. There are places in Albania (for example the city of Shkodra about an hour away from the training site and that may have a volunteer) that only have power for two hours a day. What is more typical is for the power to be off for some hours every day (anywhere between 2 and 12) on a vaguely predicable schedule. That means, definitely bring a second (or even a third) battery for your laptop possibly with an external charger. You may want to look into a solar charger but I\'ve never used one so I cannot make any recommendations.\r\n\r\n3. Yes. I definitely recommend a USB keychain memory. We use them all the time at the PC office and since we\'ll be giving you some data, this might be a handy way of doing this. I\'m not sure how easy this would be at an internet cafe because their USB ports are usually on the back of their computers. Make sure you get one with a USB cable extension for cases like that.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-08-28 10:57:59', 1084, 0),
(1092, 15, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', '(in everyday military life)\r\n\r\npistol ... from píala (= pipe,fife - music instrument)\r\nhowitz ... from houfnice (houf = crowd,drove)', '2003-08-28 13:37:05', 1087, 0),
(1093, 20, 'Whitney', 'whitmirts@hotmail.com', 'March group of PCV', 'Ill be a PCV in March doing English Education and I just wanted to establish contact with those that are leaving soon and maintain until I arrive in March.\r\nGod Speed', '2003-08-28 23:31:56', 0, 0),
(1094, 15, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', '... howitzer ...\r\n\r\npolka ... a lively Bohemian dance tune in 2/4 time (2/4 = 1/2 = půlka)', '2003-08-29 20:20:31', 1092, 0),
(1095, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'These two words come from Czech origin as well:\r\n\r\n<u><b><i>Pram:</i></b></u>A small dinghy having a flat, snub-nosed bow.\r\n\r\n<I><B><U>Calash</i></U></B>A light carriage with two or four low wheels and a collapsible top.\r\n\r\nThough they are not widely used today, they still coined from Czech words. ', '2003-08-30 00:19:52', 1085, 0),
(1096, 20, 'JP Piorkowski', 'jpiorko1@excite.com', 'Dictionaries and Gifts', 'Ckemi! \r\n\r\nThe information on the CD-Rom is extremely informative and helpful. Thank you for putting it together.  I just hope the song and dance routine will be included in the training program! \r\n\r\nTwo questions:\r\n\r\n1. Will the Peace Corps supply Albanian-English dictionaries with our training materials? \r\n2. What types of gifts do you suggest for our host families?  Friends have suggested can-openers and wine-openers.  Your thoughts?\r\n\r\nI look forward to meeting everyone in a few weeks! Thanks again.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nJP\r\n\r\n', '2003-08-31 20:18:56', 0, 1),
(1097, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Dictionaries and Gifts', 'Dear JP,\r\nI\'m glad you liked the CD. Here are your answers: 1. Yes, Peace Corps will provide you with a dictionary - such as there is (the selection is rather limited). It will be the Hippocrene dictionary which is the only bi-directional dictionary that can be carried around. \r\n\r\n2. My personal recommendation for gifts for family and others is anything that has some images of where you come from (be it can openers, fridge magnets or calendars).\r\n\r\nDominik\r\n\r\nPS: The song and dance routine will of course be a part of your training, but it will be you who will be doing the singing and dancing.', '2003-09-01 00:55:09', 1096, 0),
(1098, 13, 'Veronica', 'stormborne@hotmail.com', 'Useful Czech Phrases Translation?', 'Hi everyone. I\'m new to the forum and I am wondering if anyone would be willing to help me translate some alternate Czech phrases? I have a friend in the Bohumin area of the CZR and, although he speaks English very well, I would really enjoy being able to converse more to him in Czech. Moreover, I am hoping to visit the country later this year. I have already written my own little \'translation book\' from information found on the internet, but it\'s all pretty basic know-how. I\'d like to learn more slang, how to give compliments & so on (at least until I can buy more books on the language). I have a number of sentences that would be very helpful to me if converted from English to Czech (which I\'ll list below), so if someone has the time to help me learn how to say these phrases, I\'d really appreciate it.\r\n\r\n\"Really?\"\r\n\r\n\"Okay.\"\r\n\r\n\"That\'s great!\"\r\n\r\n\"That\'s very nice of you.\"\r\n\r\n\"I would really like that.\"\r\n\r\n\"I don\'t know.\"\r\n\r\n\"Will you show me?\"\r\n\r\n\"Cool!\" (or any other happy slang word)\r\n\r\n\"What\'s new?\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s so beautiful.\"\r\n\r\n\"I hope you enjoy/like it\" (as when giving a present)\r\n\r\n\"I love it\"\r\n\r\n\"Let\'s go!\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s very nice to meet you.\"\r\n\r\n\"I think you are all very talented.\"\r\n\r\n\"I like it.\"\r\n\r\n\"I don\'t like it.\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s delicious!\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s really hot/cold out here.\"\r\n\r\n\"You\'re cute. (just in case...;) )\"\r\n\r\n\"Go away. (also just in case)\"\r\n\r\n\"Oh, shut up\"\r\n\r\n\"Just a minute...\"\r\n\r\n\"I\'m coming.\"\r\n\r\n\"I don\'t drink/smoke.\"\r\n\r\n\"You have a very nice family.\"\r\n\r\n\"Excuse me, do you know where the ______ is?\"\r\n\r\n\"So, what are your interests?\"\r\n\r\n\"Thanks for having me over.\"\r\n\r\n\"I\'ll see you later.\"\r\n\r\n\"Take care.\"\r\n\r\nThat\'s currently all I can think of. Again, if anyone would be willing to translate some or all of these phrases to Czech (some help regarding pronounciation would also be great) I\'d be one happy American ;). I know it\'s a lot to ask, but I would, truely, be ever so grateful.\r\n\r\nThanks for reading,\r\n\r\nVeronica', '2003-09-01 03:08:08', 0, 0),
(1099, 13, 'brett', 'idiobrett@hotmail.com', 'verbs', 'I want to know if any one can recomend a good english - czech book I am\r\ninterested in learning about infinitive verbs and their conjugations. for example...to read = cist\r\n\r\nI read = ja ctu\r\nyou read = ty ctes\r\nhe, she, it reads = on,ona,ono cte\r\nwe read = my cteme\r\nyou read (formal) (( vosotros in spanish)) = ???\r\nthey read = oni ctdu\r\n\r\nthe pattern must be outlined... this is what I seek. Please help....\r\nvisit my web page....www.idiomusic.com\r\n\r\nbrett', '2003-09-04 07:35:59', 0, 1),
(1100, 20, 'Robbie Hammer', '', 'Surge Protectors', 'Can Surge Protectors be to be used for Albanian voltage be bought in Albania? Thanks for your help.', '2003-09-04 08:46:27', 0, 0),
(1101, 20, 'Robbie Hammer', '', 'Dress Jackets', 'One more question, last one, I promise..\r\nIs a Dress Jacket/Sports Coat needed for the men?\r\nThanks Again.\r\nRobbie', '2003-09-04 23:10:17', 0, 0),
(1102, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Surge Protectors', 'Yes, they can be bought in Albania but it may be a while before you get the chance to buy it. The ones we\'re using are APC SurgeArrest e10 rated for 220-240V and 50/60Hz, so I recommend you get something similar (ours are pretty bulky) before you leave.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-09-06 16:47:26', 1100, 0),
(1103, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Dress Jackets', 'You should bring some attire suitable for occasions when you need to project a professional image. A business suit is ideal for men. This is something that can be bought in Albania, but I hear it is hard to find good deals (but I haven\'t researched it myself). Hope this answers the question.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-09-06 16:53:25', 1101, 0),
(1104, 20, 'sabina', 'subeans@hotmail.com', 'vegetarian', 'Hi\r\n\r\nI was just wondering if you could tell me what the Albanian diet consists of.  The reason why I am asking is that I have been a strict vegetarian for over ten years, and I am a little worried that this might be a problem for my host family, if the Albanian diet is very meat based.\r\n\r\nAny input you could give me, which would help me to prepare myself would be great.\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\nSabina', '2003-09-06 23:55:13', 0, 1),
(1105, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: vegetarian', 'Sabina,\r\nthere\'s good news and there\'s bad news. As a pretty strict vegetarian myself, I\'ve travelled the world and the only compromise I make is the occasional soup based on meat broth. I find that each national cuisine has a vegetarian substrate to which the meat is added. Albania is no different. There are lots of salads in most restaurants and I usually have fried cheese with it. There are also some traditional dishes that are usually meat based (one I like is Fërgesë) that can be prepared without meat. So in short, I\'ve been here for two months and I haven\'t suffered. The other good news is that we are asking host families if they would be willing to accommodate vegetarians (and some said yes) and the first few days you\'ll be spending at the hotel there will be vegetarian versions of traditional Albanian dishes on the menu.\r\n\r\nNow, the bad news is that Albanian\'s (like pretty much any other culture I\'ve visited) consider themselves to be great meat eaters. So to treat a guest, meat will be served. Even if a family knows you are a vegetarian, it may take them a while to understand what that really means. I\'ve asked once for a dish without meat and it came with ham. The waiter simply didn\'t understand why I didn\'t want it. So there will be challenges. Variety and price of vegetables in the winter will be very high up on the list of these. \r\n\r\nPrepare yourself to be flexible and cut some corners (like eating just a side dish) but if you don\'t want to, nobody can force you to eat a slab of beef. Plus most people will think you are a very odd specimen indeed so prepare yourself for lots of whys and hows.\r\n\r\nGood luck and see you soon.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-09-07 16:08:32', 1104, 0),
(1106, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: verbs', 'Brett, \r\ncan\'t really recommend a book but actually the formal form is just the same as the plural form - e.g. (vy) ctete is used for both, formal addressing 1 person and a group. \r\nThis applies to the present/future tense but the past tense combines an auxiliary in plural and a verb in singular (for formal) - e.g. (vy) jste cetl/cetla (BUT: vy jste cetli for a group). \r\nHope this helps a bit,\r\nJana', '2003-09-08 16:50:59', 1099, 0),
(1107, 20, 'Steve Tanghe', 'stanghe@hotmail.com', 'Re: Dress Jackets', 'Dominik:\r\n\r\nHere is a follow-up question on the subject of dress. Can you suggest appropriate attire to wear on a daily basis for 1-training in the Lezhe area & 2-a typical site posting? \r\n\r\nThanks,\r\n\r\nST', '2003-09-08 21:42:33', 1103, 0),
(1108, 14, 'Darren Napier', 'd.a.napier@talk21.com', 'Holesovice & Bubenec History', 'When visiting Prague I have always stayed in the Holesovice and Bubenec area. After spending alot of time in the area I now have the thirst for knowledge and would like to learn more about the areas history. \r\n \r\nThe best way I have concluded is to write a book on the history of Holesovice and Bubenec or at least attempt to. I am asking for the help of your readers to compile a book of memories, photos (old and new) stories and general historical facts that would build up a picture of the area of Holesovice and Bubenec. I hope to be in Prague November or December to carry out some research.\r\n \r\nI pick up books about Prague hoping to see something about the area I stay in but they all seem to cover the Prague 1 and 2 areas.\r\nAs a regular tourist to Prague I look at the city as a whole and not just the centre which most tourists do. I have had some great times in Holesovice and met some great people and would like to give something back to them as well as spreading the word to other tourists.\r\n \r\nI hope your readers can help me. If anyone can help me and be part of this book then either e-mail myself on d.a.napier@talk21.com or write to me at Darren Napier, 34 Ellicks Close, Bradley Stoke, Bristol, BS32 0ES, England.\r\n \r\n \r\nDarren Napier\r\nBristol, England       ', '2003-09-09 05:18:04', 0, 1),
(1109, 20, 'Nathan Lew', 'natelew@hotmail.com', 'boots again', 'Sorry I had another question.  In the recommended dress section it says \"warm, insulated, winter boots\".  I just bought a pair of winter boots online and they\'re nice, but also large, cumbersome, and somewhat unwieldy to walk in.  They would be great to walk down the streets of Aspen or some other snowy place, but unless we\'re going to be posted in places where it snows regularly, I\'m thinking that regular hiking boots which are smaller and more comfortable would be better.  I guess what I\'m asking is, are \"winter boots\" going to be necessary or useful, or would hiking boots be just fine?  Like, does it snow there?  Thanks a lot.', '2003-09-09 08:41:39', 0, 0),
(1110, 20, 'Chris Wrona Giallongo', 'cwronagiallongo@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: boots again', 'Nathan,\r\n\r\nI wish I could tell you that you will definitely be posted in a place where you will be dealing with a snowy winter.  There are sites that some of you will be in where there will be significant snowfall in the winter, and real winter (snow) boots will be welcome.  There are other sites where there will be little if any snow in winter.  The challenge is that we are not able to tell you right now exactly where you will be posted.  So you have a few choices:\r\n\r\n1.  Take the chance that you\'ll be posted in one of those sites and bring them.\r\n2.  Leave them home and have someone send them once you know that you\'ll need them.  (Keeping in mind the uncertainties of the mail system here)\r\n3.  Leave them home and buy something here when you know that you\'ll need them. (Then you\'re dealing with issues of quality, size availability, etc.)\r\n\r\nAnother alternative is to get some of the smaller scale, insulated boots that are out there, rather than the cumbersome, Sorel-type boots.  I lived in a snowy place in the States and had some boots that were not much more bulky than some good hiking boots, and they were great for winters there.  I expect that they\'ll be good for here too.  A good website for these items, in case you don\'t already know about it is: www.sierratradingpost.com.  Good outdoor stuff, good prices, good service.\r\n\r\nGood luck and we\'ll see you soon!\r\n\r\nChris', '2003-09-09 16:10:23', 1109, 0),
(1111, 20, 'Chris Wrona Giallongo', 'cwronagiallongo@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Dress Jackets', 'Steve and All,\r\n\r\nI\'d like to answer your question based on my own observation, having traveled throughout much of Albania and meeting with many of the people that you will all be working with; as well as based on conversations with Albanian staff members here.  The answer has not only to do with fitting in appropriately in your training and work setting, but also to do with representing yourself and Peace Corps.\r\n\r\nSo, without sounding like a lecturing grandmother, I\'d like to start by suggesting that you keep in mind that you will be under constant scrutiny -- a source of interest and even entertainment(when I was a volunteer in Nepal, we called ourselves \"Nepali TV\").  So the representation part is about the fact that what you do, what you look like, how you appear, no matter what the setting, will have an effect on how you are viewed as an individual, as well as how your colleague-trainees are viewed, and how Peace Corps is viewed.  I don\'t mean to make this sound like a burden, but it is something to always keep in mind in terms of dress, activities and behavior -- even in your off time.\r\n\r\nThat being said, I also don\'t want you to come here loaded down with suits and find that there are only a few occasions to where them; or feel that you can never break down and put on a pair of shorts and have fun.\r\n\r\nSo, professonal dress for both training and your work site will likely be in the category of \"business casual\".  There will be a few occasions when you will need a suit or a nice sport coat, tie and pants and you should be prepared for those.  You will probably find yourself also wearing a tie fairly regularly -- at least, that has been my obervation -- I frequently see mayors and male staff members wearing them in their work places, and it didn\'t seem that it was just because the Peace Corps staff was there visiting.  In addition, they were usually wearing collared, buttoned shirts (not polo-type or T-shirts)and twill or docker-type pants, and sometimes suit pants (wool, gabardine, etc.).  Jeans, shorts, t-shirts, tennis shoes, sandals (like TEVAs or flip-flops), or tank tops would not be appropriate in a professional setting at any time.\r\n\r\nFor women, the situation is the same, again for both training and your two-year placement:  you will see a more formal suit or dress worn on occasion and should bring something for those situations or at least have a nice jacket to put over a nice dress.  Everyday wear otherwise will be a skirt (knee-length or longer) and blouse or sweater; or some nicer pants (no jeans or 5-pocket styles) and a blouse or nice top.  Although I have infrequently seen women in shorts in Tirana, I haven\'t seen them anywhere else and wouldn\'t suggest that you expect to be able to wear shorts in your site (but you may want to bring some for vacations to the sea or other places you might choose to go).  More common and appropriate for more casual settings are capri-type pants.  Again, in the professional setting, no t-shirts, but nicer pullover tops are ok.  Sleeveless tops seem to be ok in most places around the country in the summer (and it does get very hot), and though you may see them in Tirana, I wouldn\'t recommend spaghetti straps  or halter tops for the professional setting.\r\n\r\nShoes are also an important consideration, especially for women.  Roads and sidewalks are in bad shape.  You will likely be walking through mud after a rain storm.  Summers are dry and dusty.  Unless you are really adept and totally comfortable in higher heels, I would recommend flats or low, solid heels.  Nice slides seem to be ok in most settings during the summer, but not thong-type sandals.  I myself am a sworn flats wearer and I\'ve still fallen at least twice!  My husband, a medical practitioner, figures that opening a podiatry practice here would be profitable.\r\n\r\nAll kidding aside -- you should think about how you can fit in -- your colleagues will be dressed neatly and professionally, but not be outfitted in very expensive clothing.  You will want to do the same, without standing out.\r\n\r\nOK - hope that helps you all.  We\'ll see you here soon!\r\n\r\nChris', '2003-09-09 16:45:36', 1107, 0),
(1112, 15, 'Vlad', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'ROBOT', '2003-09-10 03:45:34', 1085, 0),
(1113, 15, 'vlad', 'vladcerny2002@yahoo.com', 'Re: Internet Provider', 'volny.cz   seznam.cz   \r\nit is free but you have to pey the phone by minute even local call so it\'s not free anyway sorry about my english i will look some more and i\'ll let you know vlad', '2003-09-10 03:55:13', 1039, 0),
(1114, 20, 'Eva Kovacs', 'email4eva@yahoo.com', 'No CD, Water', 'Hello!\r\n\r\nJust for reference, I have not yet received the CD. Since I moved out of my place at the end of August, my mail is being forwarded... thus perhaps the delay? It might have been nice to receive the packing guidelines via email, as time is passing pretty quickly now.\r\n\r\nWater: You wrote in an earlier post that bottled water is readily available... so for traveling, can we anticipate being able to buy water along the way? Or will there be a need to treat water with iodine while traveling?\r\n\r\nThank you!\r\nEva', '2003-09-10 04:01:32', 0, 0),
(1115, 20, 'Nadia', 'nziyadeh@yahoo.com', 'Insurance?', 'Hi all -\r\nI know Nate posted this question on the Yahoo group, but I\'d like to get Chris\'s input on this:\r\n\r\nShould we get insurance? If so, which one? Clements? Or the pink Peace Corps Personal Articles Insurance?\r\n\r\nThanks!!\r\n-Nadia', '2003-09-10 04:23:33', 0, 0),
(1116, 15, 'vlad', 'vladcerny2002@yahoo.com', 'Re: Internet Provider', 'So the net conection 971 101 202, name - atlas, password - atlas', '2003-09-10 14:55:45', 1039, 0),
(1117, 20, 'JP Piorkowski', 'jpiorko1@excite.com', 'Tennis anyone?', 'I am a little embarrassed to broadcast this question to the whole group, but have you seen any tennis courts since you have been in Albania? I ask because 1) I am an avid tennis player/teacher and 2) would be interested in starting a youth development program through tennis in my spare time.  A humanitarian/development org that Ive worked for pledged to send resources if I can get something off the ground.  I just wanted to get your feedback before going ahead and packing my rackets.\r\nCheers,\r\nJP', '2003-09-10 20:21:18', 0, 1),
(1118, 20, 'JP', 'cwronagiallongo@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Tennis anyone?', 'JP,\r\n\r\nI haven\'t seen any tennis courts myself, although I\'m guessing there might be some on the US Embassy residential compound.  You won\'t have open access to that generally, and it\'s in Tirana, so you\'re not likely to be here that often to use it.  But, if you want to do more research, I\'d recommend doing a google search on tennis and Albania -- I wanted to see if there would be opportunities for my daughter to continue with the gymnastics training she was doing in the States and found an Albanian Gymnastics Federation with some contact info.  Perhaps there\'s something similar for tennis.\r\n\r\nChris Wrona Giallongo\r\nProgramming & Training Officer\r\nPeace Corps Albania', '2003-09-11 12:16:22', 1117, 0),
(1119, 20, 'Chris Wrona Giallongo', 'cwronagiallongo@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Insurance?', 'Nadia\r\n\r\nI have to admit, I\'m not a good person to ask on this one.  I\'d say that if you are bringing things of significant value -- a laptop, a digital camera, other electronics -- then you might want to get insurance coverage on them.  I didn\'t have any of those items when I was a volunteer (it wasn\'t THAT long ago, but definitely pre-laptops, digital cameras and PDAs) and so didn\'t have to make that decision.  I\'m sorry to say that I can\'t do a good analysis on whether Clements or the Peace Corps policy would be best.  I\'d go with the most cost-effective deal.\r\n\r\nChris Wrona Giallongo\r\nProgramming & Training Officer\r\nPeace Corps Albania', '2003-09-11 12:22:29', 1115, 0),
(1120, 17, 'Iuli', 'iuli111@yahoo.com', 'Re: :o)', 'Viděla jsem mnoho lidí, kteří se učí četinu. Jsem z Rumunska a tento jazyk libí se mi moc. Ráda bych mluvila česky někým, kdo je rodilý mluvčí. Umím anglicky a francouzsky velmi dobře, ale česky jen málo. ', '2003-09-12 14:28:20', 1047, 0),
(1121, 20, 'Enejda', 'egurja@al.peacecorp.gov', 'Re: Tennis anyone?', 'Hello JP,\r\nWell tennis is not a very widely practiced sport in Albania. As Chris mentioned there are a few places but mainly in Tirana or beach resorts in Durres. I know there are two courts in Tirana that are open to the public, which one  can book or schedule to play tennis with an instructor. One is on the grounds of the Rogner Europapark Hotel(and it costs $10 an hour to play). So, chances are there will not be courts in any other smaller towns. As for starting up something depending on resources and interest it might be a good idea. \r\nSee you soon\r\n\r\nEnejda Gurja\r\nTraining Manager\r\n\r\n\r\n', '2003-09-13 02:12:41', 1117, 0),
(1122, 20, 'Enejda', 'egurja@al.peacecorp.gov', 'Re: Tennis anyone?', 'Hello JP,\r\nWell tennis is not a very widely practiced sport in Albania. As Chris mentioned there are a few places but mainly in Tirana or beach resorts in Durres. I know there are two courts in Tirana that are open to the public, which one  can book or schedule to play tennis with an instructor. One is on the grounds of the Rogner Europapark Hotel(and it costs $10 an hour to play). So, chances are there will not be courts in any other smaller towns. As for starting up something depending on resources and interest it might be a good idea. \r\nSee you soon\r\n\r\nEnejda Gurja\r\nTraining Manager\r\n\r\n\r\n', '2003-09-13 02:19:20', 1117, 0),
(1123, 20, 'Enejda', 'egurja@al.peacecorp.gov', 'Re: What\'s available in Lezhe?', 'Hello Teri and sorry for not replyig earlier. Well there are hair salons in every town in Albania that do cuts, colors, etc. As for hair color you buy and do yourself, you can find a variety of product (perhaps not the same brands  as in the US) although for hair coloring it is better to go to a salon. I am sure it won\'t be a problem finding a good hairdresser here.\r\nSee you soon\r\n\r\nEnejda Gurja\r\nTraining Manager', '2003-09-13 02:43:28', 1076, 0),
(1124, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: No CD, Water', 'Oops. Seems this one sort of feel through the cracks. Here\'s a quick response.\r\n\r\nSorry, about the CD Eva. I don\'t think it contains any vital packing advice that wasn\'t already contained in the Welcome Book, though. <b>Just to summarize:</b> pack a change of clothing into your carry on. Delayed luggage is not uncommon.\r\n\r\nAs for water, yes, bottled water (both fizzy [me gaz] and flat [pa gaz]) is readily available. Plus you will eventually receive a water filter. In other words, you should be able to keep hydrated without too much trouble at all (although, occasionally, a bit of planning might be required).\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-09-13 20:29:14', 1114, 0),
(1125, 13, 'Veronica', 'stormborne@hotmail.com', 'Re: Useful Czech Phrases Translation?', 'Nevermind. :)', '2003-09-14 12:17:20', 1098, 0),
(1126, 15, 'Mira', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'Maybe \"kolac\" and \"kolacky\" don\'t count, but they should. Jewel sells kolacky, so I\'d say it\'s assimilated enough to count as a word used in \"everyday American life\". \r\n\r\nIt\'s even at dictionary.com:\r\n\r\nkoˇlacˇky    ( P )  Pronunciation Key  (k-lächk, kläch-) or koˇlaˇche (k-läch, klä-)\r\nn. pl. kolacky, or kolache also koˇlaˇches \r\nA sweet bun made from yeasted dough and usually having a fruit or poppy seed filling. \r\n[Czech kolac, wheel-shaped cake, pl. of kolac, from kolo, wheel. See calash.]', '2003-09-15 11:06:40', 1085, 0),
(1127, 13, 'Leah', 'lialiasrs04@aol.com', 'Re: Meeting Czech People', 'hey,\r\n  maybe my sister can help you. she is from texas but now lives in cz. she teaches english there and speaks czech very well. her e-mail is: sherristep@volny.cz.\r\n-Leah', '2003-09-16 02:15:06', 1082, 0),
(1128, 20, 'Anna Gutierrez', 'annalissa76@yahoo.com', 'Re: Dress Jackets', 'Hi Chris - thank you so much for your detailed response. One more question - are we expected to dress up (i.e. in business casual) when we arrive in Tirana or can we wear casual clothes? Just trying to figure out clothes to put in my carry-on luggage.\r\n\r\nthanks!\r\nanna', '2003-09-16 04:05:08', 1111, 0),
(1129, 20, 'Anna Gutierrez', 'annalissa76@yahoo.com', 'Re: Dress Jackets', 'Hi Chris - thank you so much for your detailed response. One more question - are we expected to dress up (i.e. in business casual) when we arrive in Tirana or can we wear casual clothes? Just trying to figure out clothes to put in my carry-on luggage.\r\n\r\nthanks!\r\nanna', '2003-09-16 04:05:48', 1111, 0),
(1130, 13, 'Veronika', 'stormborne@hotmail.com', 'Re: Diky Michal :)', 'Michal, thanks for the e-mail. I just can\'t express my gratitude--but I think I\'ll be able to travel to Ostrava with more confidence. And I know my Czech friends will be so happy. :)\r\n\r\nI definitely owe you one. If you ever need anything translated into Japanese, just let me know...;)\r\n\r\nVeronika', '2003-09-16 11:13:33', 1125, 0),
(1131, 20, 'Chris Wrona Giallongo', 'cwronagiallongo@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: Dress Jackets', 'Anna,\r\n\r\nSince you and your colleagues are the first Peace Corps Volunteers to arrive in Albania after a 6-year absence, your arrival is actually being treated more significantly than a Peace Corps group arrival would ordinarily be.  We recently learned that the US Ambassador to Albania, Mr. James Jeffrey, and Albanian government officals (and therefore the press) are planning to be at the airport to greet you, so we are asking you to be dressed in business casual.  If I\'m right on your flight intinerary, you\'ll have about 3 or 4 hours in Vienna and you could do the overnight flight in more casual clothing and then change into something nicer for the flight to Tirana when you are in Vienna.\r\n\r\nSee you soon.\r\n\r\nChris', '2003-09-16 12:10:44', 1129, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1132, 13, 'marc Hermans', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'the word sourozenec', 'Sourozenec means \'brother or sister\', but I can\'t find a synonym in Dutch or English. Is there an exact translation in English, and is it used in colloquial Czech or is it a word that\'s only used in a litteral contence', '2003-09-23 20:28:57', 0, 0),
(1133, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: the word sourozenec', 'As I know, there is no exact one-word translation in English and probably also in Dutch.\r\n\r\nThis word is used daily as often as the words \"bratr\" (brother) or \"sestra\" (sister) are. I would rather ask someone \"Má sourozence?\" than \"Má bratra nebo sestru?\" (Do you have brother or sister?)\r\n\r\nYes, someone but not everyone may intuitively feel \"sourozenec\" more formal (not completely litteral) but it is not bookish using this word in everday speech.', '2003-09-24 15:54:08', 1132, 0),
(1134, 13, 'mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'ano - jo', 'Thanks a lot for the quick reply on my last question.\r\nHere another one. I read in a studybook, that the word for yes is \'ano\',and that in the Prague-region one uses \'Jo\'. But during my visits in Cheb and Cesky Krumlov, I noticed that many people use \'Jo\' instead of \'Ano\'. \r\nWhat is most common used? Is it all right to use \'Jo\', or is it a word that is used only by youngsters or in certain parts of the country??\r\nBye ', '2003-09-24 17:12:48', 0, 0),
(1135, 13, 'nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: the word sourozenec', '\r\nsourozenec = sibling', '2003-09-25 12:24:01', 1132, 0),
(1136, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: ano - jo', 'I think the main difference here is \'ano\' is for writing and \'jo\' for verbal uses (the only ocassion for spoken \'ano\' I can think of is for getting married). \r\n\r\nI would not expect anyone to get offended if you say \'jo\' but in a very formal situation I would probably avoid it as monosyllabic answers don\'t feel appropriate (but I do the same in English, so maybe that\'s no different).\r\n\r\nIn this case it\'s probably better to say \'taky si myslim\' (=that\'s what I think, too), \'souhlasim\' (=I agree) or something similar depending on the context.\r\n\r\nOn second thoughts, though, some of my moravian friends use \'ano\' in speach quite happily, so there might be some kind of regional variation. Nevertheless, you\'ll be understood which ever you use.', '2003-09-26 16:52:11', 1134, 0),
(1137, 15, 'Kev', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'ahoj. Depending on the dialect some will pronounce ah-hoe\r\nbut some will pronounce  Ah hoy. like a hello in nautical terms...ahoy!!', '2003-09-29 09:04:21', 1085, 0),
(1138, 15, 'Kevin Martin', 'kmart0087@yahoo.com', 'Re: Czech teacher needed in Atlanta, GA', 'Hey Georgia!!  I am from Savannah. I dated a girl and worked with students from prague this summer. Also, a guy who frequented my bar a lot named George Frank is from Czech Rep. His son attends Georgia Tech. I\'m not sure how much czech his son knows since his son is American, but I\'ll ask.', '2003-09-29 09:07:35', 1004, 0),
(1139, 15, 'Kevin Martin', 'kmart0087@yahoo.com', 'czech hard/easy?', 'czech words are easy to learn and remember. conversational czech is hard to pick up. but english is the hardest language to learn as a second language strictly because we have so many meanings for one word and we pronounce things in difficult ways. for example how would you pronounce  oug.   \"oog\"? \"owwwg\"?\r\nnow add a T and an H   Tough.  Uf. or....the word..better\r\n\r\nOur food is \"better\" than yours...or...You \"better\" get out of here.\\\r\nget it?', '2003-09-29 09:15:04', 0, 0),
(1140, 14, 'Jan Vanek jr.', '', 'Re: Holesovice & Bubenec History', 'Czechs been there done that - look for \"Kniha o Praze 6\", \"Kniha o Praze 7\" and there\'s to be one specifically about Bubenec next year: http://www.praha6.cz/aktuality/aktualita.php?IDakt=342', '2003-10-01 17:37:43', 1108, 0),
(1141, 16, 'Ray Garvey', 'ray.garvey@caramail.com', 'Great website', 'Someone deserves a big congratulations, this website is the best I\'ve found pertaining to the language and culture of The Czech Republic.  I hope to go through the language course and try to learn at least the basics.  My last business trip to the Czech Republic in July, 2003, I was able to meet a lot of wonderful people and I see a lot happening there.  If anyone wants to trade English lessons with Czech lessons, I am open to making some kind of deal.\r\n     I hope this site gets many visitors and I hope it\'s content remains free because it provides a great opportunity for people to learn the language.\r\n    Best Regards,\r\nRay Garvey\r\nUSA', '2003-10-04 04:52:36', 0, 1),
(1142, 13, 'Michael Cejka', 'Mcejka@yahoo.com', 'Want translation of last name', 'My last name is cejka.  would like translation of that to english if possible , or a web site where I can translate Czech to English.  thanks\r\n', '2003-10-07 06:43:27', 0, 0),
(1143, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Want translation of last name', 'Čejka (C with hacek) means lapwing (or perhaps plover) - a bird of genus Vanellus.', '2003-10-07 13:27:15', 1142, 0),
(1144, 13, 'Nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.cz', 'Re: Want translation of last name', 'For translations try Czech-English & English-Czech on-line dictionary at\r\nhttp://web.volny.cz/najdito/slovnik.php - choose \"z angličtiny do četiny\" (from Eng to Cz).', '2003-10-08 17:30:13', 1142, 0),
(1145, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: ano - jo', 'I feel a little silly stating an opinion on such a \"touchy feely\" thing as the ano/no/jo choice when I\'m not a Czech, but here\'s my 0.67 CZK, er, two cents, as someone whose adopted hometown is Brno...\r\n\r\nano - highly formal speech, or less formal speech when what you\'re really saying is \"I\'m still listening,\" or when you want to really emphasize your agreement, or when you\'re pissed off.\r\n\r\nno - quite informal agreement with the main purpose of moving the conversation along. Of course \"no\" is a very tricky word for foreigners at first, because it can mean everything from \"sure, I agree with you\" to \"weeeeell\" to \"yeah right,\" depending on the context, the tone of voice, the words around it, etc.\r\n\r\njo - really informal agreement, really enthusiastic agreement, really unenthusiastic agreement, really rushed agreement.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-10-10 17:01:00', 1136, 0),
(1146, 13, 'mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'S tebou me bavi svet', 'Who can give me a translation of the titel of this movie. Thanks', '2003-10-16 19:34:19', 0, 0),
(1147, 14, 'mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'trip to czech republic', 'Hi, I\'m Mark from Belgium. Next year we\'ll spend our holidays in  the Czech Republic for the 4th time. In the last years, we\'ve been in Cesky Raj (near Turnov), Cesky Krumlov, Marianske Lazne and Cheb. Has anybody some interesting tips for a next trip? We travel by car and always try to find a little apartment or house with a kitchen (we have a little child that HATES restaurantvisits - very anoying). Normally we don\'t book our stayings in Belgium but try to find something on the spot. This year we wanted to stay in the Beroun-area but couldn\'t find a place to stay - very anoying with a tired young child at the back). So next year we want to be a bit more prepared and look for areas where accomodation WITH KITCHEN is more common. TIPS are welcome\r\n', '2003-10-16 22:14:47', 0, 0),
(1148, 13, 'Dmitri Lovermann', '', 'Re: S tebou me bavi svet', 'I`ll try:\r\n\"The world seems to be better with you\".\r\nor\r\n\"The world is better with you\".', '2003-10-17 17:32:08', 1146, 0),
(1149, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: S tebou me bavi svet', 'the verb \'bavit\' is a bit tricky to translate. it\'s got this enjoying and entertaining and having a good time feel to it that \'to like\' doesn\'t. the world-for-world translation would be something like \'With you, I like the world better\' but the message is more \'You make me to enjoy life\' (possibly \'the life\' - i\'m still hopeless with articles).\r\n\r\nold-ish but nice movie, enjoy (i think it it\'s been subtitled)\r\n', '2003-10-17 21:03:56', 1146, 0),
(1150, 14, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: trip to czech republic', 'that must have been quite difficult. unfortunatelly, i don\'t any contacts in that area but have you tried the expat.cz page? there might be sb with the right tel no.\r\ngood luck,\r\njana', '2003-10-17 21:14:19', 1147, 0),
(1151, 20, 'Charmin Lindholm', 'sscharmie@hotmail.com', 'Re: March group of PCV', 'Hi! \r\nI\'ll be in Albania teaching english as well. What to pack? How to prepare? Oye! So much to think about and organize.\r\n\r\nYou can contact me at sscharmie@hotmail.com\r\nCharmin Lindholm\r\n', '2003-10-18 08:36:44', 1093, 1),
(1152, 13, 'steve', '', 'Re: the word sourozenec', 'I think it\'s comparable to the German word Geschwister', '2003-10-20 01:31:10', 1132, 0),
(1153, 17, 'nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: :o)', 'Ahoj Luli,\r\ntvoje četina je moc dobrá - drím palce, aby ti nadení pro studium vydrelo a brzy jsi mluvila jako rodilý mluvčí. :)\r\nKrásný den!\r\n', '2003-10-21 20:13:24', 1120, 0),
(1154, 13, 'Laura', 'Arual_@luukku.com', 'Business in Czech', 'Hi!\r\n\r\nCould you tell me how to do business or work with people in Czech?:)\r\nIs there some kind of rules or values saying what kind of person is good or bad worker, or what kind of values are important in Czech business environment?\r\n\r\nThank you!!', '2003-10-21 21:18:30', 0, 1),
(1155, 13, 'Shaul Sharoni', 'pollinia@012.net.il', 'Re: the word sourozenec', 'Regarding your enquiry, the English term that incorporates both sister & brother is \"sibling(s)\", used collectively to refer to an array of sisters and brothers in a single family/in general', '2003-10-23 02:31:46', 1132, 1),
(1156, 15, 'Milan', 'mynamemilan@seznam.cz', 'Re: czech hard/easy?', 'Dear Kevin\r\nin case Czech is pretty easy for you I don\'t understand why you didn\'t write your email in Czech:-)You seem to be pretty good at Czech what about Czech writing next time:-)\r\nBye milan', '2003-10-25 21:58:13', 1139, 0),
(1157, 15, 'milan', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Konečně nemusím na této straně psát anglicky,i kdy ji mám velice rád.Myslím si,e otázka o snadnosti či nesnadnosti je tak \"trochu mimo mísu\".Znám struktury pouze dvou jazyků,tedy českého a anglického.Jak tedy porovnávat zbytek,nehledě na to,e nikdo nikdy jetě nesestavil kritérium,co je či není těké.Já velice obdivuji cizince mluvící česky,protoe u jsem si také přečetl,e četina je podle vědců PRÝ jeden z nejtěích jazyků,ale pro mne je to pouhé PRÝ......\r\nS pozdravem Milan', '2003-10-25 22:29:27', 343, 0),
(1158, 15, 'Milan', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'I don\'t want to discuss whether |Czech is hard or easy but I am interested in what people regard as the most interesting idiom,for example funny or strange or ridiculous.Thank you \r\nMilan', '2003-10-25 22:34:33', 0, 0),
(1159, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Re: ano - jo', 'Would it be at least somewhat equivalent to \"Yeah\" vs. \"Yes\"?', '2003-10-26 11:05:59', 1145, 0),
(1160, 15, 'Mira', '', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'My favorite is \"mít hlavu jako koleno\", just for its descriptiveness.', '2003-10-26 11:14:15', 1158, 0),
(1161, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Business in Czech', 'Laura,\r\nThere\'s no quick or simple answer to that. Generally speaking, values tend to be very culture specific and since you appear to be considering doinig business/working in an environment you\'re not very familiar with, the best recommendation I can give you is to either contact cross-cultural communication consultants or, if your budget would not accommodate that, spend some time on research (I think a good starting point might be Hofstede\'s four dimensional model of work-related cultural values, I\'m sure google will offer you a good selection of pages related to this topic).\r\nGood luck,\r\nJana', '2003-10-27 16:16:13', 1154, 0),
(1162, 17, 'klara', '', 'Re: :o)', 'jak se mate, kde jste?', '2003-10-28 03:40:07', 1153, 0),
(1163, 17, 'Petra', '', 'vizum do ameriky', 'Mam moznost zaridit Cechum studijni pobyt v Kalifornii, s pracovnim povolenim na 2 roky,kazdy to dostane!!!!!! Mate zajem ,poslu podrobnejsi informace!!', '2003-10-28 03:42:48', 0, 1),
(1164, 17, 'petra', '', 'Re: :o)', 'Ahoj LUli , \r\nmluvis bajecne a bez chyb. Mluvis take jinymi jazyky?Petra', '2003-10-28 03:46:13', 1120, 0),
(1165, 16, 'Philip', 'kielhurn@mbox.dkm.cz', 'čestiná', 'wish to find someone (Czech) that wants to improve on their English in exchange for Czech lessons. Prefer someone young that still has the cases in their head fresh! Older Czechs Ive met tend to have forgotten about them:{ \r\n(its normal) Write if in Prague', '2003-10-29 00:43:27', 0, 1),
(1166, 16, 'Milan', 'brozmilan@seznam.cz', 'Re: čestiná', 'Dear Philip\r\non provided you send me an e-mail to my address brozmilan@quick.cz,I may solve your problem.Or you can call me 732 717 299,I\'ll tell you more.Bye\r\nMilan', '2003-10-29 03:46:33', 1165, 0),
(1167, 16, 'Milan', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: čestiná', 'I\'m very sorry about that.My e-mail is like this:brozmilan@quick.cz ,not the last one.', '2003-10-29 03:48:00', 1165, 0),
(1168, 13, 'Jakube', 'cotterj1@yahoo.com', 'Czech Women \"Blazen\"', 'Is it just me ir are all Czech Women MAD !!!!!!!!!!!!', '2003-10-29 09:13:39', 0, 1),
(1169, 13, 'stu', 'stuart.mackinnon@hefcw.ac.uk', 'Czech Translation urgently needed !', 'Hi there,\r\n\r\nIm off to prague next week and have a nut allergy - i would be extremely grateful if somebody could provide the czech translation at the email address given.\r\n\r\nThanks in advance, Stuart', '2003-10-30 19:42:47', 0, 1),
(1170, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Translation urgently needed !', 'allergy = alergie\r\nnuts = orechy\r\n\r\ni\'m not sure if you get the warnings on all products, if you\'re really sensitive to nuts i recommend you to check with locals.', '2003-10-31 18:10:36', 1169, 0),
(1171, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Women', 'nop, j. it\'s you :)))', '2003-10-31 18:11:48', 1168, 0),
(1172, 13, 'Stuart', 'stuartm@mailasia.com', 'Re: Czech Translation urgently needed !', 'Thanks Jana - so how would i say \'Does this meal / food contain any nuts??\'\r\n\r\nThanks in adavance, Stuart', '2003-11-03 19:02:03', 1170, 0),
(1173, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Czech Translation urgently needed !', 'Stuart, you need to say Nejsou v tomto jidle orechy (sorry I\'ve lost my czech keyboard) rough pronunciation is Ney-so f-tom-to jeed-le or-shek-ie You could also say Mam alergii na orechy (maam a-ler-gi-e na or-shek-ie). Avoidance of nuts in Prague is imperative ref Jakub\'s posting', '2003-11-04 02:10:08', 1172, 0),
(1174, 15, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'Milan, I think this subject should have generated loads of replies, as a quickie I\'ll go for \"mit opici\" (hangover) though my more expert wife\'s favourite is \"liny jako prase\" aimed in my direction', '2003-11-04 02:19:03', 1158, 0),
(1175, 15, 'kevintoo', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: czech hard/easy?', 'Milan, I think Kevins problem is he is still struggling with english', '2003-11-04 02:23:54', 1156, 0),
(1176, 20, 'Todd Kaercher', 'titania@minn.net', 'families', 'I am the mother of one of the trainees and was wondering if any other families and friends look here to see some news of the Albanian delegation.  It might be fun to get a dialogue going between us.', '2003-11-05 22:34:35', 0, 0),
(1177, 20, 'Dominik Luke', 'dlukes@al.peacecorps.gov', 'Re: families', 'Hi, feel free to start a discussion but since the site was only intended for those coming to Albania, no new information will be posted until early next year which is when the next group will come.\r\n\r\nYou can find some more pictures of this group at <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/traininginalbania03>http://www.bohemica.com/traininginalbania03</a>.\r\n\r\nDominik\r\n____________________________\r\nDominik Luke\r\nPre-Service Training Director', '2003-11-05 22:40:46', 1176, 0),
(1178, 15, 'lenka', 'lenkazontkova@seznam.cz', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'Mrzne az prasti\r\nZpity pod obraz\r\nBlby jak tago\r\nVstat hrobnikovi z lopaty\r\nSkareda jak ropucha\r\nHladky jak detska prdelka\r\nI have more of them but I would be here all day......', '2003-11-06 11:23:38', 1174, 0),
(1179, 17, 'Dima', '', 'Re: :o)', 'Jen do toho! Pokud má dotazy anebo nebude ti jasné, co znamená ku příkladu \"Proti gustu ádný diputát\", tak nevahej a ptej se :) Ona ta četina je ve skutečnosti těká, ale ty jsi dobrá a jak se koukám zvladá to bravůrně :) Já jsem potřeboval hodně času na to, abych se pořádně naučil česky, i kdy stále dělám chyby. No a kdy u jsme u jazyků, co kdybys ty nám předvedla něco z rumuntiny? :)', '2003-11-07 20:03:12', 1120, 0),
(1180, 20, 'Todd Kaercher', 'titania@minn.net', 'Re: families', 'Thanks so much.  My son seemed to think there might be Halloween party pix posted.  Do you know where they might be?', '2003-11-09 22:09:41', 1177, 1),
(1181, 20, 'Donna Borgford-Parnell', 'borgford@u.washington.edu', 'Re: families', 'My son is also one of the trainees.  I look at this page every once in awhile.  I have passed it along to my co-worker that is going to be in the next group of trainees that is going to Albania in March (she just found that out last week).  Our son is having the time of his life--his host family is wonderful & the country & people have been amazing.  I know that he wishes that he had brought warmer clothes--I don\'t think that any of us could anticipate what it is like to not having central heating--no matter how many stories we read about other volunteers being cold.\r\n\r\nIt would be great to have a place to keep in contact with other families.  We are planning a trip to Albania in May 2004--so it would be great to hear if others are planning to go & how they are planning to get there.', '2003-11-11 21:31:52', 1176, 0),
(1182, 20, 'Donna Borgford-Parnell', 'borgford@u.washington.edu', 'Re: PST 7', 'Will this page/forum be used by the next group or will you be creating a new page for PST 7?  My co-worker will be coming in the next group & I passed along this page to her, but I know that she would like to be able to contact others from her group right away.', '2003-11-11 21:39:29', 1177, 0),
(1183, 20, 'Donna Borgford-Parnell', 'borgford@u.washington', 'Re: families', 'Another web page that I look at frequently is: http://peacecorpsalbania.blogspot.com/\r\n', '2003-11-11 21:41:49', 1176, 0),
(1184, 13, 'xaria', 'rcstokes@hotmail.com', 'New Love', 'I have recently met and begun to fall in love with a beautiful czech man. Could someone please assist me . . . I would like to tell him in a letter \"I miss you\" (in czech) and \"You inspire the beauty in my dreams\" (in czech)\r\n\r\nIf anyone can help me, I will be ever-so-grateful. Thank you! \r\n', '2003-11-12 06:10:07', 0, 1),
(1185, 20, 'Dominik Luke', '', 'Re: PST 7', 'Since I\'m coming back for the next PST, I will keep the same page open. I\'m not sure how much will be added but the discussion board will remain active and staff will occasionally review it and respond to questions.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-11-12 12:11:02', 1182, 0),
(1186, 16, 'Dmitri Lovermann', '', 'příprava ke stání zkouce z četiny', 'Můe mi někdo pomoci? Potřeboval bych se připravit ke státní zkouce z četiny. Do sjs.cz se mi nějak nechce. Nemám chu sedět ve velké skupině, kde jsou i takoví, kteří se ptají na různé věci, které jsem se naučil jetě v prvním ročníku jazykovky. Moná, e by se ke mě připojila má známá. Take byla by to výuka ve dvou. Jsme skoro na stejné úrovni a tak jsme se rozhodli, e individuální příprava bude mnohem efektivnějí, ne ve skupině.\r\n\r\nZajímá mě, jestli se dá vůbec sehnat vyučujicího, který u má zkuenosti v přípravě cizinců ke zkoukám z četiny?\r\n\r\nJo, a jetě jedná věc: za nějakou dobu (nevím ale kolik uplyne let..) budu chtít sloit i překladatelskou zkouku. Jak se k ní připravím? Jsou na to nějaké zvlátní kurzy?', '2003-11-12 13:10:40', 0, 0),
(1187, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: New Love', 'Bohemica\'s volunteer love-letter translation service to the rescue once again...\r\n\r\nI miss you ==> Chybí mi\r\nYou inspire the beauty in my dreams ==> Jsi inspirací krásy mých snů.\r\n\r\nOthers will probably offer better translations of the latter, this is just if you\'re in a hurry to get your letter out.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:26:38', 1184, 0),
(1188, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: make to enjoy (WAS: S tebou me bavi svet)', 'Ahoj Jano!\r\n\r\nAh, illogical English. Sometimes you use the infinitive after a helper verb (He caused me to stub my toe), sometimes you don\'t (He made me stub my toe). Looking at problems like this in English, Czech seems easy in comparison. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:32:15', 1149, 0),
(1189, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: ano - jo', 'That\'s a good starting point for understanding it, but more for Czechs than for English-speakers, since if you learn that you should \"ano\" for \"yes,\" you can end up, in my opinion, sounding too formal (or even mildly peeved) some of the time.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:35:43', 1159, 0),
(1190, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'I\'m a grammar freak, so the one gets me is the much dryer \"v tomto slova smyslu.\" It\'s one of the few phrases that still gets me mumbling in English (\"in this of-the-word sense, in this of-the-word sense.... AAAARGH!!!\").\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:56:49', 1178, 0),
(1191, 16, 'Erik', '', 'Re: příprava ke stání zkouce z četiny', 'O zkouskach vim jenom to, ze existuji, ale moje zkusenost je takova, ze se clovek muze zivit prekladanim uplne v pohode bez certifikatu. Doporucil bych se optat jeste v Yahoo group Czechlist, diskusni forum predevsim prekladatelu CZ<-->EN. Uvidis mnohem sirsi a asi i fundovanejsi skalu nazoru na otazku \"stoji zkouska za to\" a nejspis i na otazku \"jak se na ni nachystam\".\r\n\r\nOmlouvam se za tsestinu, su linej :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 19:06:10', 1186, 0),
(1192, 16, 'Dima', '', 'Re: příprava ke stání zkouce z četiny', 'No, je to pro mě jasná věc, e ivnostenské oprávnění podnikat jako překladatel dostanu i bez jakékoli zkouky, i kdy se mi to zdá blby (dy i cizinec, který ani nemluví česky můe poskytovat sluby v oboru překladání a tlumočnictví). Ale jde mi o to, e já bych chtěl být tím opravdovým překladatelem, ne jako jsem teď.. :) Ale hlavní asi bude to, e tím překládaním vydělávat na chleba nechci. Moná jen tak, jako student. A i to razítko překladatele můe hodit jako taková řekl bych záchrana  v budoucnosti. Nikdo neví, jak se změní jeho ivot a překladatelem bych si nějaký minimum zapespečil. Take teď bych se zkusil připravit a slouit ty zkouky jen pro to abych si dokázal, e jsem v tom dobrej. Co? Nejsem ', '2003-11-13 04:28:19', 1191, 0),
(1193, 16, 'Erik', '', 'Re: příprava ke stání zkouce z četiny', 'Ani mi nelo o oprávnění, i kdy můu tentokrát u 100% potvrdit, e to dostane v podstatě za to, e se na paní v . ú. bude hezky usmívat. No vlastně je potřeba diplom, ale ten můe být z čehokoli -- já ho třeba měl z francouztiny a public relations.\r\n\r\nlo mi o skutečné ivobytí -- a tady zase, podle mého, o nějaký nějakou skupinou vymýlený certifikát nejde, jsem si skoro jistý, e větina překladatelů co znám (soudní jsou samozřejmě vyjímkou) nic takového nemá -- má schopnost překládat, dobrou obchodní pověst, vypěstovanou klientelu, atd. -- tyto zajistí ivobytí, ne certifikát. Chtěj ho třeba jako trofej, ale nečekej, e bez něj to nepůjde nebo e s ním to půjde jako po másle.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-13 15:44:00', 1192, 0),
(1194, 16, 'Milan', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: příprava ke stání zkouce z četiny', 'ádný papír jetě nikdy nedokázal,e má někdo patent na rozum.Já vaí četině rozumím a je mi umafuk,e na to nemáte papír.Ono se to stejně vdy pozná,jestli tu řeč umí nebo ne.I bez papíru.A nemusí nikomu nic dokazovat.......................................', '2003-11-14 01:45:20', 1192, 0),
(1195, 17, 'Erik', '', 'Re: :o)', 'Těká? Moná. Je vak aspoň logická! No větinou. Co o angličtině nelze říci...\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-14 17:03:45', 1179, 0),
(1196, 16, 'Dima', '', 'Re: příprava ke stání zkouce z četiny', 'Tady bych nesypal do jedného pytle řeč a psání. Jsou to dvě četiny a kadá je jiná. Kadé se učíme. Hodně cizinců se domluví, ale je spousta takových, kteří pravopis nezvladají. Důvodem je nejčastěji to, e větinou mluví, a ne píou. I já dělám chyby přestoe píu dost... \r\n\r\nKdy se tak podívám na svůj předelý příspěvek, citím, e něco není v pořádku. A není to kvůli chybám. Něco se tam prostě nesedí.. Asi postřebuji více praxe.', '2003-11-14 18:16:10', 1194, 0),
(1197, 17, 'Dima', '', 'hovorové výrazy', 'Asi se budete smát, ale občas potkám nějaký výraz, kterému nerozumím. Nael jsem seznam takových obrátů:\r\n\r\n1. \"...nemá kredit k tomu aby kafral do...\" (Pokud správně chápu, znamená to: ...nemá právo se vměovat do ... (ve smyslu \"Co je mu do toho..!))\r\n\r\n2. \"nemá ani zbla představu\" (znamená to \"nemá ádnou představu\"?)\r\n\r\n3. \"kdy jeho firma dostane na frak\" nebo \"demokracie dostane na frak\" -- ? Tomu vůbec nerozumím.\r\n', '2003-11-14 18:28:02', 0, 0),
(1198, 17, 'Milan', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: hovorové výrazy', 'Přiznám se,e ačkoliv tu iji více jak 26 let,ten první výraz je tedy ,alesponĄpro mne,úplnou novinkou.Ale v podstatě bych řekl,e ty první dva výrazy jsi pochopila celkem správně.\r\n\r\nKdy někdo dostane na frak,znamená to,e v něčem neuspěl.Hodně se to pouívá ve sportu.Kdy např. Sparta prohraje se Slavií 0:6,tak můe říct,e spartĄani dostali pěkně na frak.\r\n\r\nU firmy to tedy znamená,e v něčem neuspěla.', '2003-11-15 04:10:47', 1197, 0),
(1199, 17, 'petmal', 'pet.malik@tiscali.cz', 'Re: hovorové výrazy', 'Ahoj, při jaké příleitosti byla řečena ta první věta?', '2003-11-18 02:17:31', 1197, 1),
(1200, 13, 'Nikol Mitchell', '', 'How to say Merry Christmas in Czech', 'How do you pronounce Vesele Vanoce?  My 7 year old son is doing a report on the Czech Republic and needs to learn how to say Merry Christmas.  I\'ve found the term \"Vesele Vanoce\" but I do not know how it is pronounced.\r\n\r\nThank you.', '2003-11-18 09:15:29', 0, 0),
(1201, 13, 'DIMA', '', 'Re: How to say Merry Christmas in Czech', 'I think VEHSEHLEH VAANOHTSEH :) Anyway i can record it later ..', '2003-11-18 16:14:02', 1200, 0),
(1202, 17, 'Dima', '', 'Re: hovorové výrazy', 'Teď nemůu najít, kde jsem si to přečetl. Je to jeden ze dvou: Pavel Eisner nebo ekonom Robert Holman. A najdu, tak vám řeknu. Děkuji za vae odpovědí.\r\n\r\nP.S. Já jsem CHLAP. Celé jméno zní: Dmitri. Pouívám zkrátku Dima. :)', '2003-11-18 16:18:58', 1198, 0),
(1203, 13, 'Lorenzo', 'exilems72@hotmail.com', 'Passive constructions', 'Hi everyone,\r\n\r\nI\'m sort of digging into Czech grammar  and I would like to ask a question about passive constructions. \r\nCould someone here please give me a brief (or extended if possible) overview of Czech passive constructions?\r\n\r\nWhat I know is that you need to add the auxiliary verb \"být\" to the passive participle of the verb as in \"Je milována\" (she is loved) or \"Kniha je čtena\" (The book is read). But I think it works differently with sense verbs (chutnat, čichat, dotýkat se, slyet, vidět) as I know the translation of \"When the sky is clear mountains can be seen from there\" should be as follows: \"Kdy je jasná obloha, jsou odtamtud vidět hory\" and here we have být+infinitive+noun (in accusative but I know it could also be in the nominative).\r\nIs that the way passive sentences are build with all sense verbs? Or is this just an exception?\r\nFor instance, would it be correct to say \"Potuček je slyet\"?\r\n\r\nBy the way, it seems to me that passive is not much used in Czech. I think it depends on the fact that, thanks to the use of cases, there\'s the possibility of switching \"subject\" and \"object\" in a sentence giving prominence on either one or the other element.\r\n\r\nThank you!\r\n\r\nLorenzo', '2003-11-18 21:58:37', 0, 0),
(1204, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'No time for a full reply, just want to comment on the last part -- \"the passive is not used that much in Czech.\" Intuitively I wouuld agree, though it may just be that I don\'t notice the cases where it is used in Czech and not English as much as those where it is used in English and not Czech. Certainly <b>reflexive</b> constructions take a bite out of the uses of passive constructions:\r\n\r\nBorscht is eaten regularly throughout Central and Eastern Europe.\r\nBorč <b>se jí</b> pravidelně po celé střední a východní Evropě.\r\n\r\nHe was born on Christmas Day.\r\n<b>Narodil se</b> o tědrý den.\r\n\r\nI went to have my hair cut.\r\nel jsem <b>si nechat ostříhnout</b> vlasy.\r\n\r\nOf course there are also other Czech constructions that replace the passive, including the use of word order to express topic-focus perspective (I THINK that\'s what it\'s called), that is, the aspect you mentioned above:\r\n\r\nAnd here you see a building praised by King Bob himself.\r\nA zde najdete budovu, kterou pochválil samotný Král Bob.\r\n\r\nIf the passive is used because \"there is no actor\", Czech sometimes resolves the same problem by letting \"them\" take care of the job:\r\n\r\nThe victims were EATEN ALIVE!!\r\nOběti snědli JETĚ IVÉ!!\r\n\r\nBTW sense verbs DO have less of a tendency to involve the passive than in English, but I\'m not qualified to say if they never use them.\r\n\r\nAnd on his bad days, his stink can be smelled even in the next room over.\r\nA kdy má zrovna patný den, je cítit ten jeho smrad i ve vedlejí místnosti.\r\n\r\nLikewise \"je slyet\"; you already mentioned \"je vidět\". \"Je chutnat\" is something imaginable only perhaps in science fiction or fantasy (AFAIK you can\'t say \"Jejích víno je chutnat na kadém ročníku Hruické koti\" for example), and since \"je cítit\" is already taken for the sense of smell, and since \"can be felt\" in the LITERAL sense of feeling is not very useful... well...\r\n\r\nEnough for now.', '2003-11-20 15:59:44', 1203, 0),
(1205, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'Také znám ty PRÝ, a myslím, e by je měl člověk úplně ignorovat, protoe tato mylenka se prostě stala rozířená a íří se jetě dál a dál vlastní sílou, ne vlastní pravdivostí. Neříkám, e to není moná pravda, jenom e to není pravda jen proto, e se to tolik traduje.\r\n\r\nOsobně znám francouztinu a troililinku němčinu. Myslím, e co do \"oficiální\" struktury, četina ji má sloitějí ne tyto jazyky a angličtina, ale co do \"neoficiální\", těko popsatelné struktury, četina jí má míň, a abych pravdu řekl, je často snaí se naučit to, co se podává v učebnicích... na druhé straně, učebnice četiny bohuel IMHO zanedbávají česky systém vyjadření důleitost slov ve větě pomocí slovosledu, co se sice nehodí do první kapitoly, ale kdy se člověk vůbec nenaučí o co gou, můe se mu zdát česky slovosled jako íleně frustrující chaos.\r\n\r\nKadopádně si nedovedu představit, e bych se naučil třeba arabsky nebo urdu rychleji ne česky... ale kdybych byl arab, bylo by tomu naopak. Take opatrně s tím \"tento jazyk \'je\' nejtěí\"...', '2003-11-20 16:10:07', 1157, 0),
(1206, 16, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: příprava ke stání zkouce z četiny', 'Abych pravdu řekl, stydím se \"před\" tebou, protoe jsem tady deset let a ani teďka neumím česky tak dobře jak ty. Je sice pravda, e jsem se tomu skoro nevěnoval (na kurzy četiny jsem např. chodil jen tak půl roku), ale stejně. Kadopadně se TY nestyď.', '2003-11-20 16:14:06', 1196, 0),
(1207, 13, 'Erik', '', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'One more example of an English contruction eliminated in Czech:\r\n\r\nI don\'t know how much money can be withdrawn from that account.\r\nNevím kolik peněz <b>lze</b> z tohoto účtu vytáhnout.\r\n\r\nBy the way, take my exact wording in Czech with a pound of salt -- I\'m an American. But the \"anti-passive\" constructions should be sound.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-20 19:35:43', 1204, 0),
(1208, 20, 'Todd Kaercher', '', 'Re: families', 'I\'m so glad to hear from a mom!  My son Mark Kritz is having the time of his life, too.  He is getting a little sick of school and ready to get out and do something.  I hope we can keep this dialogue.  If they would like to make some place else for the families to keep in touch, that would be great.  I\'ve made some bumper stickers that say Proud Mother (aunt, grandmother, etc.) of a Peace Corps Volenteer with the Albanian flag.  I can send you the graphic if you want.  I\'ve asked about sweatshirts and hats with Peace Corps on them and no one seems to think they exist.  Maybe we could start.', '2003-11-20 23:25:22', 1183, 1),
(1209, 13, 'Stef', '', 'Happy Birthday', 'How do you write and say Happy Birthday in Czech? Thanks!', '2003-11-21 10:16:58', 0, 0),
(1210, 13, 'Lorenzo', 'exilems72@hotmail.com', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'Thank you, Erik, for your enlightening explanation and examples! \r\nI had not thought of reflexive constructions as a way to get around passive but thats how it works ! (also in Italian which is my mother tongue so I should have known!)\r\nYou wrote that youre American so you speak Czech as a foreign language? Just curious.\r\n\r\nLorenzo', '2003-11-21 16:51:02', 1207, 0),
(1211, 13, 'Lorenzo', '', 'Re: Happy Birthday', 'Hi Stef,\r\n\r\nit\'s \"Ve nejlepí k narozeninám\"\r\n\r\nI\'m not sure how to render the pronunciation of these phrase in English but maybe the following could do \"Fsheh naylehpshi gh nahrozehninahm\"\r\n\r\nLorenzo', '2003-11-22 01:25:55', 1209, 0),
(1212, 13, 'Tony Peachment', 'apeachment@tiscali.cz', 'Vdyt´', 'I\'m stuck for a rule (or at least a rough translation) for vdyt´. It has to be one of the most common utterances in spoken Czech and, at times I use it myself but I never know if it is correct. \r\n\r\nMy rough idea is that it is used when you correct a negative statement (?).\r\nOdpověd je \'strom\'\r\nVdyt jsem to řekl!!!\r\n\r\nAnd while we\'re at it, could anyone give me an overview of the rules of ke bych.\r\n\r\nDiky\r\n\r\nTony', '2003-11-24 19:22:12', 0, 1),
(1213, 20, 'Donna & Jim Borgford-Parnell', 'borgford@u.washington.edu', 'Re: families', 'It would be great to get your graphic for the bumper stickers.  What program did you use to create the bumper sticker?  It would be great to have something to display to let others know how proud we are of our son & how excited we are that he is in Albania.  You can send the graphic directly to me at: borgford@u.washington.edu ', '2003-11-25 09:15:34', 1208, 0),
(1214, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Vdyt´ (and  Ké bych)', 'After mulling over several examples culled from a corpus, I\'d say vdy is all about <i>emotionally stating an assertion imposing some other (sometimes only implied) assertion</i>. Here are some examples.\r\n\r\n<i>\"To vechno v té tmě?\" zeptal se Wolfe. \"Samozřejmě. Znám tu kadý kámen, <b>vdy</b> jsem se tady narodila.[....]\"</i>\r\n\r\nHere the word vdy emphasizes the <b>weight</b> of the statement \"I was born here\" <b>as an argument overriding another one</b>; I would compare it to English \"after all\".\r\n\r\n<i>Řekl mi... ale <b>vdy</b> je to jedno, co mi řekl.</i>\r\n\r\nHere the word vdy strengthens the statement \"je to jedno,\" standing in opposition to the preceding between-the-lines assertion that it was worth recounting what so-and-so said.\r\n\r\n<i>Reportér, pochopitelně mu, právě prohlásil, e dolo k nepříjemnému kontaktu nohou hráčů, místo aby řekl jasně, e si vzájemně nakopali. Bylo by to v naprostém pořádku, <b>vdy</b> kopaná je od toho, aby se při ní kopalo!</i>\r\n\r\nHere again the word vdy emphasizes a statement in opposition to one just made (again, between the lines): the assertion that soccer, i.e. \"the kicking game\" (i.e. in slightly moldy Czech, <i>kopaná</i>) is all about people kicking (no matter what the target) during it, placed in opposition to the mentioned reporter\'s implied statement that people kicking each other during soccer is not good.\r\n\r\nenská role, mateřská funkce, manelská povinnost... Skončí to příerným slovem samoivitelka, co je dokonalá ukázka zrůdností a uráek českého jazyka, kterých je schopna mutina. Proboha, <b>vdy</b> jsme se díky muskému hraní na vojáky, nezodpovědně přetahovanému do civilu a do dob mírových, ocitli... Ano, tam, kde jsme. V paternalistickém světě.\r\n\r\nIn this case, the assertion being countered is either way back before the start of the quote, or is assumed to be hanging in the air. I suppose it\'s something like \"women have little/no value\". Above all, you can feel the emotion wafting from this vdy, although as always, its actual pronunciation is a nervous, tense, calm before the storm of an upcoming word.\r\n\r\nIn fact, this is true in general: vdy indicates emotional opposition to an assertion, and though it is not itself pronounced with anything more than perhaps a shift up the musical scale one\'s voice is \"singing\", soon after it tends to come an explosion representing anger, resignedness, self-assuredness, whatever that led to using vdy:\r\n\r\nVdy jsem se tady NARODila (yes, I know, accent always on the first syllable... but I swear it sometimes leaks through to the second in cases like these)\r\nVdy je to JEDno\r\nVdy kopaná je od toho, aby se při ní KOPALO!\r\n\r\nEtc.\r\n\r\n----------------------\r\n\r\n<i>Ké bych</i> is not something you\'ll be using or hearing often...\r\n\r\nIn and of itself, \"ké\" is hyperformal (thus making it occasionally colloquial) and is basically like English\'s imperative \"may.\" Again examples (this time from the \'net, since ke is so rare that it doesn\'t appear in my minicorpus):\r\n\r\n<i>Ke [sic] by zato dostal tu rakovynu [sic]. Sviňa bilá [sic].</i>\r\nMay da basterd get cancer. Honky sh*thead.\r\n\r\nBut more relevantly... ké bych. I\'d most compare it to \"if only\". Like anything involving ké, it has Biblical undertones. \r\n\r\n<i>Achjo holky, <b>ke [sic] bych</b> vám to mohla říct jak přibrat, pro mě je to taaak lehké asi jako pro vás ubrat na váze ( co bych zase potřebovala poradit já )... at se člověk snaí jak se snaí zhubnout tak se nedaří :((</i>\r\n\r\nSpeaks for itself, I think. (These \"sic\"-d ke\'s are because I first looked for ke instead of the correct ké, and I don\'t want to throw away my examples...)\r\n\r\nThe rest of these are from my minicorpus (which merely couldn\'t cope with the misspelling).\r\n\r\n<i>Smí vůbec být ena tak troufalá? Och, Boe, <b>ké bych</b> to věděla!</i>\r\n\r\n<i>Ach, <b>ké bych</b> věděl, komu za to poděkovat!</i>\r\n\r\n<i><b>[...]ké bych</b> byl i nadále tak zdravý jako teď.</i>\r\n\r\nThe last one is not \"if only\", but rather the imperative \"may\" or \"let\" (May I be as healthy in the future as I am now); I think it\'s clear enough from the context when it\'s \"if only\" and when not.\r\n\r\nHope that helps!\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-25 16:34:31', 1212, 0),
(1215, 13, 'Erik', '', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'Yup!\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-25 16:34:51', 1210, 0),
(1216, 13, 'Anthony Peachment', 'apeachment@tiscali.cz', 'Re: Vdyt´ (and  Ké bych)', 'Thanks a lot, that helped. However your view that ké is hyperformal slightly perplexes me. I have heard it relatively often, of course not as often as přát si. How else does one say; \"I wish I were at home\" \"ké bych byl doma.\"\r\nI would agree that the use of the past ké is old-fashioned. \"I wish I had seen it\" \"Ké bych býval to viděl.\"\r\nWhat do you think?', '2003-11-27 00:21:19', 1214, 1),
(1217, 13, 'Anthony Peachment', 'apeachment@tiscali.cz', 'Re: Vdyt´ (and  Ké bych)', 'OK, you could say \'u abych byl doma\'', '2003-11-27 15:16:45', 1216, 1),
(1218, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Vdyt´ (and  Ké bych)', 'Are you really foreigner?I think ypur explanation is pretty good.You\'re more like a Czech person.\r\nMilan', '2003-11-29 04:30:35', 1214, 0),
(1219, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Vdyt´ (and  Ké bych)', 'I\'m too busy lately to reply in full; here\'s a short statement that hopefully sums up what I\'d say in a long one:\r\n\r\nthe line between \"hyperformal\" and \"informal\" is often thiner than between \"formal\" and \"informal\".\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-12-01 18:12:12', 1216, 0),
(1220, 14, 'Paul Utesch', 'pauly_boj@hotmail.com', 'Re: trip to czech republic', 'Go to Olomouc!  I lived in Olomouc as a student for a while, and it was awesome.  It has things you\'d like in big city, but has small city mentality.  The people are really polite.  It is very historic, and has a very practical tram system.  I think they have an english version to their www.olomouc.cz website, but I cannot find it.  Give it a try!', '2003-12-02 03:37:32', 1147, 0),
(1221, 14, 'Paul Utesch', 'pauly_boj@hotmail', 'Re: more wierd-arse questions from nickie!', 'Hey Nickie... I was a foreign exchange student in my junior year of high school... I went to Olomouc, Czech Republic.. great place.  It sounds like you\'re a fun loving dude... and if you ever have any questions, drop me a line, because I know where you\'re coming from.  Well.. except, I guess I don\'t know where you\'re from?  I\'m guessing from the family situation it\'s USA?  I am from Iowa... but yeah, if you need any advice or encouragement, ask me.. Czech is the BEST choice... the place is awesome.  I\'m going back for college at Charles University in 2004... just e mail me dude', '2003-12-02 03:53:12', 320, 0),
(1222, 20, 'Mary Todd Kaercher', 'titania@minn.net', 'Re: families', 'I have sent the graphic to the Borgford-Parnell\'s.  Anybody else want it?  Also, I\'m ordering sweatshirts for Xmas.  I think I can get them made for about $40.  Anybody interested?', '2003-12-02 05:31:05', 1213, 0),
(1223, 14, 'Anja', 'abs@gearbulk.com', 'Re: trip to czech republic', 'Hi Mark, I\'ll send you an email with some possebilities in Sumava if you\'re interested. Kvilda or Horska Kvilda. Full nature and relaxation.', '2003-12-03 17:29:25', 1147, 0),
(1224, 13, 'Cindy', 'riverdancer49@hotmail.com', 'Grandma', 'I\'ve been searching a long time.  All I want to know is what is the Czech name for Grandmother.  My grandmother was born in Prague and at the age of 4 moved to Austria.  She has been gone many years now but THIS time of year still brings back fond memories of her wonderful baked goodies, and the bubbles lights on her Christmas tree.  WE called her Babi(phonetically Bubbi).  Is that Czech?  All of a sudden at my age (51) I yearn to learn more about my heritage.  I find that in looking at some of her recipes, my mom\'s recipes, that there is an Austrian influence, Jewish as well as German. I know nothing of HER parents but when visiting my Dad next time plan to ask lots of questions :).  If anyone can help with the Grandma question, I would love to hear from you.  ', '2003-12-05 00:50:49', 0, 1),
(1225, 13, 'Cindy', 'riverdancer49@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Women', 'hmmmmmmm, am wondering what you mean by mad? ', '2003-12-05 00:55:33', 1171, 1),
(1226, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Grandma', 'Cindy, babi is short for babička the czech for grandmother. Babi is exactly what my daughter calls her czech grandmother and I think is used by  most czech children. ', '2003-12-05 04:30:53', 1224, 0),
(1227, 17, 'jitka', 'pjitka@post.cz', 'Re: vizum do ameriky', 'Ahoj, Petro, \r\nobjevila jsem prave tvoji nabidku. Mam zajem dozvedet se neco podrobnejsiho.  O jake studijni obory se jedna, existuje nejaka vekova hranice atd.?  Muzes postlat? Dekuji\r\nHezky den preje \r\nJitka\r\n(Co ty tam studujes?)', '2003-12-05 19:16:45', 1163, 0),
(1228, 13, 'jitka', 'pjitka@post.cz', 'Re: Passive constructions/1.', 'Ahoj Eriku,\r\npokud uz vysvetlujes cestinu na takove urovni, doufam, ze muzu odpovidat cesky. Cetla jsem Tvoje prispevky, ale protoze anglicky az tak dobre neumim, nerozumela jsem uplne vsemu a tudiz se s Tebou ani nemuzu poustet do nejake \"odborne\" jazykove diskuse. Ale pro presnost - dovolim si par oprav Tvych ceskych textu.\r\n1.Narodil se o Stedry den. > Narodil se na Stedry den /na Mikulase... \r\n                             3.os.c.j - nejedna se o pasivum (stejne: narodil \r\n                             jsem se ...\r\n2.Sel jsem si nachat ostrihnout vlasy.(perf.) >lepe.. ostrihat vlasy (imperf.)', '2003-12-05 19:51:31', 1204, 0),
(1229, 13, 'jitka', 'pjitka@', 'Re: Passive constructions!2.', '3. dalsi 3 ceske preklady nejsou v pasivu - ve srovnani s anglickym textem (chtel jsi tim ukazat rozdil mezi Cj a Aj?)\r\n4.  \"je slyset\", \"je videt\" , \"je citit\" nejsou pasiva. Vyraz \"je chutnat\" se neda pouzit. Posledni vetu muzeme vyjadrit pasivem napr.takto: Jejich(3.os.pl., takze kratke i) vino se ochutnava (\"kostuje\") na kazdem rocniku Hrusicke kosti. \r\n\r\nJen jeste dodatek - velice nerada \"deformuji\" svuj rodny jazyk tim, ze pisu bez carek a hacku, ale bohuzel se mi nedari doplnit je do sveho programu v pocitaci.  ', '2003-12-05 20:09:01', 1204, 0),
(1230, 13, 'jitka', 'pjitka@post.cz', 'Re: Passive constructions/3.', 'Eriku,\r\n-jeste jedna posledni oprava:\r\nNevim kolik penez lze z tohoto uctu vytahnout. > Nevim, kolik penez lze z toho uctu \"vybrat\" /\"vyzvednout\".( v souvislosti s penezi! a bankou/uctem)\r\nSloveso \"vytahnout\" muzes pouzit ve spojenich jako:\r\n... penize z penezenky/kapsy..\r\n... trn z paty\r\n... nekoho z \"bryndy\" atd.\r\nProzatim je to vsechno,\r\nhezky den preje \r\nJitka', '2003-12-05 20:16:45', 1207, 0),
(1231, 13, 'jitka', '', 'Re: New Love', 'Nevim, jestli se jeste nekdy vracis ke stare poste, ale treba ano.Pak se Ti mozna zalibi i druhy vyraz pro anglicke \"I  miss you\" : Styska se mi po tobe.\r\nZdravi Jitka', '2003-12-05 20:20:37', 1187, 0),
(1232, 13, 'Cindy', 'riverdancer49@hotmail.com', 'Re: Grandma', 'Kevin, thank you very much!', '2003-12-05 21:23:31', 1226, 1),
(1233, 13, 'Danny Lankford', 'lankforddl@yahoo.com', 'Pimsleur Audio', 'ahoj,\r\n\r\nI\'ve been trying to find  the Pimsluer audio tapes on MP3 or in some digital format. Does anyone know where these can be found or if they even exist? \r\n\r\nděkuji,\r\nDanny', '2003-12-09 12:16:01', 0, 1),
(1234, 17, 'Nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: :o)', 'Mám se báječně a jsem v Praze. Zrovna krásně sněí a venku panuje předvánoční nálada. Jak se daří vám? :) N.', '2003-12-16 14:35:03', 1162, 0),
(1235, 13, 'Félix', 'beafele@eremas.com', 'beatove', 'Queridos amigos,\r\nnecesito ayudo con la palabra \"beatove\". &#191;Tiene algún significado en checo? Muchas gracias por anticipado.\r\nFélix, Espa&#241;a\r\n\r\nHallo Freunde,\r\nVielleicht köntet ihr mir helfen. Bedeutet etwas \"beatove\" in czech?\r\nVielen Dank und herzliche Grüße\r\nFélix, Espa&#241;a\r\n\r\nDear friends,\r\nI need some help from you with the word \"beatove\". Does it mean anything in czech?\r\nThank you very much in advance.\r\nFélix Medina', '2003-12-18 22:10:56', 0, 0),
(1236, 13, 'unknown', '', 'Re: Pimsleur Audio', 'try to find out czech lessons throuhg p2p webs', '2003-12-20 22:52:52', 1233, 0),
(1237, 13, 'jana', '', 'Re: beatove', 'hi felix,\r\nit\'s just a guess but depending on context i\'d understand it as something having to do with modern music. \"beat\" is not a czech word but many borrowed words slipped from their original meaning. the ending \"-ove\" is one of the options to modify the stem to another word class (the morphology of czech language is quite rich). as an example, \"samet\" (velvet) can be modified to \"sametovy\" (velvety) in this way.\r\ni hope this helps, if you need a more precise meaning, pls let me know the context (particularly the noun following the \"beatove\").\r\nmerry xmas,\r\njana', '2003-12-21 18:56:11', 1235, 0),
(1238, 13, 'Cyndi', '', 'Re: phrase', 'I need to know how to phrase\r\n\"We love our grand children.\" in Czech.  This is for a Christmas present so please help fast.', '2003-12-22 00:41:32', 0, 1),
(1239, 13, 'A. Soukup', 'aws@pld.com', 'conjugations', 'I\'ve just started researching czech.  I am having trouble finding information over conjugating verbs.  The present tense seems straight forward enough.  But, I am having trouble finding information about the other tenses.  Does anyone know of any conjugation tables or such that may be of some use.\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n\r\nAndy', '2003-12-22 11:20:37', 0, 1),
(1240, 13, 'Félix', 'beafele@eresmas.com', 'Re: beatove', 'Thank you very much Jana. There is no context, I mean, \"beatove\" is a surname here in Spain and I was wondering if it could come from Czech Republic. Your answer has been very kind.\r\nI wish you merry christmas and an extraordinary happy new year for you and your lovely country.\r\nFélix', '2003-12-24 00:23:43', 1237, 0),
(1241, 13, 'lovermann', '', 'Re: conjugations', 'http://www.lingvisto.org/cze/konjugacio.pdf\r\nThis document was made by me for russian-speakers, so the translations and comments are in russian, but the conjugation forms of 12 verbs are in czech. ', '2003-12-24 03:55:24', 1239, 0),
(1242, 13, 'lovermann', '', 'conjugations -- la plej utila pagxo', 'Let mi present on of the most useful resourses on verb conjugation:\r\n(123 czech verbs)\r\nhttp://www.verba.org/verbi/all_verbs_index_cs.html\r\n\r\nOne can find there thousands of other cojugated verbs in many languages.', '2003-12-24 15:39:23', 1239, 0),
(1243, 13, 'Mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'translation of the word \'asi\'', 'Hi, can somebody give me a translation of the word ASI as used in the next sentence: \"Asi bych nechtel prozivat.... \". Sorry, I don\'t have a Czech keyboard.\r\n', '2003-12-24 15:52:10', 0, 1);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1244, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: translation of the word \'asi\'', 'Hi!\r\nIt depends on content, but the sentence should mean something like \"I would rather not to experience (something)\" or, literaly, \"Perhaps I would not want to experience (something)\".\r\n', '2003-12-25 21:33:21', 1243, 0),
(1245, 15, 'Michael Fiala', 'fiala@interlog.com', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'Erik, relax!\r\nIt really means: In essence...\r\nCheers, Michael F.', '2003-12-27 09:25:37', 1190, 0),
(1246, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: phrase', 'Cindi, the answer too late is \"Mame radi nase vnocata\" sorry did not see until after Xmas (and also I am without czech keyboard so haceks etc are missing). I hope you can use it at another time. You say it like ma-meh rah-dee nash-eh fnot-cha-ta.', '2003-12-28 23:35:27', 1238, 0),
(1247, 13, 'mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'Levou botu', 'Here\'s another sentence from my coursebook that I can\'t translate properly: Levou botu úz má obutou a ted´ si obouvá pravou botu. It is about a boy putting on his shoes at morning but I don\'t understand the exact meaning of the sentence.  \r\nBy the way, are verbs like obouvat used in common czech??? ', '2003-12-29 14:04:27', 0, 1),
(1248, 13, 'don', 'donek@xs4all.nl', 'help - translation', 'Ahoj Bohemica!\r\nWould someone be so kind as to assist me?\r\nI\'m trying to compose an imaginary book title in the style of the Harry Potter titles (for example: \'Harry Potter and the Enchanted Broom-Cupboard\')\r\nWhat I want to translate is:\r\n\"Will o\' the wisp Jirout and the Mystery of the Missing T613S spare-parts\"\r\nMy own effort (so far) is:\r\n\'Bludi?ka Jirout a záhada .. chyb?íjícy T613S sou?ástky\' \r\nI\'m having trouble with \'mystery\' (I would like, if possible, the nuance \'enigma\') and how to make the adjective agree.\r\nAny advice gratefully received.\r\nPozdravem,\r\nDon\r\nP.S. Just to explain the background: I have a Tatra T613S, and my friend Zdislav gave a large quantity of spare parts to our friend Jirka (Jirout) who would bring them from Jezenik to me in Holland. That was more than two months ago, and it has become a standing joke. Jirka is well known for being an unpredictable whirlwind, always on the move and somewhat unreliable.\r\nP.P.S. Also I notice that I cannot type the letter with accent hook (háéek) here, it appears as an e with a backward-sloping accent. But I think the meaning will be clear. ', '2003-12-29 22:13:55', 0, 1),
(1249, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: help - translation', 'Hi Don!\r\n\r\nI would translate it as \"Bludička Jirout a záhada* chybějících součástek T613S\"\r\n*or \"tajemství\"\r\n\r\nadjectice forms are \"záhadný\" and \"tajemný\"\r\n\r\nOr, more freely, \"Bludička Jirout a záhadné/tajemné zmizení součástek T613S\", but I am not sure whether this is the exact meaningINSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1350, 14, 'a_7', 'amraam_7@seznam.cz', 'Re: burning the witch-april 30', 'This tradition symbolizes the end of winter. Winter - cold, ugly, is burned as spring comes. The death of the witch means the end of winter :)', '2004-04-25 21:47:14', 1319, 1),
(1351, 17, 'a_7', 'amraam_7@seznam.cz', 'Re: hovorové výrazy', '1 & 2 - správně\r\n3 - dostat na frak =  utrpět nějakou újmu', '2004-04-25 21:52:44', 1197, 1),
(1352, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Let\'s do', 'Dear J\r\nyou are right ,it is not literary Czech.But I would really translate Let\'s go as Pojd\'me since I don\'t find any trouble to say it I think it is possible to read it in books as well.Bye.', '2004-04-25 23:42:52', 1349, 0),
(1353, 13, 'Paul', 'Psmith325ti@aol.com', 'How to say....', 'Can somebody tell me how to wish somebody a \'good relaxing holiday\' and \' take care\'\r\nThanks', '2004-04-27 23:38:24', 0, 0),
(1354, 13, 'amathes', '', 'Re: How to say....', '\'good relaxing holiday\' = uij(te) si dovolené\r\n\'take care\' = měj se', '2004-05-09 20:14:41', 1353, 0),
(1355, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Translation', 'Hi, \r\n\r\nI\'m having visitors from Czech in two weeks time and would be very gratefull if somebody could assist me with the translation & pronounciation of a few words.  \r\n\r\nBreakfast\r\nBrunch\r\nLunch\r\nSupper\r\nWine list\r\nSoup\r\nVenison\r\nSauce\r\n', '2004-05-12 18:56:18', 0, 1),
(1356, 16, 'Valja', 'vpopovich@yandex.ru', 'Looking for a Czech tutor in Kijev', 'Hello\r\nI am looking for a Czech native speaker to take private lessons in Kijev, Ukraine.\r\n\r\nMy tel. No. is +38 050 9396932\r\nEmail vpopovich@yandex.ru\r\n\r\nAny info would be very much appreciated.\r\n\r\nThank you\r\n\r\nValja', '2004-05-12 19:44:07', 0, 1),
(1357, 14, 'T', 'Trany13@hotmail.com', 'individual debt in the Czech Republic--jail time?', 'Does anybody know what happens to a person in regards to an unpaid debt in the Czech Republic?  I have heard that the person who does not pay goes to jail.  So, is there a \"debtor\'s prison\"?  Thanks--I\'m curious.', '2004-05-16 19:36:59', 0, 0),
(1358, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Reply to sorry', 'Can you help me - what should I say in English, when somebody says sorry to me. Is it \"no worries\"? Thank you very much.', '2004-05-17 01:59:14', 0, 0),
(1359, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Re: Reply to sorry', 'Well, I\'m American, and here we\'d only say \"no worries\" in reply to \"sorry\" mockingly or ironically, or if we had delusions of being Australian. So I\'d say \"no problem\", or \"don\'t worry\", or something of that nature.', '2004-05-17 07:32:15', 1358, 0),
(1360, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: Reply to sorry', 'Thank you very much. It is a very frequented situation and I had never known what to say before you answered me.', '2004-05-18 18:27:53', 1359, 0),
(1361, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nin all of the below, keep in mind that my \"y\"s are as in yellow, and my \"h\"s are just used to indicated that it\'s an (English) short vowel, nothing more. \"Zh\" below should be pronounced s-as-in-pleasure. \r\n\r\nBreakfast - snídaně (SNYEE dahn yeh)\r\n\r\nBrunch - doesn\'t really exist in Czech, a good translation might be \"větí snídaně\" (VYEHT shee   SNYEE dahn yeh) -- literally \"a relatively-large (or quite-large) breakfast\") -- a Czech reading this answer and who recovers from the initial shock of my not translating it as \"a larger breakfast\" might be able to help. You might also try \"mení oběd\" (MEN shee OH byehd), a quite-small lunch, or the two in short succession.\r\n\r\nLunch - oběd (OH byehd)\r\n\r\nSupper - večeře -- this is a hard one because of the ř. Try (VEH cheh zheh). Zh is not the correct pronunciation of ř, but your aim here is to be understood (which you will be), not to shock them with your perfect pronunciation.\r\n\r\nWine list -- the literal equivalent \"seznam vín\" (list of wines - Czech uses \"of\" a lot because it doesn\'t have to use \"of\" to say \"of\"... well, never mind) is as far as I know not used in Czech. Try simply \"vína\" (VEE nah) -- wines. If there\'s any confusion, you can fall back to \"seznam vín\" (SEHZ nahm   VEEN).\r\n\r\nSoup -- polévka (POH leh fkah). The \"yeh\" should be pronounced a bit longer -- say 50% longer -- than the average length you\'re giving to a syllable. This is what\'s called a \"long vowel\" in Czech. (Their long vowels change only in length of pronunciation. They\'re generally marked by the slanting accent over the vowel. Some of the phrases above had long i, but \"ee\" in English tends to naturally have the right length for this.)\r\n\r\nVenison - apparently zvěřina (ZVYEH zhee nah). I\'m not enough of a fajnmekr (a jovial-to-ironic word for connoisseur) to be sure.\r\n\r\nSauce - omáčka (OH mah chkah). Again, drawl out the \"ah\", as it\'s a long vowel.\r\n\r\nIf you suspect your browser isn\'t displaying accented Czech letters properly, here they are in the awkward \"low Czech support friendly\" version. \' is a bottom left to top right accent, and ^ is the \"bird\'s wings\". Yeah, it\'s upside down. I\'ve put them after the letters they\'re above:\r\n\r\nsni\'dane^\r\nve^ts^i\' sni\'dane^ / mens^i\' obe^d\r\nobe^d\r\nvi\'na / seznam vi\'n\r\npole\'vka\r\nzve^r^ina\r\noma\'c^ka\r\n\r\nDobrou chu (Bon appetit)!\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-05-19 19:14:54', 1355, 0),
(1362, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: individual debt in the Czech Republic--jail time?', 'Generally, they go to the Bahamas ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-05-19 19:27:42', 1357, 0),
(1363, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Let\'s Do and Let\'s Go', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nHere\'s an \"outsider\'s\" view:\r\n\r\nPojďme really is quite common. It has in my opinion the advantage of sounding like a suggestion rather than a command. Napijme se sounds to my ears slightly authoritarian and maybe a little too formal. That\'s one disadvantage of the fact that Czech for foreigners textbooks are generally so insistent on spisovná Czech -- if you\'re not going to be in formal situations, it\'s so formal as to sound a bit odd.\r\n\r\n\"Let\'s do\" is a bit tricky, because it\'s a rather odd phrase in English.\r\n\r\nLet\'s do lunch -- when it comes to business, here it\'s America that\'s more informal than Czech. Though it depends. More importantly, there\'s no Czech phrase that so plainly carries the meaning \"...and discuss this at that lunch.\" Maybe \"Pojďme si o tom popovídat na obědě\", \"Popovídejme si o tom na obědě\", \"Dejme si oběd, tam si o tom popovídáme.\"\r\n\r\nLet\'s do the time warp again - Pojďme si jetě jednou zatančit time warp. ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-05-19 19:38:16', 1346, 0),
(1364, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi Erik\r\n\r\nThank you very much for your time and detailed explanation, you were of great help.\r\n\r\nSharon \r\nSouth Africa', '2004-05-19 20:05:16', 1361, 0),
(1365, 13, 'Patt', '', 'Re: Translation', 'Wine list can be more accurately translated as \"vinný lístek\", a term that is used in Czech (similar to \"jídelní lístek\" = menu). Pavel from Brno.', '2004-05-20 22:21:14', 1361, 0),
(1366, 13, 'Al', 'wwadomr@aol.com', 'List of Months', 'I would like to get names of months in Czech/English> Thaks you very much in dadvance for your prompt response.\r\n\r\nResoectfully yours, Al   ', '2004-05-28 23:27:15', 0, 0),
(1367, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: List of Months', 'January  leden \r\nFebruary  Únor \r\nMarch  březen \r\nApril  duben \r\nMay  květen \r\nJune  červen \r\nJuly  červenec \r\nAugust  srpen \r\nSeptember  září \r\nOctober  říjen \r\nNovember  listopad \r\nDecember  prosinec ', '2004-05-29 01:51:16', 1366, 0),
(1368, 16, 'ashe', 'bluevolcanicash@netscape.net', 'good site?', 'does anyone know where i can get a czech pen pal. i hear that talking is a good way to learn', '2004-05-29 04:14:26', 0, 0),
(1369, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Reply to sorry', 'To put an english UK perspective on this we have varied responses to someone saying sorry. \'No worries\' will have Austrailian connotations, but is  acceptable, its quite common nowaday\'s (lightheartedly). We\'re more likely to say something like \'thats okay\' or it \'it doesn\'t matter\'. We might even say if indignant \'I should think so!\'. More formally or in  writing we sometimes say \'apology(ies) accepted\'.', '2004-05-29 21:11:55', 1360, 0),
(1370, 13, 'greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', 'how would you say this in Czech', 'how would you say \"what\'s up\" as a slang meaning in Czech?', '2004-06-02 06:57:51', 0, 1),
(1371, 14, 'Greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', '', 'What things or expressions do the Czech people consider rude or insulting?\r\nand what other languages are spoken in the Czech republic other than Czech or Slovak?', '2004-06-02 07:02:22', 0, 1),
(1372, 14, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Languages in CR.', 'The Czech language is the only language spoken in the Czech Republic. The Slovak language has a special legal status. Every ethnic group has his own language, of course, but ethnic minorities are small in our country.\r\n\r\nCzech + Moravian 94.4%, Slovak 3.1%, Polish 0.6%, German 0.5%, Silesian 0.4%, Roma 0.3%, Hungarian 0.2%, other 0.5% (1991)', '2004-06-02 15:08:16', 1371, 0),
(1373, 13, 'Jakubeku', '', 'Re: how would you say this in Czech', 'Co je???', '2004-06-04 03:51:49', 1370, 0),
(1374, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: how would you say this in Czech', 'I\'d say you would only say that if you meant \"What\'s up\" in the sense of \"What\'s wrong?\" Otherwise, how about \"Co je nového?\"\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\n', '2004-06-04 17:21:22', 1373, 0),
(1375, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Languages in CR.', 'Silesians with their own language? News to me...', '2004-06-04 17:23:50', 1372, 0),
(1376, 14, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Languages in CR.', 'Some people declared themselves to be Silesians. Maybe they also invented their own language.\r\n\r\nDitto for the Moravians.\r\n\r\nThe original census data (1991) were:\r\n\r\nCzech 81.2%, Moravians 13.2%, etc. (I merged Czechs and Moravians arbitrarily.)', '2004-06-07 15:44:56', 1375, 0),
(1377, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Pronounciation', 'Hello\r\n\r\nPlease help with pronounciation of dekuji - thank you\r\n\r\nThank you in advance\r\n\r\nSharon', '2004-06-07 18:54:28', 0, 0),
(1378, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Pronounciation', 'DYEH koo yee\r\n\r\n--\r\nErik', '2004-06-09 21:33:01', 1377, 0),
(1379, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Re: Pronounciation', 'Dear Erik\r\n\r\nThank you, I\'m going to visit friends in Czech in August and am trying to learn the basics.  At least I can say please and thank you and pronounce it correctly.\r\n\r\nRegards\r\nSharon', '2004-06-10 18:34:37', 1378, 0),
(1380, 13, 'Laura Hadley', 'lhadley@cs.umd.edu', 'Czech and Slovenian translators needed for children\'s books', 'My name is Laura Hadley, and I work with a project called the International Childrens Digital Library, at www.icdlbooks.org.  It showcases childrens books from around the world on the web.   Currently we are trying to include information on our books (such as the summary) in the original language of the book in addition to English.  We have a few books in Czech and Slovenian, and are having a hard time locating volunteer translators for this project in this language.  The book information to translate is fairly brief, so time commitment would be minimal.  This is a wonderful way to give back to the world community and help children.  Would it be possible to send this out to any possible Czech and Slovenian speakers you know that might be interested?  Please let me know if you have any questions or if you need more information at lhadley@cs.umd.edu.\r\n\r\nThank you very much,\r\nLaura Hadley\r\n', '2004-06-10 19:44:40', 0, 0),
(1381, 14, 'Greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', 'Should I learn Czech?', 'I am really interested in learn Czech.But I wanted to know how the women look over there. Because if I were to learn Czech and not put it to good use because, I dont like any of thier women then Its put to waist. So what are the features/characteristics of the women there? Do they look similar to German women? Do most of them have blue eyes?? If any one could message me on this information I would really appreciate it. Thanks!', '2004-06-11 08:10:38', 0, 1),
(1382, 13, 'greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', 'In what countries in Europe do they speak Czech?', 'Do you know how many countries in Europe speak Czech? Because I want to learn a popular Slavic language besides Russian. And I\'m having trouble with choosing that one language. So could you give me some advice?', '2004-06-11 08:16:00', 0, 1),
(1383, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech and Slovenian translators needed for children\'s books', 'ok, Laura, can\'t promis much time or prompt responses but can try to have a quick look on the texts.\r\npls get in touch (slavek777@hotmail.com),\r\njana', '2004-06-14 20:36:37', 1380, 0),
(1384, 14, '*', '', 'Re: Should I learn Czech?', 'Come and see...\r\nI suppose you won\'t be disappointed. Nothing more can be said.', '2004-06-15 05:01:36', 1381, 0),
(1385, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: In what countries in Europe do they speak Czech?', 'One country in Europe speaks Czech - the Czech Republic. Another country understands but does not speak it - Slovakia. \r\n\r\nYou won\'t find a Slavic language other than Russian that\'s spoken in more than one country, as far as I know. Perhaps Serb or Croat, but the post-Yugoslavian countries are kind of miniature (and many Serbs probably won\'t like hearing Croat and vice versa, which is why there are now Serb and Croat even though until recently one spoke of Serbo-Croat). If you\'re going for raw speaker count, perhaps Polish would be better. Though we\'d all be happiest here to see you speaking Czech ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-06-16 14:16:17', 1382, 0),
(1386, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech and Slovenian translators needed for children\'s books', 'I\'m about the same as Jana here... will do what I can, won\'t be much, but I\'ll be glad to help as much as I\'m able. The address is (in antispam form) erik at sky dot cz.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-06-16 14:18:29', 1380, 0),
(1387, 16, 'Alena', 'alena13@telus.net', 'Problem with registration', 'Hi,\r\nI have some problems registering myself in the teachers\' database on this website. It says I have to be a user of \'global internet\'...whatever that is.\r\nCan somebody help?\r\nThanks, Alena', '2004-06-18 04:59:59', 0, 0),
(1388, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'zapředenou', 'What verb does \"zapředenou\" come from in:\r\n\r\n  Je sama písní zapředenou v čase\r\n\r\nThe translation I have says \"woven\" but I can\'t make it fit a conjugation of tkat.\r\n', '2004-06-20 22:03:45', 0, 1),
(1389, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: zapředenou', '\"zapředený\" is not \"zatkaný\", but metaphorically it is equivalent (spinned in time, woven in time)\r\n\r\npřísti = to spin (to make thread)\r\ntkáti = to weave (to make cloth, fabric)', '2004-06-22 16:47:57', 1388, 0),
(1390, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: zapředenou', 'Hope you don\'t laugh at me for nitpicking a minor point, but isn\'t it about time to just let the \"-i\" infinitives die? Nobody uses them in real life, and many dictionaries are starting to use the real-world ones as well. (That\'s my memory of the LEDA dictionary at least, and a quick check of some on-line translation dictionaries shows the same.) Using them seems hyperformal to me.\r\n\r\nJust a thought. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-06-22 19:01:51', 1389, 0),
(1391, 16, 'NK', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: good site?', 'http://www.aj.cz/penfriends/default.asp', '2004-06-23 20:15:03', 1368, 0),
(1392, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'Re: zapředenou', 'Thanks for the answer. Could you tell me one more thing. \r\n  \r\n  Je sama písní zapředenou v čase\r\n\r\nIs it that přísti is being conjugated into the \"present participle\" instrumental case in order to go with písní being in the instrumental case. If this is right, what does use of instrumental case indicate here?\r\n\r\n', '2004-06-24 05:12:03', 1390, 1),
(1393, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: zapředenou', '(Ona) je písní zapředenou ...\r\n\r\nOna - subject\r\nje písní ... - predicate\r\nzapředenou - attribute of \"píseň\" (it must be in the same case)\r\n\r\nWhen the predicate is in the nominative, it expresses a substantial (fundamental) quality of the subject of the sentence. \r\nWhen the predicate is in the instrumental, it expresses an acquired and transient quality of the subject. \r\n\r\nSome examples: \r\n\r\nA) (nom. is necessary)\r\n\r\n\"Velryba není ryba.\" (The whale is not a fish.) \r\nThis is a generally accepted fact, something that is not changeable. \r\nIf you (wrongly) said \"Velryba není rybou\", it would sound like the whale is not a fish today but may be a fish tomorrow.\r\n\r\n\"Eva je mladá dívka.\"\r\n\r\nB) (instr. is necessary) \r\n\r\n\"Petr je učitelem.\" (Petr is a teacher.) \r\nHe\'s a teacher because he decided to become one. He may change his profession later.\r\n\r\n\"Eva je ákyní naí koly.\"\r\n\"Otec je členem spolku chovatelů holubů.\"\r\n\"Václav Havel ji není prezidentem ČR.\"\r\nAlso: \r\n\"Hudba je mu vím (instr. of \"ve\").\"\r\n', '2004-06-24 17:22:38', 1392, 0),
(1394, 17, 'Oranje', 'oranje@knvb.nl', 'D&#234;kuji', 'D&#234;kuji from Holland!\r\n\r\nczechia rules :-)', '2004-06-24 21:43:11', 0, 0),
(1395, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: infinitiv', '\"Nobody uses them in real life, ...\"\r\n\r\nTo je pravda. V běném kontextu zní infinitiv na -ti divně, a směně.\r\nMimo běný kontext nebo jako izolované slovo je infinitiv na -ti výraznějí, ale přesto zní větinou neutrálně.\r\n\r\nNapř.:\r\n\r\n\"Tkáti je infinitiv.\" (netypické pouití - infinitiv tkáti je zde podmět, nezáleí na jeho významu, ale formě)\r\n\r\n\"Co znamená to weave?\" - \"Tkáti.\"\r\n\r\nv účetnictví: \"Má dáti\" - \"Dal\" (to je ustálený obrat)\r\n\r\n---\r\nVe slovnících je to téměř jedno. Mám slovníky s infinitivy na -ti (větinou do r. 1970) i s infinitivy na -t (po r. 1970). Kdy je pouívám střídavě, vůbec nevnímám rozdíl. Ve slovnících má infinitiv na -t velkou výhodu v tom, e sazeč uetří velké mnoství liter \"i\". :-)\r\n\r\n---\r\nV humoristických románech je infinitiv na -ti hojně vyuíván. Poručík Dub, v civilu profesor četiny, říká např.:\r\n     \"Mu ten, pane plukovníku,\" představoval vejka, \"dělá ze sebe blba, aby kryl svým idiotstvím svá darebáctví. Nejsem sice obeznámen s obsahem dodaného s ním spisu, nicméně domnívám se, e ten chlap zase něco provedl, ale ve větím měřítku. Kdybyste dovolil, pane plukovníku, abych se mohl seznámiti s obsahem spisu, mohl bych vám rozhodně dáti eventuelně určité direktivy, jak s ním naloit.\" (Dub pouívá inf. na -ti, ale není důsledný)\r\n\r\nJá vím, \"vejk\" je starý více ne 80 let, nicméně si myslím, e inf. na -ti lze podobně vyuít i dnes.\r\n\r\n----\r\n(Toto je můj subjektivní názor, lidé mladí mohou míti jiný. :-)\r\n\r\nS pozdravem\r\nVladimír\r\n', '2004-06-25 13:42:00', 1390, 0),
(1396, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: infinitiv, intrumentál', '\"...infinitiv tkáti je zde podmět,...\" Oops! Ouha!\r\n\r\nhloupá chyba - správně má být \"podmětem\" (instr.) - infinitiv není podmět, leč ve větě můe být podmětem', '2004-06-29 17:28:29', 1395, 0),
(1397, 15, 'Baptsite', 'fantabenz@hotmail.com', 'young french in pragua', 'Hi everybody,\r\nmy name is baptiste, I\'m 17 and i\'m french.\r\nI really wanna organise a trip in a great capitale of europe with some friends. I thought at Vienna and sure Pragua, to learn a lot about your culture. I want to discover lots of things and to meet lots of young people from there, that\'s why i let this message on the forum, if some of you wants to join me by mail and help me to discover your culture but also to discover mine, and maybe to meet in pragua, there is no problem,\r\njoin me on fantabenz@hotmail.com\r\nI\'m really interested by your culture and I already thank you \r\nFrenchly\r\nBapt', '2004-06-29 18:37:58', 0, 0),
(1398, 14, 'Baptiste', 'fantabenz@hotmail.com', 'a french in pragua', 'hi i\'m a young french man who really want to learn more about your culture, and i\'m decided to come in Pragua with some friends...\r\nI\'m just looking for some young czech who would help me to know more about your capital, about youngs there, about you....\r\nWe are ready to share lot\'s of things, so send me a mail at fantabenz@hotmail.com if you\'re ready to help us....\r\nThank you very much\r\nBaptiste\r\nfrance', '2004-06-29 18:43:52', 0, 0),
(1399, 17, 'baptiste', 'fantabenz@hotmail.com', 'a french in pragua', 'hi i\'m a young man who really wants to discover your wonderful country, that\'s why i\'m looking for some people to talk with, who could teach me lot\'s of things about your culture.\r\nI love my country and i love europa and travels, i\'m ready to share lot\'s of experiences,\r\nlet me know if you want to help me...\r\nfantabenz@hotmail.com\r\nThanks\r\nBapt\r\nFrance', '2004-06-29 18:47:56', 0, 0),
(1400, 14, 'Hesia', 'hesia@post.cz', 'football', 'Hallo\r\n\r\nI would like to write you that in Poland we enjoy every Czech victory, we keep finger\'s crossed for you, we are with you with whole our hearts ;-)\r\nGood luck !\r\n\r\nHesia', '2004-06-30 02:05:15', 0, 0),
(1401, 14, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: football', 'where were you last night then', '2004-07-03 04:08:28', 1400, 0),
(1402, 13, 'Paul', 'Psmith325ti@aol.com', 'Translation', 'Hi, hope someone can help, how do you say \'how are your parents?\' and \'Lot\'s of love\'\r\nthanks', '2004-07-07 00:13:50', 0, 0),
(1403, 13, 'Kim Van Dyke', 'kvandyke@berkshire.net', 'Questions about free online text translator', 'Hi, everyone. I am curious about the accuracy of a free online text translator. (Translate Now! on the left column at http://foreignword.com/Tools/transnow.asp?p=files/f_source.htm). I am trying to conduct a small e-mail business transaction and need to ask a czech-speaking person the following: \r\n\r\nWhat payment methods do you accept?\r\n\r\nThis is the result:\r\n\r\nJaký mzda metody činit tebe přijmout?\r\n\r\nIs that even close to how it would really be said in czech? How does it actually translate? Would I czech laugh his/her head off if I sent that?\r\n\r\nThanks for your help!\r\n\r\nKim\r\n\r\n', '2004-07-16 05:44:33', 0, 1),
(1404, 13, 'Nadia', 'tobiskova@atlas.cz', 'Re: Questions about free online text translator', 'Hi Kim,\r\n\r\nThe translation is very far from how it would be said in Czech. The message we (Czechs) learn from the translated text is something like \"What salary of method make you receipt?\" \r\n\r\nNadia', '2004-07-20 19:54:50', 1403, 0),
(1405, 13, 'Charles Marik', 'tmarik3@excite.com', '', '', '2004-07-21 07:45:41', 0, 0),
(1406, 13, 'Charles Marik', 'tmarik3@excite.com', 'name meaning?', 'hello, my family name is Marik. When my ancestors came to america the name was originally Marikova but shortened around 1880. I was just wandering if there was any translation for either Marik or Marikova. Any help would be greatly appreciated.\r\n', '2004-07-21 07:47:16', 0, 1),
(1407, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: name meaning?', 'Marik, in Czech Mařík (hacek above r, long i) is probably derived from Máří (in English Mary), i.e. Mary\'s husband (maybe Mary was a dominant wife) or son (Mary was single).\r\n\r\nIn the Slavic languages the suffix \"-ova\" forms the feminine surnames, e.g. Navrátilová (her father - Navrátil), thus Maříková (Marikova) is a woman (a daughter or a wife of Mařík).\r\n\r\nAnother meaning (in old Czech):\r\n\r\nmařík - fribble, waster, prodigal; derived from the verb \"mařit\" = to mar (time, money)\r\n\r\nBTW, \"Kája Mařík\" (Kája = Charlie) was a popular book for boys.\r\n\r\n', '2004-07-21 13:13:41', 1406, 0),
(1408, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Questions about free online text translator', 'Like Nadia said.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, the current generation of online translators, especially those available for \"minor\" languages like Czech, is very inadequate for Slavic languages, which use quite different syntax from English and which indicate grammar using word endings. Even the best online translators only make a halfhearted attempt at grammar and syntax; the one you quote didn\'t even do that; just a series of \"first translations in the dictionary\" for each word in a row, with no accounting for word endings (except in the case of tebe... and it got that one wrong).\r\n\r\nFind a good CZ->EN human translator -- they\'re not expensive by US standards -- and forget about online translations for at least a decade or so.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-07-21 20:46:01', 1403, 0),
(1409, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation', 'how\'re your parents = jak se maji rodice?\r\nlots of love does not have direct equivalent (at least i can\'t think of one). if you mean it as a card/letter ending, i tend to use \'zdravim\' (zdravit = +/- to greet) but i guess it does not really convey the meaning. hm, can\'t really think of anything suitable, probably \'myslim na vas/tebe\' (i think of you-plural/you-singular, you\'re on my mind). \r\n\r\nhope this helps a bit,\r\njana', '2004-07-21 21:03:52', 1402, 0),
(1410, 14, 'Petr', '', 'Re: Should I learn Czech?', 'So what are the features/characteristics of the women there?\r\n\r\nThey are beautiful, charming and loving, good in cooking and every other type of domestic service. I think, they are very practical, clever a foresighted.\r\nMany of them have no problem to spend time at home and raise their children.\r\n(Of course, all of this depence on the type of woman - if you are looking for a women in a bar or at disco, most of thing written above is not true :)\r\n\r\nDo they look similar to German women? \r\n\r\nNo way!!! German women do look more as if they were \"Barbie dolls\"\r\nCzech women have beautiful eyes, although most of them do not have blue ones.\r\n\r\nAbout learning Czech language:\r\nMy friend - Czech girl married an American who was learning Czech - she appreciated that. But you should do that because you really want to, not just because of some girl :)\r\n\r\nGood luck!!!', '2004-07-22 04:43:10', 1381, 0),
(1411, 13, 'De Zikis (nee: Janda)', 'dzikis@midwestern.edu', 'Checkmark', 'My grandmother use to say that she would put a hachic (?) by an item on a list.  Meaning that she would put a hash mark or check mark.  I would like to know the spelling and the pronunciation of this word (if there is such a word?).\r\nThank you.', '2004-07-24 00:15:01', 0, 0),
(1412, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Checkmark', 'The spelling is HACEK and for the pronuntiation see Merriam-Webster On-line Dictionary.\r\n\r\nhttp://www.m-w.com/cgi-bin/dictionary?book=Dictionary&va=hacek&x=13&y=11\r\n', '2004-07-26 13:41:58', 1411, 0),
(1413, 14, 'jana', '', 'Re: Should I learn Czech?', 'Greg,\r\nI\'ve been resisting quite nicely but I think I\'ll comment a little after all.\r\nBasicaly, surprising as you might find it, the command of Czech language is not limited to females and is fully unrelated to any kind of colouring (or any other \"parameters\", for that matter). I don\'t quite understand your motivation but learning a new language can be a very stimulating and enriching experiance worth your while, needless to say a way to widen your cultural horisonts.\r\nGood luck with learning whatever language you decide to go for,\r\nJana', '2004-07-28 20:16:16', 1381, 0),
(1414, 14, 'Hesia', 'hesiula@post.cz', 'Re: football', 'I was drinking with my Czech friends! ;-(\r\n;-)\r\n\r\nHesia', '2004-07-29 17:27:08', 1401, 0),
(1415, 21, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Problem se zarovnavanim', 'Je treba opravit textbox, aby se zarovnavalo (wrap), kdyz studentyi pisou souvisly text. (v tuit-ew).', '2004-07-30 16:56:51', 0, 0),
(1416, 13, 'cory', 'cniedfeldt@rsh.net', 'czech words', 'Hello, \r\nI\'m trying to learn how to say a few phrases in Czech.  They are\r\n\"What is your name?\"\r\n\"Do you like it here in America?\"\r\n\"You are very nice and very pretty\"\r\n\r\nThank you', '2004-08-09 21:15:57', 0, 0),
(1417, 13, 'hombre_sincero', '', 'knedliky', 'Hi there good people!\r\nI love \"knedliky\", but how should I correctly translate it in English? :-)\r\nThanks in advance ', '2004-08-10 20:12:23', 0, 0),
(1418, 13, 'hombre_sincero', '', 'knedliky', 'Hi there good people!\r\nI love \"knedliky\", but how should I correctly translate it in English? :-)\r\nThanks in advance ', '2004-08-10 20:27:26', 0, 0),
(1419, 13, 'Jirina Fuchsova', 'jfuchs@sprynet.com', 'Re: Questions about free online text translator', 'The existing so-called on-line trnslators\r\nwere devised by people who, unfortunately,\r\nhad no idea what Czech language really is...:-)\r\n   I would suggest the same as the contributor\r\nbefore: avoid \"on-line translator\" like a\r\nplague and contact the nearest living\r\nexperienced translator.\r\n   The previous person contributing here\r\nwas right: when one recives the fruits of\r\nlabor from on-line translator, the only thing\r\nleft to do is to bang your head against\r\n a wall in utter frustration, or, laugh\r\nthe next 24 hours at the least...\r\n   P.S. This advice comes from experience.....:-)', '2004-08-12 08:48:16', 1408, 0),
(1420, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: czech words', '\"What is your name?\" - Jak se jmenujes? (informal)\r\n\"Do you like it here in America?\" - Jak se ti/Vam tu libi? (ti = informal, Vam = formal)\r\n\"You are very nice and very pretty\" - careful with compliments, quite significant culture differences, nice in this sense = mila/mily (mila = fem, mily = masc) and pretty = hezka/hezky (again, a=fem, y=macs). my best advice would be to try a bit more indirect ways (that\'s very nice of you = to je od tebe/Vas moc mily)\r\nhope this helps,\r\njana', '2004-08-18 16:49:01', 1416, 0),
(1421, 13, 'Jira', '', 'Re: knedliky', 'Dumplings, I think.', '2004-09-01 18:35:47', 1418, 0),
(1422, 13, 'mike kren', 'mjkren@comcast.net', 'Re: knedliky', 'Small dumplings\r\n', '2004-09-11 02:24:48', 1418, 0),
(1423, 13, 'mark', '', 'Lhota', 'Driving through the Czech republik, I noticed that the word \'Lhota\'is frequently used in placenames. But I couldn\'t find it in my dictionary. Does the word \'Lhota\' means something???', '2004-09-14 15:02:30', 0, 1),
(1424, 13, 'J', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Lhota', 'Yes, \"Lhota\" is medieval form of the word \"Lhůta\" which means a term or a time limit. It denoted the fact that newly established village was freed of taxes for some time, usualy a few years, to support the settlement and boost grow.', '2004-09-15 23:30:58', 1423, 0),
(1425, 13, 'John', '', 'Re: czech words', 'I\'m very limited in the Czech language yet, but \"You are very nice and pretty\" might translate as \"jsi velmi hesky a krasne.\" [\"Sih vel-mee hess-key uh krah-sneh\"] \"What is your name?\" = \"jak se jmenujete?\" [\"Yuk seh men-oo-yet-eh\"] \"Do you like it here in America?\" - hmmm, good question ... try: \"mate rad Amerika?\" [\"Mah-teh rahd America\"] Remember, emphasis is always on the first syllable.', '2004-09-20 11:24:37', 1416, 0),
(1426, 13, 'John', '', 'Actually ...', '... try to say it as \"Dyekwee,\" pronouncing the \"D\" almost as if it were followed by a soft \"g\" sound. It\'s kind of hard to explain. If you purchase one of those simple travel language courses that come with a cassette tape, you\'ll know what I mean. A few years back I found one at a bookstore for only $9.95.\r\n\r\nGood luck in the Czech Republic. I envy ya. :-)', '2004-09-20 11:37:44', 1379, 0),
(1427, 13, 'Lorenzo', '', 'Re: Checkmark', 'It should be correctly written \"haček\"... \"C\" with haček by the way ;-)\r\n\r\nP.S. Haček means little hook', '2004-09-20 13:55:55', 1412, 0),
(1428, 13, 'Danielle', '', 'Re: czech words', 'How do you say Peace is possible in czech ', '2004-09-21 22:53:28', 1420, 0),
(1429, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: knedliky', 'Just \"dumplings\" would be better, I think.\r\n\r\nCzech has a tendency to \"diminutize\" everything, and if you translate all the diminutives literally, you tend to end up with something that sounds unnatural in English. This would probably be one of those cases, I believe.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-09-22 21:17:19', 1422, 0),
(1430, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Lhota', '...so the next time someone mentions, say, Červená Lhota to you, you can say \"I really like it over there in Red Tax Vacation\" and confuse the heck out of them. :-))', '2004-09-22 21:19:13', 1424, 0),
(1431, 13, 'jana', '', 'Re: czech words', 'more details pls, not sure what message you\'re trying to convey', '2004-09-22 22:29:35', 1428, 0),
(1432, 13, 'ABartonek', '', 'Re: knedliky', 'white bread dumplings would be my guess..', '2004-09-28 19:24:11', 1418, 0),
(1433, 13, 'Frank', 'FCernik@yahoo.com', 'Re: knedliky', 'Knedliky = Dumplings; Knedlik = Dumpling\r\n\r\nThere are several variations of dumplings, i.e. Bread Dumplings (houskove k.), Potatoe D. (bramborove k.) or Fruit D. (ovocne k.) served with sweat ricotta cheese or fried bread crumbs or even milled poppy seeds and bit of melted unsalted butter. In soups, you can also find \"knedlicky\" (with the accent above \"c\") - i.e. Liver D. (jatrove k.)\r\n', '2004-09-28 19:32:13', 1418, 1),
(1434, 13, 'Boban', 'srpska_nekultura@yahoo.com', 'Re: knedliky', 'dumplings, Czech dumplings, potato dumplings, these should work.', '2004-09-29 22:04:29', 1418, 0),
(1435, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Czech Genealogical Help', 'Attempting to research family history from the town of Mydlovary.  I have been trying to translate passages from a book using a Czech dictionary from the local library.  Need help with the below sentences!!  \r\n\r\nTomu se odecetl jeho prodil 119 kop.\r\nDrive se zde rikalo take Kyfysar.\r\nRoku 1665 pustil Kaspar Pecka tuto zivnost Lukasovi, svemu vlastnimu synovi, za 300 kop grosu.\r\n\r\nAny help would be greatly appreciated.\r\n\r\n', '2004-10-04 20:26:38', 0, 1),
(1436, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'For a map showing Mydlovary\'s location within the country, see:\r\nhttp://mesta.obce.cz/vyhledat.asp?id_org=2621\r\n\r\nThe passages in question read:\r\n\r\nAnd that man\'s share (?), 119 kopas [of gro], was deducted from his something-mentioned-in-the-previous-sentence.\r\nIn the past, this place was also called Kyfysar.\r\nIn the year 1665, Kaspar Pecka passed this business on to Lukas, his own son, for 300 kopas of gro.\r\n\r\nRegarding the kopa and the gro:\r\n\r\nMy source here is http://www.ilcik.cz/download/penize.htm.\r\n\r\nCzech and Moravian minting is divided into four main periods:\r\n\r\n- the dinar period (roughly 950 to 1300)\r\n\r\n- the gro period (1300 to 1520)\r\n\r\n- the tolar period (1520 to 1892)\r\n\r\n- the crown period (1892 to present)\r\n\r\n[...]\r\n\r\n[Greatly paraphrased/\"relevantized\":]\r\n\r\nAlthough I have no such information for the 1600s, the author of the page quoted here gave average prices for the period from 1550 to 1600. (A \"dinar\" in the below means a seventh of a gro.) A pound (presumably - \"libra\") of beef was 4 to 4.5 dinars, a pound of mutton 3.5 to 4 dinars, a really big 10-12 lb. loaf of bread was 6.5 to 7 dinar, a máz (quart and a half, I think*) of wine was 5 to 6 dinars, a vědro (approximately one buttload) of wine was one gold piece, i.e. 30 gro, 1000 smithed nails was 10 gro, a horseshoe was 1-2 gro, a day\'s work in the vineyards brought 2 gro, a day\'s work as unskilled laborer 8-10 gro, a day\'s work as a skilled laborer (carpenter) - 25 dinars. After 1600, prices doubled, and after 1610, they went up a further 50%; wages, however, grew at about half this rate.\r\n\r\nTolar Coinage\r\n\r\nThere were 20 gro to the tolar, but gro were also still sometimes counted in kopas, as they had been in the gro period. It seems to me from the quoted text that the kopa was synonomus with the hřivna kopová, and thus a kopa was 60 gro (assuming the gro customs continued into the tolar period lock, stock, and barrel).\r\n\r\n* All guesses at units of volume are with the help of http://www.converter.cz/j_objem.htm, which gives the relation of various units of volume to one cubic meter.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-05 20:20:39', 1435, 0),
(1437, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Erik\r\n\r\nThanks for the help!!\r\n\r\nCan you help with the following paragraph. It relates to my great great great grandfather. \r\n\r\nRoku 1803 postoupil Jan Pecka zivnost svemu synu Matejovi, ktery v ten cas slouzil u cisarske, \"artilerie\" (=delostrelectva).  Synovi zanechal par kobyl, par volu, 2 kravy, 1 jalovici, 8 ovci, 1 prasnici a naradi v celkove cene 400 zlatych 40 krejcaru - i se statkem pak melo vsechni dohromady cenu 843 zlatych 5 krejcaru.  Matej Pecka se narodil 8. 1. 1770 a ozenil se s Marianou Krejcarovou ze Zbudova.  Pozustala udova Mariana predala roku 1844 zivnost synu Vojtechovi Pechovi za 412 zlatych 25 krejcaru.  Roku 1876 byla vlastnici usedlosti Johanka Peckova, rozena Novakova ze Zahaji.\r\n\r\nMy cousin was able to visit the town of Mydlovary and Zahaji in August of this year.  The mayor of the town gave him the book where these translations mention my ancestors.  Any help that you can give is greatly appreciated since trying to make these translations from different dictionaries is difficult to say the least.\r\n\r\nThanks Again, Tony', '2004-10-07 07:32:19', 1436, 0),
(1438, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Sure. \r\n\r\nIn the year 1803, Jan Pecka passed on his business to his son Matěj, who was at that time serving in the imperial artillery. He left his son a couple of mares, a couple of oxen, 2 cows, 1 heifer, 8 sheep, 1 sow, and tools with a total value of 400 gold pieces and 40 krejcars.  Counted together with the farm, the inherited property had a sum value of 843 gold pieces and 5 krejcars. Matěj Pecka was born on January 8th, 1770, and married Mariana Krejcarová [heh... her last name is from the word krejcar...] from Zbudov. The widow Mariana passed on the business in 1844 to her son Vojtěch Pecka, for 412 gold and 25 krejcars. In 1876, the estate\'s owner was Johanka Pecková, née Nováková, from Záhají [I think those are the accent marks - but then you should know in any case].', '2004-10-07 17:46:41', 1437, 0),
(1439, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Slight correction -- the occurrences of \"couple\" above should read \"pair\" (i.e. a breeding pair).\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-07 17:48:23', 1438, 0),
(1440, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'oh wait, that makes no sense either. Well, whatever.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-07 17:48:54', 1439, 0),
(1441, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Hi Erik\r\n\r\nCan you shead som light on these words for me:  pozustale, prizenil, mela, vyhorela, pustil, kterou and vrchnost.\r\n\r\nThis sentence also has me stumped.  Po nem se teto mydlovarske zivnosti zacalo rikat \"U Pecku\"  \r\n\r\nHere is what I came up with; After___ with this Mydlovar business ___ tell \"___ Pecka\"\r\n\r\nRoku 1663 pro chudobu nesplacel prodily.  In the year 1663 because of poverty _____ sell.\r\n\r\nI dont understand how uvadi is used in this sentence.  1670 uvadi gruntovnice: \"Prijdouc Lukas o sve zdravi, pustil 14. kvetna 1670 grunt Ambrozovi Kovari za 300 kop\"  \r\n\r\nHere is what I have: 1670 introduced the farmers wife: \"Prijdouc Lukas over her health, _____14. _______ 1670 the entire farm to Ambrose Kovari for 300 kopas\"', '2004-10-13 21:23:45', 1440, 1),
(1442, 17, 'Milena Kasic', 'milencekasic@hotmail.com', '', 'Ahoj!\r\nChtela bych trosicku diskutovat o falecnych pratelech (mislim ze to neni spravny pad!!!) Nekdo mi rekl ze nejaky Polak na Ceske Televize rekl neco jako : Ceska Republika pachne zahodem! Chtel rict ze ceska republuka voni Zapadem ale v Polstine pachne znamena voni a zahod zapad!!!!!!   ', '2004-10-14 15:48:45', 0, 0),
(1443, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Words can take on a lot of various endings in Czech, so I\'ve cut off the endings for some of the words you want help on and replaced them with \"-\".\r\n\r\npozůstal- : adjective/noun meaning \"relative of the deceased.\"\r\npřienil: married into.\r\nvyhořel-: burned down (even though it\'s formed from \"out/up\"+\"burned\").\r\npustil-: various meanings, anything from \"let go\" to \"let in\" to \"let out\" to things like \"got into\" or \"took on\" (when used with the word \"se\" -- \"him/herself\")\r\nkter-: that/which.\r\nvrchnost: feudal lord, as far as I can tell. Whoever was the local power-that-be, I guess.\r\n\r\n<b>Po nem se teto mydlovarske zivnosti zacalo rikat \"U Pecku\"</b><br>\r\nOK, actual Czechs reading this will probably laugh at me for one of the interpretations below, but I\'m not sure which of the following this means, without the context:\r\n\"After this [event], this Mydlovary estate [AHA! I should have been calling ivnosti \"estates\" in the previous replies, rather than businesses] began to take on the folk name \"U Pecků.\"\r\n-or-\r\n\"this Mydlovary estate began to take on the name \'U Pecků\' in honor of him.\" (I.e. his name was Mr. Pecka, so it began to take on the folk name \"U Pecků\", that is, \"The Peckas\'.\"\r\n\r\n(I understand your confusion with the word \"říkat,\" whose use here has nothing to do with its usual meaning of \"talk.\" At least not directly. Something-3rd case + se říkat + some name = something has such and such a folk name -- \"it\'s said\" (říká se) that way. Also used for \"you sure can call x a y.\")\r\n\r\n<b>Roku 1663 pro chudobu nesplacel prodily.</b><br>\r\nHeh, you\'re better at this than you think, that is: you got the non-obvious first part right. The second part, well, a Google search for prodíl comes up empty. Maybe it\'s \"podíl\" -- share -- instead? Or maybe the word is real, but just too obscure to be anywhere on the Czech web -- certainly no impossibility.\r\n\r\n<b>1670 uvadi gruntovnice: \"Prijdouc Lukas o sve zdravi, pustil 14. kvetna 1670 grunt Ambrozovi Kovari za 300 kop\"</b><br>\r\nA 1670 entry in the land record book (deed of registers or whatever we call it) states: \"Luká, having lost his good health, has bequeathed [yeah, outdated word - hey, that use of pustit in Czech is outdated too :-)] his <i>grunt</i> -- his <i>Grund</i>, his ground, his land -- to Ambro Kovář for 300 kopas on May 14th, 1670.\"\r\n\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-14 21:17:14', 1441, 0),
(1444, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Hey Erik\r\n\r\nIt appears that your have a good command of the Czech language.  How did you learn the language, are you living in the Czech Republic?  Now that I have confirmed where my ancestors originated, my wife and I are hoping to make a return trip to the Prague and visit the town of Mydlovary where my grandfather was born.  \r\n\r\nMy cousin made the trip this past August and some of the translations you have been helping with are a result of his trip.  My grandfathers house, Mydlovary 13 is still standing however it is no longer in the family.\r\n\r\nI would like to be able to have a working knowlege of the language, what would be a good way to begin.  ', '2004-10-15 20:49:11', 1443, 0),
(1445, 13, 'katka', 'k.knotova@email.cz', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'ahoj eriku, chtěla jsem se vrhnout na překlad těch někdy i pro čecha záludných vět, ale vidim, e není třeba. nečetla jsem sice úplně vechno, nicméně i tak ti musim sloit poklonu. česky teda válí! jak ses to prosimtě naučil? kdyby sis čistě náhodou s něčím nevěděl rady, tak se ozvi a ráda ti poradim. katka', '2004-10-18 03:05:13', 1443, 1),
(1446, 13, 'katka', 'k.knotova@email.cz', 'Re: Checkmark', 'one more  correction, the spelling is háček /pronounced as - my guess of english transcription- haatschek/. háček also means this \" Ą \"diacritic sign above letter \"c\" in this word', '2004-10-18 03:16:17', 1427, 1),
(1447, 13, 'katka', 'k.knotova@email.cz', 'Re: infinitiv, intrumentál', 'tkaáti je podmětem bych teda řekla jen tehdy, kdy bych to chtěla setsakramentsky zdůraznit, např. bzch to někomu vysvětlovala. jinak se běně říká \"...je podmět\"', '2004-10-18 03:28:29', 1396, 1),
(1448, 13, 'katka', '', 'Re: Translation', 'hi, how did you come up with this?: \' is a bottom left to top right accent. do you write it that way? the normal way of writing our čárka is from the top to the bottom.', '2004-10-18 03:44:04', 1361, 1),
(1449, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'I\'m afraid I can\'t provide advice on a good way to begin learning the language well from over in the States. (I live in the CR). Besides formal learning, I read a lot of children\'s books, which isn\'t really an option over there. I think you can find a lot of ideas on this very site, however, including earlier entries on the forum (if you change the settings, you can see pretty far back in time).\r\n\r\nI\'m sorry I can\'t help more.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-18 18:46:41', 1444, 0),
(1450, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Hlavně by mě zajímalo, jestli \"po něm\" v posledním překladu znamenalo \"po události z předchozí, necitované věty\" nebo \"po panu Peckovi\". :-)\r\n\r\nA díky. Jsem tady jedenáct let (tak dlouho?!?), moc by se mi tady bez té četiny ani nelíbilo. :-)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nJéé, zapomněl jsem dát jméno, a pak to nějak ani nezaregistrovalo. Snad to teďka nebude dvakrát...\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-18 19:07:02', 1445, 0),
(1451, 17, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: falené přátelé', 'Vím, e to asi do fora o četině nepatří, ale znám hodně falených přátelech ve směru četina --> angličtina. \r\n\r\nNejzločinnečtějí: actualize, actual (od aktualizovat) -- má to být current, relevant, apod.\r\n\r\nK tomu příběh: jedna mnou příli nemilovaná firma na začátku dějin českého webu měla kromě obvyklých public relations lí jetě \"actual news\" (znamená \"skutečné  zprávy)... čet jsem si to a řek si \"aspoň nějaké skutečné zprávy místo jen lí\"!\r\n\r\nDálí most wanted: \r\n\r\nrealise (aspoň co se američtiny tyče, brittina nevím 100-pro) -- má to být execute, implement, do, nebo vůbec to slovo vynechat (realizovat zařizování \r\norganizace --> organize :-), nebo přinejhorím coordinate the organizing of). AmE znamená \"realize\" jen \"uvědomit se\" a na pár hodně specifických vyjímek, kde to jde pouít \"česky\" (to realize a dream)\r\n\r\ncontrol (aspoň co se američtiny tyče, brittina nevím 100-pro) - má to být check, verify, inspect. \"Control\" znamená \"řidit\".\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-18 19:17:01', 1442, 0),
(1452, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Obě monosti jsou ve hře. Záleí skutečně na kontextu.\r\n\r\nV roce 1666 dolo k poáru. Po něm se ... - t.j. po poáru\r\n\r\nV roce 1666 usedlost koupil p. Pecka. Po něm se ... - t.j. po p. Peckovi\r\n\r\nnebo dokonce\r\n\r\nV roce 1666 dolo k poáru, který zaloil Luká Pecka. Po něm se ... - nejednoznačné :-)\r\n\r\nBTW, pochybuji, e v roce 1670 bylo správně: Přijdouc Luká o své zdraví, pustil ...\r\n\r\nSprávně je (a snad i bylo v r. 1670 - a na pravopis): Přied o své zdraví, Luká pustil ...\r\n\r\nnebo Kdy byl přiel o své zdraví (= plusquamperf.), Luká pustil ...', '2004-10-18 20:38:15', 1450, 0),
(1453, 13, 'katka', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', ' troufám si říct, e s téměř stoprocentní pravděpodobností jde o význam, e se ivnosti říkalo u Pecků po tom Peckovi. chalupy menily jména podle majitelů dost často. koneckonců, i kdyby to bylo po poáru, pro přejmenování ivnosti musel být jiný důvod ne samotný poár.\r\n', '2004-10-19 00:14:51', 1452, 1),
(1454, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Here is another line from the archives.\r\n\r\nPozdejsi pisari pripisovali do berni ruly poznamky a z nich se muzeme napr. dozvedet, kdy ktera hospodarstvi vyhorela:\r\n\r\n-- roku 1671 vyhorely usedlosti:  Pecka, Kaplan, Kocian\r\n\r\n-- roku 1709 vyhorela temer cela ves; vyhorely usedlosti:  Erhart, Stefl, Tesar, Hasek, Ondok, Krejci, Mochtl, Pecka, Kaplan, Kocian, Klikar', '2004-10-20 03:00:56', 1449, 1),
(1455, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Nejde o to, po kom a proč se nazývá cosi \"U Pecků\", ale o to, jak přeloit výraz \"po něm\". Erik správně poznamenal, e bez znalosti předchozí věty není překlad do angličtiny jednoznačný. Zájmeno \"něm\" můe zastupovat cokoliv muského nebo středního rodu, pokud je to zmíněno v předchozí větě (tu ale neznáme!). Já jsem pouze spekuloval, jak by mohla předchozí věta vypadat, aby to mělo vliv na překlad.', '2004-10-20 17:46:08', 1453, 0),
(1456, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', '> BTW, pochybuji, e v roce 1670 bylo správně: Přijdouc Luká o své zdraví, pustil ... \r\n\r\n...a jsi si jistá, e tenkrát jetě nebyli úředníci-katové četiny? :-))\r\n\r\nerik', '2004-10-20 17:47:13', 1452, 0),
(1457, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'First off: in the Czech blah blah that\'s been piling up, we\'ve been discussing whether the estate named \"The Peckas\'\" received its name \"after Mr. Pecka\" or \"after some event.\" Verdict: it really is ambiguous without knowledge of the preceding sentence.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nRegarding the latest translation:\r\n\r\nLater scribes added comments to the tax records [literally \"taxroll\"], and from these we can learn, for example, which estates burned down, and when:\r\n\r\n-- in 1671, these estates burned down: Pecka, Kaplan, Kocián\r\n\r\n-- in 1709, nearly the entirely village burned down. Specifically, these estates burned down: Erhart, Stefl, Tesar, Hasek, Ondok, Krejci, Mochtl, Pecka, Kaplan, Kocian, Klikar. [Ouch!]\r\n\r\nErik\r\n', '2004-10-20 17:58:40', 1454, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1458, 13, 'stelingo', 'stelingouk@aol.com', 'nastavany', 'Can anybody tell me what the word \'nastavany\' means please? Can\'t find it in my dictionary.', '2004-10-22 03:24:44', 0, 1),
(1459, 13, 'stelingo', 'stelingouk@aol.com', 'Re: zapředenou', '\"Petr je učitelem.\" (Petr is a teacher.) \r\n\r\nVladimir, you say the instrumental is necessary in this type of sentence but I have seen/heard many examples where the nom is used, including my grammar books i.e. \'Petr je učitel\'', '2004-10-22 03:31:30', 1393, 1),
(1460, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: nastavany', 'A czech will probably correct me here, but it seems to me it\'s most likely a typo,  and the word should have been\r\n\r\nnastavený\r\n\r\nor\r\n\r\nnatvaný.\r\n\r\nIs the word also accentless in the text you have? If it\'s not, then it will be fairly easy to guess which typo is there, based on there being a \"\" (then it\'s natvaný) or a \"s\" (then it\'s nastavený).', '2004-10-22 15:41:15', 1458, 0),
(1461, 17, 'Milan', 'schonderg@aol.com', 'Re: Komicka Hra an Slova v Ruznych Jazycich', 'Ahoj Mileno,\r\nRad jsem cetl Vas vtipnou slovni hru. Moc jsem se ji zasmal protoze me se strasne libi tato forma humoru. Mam take par znamych Polaku a ten Vas vtip se jim take moc libil. Lidi kteri mlvi vic cizich jazyku maji lepsi pojem o zivote.\r\nDovolet abych se trchu predstavil, ja jsem se narodil v Cechach a emigroval jsem s rodici do spojenych Statu v roce 1966. Rodice pred par lety zemreli a ted Cesky troch zapominam coz nemam s kym mluvit.\r\nVase vyjadreni \"o falecnych pratelech\" je ve spravnem padu ale ja myslim ze se to rika \"o falesnych pratelich\" a to s ve falesnych je jako anglicke \"sh\" (nemame hacky na psaci klavesnici.\r\nJa jsem ted akorat poznal ze vetsina mych \"pratel\" byly falesni protoze jsem se akorat rozvedl z manzelkou a tak jem tim stratil vetsinu pratelu a tak hledam nove. \r\nZdelte mi prosim od kud Jste a ktere jazycky mluvite.\r\nJa mluvim Anlicky, Madarky a Rusky a trochu Hebrejsy a Yiddish.\r\nS Pozdravem, \r\nMilan.', '2004-10-28 01:21:17', 1442, 1),
(1462, 17, 'Milan S', 'schonderg@aol.com', 'Re: Můj oblíbený český film', 'Souhlasim, ze Obchod na Kurze je krasny klasicky film. Moje oblibene filmy jsou filmy komicke direktora Jiriho Weisse jako Murder Czech Style (nevzpominam si jak se to jmenuje cesky) a novejsi filmy Jiriho Menzla jako Vesnicko Ma Strediskova oba s Rudolfem Hrusinskym.', '2004-10-28 02:15:55', 444, 1),
(1463, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@sbcglobal.net', 'History', 'Erik\r\n\r\nJust changed my computer system and upgraded to a dsl service, so my email address has aslo changed.\r\n\r\nI need somemore help with this sentence:  Opis schwarqenberskeho sumare, porizeneho roku 1685 za casu knizete Ferdinanda Arnosta ze Schwarzenbergu a hejtmana Eliase Frantiska z Heidlbeku pisarem Ondrejem Schank - Frankstatterem.\r\n\r\nAlso, I cant find any reference to the work neslo in any of my dictionaries, the \"s\" has a u shaped mark above it.\r\n\r\nTony', '2004-10-30 03:16:37', 0, 0),
(1464, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: History', '> Just changed my computer system and upgraded to a dsl service, so my email address has aslo changed.\r\n\r\nNo problem. Above all it\'s a good thing that we\'ve started a new thread, as the old one was stretching most of the way across the screen. :-)\r\n\r\n> Opis schwarqenberskeho sumare, porizeneho roku 1685 za casu knizete Ferdinanda Arnosta ze Schwarzenbergu a hejtmana Eliase Frantiska z Heidlbeku pisarem Ondrejem Schank - Frankstatterem.\r\n\r\nTranscription of the Schwarqenberg [more likely actually Schwarzenberg] Summary [??], prepared in 1865 in the time of Count* Ferdinand Arnot of Schwarzenberg and Marshall Eliá Frantiek of Heidlbek [Heidelberg?] by the scribe Ondřej Schank-Frankstatter.\r\n\r\n* kníe is often translated as prince, but overfrequently translating kníe as prince seems to be a Czechism, and this guy being a prince doesn\'t make much sense to me.\r\n\r\n> Also, I cant find any reference to the work neslo in any of my dictionaries, the \"s\" has a u shaped mark above it.\r\n\r\nIt\'s no wonder, since it\'s very different from the infinitive, and of course the infinitives are used as headwords. It\'s the negative past tense of \"jít\", to go. Note that just like the English \"go,\" it\'s used in a lot of idioms, so its meaning in a given sentence may not actually be \"did not go.\" For example, \"nelo to obnovit\" means \"it-was-not-possible it to-renew,\" that is, \"it [or that] could not be renewed [or restored].\"\r\n\r\nCzech is a lot like English (and French, and probably most languages) in that the more fundamental a word is, the more likely it is to be quite irregular, grammar-wise... unfortunately. Usage and words are a bit like water and rocks... the former in large quantities tends to deform the latter over time. (Sometimes it\'s actually a case of a much-used word keeping an old grammar structure that dies out for the rest of the language, but the end result for the poor learner is the same.)\r\n\r\nI remember being quite frustrated by this when I was learning Czech since, unlike the case with French, I had only limited formal education in the language.\r\n\r\nA common trick of mine when I can\'t find a word in the dictionaries is to do a google search for:\r\n\r\n[the word in question, without these square brackets] \"English version\" [with the quotes]\r\n\r\nIf I\'m lucky, this pulls up a page or two twinned with an easily accessible English version of itself, and I can see how the word tends to be translated (though given the sorry state of the field of translation in the CR, this sometimes needs to be taken with a grain of salt). \r\n\r\nYou can sometimes also get some good results from the volny.cz dictionary. I can\'t seem to create a link that successfully leads straight to it, so:\r\n\r\n- visit web.volny.cz\r\n- click the \"slovník\" tab in the group of tabs towards the top.\r\n\r\nThis is actually just Volny\'s branding of the Millenium dictionary, which, though short on quality (and allegedly ethics, as much of it is allegedly scan-stolen), is long on content. Naturally, it also uses infinitives and nominative singular as headwords, which is a pain for learners, but it includes a lot of phrases, and sometimes you\'ll luck out and one of them will contain the conjugation or declension, respectively, that you\'re working with.\r\n\r\nYou can also find many resources and links to resources in other parts of this website (Bohemica), of course!\r\n\r\nHope that helps!\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-11-01 16:18:46', 1463, 0),
(1465, 13, 'SwierQ', 'swierszcz@irc.pl', 'Translation! PLS Help', 'I nedd a Birthday grettings in Czech language. If somebody could write for me short grettings i will be plesed. my mail is swierszcz@irc.pl . Pls Help its very important for me!', '2004-11-04 19:45:18', 0, 0),
(1466, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@sbcglobal.net', 'Re: History', 'I could use some help with this one!!\r\n\r\nRoku 1565 utratil hlubocky purkrabi a pisar Tomas ve Zbudove 12 grosu, kdyz jednali u tamniho rychtare ohledne sirotcich zalezetosti.\r\n\r\nHere is something that my cousin and I werent sure about.  When did the Communist Party come into power in the CR and did it have any effect on the small family farms in areas such as Mydlovary?  \r\n\r\nThanks, Tony', '2004-11-06 03:43:02', 1464, 1),
(1467, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: History', '> Roku 1565 utratil hlubocky purkrabi a pisar Tomas ve Zbudove 12 grosu, kdyz jednali u tamniho rychtare ohledne sirotcich zalezetosti.\r\n\r\nIn 1565, the proconsul/burgrave[1] and scribe Tomá in Zbudov spent 12 gro when they were negotiating with that town\'s reeve/viscount [2] on matters related to orphans.\r\n\r\n[1] These are the two translations I found for this word. Considering I really have no idea what a proconsul or burgrave actually *are*, I suppose it\'s all the same...\r\n\r\n[2] Ditto.\r\n\r\n> Here is something that my cousin and I werent sure about. \r\n\r\n> When did the Communist Party come into power in the CR...\r\n\r\nFebruary 1948 (\"Red February,\" \"Victorious February\").\r\n\r\n> And did it have any effect on the small family farms in areas such as Mydlovary?\r\n\r\nGenerally, they ceased to exist. During collectivization, nearly everyone added their farmland to the cooperatives (JZDs), whether voluntarily, via confiscation, or simply under threat.\r\n\r\nHere\'s a nice introduction to that period, in English:\r\n\r\nhttp://archiv.radio.cz/history/history13.html\r\n\r\nAnd here\'s a bit more on collectivization specifically, thanks to totalita.cz (\"totalitarian-state.cz\").\r\n\r\nStage One - 1949\r\n\r\nOn Feb. 23rd, the Communist-controlled parliament accepted, in accordance with a resolution of the ÚV KSČ [the Central Committee of the Czechoslovak Communist Party -- the Communists were VERY acronym-happy, and had to be, frankly, because everything had a bureaucratic, overlong name], the Act on Unified Agricultural Cooperatives (JZDs).\r\n\r\n[That is: after a year of mucking up more important things, the Communist leaders got around to mucking up agriculture. They did so via the usual path: they wrote a resolution, and Parliament rubber-stamped it into law.]\r\n\r\nThe campaign promoting creation of JZDs was launched quickly: in April. The campaign did not, however, meet with a positive response, and did not bring the expected results. At the end of 1949, only 2,039 JZDs existed, and these were generally Type I. [Dunno what the differences between the types was, but I guess we can assume that Type I was a weak type.]\r\n\r\nNeedless to say, the JZDs\' chairmen were installed on the basis of decisions by the district committees of the KSČ.\r\n\r\n[On the totalita site, the text is interrupted here by a nice, or rather \"nice,\" photo of two poor souls being tried as saboteurs of the national economy for the felony of \"grain feeding\"; my hunch is that they fed their livestock with their grain rather than giving it up to the JZD, but really God only knows.]\r\n\r\n\r\nStage Two (1950)\r\n\r\nIn this year, it was ordered that all JZD\'s be transformed into Types 3 and 4. This met with great resistance, whose outcome was an attempt at mass exit from the JZDs.\r\n\r\nThe Communists responded with the \"buying up\" [\"výkup\" -- quotes are authors\'] of agricultural equipment. The majority of machines was \"vykoupena\", but with no economic effect. The machines were immediately taken out of operation and sent to the scrap piles. The [JZDs\'] machinery stations* did not utilize them.\r\n\r\n\r\nStage Three (1952)\r\n\r\nOn June 3rd, 1952, a resolution of \"the party and the cabinet\" [as the phrase of the era went] was issued regarding further development of JZDs. What came next was quite simply a head-on attack on villages and their inhabitants. The attack was led in each district by a three- to four-member committee that was an attache of the district committee [OV] of the Communist Party.\r\n\r\nBrigades of functionaries rode out to rural areas, visited farmers, and \"convinced\" them to join the JZDs. The methods used for convincing became ever harsher. Most of the new JZDs were formed against the farmers\' will and in the face of farmers\' resistance.\r\n\r\n\r\n* Machinery Stations\r\n\r\nState organization newly founded in 1948, which owned agricultural machinery. Shipments of machines from these stations for the private sector did not exist.\r\n\r\n...Looking back at totalita (as I have been translating using a copy of the text that I pasted into a text editor), I see it offers a few of the definitions I regretted not having above (like the various Types). But this is already a bit long for an unsolicited explanation. :-)\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-11-08 16:28:14', 1466, 0),
(1468, 13, 'Clara', 'cvance75@aol.com', 'Translation needed Czech to English Kubicek Grave stone', 'I have been reseaching my family history and found the grave stone of my Great Grandparents Terezie and Josef Kubicek it is a two sided stone and on Tereze side it says ZDE ODPO CIVA Tereze Kubicek  ROZENA V ROKU 1855 ZEMRELA V ROKU 1901. Also there is a small stone that says MATKA. Can anyone can translate these words to English Please..Thank You,Clara', '2004-11-08 23:03:33', 0, 1),
(1469, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation needed Czech to English Kubicek Grave stone', 'Hello Clara,\r\n\r\nSure. \r\n\r\nThe inscription on the main stone reads:\r\n\r\nHERE RESTS Tereza Kubíček BORN 1855 DIED 1901.\r\n\r\nThe small stone reads:\r\nMOTHER\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-11-09 15:15:11', 1468, 0),
(1470, 13, 'Clara', 'cvance75@aol.com', '', 'Erik, Thank you !!!Clara', '2004-11-09 21:24:26', 0, 1),
(1471, 17, 'Jan Vaněk jr.', 'jan . vanek . jr @ gmail', 'Re: falené přátelé', 'Naopak je jich taky spousta: eventually -> eventuálně, nepříjemně se rozmáhá pathetic -> patetický (ve smyslu ubohý, opovreníhodný) a na spoustu dalích si teď samozřejmě nevzpomenu, i kdy si na ně naráím v jednom kuse.\r\n\r\nV roce 1990 a pak 1996 vyla celá kníka Zrádná slova v angličtině (Josef Hladký, SPN); o Sparlingově English or Czenglish? ani nemluvě. A koukám, e existují i Zrádná slova v poltině a četině.', '2004-11-25 19:31:26', 1451, 0),
(1472, 17, 'milan', 'mynamemilan@seznam.cz', '', 'Hello everybody\r\nI don\'t know if you could help me but I told myself I could try it.My name is Milan and at the moment I am living in a nice city Bournemouth which is in the south of England.I seem to stay here for a longer time so I was wondering if there was any Englishman I could teach Czech.If you knew about anyone please let me know and write.Thanks a lot.\r\nMilan', '2004-12-04 16:45:51', 0, 0),
(1473, 13, 'Arzuv', 'famousasia@yahoo.com', 'I am looking for someone to study czech with', 'Dear all,\r\n\r\nI would like to invite anyone who would be interested in studying with me czech. I am planning on starting as soon as possible, the only problem is that there aren\'t any courses starting at this time and only after two months it is too late for me. \r\n\r\nThank you very much in advance!\r\n', '2004-12-14 19:38:47', 0, 0),
(1474, 17, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: falené přátelé', 'Jé, dlouho jsem v této části sajtu nebyl, take toto mi ulo. Napadl mě jetě sympathetic -] sympatický -- dalí případ, kde přinejmením DE, CZ, NL a FR jsou zajedno, jen angličtina ne. Mimochodem často naráím na rozhovory Čechů a dalích Evropanů, kterým bych nerozuměl nebýt zkueností s jinými evropskými jazyky ne angličtina.\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\nP.S. Sparling je bůh, v ideálním světě by jeho kníka byla zásadní součastí naprosto kadého kurzu angličtiny v Česku.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-12-20 21:27:10', 1471, 0),
(1475, 13, 'Lada', 'lada_moskva@rambler.ru', 'question about past tense', 'Ahoj přátelé!\r\n\r\ncould somebody help me?\r\nI know the rule how to make past tense, for example one must say \"byli jsme doma\", but at the same time i saw such sentence:\r\nvčera byla děvčata v divadle , so my question is where is \"jsou\"? and are both variants with \"jsou\" and without it right?\r\n\r\nDěkuji.', '2004-12-29 21:16:00', 0, 0),
(1476, 13, '&#51060;&#47492;&#50630;&#51020;', '', 'Please recommend...', 'I want learn Czech more...\r\nPlease recommend some FREE sites...\r\n(Because here is little bit...--)', '2005-01-02 14:29:56', 0, 0),
(1477, 13, 'Jeremy', 'jking@fst.zcu.cz', 'degree equivalents', 'I\'m trying to find equivalents for Czech and English university degrees.It\'s starting to get a bit tricky! Does anyone know if a list exists? ', '2005-01-03 16:00:09', 0, 1),
(1478, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question about past tense', '> where is \"jsou\"?\r\n\r\nFor 3rd person, the helper verb is unnecessary (and even forbidden) for forming the past tense.\r\n\r\n> and are both variants with \"jsou\" and without it right? \r\n\r\nOnly without.\r\n\r\nIncidentally, you\'ll occasionally also see this form:\r\n\r\nJá byl nemocný.\r\n\r\nLeaving out the helper verb when you leave in the personal pronoun like this is also permissible, although rare. I wouldn\'t recommend you use it yourself, however, as it often sounds strange, and I can\'t say exactly when that \"often\" is.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-03 17:37:36', 1475, 0),
(1479, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nI don\'t, but perhaps I can recommend some equivalents for particular words if you mention some?\r\n\r\nKeep in mind that not only are Czech and British degrees different, but so are British and American, so it\'s a real mess...\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-03 17:39:59', 1477, 0),
(1480, 13, 'Lada', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Děkuji Erik!\r\nCould you(or someone else) please answer some more questions though not about past tense?\r\n\r\nwhat is the difference between rudý a červený? why do we say Rudé naměstí ALE červené víno?\r\n\r\nand what is the difference between anebo a nebo?\r\n\r\nThank you in advance.', '2005-01-03 22:00:29', 1478, 0),
(1481, 13, 'Jeremy', 'jking@zcu.fst.cz', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Hi Erik\r\nI know Ing is similar to a master\'s and Bc is similar to a bachelor\'s degree (please correct me if I\'m wrong!), butwhat about Mgr,  MUDr,  JUDr, CSc  and Doc. ? I\'m looking for British English equivalents if possible...Thanks very much for your interest\r\nJeremy', '2005-01-04 17:43:47', 1479, 1),
(1482, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Unlike in English, the color associated with Communism in Czech (and perhaps not only in Czech) is not red, but scarlet... perhaps because it is closer to the color of blood (the blood shed by the struggling workers, of course). \"Rudý\" means scarlet. (Social democracy, meanwhile, is associated with the color pink. Of course -- we all know they\'re just pinkos in disguise...)\r\n\r\n> and what is the difference between anebo and a nebo? \r\n\r\nUhhh... good question. Well, I see that anebo gets 857,000 hits in Google versus only 490,000 for a nebo. My feeling is that a nebo is closer to the English \"and/or,\" whereas anebo is closer in meaning to the English phrase \"or perhaps.\"\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-04 21:41:25', 1480, 0),
(1483, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'I\'m an American, so I can\'t give reliable British terms. Perhaps you\'ll be helped at least a little from this viewpoint. of an American immigrant to the CR.\r\n\r\nIng. is like a master\'s degree for a <i>technical</i> field, whereas Mgr. is like a master\'s degree for an <i>arts/humanities</i> field.\r\n\r\nI too would compare Bc to our Bachelor\'s.\r\n\r\nJUDr. is a law doctorate, and MUDr. a medical doctorate.\r\n\r\nDoc. I\'m not sure, though I do know it\'s something that can be in addition to the ordinary degrees. It might stand for Docent, which is, uh, docent. Whatever that is. (Czechs? Please help...?) \r\n\r\nCSc... crap, I don\'t know. Aha, here\'s some fellow being a blowhard and going on about it in the discussion forum on President Klaus\'s site a while back:\r\n\r\n[...] CSc [means] Candidatus Scientiarum, and it\'s the former equivalent of today\'s PhD. \r\n\r\n(Do keep in mind that information you acquire for free on the Internet is worth almost as much as you paid for it.)\r\n\r\nOne interesting side note -- from an American\'s perspective, the Czechs are absolute degree nuts. If a fellow is a MUDr. Doc Whatchamacallit, he makes double extra sure to include all the titles every time he mentions his names and heads off to mudrovat (to talk like a blowhard) :-) Case in point: a search for all five abbreviations \"bc csc judr mudr doc\" (without the quotes) will bring up an awe-inspiring 8,520 hits on Google.', '2005-01-04 21:56:33', 1481, 0),
(1484, 13, 'Lada', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Thank you for your answers!\r\nTo tell the truth i am surprised that its scarlet colour which is associated with revolution but Red squere (in Moscow) doesnt deal with it anyhow, i am a bit confused...\r\nalso as i am not native english speaker, i dont know who are pinkos...\r\n\r\nhere\'s another question:)\r\nif as you said \"byla jsem doma\" is wrong, but if we speak about things? you know in my book there s a rule that we must use always \"jsem, jsi, je, etc.\" in the past.\r\nAhoj!\r\n\r\n', '2005-01-04 22:11:10', 1482, 0),
(1485, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'A. Graduates (alumni): 1. Bc.(bachelor) 2. Mgr.(magister=master) or Ing.(ingenieur) 3. Dr.(doctor)\r\n\r\nIng. - technical universities, e.g. ČVUT, VCHT (chem. technology)\r\nMgr. - other universities, e.g. Charles University, not only for arts/humanities field, also mat./phys., biology, astronomy, pharmacy, etc.\r\n\r\nDr. is usually specified:\r\nRTDr. rerum technicarum doctor (very rare)\r\nRNDr. rerum naturalium doctor - mat. phys. biology\r\nPhDr.(philosophy), JUDr., MUDr., MVDr., PaedDr.(paedagogy), etc.\r\n\r\nB. university teachers: 1. (assistent) 2. Doc.(docent) 3. Prof.(professor)\r\n\r\nC. scientists: 1. -- 2. CSc.(candidatus scientiarum) 3. DrSc.(doctor scientiarum)\r\n\r\nYou can read:\r\n\r\nDoc. Ing. XY, CSc.\r\nProf. MUDr. XY, DrSc.', '2005-01-05 14:12:26', 1483, 0),
(1486, 13, 'Jeremy', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'You\'re a gent and a scholar!', '2005-01-05 14:47:32', 1483, 1),
(1487, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Erik was right. CSc. is now Ph.D. (philosophiae doctor)\r\n\r\nIn Czech:\r\n\r\nBc. - bakalář \r\nBcA. - bakalář umění (... of arts)\r\n\r\nMgr. - magistr\r\nMgA. - magistr umění (... of arts)\r\n\r\nJUDr. - doktor práv (z lat. juris utriusque doctor)\r\nMUDr. - doktor medicíny (z lat. medicinae universae doctor)\r\nMVDr. - doktor veterinární medicíny (z lat. medicinae veterinariae doctor)\r\nPaedDr. - doktor pedagogiky (z lat. paedagogiae doctor)\r\nPhMr. - magistr farmacie - dřívějí titul (z lat. pharmaciae magister)\r\nPharmDr. - doktor farmacie\r\nPhDr. - doktor filozofie (z lat. philosophiae doctor)\r\nRNDr. - doktor přírodních věd (z lat. rerum naturalium doctor)\r\nRSDr. - doktor sociálních věd\r\nThLic. - licenciát teologie\r\nThDr. - doktor teologie\r\n\r\nIng. - inenýr\r\nIng. arch. - inenýr architekt\r\n\r\nakad. mal. - akademický malíř (painter)\r\nakad. soch. - akademický sochař (sculptor)\r\nakad. arch. - akademický architekt\r\n\r\nMBA - Master of Business Administration - titul získaný po absolvování specializovaného postgraduálního vzdělání manaerského\r\nEuroinenýr - titul mohou získat po dvouleté praxi absolventi 21 studijních oborů Vysokého učení technického v Brně\r\n\r\nPo roce 1990 dolo ke změně v získávání titulů:\r\n\r\nIng., MUDr. a MVDr. - praxe přidělování titulů stejná, získává se bez rigorózního řízení, tedy automaticky po absolvování přísluné vysoké koly\r\nMgr. - automaticky získávají absolventi univerzitních, uměleckých a teologických fakult\r\nDr. - je moné získat pouze po absolvování tříletého postgraduálního studia\r\n\r\nVědecké hodnosti - uvádějí se za jménem:\r\nCSc. - kandidát věd (z lat. candidatus scientiarum)\r\nDrSc. - doktor věd (z lat. doctor scientiarum)\r\n(tituly CSc. a DrSc. se od 1. 7.1998 neudělují)\r\n\r\nDr. - doktor - se píe za jménem a nahrazuje i tituly kandidát věd a doktor věd (CSc., DrSc.).\r\nPh.D. - titul Ph.D. se uvádí za jménem, např.: Ing. Jan Novák, Ph.D. (pozn.: \"Ph.D.\" psáno bez mezery)\r\n- byl převzat z anglosaských zemí\r\n- doslovný překlad je doktor filozofie\r\n- uděluje se absolventům studia v doktorských studijních programech\r\n- je srovnatelný s dřívějím titulem kandidát věd - CSc. \r\nTh.D. - doktor - v oblasti teologie\r\n\r\nZkratky vědecko-pedagogické hodnosti profesor a docent se píí s počátečním písmenem malým (prof., doc.).\r\n\r\nDr.h.c. - doctor honoris causa - čestný doktor - titul udělovaný bez zkouek za vynikající zásluhy o vědu, umění, stát apod.\r\n\r\nDiS. - diplomovaný specialista v oboru, uvádí se za jménem - získává ho obvykle absolvent vyí odborné koly po studiu\r\nzakončeném absolutoriem (obhájení diplomové práce)\r\n', '2005-01-05 19:16:25', 1486, 0),
(1488, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Rudý is nearly the same colour as červený, but rudý is more pathetic and elevated. So we have: Rudý říjen (Red October), Rudá armáda (Red Army), rudá hvězda (red star), rudý prapor (red flag), Rudé právo (newspaper, now only Právo), but also Rudé moře (Red Sea), Rudé náměstí, rudá skvrna (red sunspot), rozhavený do ruda (red-hot), etc. Mostly it is rather cliché than an exact colour determination.', '2005-01-05 20:33:13', 1484, 0),
(1489, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'We must use \"jsem, jsi, jsme, jste\" in the past tense, but \"jest, jsou\" (3rd person sing./plur.) is archaic, e.g. \"vstal jest z mrtvých\", \"přili jsou z východu\", etc. Now: \"vstal z mrtvých\", \"přili z východu\".\r\n\r\nPast tense \"nésti\":\r\n\r\n1. nesl (nesla, neslo) jsem\r\n2. nesl (nesla, neslo) jsi\r\n3. nesl (nesla, neslo)\r\n\r\n1. nesli (nesly, nesla) jsme\r\n2. nesli (nesly, nesla) jste\r\n3. nesli (nesly, nesla)\r\n\r\n\"nesl jest\" and \"nesli jsou\" is archaic\r\n', '2005-01-05 21:26:30', 1484, 0),
(1490, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Y\'know, I dunno. To me, the color (I mean, the visual color) used in association with Communism in the CR is scarlet, not red.\r\n\r\nOff-topic... \"Pathetic\" here is a Czechism, a false cognate. We would say \"bombastic\" or one of its synonyms.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-06 00:56:56', 1488, 0),
(1491, 13, 'Jeremy', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Wow! Marvellous, Vladimir. Thank you!', '2005-01-06 13:33:38', 1487, 0),
(1492, 13, 'Lada', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Thank you Vladimír for such detailed explanation:)\r\n\r\nDoes anybody know is there a list for short czech names (i mean like Julek for Julius)\r\n\r\nDěkují', '2005-01-06 23:08:13', 1489, 0),
(1493, 14, 'Daphne', '', 'Re: Czech national symbols (flag)', 'Maybe...\r\nIt\'s can be from Moravian symbol...\r\nThe Moravian eagle is red and white like chess board...\r\nSorry about my bad grammar...', '2005-01-07 11:42:48', 298, 0),
(1494, 13, 'Amit', 'amit_chawla_1@rediffmail.com', 'Learning Czech Language through Internet', 'Hi Forum:\r\nGreetings from India!\r\n\r\nI am interested to learn Czech language through Internet. Please let me know some free websites.I am beginner and starting from scratch. I really thanks to Miss Marie who provided me information to join this forum.\r\n\r\nAn early reply will be appreciated!\r\n\r\nThanks and Regards\r\nAmit Chawla\r\n_________________________________________________________________________\r\nAmit Chawla\r\nEmail:amit_chawla_1@rediffmail.com\r\n', '2005-01-07 13:30:57', 0, 1),
(1495, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'www.vestirna.com, click on JMÉNA', '2005-01-07 14:42:00', 1492, 0),
(1496, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'January', 'January...\r\nLeden or Ledna?', '2005-01-07 18:39:45', 0, 0),
(1497, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: January', 'Leden. Or rather, leden. (It\'s not capitalized like in English.)\r\n\r\nLedna is the second grammatical case, the genitive. \r\n\r\nNominative: Leden je krásný.\r\nGenitive: Od ledna budu tady.\r\nDative: Some cute sentence with \"to January\" (k lednu) goes here.\r\nAccusative: Vybrali leden.\r\nVocative: Ó leden, jak tě miluji! :-)\r\nLocative: Přiel v lednu.\r\nInstrumentál: S lednem přicházejí největí zimní deprese.\r\n\r\nOK, Vladimir can now start castigating me for my horrible Czech. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-07 20:34:48', 1496, 0),
(1498, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: January', 'OK, I must \"castigare et punire\".\r\n\r\nThe vocative is \"ledne\"!\r\n------------------------\r\n', '2005-01-07 21:25:04', 1497, 0),
(1499, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'January', 'January...\r\nLeden or Ledna?', '2005-01-07 22:14:33', 0, 0),
(1500, 14, 'Lada', '', 'czech names', 'Hello!\r\nI\'d like to know what names are the most popular? i was shoked when i knew that there are so many german names v četině ...are they also popular? and do people really give to children such names like Taňa, Nad´a while the full names are Tat´jana a Naděda.\r\n', '2005-01-10 22:58:00', 0, 0),
(1501, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'I have a question...', 'What is\r\n\'May I beg your pardon?\'\r\nin czech?\r\n(It is not Real begging^.^)', '2005-01-11 19:27:41', 0, 0),
(1502, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'Re: January', 'Oops...I Sended twice...\r\n-_-a', '2005-01-11 19:33:51', 1499, 0),
(1503, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: I have a question...', 'Its \"Prosím?\".\r\n\r\nSeriously. Just say the word with the same kind of voice you use when saying \"May I beg your pardon?\".\r\n\r\nThe word \"Pardon\" does exist in Czech. But it\'s only used when you something like bump into somebody or misspeak (\"Pardon, myslela jsem Honzu, néé Láďu\").\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-11 20:42:38', 1501, 0),
(1504, 13, 'Bernard Lock', 'bernard@brancaster.org.uk', 'Use of Já', 'I should like help please to understand the use of \"Já\" in the title of a book of Czech folk songs and carols, ŤJá, pínickať (Petr Jánský, 1994).  Am I right in thinking that this is not the normal use of \"Já\"? ', '2005-01-11 23:45:25', 0, 1),
(1505, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Use of Já', 'What is the \"normal\" use of a word? \"Já, písnička\" means \"I, song\". It is similar to the title \"I, Claudius\" (Robert Graves\' novel).', '2005-01-12 13:10:37', 1504, 0),
(1506, 13, 'Bernard Lock', 'bernard@brancaster.org.uk', 'Re: Use of Já', 'To Vladimír\r\n\r\n    thank you for your help.  I tried that meaning at first, but wasn\'t really prepared for such a \'living\' personification of song.  I shall now put my trust in the language much more.\r\n\r\n    Thank you too for the website.  It is excellent.\r\n\r\n    Bernard\r\n', '2005-01-12 17:29:54', 1505, 0),
(1507, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Use of Já', 'A little off-topic; I\'m bored:\r\n\r\nYou\'re doing well to \"study\" Já, písnička, as it\'s got a firm place in the Czech culture and in the very Czech (if gradually dying) tradition of \"tramping\" and of campfire singing. You will be able to build many bridges to the hearts of Czechs with the songs in that book.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-12 20:29:16', 1506, 0),
(1508, 15, 'love', 'nonamewebm@yahoo.fr', 'Zuzana', '\r\nplease help me \r\n\r\nhttp://pageperso.aol.fr/Tpetit224/\r\n\r\n', '2005-01-15 21:07:39', 0, 1),
(1509, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: czech names', 'Jan is probably the number one male name, and Jana might well be the number one female name. As to \"nicknamish\" official first names... (like, say, Láďa instead of Ladislav ;-)) apparently yes, these do happen. In any case, in day to day conversation, I\'d say you\'re even less likely to hear a Tajana being called Tajana instead of Táňa in the CR than you are to hear a Samuel being called Samuel instead of Sam in the US.\r\n\r\nFor many, many years, the fates of the Czech lands and what is now Austria and, to a smaller extent, what is now Germany were intertwined. This had to have an effect on naming, even on first names, and it did. Above all, you\'ll find people named Jan but called Honza (from Hans) a lot more often than you\'ll find those called Jenda.\r\n\r\nI checked the Internet for a page with information on the most popular first names, but alas. With only 10 million Czechs in the world, none have yet had the charity to provide such information on the Web, especially not for free. I did find one interesting-looking site:\r\n\r\nhttp://bimbo.fjfi.cvut.cz/~karpisek/kalendar/data_cz/prehledy/kapjmena.php?pristup=\r\n\r\n...but, since it is registration-only (although registration is, mind you, free), I can\'t say more.\r\n\r\n', '2005-01-17 16:45:59', 1500, 0),
(1510, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: I have a question...', 'I\'m not sure this \"may I beg your pardon\" is not more along the lines of \"nezlobte se\"? If it is meant more as I\'m sorry to trouble you.', '2005-01-18 06:14:38', 1501, 0),
(1511, 15, 'June', 'jechante17@yahoo.com', 'Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'Has any of you attended the Czech language summer school at Masaryk University in Brno? I am interested in knowing (1) How good the teachers are (2) What is the housing like, and is it far from the school where the classes are held (3) Is there a mini-refrigerator in each dorm or hotel room (4) Is the provided food edible? I had a bad experience at the Univerzita Karlova (Charles University). I found that the level of teachers is uneven ... some are highly competent, while others are not. Also, while the dorm rooms were liveable, the food (except for breakfast) was horrible. Dinner was a piece of meat that was mostly fat, swimming in gravy, with 6 knedliky and sugary & greasy zely. The vegetarian meal one night was three coconut-covered donuts. Unreal! I\'d appreciate hearing from anyone who has had experience in Brno during the summer. Dekuji!', '2005-01-18 07:58:54', 0, 1),
(1512, 15, 'June', 'jechante17@yahoo.com', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'I believe that mish-mash originated in Czech. It is spelled mismas in cesky ... that\'s with a hacek over each s!', '2005-01-18 08:06:03', 1085, 1),
(1513, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'I attended classes not at the Summer School, but just in the Katedra četiny pro cizince during the \"school year\", about ten years back, so there\'s not much I can say, but I\'ll say what I can.\r\n\r\n1) They were good, IME.\r\n\r\n2) Dunno; I had my own housing and of course the school offered none. Probably you\'d be put up at the koleje, basically a plain old dorm, though perhaps not as fancy as you\'re used to from the States (if you\'re from the States).\r\n\r\n3) Probably not.\r\n\r\n4) Dunno if food is provided at all. If you get typical menza (cafeteria) food or the chance to buy menza tickets (and thus eat cheaply)... yep, what you write about matches my memories of menza food. Although I would have hoped that the quality has gone up over the years, I see that at the very least the UK (the college, not the country) menza is still where the MU one was back then, and so I wouldn\'t be surprised if the MU one is also treading water.\r\n\r\n> Dinner was a piece of meat that was mostly fat, swimming in gravy, with 6 knedliky\r\n\r\nAKA in Franta Farmboy\'s eyes: delicious. :-)\r\n\r\n> sugary & greasy zely\r\n\r\nSweet-and-sour is quite a typical flavor for kysané zelí*. Downright sugary is not, however; blame that on the menza.\r\n\r\n> The vegetarian meal one night was three coconut-covered donuts. \r\n\r\nThe Czech mentality is that certain sweet breadlike foods can be considered \"meals,\" and they look at you funny when you look at them funny about it. I know, I don\'t get it either.\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\n* Not zely - -y is a plural ending for many masculine and feminine nouns, while zelí is a neutral and above all collective, \"unpluralizable\" noun, just like cabbage in English for that matter (but OK, OK, I know endings in Czech are a bit of a nightmare to master).', '2005-01-18 21:26:14', 1511, 0),
(1514, 14, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: czech names', 'See http://www.mvcr.cz/statistiky/jmena/muzi/poradi.html', '2005-01-21 21:14:32', 1500, 0),
(1515, 13, 'Paul Kochis', 'kochisp@sbcglobal.net', 'Question on origin of my last name', 'I have been doing some research on my family history.  My great grandfather was supposedly from Czech.  I am trying to find out if that is true.  Can anyone tell me what my last name means?  The original spelling was Koc\'is.  Any help would be greatly appreciated. ', '2005-01-24 00:07:57', 0, 1),
(1516, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'There is no word \"Kočis\" in modern-day Czech. A googling of the word reveals a mere 3 dozen pages, all of them (including the sole Czech reference) of them referring to non-Czechs -- mostly Poles, Latvians, Lithuanians, Hungarians, and Slovaks. Interesting is this reference from Slovakia, even though it turns out to be a Koči rather than a Kočis:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.cassovia.sk/korzar/clanok.php3?sub=31.12.2004/5916KV\r\n\r\nIn the Koice phone book you\'ll find surnames that will make you smile despite yourself\r\n\r\n[...]\r\n\r\nIn connection with the surname Koči there is the well-known rhyme Koči, po pivo mi skočí (Koči, you\'ll run grab me a beer)... This doesn\'t apply for director of the Regional Administration of the Slovak Statistics Office Ing. Koči; nobody has ever sent him for a foamy brew. \'About all that\'s connected to my surname are tongue-twisters. For example Hungarians [Koice lies near Hungary] have trouble spelling it. Meanwhile, I\'m just an ordinary Slovak Koči, so write it like you hear it. Even more confounded by my surname are the English and others who speak English. Most often they end up with \'Koic.\' Even worse was when I once presented myself and said \'I\'m Koči from Koíce [z Koíc].\" They repeated after me something like Koic z Kočis.\' As a boy, where E. Koči grew up in a village, where people aren\'t called by their first name and surname, but usually by their grandfather and greatgrandfather, people mostly know him a Emil from Sopek.\"\r\n\r\nLooking at all the hits, I\'d say somewhere in the border region between Hungaria and Slovakia, perhaps.\r\n\r\nIf it is instead Koči, however, we get a vastly larger number of hits, a much stronger tie to the Czechoslovak region, and finally some true Czech references.\r\n\r\nCouldn\'t find an etymology, though.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-24 15:26:58', 1515, 0),
(1517, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: czech names', ':-O\r\n\r\nYou are a god.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-24 15:27:56', 1514, 0),
(1518, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'Kočis is probably an improper Czech (Slovak, etc.) transcription of Hungarian \"kocsis\". The correct transcription is Koči (in Hungarian: sz = s, s = , cs = č). The Hungarian word \"kocsis\" means \"driver of a coach (horse-drawn carriage)\", derived fron \"kocsi\" = Eng. coach, Fr. coche, Ger. Kutsche, Cz. kočár, etc. See Merriam-Webster (www.m-w.com): \"coach\". The Czech equivalent of \"kocsis\" is \"kočí\".', '2005-01-24 16:51:57', 1516, 0),
(1519, 14, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: czech names', 'Velký Bratr o nás ví vechno!', '2005-01-24 18:20:45', 1517, 0),
(1520, 13, 'Paul Kochis', 'kochisp@sbcglobal.net', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'So does that mean that Kocis came from Hungary?  Would that mean that my family may actually be from Hungary then, and not Czech.  I was tlod my Great Grandfather was from Czech, and Great Grandmother was from Austria.  So that could be incorrect?  My Grandfather changed the spelling to Kochis so people could relate to the h sound in the name.  It is pronounced the way this sounds, (coachis).', '2005-01-25 04:35:24', 1518, 1),
(1521, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'The Austro-Hungarian empire was a great melting pot, or at least a salad bowl with dressing soaking into absolutely everything. Your great-grandfather may well have lived in the Czech lands, or even more likely modern-day Slovakia, and still have had Hungarian heritage. More common was the migration of people from the periphery to the center (as the vast number of Czech-named businesses remaining in Vienna to this day will attest) or from one part of the periphery to the other, and Hungarians were more common in what is now Slovakia than what is now the Czech Republic, but it\'s certainly possible.\r\n\r\nAs Vladimir noted, a Czech or Slovak could very well have mistranscribed Kocsis to Kočis... to a speaker of one of those two languages (perhaps more likely Czech, with its weaker contact with Hungary) with no understanding of Hungarian, the \"cs\" would clearly suggest a \"č\" (a \"ch\"), but the \"s\" being \"sh\" would not be so clear.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-25 17:53:13', 1520, 0),
(1522, 13, 'Paul Kochis', 'kochisp@sbcglobal.net', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'So does that mean that the spelling kocis could be wrong?  I notice you keep writing koeis.  Also what is that mark called that goes over the \"e\" when you type it, I can\'t figure out how to type it.', '2005-01-25 21:54:48', 1515, 1),
(1523, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'Hi Paul,\r\n\r\nIt\'s not we who are writing kočis, but rather your computer. The character in question is c-with-hacek (with the little \"v\" over the top). \r\n\r\nA short excursion into the wide world of what are called \"character set problems\" is needed here. Letters are stored on a computer as numbers, and the lists of what characters we are working with and what numbers they are associated with are called \"character sets\" or \"code pages.\" (Though those are probably subtly different, they\'re all the same to me... I\'m not such an expert.) For an imaginary example: \"1\" means \"a\", \"2\" means \"b\", 27 means \"A\", 52 means \"Z\", etc.\r\n\r\nNow there are many code pages that are quite similar, until you get into special characters, at which point everything is mucked up. Such is the case with Windows-1250 (the code page for Central European languages) and Windows-1252 (the code page for Western European languages). If your computer is using the code table for West Europe to show you material encoded in the Central European codepage, then a-z, A-Z, 1-0, space, and other basics will be OK, but most accented characters will be represented incorrectly. For example, c-with-hacek will be shown as e-grave. Basically the computer sees it should show you \"character number 125\" (or whatever) and, because it\'s using an almost-right-but-not-quite list to look up what\'s what, it mistakenly thinks \"character number 125\" should be e-grave.\r\n\r\nSolution: \r\n* First double-check that it\'s not just your Web browser\'s settings that are wrong. I assume you\'re using Internet Explorer? Go to the View menu, then the Encoding menu. If there\'s an item called \"Central European - Windows\", switch to it. Now the question is only why the encoding wasn\'t autodetected (every page on Bohemica.com declares itself to be encoded in Windows-1250, so there\'s no excuse for IE not to know the encoding).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWow, I can imagine you\'ve been pretty confused all this while seeing an e-grave everywhere instead of the c-with-hacek...\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAlthough there are various, somewhat complicated ways to type e-grave and, more importantly, c-with-hacek without having the national keyboard layout installed, it\'s a lot simpler just to install the needed keyboard layout once and then just switch keyboards to type Czech characters. Which Windows are you on?\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-26 16:19:18', 1522, 0),
(1524, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Missing a point in my \"solution\"', 'I forgot to write the part about what to do if you find you\'re not even offered the chance to use Central European encoding to show the page!\r\n\r\nIn that case, you\'ll have some Windows installing work to do... here again, the question is: which version are you on?\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-26 16:21:12', 1523, 0),
(1525, 15, 'Jan Kuzebauch', 'etroi@seznam.cz', 'writing with somebody', 'I am from the Czech Republic and I want to write with somebody from the USA ( czech, english ) through ICQ or E-mail. I think it will be profitable for me and for you.', '2005-02-05 01:13:16', 0, 0),
(1526, 15, 'Gary Toops', 'gary.toops@wichita.edu', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'I attended the Czech summer school at Masaryk University in Brno about 10 years ago, so I don\'t know if my experience is still repeatable.  We were housed in a student dormitory, two to a room, with each double room sharing a connecting bathroom.  Getting to classes required taking a short (approx. 5 minute) streetcar ride.  The food was very good, served in a large dining hall near where the classes were held, not in the dormitory.  Some afternoons I would, however, get lazy, skip supper, and simply start drinking beer at one of two pivnice located directly behind the dormitory.  If I got hungry, I could always order smazeny syr and hranolky (among other things).  It was a great way to meet and converse with Czechs.  The excursions were well organized, and the students participating in the summer school came from many different countries.  My roommate was from France, and he and I usually conversed in French.  The night life was okay back then, but I gather from browsing the Web that it has gotten much better in the past ten years.  Brno is not very big, so getting around town late at night even by cab is not prohibitively expensive or inconvenient.  As for Prague, I last attended a summer school there in 1997.  While the food situation was more than adequate, I found the instruction deficient.  Generally speaking, the more advanced you are, the less likely you are to get good teachers, by which I mean the young, enthusiastic ones.  The stodgy older teachers seem to be assigned almost invariably to the advanced classes.', '2005-02-06 06:52:04', 1511, 1),
(1527, 15, 'Gary Toops', 'gary.toops@wichita.edu', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'I attended the Czech summer school at Masaryk University in Brno about 10 years ago, so I don\'t know if my experience is still repeatable.  We were housed in a student dormitory, two to a room, with each double room sharing a connecting bathroom.  Getting to classes required taking a short (approx. 5 minute) streetcar ride.  The food was very good, served in a large dining hall near where the classes were held, not in the dormitory.  Some afternoons I would, however, get lazy, skip supper, and simply start drinking beer at one of two pivnice located directly behind the dormitory.  If I got hungry, I could always order smazeny syr and hranolky (among other things).  It was a great way to meet and converse with Czechs.  The excursions were well organized, and the students participating in the summer school came from many different countries.  My roommate was from France, and he and I usually conversed in French.  The night life was okay back then, but I gather from browsing the Web that it has gotten much better in the past ten years.  Brno is not very big, so getting around town late at night even by cab is not prohibitively expensive or inconvenient.  As for Prague, I last attended a summer school there in 1997.  While the food situation was more than adequate, I found the instruction deficient.  Generally speaking, the more advanced you are, the less likely you are to get good teachers, by which I mean the young, enthusiastic ones.  The stodgy older teachers seem to be assigned almost invariably to the advanced classes.', '2005-02-06 07:12:27', 1511, 1),
(1528, 22, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Welcome to metaphor forum', 'Welcome to metaphor forum. Any and all contributions on metaphor are welcome. I myself am more inclined toward the conceptual metaphor theory but I am aware that there is a lot of other research that is valuable out there that has a lot of interest to say on the subject.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2005-02-06 17:10:45', 0, 0),
(1529, 15, 'June', 'jechante17@yahoo.com', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'Gary: Thanks very much for the info. I\'m curious to know whether the school you attended in Prague in 1997 was the Letni Skola of the Univerzita Karlova. Thanks again! June', '2005-02-06 19:51:10', 1526, 0),
(1530, 14, 'juliet007', 'juliet007@wp.pl', 'fables, tales and cartoons', 'Hi,\r\nI study bohemistics in Wroclaw. I\'ve got to write a paper on Czech culture. So I thought I could write about this weird thing I have noticed in Czech and only there. WHY DO CHECH PEOPLE LOVE FAIRYTALES? You know, all those princesses, kings, fairytales etc.\r\n\r\nWhere can I find any material about it?\r\nI\'ll be veeeery thankful for any infos.\r\nJuliet\r\n\r\nPs. The paper is due this Friday! :(', '2005-02-07 16:43:01', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1531, 14, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: fables, tales and cartoons', 'Why do you think that only Czech people like fairytales? Just look at the Red Bull campaign which has a princess in a tower - you\'ll find that that did not originate in the CR and is quite easily understandable in all cultures. Here in England fairytales are extremely common - just look at Terry Pratchett\'s books which spoof many of them. Or Buffy the Vampire Slayer in the US. Or the Russians with all the baba yaga stuff. They also contributed the great Morphology of the fairytale by Vladimir Propp. Which might provide a useful angle for you.\r\n\r\nThe really interesting question is, what are the differences between fairytales in various cultures. Czech culture has <i>vodník</i> which has no equivalent in English folklore but has a very limited lore regarding <i>vampires</i> or no mention of a <i>toothfairy</i>. \r\n\r\nThe cultural representations may also be influenced by notable examples in film and print. In Czech, the tradition of Němcová and Erben is still very strong but there was also a series of films in the 50s and 60s which were based on a fairytale and are still regularly repeated on TV. Just off the top of my head: Pyná princezna, Princezna se zlatou hvězdou na čele, Sůl nad zlato, Císařův pekař a pekařův císař, Jak se budí princezny, and others with a fairytale or fantastic theme e.g. Jak utopit doktora Mráčka. There were a few TV shows that might feed off this, most notably Arabela (which was popular in many countries, I met people who know it in Romania) and many others for children only, including the good night story on TV called Večerníček. But don\'t forget that one of the most enduring Christmas films based on a fairytale is Mrazík, which is Russian. I\'m not sure about Polish culture, but I\'m sure that in Russia, a similar tradition could be drawn up. Of course, there was also the fairytale of communism. \r\n\r\nIf would of course be interesting to compare fairytale representations in the popular cultures of different traditions. There would be many differences and it is possible that certain fairytale representations (such as princezna) would be more common in the Czech culture than others.\r\n\r\nDominik\r\n\r\n', '2005-02-07 16:59:42', 1530, 0),
(1532, 14, 'juliet007', 'juliet007@wp.pl', 'fables, tales and cartoons', 'Hi,\r\nI study bohemistics in Wroclaw. I\'ve got to write a paper on Czech culture. So I thought I could write about this weird thing I have noticed in Czech and only there. WHY DO CHECH PEOPLE LOVE FAIRYTALES? You know, all those princesses, kings, fairytales etc.\r\n\r\nWhere can I find any material about it?\r\nI\'ll be veeeery thankful for any infos.\r\nJuliet\r\n\r\nPs. The paper is due this Friday! :(', '2005-02-07 17:00:57', 0, 0),
(1533, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'topic for a paperwork', 'I guess you\'re right.\r\nAnd of course, I know Arabella (everybody knows arabella!!) :)\r\nIt\'s just that my grammar teacher (who is Czech) always says it should be a very interesting topic for an article, cause Czech people (adults as much as children) love those \"princezna\'s stories\".\r\nAnd they are often in tv (mAm Ceskou jedinku).\r\n\r\nMaybe You could also say, what you think about Czech movies after 1990. Do they complete into some kind of tendency or sth?? (I really need an interesting topic for this semestralku!)\r\nThanks,\r\njuliet', '2005-02-07 17:08:56', 1531, 0),
(1534, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'topic for a paperwork', 'I guess you\'re right.\r\nAnd of course, I know Arabella (everybody knows arabella!!) :)\r\nIt\'s just that my grammar teacher (who is Czech) always says it should be a very interesting topic for an article, cause Czech people (adults as much as children) love those \"princezna\'s stories\".\r\nAnd they are often in tv (mAm Ceskou jedinku).\r\n\r\nMaybe You could also say, what you think about Czech movies after 1990. Do they complete into some kind of tendency or sth?? (I really need an interesting topic for this semestralku!)\r\nThanks,\r\njuliet', '2005-02-07 17:09:07', 1531, 0),
(1535, 14, 'Dominik Luke', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: topic for a paperwork', 'I haven\'t followed Czech movies much after the 1990s but I think the trend continues. You have the successful Princezna ze mlýna (1 and 2) and the Drvotěp variation by Svěrák (Zdeněk not Jan). Which reminds me, another fairly uniquely Czech character is \'čert\' which is usually depicted in a much more benign way and more removed from any Biblical undertones than elsewhere. See films like S čerty nejsou erty, Dalskabáty hříná ves, or again Arabela. There is a certain tendency to sanitize things in Czech postwar culture, for instance Pleva\'s reworking of Robinson Crusoe into a proponent of multiculturalism vs. the original colonial bigot you find in Defoe.\r\n\r\nAnother tack you could take, is the fantastic in Czech film and culture. You have stuff like Návtěvníci, Létající Čestmír, Spadla z oblakov (which is actually Slovak) on TV but also Adéla jetě nevečeřela, Zítra ráno vstanu a opařím se čajem, Pane vy jste vdova, Co takhle dát si penát or even Kdyby tisíc klarinetů which formed much of the imagery of the 60s, 70s and 80s, pretty much in conjunction with fairytale. (But then you have stuff like Sexmise and Solaris in Poland, so that may not be as interesting).', '2005-02-07 17:20:23', 1533, 0),
(1536, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'thank you', 'thank you', '2005-02-07 17:23:03', 1535, 0),
(1537, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'thank you', 'thank you', '2005-02-07 17:23:19', 1535, 0),
(1538, 14, 'Daniel Butler', 'daniel.butler@atlas.cz', 'Czech family names', ' I have been living in Prague on and off for the last 8 years and keep wondering about the REAL origin of Czech family names.  I am no great linguist, researcher or \'name\' anthropologist but you might agree with me when I say that around the world there are perhaps 3 most common origins for family names: Using czech and English examples, the place of ancestry, Jan Nemec - John German; the profession or trade, Pavel Mlynar\' - Paul Miller (or Butler, Baker, Smith, etc); and the family original first name such as Johnson, Paulson, Richardson or in some double examples in a Russian name Michal Michalovich, Petr Petrovich. \r\n\r\nBut only in Czech do you come across more visual and sometimes more amusing last names. For example : Pane Pobud - Mister Smelly place, Richard Skacel - Richard Sound-of-tree-falling, Pepa Pospisal - Joe Running-late; Tomas Cibule - Tom Onion.   \r\n\r\nI have asked time and again why do Czech names appear to be 10 times more colorful than the names of many other languages. I am sure there are others but I can only think of one other culture that has something as visual and descriptive and that is of the North American Indians with names such as Running-bear or Sitting-bull. (And if you do not beleive me, pull out the white pages and your Cesko-Anglicko dictionary and start translating the names; a few bottles of Budvar or Gambrinus and a few friends makes it even more enjoyable). \r\n\r\nFrom what I am told this descriptive naming system doesn\'t even appear in Poland, Hungary, or  other neighboring countries to even a small extent. My simple question is, Why in Czech Republic, did these names begin? I have heard theories about the controlling and yet foreign ruling empire may have enforced a census and, as a protest, the local peasantry decided to ridicule the system by naming themselves in more amusing manner. Any other theories - but hopefully the truth - would be greatly appreciated. ', '2005-02-07 20:44:04', 0, 1),
(1539, 13, 'Gerard', 'gmcgrana@tcd.ie', 'Words of a song', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nI have a Czech song on CD but sung by a Swedish man.  I would like to have the lyrics in Czech, so i can learn it myself!!  Does any one here have the words??\r\n\r\nMany Thanks\r\n\r\nGerard', '2005-02-08 02:04:23', 0, 1),
(1540, 13, 'Gerard', 'gmcgrana@tcd.ie', 'Forgot Name of Song', 'Sorry!!\r\n\r\nThe song is called Zadnej nevi jako ja!  \r\n\r\nGerard', '2005-02-08 02:07:18', 1539, 1),
(1541, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech family names', 'I\'d say that generally, such last names started as nicknames given to someone by the local community and carried down from generation to generation. I find any \"protest by silly surname\" theory a bit implausible. I doubt that there is anything specifically Czech about such nicknames; perhaps what is specifically Czech is that they weren\'t *killed off* via the censuses. Now this fact could indeed be related to the fact that a nationality speaking another language ran the first censuses. It\'s hard to tell someone they\'ll have to get another name than their old name of \"Ate a kid\'s porridge\" (Snědldítětíkai) if you don\'t know what it means. But it\'s hard to say.\r\n\r\nYou can find a short but interesting work on Czech surnames at the following address:\r\n\r\nhttp://members.tripod.com/~zlimpkk/Genealogy/czechsurnames.html\r\n\r\nI think you\'ll especially like section h). :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-02-09 17:42:26', 1538, 0),
(1542, 14, 'Daniel Butler', 'daniel.butler@atlas.cz', 'Re: Czech family names', 'Erik, I appreciate the reply. And thanks very much for the link. This list of potential name origins could be applied in many countries. In fact, using that presentation of THE ORIGIN OF CZECH SURNAMES I would indeed strenously suggest that my case is indeed on more solid ground. Especially in the case of h) Surnames describing the activity or status. and to a lesser extent even c) Personal Features of an Individuum. (Perhaps there should be another categorization, j) silly names arising from having drunk too much beer). \r\nAgain, I will suggest that there are more curious and amusing names in the Czech Republic than other European countries. I mean, really. Take a look at this sample (from the page you linked to) and consider that there are hundreds more, just as enjoyable. I can think of only 10 or 20 that are amusing in English! \r\nPRECECHTEL  > And-he-wanted-it-after-all, \r\nSTOJESPAL > He-slept-standing , \r\nZLAMALJELITO > He-broke-the-black-pudding), \r\nSNEDLDITETIKASI > He-ate-the-squash-to-the-child),\r\nor even NASRALVHRNEC > (I won\'t write this one either, but its what they did in the olden days before toilets). \r\nI certainly would welcome other entrants to this discussion to learn more. I just enjoy hearing a new and amusing Czech name. \r\n', '2005-02-09 19:20:43', 1541, 1),
(1543, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech family names', 'Or even just Brzobohatý (Quick-rich... OK, this is in English too) and Zřídkaveselý (Rarely-happy).\r\n\r\nWhat I really can\'t figure out is why the recipients of some of these names didn\'t refuse to let people call them such names... either community pressure to take the nickname you were given was really strong, or people turned it around into a badge of pride, or something. One thing I can judge, since it involves the present day, is that I saw a lot more nicknames among young adults (when I was still -- just barely -- one myself) than I did growing up in the States, and you\'ll still find some here and there among people of career age.\r\n\r\nDo note, however, that sometimes it\'s easier to notice strange things in a foreign language than one\'s own. A Czech might point out plenty of strange English names for our amusement, assuming he could get past the severe mutations of English (compared to Czech) over the centuries.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-02-10 22:24:59', 1542, 0),
(1544, 16, 'Dalibor Tobiá', 'dobiasd@yahoo.com', 'Lektorát četiny v Moskvě', 'Dobry den,\r\nprosim Vas o zverejneni informace o moznosti nastoupit od sk. roku 2005/6 na misto lektora cestiny na Fakulte cizich jazyku Lomonosovovy statni univerzity v Moskve.\r\nNaplni prace je vyuka cestiny a vedeni odbornych kursu dle zajmu a specializace lektora. Pozadavky: abslovent filologickeho oboru, nejlepe cestiny, s cestinou na urovni rodilych mluvcich a alespon zaklady rustiny, znalosti ruskych realii, spolehlivy a pracovity, se zajmem o danou praci a iniciativni, ochotny spolupracovat na rusko-ceskych projektech. Jedna se o misto na 1-3 roky, placene zcasti MSMT CR a zcasti MSU. Zajemce prosim o zpravu na adresu:\r\ndobiasd@yahoo.com. Dekuji.\r\nS pozdravem\r\n\r\nDalibor Dobias', '2005-02-17 23:28:12', 0, 0),
(1545, 13, 'Joe Bouril', 'bourilfamily@comcast.net', 'name and meaning', 'My research shows that \"bouril\" translates as \"stormed\". I was wondering if it refers to the past tense in terms of: weather, charging in or out, a grand entrance or tour, or the like. Any input is greatly appreciated.', '2005-02-18 07:20:44', 0, 1),
(1546, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: name and meaning', 'Bouril (in Czech it would be Bouřil) is actually a past participle of the verb bouřit (bourit - \"r\" with a little hook over it) that is associated with the noun bouře - a storm. So Bouril meant someone whose personality was like a storm. It is my explanation. It could also be contaminated with the verb \"bořit, bourat\", past participles \"bořil, boural\", meaning to destroy (a building). Pavel.', '2005-02-20 19:20:15', 1545, 0),
(1547, 22, 'cedric detienne', 'cedricdetienne@yahoo.fr', '', 'I created a French-speaking forum on the metaphor! \r\n\r\nI am happy that there is another forum! \r\n\r\nhttp://www.info-metaphore.com\r\n\r\n', '2005-02-22 03:54:40', 0, 0),
(1548, 14, 'Dagmar Dornbierer', 'dornbierer@stauffacher.com', 'Re: Czech family names', 'You might know Pavel Eisner - a a highly gifted czech linguist, musician and author of wonderful books on Czech language. Those books are quite untranslateable, well, they are dealing with czech... Eisner wrote some nice chapters on czech family names, on czech \"language behaviour\" and a special chapter on the name \"Maria\", which was the most used female name until the mid of the 20th century.', '2005-02-22 17:19:47', 1538, 0),
(1549, 22, 'Cédric detienne', 'cedricdetienne@yahoo.fr', 'transdisciplinary metaphorology', 'I created a website of documentation on the metaphor. Its goal is to constitute a \"transdisciplinary metaphorology\". Does this concept mean something for you? What do you think of this project? \r\n\r\n(The website www.info-metaphore.com is French-speaking but it will be translated soon into English... If you want help me to translate this website, send me a mail)\r\n\r\nhttp://www.info-metaphore.com\r\n\r\n', '2005-02-23 01:17:21', 0, 1),
(1550, 22, 'Melvyn Clarke', 'zehrovak@volny.cz', 'Guardian article', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nThought you might like to know there\'s an interesting item in the Guardian on use of metaphor in different cultures:\r\n\r\nHow time flies \r\n\r\nFor the Aymara people living in the Andes, the past lies ahead and the future lies behind. Laura Spinney looks at how different languages reflect, and shape, our conception of time\r\n\r\nhttp://www.guardian.co.uk/life/feature/story/0,13026,1423455,00.html\r\n\r\nBTW it seems the ancient Greeks had a similar conception of time - according to the following essay:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.cosmos-club.org/journals/1995/knox.html\r\n\r\nBR\r\n\r\nMelvyn\r\n', '2005-02-26 05:01:17', 0, 1),
(1551, 15, 'sokki', '', 'www.sokker.org', 'ako te interesira football to je dobar manager', '2005-03-01 21:11:11', 0, 0),
(1552, 14, 'Cara', 'Javajunkie56@aol.com', 'War Of The Newts', 'Hi there - I would like to find a copy of War Of The Newts in Czech for my father. does anyone have any ideas? Thanks', '2005-03-02 21:54:18', 0, 1),
(1553, 14, 'Steven Santayana', 'gloin@hotmail.com', 'Exchange Program', 'I am currently considering, going to czech in the AFS exchange program as a student, I dont speak czech but would like to learn, I am only 14, does this sound over my head or possibly to difficult for me to undertake? Or is this a good idea? \r\n\r\nThank You Steven Santayana', '2005-03-04 08:31:29', 0, 1),
(1554, 14, 'jana', '', 'Re: Exchange Program', 'Hi Steven,\r\nI think learning another language is always a good idea. At the same time, coping with native speakers, and an environment that is culturally quite different from the one you\'re familiar with, might be quite a big step to take without preparation (exchange programs should help with this but I don\'t really know AFS).\r\n\r\nYour age might make it easier and more difficult at the same time - easier because language learning gets harder with age and more difficult because felling lonely and homesick is easier to overcome with experiance.\r\n\r\nMy best advice would be to think about your motivations, why exactly do you want to take part in an exchange program? Why Czech, what do you know about the language and culture? What do you actually expect to learn? Are you ready for both - good and bad experiance (both are valuable for learning but not easy to live through)? \r\n\r\nI went to London and lived (actually still do, started a university and am doing my MPhil now) but I was twice your age and spoke English more or less fluently. It didn\'t help that much, I could understand the words (so would you, many Czechs can speak some English) but I had no clue what people actually meant (the culture really is quite different). Hard, very hard (well, I had to manage the finance on the top of it) but opened my eyes the way I wouldn\'t think possible any other way.\r\n\r\nWhatever you decide, I wish you good luck and lots of fun.\r\nJana', '2005-03-05 22:50:37', 1553, 0),
(1555, 22, 'Pattarawan Youyen', 'pttwn_2p@yahoo.com', 'Re: Welcome to metaphor forum', ' I am sure that the metaphor forum will provide the chance for people to share some linguistic ideas.\r\n I am really interested in (conceptual) metaphor and now doing kind of contrastive study paper on metaphorical expressions using body parts in Thai and Japanese.\r\n', '2005-03-07 21:11:53', 1528, 0),
(1556, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: War Of The Newts', 'Sending answer by e-mail, in case you don\'t return here. (Announcing here, so nobody duplicates my work.)\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-03-08 18:12:37', 1552, 0),
(1557, 13, 'Petoro', 'petrvondrous@email.cz', 'Re: I have a question...', 'may i beg your pardon could be translated\r\nlike that ^Nezlobte se prosim.^\r\nor ^Muzete mi odpustit?^\r\nor easily: ^Prominte.^ = Sorry.\r\n\r\ni am not using diacritics, tak se nezlobte :)', '2005-03-10 10:48:23', 1510, 0),
(1558, 13, 'Kerr', 'kafmcg@hotmail.com', 'I need help with some translation please', 'Hello, \r\n\r\nIf possible, I would love to have the following phrases in English translated into Czech:\r\n\r\n  \"I love to learn!\" and \r\n  \"Sincerely\" (this greeting will be used at the end of a letter, to \r\n     sign off with)\r\n\r\nI hope someone will be able to help me, I\'d really appreciate it.  Thank you so much!\r\n\r\nKerr', '2005-03-11 20:41:29', 0, 1),
(1559, 13, 'Jarek', 'tomiczek@gmail.com', 'Re: I need help with some translation please', 'Hi,\r\none way how to say that, might be:\r\n\"Velice rád se učím.\" - I like learning a lot.\r\n\r\nA formal greeting at the end of a letter might be:\r\n\"S pozdravem\", or informal \"Zdraví\".\r\n\r\nZdraví,\r\nJarek\r\n', '2005-03-12 04:50:36', 1558, 0),
(1560, 13, 'Jarek', 'tomiczek@gmail.com', 'Re: I need help with some translation please', 'Hi,\r\none way how to say that, might be:\r\n\"Velice rád se učím.\" - I like learning a lot.\r\n\r\nA formal greeting at the end of a letter might be:\r\n\"S pozdravem\", or informal \"Zdraví\".\r\n\r\nZdraví,\r\nJarek\r\n', '2005-03-12 04:50:36', 1558, 0),
(1561, 13, 'Jarek', 'tomiczek@gmail.com', 'Re: Forgot Name of Song', 'What about this one? \r\n\r\n\r\nádnej neví jako já\r\njak se seče otavička\r\nádnej neví jako já\r\njak se seče otava\r\n\r\nOtava se pěkně seče \r\njako kdy se pole vleče\r\nádnej neví jako já\r\njak se seče otava\r\n\r\nádnej neví jako já\r\njak je v noci cesta dlouhá\r\nádnej neví jako já\r\njak je v noci cesta zlá\r\n\r\nKdy já příjdu k svý Dorličce\r\nona mi otevřít nechce\r\nádnej neví jako já\r\njak je v noci cesta zlá', '2005-03-12 05:15:56', 1540, 1),
(1562, 13, 'Ms. Hresko', 'chsexc@aol.com', 'My Last Name', 'Does anyone know the origin of my last name? Is there a masc. and femin. version of it? I have heard that if I lived in the Czech. Repblic since I am female I would be Ms. Hreskova (instead of Hresko).', '2005-03-13 10:15:51', 0, 1),
