(1621, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: hospoda, hospodarnost, Hospodin', 'I don\'t know, but it\'s clearly quite related to caring for people or things. It\'s also in (just examples; many more could be found)...\r\n\r\nhospodář (farmer, basically)\r\nhospodářský (the intersection of the words \"financial\" and \"economic\")\r\nhospodařit s (to manage (a resource))\r\nhospodařství (essentially \"economy\")\r\nhospodský (barkeeper)\r\n\r\nHospodárnost is economy in the sense of efficiency. It\'s not a word you see very often, though.\r\n\r\nThere\'s a \"deeper\" root here, and that\'s hos- itself. Outside of the hospod- words, it usually (always?) appears as host-; it appears in host (guest), hostitel (host), hostinec (another word for a pub), vyhostit (to throw (a person) out of a place or country), etc.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-05-25 18:23:36', 1618, 0),
(1622, 13, 'Pam', 'Psam57@aol.com', 'Help with a gift', 'I have a friend of Czech heritage who has just moved into a new home. Could someone suggest a Czech phrase meaning \"Home Sweet Home\" or \"Welcome to my Home,\" etc? \r\n\r\nMany thanks!', '2005-05-26 11:59:55', 0, 1),
(1624, 13, 'HEVEN', '', 'It is a question.', 'Please teach the word stomach in the Czech Republic word. \r\n', '2005-05-27 18:58:53', 0, 0),
