(1698, 14, 'Jacek', 'alcor69_69@yahoo.com', 'I found the best calling card ever from the US to CZECH Republic', 'I have been using www.callingforcheap.com for a couple months now. \r\nI call home to the CZECH republic for  cents a minute, no connection fees/maintenance fees and nearest minute rounding! \r\n\r\nI LOVE the online account management, SPEED DIALING and NO PINS EVER!\r\n\r\nI used to go to the local convenience store to buy prepaids, but all the extra fees drove almost drove me crazy.. a 2 hour card was really worth an hourINSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1760, 13, 'pfk', 'mk85346@alltel.net', 'script for the written Czech language', 'Hello, Where can I find the Czech alfabet in various \r\nwritten language forms? I am not sure what time period\r\nI am looking for, but 18th or 19th century written forms\r\nwould be best. I am trying to translate a 1914 hand written\r\nletter and am having a hard time recognizing the letters.\r\nThank You,\r\nmk85346@alltel.net', '2005-08-24 00:00:33', 0, 0),
(1761, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: script for the written Czech language', 'You can find various manuscripts via this search:\r\n\r\nhttp://images.google.com/images?q=rukopis&hl=en&btnG=Search+Images\r\n\r\n...but as far as a table saying \"this letter of the alphabet looked like this in this era\"... I don\'t think you\'ll have much luck. If you upload it to an upload service like http://www.uploadit.org/ and publish a link to where you put it, I and the other sage folks here can take a look at it.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-26 17:13:40', 1760, 0),
(1762, 13, 'Isabelle', 'info@labradorclothing.com', 'Can anyone translate this czech review?', 'Hi,\r\nWe had a review of our website on a czech website, but are unable to understand what it says, could anyone give us an overall translation?\r\n\r\nThanks.\r\n\r\nIsabelle.\r\n\r\nKolonne Uniform\r\nproč si domů pořídit labradora/rku\r\n\r\nNe nebudu vám kazit oběd (či večeři nebo co zrovinka děláte) líčením nechutných sexuálních praktik, kde figuruje velký černý pes, \"Ocelový Dan II\" a velká zásoba baterií Duracell, které vydrí tak dlouho a vechny ty nechutnosti. Málem bych zapomněl na sklenici malinového demu, dva kusy policejních pout (vzor CzechTek05) a dvanáct velkých kosetních svící s portrétem JanaPavla II. Labradora si do paneláku radi nepořizujte, místo toho si pořiďte pěkné tričko. Naivky a naivové se mohou obléknout u Naive Clothing. Docela dobře ílená je i prezentace tričkárny Tight Knickers. Dramaticky budete vypadat v oblečení z Drama store, kde najdete hadry různých značek, kníky, časáky a různý kravinky. Dobrou stravou (pastvou) pro oči jsou i věcičky z masa a sýra.\r\n', '2005-08-27 17:21:17', 0, 1),
(1763, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Names of floors; Russian is American style', 'I noticed. Russian is American (Japanese) style; that is the floor one up from the ground floor is \"vtaroi etaji\" (the second floor). \r\nBut in Czech language it is \"prvni\" the first floor. The English style.\r\nDoes anyone know why Russian and American have this system?', '2005-08-29 06:17:09', 1755, 0),
(1764, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'říci and říct are the same word?', 'Some dictionary site \"říct.\" And other \"říci.\" I imagine this is because of the history of the change of writing rule.\r\nPlease tell me the history of these two words.\r\n', '2005-08-29 06:23:05', 0, 0),
(1765, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: říci and říct are the same word?', 'These are just the -[something]ti and -[something]t endings for the same verb\'s infinitive, something that exists for many (all?) verbs in Czech, not just here.\r\n\r\nAs far as I know: the -[something]ti endings for infinitives instead of -[something]t endings are formerly (until about 50, 70 years ago) required, then for formal speech only, and gradually becoming archaic. I would never use them in speech unless I was in a very formal situation (and I would be a little worried about misusing them, and also worried about sounding too informal by not using them -- formal writing makes me nervous overall. Generally I try to aim for the \"high end of spoken Czech\" -- not risking -ti endings, but also not using colloquial -ý and colloquial -ama endings, etc.). I sometimes use them in formal writing. With any highly formal trait there\'s always the risk of being hyperformal. (Sometimes in relaxed situations people use hyperformal style just to be funny, but this is hard for a foreigner to do well.)\r\n\r\nCheers!\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-08-29 18:31:03', 1764, 0),
(1766, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Can anyone translate this czech review?', 'Kolonne Uniform\r\nor: on why you should get yourself a Labrador for your home\r\n\r\nNo, I don\'t want to spoil your lunch (or supper or whatever you\'re doin\' right now) by describing the disgusting sexual practices where the big black dog \"Iron Dan II\" and Duracell batteries \"built to last\" come into play, or all the other disgusting things. And I almost forgot to mention the glass of raspberry jam, two sets of police handcuffs (CzechTek 05 style) and twelve large church (?) candles with a portrait of John Paul II. I\'d advise you *not* get a Labrador; buy yourself a nice t-shirt instead. The naive and naif among you can get them at Naive Clothing. Another nicely crazy thing here is the presentation of the Tight Knickers t-shirt enterprise. You\'ll look dramatic in clothing from the Drama store, where you\'ll find clothing of various brands, books, mags, and various silly stuff. The meat and cheese items are also a feast for the eyes.\r\n\r\nWeird review, huh?\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-29 18:38:42', 1762, 0),
(1767, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: říci and říct are the same word?', 'Thanks, Erik. Your response is always quick. I appreciate it.', '2005-08-30 07:56:26', 1765, 0),
(1768, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Is \"ústa\" singular or plural?', 'I understand that the mouth is a concept of a vacant space. One thing. It is not lips or tongue. But do Czech people understad moouth as an organ composed of two parts? Just as lips? Or it just happened that expressions of mouth and mouths are the same? Ooooo. This stupid question never let me sleep at night. ', '2005-09-01 06:14:45', 0, 0),
(1769, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Is ústa singular or plural?', 'Ústa is a noun of a type called \"pluralia tantum\", which is just a fancy Latin way of saying \"always plural, even when it\'s singular.\" Another common example is dveře. So ústa is actually nom. pl. of \"ústo\", but \"ústo\" sort of doesn\'t exist.\r\n\r\nI won\'t really answer the philosophical part of your question, because I can only give silly answers. The only answer I can give is because \"one day, Czech speakers reached the consensus that a singular can never be used for certain nouns, and ústa is one of them.\"\r\n\r\n(They are often (always?) things that come in pairs, and I\'m thus tempted to talk about them in relation to the phenomenon called the \"duál\", which always deals with things in pairs and leads to formally required (as opposed to colloquial) 7th-case -ama endings. Fortunately, I don\'t understand duál much, so I won\'t muddy this discussion much with it.)\r\n\r\nBy the way, when you need to \"count\" these words, you use... :\r\njedná ústa\r\ndvoje ústa\r\ntroje ústa\r\nčtverá (I think) ústa\r\n...if you need to count higher, ask somebody else. If you need to decline those adjectives CORRECTLY, also ask somebody else, though I think it\'s \r\njedna ústa\r\nz jedných úst\r\nk jedným ústám (???)\r\nna jedna ústa\r\njedná ústa! (try finding a practical reason to use THAT phrase!)\r\nv jedněch ústech (???)\r\njednama ústama (?)\r\n\r\n...and a different set for dvoje/troje, and a different set for čtverá :-/ This is one of the parts of Czech where I am at my weakest.\r\n\r\nIn practice, you will \"of course\" when speaking with friends hear jedný ústa, not jedná ústa. Unless your friends are ministers or professors. :-)\r\n\r\nFun fact: An old Czech saying is that it\'s not for nothing that we have dvě ruce a jen jedna ústa. (One mouth; one \"set of lips\" I guess, even though lip is \"ret\".)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-09-01 21:06:16', 1768, 0),
(1770, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Is ústa singular or plural?', '\'Plurale tantum\' is a noun which is formally in plural, but denotes a single object. It is not related to the dual number. Often it denotes a thing which can be divided in two identical or symmetrical parts: e.g. nůky, kletě (scissors), kalhoty (pants), etc. but not necessarily: hodiny (clock, litterally hours). Sometimes the plural has no apparent reason: játra (liver).\r\n\r\nAgreement in number and gender:\r\n\r\nTy hodiny se zastavily. (fem. plur.)\r\nJátra alkoholika jsou zvětená. (neutr. plur.)\r\nÚsta se zavřela.\r\nAtény (Athenae, in Old Greek and Latin also plurale tantum) byly zaloeny ... (fem. plur.)', '2005-09-02 13:53:05', 1769, 0),
(1771, 13, 'Vladimír', '', 'Re: Is ústa singular or plural?', '\"Jeden\" is declined like \"ten\":\r\n\r\nta jedna ústa (města)\r\ntěch jedněch úst (měst)\r\ntěm jedněm ústům\r\nta jedna ústa\r\njedna ústa!\r\n(o) těch jedněch ústech\r\ns těmi jedněmi ústy\r\n', '2005-09-02 15:07:47', 1769, 0),
(1772, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Is ústa singular or plural?', 'Thank you very much, Erik and Vladimir. Now I can sleep well. (By the way, Japanese language does not have plural forms of (almost all) nouns.)', '2005-09-05 06:03:39', 1771, 0),
(1773, 13, 'seija', 'natsi@heathen.zzn.com', 'What are these words in english: \"Due nesmrtelných\"', '?????????\r\ncould someone translate this please:\r\n\r\nDue nesmrtelných', '2005-09-08 23:45:48', 0, 0),
(1774, 13, 'seija', 'natsi@heathen.zzn.com', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'Due \r\n\r\nthat word is perhaps \"soul\" or \"inner\"\r\n\r\nbut what is \"nesmrtelných\" ???', '2005-09-08 23:49:48', 1773, 0),
(1775, 13, 'JV', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Names of floors of a building', 'Don\'t forget the difference between patro and podlaí.\r\n\r\npřízemí = 1. podlaí\r\n1. patro = 2. podlaí\r\netc.\r\n\r\nNotice that only podlaí is technically correct term, while patro is semi-colloquial.\r\n\r\nJV', '2005-09-09 16:20:17', 1752, 0),
(1776, 13, 'jv', '', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'Perhaps \"souls of immortals\"?', '2005-09-09 16:27:23', 1773, 0),
(1777, 13, 'maly zajic', 'tibunny3@yahoo.com', 'milenka - milenec', 'Ahoj everyone,\r\n\r\nPlease forgive me if the topic of this post offends some of you, but I would like to have a clarification on \"milenka\" and its masculine counterpart. I have heard a few explanations from czech women and men, but none were really satisfying in my opinion. It seems that the word \"milenka\" describes a lover, but without emotional bond, as opposed to \"milacek\". Is that right? If so, I\'m curious to hear your opinion on it, because in english/french/german, there\'s no such word to distinguish a purely physical relationship.\r\n\r\nDiky moc :0)', '2005-09-09 16:35:56', 0, 1),
(1778, 13, 'maly zajic', 'tibunny3@yahoo.com', 'milenka - milenec', 'Ahoj everyone,\r\n\r\nPlease forgive me if the topic of this post offends some of you, but I would like to have a clarification on \"milenka\" and its masculine counterpart. I have heard a few explanations from czech women and men, but none were really satisfying in my opinion. It seems that the word \"milenka\" describes a lover, but without emotional bond, as opposed to \"milacek\". Is that right? If so, I\'m curious to hear your opinion on it, because in english/french/german, there\'s no such word to distinguish a purely physical relationship.\r\n\r\nDiky moc :0)', '2005-09-09 17:06:03', 0, 1),
(1779, 13, 'Dominik Luke', 'forums@bohemica.com', 'Re: milenka - milenec', '\"milenka\" really means mistress with all the emotional and sexual implications you find in English. \"milenec\" is the male counterpart to \"mistress\" which English does not have. \"miláček\", on the other hand, describes emotional bond only - it can be used about friends and family or even animals.\r\n\r\n', '2005-09-09 17:07:57', 1777, 0),
(1780, 13, 'zajiceck', '', 'Re: milenka - milenec', 'Thank you Dominik for your useful explanation. Again, I had clearly the impression, talking to czech people, that addressing a woman as \"Milenko\" meant that there was only sexual connotations to the relation, and nothing emotional. They told me that if you did not care about the person, you should use \"Milenko\" and if you did, \"Milacku\". But maybe it\'s just their interpretation.', '2005-09-10 14:13:39', 1779, 0),
(1781, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'The spirit/soul (probably spirit) or spirits/souls of the immortals.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-09-12 17:17:42', 1773, 0),
(1782, 13, 'Craig', 'craig_leigh_harrison@yahoo.co.uk', 'Please could somebody translate this message of my girlfriend', 'Stra&#185;n&#236; moc mi chybí&#185; a mám t&#236; stále p&#248;ed o&#232;ima.V Praze jsem &#232;ekala u&#190; \r\njen chvíli, dala jsem si párek v rohlíku.Cesta byla v pohod&#236;, &#232;etla \r\njsem si a u&#190; mi chybí jen 50 stran.U&#190; se t&#236;&#185;ím a&#190; zase napí&#185;e&#185;, zachvíli \r\np&#249;jdu ven asi na kole, proto&#190;e je tu zase horko - 35 stup&#242;&#249;. Tak ať se \r\nti da&#248;í v práci, miluju t&#236; !!!!!!!!!\r\n', '2005-09-29 02:20:23', 0, 1),
(1783, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Please could somebody translate this message of my girlfriend', '\"I really miss you and can always imagine you in my mind. In Prague, I only kept on waiting a little bit longer; I had a Viennese hot dog. The trip was fine; I was reading and I only have 50 pages left. I\'m already looking forward to your next letter, in a while I\'ll be heading out on my bike, because it\'s hot here again -- 35 degrees. [95 degrees F.] So -- good luck in your work, I love you!!!\"\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-09-30 14:33:13', 1782, 0),
(1784, 14, 'ajjus', 'catch_cas@yahoo.com', 'desperately seeking sunshine', 'Hello all,\r\nI tried everything and this will be my last try. I\'m trying to find my friend, who I\'m unable to get in touch with.\r\nFirst name: Gabriela/Gabina\r\nLast name: Vbracikova (not sure of the spelling)\r\nCity: Prague\r\nHer email: sunshine@email.cz\r\n\r\nShe\'s been emailing me, but unfortunately my replies to her get bounced back. Looks like she has some kind of email bocker.\r\n\r\nI will truely appreciate any help offered in trying to find my lost friend.\r\nHere\'s a link to my post regarding this in another forum.\r\nhttp://www.myczechrepublic.com/boards/viewtopic.php?t=3582 \r\n\r\nthanks!\r\ncatch_cas@yahoo.com', '2005-10-02 23:15:29', 0, 1),
(1785, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: desperately seeking sunshine', 'I could try to email her in your name, in case my address is better for avoiding the spam filter, but I really would feel a bit strange doing so if I couldn\'t mention your name in the header... and \"catch cas\" and \"ajjus\" just don\'t feel like a well-guaranteeed of letting her know it\'s about you. :-) May I ask for your first and last name or at least first name and last initial, so I can mention them in the subject line? I\'ll give it a whirl if you do.\r\n\r\nThere is a realistic chance I\'ll have better luck -- Yahoo accounts are among the most frequently blocked by various ISPs due to the historically high spammer population there. The weird \"connection\" failures reported by the one guy on that other thread aren\'t too encouraging, but hopefully these were a temporary or unnecessary problem.\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-10-04 16:58:18', 1784, 0),
(1786, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'byvat, frequentative', 'Can someone tell me why bývat is used in the following sentence rather than just a normal past tense?\r\n\r\nAby se uředník nezdroval měněním peněz bývá u pokladny tabulka s nápisem \".....\"\r\n\r\n', '2005-10-12 03:54:40', 0, 1),
(1787, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: byvat, frequentative', 'One usage of the frequentative is for the same purpose as the English phrases \"tends to smurf\", \"usually smurfs\", and \"generally smurfs\". So the quoted sentence means:\r\n\r\n\"To prevent unnecessary delays for clerks exchanging money, there is usually a sign saying \".....\" next to the register.\"\r\n\r\nFor what it\'s worth, the sentence literally says:\r\n\r\nIn-order-that himself clerk/bureaucrat/official does-not-delay by-the-exchanging of-money there-tends-to-be by register table/small-sign with writing-on-a-sign \"....\". \r\n\r\nCzech is pretty weird for a Western-language speaker if you look at it literally. :-)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-10-12 15:39:45', 1786, 0),
(1788, 13, 'Maria', 'm8m@iname.com', 'Urgent Help with EN to CZ small editing of translation.', 'I am flying to Prague for urgent business on Friday. I am intolerant to foods with dairy (milk) products and gluten and was hoping someone could help with a small piece of text to help me in restaurants etc. I have the text in English and a similar text in czech (which needs editing). Could someone help me make the small changes to the czech text to match the english?\r\n\r\nEnglish:\r\n\r\nI have an illness and have to follow a strict gluten free and milk/milk product free diet. I may therefore become very ill if I eat food containing flours or grains of wheat, rye, barley and oats or any milk or milk products.\r\n\r\nI can eat food containing rice, maize, potatoes, all kinds of vegetables and fruit, eggs,  meat and fish  as long as they are not cooked with wheat flour, batter, breadcrumbs or sauce or any milk products.\r\n\r\n&#8298; Does this food contain flour or grains of wheat, rye, barley or oats or any milk products? If you are at all uncertain about what the food contains, please tell me. \r\n\r\n&#8298; Can you recommend a meal / food?\r\n\r\nThank you for your help\r\n\r\n***************\r\nCzech: (this needs to be changed to incorporate the intolerance to milk and milk products as per the enlgish above) \r\n\r\nOnemocněl jsem Celiakií a musím dodrzovat přísnou glutenovou (bezlepkovou) dieu. \r\n\r\nMohl bych vázně onemocnet, kdybych jedl ptraviny obsahující mouku nebo pčeničná zrne, ječmen, oves, ito. \r\n\r\n\r\nObsahují tyto potraviny mouku nebo pseničná ći itna zrna, ječmen, oves? \r\n\r\n\r\nJestlie si nejste jist sloením potravin, prosím řekněte mi to. \r\n\r\n\r\nMohu jíst potraviny obsahující rýi, kukuřici, brambory, zeleninu a ovo&#339;, vajíčka, sýry, mléko, maso a ryby, pokud nejsou připravovány s \r\npeniénou moukou, zásmakou, osmaeným chlebem nebo s omačkou. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDékuji Vám. \r\n\r\n\r\nMany Thanks\r\nMaria\r\nm8m@iname.com\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n', '2005-10-13 07:14:51', 0, 1),
(1789, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Urgent Help with EN to CZ small editing of translation.', 'Hi Maria,\r\n\r\nI\'m not Czech and therefore the above may contain grammatical errors, but considering that your health is at line, I figure it\'s better to have something (as long as it\'s understandable) on time than to have Shakespeare too late.\r\n\r\nThe Czech text cited diverges content-wise in a few spots from your English text; in these spots I\'ve gone by the English text.\r\n\r\nThe second bullet point at the end of the Czech text doesn\'t match your second point at all, but is still perhaps useful. (It reads: if you are not sure of a particular food\'s composition, please say so.) I\'ve included it, as a third bullet point in list.\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\n--\r\n\r\n\r\nTrpím nemocí celiakie a musím proto dodrovat přísnou dietu bezlepkovou a bez mléčných výrobků. Mohla bych toti vázně onemocnet, kdybych jedla potraviny obsahující mouku nebo pčeničné zrno, ječmen, oves, ito, nebo mléčné výrobky. \r\n\r\nNaopak nejsou problémem jídla obsahující rýi, kukuřici, brambory, jakákoli zelenina či ovoce, vejce, nebo rybu - pokud ovem nejsou vařené s peničnou moukou, těstem, osmaeným chlebem atp., ani s omáčkou nebo mléčné výrobky.\r\n\r\n* Obsahují tyto potraviny mouku nebo pseničná či itná zrna? ječmen? oves? \r\n\r\n* Můete doporučit s ohledem na tuto situaci některé jídlo?\r\n\r\n* Jestlie si nejste jist sloením potravin, prosím řekněte mi to. ', '2005-10-13 20:49:05', 1788, 0),
(1790, 13, 'Eva Knight- Malciak', 'evamalciakova@seznam.cz', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'It means The souls of the immortals ones.', '2005-10-16 02:12:12', 1773, 0),
(1791, 13, 'Eva Knight-malciak', '', 'Re: milenka - milenec', 'Hello!\r\nI suppose that \"lover\'in English means, unlike in Czech, somebody having a sexual relationship without neccessary being in love with that person.If you want to imply that there is something emotional going on you would probably use word \"moje laska\"- my love. You wouldn\'t use \"milacek\' when speaking about that person. You use it when talking to that person.So the word milenka in czech is rather a cold word, used  mainly for somebody having an affair when married.( on ma milenku, milence). I hope this helps.\r\n\r\nBest wishes Eva Knight-Malciak\r\n    ', '2005-10-16 02:37:33', 1778, 1),
(1792, 14, 'Ela Szlufik-Szczesna', 'giwia@gazeta.pl', 'looking for a Bohemian pen pal', 'I have Bohemian roots. My ancestors came to Poland from Bohemia in 19c .\r\nI am married, about 40 years old, looking for a pen pal who would like to tell me more about the motherland of my ancestors', '2005-10-16 17:20:58', 0, 1),
