\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Main\r\n      Czech radio station.\r\n      \r\n      </span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"180\" valign=\"top\" style=\"padding-left: 3.5pt; padding-right: 3.5pt; padding-top: 0cm; padding-bottom: 0cm\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:0cm\"><big><b>&nbsp;<span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">\r\n      \r\n      </span></b></big></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"150\" valign=\"bottom\" style=\"padding-left: 3.5pt; padding-right: 3.5pt; padding-top: 0cm; padding-bottom: 0cm\">\r\n      <p class=\"NormalFirst\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><a href=\"http://www.radia.cz\">www.radia.cz</a></span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"297\" valign=\"bottom\" style=\"padding-left: 3.5pt; padding-right: 3.5pt; padding-top: 0cm; padding-bottom: 0cm\">\r\n      <p class=\"NormalFirst\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Links\r\n      to dozens of Czech radios broadcasting over the Internet.\r\n      \r\n      </span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>', '2002-10-12 21:14:23', 1735, 85, 5),
(40, 'Czech and the Computer', 'Dominik Luke', 'How to make your computer display, type and print Czech letters.', '<h1 align=\"center\"><!--webbot bot=\"HTMLMarkup\" startspan --><style type=\"text/css\"><!-- :hover {font-weight:bold; color:red} --></style><!--webbot bot=\"HTMLMarkup\" endspan --><a name=\"_Toc441309773\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech</span></a><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nand the Computer</span></h1>\r\n<h2><span lang=\"EN-US\">Why bother?</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNoIndent\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">If youve decided to take the\r\nplunge and learn Czech, chances are that you will want to type a letter to your\r\nCzech friends, type up a list of words, or perhaps even keep a diary. True, all\r\nthese things can still be done with ink and paper, but so they can be chiseled\r\ninto stone. If you find it difficult to interact with chewed up flattened wood,\r\nyou may appreciate this guide to setting up the computer to be friends with\r\nCzech. All operating systems have their Czech versions, too, which can be quite\r\neducational but also time consuming for beginner. Czech fonts and keyboard are\r\nthen obviously set as the default. In that case, you can use the procedures\r\ndescribed below to make using English more natural.</span></p>\r\n<h2><span lang=\"EN-US\">Fonts?</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNoIndent\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The bone of contention, are\r\nobviously all those Czech letters with accents. There are 14 of them (plus 14\r\nmore if you count, as the computer does, the capital letter separately).\r\nUnfortunately, the original designers of the computing machines (Americans) not\r\nonly failed to foresee the coming of the year 2000, they were also kept in the\r\ndark about the fact that there are more foreign languages than Spanish and\r\nFrench. Thus an entirely new industry sprung up to deal with the mess.\r\nFortunately, along with the unstoppable globalization, the situation has changed\r\ndramatically (mostly for the better). The news is particularly good if your\r\ncomputer runs Windows 9x/NT. In that case, all you have to do is change the\r\nkeyboard (described below) and most common text editors will automatically be\r\nable to write Czech correctly. Things are a little more complicated for\r\nMacintosh lovers and the lost souls plodding into the new millennium with\r\nWindows 3.1. In that case, you have to install additional fonts. They are\r\navailable for free on the Internet, either through Apple or Microsoft, or at\r\nthird party sites, such as <a href=\"http://www.muselik.com/czech/fonts\">http://www.muselik.com/czech/fonts</a>. If money is no\r\nobject you can buy a whole third-party language package from a software vendor,\r\nalthough it may be a little difficult to find in this day and age.</span></p>\r\n<h2><span lang=\"EN-US\">Keyboard</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNoIndent\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">If youre wondering where all\r\nthese characters fit on the keyboard and whether youll have to go through a\r\nCtrl-Alt-876 rigmarole every time you want to type </span><b><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">ř</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">,\r\nthe trick is relatively simply. If you only need to insert a Czech character\r\nonce in a while (such as in an address) you can use the Character Map (a utility\r\nprovided with the operating system), or the Insert/Symbol function in MS Word.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">For\r\nserious typers, however, this is not a viable solution. They have to change the\r\nkeyboard layout. This is a little tricky with Windows 3.1 and the Mac but\r\nWindows NT/9x handle this very easily. Click on the Start button and select\r\nSettings and Control Panel. Then open the Keyboard panel. Click on the Language\r\ntab at the top and then the Add button. Choose Czech and click OK. You may be\r\nasked for the install CD-ROM, so have that ready. <o:p>\r\n</o:p>\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">After\r\nyou have added the layout, you can jump between the two layouts by typing\r\nAlt-Shift, or clicking an icon which will have appeared in the bottom-right\r\ncorner. Make sure you check Enable indicator on taskbar in the Keyboard\r\ncontrol panel. <o:p>\r\n</o:p>\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">The\r\nlayout of the Czech keyboard is very simple. Most of the accented letters are\r\nplaced in the place of numbers and numbers are then typed through Shift. </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Ú\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">replaces </span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">[ and </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">ů</span></i>\r\ndoes away with <span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"></span>;<span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">.\r\nOther diacritics are all displaced though. Experiment with the keyboard layout\r\nto find what is where. Letter such as $ or @ have to be entered through Ctrl-Alt\r\nor you can switch the layouts. Accented capital letter are fairly rare and\r\nthats why they are entered through an intermediate key. For example, to get\r\n</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Ř</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">, type + while the Czech keyboard is on and then R, etc.\r\nFor letters with </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">´</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">,\r\ntype =. The same goes for small </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">ň</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">,\r\n<i>ď</i>, <i></i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"> </span></i>which\r\nare also very rare.<span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"><o:p>\r\n</o:p>\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">To\r\nremember which key is which, it is possible to buy transparent stickers to put\r\non individual keys, or you can invest into a felt tip pen. Computers bought in\r\nthe Czech Republic will automatically come with a keyboard with dual markings.<o:p>\r\n</o:p>\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">Now, the\r\nCzech keyboard layout has one annoying detail: Z and Y are switched. To fix that\r\ngo back to the keyboard panel and select the Czech keyboard, click on the\r\nProperties button and choose Czech (QWERTY). This will set up the Czech keyboard\r\nlayout except for the Y/Z swap. <o:p>\r\n</o:p>\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">For Mac and Win 3.1<span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nEN-US\">, you have to rely on the Internet again. You can find a free program\r\nwhich lets you design your own layout on <a href=\"http://www.sil.org\">http://www.sil.org</a>. If you buy an\r\nindependent package, a keyboard layout program will come with it.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\"><o:p>\r\n</o:p>\r\n</span></p>\r\n<h2><span lang=\"EN-US\">Spelling Checker</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNoIndent\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">To check spelling of your documents\r\nyou have to have the spelling checker for Czech. You can buy a Proofing Package\r\nfor all major text editors for around $50. So far, there is no effective Grammar\r\nChecker for Czech due to the complex system of endings and fluid syntax.\r\nThesauri are not readily available for Czech even in print so if one comes with\r\nyour spelling checker, it might be quite limited.</span></p>\r\n<h2><span lang=\"EN-US\">Online</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"NormalNoIndent\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Even if you dont want to write\r\nanything in Czech, there are so many Czech sites on the Internet, you may find\r\nyourself wishing your browser would display all the accented letters correctly.\r\nSo if you wish to read Czech papers (and all the major ones and many minor are\r\nfully online), this might come useful. First, upgrade to Netscape 4 or above or\r\nInternet Explorer 4 or 5 (both are free). That might actually be enough, because\r\nsome pages may display automatically with the right letters. If they dont,\r\nselect Encoding (or Character Set in Netscape) in the View menu and choose\r\nCentral European (Windows) or (ISO). This should fix the problem. Additionally,\r\nmany Czech sites have a section called </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Kódování</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nwhich lets you choose further options. Mostly, you will not need that. This\r\nsection will come in handy if you decide to cling to your old browser (or if\r\nyour old computer cant cope with it).</span></p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:15:57', 1999, 85, 6),
(41, 'About the Author', '', '', 'A linguist at heart, Dominik has been scrounging up a living teaching Czech language and culture to foreigners for the past eight years. He has worked for the US Peace Corps both in the Czech Republic and other countries as a language specialist. To see what its like for his clients he has traveled and worked in over 20 countries around the world (most recently Kazakhstan). Dominik has contributed several articles on language and culture to various publications. He co-authored a Czech textbook which is used by Collegium Hieronymi Pragensis. Currently, he teaches Czech language at University College London. His latest project is transferring Czech language learning materials on the web. You can read more on Czech grammar and related topics on http://www.bohemica.com.', '2002-10-12 21:16:35', 926, 85, 7),
(43, 'It\'s a challenge', 'Dominik Luke', 'How to translate and use the word \'challenge\'', '<h1><span lang=\"EN-US\">Its a challenge!</span></h1>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">According to some language is the\r\nperfect window to the spirit of a nation and if they are right Americans are\r\nextremely fond of adversity. One of the most popular words in the vocabulary of\r\nyour average expatriate entrepreneur is challenge. He writes it in his\r\nemails home, she shares it with her friends over coffee at the Globe, and he\r\nencourages Czech co-workers to seek it, or at least if things go wrong to use it\r\nas an excuse. Czechs, on the other hand, find coping with this rather\r\nchallenging. If they look up challenge in an average dictionary (and most are\r\nbelow average) they will be told that it means </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">výzva</span><span lang=\"CS\"> </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">which is not\r\nvery good because it is typically used in the context of duels, and although\r\ntodays permissive society offers ample opportunity for defending a ladys\r\nhonor, few people get up early enough. So what other words are there to take up\r\nthe slack? There is the good old </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">problém</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nwhich will take of sentences such as We had to overcome a lot of\r\nchallenges. It wont fit the bill, though, when it comes to facing a\r\nchallenge so you have to substitute </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">úkol </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(task) or </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">obtíný\r\núkol</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(a\r\ndifficult task).</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Another problem will soon rear its ugly\r\nhead if English decides <i>to </i>challenge. There you have to apply your\r\nknowledge of the situation. You can </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">popřít </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(refute), </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">namítnout\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(raise an objection), or </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">brát\r\nv pochybnost </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(to doubt). If you challenge\r\nsomebody to prove their point, you can </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">go back to <i>vyzvat </i></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(invite to) although you\r\nmust be aware that it is fairly strong and may result in a confrontation. It is\r\nalso more formal and better suited to the parliamentary floor rather than a pub\r\nbrawl.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Once you start talking about <i>challenging\r\n</i>things, you invite further hardship. Turning </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">vyzvat\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">into an adjective is no good as it gives you </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">vyzývavý </span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">which\r\nmeans <i>provocative </i>with a sexual subtext. <i>Problematický </i></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">may\r\nbe an alternative but the best option probably is </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">těký\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(difficult).</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">No matter what word you choose in Czech,\r\nremember that it will never have the full meaning such as it might have in a\r\nsentence Its a difficult job for me but I like the challenge. To make\r\nit comprehensible and translatable it would be best to rephrase it into\r\nIts a difficult job for me but I dont mind because it will help me\r\nlearn more or whatever it is you mean by challenge.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">What is the bottom line, then? If\r\nyoure speaking to Czechs, be it formally or informally, avoid challenge\r\nlike the plague. This is especially important for business consultants who come\r\nin and fill company mission statements with challenges and then watch in despair\r\nas the hapless translator butchers their slick piece of prose into an\r\nincomprehensible jumble. So if youre one of these people, heres a\r\nchallenge for you: do without.</span></p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:22:55', 1231, 86, 20),
(44, 'How do you think you\'re going', 'Dominik Luke', 'The complexities of expressing the word go appropriately in Czech!', '<h1><span lang=\"EN-US\">How do you think youre going?</span></h1>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">If Americans wish to transport\r\nthemselves form point A to point B they have it easy. You want to stroll around\r\na bit, all you have to do is get up and go. You want to see a movie, go for it.\r\nYou want to visit your mother in another city? Start your trusted pick-up and\r\ngo. Go, go, go! The word is ubiquitous and universal. Anything needs to get\r\ndone, somebody goes and does it. Not so in Czech. Or rather not so simple.\r\nCzechs need to be much more precise about the means they employ in their comings\r\nand goings. Basically, you need to differentiate between two basic words: </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">jít</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">meaning <i>go </i>and </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">jet</span><span lang=\"CS\"> </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">meaning, yes, you\r\nguessed it, <i>go</i>. The difference is that while </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">jít</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">is confined to goings that take place on foot,\r\nwhile <i>jet </i>is used for when you go by some sort of vehicle be it car,\r\nbike, train or horse. That remains true as long as you remain on the ground. As\r\nsoon as you get your head in the clouds or set out on the water, </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">letět\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(fly) and </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">plout\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(float) become a necessity. So while in English <i>Im\r\ngoing to the park. Im going to </i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Brno</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">.\r\nIm going back to America. </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">make do with simple <i>go</i>,\r\nin Czech, you can have much more fun with: </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Jdu\r\ndo parku. Jedu do Brna. Letím do Ameriky. </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">If you\r\nsay, as students of Czech so often do</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">,\r\n<i>Jdu do Brna</i>, </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">you imply that you intend to walk\r\nthere, which is amusing to Czechs, especially when asserted in Prague.\r\nSometimes, you can use one verb as the generic substitute. For example, going to\r\nthe movies, concerts and discos will use </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">jít</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">regardless of your means of transportation, and to\r\ngo to London will use <i>jet </i>despite the fact that both plane and boat may\r\nbe used.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">These verbs are called verbs of motion\r\nand they are in a class of their own. They have a special future tense and they\r\nare very generous with the use of prefixes. This is where you can see what a\r\nwonderful Lego-like language Czech is. While in English, you have <i>go, arrive,\r\nleave</i>, Czech simply adds a directional prefix to the verb in question,\r\nnamely<i> </i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">při- </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">for\r\ndirection to and </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">od-</span><span lang=\"CS\"> </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">for direction away\r\nfrom, ending with </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">přijít\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(come on foot), </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">odejít </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(leave on foot), </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">přijet\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(arrive by vehicle), </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">odjet\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(leave by vehicle), </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">přiletět</span><span lang=\"CS\"> </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(arrive by\r\nplane), and so on and so forth. There are also prefixes for going over, under,\r\naround, running away, going just a little bit and coming together, but that is\r\nanother story.</span></p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:24:17', 1374, 86, 30);
INSERT INTO `xc_articles` (`xca_id`, `xca_name`, `xca_author`, `xca_perex`, `xca_text`, `xca_time`, `xca_readers`, `xca_section`, `xca_priority`) VALUES
(45, 'The daily <i>chleba</i> and <i>rohlík</i>', 'Dominik Luke', 'Bread may seem simple enough but Czechs are not simple people. All the different words that could be translated into English as <i>bread</i>.', '<h1 style=\"margin-top:0cm\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The daily </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">chleba</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">and </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">rohlík</span></i></h1>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The tendency of Americans to call nearly\r\neverything made of dough bread is unbelievable to a Czech. Their British cousins\r\nare a little more discerning but still fall short of the Czech standard. I have\r\ncounted no fewer than nine different words that are commonly used and which\r\ncould be translated basically as bread. If you look in a dictionary, the\r\ntranslation of bread will be </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">chléb</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">and textbooks typically start you off with the\r\ncolloquial version </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">chleba</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">.\r\nBut English is prone to apply the term <i>bread </i>to anything made of dough\r\nthat isnt sweet. Although the term bread-roll is available it is rarely\r\npulled out of the lexical hat. All in all Americans will ask for bread and be\r\ncontent with basically anything that doesnt actually have whipped cream on\r\nit. Not so Czechs. If you want to name bakers products accurately, you have\r\nto master a combination of criteria depending on the type of dough, shape and\r\nmethod of assembling the shape. Heres a list of the most important ones.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">There is, of course, </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">chleba</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(a.k.a.</span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">\r\nchléb</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">) which can be <i>kulatý</i>\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(round)</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">,\r\n<i>tmavý </i></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(dark)</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">,\r\n<i>celozrnný </i></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(whole-grain) and many others.\r\nThats where it stops. Everything else is subsumed under the category of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">pečivo\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">which can be loosely translated as bread that\r\ncannot be translated as</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\"> </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">chleba</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">.\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">The two key representatives of this category are </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">rohlík</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"> and </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">houska</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">. They are made of the same dough but </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">rohlík\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">is sort of an elongated roll bent into a slight\r\nC-shape (and is often translated as roll) while </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">houska</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nis round, bun-shaped made by weaving together strips of dough. </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Houska\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">has a variant called </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">emle\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">which is a true bun with a star-shape cut into its\r\ntop. For those venturing East of Prague it may be important to know that\r\nMoravians do not know the word </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">houska\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">and use <i>bulka </i>instead.\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">If </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">houska</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nor </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">rohlík</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">are made of dark bread-like dough, they will be\r\ncalled </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">dalamánek</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">.\r\nWhole-grain <i>r</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">ohlík</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">s have recently become popular under the\r\nname </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">kornpic</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">.\r\n<i>Rohlík</i>s </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">made of slightly sweet dough are\r\ncalled </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">loupák</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">. There is also a larger version of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">houska</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">going under the name of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">pletenka </span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">or <i>pletenec</i></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">.\r\nThere are two kinds of baguette: one made of slightly heavier dough is called </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">veka</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">and the other, slightly lighter, is a comparative new\r\ncomer and is called familiarly </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">bageta</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">.\r\nIf you slice <i>veka</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\"> </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">and\r\nplump potato salad, pickles and ham you will create </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">chlebíček\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">a sort of cholesterol\r\nshot extremely popular in Czech delis. <o:p>\r\n</o:p>\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Chleba </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">has\r\ngreat importance for the Czech culture. There are at least 86 proverbs, sayings\r\nand superstitions containing the word mostly as metaphor for livelihood. One\r\nsaying </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Vude je chleba o\r\ndvou kůrkách</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(Everywhere\r\nthe bread has two crusts) is imparting to us the great truth that life is\r\ndifficult everywhere. It also shows that Czech proverbs dont always know what\r\ntheyre talking about. On Czech bread, there are two sorts of crust </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">(<i>kůrka</i>):\r\nthe upper, brown and crunchy is the good part, that everybody likes and the\r\nlower which the bread lay on while being baked and is not nearly as exciting to\r\neat.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"> On my travels, I have always been struck by how\r\nmany countries break that rule. Indeed, as Im writing this, in good old\r\nLondon, Im munching on a slice so singularly crustless that it would have an\r\nold Czechs head spinning with nausea for a week.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czechs are proud of their bread and\r\nconsider sliced bread an abomination rather than a benchmark for the greatness\r\nof inventions. There is a proverb </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">Kdo se nesrovná s&nbsp;chlebem,</span><span lang=\"CS\"> </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">nesrovná\r\nse s&nbsp;lidmi</span><span lang=\"CS\"> </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(If you\r\ncannot make peace with bread, you cannot make peace with people) meaning that if\r\nyou cannot cut bread properly youre a no good blackguard<i>. </i>Especially\r\nin the past, bread had a near-religious significance. As in many cultures,\r\nvisitors were welcomed with loaf of bread salt (a custom still being revived at\r\nofficial functions) and it was a portent of bad luck if somebody broke a piece\r\noff a loaf. Presumably, making a bread dip in the last century would have you\r\nburnt at the stake.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">It is therefore a wise visitor who\r\nfollows these bread-related dos and donts. Never refer to anything but </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">chleba</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">as bread. Never spread butter on the round part of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">rohlík</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">, </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">thats\r\nwhat the flat part is for, unless you want to look very very stupid. Dont\r\nimply that there is no difference between </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">houska</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">and </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">rohlík</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nbecause there is. </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">And finally, never ever say that <i>knedlík </i></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">is\r\nbread. It is a wrong and heretic statement which should have you sent back to\r\nwherever you came from. But that is another story.</span></p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:25:41', 1506, 86, 40),
(46, 'A piece of <i>koláč</i>!', 'Dominik Luke', 'All the words for sweets and pastries important in the Czech culture.', '<h1><span lang=\"EN-US\">A Piece of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">Koláč</span></i></h1>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Koláč</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">or rather kolac (pronounced koh-lahtch) is one of the\r\nfew contributions Czech has made to the English vocabulary, albeit only used in\r\nselected regions of the United States (i.e. Minnesota, Texas or Nebraska). It\r\ncan be likened to a sweet two-inch pizza, meaning that it has a large crust and\r\na filling made of some sweet substance such as plum-jam, sweet cream cheese, or\r\npoppy-seed. It has to be made of yeast-based dough and therefore cannot be\r\nreally be translated as <i>cake</i> even though that is its most commonly used\r\nEnglish equivalent.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">Also\r\nthe English idiom <i>a piece of cake </i>seems to imply that it is something\r\neasy, on the other hand, the Czech saying </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">Bez práce nejsou koláče </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(Without work,\r\nthere are no cakes) makes it clear that without a good amount of sweat and toil,\r\nnobody can enjoy their </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">koláč</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">.\r\nAnother controversial saying </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Koláč\r\nse rychle přejí, ale chleba nikdy </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">states that\r\nwhile you never grow tired of bread, eating </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">koláč</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">es\r\nevery day will get old pretty fast. Well, I dont know.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">As I said, </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">koláč</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">is limited to very specific type of pastry but\r\nthere are many other kinds of sweets that exact their influence on Czech teeth.\r\nTo stick to the pizza metaphor, a calzone equivalent of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">koláč\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">is </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">buchta\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">which is basically the same thing except that the\r\nfilling is inside and it is cube-shaped. The filling can be </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">tvaroh </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(sweet cheese curds), </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">povidla\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(special plum-made jam) and </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">mák\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(poppy seed). In fact, the latter is so popular\r\nthat the Czech settlers in the new world gave rise to a saying that you can\r\nalways tell a Czech by the poppy seed in their teeth. </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Buchta</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nplays an important role in Czech mythology. The Czech dullard turned princess\r\ngetter was always equipped with a pack of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">buchta</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">s<i> </i>which\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">he then traded in for a magic sword at the nearest\r\nturn by being nice to an old man and sharing his lunch. Yes, it is true, </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">buchta</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\ncan be served as full lunch which can be baffling to a foreigner used to his\r\nBigMac with large fries.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The closest Czech comes to a cake is </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">dort</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">.\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">Most cakes (except perhaps fish cakes) can be\r\ntranslated as </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">dort</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">.\r\nIt is what you get on your birthday (occasionally with a girl in bikini baked\r\ninto it) to blow off candles on and share with your friends. There is not much\r\nthat can be said about it because it comes in so many varieties, mostly\r\ncircular, and with special kind of cake dough called </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">pikot </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">as its base.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Of special importance are two seasonal\r\npastries that are extremely important. They are </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">vánočka\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">a woven loaf-size cake which is an indispensable\r\ndecoration of the Czech Christmas table. And </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">mazanec\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">round Easter cake made of the same dough but in\r\nthe shape of a round loaf of bread. Easter has another sweet called </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Beránek</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n(little lamb) a lamb shaped cake made of slightly different dough than </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">mazanec</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">.\r\nChristmas is of course also the time of baking cookies called </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">vánoční\r\ncukroví </span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">which comes in\r\nall shapes and sizes.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">That is not the limit of delights\r\navailable to the Czech sweets-lovers. First, there is a variety of pastries.\r\nYour friendly neighborhood bakery will supply you with </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">kobliha\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">a doughnut with filling inside, or </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">vdolek\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>with\r\nthe filling on top. <i>áteček </i>(also the name for a little triangular\r\nscarf) </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">is just like </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">buchta\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">but in a triangular shape. <i>H</i></span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">řeben </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">is a comb-shaped </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">áteček</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nnot very popular with bald people but otherwise a bakery staple. There are also\r\ntwo popular foreigners on the pastry shelves </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">croisant</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"> and </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">piroh </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">plus many many others that havent\r\nreceived widely recognized names.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">If you want to say youve been to the\r\nCzech Republic you should not omit</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\"> </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">trůdl</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">\r\nofficially called <i>jablečný</i> <i>závin</i></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nwhich is a delicious apple pie the Czechs share with Germans. To neglect </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">bábovka\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">in your studies would be a sin. It is a popular\r\ngrandmothers cake as the name suggests and is a frequent accompaniment to a\r\ncup coffee and grandmotherly interrogation.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">If you want to\r\ndisplay indepth knowledge of Czech culture, learn about two regional\r\nspecialities. <i>Hořické trubičky</i></span><span lang=\"CS\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">are\r\nwafer tubes made in the East-Bohemian town of </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Hořice</span></i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">\r\nand very popular with the countrys youths. Another one is <i>perník</i></span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(ginger-bread) which is what the city of </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Pardubice</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">excels in alongside the slightly more famous semtex.\r\nGingerbread hearts available at country fairs are a popular way of expressing\r\naffection to the opposite sex and if you describe your latest victory over a\r\ndrunk in the pub to a Czech audience with the words </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Ukázal\r\njsem mu zač je v&nbsp;Pardubicích perník </span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">(I\r\nshowed him how much </span><i><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">perník</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">is in </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Pardubice)</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nthey wont even know you came from another planet.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Many of these\r\nwords and sweets they denote date back to the 14th century and they hold a\r\nspecial place in Czech heart and occasionally stomachs.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nIf you really want to understand what being a Czech is all about go out and try\r\nthem all. Its a </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">sweet\r\nsweet world.\r\n</span></p>', '2002-10-12 21:26:59', 1812, 86, 50),
(47, 'Nobody\'s perfect', 'Dominik Luke', 'English has an abundance of tenses while Czech only has three. How can you handle the perfect tenses.', '<h1><span lang=\"EN-US\">Nobodys perfect</span></h1>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech beats English on cases seven nil\r\nbut suffers an ignominious defeat of 3:14 when the game turns to tenses. Czech\r\nhas to do with past, present and future where English has four kinds of each\r\nplus two extras in the futures department. For one present in Czech, English\r\nboasts present simple, present progressive, present perfect and present perfect\r\nprogressive. Perfects, as you can see, play a significant role in the tense\r\nmake-up of Shakespeares tongue and they are surprisingly lacking from Czech.\r\nDoes it make any difference when youre learning Czech? Damn right! It would\r\nbe extremely surprising if it didnt. It makes it difficult to say things like\r\n<i>Ive lived here for a year</i> (Present Perfect) and impossible to even\r\napproach saying <i>When I come, the dishes will have been washed </i>(Future\r\nPerfect).</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">That is not to say that Czech is\r\ndeficient in some way but goes about saying things in a different way.</span></p>', '2002-10-12 21:28:19', 1671, 86, 60),
(48, '%7', '', '', '', '2002-10-12 21:30:15', 1777, 19, 100),
(49, 'Czech Republic Quick Facts', 'Dominik Luke', 'The basic statistics and facts about Czech geography, population and politics.', ' <table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"margin-left:-10.7pt;\r\n border-collapse:collapse;mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n    <tr>\r\n      <td width=\"321\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:240.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">&nbsp;\r\n        <p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n        <table border=\"0\" width=\"100%\">\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\r\n              <p align=\"center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><img src=\"images/czechculture/history/czech_flag.jpg\" v:shapes=\"_x0000_i1025\" width=\"154\" height=\"95\"></b><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:\r\n  12.0pt\"><br>\r\n              Czech Flag</span></b></td>\r\n          </center>\r\n          <td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\r\n            <p align=\"right\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:\r\n  12.0pt\">9  16 centuries<br>\r\n            Czech Kingdom</span></i></p>\r\n            <p align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:\r\n  12.0pt\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">1600s  1918<br>\r\n            part of Habsburg Empir</i><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal; font-size: 14.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt\">e</i></span></td>\r\n        </tr>\r\n      </table>\r\n      <center>\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"right\" style=\"margin-left:120.5pt;text-align:right\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"right\" style=\"margin-left:124.1pt;text-align:right\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n      </center></td>\r\n      <td width=\"307\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:230.25pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"center\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:EN-US\">&nbsp;</span></i><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:\r\n  12.0pt\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span></span></b><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:\r\n&quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">Geography Quick Facts</span></b></p>\r\n        <table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"margin-left:3.5pt;\r\n border-collapse:collapse;mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:2.0cm;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\">Name:</b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"208\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:155.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\">Czech\r\n              Republic (&#268;eská republika)</p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:2.0cm;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\">Regions:</b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"208\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:155.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\">Bohemia\r\n              (&#268;echy), Moravia</p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:2.0cm;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\">Area:</b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"208\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:155.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\">78,864\r\n              sq km (30,450 sq miles)</p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:2.0cm;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\">Bordering countries:</b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"208\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:155.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\">Austria,\r\n              Germany, Poland,<br>\r\n              Slovakia</p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:2.0cm;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\">Capital:</b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"208\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:155.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\">Praha\r\n              (Prague)  1,214,000 inhabitants</p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"76\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:2.0cm;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\">Major Cities:</b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"208\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:155.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\">Brno\r\n               388,000<br>\r\n              Ostrava  327,000<br>\r\n              Plze&#328;  173,000</td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n        </table></td>\r\n    </tr>\r\n    <tr>\r\n      <td width=\"321\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:240.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"center\" style=\"margin-top:0cm;text-align:center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Arial;\r\nmso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">Politics Quick Facts</span></b></p>\r\n        <table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"margin-left:3.5pt;\r\n border-collapse:collapse;mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:99.25pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Government type:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"151\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:4.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">parliamentary\r\n              democracy</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:99.25pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">President:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"151\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:4.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Václav\r\n              Havel (no party)</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:99.25pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Prime Minister:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"151\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:4.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Milo\r\n              Zeman (&#268;SSD)</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:99.25pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Chairman of Parliament:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"151\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:4.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Václav\r\n              Klaus (ODS)</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:99.25pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Leading Party:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"151\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:4.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">&#268;SSD\r\n              (Social Democratic Party) 32%</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"132\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:99.25pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Leading Opposition Party:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"151\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:4.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">ODS\r\n              (Civic Democratic Party) 27%</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n        </table></td>\r\n      <td width=\"307\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:230.25pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:left\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:left\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:14.0pt;\r\nmso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">1918  1938 Independent\r\n        </i><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal; font-size: 14.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 10.0pt\">s</i><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">tate</i><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal; font-size: 14.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 10.0pt\">\r\n        -&nbsp;</i><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\"> Czechoslovakia</i></span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt\">1938\r\n         1945 Czech and Moravian Protectorate</span></i></p>\r\n        <p>&nbsp;</td>\r\n    </tr>\r\n    <tr>\r\n      <td width=\"321\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:240.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"right\" style=\"margin-top:24.0pt;margin-right:0cm;\r\nmargin-bottom:0cm;margin-left:-7.1pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:right\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt\"><br>\r\n        1948  1989 Czechoslovak Socialist Republic governed by Communist\r\n        Party</span></i></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"right\" style=\"text-align:right\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\">1989\r\n        uprising initiated by student demonstration</i><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal; font-size: 14.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 10.0pt\">s</i><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\"> topples communist regime</i></span></p>\r\n        <p>&nbsp;</td>\r\n      <td width=\"307\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:230.25pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"center\" style=\"margin-top:0cm;text-align:center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Arial;\r\nmso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">Demographic Quick Facts</span></b></p>\r\n        <table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"margin-left:3.5pt;\r\n border-collapse:collapse;mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Population:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">10,000,000\r\n              people</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Language:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Nationalities:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech\r\n              (94%), Slovak (2%), Romany/Gypsy (2-3%), Polish (0.6%), German\r\n              (0.5%)</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Religion:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">atheist\r\n              (40%), Roman Catholic (40%), Protestant (5%), other (16%)</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Life Expectancy:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">male\r\n              70.4 years<br>\r\n              female 77.3 years</span></td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n        </table>\r\n        <p>&nbsp;</td>\r\n    </tr>\r\n    <tr>\r\n      <td width=\"321\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:240.95pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"center\" style=\"margin-top:0cm;text-align:center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Arial;\r\nmso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">Economy Quick Facts</span></b></p>\r\n        <table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"margin-left:3.5pt;\r\n border-collapse:collapse;mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">GDP:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">US$27,500\r\n              million</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Currency:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech\r\n              Crown (K</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">&#269;</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">)<br>\r\n              (app. US$1 = 30 K</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">&#269;</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">)</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Major Industries</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">fuels,\r\n              ferrous metallurgy, machinery and equipment, motor vehicles,\r\n              glass, textile, chemicals, armaments</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n          <tr>\r\n            <td width=\"95\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:70.9pt;background:#F2F2F2;mso-shading:\r\n  white;mso-pattern:gray-5 black;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Agriculture:</span></b></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n            <td width=\"189\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:5.0cm;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n              <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:3.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">grains,\r\n              potatoes, sugar beets, hops, fruit, hogs, cattle, poultry, forest\r\n              products</span></p>\r\n            </td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n        </table>\r\n        <p>&nbsp;</td>\r\n      <td width=\"307\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:230.25pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:24.0pt;text-align:left\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:14.0pt;\r\nmso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt\">1993  Slovakia breaks away from Czechoslovakia</span></i></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt\">since\r\n        1993  Czech Republic</span></i></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"center\" style=\"text-align:center\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><img src=\"images/czechculture/history/cr_crest.jpg\" v:shapes=\"_x0000_i1025\" width=\"83\" height=\"101\"></span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"center\" style=\"text-align:center\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\nnormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt\">Czech\r\n        Crest</span></b></p>\r\n        <p>&nbsp;</td>\r\n    </tr>\r\n  </table>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:39:38', 10106, 31, 10),
(50, 'Czech Geography', 'Dominik Luke', 'Elementary facts about Czech geography.', '<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The Czech Republic is situated\r\napproximately in the geographical center of Europe and has an area of 78,866 sq.\r\nkm. It is a landlocked country 326 km from the Baltic and 322 km from the\r\nAdriatic see. It shares borders with Germany (810 km), Poland (762 km), Austria\r\n(466 km) and Slovakia (265 km). The highest point of elevation is the peak of <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">Mt.\r\nSn&#283;ka</i> (1,602 m above sea level) and the lowest point of elevation is\r\nnear Holensko where the River Labe leaves Czech territory (117 m above sea level).</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"NormalFirst\" align=\"center\" style=\"margin-top:0cm;margin-right:-35.55pt;\r\nmargin-bottom:0cm;margin-left:-35.45pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:center\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n<img src=\"images/czechculture/history/cr_map.gif\" v:shapes=\"_x0000_i1025\" width=\"681\" height=\"429\"></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">&nbsp;Czech Republic is divided into Bohemia\r\n</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">(<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">&#268;echy</i>)\r\nand Moravia (<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">Morava</i>). </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">The\r\nlanguage and culture are similar, although some parts of Moravia are known for\r\ntheir specific accent. The Moravian culture is generally more friendly and laid\r\nback than that of the people in Bohemia. Moravia is also predominantly wine\r\ndrinking while most beer is produced and drunk in Bohemia.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The capital of Bohemia is Prague (<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">Praha</i>)\r\nand the capital of Moravia is <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">Brno</i>,\r\nalthough it has no special administrative status.</span></p>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size: 11.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 10.0pt; mso-fareast-font-family: Times New Roman; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-fareast-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-language: AR-SA\">Administratively\r\nCzech Republic is divided into regions (<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">kraj</i>)\r\nwhich in turn consist of counties (<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">okres</i>),\r\neach with an administrative center. The structure and powers of local government\r\nas well as the geographic layout of regions and counties is a topic of much\r\npolitical debate.</span>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:41:59', 7630, 31, 20);
INSERT INTO `xc_articles` (`xca_id`, `xca_name`, `xca_author`, `xca_perex`, `xca_text`, `xca_time`, `xca_readers`, `xca_section`, `xca_priority`) VALUES
(51, 'Top 10 Figures of Czech History', 'Dominik Luke', '', '<table style=\"BORDER-COLLAPSE: collapse; mso-table-layout-alt: fixed; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" border=\"0\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>1.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nSvatý Václav<br />\r\n(St. Wenceslas  900s)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Patron of the Czech lands. Peace-loving king opposing war with the Germans, killed by his brother Boleslav. According to legend he and his soldiers lie under the mountain Blan</span>\r\n <span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">ík </span>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">and will rise to help the Czech nation in the gravest hour of need.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>2.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nKarel IV. (Charles IV.) (1316  1378)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Holy Roman emperor (135578) and founder of<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>\r\nCharles University (1348). Under his rule, Czech lands were the political and cultural center of Europe.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>3.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nJan Hus<br />\r\n(1372 - 1415)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Reform preacher and linguist, author of modern Czech spelling. Burned at stake for heresy. Inspired the Hussite movement.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>4.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nJan ika (d. 1424)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Successful military leader of the Hussite forces. Under him, Czech rebels defeated crusaders sent by the Roman emperor. Famous for directing battles even being blind.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>5.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nJan Ámos Komenský (Comenius)<br />\r\n(15921670)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Famous educator and bishop of the Czech Brethren, sometimes nicknamed the Teacher of the nations. Wrote the first modern compendium of pedagogy. Was exiled during 30-Year War and worked on the educational systems of Sweden and Holland.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>6.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nAntonín Dvořák<br />\r\n(1841-1904)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">World-famous composer. Spent several years in the United States, where he wrote his most famous symphony From the New World.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>7.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nT. G. Masaryk<br />\r\n(1850-1937)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Politician and philosopher. First president (1918-35) of the pre-war Czechoslovakia, founded at the end of World War I after the collapse of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>8.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nJaroslav Haek<br />\r\n(1883-1923)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Popular Czech writer and humorist, author of the Good Soldier vejk, a&nbsp;book translated into over 20 languages.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>9.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>\r\nKarel Čapek<br />\r\n(1890-1938)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Leading novelist and playwright of the First Republic. Coined the word robot in his play R.U.R.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 14.2pt; TEXT-INDENT: -14.2pt; TEXT-ALIGN: left; mso-list: l31 level1 lfo5; tab-stops: list 14.2pt\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>10.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\"> </span>\r\nVáclav Havel<br />\r\n(b. 1936)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">First president of Czechoslovakia after the fall of communism. Human rights fighter and playwright.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"></td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 124pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"165\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"TEXT-ALIGN: left\" align=\"left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><b>Jára Cimrman<br />\r\n(1865  1898 <br />\r\n<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp;</span>\r\n 1915  1927)</b>\r\n </span>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"PADDING-RIGHT: 3.5pt; PADDING-LEFT: 3.5pt; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0cm; WIDTH: 336.6pt; PADDING-TOP: 0cm\" valign=\"top\" width=\"449\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">A fictious figure from the turn of the century, attributed with most famous inventions. Cimrman was created in the late 60s by a group of Czech actors who produce plays in his name. Gained enormous popularity on the scale of Monty Python in Great Britain.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p>When this list was drawn up in 1999, I was going purely by instinct as to who the important people are. And now, in June 2005, I finally had a chance to see if my instinct was right, when Czech TV ran a national poll to find the greatest Czech of all time (see <a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech\">http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech</a>\r\n ). And by and large, I was correct.&nbsp;Only two of mine don\'t appear in the Czech TV top 10. They are St. Wenceslas and Karel Haek who ranked 17 and 38 respectively. Jan Werich and Boena Němcová were very near the top of my list back then but I excluded them based on of how much interest they are to an international audience. Those who can read Czech, can read more about all the winners on these links.</p>\r\n<ol><li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_kareliv\"><strong>Karel IV.</strong>\r\n </a>\r\n</li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_masaryk\"><strong>Tomá Garrigue Masaryk</strong>\r\n </a>\r\n</li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_havel\"><strong>Václav Havel</strong>\r\n </a>\r\n</li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_komensky\">Jan Ámos Komenský</a>\r\n </li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_zizka\">Jan ika</a>\r\n </li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_werich\">Jan Werich</a>\r\n </li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_hus\">Jan Hus</a>\r\n </li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_dvorak\">Antonín Dvořák</a>\r\n </li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_capek\">Karel Čapek</a>\r\n </li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/dokumenty_osobnosti_nemcova\">Boena Němcová</a>\r\n </li>\r\n</ol>\r\n<p>There is also the greatest villains poll that might be of interest <a href=\"http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/padouch\">http://www.czech-tv.cz/specialy/nejvetsicech/padouch</a>\r\n&nbsp;headed by the first communist president Klement Gottwald with the last communist president Gustáv Husák at number 10. Interestingly, the current president and former prime minister Václav Klaus famous as the chief architecht of the Czech privatization and a populist Eurosceptic made it to number 3 (although he\'s also at number 18 of the greatest Czechs) and Stanislav Gross (a prime minister from 9/2004 to 4/2005) is at number 2.<br />\r\n<br />\r\n</p>\r\n<h3>3 Famous people born in the Czech Republic</span />\r\n </h3>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 18pt; TEXT-INDENT: -18pt; mso-list: l19 level1 lfo6; tab-stops: list 18.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>\r\n Madeleine Albright  American Secretary of State born in Moravia</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"MARGIN-LEFT: 18pt; TEXT-INDENT: -18pt; mso-list: l19 level1 lfo6; tab-stops: list 18.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">2.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>\r\n Sigmund Freud  Austrian psychologist born in P</span>\r\n<span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: CS\">říbor.</span>\r\n </p>\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\">3.<span style=\"FONT-WEIGHT: normal; FONT-SIZE: 7pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; FONT-STYLE: normal; FONT-VARIANT: normal\" new=\"\" times=\"\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>\r\n </span>\r\nTom Stoppard  English dramatist born in Zlín.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nMore famous people born in the Czech lands&nbsp;suggested by Jirka Hana:<br />\r\n<ul><li>Franz Kafka, Milan Kundera (writers) </li>\r\n<li>Gustav Mahler (composer) </li>\r\n<li>F. Porsche (automobile designer) </li>\r\n<li>Georg Mendel (founder of the field of genetics) </li>\r\n<li>K. Goedel, G. Gentzen (mathematicians) </li>\r\n<li>Edmund Husserl (philosopher) </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>Some other famous people with roots in the Czech lands are John Kerry, the failed presidential candidate in the 2004 US elections and Ray Kroc (Croc), the founder of MacDonalds. <br />\r\n<br />\r\nIt is important to remember, however, that most of these people have their roots in the now nonexistent German or German Jewish community either in Prague or in Moravia and had very limited links to Czech culture (although Kafka made it to 55 and Freud to 98 on the greatest Czech poll).<br />\r\n<br />\r\n</p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:44:02', 10279, 31, 30),
(52, 'Most important events in Czech history', 'Dominik Luke', 'Century by century and zooming in on the last 100 years. Also describes the importance of number 8 for Czech history of the last two centuries.', '<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">Century by Century Break-Down of Czech History</span></h3>\r\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;\r\n mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;border:none;mso-border-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\" bgcolor=\"#C0C0C0\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Century</span></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-left:none;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\" bgcolor=\"#C0C0C0\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech Lands</span></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-left:none;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\" bgcolor=\"#C0C0C0\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Rest of the World</span></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">up\r\n      to 1100s</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Celts,\r\n      Germanic Tribes, Slavic Tribes, The Great Moravia, P&#345;emyslids,\r\n      introduction of literacy (Cyril and Methodius), introduction of (western)\r\n      Christianty,</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Tribes\r\n      moving around, spread of Christianity over Europe</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1200</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech\r\n      kingdom-Czech rulers obtain hereditary right to the title King, P&#345;emysl\r\n      Otokar II. Czech king controlling large parts of Central Europe</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Wars,\r\n      famines, crusades</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1300</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Charles\r\n      IV., Charles University founded, Bible translated into Czech</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Renaissance\r\n      starting, 100-year war, Chaucer</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1400</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Burning\r\n      of Jan Hus, 1<sup>st</sup> defenestration, Hussite wars, Ji&#345;í z&nbsp;Pod&#283;brads\r\n      peace mission</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Joan\r\n      of Arc, America discovered, invention of printing press</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1500</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Polish\r\n      and Hapsburg rulers, arts and sciences flourish, new translation of Bible\r\n      sets standard for Czech language</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">America\r\n      being explored, Luther</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1600</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Comenius,\r\n      2<sup>nd</sup> defenestration, big execution, exile, begining of\r\n      thirty-year war, exile of Czech nobles, germanization</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Shakespeare,\r\n      revolutions, 30-year war</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1700</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Under\r\n      the Hapsburgs, baroque and rather dull times</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">French\r\n      revolution, rise of Napoleon, USA gains independence</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1800</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Fighting\r\n      for more independence, national revival, toying with the idea of\r\n      panslavism, still under the Hapsburgs, National Theater built (twice)</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Fall\r\n      of Napoleon, more wars and crystalization of modern nations</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left;page-break-after:avoid\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1900</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">New\r\n      republic together with Slovakia, split with Slovakia, WWII, communism,\r\n      fall of communism, split from Slovakia</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">WWI\r\n      &amp; II, Cold War, crises and conflicts</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<h3 style=\"page-break-before:always\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">20<sup>th</sup> Century\r\nin the Czech Lands</span></h3>\r\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;\r\n mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;border:none;mso-border-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\" bgcolor=\"#C0C0C0\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Period</span></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-left:none;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\" bgcolor=\"#C0C0C0\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech Lands</span></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-left:none;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\" bgcolor=\"#C0C0C0\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:\r\n  normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Rest of the World</span></b></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1918  1938</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">So-called\r\n      First Republic, time of economic prosperity and democracy</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">recovering\r\n      from WWI</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1938</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Losing\r\n      Sudetenland, Slovakia separated</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">gathering\r\n      clouds, Austria Annexed by Germany</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1939</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Protectorate\r\n      starts: annexed by Germany results: closed University</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Preparing\r\n      for showdown: non-aggression pact between Russia and Germany, Germany\r\n      attacks Poland, Brittain and France declare war</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1945</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Prague\r\n      insurgence (Praské povstání), end of war, repatriation of Germans\r\n      from Sudeten Land</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Berlin\r\n      falls, atomic bomb used in Hiroshima and Nagasaki</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1948</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Communists\r\n      take over government (having won elections in 1946)</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">State\r\n      of Israel proclaimed, Marshall Plan begins</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1950s</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Hard\r\n      core communism</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">McCarthism\r\n      in USA, Stalins death, Korean War</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1968</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Following\r\n      a liberal trend of the 60s so-called Prague spring ends in an invasion by\r\n      allied forces</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Martin\r\n      Luther King killed, Vietnam war peaks (10.000<sup>th</sup> plane shot\r\n      down), R. Kennedy shot</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1970s</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Normalization,\r\n      Charter 77</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Man\r\n      on Moon, oil shock, end of Vietnam war, war in Cambodia</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1989</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Velvet\r\n      Revolution</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Communism\r\n      not doing well world wide: fall in Romania, E. Germany, Hungary, Bulgaria\r\n      and Poland, George Bush becomes president</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td width=\"99\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:74.4pt;border:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1993</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"310\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:232.6pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Split\r\n      with Slovakia</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"205\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:153.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:\r\n  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .5pt;border-right:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;mso-border-left-alt:solid windowtext .5pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Bill\r\n      Clinton becomes president</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">The importance of number 8 in modern Czech history</span></h3>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:18.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l18 level1 lfo7;\r\ntab-stops:list 18.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1918</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n Foundation of Czechoslovakia after the collapse of the Austro-Hungarian\r\nEmpire.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:18.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l18 level1 lfo7;\r\ntab-stops:list 18.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">2.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1938</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n Slovakia breaks off, start of the so-called Second Republic.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:18.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l18 level1 lfo7;\r\ntab-stops:list 18.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">3.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1948</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n Communist government takes power for 41 years.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:18.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l18 level1 lfo7;\r\ntab-stops:list 18.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">4.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1968</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n Prague reforms stopped by invasion of allied armies of East-Bloc countries.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:18.0pt;text-indent:-18.0pt;mso-list:l18 level1 lfo7;\r\ntab-stops:list 18.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">5.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n</span></span><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1989</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n Velvet Revolution caused the fall of the communist government.</span></p>\r\n<p align=\"left\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:47:06', 7191, 31, 40);
INSERT INTO `xc_articles` (`xca_id`, `xca_name`, `xca_author`, `xca_perex`, `xca_text`, `xca_time`, `xca_readers`, `xca_section`, `xca_priority`) VALUES
(53, 'Czech National Prides', 'Dominik Luke', 'Famous writers, composers, politicians and other things Czechs are proud about.', '<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">Overview</span></h3>\r\n<table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;\r\n mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;mso-padding-alt:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt\">\r\n  <tr style=\"height:36.0pt\">\r\n    <td width=\"165\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:124.0pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"TableHeading2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Points of national pride</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"449\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:336.6pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Beer, ice hockey, soccer,\r\n      classical music, jazz, humor, women, Czech, ingenuity, history, science,\r\n      film, architecture, Prague, food.</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr style=\"height:36.0pt\">\r\n    <td width=\"165\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:124.0pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"TableHeading2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Culture</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"449\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:336.6pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Literature:\r\n      </span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">Jaroslav Haek, Karel &#268;apek, Bohumil\r\n      Hrabal, Ludvík Vaculík, Josef kvorecký, Milan Kundera, Jaroslav\r\n      Seifert, Václav Havel.</span></p>\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Film:\r\n      </span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">Milo Forman, Jan Sv&#283;rák, Zden&#283;k\r\n      Sv&#283;rák, Ji&#345;í Menzel.</span></p>\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Music:\r\n      </span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">Bed&#345;ich Smetana, Antonín Dvo&#345;ák,\r\n      Leo Janá&#269;ek, Bohuslav Martin&#367;, Ji&#345;í Stívín, Lubo\r\n      Andrt, Ian Hammer, Emil Viklický.</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr style=\"height:36.0pt\">\r\n    <td width=\"165\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:124.0pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"TableHeading2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Important People</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"449\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:336.6pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Karel IV., Jan Hus, Jan ika,\r\n      Ji&#345;í z Pod&#283;brad, J.A. Komenský, T.G. Masaryk, Václav Klaus, Václav\r\n      Havel, Milo Zeman</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr style=\"height:36.0pt\">\r\n    <td width=\"165\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:124.0pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"TableHeading2\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Past and current events</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td width=\"449\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:336.6pt;padding:0cm 3.5pt 0cm 3.5pt;\r\n  height:36.0pt\">\r\n      <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Founding of Charles University\r\n      (1348), Burning of Jan Hus (1415), Battle of White Mountain - start of\r\n      30-year war (1620), National revival (19<sup>th</sup> century), Foundation\r\n      of Czechoslovakia (1918), Partial occupation by Germany (1938), End of\r\n      Second World War (1945), Communist party takes power (1948), Invasion by\r\n      allied forces (1968), Charter 77 (1977), Velvet Revolution (1989), Split\r\n      with Slovakia (1993).</span></p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">3 Famous Composers</span></h3>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Bed</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">&#345;ich Smetana</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\"> (1828  1884) Popular\r\ncomposer of last century. Like Beethoven composed some of his best works while\r\ncompletely deaf. His works include: Bartered Bride, Dalibor, The Kiss, My\r\nCountry.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">Antonín\r\nDvo&#345;ák</span></b><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">(1841-1904\r\n see above) Other works: Slavonic Dances, Sabat Mater, Rusalka.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Leo\r\nJaná&#269;ek </span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">(1854  1928) Most famous Czech\r\ncomposer of this century. His operas include: </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">Ká&#357;a Kabanová, V&#283;c Makropulos, Lika Bystrouka.</span></p>\r\n<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">5 Important Writers</span></h3>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Jaroslav\r\nHaek</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\"> (1883-1923) Writer and journalist. Wrote\r\nsatirical short stories. During WWI took part in the Russian Revolution. Creator\r\nof Good Soldier vejk a popular satire from WWI translated into many over\r\n20 languages including English.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Karel\r\n&#268;apek</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\"> (1890-1938) Writer, journalist and\r\nplaywright. His 1928 sci-fi drama R.U.R. first used the word robot. His other\r\nworks include <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">War of the Newts</i>, <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">White\r\nIllness</i>, <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\">The Macropulos Thing</i>, <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">Krakatit</i>.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Bohumil\r\nHrabal</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\"> (1914-97) Major works: <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\">I Served the King of England, Larks on a String, Closely Watched Trains,\r\nToo Loud a Solitude.</i></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Josef\r\nkvorecký</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\"> (*1924) Emigrated after 1968.\r\nCurrently lives in Canada. Started and runs publishing firm 68Publishers.\r\nMajor works: <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">The Cowards, Sins for Father\r\nKnox, Swell Season, Tank Platoon.</i></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Milan\r\nKundera</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\"> (*1929) Emigrated after 1968. Currently\r\nlives in France. Wrote his last novel in French. Major works: <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\">The Joke,<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes\">&nbsp; </span>Unbearable\r\nLightness of Being, Laughable Loves, Immortality.</i></span></p>', '2002-10-12 21:49:04', 6447, 31, 50),
(54, 'Czech Mythology', 'Dominik Luke', '<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-top:0cm\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Historical myths\r\nare often better known than facts. Here are </span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nRU\">three</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"> commonly</span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-an', '<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">Praotec &#268;ech (Forefather Czech)</span></h3>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech tribes led by the father &#268;ech\r\nstopped their journey at the &#344;íp mountain (about 50 km north of Prague).\r\nThere he beheld the land of milk and honey and decided to settle. His\r\nbrother Lech had separated from his tribe earlier and became the founder of the\r\nPolish nation.</span></p>\r\n<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">P&#345;emysl and Libue</span></h3>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Countess Libu</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">e,\r\n&#268;ech</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">s grand daughter, ruled the Czech people\r\nafter the death of her father Krok. She was gifted with prophetic powers and\r\nforesaw the founding of Prague. She resided at Vyehrad and in one of her\r\nprophetic moments declared: I see a large city whose fame touches the\r\nstars. She then sent builders into the woods where they found a man making a\r\nthreshold (práh) which also gave a name to the new city (i.e. Praha).</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Once, when presiding over her court, she\r\nmade a decision between two brothers. The one in whose disfavor she had decided,\r\nproclaimed: Sorry are the men who let themselves be ruled by a woman! Libu</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">e</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">\r\nbecame angry and promised the men a firm rule by a man. She sent a delegation to\r\nher secret lover P&#345;emysl to call him to become the king of the Czechs. P&#345;emysl\r\nwas a farmer and the delegation led by Libues horse found him plowing his\r\nfields. Thats why he is known as P</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">&#345;emysl\r\nOrá&#269; (the&nbsp;Plower). </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">Thus the P&#345;emyslid\r\ndynasty ruling the Czech lands for over 300 years was founded.</span></p>\r\n<h3><span lang=\"EN-US\">árka (Maiden War)</span></h3>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Some women, discontent with the rule of\r\nmen, went away and founded a castle called D</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">&#283;vín</span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nRU\">, from where they fought men with great success.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:RU\">One of the\r\nmaidens, </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">árka,</span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:RU\">\r\nlured Ctirad and his men, who had slain man</span><span lang=\"EN-US\">y women,</span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nRU\"> into a trap by pretending to have run away from the women</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:RU\">s\r\ncastle. After Ctirad, having fallen in love at first sight, had celebrated this\r\nencounter and fallen asleep, women stole upon him and his men, who were\r\noverpowered and killed. Ctirad himself was executed at the wheel.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:RU\">Men, angered\r\nby Ctirad</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nRU\">s death stormed D</span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">&#283;vín</span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:RU\">,\r\nthe castle of the women fighters, and killed all women who resisted including </span><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:\r\nCS\">árka</span><span lang=\"RU\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:RU\">.</span></p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n', '2002-10-12 21:51:37', 7438, 31, 60),
(55, 'Czech Food', 'Dominik Luke', 'Food is an important part of any national identity. This is what the Czechs think about their cuisine.', '<p class=\"NormalFirst\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">In his book <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\">Chrám i tvrz</i> (A Cathedral And A Fortress), Pavel Eisner a famous\r\nCzech writer and translator writes about Czech food as one of the blandest and\r\nleast healthy of all. In this it is rivaled probably only by the Ukrainian and\r\nPolish cuisines. Curiously, as Eisner himself noted, Czechs hold a misguided\r\npride in their food. They are especially proud of a Czech specialty <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">knedlík\r\n</i>which is basically boiled bread dough typically served with a healthy (sic!)\r\nserving of fatty pork and sauerkraut. Typical Czech meal contains meat and <i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:\r\nnormal\">knedl</i></span><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">ík</span><span lang=\"CS\">\r\n</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">or potatoes. Vegetables, if present at all, are\r\nboiled. Some claim this is done to ensure total absence of vitamins, and if\r\npossible, flavor. Salads in Czech cuisine contain a lot of mayonnaise,\r\ncheese, salami and sometimes no vegetables at all. Lettuce is a seasonal\r\nvegetable and is not used in salads outside of the summer.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Czech typical selection of spices\r\nconsists of salt, ground black or red pepper. Other popular spices are dill,\r\ncaraway, marjory or thyme. Another popular condiment applied liberally with many\r\nitems on the table is ketchup. It is common to see pizzerias offering ketchup.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Some foreigners may be surprised that\r\neven sweet foods (for example pancakes) are served as the main course at a lunch\r\nor dinner.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The bottom line is that Czech food takes\r\nsome getting used to. But there are many who do begin to like it and discover\r\npossible intricacies, especially if they get to sample the output of a genuine\r\ngrandmother.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:6.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Vegetarians\r\nmay find it difficult to eat well unless they cook for themselves. Many\r\nrestaurants advertise meatless meals which may, however, contain ham (</span><i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"mso-ansi-language:CS\">unka</span></i><span lang=\"EN-US\">)\r\nor be made with lard (<i style=\"mso-bidi-font-style:normal\">sádlo</i>).</span></p>\r\n<div align=\"center\">\r\n  <center>\r\n  <table border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;\r\n mso-table-layout-alt:fixed;mso-padding-alt:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt\">\r\n    <tr>\r\n      <td width=\"604\" valign=\"top\" style=\"width:452.75pt;border:solid windowtext .75pt;\r\n  padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt\">\r\n        <p class=\"TableFirstColumn\" style=\"tab-stops:right 439.45pt\"><b style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight:normal\"><span lang=\"CS\" style=\"font-size:19.0pt;\r\n  mso-bidi-font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Arial;mso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;\r\n  mso-ansi-language:CS\">Jídelní lístek<span style=\"mso-tab-count:1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n        </span>Menu</span></b></p>\r\n        <div style=\"border:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext .75pt;padding:0cm 0cm 1.0pt 0cm\">\r\n          <p class=\"MenuHeading\"><span lang=\"CS\">Polévky<span style=\"mso-tab-count:1\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp